modiﬁed LPNs displayed a more compact structure due to stronger
hydrophobic interactions between the aliphatic chains. In contrast
to the higher ﬂuidity of the L 5 analogues of shorter chain length
(L5N8 and L 5N12), L 5N14- and L 5N16-modiﬁed LPNs displayed smal-
ler average molecular areas due to stronger hydrophobic interac-
tions. The ﬁnding that LPNs modiﬁed with the L 5 analogues of
shorter chain length interact stronger with PS than LPNs modiﬁed
with the L 5 analogues of longer chain length, indicates that the ﬂu-
idity of LPNs inﬂuences their interaction strength with PS.
3.4.3. Effect of L 5 content on interactions between LPNs and PS
Next, we investigated the effect of L 5 content on the interactions
between LPNs and PS using an L 5 analogue of medium aliphatic
chain length, i.e.,L 5N12, at pH 5.4/ionic strength 150 mM. The inter-
actions between LPNs and PS were found to be dependent on the
L5N12 content. The particle size distribution of LPN/PS mixtures
was compared at different L 5N12 contents, i.e. ranging from 0:100
to 100:0 ( Fig. 5 A). For 15 % L 5N12-modiﬁed LPNs, negatively
charged Curosurf /C210SUVs were observed to fuse with the LPNs,
resulting in the appearance of a peak in the DLS measurement.
The size of LPNs incubated with Curosurf /C210SUVs shifted towards
higher sizes with increased L 5N12 content, indicating stronger
interaction. At 50 % (w/w) L 5N12, it is likely that aggregates are
formed. However, L 5N12-siRNA lipoplexes incubated with Curo-
surf/C210SUVs displayed similar size distributions as lipoplexes, which
might be related to the high miscibility of L 5N12 with PS.
Continuous LPN deposition was also detected using QCM-D
without shedding during buffer rinse. For all formulations contain-
ing more than 15 % L 5N12, extensive deposition was observed after
injection ( Fig. 5 B-F). In parallel to the expected large shift in fre-
quency, a large DD was also detected (*) during injection of the for-
mulation. However, both the frequency and the dissipation
decreased after reaching a maximum ( Fig. 5 D-G), possibly due to
disruption of PS bilayers and removal of both PS molecules and
LPNs. This is supported by the formation of LPNs/SUVs aggregate
or large vesicles at L 5N12 contents above 50 %. With increasing
L5N12 content, the afﬁnity of LPNs to PS was increased, the time
required to reach maximum frequency and dissipation change
point (*) was shorter, and the slope of the Df - DD plots was
reduced (Fig. 5H), the latter related to faster kinetics of LPN binding
to PS.
In the lungs, surfactant lipids form a lipid monolayer at the
alveolar air–liquid interface, which is associated to membrane
reservoirs, including MLVs. The structural change of MLVs in the
presence of LPNs was studied using cryo-TEM ( Fig. 6 ). The MLVs
were polydisperse, containing a mixture of multilayer stacks, unil-
amellar vesicles, and multi-vesicular bodies ( Fig. S8), which deﬁne
the typical structure of PS [26]. As expected, non-modiﬁed PLGA
nanoparticles displayed a spherical shape ( Fig. 6 A), and showed
limited interaction with PS vesicles ( Fig. 6 B, red arrow), which is
consistent with the QCM-D results. In addition, 15 % and 50 %
(w/w) L5N12-modiﬁed LPNs displayed spherical nanoparticles with
a size consistent with the particle size measurements and an
electron-dense core ( Fig. 6 C and D ), as reported previously [26].
The siRNA-loaded LPNs modiﬁed with 15 % (w/w) L 5N12 exhibited
a strong interaction with PS MLVs, generating negatively curved
structures in the MLVs (red arrow, Fig. 6E). The resultant structures
might facilitate interaction between the surfactant ﬁlm and the
multilayered structures that are attached in the alveolar ﬂuid
(the surfactant reservoir) [3]. Further increase in the lipidoid con-
tent to 50 % altered the interactions between surfactant layers that
maintain PS structure, e.g., membrane-opened vesicles and smaller
unilamellar vesicles (red arrow, Fig. 6F), which might be related to
the possible nanomaterial-induced withdrawal of SPs. Taken
together, the increased surface charge of LPNs not only leads to fas-
ter LPN binding kinetics to PS, but also enhances the interaction
strength with PS.
3.5. Effect of pathological microenvironments on the interaction
between LPNs and PS
To get insights into the effect of the local environment on LPN-
PS interactions, siRNA-loaded LPNs modiﬁed with L 5 analogues of
different chain lengths were used. Since siRNA-loaded LPNs modi-
