in the ACT4 standard‑dose and ACT5 arms at 6-months, with interpretation of ACT3 sexual function limited by 
small numbers.
Conclusion: PLATO demonstrates the feasibility of risk‑adapted radiotherapy dosing, with excellent PRO 
compliance. Most quality-of-life deficits improved by 6-months, although persistent impairments remained in 
ACT5 patients. Follow‑up to 36-months will further define late effects.
Introduction
The standard of care for the treatment of localised anal squamous cell 
carcinoma (ASCC) has long been established as radiotherapy combined 
with concurrent mitomycin (or cisplatin) and fluoropyrimidine chemo-
therapy[1–6]. Despite concurrent chemoradiotherapy offering high cure 
rates, this treatment is associated with substantial acute and late adverse 
events, in particular bowel, urinary, sexual and skin‑related problems 
impacting on long‑term functioning and quality of life[7,8]. Imple-
mentation of intensity-modulated radiotherapy (IMRT) has enabled 
normal tissue sparing, reducing toxicity while maintaining tumour 
control[9]. However, as toxicities have become more manageable with 
IMRT, clinical practice has diverged internationally, with some centres 
escalating doses to ≥ 60  Gy. Research by our national group evaluating 
tumour control probabilities in both early and late-stage disease 
demonstrated differing dose–response curves and highlighted the need 
to evaluate individualised radiotherapy dose based on tumour stage, 
considering de-escalation in early-stage tumours and dose escalation in 
locally advanced tumours. The Personalising Radiotherapy Dose in Anal 
Cancer (PLATO) platform was developed to examine risk–adapted 
radiotherapy dose optimisation for anal cancer[10].
Systematic reviews have highlighted the lack of data on quality of life 
in anal cancer[7,8]. Of the six randomised controlled trials (RCTs) in 
anal cancer, only one published PRO results, reporting data up to two- 
months post completion of treatment; ACCORD-03[11]. One of the 
barriers to reporting has been the lack of a disease-specific patient re-
ported outcome (PRO). Therefore, previous studies relied on question-
naires developed for other tumour types, limiting their ability to capture 
anal‑cancer‑specific complications[12]. Recognising this limitation, the 
EORTC developed the first anal‑cancer‑specific PRO measure, the 
QLQ‑ANL27, which completed international validation in 2022[13]. 
The QLQ-ANL27 was integrated into the PLATO trial platform in 2017 
following Phase 3 development; it is the first trial to include and report 
results with this measure[14]. We reported our 6-month PROs for ACT4, 
using the EORTC QLQ-C30 and QLQ-ANL27 alongside clinical response 
rates, demonstrating the sensitivity of these PROs to treatment‑related 
changes in sexual, bowel, and global functioning in anal cancer[15].
The PLATO risk‑adapted radiotherapy platform trial offers an op-
portunity to understand not only the impact of treatment on the tra-
jectory of patient experience during the 3-year follow up of the 
individual trials, but also to understand the impact of different stages of 
the disease on symptoms at baseline and throughout follow up. This 
paper reports on the PROs from ACT3, ACT4 and ACT5 up to 6-months 
post-end of treatment.
Methods
Study design
PLATO is a trial platform which uses a master protocol approach to 
evaluate three risk stratified multicentre trials (ACT3, ACT4 and ACT5)
[10]. ACT3 is a non‑randomised phase II trial designed to determine 
whether a highly selective lower‑dose chemoradiotherapy policy results 
in acceptably low rates of 3‑year locoregional failure (LRF) for small 
anal margin lesions treated by local excision. ACT4 is a randomised 
non‑comparative phase II trial designed to assess whether, in early‑stage 
disease, lower‑dose chemoradiotherapy achieves an acceptably low rate 
of 3‑year LRF with reduced toxicity. ACT5 is a randomised comparative 
phase II/III trial with an internal pilot designed to evaluate whether 
dose‑escalated chemoradiotherapy for locally advanced anal cancer 
improves locoregional failure-free survival. The primary outcome is LRF 
rate at 3-years post-randomisation for ACT3 and ACT4 and is LRF-free 
survival at 3-years post-randomisation for ACT5. Trial approval was 
granted from Bradford Leeds Research Ethics Committee(16/YH/0157); 
ISRCTN registry (ISRCTN88455282).
Participants
Eligible patients were 16 years or older with biopsy proven invasive 
primary squamous, basaloid or cloacogenic carcinoma of the anus. Pa-
tients were ECOG performance status 0–1 for ACT4/5 and 0–2 for ACT3; 
patients who were HIV positive on antiretrovirals and CD4 counts > 200 
were eligible (Full details pp1-2). ACT3 patients were staged as T1N0/ 
NxM0 anal margin (perianal) tumours and anal canal superficial inva-
sive squamous cell carcinoma (SISCCA) (version 7.0 of the AJCC 
[American Joint Committee on Cancer])[16]. ACT4 patients were 
staged as T1/T2 (≤4cm), N0 or Nx anal canal or T2 (≤4cm) N0 or Nx 
anal margin (in situ or treated with prior local excision). ACT5 patients 
were staged as T3/4N0 and Tany node positive anal canal or margin. All 
patients provided written consent. Patients provided additional consent 
to take part in the PRO component of data collection. Self-reported data 
on patient gender was collected at baseline (male and female options 
provided). Data on ethnicity was not collected. Patient representatives 
have been involved throughout.
Randomisation, masking and procedures
ACT3 was non-randomised with participants with local excision 
margins > 1 mm offered observation and those ≤ 1 mm margins were 
offered adjuvant chemoradiotherapy (41.4 Gy in 23 fractions) with 
concurrent mitomycin day 1 (12 mg/m2) and capecitabine(825 mg/m2) 
twice daily on days of radiotherapy. In ACT4 patients were randomly 
assigned (in a 1:2 ratio respectively) to receive either standard dose 
IMRT (50.4 Gy 28 fractions) or reduced dose (41.4 Gy in 23 fractions) 
with concurrent mitomycin and capecitabine (as before). In ACT5 pa-
tients were randomised (1:1:1) in 28 fractions to standard dose IMRT 
(53.2 Gy), escalated dose 1 (58.8 Gy) or escalated dose 2 (61.6 Gy) with 
concurrent mitomycin and capecitabine or 5FU (1000 mg/m2) days 1–4 
and days 29–32 (Full details Appendix p3). Randomisation for ACT4 and 
ACT5 was performed using a 24-hour automated system based at the 
Leeds Institute of Clinical Trials Research. Patients and investigators 
were not masked to treatment allocation.
Procedures
PROs were collected on paper or electronically depending on patient 
preference, at baseline, in the last week of treatment and 6-weeks, 6, 12, 
24 and 36 months post end of treatment, via the validated EORTC QLQ- 
C30 and QLQ-ANL27 questionnaires[13,17]. Participants completed the 
initial Phase 3 version of the QLQ-ANL27 as the final Phase 4 validation 
of the questionnaire had not been completed[13,18]. We report using 
the Phase 3 version of the QLQ-ANL27 scoring manual. There were 
minor changes to the questionnaire following Phase 4; modifications to 
(1) the ordering of questions related to sexual function and subsequent 
scoring; (2) the addition of non-scored conditional questions to improve 
readability and flow, and (3) changing the bowel scale from a function 
A. Gilbert et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Radiotherapy and Oncology 223 (2026) 111706 
2

scale to a symptom scale. PRO data collection was paused from 30/04/ 
2020 – 19/05/2021 due to COVID-19 (Appendix page 11).
Outcomes
The primary objective of the study is to assess locoregional failure 
(LRF), defined as failure at the primary site (local) and/or surrounding 
nodal sites (regional). For ACT3 and ACT4, the primary endpoint is 3- 
year LRF-free rate, and for ACT5 the primary endpoint is 3-year LRF- 
free survival (assessed using a time to event analysis).
PROs as assessed by EORTC QLQ-C30 and QLQ-ANL27 from baseline 
up to 3-years are a key secondary endpoint. We present the analysis of 
PROs over the 3 trials up to 6-months, with the aim of reporting the 
trajectory of patient experience from baseline disease-related symptoms 
to early treatment-related side effects.
Statistical analysis
As PROs were a secondary outcome, sample sizes were established on 
the basis of the primary outcome with sample size targets of 90, 162 and 
459 for ACT3, ACT4 and ACT5 respectively [10]. Analysis took place 
when the participants had been followed up for 6-months post-end of 
treatment (or trial entry for the ACT3 observation arm). Baseline ques-
tionnaires were to be completed at time of trial entry; for ACT3 patients, 
this was < 3-months from local excision. The analysis was performed 
using SAS version 9.4 (SAS Institute). There was no formal statistical 
comparison of PROs between the different trials or trial arms, as per 
design. Observations between trial arms are exploratory as the sample 
size is not powered for comparative analyses. Summaries use the 
intention to treat population for ACT3 and ACT5, and the modified 
intention to treat population for ACT4, where participants are included 
in their assigned arm if they received at least one dose of trial treatment. 
EORTC-QLQ guidelines were used for analyses and management of 
missing data, with missing values in scaled items derived by imputation
[13,19]. Higher symptom scores reflect a greater level of symptoms, 
whereas higher functional scores reflect a better level of functioning. 
Changes in mean scores were classified as per Osoba et al as a small 
change of 5 to 10 points (unlikely to be clinically relevant), moderate 
change 10 to 20 points, or a large change > 20 points[20]. We report 
graphically the PRO results of key symptoms and functional issues as 
defined in CORMAC and FDA guidance[12,21]. At the point of final 
analysis for all trials, the missing data mechanism for missing items 
within returned questionnaires will be assessed; this missing data is 
currently assumed to be missing at random, however, we included an 
initial evaluation of missing data by trial arm and gender. Additionally, 
exploratory analyses for differences in sexual function scores based on 
age (<60 and > 60 years) and by stoma status for ACT5 patients was 
carried out.
Role of the funding source
The funders of the study (Cancer Research UK, CRUK, and Stand Up 
to Cancer) played no part in study design, data collection, data analysis, 
data interpretation or writing of this report. All authors had full access to 
trial data and had final responsibility for the decision to submit for 
publication. CRUK reviewed, approved and funded the study design 
(A24480: CRUK/15/007).
Results
Between 1 February 2017 and 31 August 2023, 709 patients were 
recruited across 36 UK sites; 706 formed the mITT population, which 
includes all recruited ACT3 and ACT5 participants, and ACT4 partici-
pants who received at least one dose of trial treatment. In ACT3 83 
patients were recruited; 39 patients underwent local excision alone 
(observation arm) and 44 patients received adjuvant CRT (41.4 Gy). In 
ACT4, 163 patients were recruited, with 160 used in the mITT analysis 
after three patients were withdrawn prior to commencing treatment as 
they became ineligible. Within the mITT population, 55 patients 
received standard dose (50.4 Gy) and 105 patients received reduced 
dose (41.4 Gy). In ACT5, 463 patients were recruited: 154 received 
standard dose (53.2 Gy), 155 received 58.8 Gy and 154 received 61.6 Gy 
in 28 fractions. Across ACT5, 82 % received capecitabine and 18 % 5FU. 
Table 1 shows baseline characteristics from the three trials. Notable 
differences included a higher proportion of patients who were HIV 
positive in the ACT3 observation arm (7.7 %, vs ACT3 adjuvant 0.0 % vs 
ACT4 3.1 % vs ACT5 1.9 %) and male (38.5 %, vs ACT4 26.9 % vs ACT5 
26.8 %). ACT4 participants were also proportionally older (Median 66.0 
years; IQR 58.0–71.5) compared with the other two trials (ACT3 59.0 
years; IQR 50.0–66.0); ACT5 61.0 years; IQR 53.0 – 68.0). All other 
baseline characteristics (except stage and tumour size) were equivalent 
across the trials.
PRO consent was 98.9 % (698/706), with 86.0 % completion at 
6–months (Fig. 1; Table 5/Fig. 1 Appendix pp12-13). Completion rates 
for the questionnaires after baseline ranged between 75.5 % and 95.3 % 
in the different trials arms with no apparent differences seen by trial arm 
or gender (Appendix p16). 19 % opted to complete online, 81 % on 
paper, with 15 % of those choosing online changing modality to paper 
during follow up. There were no gender or age differences between 
online and paper choice (male 24.2 %, female 17.0 %; online mean age 
59 years vs 61 years for paper). 22 of the 36 sites (61.1 %) recruited 10 
or more patients. Although overall PRO completion rates were not 
different by centre, of these 22, 10 sites had less than 15 % of their 
recruited patients choosing online, with six sites not recruiting any pa-
tients to online completion. ACT3 and ACT4 participants had similar 
PRO scores at baseline. In contrast, ACT5 patients reported markedly 
worse function and symptom scores (>10–point absolute difference) for 
multiple issues at baseline, including worse social and role functioning, 
bowel function, pain and anorectal pain, toilet dependency, planning 
activities, cleaning yourself more often, fatigue, loss of appetite and 
constipation (All PRO data: Appendix Table 3/4 pp4-10).
For patients who received treatment, almost all aspects of global 
QOL, function and symptoms deteriorated in the final week of chemo-
radiotherapy (Fig. 2a-h). However, most issues rapidly resolved to 
baseline levels by 6-weeks to 6-months across the trials. Specifically, 
large to moderate declines in scores (>10 points) at the end of treatment 
were reported for global QOL, role, cognitive and social function, along 
with large to moderate deteriorations in symptoms of fatigue, nausea 
and vomiting, diarrhoea, loss of appetite, general and anorectal pain, 
bowel symptoms, sexual function (male and female), urinary frequency, 
toilet dependency, planning activities and cleaning yourself more often. 
By 6-weeks, most initial large declines in scores had reduced to mod-
erate impairments, with fatigue, urinary frequency and bowel function 
demonstrating a comparatively slower recovery. Further improvements 
were evident for most issues by 6-months. However, despite returning to 
baseline level scores, ACT5 participants continued to report residual 
deficits at 6-months, with absolute mean scores remaining > 10points 
worse than ACT3/ACT4 participants for bowel function, toilet de-
pendency, and cleaning yourself more often. Importantly, in ACT5, for 
patients with a stoma, bowel function scores remained relatively un-
changed (<10 points) from baseline scores at the end of treatment and 
over the 6-month follow up period across all arms.
In the ACT3 observation arm, scores were generally stable over 6 
months, but patients reported a moderate decline (10–20 points) in 
global QOL and social function scores and moderate increase in toilet 
dependency and planning activities. For all patients receiving treatment, 
at 6 months general pain and anorectal pain had improved to baseline or 
better than baseline.
At 6‑months, participants in the ACT3 adjuvant‑arm reported mod-
erate declines in bowel function (non-stoma), toilet dependency, plan-
ning activities, cleaning yourself more often, urinary frequency, and 
female sexual function, although these absolute scores were comparable 
