symptoms and functioning at the end of treatment with partial recovery 
at 6-weeks and further improvements for most issues by 6-months. 
Although these findings are clinically intuitive, this is the first time, to 
our knowledge that the quantitative differences in presenting symptoms 
and trajectory over time has been reported in the literature. In partic-
ular, we highlight the significant symptom and functional burden of 
patients with more advanced disease (ACT5) at baseline as well as the 
ongoing burden of symptoms and functional impact sustained up to the 
6-month timepoint. It is important to highlight the inherent disease- 
stage differences to improve supportive care, although with no notable 
differences observed between the different ACT5 dose schedules at this 
early timepoint.
This is in part due to the availability for the first time of an anal 
cancer specific validated questionnaire, the EORTC QLQ-ANL27. Our 
findings echo the measurement properties of the QLQ-ANL27 reported 
in the Phase 4 validation study, where non‑stoma bowel symptoms, 
anorectal pain and vaginal symptom scales were sensitive to change 
during and after treatment, and separated participants by treatment 
phase and performance status[13]. While we carried out the analysis on 
the Phase 3 version of the QLQ-ANL27, it will be important to reanalyse 
our data using the updated sexual item scoring to assess the impact on 
findings. To our knowledge, outside of the PLATO trials and the ques-
tionnaire development work, only one other cross-sectional study has 
reported results using the QLQ-ANL27, with previous work commonly 
using EORTC QLQ-CR29/CR38 as a substitute[7,22]. In this study, pa-
tients completed the questionnaire at > 7 months after the end of 
treatment and received median doses to the primary tumour over 55.8 
Gy. The key finding was that age-matched patients treated with IMRT 
had markedly better scores for most issues on the QLQ-ANL-27 
compared to 3D conformal techniques although the numbers were 
small. This is interesting in light of our findings for patients with a stoma 
from baseline, who reported stable bowel function scores over the 
course of treatment and follow up, highlighting the benefits of IMRT in 
reducing dose to bowel in improving the tolerability of treatment.
Only two of the landmark anal cancer RCTs have collected or re-
ported PROs. ACT1 reported only in abstract format that overall quality 
of life was improved with the addition of concurrent chemotherapy to 
radiotherapy[23]. ACCORD03 reported results in 119 patients using 
EORTC QLQ-C30 at baseline and 2-months after treatment and similarly 
found improvement in pain and return to baseline or better than base-
line scores for many domains by 2-months[11]. Two prospective lon-
gitudinal studies of patients treated with IMRT collected data at multiple 
timepoints and reported similar large declines in multiple domains of 
the QLQ-C30 at the end of treatment with recovery seen by 6-months
[8,24,25].
Our results significantly add to this literature, as the largest pro-
spectively collected PRO dataset across multiple centres with high PRO 
consent and excellent completion rates. The use of a sensitive, validated 
disease-specific PRO enables direct comparison of dose-modulation 
strategies in both acute and late side effect settings and the impact of 
disease stage on symptom burden. We used a conservative clinically 
meaningful difference threshold (>10 points change or group-difference 
in mean scores) to assess for more impactful group-level differences and 
change from baseline for EORTC QLQ-C30 and QLQ-ANL27, with these 
thresholds extrapolated from the EORTC QLQ-C30 literature. There is a 
wider literature on meaningful thresholds within 5–10 range for the 
QLQ-C30, however, when looking for evidence to support a new treat-
ment, a 10-point threshold is advocated[26,27]. Our change from 
baseline scores reflect those observed within the Phase 4 QLQ-ANL27 
validation publication – moderate effects sizes associated with mean 
score change of 17 points, and large with > 30 points[13]. Completion 
rates were high throughout all follow up timepoints and with no notable 
differences seen in missing data through our exploratory analyses, 
however, there could be completion bias in the results seen. Later follow 
up beyond 6-months will strengthen these results and enable further 
evaluation of late effects and the impact of dose-modification on normal 
tissue response in particular the initial findings that sexual function at 6- 
months appeared better following dose-reduced treatment in ACT4. 
Exploratory analyses of the impact of stoma and age on sexual function 
will be interesting to explore further at later timepoints. Interestingly, it 
will also be important to follow up the moderate declines in global QOL 
and social function alongside worsening toilet dependency and planning 
activities scores in the ACT3 observation arm, which may reflect 
ongoing postoperative recovery and remodelling following local exci-
sion. However, for the limitations of this work it is important to high-
light the smaller sample size within ACT3 limiting interpretation of 
sexual function scores due to gender split and that since data was not 
collected on ethnicity, menopause and relationship status, exploratory 
analyses for these characteristics were not possible. In addition, it was 
interesting to observe the relatively low pick-up rates of electronic 
completion of PROs, this is likely in part due to all patients completing 
PROs on paper at baseline and complicated by logistical issues during 
the COVID-19 pandemic where online completion was not available. 
However, we identified a possible centre preference to offering paper 
PRO completion over online completion and in these cases, it is not clear 
if this reflected a true patient choice of completion method. However, as 
completion rates were high with low rates of missing data, the paper 
approach clearly was the patient preference.
For future work, we plan to report PRO results across the three trials 
at 3-years to assess late side effects and carry out an additional analysis 
using the Phase 4 scoring update to compare and contrast findings for 
sexual issues with the modified scoring manual. The changes to the 
scoring for sexual issues were based on poor psychometric performance 
in the hypothesised scoring structure (at Phase 3) within the Phase 4 
validation[13]. This resulted in a revised scale structure with 3 single 
items for interest in sex, sex life, and pain during intercourse, 1 item on 
erectile function for male patients and a 3-item scale for vaginal symp-
toms for female patients. These scoring changes should enable better 
differentiation of clinically meaningful differences within the reanalysis 
of our data for sexual function.
In conclusion, these findings highlight the sensitivity and utility of 
the EORTC QLQ-ANL27 in detecting acute treatment effects across the 
three trials and also, persistent disease-related impairments in patients 
receiving dose-escalated therapy (ACT5). Incorporating validated, 
disease-specific PRO measures such as QLQ-ANL27 is essential for 
guiding future treatment optimisation. Longer-term follow-up to 36- 
months will provide critical insights into the late impact of risk- 
adapted radiotherapy.
