to those in the ACT3 observation arm and ACT4 at the same timepoint. A 
large decline was also reported in male sexual function scores in ACT3 
adjuvant arm; however, interpretation is limited by very small numbers 
(four participants). At the 6-month timepoint in ACT4, both treatment 
groups reported a moderate deterioration in toilet dependency relative 
to baseline. For female patients, a large deterioration in sexual function 
scores was reported at the end of treatment in standard-dose and mod-
erate decline in the reduced-dose arm. Female sexual function improved 
to near‑baseline levels by 6-months in the reduced‑dose arm, but no 
comparable improvement was seen in the standard‑dose arm. For male 
patients in ACT4, a large decline for standard-dose and a small decline 
for reduced-dose was reported at the end of treatment; sexual function 
returned to baseline following reduced‑dose IMRT, whereas persistently 
lower scores were reported after standard‑dose. At 6-months, partici-
pants in ACT5 reported moderate declines in male and female sexual 
function and moderate improvements in pain, with all other issues 
returning to baseline or close to baseline levels, albeit with residual 
absolute score deficits compared with ACT3 and ACT4. In further 
exploratory analyses, there was some evidence that younger female 
patients (<60 years) in ACT4 and ACT5 reported worse sexual function 
scores at 6-months than patients > 60 years (>10 points difference); 
with no differences observed for men (note small numbers). There was 
also a trend towards slightly poorer sexual function scores (>10 points 
difference) for men and women with a stoma compared to those without 
in ACT5, although again, numbers are small (Appendix pp17-18).
In summary, noting that the sample size is not powered for statistical 
comparison between trial arms, for male and female sexual function, 
scores remained > 10 points below baseline at 6–months in ACT4 
standard–dose and across all ACT5 arms, whereas ACT4 reduced–dose 
arm returned to baseline. ACT3 adjuvant arm patients reported mod-
erate declines in female sexual function at 6-months, with equivalent 
absolute scores to ACT4 reduced dose arm. The small numbers in ACT3 
make meaningful interpretation of male sexual function challenging.
Discussion
This analysis of the PROs up to 6-months for the PLATO trial plat-
form shows a consistent pattern of acute deterioration of QOL, 
