4

Policy implications
European PBT infrastructure is under increasing strain. Policymakers 
face distinct strategic imperatives depending on national geography. In 
smaller, densely populated countries, centralised multi-room centres 
remain an efficient organisational model. However, in large and 
geographically extensive countries, reliance on high-capacity hubs 
located primarily in capital cities is unlikely to be sufficient to ensure 
equitable access. National infrastructure strategies may therefore 
consider decentralised single-room facilities in secondary urban regions 
where spatial exclusion is most pronounced. The candidate locations 
identified here serve as data-driven inputs for planning discussions 
rather than as implementation-ready proposals. Establishing new cen-
tres involves capital investment, regulatory approval, and workforce 
recruitment timelines that extend beyond the scope of this modelling 
framework.
Limitations
This study has several limitations. First, findings are most directly 
applicable to high-income countries with developed transport networks. 
Second, demand was assumed to scale proportionally with population 
size and did not incorporate regional variation in cancer epidemiology 
or projected internal migration patterns. Third, the baseline model 
applied uniform assumptions regarding treatment capacity (300 pa-
tients per room per year), PBT eligibility (4.3 %), and full utilisation 
among theoretically eligible patients. The assumed treatment-room 
throughput may overestimate effective capacity, while full utilisation 
of theoretical eligibility may overestimate realised clinical PBT uti-
lisation because of physician and patient preferences, clinical uncer-
tainty, referral pathways, reimbursement policies, and local eligibility 
