criteria. However, extensive sensitivity analyses covering treatment 
capacities of 150–300 patients per room, theoretical eligibility estimates 
of 4.3–15 %, separate implementation-adjusted utilisation assumptions 
of 1–2 %, and travel thresholds of 240–360 min showed that the prin-
cipal relative spatial patterns remained broadly consistent across the 
tested scenarios. Candidate locations were restricted to the ten most 
populous cities to ensure proximity to existing multidisciplinary 
oncology ecosystems; in practice, regional hubs in underserved areas 
may offer additional equity gains.
For the 2030 projections, we adopted a pragmatic scenario-based 
approach to future supply estimation. Facilities listed as “in planning” 
or with ambiguous status in the PTCOG database [7,8] were included to 
reflect an upper-bound scenario of potential capacity expansion. This 
assumption does not imply guaranteed commissioning but captures the 
plausible activation of currently latent projects under rising cancer 
incidence and increasing policy and clinical demand for PBT services. 
Conversely, projects judged to have low feasibility based on prolonged 
inactivity or regulatory discontinuation (C´ordoba, Spain; Ruˇzomberok, 
Slovakia; Lausanne, Switzerland) were excluded.
Finally, the E2SFCA method measures potential spatial accessibility, 
which is the chance to reach treatment based on existing infrastructure, 
rather than actual utilisation. The real uptake of PBT depends on addi-
tional factors, including clinician awareness and referral practices, pa-
tient preferences, institutional incentives, and insurance or 
reimbursement pathways, none of which are included in the model. 
Additionally, country-specific cancer registry data would provide more 
detailed estimates of demand. GLOBOCAN provides the only cross- 
nationally comparable incidence dataset, and its estimates are them-
selves derived from national registry data where available.
Conclusions
Substantial capacity deficits and pronounced spatial inequities 
continue to characterise the European proton beam therapy landscape. 
Our projections suggest that, without targeted policy intervention, these 
disparities will intensify under baseline supply–demand assumptions by 
2030, as cancer incidence rises and infrastructure expansion stagnates in 
traditionally well-served regions. We demonstrate that while central-
ised, high-throughput centres remain efficient in smaller countries, they 
are structurally insufficient to ensure equitable access in geographically 
extensive nations. In such settings, existing infrastructure should be 
complemented by decentralised networks utilising single-room facil-
ities. By identifying specific locations where additional capacity yields 
the greatest reductions in spatial inequality, this study provides 
actionable evidence to inform strategic planning of proton therapy 
infrastructure across Europe.
