Methods
Data sources
Data on operational and planned PBT centres were retrieved from the 
Particle Therapy Co-Operative Group (PTCOG) database [7,8]. The 
study included 31 countries: the EU-27, the UK, and EFTA states (Ice-
land, Norway, and Switzerland). Liechtenstein was excluded due to its 
reliance on cross-border healthcare agreements. The operational status, 
room capacity, and geographic coordinates of each centre were verified 
against institutional reports and public documentation to distinguish 
clinical treatment rooms from research infrastructure. Facilities 
providing only ocular therapy were excluded because of distinct clinical 
pathways. Carbon ion therapy centres were excluded owing to distinct 
clinical indications and a substantially smaller patient population. 
Geographic accessibility was assessed across 69,918 administrative 
subregions from the Database of Global Administrative Areas (GADM 
v3.6) [9]. The highest-resolution administrative level available for each 
country was selected to maximise spatial granularity. Subregional 
population counts were derived by aggregating constrained WorldPop 
2020 data (1 km2 resolution) within administrative boundaries [10].
Supply-demand framework
Spatial accessibility was quantified using the enhanced two-step 
floating catchment area (E2SFCA) method, which integrates car travel 
times, centre capacity, and competing demand from neighbouring areas 
[11]. Travel times were calculated using the OpenStreetMap road 
network, applying a Gaussian decay function with a catchment 
threshold of 360 min; we also checked a threshold of 240 min in the 
Supplementary Materials. This threshold represents the maximum travel 
time beyond which accessibility is assumed to be negligible, consistent 
with empirical patient travel patterns [11,12]. Cross-border flows were 
excluded to reflect national reimbursement frameworks [13]; a sensi-
tivity analysis permitting unrestricted cross-border mobility is presented 
in the Supplementary Materials.
Supply capacity was capped at 300 patients per treatment room per 
year, representing an upper-bound estimate based on reported 
maximum throughputs [14], with additional sensitivity analysis at 150 
patients per room per year in the Supplementary Materials. Demand was 
modelled in three steps. Cancer incidence was estimated using GLO-
BOCAN data [15] and downscaled to the subregional level proportional 
to population size. The number of patients requiring radiotherapy was 
derived using country-specific estimated optimal utilisation rates from 
the ESTRO–HERO project [16].
A uniform PBT eligibility rate of 4.3 % of patients estimated to 
require radiotherapy was applied across countries, based on the UK 
expert consensus [17]. The sensitivity of the findings to these assump-
tions was evaluated using theoretical PBT eligibility rates of 10 % and 
15 %, separate implementation-adjusted utilisation assumptions of 1 % 
and 2 % of patients estimated to require radiotherapy, and alternative 
treatment-capacity and travel-threshold assumptions (Supplementary 
Materials). Finally, system efficiency was defined as the national 
capacity-to-demand ratio (reflecting the adequacy of supply to meet 
theoretical demand), while spatial equity was quantified using the 
weighted Gini coefficient derived from E2SFCA accessibility scores.
Projections to 2030 and location of new centres
Projections to 2030 include all facilities listed as planned or under 
construction in the PTCOG database, with demand adjusted for fore-
casted cancer incidence. The 2030 horizon was chosen because it is the 
latest date for which facility-level data on planned or committed projects 
are available in the PTCOG database. Two scenarios were evaluated: a 
baseline scenario assuming completion of all planned infrastructure, and 
an equity-optimised scenario separately evaluating, within each 
country, the effect of adding one additional single-room PBT unit. 
Candidate sites were restricted to the ten most populous cities in each 
country. An iterative simulation was conducted for each candidate to 
identify the location that minimised the national Gini coefficient of 
spatial accessibility. For the main comparative analysis, countries pro-
jected to have domestic PBT infrastructure in 2030 were ranked ac-
cording to the absolute reduction in the national weighted Gini index, 
with each country represented by its best-performing candidate loca-
tion. The broader set of country-specific candidate locations, including 
countries without projected domestic infrastructure, is presented in the 
Supplementary Materials along with further methodological details, 
including the full mathematical formulation of the E2SFCA model, 
optimisation procedure, and sensitivity analyses.
Results
Across the 31 analysed countries, 26 PBT centres were operational in 
2025, providing a total of 54 treatment rooms. By 2030, infrastructure is 
projected to expand to 43 centres and 77 rooms. Fourteen countries had 
domestic PBT capacity in 2025; Portugal and Romania are projected to 
establish their first centres by 2030. The analysis included 69,918 
administrative subregions (median area 18 km2 [IQR 6–56]; median 
population 2,109 [IQR 451–6,595]).
Estimated demand for PBT in 2025 totalled 78,500 patients, of whom 
67,000 resided in countries with domestic PBT infrastructure. Despite 
national availability, 67.2 % of these patients lived in areas with critical 
access shortages (E2SFCA < 0.25), whereas only 4.6 % resided in areas 
with high accessibility (E2SFCA > 0.75). Among subregions within 
countries operating PBT centres, the E2SFCA index ranged from 0 (no 
accessible supply within the catchment area) to values exceeding 1.0, 
indicating that supply met or exceeded theoretical demand (Fig. 1). Low 
accessibility persisted around some operational centres because their 
limited treatment capacity was shared among large populations within 
the corresponding catchment areas.
Marked heterogeneity was observed at the country level (Fig. 2). 
Austria, Czechia, and Denmark combined relatively high capacity-to- 
demand ratios (>0.80) with comparatively equitable spatial distribu-
tions (Gini < 0.35). In contrast, Norway showed the highest capacity 
surplus (ratio 1.04) alongside substantial spatial inequality (Gini 0.52). 
The Netherlands and Belgium exhibited low spatial inequality (Gini <
0.10) despite limited overall capacity, whereas France, Spain, Poland, 
and Italy combined pronounced capacity deficits (ratio < 0.20) with 
high spatial inequality (Gini > 0.50). In the sensitivity analysis simu-
lating unrestricted cross-border mobility, system-wide accessibility 
gains were minimal, as most countries (except Norway) lacked the 
surplus capacity to absorb external demand. Cross-border flows pri-
marily established access for nations without domestic infrastructure, 
specifically Croatia, Hungary, Luxembourg, Slovakia, and Slovenia 
(Supplementary Table S4).
Seventeen additional PBT centres are planned by 2030, but projected 
cancer incidence is expected to increase by 9 % over the same period. As 
a result, regional trajectories diverge (Fig. 3). Southern and Eastern 
European countries are projected to improve both capacity–demand 
balance and spatial equity through planned infrastructure expansion. 
Conversely, most Northern and Western European countries show 
declining capacity relative to projected demand.
The optimised placement of a single additional PBT room may not 
only increase treatment capacity but also substantially reduce spatial 
inequality. Table 1 presents the ten countries in which one additional 
strategically located facility produced the largest absolute reduction in 
the national weighted Gini index, with the greatest reductions observed 
in Sweden, Portugal, and Austria. Across these ten countries, the na-
tional Gini coefficient decreased by a mean of 35.4 %. Detailed results 
for all evaluated countries and candidate locations are provided in the 
Supplementary Materials.
