by philanthropic financing, with new proton therapy systems funded by 
the Amancio Ortega Foundation and distributed across several autono-
mous communities [20]. Private investment may complement public 
capacity, but the failure of a private proton therapy network in the 
United Kingdom highlights the financial risks associated with devel-
oping and operating high-cost infrastructure without sustainable patient 
volumes and reimbursement arrangements [21]. Regardless of the 
financing model, new investments should be coordinated across the 
relevant national and regional authorities and integrated with work-
force planning, referral pathways, and reimbursement policies to 
improve both overall capacity and equity of access.
Strategic optimisation of network expansion
Cancer incidence in Europe is projected to increase by approximately 
9 % by 2030, driven by demographic ageing and epidemiological trends 
[6]. Against this background, several established PBT systems in 
Northern and Western Europe show signs of relative stagnation. Without 
further capacity expansion, rising demand is likely to erode system ef-
ficiency despite historically strong infrastructure. Conversely, many 
Southern and Eastern European countries are on a trajectory of 
improvement, supported by planned investments and the establishment 
of initial domestic capacity in Portugal and Romania.
Crucially, our findings indicate that cross-border patient mobility 
cannot, at scale, compensate for domestic structural shortages. Although 
cross-border healthcare is formally enabled under EU Directive 2011/ 
24/EU, no country, aside from Norway, possesses sufficient surplus ca-
pacity to systematically accommodate foreign patients. Consequently, 
expanding domestic infrastructure is therefore likely to remain neces-
sary for addressing structural capacity gaps. Compact single-room PBT 
systems appear particularly well suited to this role. Their lower capital 
requirements and flexible siting facilitate integration into existing 
regional cancer centres [22]. Our optimisation analysis demonstrates 
that the targeted placement of such units, selected to minimise national 
Gini coefficients, yields disproportionate gains in spatial equity.
Our model captures infrastructure-based capacity but does not 
explicitly account for workforce availability. PBT delivery requires 
highly specialised personnel, and staffing shortfalls have already been 
reported at existing centres, although projected demand growth for 
radiotherapy professionals in Europe is expected to be lower than in 
other regions worldwide [23]. Our sensitivity analysis, based on a 150- 
patient annual room capacity, accounts for the increased clinical 
workload associated with complex paediatric cases, adaptive planning, 
and potential workforce shortages. Although emerging technologies 
such as FLASH irradiation and AI-driven workflows may eventually 
enhance throughput [24], current expansion is constrained by the need 
for specialised training pipelines.
