surgery. Intensification was selectively applied to non-responders (ERI 
> 13.1), who had a baseline CR probability of only 3% [16]. The 
adopted dose escalation (EQD2 62.2 Gy) was associated with a CR rate 
of 21.9%, representing a clinically meaningful increase. Study outcomes 
mirrored model predictions: no benefit was observed in patients with 
ERI > 37, whereas intermediate responders (ERI 13–37) achieved an 
approximately 20% increase in CR. These findings suggest that higher 
doses or alternative intensification strategies may be needed in highly 
resistant tumours, in line with prior data correlating BED and tumour 
regression [7,8,21]. The adaptive approach adopted in THUNDER-2 
[11,22] enabled selective dose escalation while limiting normal tissue 
exposure. Although complete responses remained limited in poor re-
sponders, the increase in TRG1–2 rates supports further investigation. 
Morphological features (tumour size, circumferential extension, nodal 
involvement) were associated with outcome, but ERI remained signifi-
cantly associated with CR at 2 years in multivariable analysis. Given the 
response-adapted design of THUNDER-2, ERI should be interpreted as a 
treatment-selection biomarker within this adaptive strategy rather than 
as a pure prognostic factor. Responders also achieved significantly 
higher TME-free survival, supporting ERI-based stratification as a 
facilitator of organ preservation without compromising oncological 
outcomes. Although poor tumour response is generally associated with a 
higher risk of distant failure, no significant differences in DFS were 
observed between ERI groups in the present study. This finding may be 
related to the limited number of DFS events, the relatively short follow- 
up, and the adaptive dose-escalation strategy applied to non-responders. 
Treatment was well tolerated, with most toxicities limited to grade 1–2 
events and only 6.4% grade ≥ 3 GI toxicity. The adaptive workflow with 
oART and cine-MRI gating likely contributed to the favourable toxicity 
profile [20,23]. No local recurrences were observed after surgery. 
Certain limitations should be acknowledged. Despite prospective 
enrollment, the single-centre design and small sample size limit sub-
group analyses. Long-term oncological outcomes require extended 
follow-up. Although the observed CR rate exceeded the historical 
benchmark and predefined target, interpretation is limited by the use of 
non-contemporaneous historical controls; the efficacy signal should 
therefore be regarded cautiously without a randomized comparison 
group. The optimal ERI threshold may also require refinement in larger 
multicentre cohorts. Despite these limitations, our results support the 
clinical utility of ERI within an adaptive response-guided strategy for 
LARC. Future studies should focus on multicentre validation of ERI and 
its integration with radiomic, biological, and AI-based predictive 
models. Future prospective investigations should include dedicated 
patient-reported outcome and quality-of-life assessments to further 
characterize the functional impact of ERI-guided organ-preservation 
strategies. ERI-guided MRI-based adaptive dose escalation proved 
feasible and safe, with the potential to support personalized organ- 
