Patients with ERI ≤ 13.1 continued the original schedule, whereas ERI 
> 13.1 triggered adaptive dose escalation: online replanning was per-
formed with dose escalation to a boost volume (PTV3, defined as re-
sidual GTV at fraction 10 with a 3-mm margin). Starting from fraction 
11, a fractional dose of 2.54 Gy was delivered to PTV3, achieving a total 
dose of 60.1 Gy (Biological Effective Dose [BED] 74.6 Gy; equivalent 
dose in 2-Gy fractions [EQD2] 62.2 Gy, α/β = 10). Daily oART was 
performed whenever relevant anatomical changes were observed, with 
daily recontouring of residual GTV/PTV3 and plan reoptimization 
[12,14]. Treatment delivery used a cine-MRI gating protocol (5% region 
of interest (ROI) threshold).
Concomitant chemotherapy consisted of continuous infusion 5-FU 
(225 mg/m2/day) or oral chronomodulated capecitabine (1650 mg/ 
m2/day). No consolidation chemotherapy was administered before 
response assessment or surgery. Surgical management entailed TME 
(anterior resection, abdominoperineal resection (APR), or total procto-
colectomy). Adjuvant chemotherapy was discussed within a multidis-
ciplinary team (MDT) framework.
2.2. Clinical restaging and response assessment
Clinical response was evaluated with contrast-enhanced pelvic MRI 
and digital rectal examination (DRE) 8–10 weeks after nCRT. Patients 
with nCR or cCR underwent rectoscopy for confirmation of organ- 
preservation eligibility. Criteria for cCR included a flat scar without 
ulceration at rectoscopy, absence of palpable mass at DRE, and MRI 
findings consistent with fibrosis without residual mass or restricted 
diffusion [12]. cCR was confirmed by a second assessment 12–14 weeks 
after nCRT [17]. Patients with confirmed cCR or nCR were considered 
for NOM (WW or local excision (LE)). Near-complete response was 
defined as absence of palpable mass, minor mucosal irregularities at 
endoscopy, and no suspicious nodes on MRI; these patients were 
considered for local excision [13,14]. Patients with partial response, 
stable disease, or progression underwent surgery 12–14 weeks after 
nCRT. Pathological complete response was defined as ypT0pN0 or 
ypT0pNx after local excision; pTis was also considered pCR. Tumour 
regression grade (TRG) was assessed histopathologically. TME-free 
survival was defined as the time from radiotherapy completion to 
TME, with non-TME patients censored at last follow-up.
2.3. Follow-up
Gastrointestinal (GI), genitourinary (GU), and hematological toxic-
ities were assessed weekly during CRT and recorded according to CTCAE 
(Common Terminology Criteria for Adverse Events) v4 within six 
months after RT completion [15]. In cases of grade ≥ 3 GI or hemato-
logical toxicity, RT and chemotherapy were temporarily withheld until 
recovery to grade ≤ 2. Late toxicity was assessed during follow-up ac-
cording to institutional practice.
2.4. Statistical analyses
The THUNDER-2 trial applied ERI to escalate radiation dose to 60.1 
Gy in non-responders, aiming to increase the CR rate by 10% above the 
historical 3% benchmark [16]. CR was defined as pCR or sustained cCR 
at 2 years, as most local regrowths occur within the first 24 months after 
treatment [17]. A sample size of 50 evaluable patients was estimated; 
accounting for a 20% dropout rate, target accrual was 63 patients. All 
efficacy and time-to-event analyses were performed on the evaluable 
population (n = 62). Descriptive and comparative analyses were per-
formed between ERI groups. Univariate and multivariable logistic 
regression identified predictors of CR, including age, sex, disease stage, 
tumour location, overall treatment time (OTT), chemotherapy, surgery 
type, surgical interval (SI), tumour extension, circumferential distribu-
tion, GTV, and ERI. Quadrant involvement was defined according to the 
rectal wall sector showing the greatest circumferential tumour extension 
and categorized as anterior, posterior, right lateral, or left lateral wall 
involvement. Kaplan–Meier analyses were performed for overall sur-
vival (OS), disease-free survival (DFS), and TME-free survival from end 
of RT. Differences between groups were assessed using the Wilcoxon 
signed-rank test (continuous variables) and Chi-square test (categorical 
variables). A post hoc receiver operating characteristic (ROC) analysis 
identified the optimal ERI threshold using the Youden index, with 
calculation of sensitivity, specificity, PPV (Positive Predictive Value), 
and NPV (Negative Predictive Value).
3. Results
Sixty-three patients with LARC were prospectively enrolled; one died 
from unrelated causes before response reassessment, leaving 62 evalu-
able patients included in all efficacy and time-to-event analyses. Of 
these, 29 (46.8%) were female and 33 (53.2%) male, with a median age 
of 68 years (range 31–94). Most had low rectal cancer (58.1%) and 
lymph node involvement (75.8%). Sixteen patients (25.8%) had tumour 
extension ≤ 3 cm. Median baseline GTV was 20.2 cc (range 5.0–50.2), 
decreasing to 9.9 cc (range 2.2–45.9) at fraction 10. Median ERI was 
13.7 (range 2.7–123.4).
Thirty patients (48.4%) were classified as responders and 32 (51.6%) 
as non-responders. No significant differences in baseline clinical char-
acteristics were found between groups, except for lymph node involve-
ment (p < 0.05; N + more frequent in non-responders: 87.5%) and 
morphological features: non-responders more frequently had tumours >
