3 cm (93.8%), while most responders had lesions involving ≤ 50% of 
circumferential extension (83.3%).
Table 1. Patient and treatment characteristics. Statistical differences 
between responders (R) and non-responders (NR) were assessed using 
the chi-square test (*) or the Mann–Whitney U test (†).
Overall, 45/62 patients (72.6%) underwent at least one oART session 
(mean 11 adaptive fractions, median 10). All patients completed plan-
ned RT. Median OTT was 36 days (range 32–53). Two patients (3.2%) 
experienced treatment interruptions due to grade 3 GI toxicity (diar-
rhea), both in the standard arm. Median SI was 14 weeks (range 9–37). 
Capecitabine was used in 56 patients (90.3%; two discontinued for grade 
3 hematological toxicity); six patients (9.7%) received 5-FU.
Table 2. Acute toxicity according to boost intensification. Data are 
presented as N (%); p-values were calculated using Fisher’s exact test. 
GU: genitourinary; GI: gastrointestinal.
Acute toxicity rates were comparable between groups, with a non- 
significant trend toward increased asthenia in the no-boost cohort (p 
= 0.055).
Among responders (n = 30), 18 patients (60%) achieved pCR or cCR 
at two years. Fourteen (46.7%) were managed non-operatively, 
including eight in a WW strategy; two of these experienced suspected 
local regrowth and underwent local excision (LE), confirming pTis. In 
total, 16 patients (53.3%) underwent surgery, including three APRs, 
with six pCRs. TRG1–2 was observed in 14 patients (46.7%).
Among non-responders (n = 32), seven (21.9%) achieved CR at two 
years. Four (12.5%) were managed non-operatively; two of these (50%) 
developed local regrowth (one salvage TME: ypT3N2a; one salvage LE: 
ypT1Nx). Overall, 28 (87.5%) underwent surgery, including four APRs, 
with five pCRs. TRG1–2 was observed in 16 patients (50.0%).
A complete response was achieved in 25 patients (40.3%) overall 
(Table 3). Fig. 1 summarizes CR, CR at 2 years, and TRG1–2 rates by ERI 
group.
Table 3. Distribution of complete response in responder and non- 
responder patients.
Fig. 2 shows ERI values for all patients. Using the validated 
threshold, ERI achieved 69.4% accuracy, 61.5% sensitivity, 75.0% 
specificity, 73.0% NPV, and 64.0% PPV for CR (71.0%, 64.0%, 75.7%, 
75.7%, and 64.0%, respectively, for CR at 2 years). Median follow-up 
was 27.2 months (range 3.5–46.8). At censoring, 60 patients (96.8%) 
were alive, 52 (86.7%) without disease progression. Eight patients 
(13.3%) developed distant metastases, equally distributed between 
groups. Among 18 NOM patients, two local regrowths (11.1%) occurred, 
both in non-responders. No local recurrences were observed after sur-
gery. ERI was the only variable significantly associated with CR in 
univariate and multivariable analyses (adjusted OR 0.912, 95% CI 
0.840–0.990; p = 0.028) (Table 4). Kaplan–Meier analyses (Fig. 3) 
showed no significant differences in OS or DFS between groups (p =
0.970 and p = 0.652, respectively), whereas responders had signifi-
cantly higher TME-free survival (38.7% vs 8.3%; HR 2.35, 95% CI 
1.29–4.26; p = 0.004). DFS analysis included 61 patients, as one addi-
tional patient was lost to follow-up after surgery and could not be reli-
ably evaluated for disease-free survival.
4. Discussion
This prospective analysis supports the clinical utility of ERI as a 
treatment-selection biomarker within an adaptive MRI-guided strategy 
for LARC. Using the validated cut-off of 13.1, ERI successfully discrim-
inated responders from non-responders, with CR rates of 60% and 
21.9%, respectively. These findings are consistent with previous reports 
[16,18] and radiomics-enhanced validations in MRIgRT settings [19], 
supporting ERI’s role as an early imaging biomarker for treatment 
adaptation [20]. The ability to stratify patients during treatment has 
important clinical implications. In our series, nearly half of responders 
were managed with organ-preserving strategies (WW or LE), while non- 
responders showed lower response rates and a greater need for radical 
