leading to recurrence and poor clinical outcomes. This resistance arises 
from complex mechanisms, involving alterations in the tumor micro-
environment (TME), enhanced DNA damage repair, dysregulated 
signaling pathways, and immune evasion strategies [7]. Notably, 
neutrophil infiltration has been implicated as a key contributor [8]. 
Clinical studies have demonstrated that increased neutrophils infiltra-
tion following radiotherapy in cervical cancer patients is significantly 
associated with lymph node metastasis, lower complete response rates, 
and higher rates of distant metastasis [9]. Patients with circulating 
neutrophils >8 × 103/mL one-week post-CCRT show significantly worse 
local control and survival outcomes [10], suggesting a strong link be-
tween neutrophil accumulation and treatment resistance.
The TME is the biological environment where tumor cells thrive, 
comprising cancer cells, stromal tissue (including blood vessels, immune 
cells, fibroblasts, and signaling molecules), and the extracellular matrix 
[11]. Neutrophils are a group of immune cells that play a crucial role in 
the TME. Under physiological conditions, neutrophils serve as the sec-
ond line of defense against pathogenic invasion, rapidly responding to 
inflammatory signals, migrating to sites of infection or inflammation, 
and phagocytosing and eliminating pathogens [12,13]. However, within 
the TME, neutrophils exhibit high heterogeneity and can undergo dy-
namic polarization in response to the immune status of the microenvi-
ronment [14]. Studies have shown that transforming growth factor β 
(TGF-β) promotes neutrophils polarization from an anti-tumor N1 
phenotype to a pro-tumor N2 phenotype [15]. The polarized N2 neu-
trophils, also known as tumor-associated neutrophils (TANs), facilitate 
tumor progression and immune evasion via multiple mechanisms, 
including enhancing tumor cell proliferation, stimulating angiogenesis, 
forming neutrophils extracellular traps (NETs), and suppressing T-cell 
function [16,17].
Targeting neutrophils prior to radiotherapy may represent a prom-
ising strategy to enhance tumor radiosensitivity. In murine models of 
radioresistant non-small cell lung cancer, combining radiotherapy with 
anti-Ly6G antibodies improved tumor response [18]. However, since 
Ly6G is murine-specific, this strategy lacks clinical translatability. 
Instead, inhibiting neutrophil recruitment via molecular targets offers a 
promising alternative. CXCR2, a chemokine receptor expressed on im-
mune and tumor cells, mediates neutrophil migration by binding 
CXCL1-8 ligands [19]. Its inhibition has emerged as a viable approach to 
reduce neutrophil-driven radioresistance. SB225002, a selective CXCR2 
antagonist, blocks ligand binding and neutrophil chemotaxis [20]. 
Preclinical studies show that SB225002 reduces neutrophil infiltration, 
enhances CD8+ T cell activation, and improves therapeutic outcomes in 
lung and nasopharyngeal cancers [21,22]. However, its therapeutic 
potential and mechanistic role in cervical cancer remain unexplored.
In summary, radiotherapy is a key treatment modality for cervical 
cancer, while neutrophils infiltration contributes to radioresistance by 
fostering an immunosuppressive TME. Our study demonstrates that 
radiotherapy induces neutrophils infiltration and establishes a TGF- 
β-enriched TME in CC, driven by CXCR2-CXCL signaling activation. 
Notably, CXCR2 inhibition with SB225002 significantly enhances the 
therapeutic efficacy of radiotherapy and CCRT by modulating the TME 
and improving CC cell radiosensitivity. These findings underscore the 
potential of CXCR2-targeted therapies as a novel strategy to enhance 
radiosensitivity in cervical cancer.
2. Methods and materials
2.1. Cell culture
The mouse cervical squamous cell carcinoma cell line U14 was ob-
tained from the Cell Resource Center, Institute of Basic Medical Sciences, 
Chinese Academy of Medical Sciences (CAMS, Beijing, China). The 
human cervical adenocarcinoma cell line HeLa was obtained from the 
American Type Culture Collection (Manassas, VA, USA). Cells were 
preserved by the State Key Laboratory of Biotherapy and the Cancer 
Center of Sichuan University. Dulbecco’s Modified Eagle’s Medium 
(DMEM) containing 10 % fetal bovine serum (FBS) and 1 % penicillin- 
streptomycin was used for cell culture, and the cells were maintained 
in a 5 % CO2 incubator at 37 ◦C.
2.2. Animals
Female Kunming (KM) mice (4–5 weeks old, weighing 25 g) used for 
U14 cell inoculation were purchased from Vital River (Beijing, China). 
Female BALB/c Nude mice (4–5 weeks old, weighing 18g) used for HeLa 
cell inoculation were purchased from Gempharmatech Co., Ltd (Jiangsu, 
China). All these mice were bred in a specific pathogen free (SPF) animal 
facility with a consistent room temperature and humidity. The animal 
studies were conducted following the guidelines set by the Institutional 
Animal Care and Use Committee of West China Second University 
Hospital., Chengdu, Sichuan, China. All animal experiments in this study 
were approved by the Institutional Animal Care and Use Committee 
(IACUC) of West China Second University Hospital, Sichuan University. 
The approval number is (2024) Animal Ethics Approval No. 025.
2.3. Apoptosis assay and cell cycle assay
The Annexin V-FITC/propidium iodide (PI) Detection Kit (BD Bio-
sciences) was used to determine the apoptosis rate of U14 and HeLa cells 
after the treatment of SB225002 combination with radiotherapy. A total 
of 4 × 10∧5 cells were seeded in 6-well plates overnight. The following 
day, the cells were treated with SB225002 (0 or 0.5 μM) for 4 h before 
exposure to 0 and 2 Gy of X-ray radiation. For cell cycle assay, cells were 
collected 8 h post irradiation, and fixed overnight at 4 ◦C. Then, cells 
were stained with PI for 15 min at 4 ◦C. For apoptosis assay, cells were 
harvested 24 h post irradiation, washed twice with cold PBS, and stained 
with Annexin V/PI. The cells were examined on NovoCyte Flow Cy-
tometer (ACEA Biosciences) within 1 h. Data were analyzed with 
NovoExpressTM software.
2.4. Colony formation assay
U14 and HeLa cells were seeded in 6-well plates at a density of 1000 
cells per well overnight. The following day, the cells were treated with 
SB225002 (0 or 0.5 μM) for 4 h before exposure to 0 and 2 Gy of X-ray 
radiation. 24 h post irradiation, the cells were cultured in drug-free 
medium for approximately 14 days. Subsequently, the cell colonies 
were fixed with a 4 % paraformaldehyde (PFA) solution, then stained 
with crystal violet. The cell colonies were photographed after natural 
drying, and the quantification of colony was performed using Image J 
software.
2.5. Cell proliferation assay
Cell proliferation was assessed using the Cell Counting Kit-8 (CCK-8, 
Dojindo Laboratories, #KJ798). U14 and HeLa cells were seeded in 96- 
well plates at a density of 8000 cells per well and allowed to adhere 
overnight. Cells were then treated with increasing concentrations of 
SB225002 (0.125 μM, 0.25 μM, 0.5 μM, 1 μM, 2 μM, 4 μM, and 8 μM) for 
24, 48, or 72 h. At each time point, CCK-8 reagent was added according 
to the manufacturer’s instructions, and absorbance at 450 nm was 
measured using a microplate reader. Each condition was tested in trip-
licate, and the experiment was independently repeated three times.
2.6. Isolation of neutrophils and transwell assay
Bone marrow cells were harvested from the femur and tibia, and then 
filtered through a 70 μm nylon mesh. After red blood cell (RBC) lysis, the 
cell suspension was gently layered onto Histopaque-1077/1119 gradient 
solutions (Sigma) and centrifuged at 700 g for 30 min at room temper-
ature. Neutrophils were carefully aspirated following the 
Y. Ni et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       Cancer Letters 638 (2026) 218153 
2

manufacturer’s instructions. Transwell inserts with 3 μm holes were 
assembled into 24-well plates. Neutrophils were added to the upper 
chamber, while the lower chamber contained various chemoattractants 
(refer to figures and figure legend). After 5 h of incubation, the number 
of migrated neutrophils (CD45+CD11b+Ly6G+ cells) was quantified by 
flow cytometry analysis.
2.7. Co-culture of neutrophils with U14 cells
Transwell inserts with 0.4 μm holes were assembled into 24-well 
plates. A total of 2 × 106 primary neutrophils were seeded into the 
upper chamber (0.4 μm pore size), while non-irradiated (nonIR) or 
irradiated (IR) U14 tumor cells were placed in the lower chamber. 
Neutrophils were treated with or without SB431542. After 24 h of co- 
culture, the expression of TNF-α and Arg-1 in neutrophils 
(CD45+CD11b+Ly6G+) was analyzed by flow cytometry.
2.8. Flow cytometry analysis
To obtain a single-cell suspension, tumor tissues were cut into small 
particles and digested with a mixture of collagenase Type I (1 mg/ml) 
and collagenase Type IV (0.5 mg/ml) for 1 h at 37 ◦C. For spleen tissue, 
spleens were ground into fragments and filtered through a 70 μm nylon 
mesh.
Next, RBC lysis buffer was added to the single-cell suspension to 
eliminate RBCs. The cell density was adjusted to 1 × 10∧6/ml. The cells 
were stained for cell-surface markers at 4 ◦C for 30 min before detection. 
Antibodies targeting mouse PerCP-cy5.5-CD45, FITC-CD11b, PE-F4/80, 
BV510 or BV421-Ly6G from Biolegend, anti-mouse CXCR2 antibodies 
from R&D Systems, and LIVE/DEAD Fixable Blue Dead Cell Stain was 
sourced from Thermo Fisher Scientific were used. All samples were 
evaluated on NovoCyte Flow Cytometer (ACEA Biosciences, Inc., San 
Diego, CA, USA), and data were analyzed using NovoExpress® software 
(1.3.0, ACEA Biosciences, Inc., San Diego, CA, USA, 2018).
2.9. Quantitative real time PCR
Total RNAs from tumor tissues and cells were extracted using the 
TRIzol® RNA extraction kit (BioTNT) according to the manufacturer’s 
instructions. The isolated RNA was then transversely transcribed into 
cDNA using the Takara kit (Dalian, China). Real-time PCR was carried 
out using SYBR Green supermix (Bio-Rad Laboratories, Hercules, CA, U. 
S.) with a two-step PCR reaction procedure on CFX Connect Real-Time 
PCR system (BioRad, USA). The expression of these genes was normal-
ized to the expression of GAPDH. The specific primer sequences used 
were as follows: GAPDH, 5′-GTCTCCTCTGACTTCAACAGCG-3′(forward) 
and 5′-ACCACCCTGTTGCTGTAGCCAA-3′(reverse); CXCL1, 5′- 
GCCGTTGGTCAGAAATACTGG-3′(forward) and 5′- GACGCCAGG-
