2.10. Western blot analysis
Total protein was isolated from tumor tissue with radio-
immunoprecipitation assay buffer (RIPA; Beyotime) containing pro-
teinase inhibitors (Millipore) and phosphatase inhibitors (KeyGen). 
Protein concentration was assessed using the BCA protein assay (Pierce, 
Thermo Fisher Scientific). 30 μg of proteins were electrophoresed in 
12.5 % sodium dodecyl sulfate (SDS)-polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis 
(PAGE), and then transferred to polyvinylidene difluoride membrane 
(Millipore). After blocking with 5 % nonfat milk in for 1 h at room 
temperature, the membranes were incubated with primary antibodies 
TGF-β (Abcam, ab215715), Mcl-1(CST, 5453S), Cyclin B1(CST, 4135S), 
CDK1(Proteintech, 19532-1-AP), p-CDK1 (CST, 4539T), Vinculin 
(Sigma, V9131-100U), β-actin (SANTA CRUZ, sc-47778) overnight at 
4 ◦C. Then, the membranes were incubated with horseradish peroxidase 
(HRP)-conjugated anti-rabbit secondary antibodies or anti-mouse anti-
bodies for 1 h at room temperature (Cell Signaling Technology, 1:5000). 
Proteins were detected after incubation using an enhanced chem-
iluminescence detection kit (Immobilon™ Western Chemiluminescent 
HRP Substrate).
2.11. In vivo therapeutic efficacy of SB225002
U14 cells (5 × 10∧6) were injected into the right flank of KM mice to 
establish a U14 subcutaneous syngeneic tumor model (n = 6 mice). HeLa 
cells (5 × 10∧6) were injected into the right flank of nude mice to 
establish a subcutaneous xenograft tumor model (n = 6 mice). In the 
U14 or HeLa RT model: one week after cancer cell implantation, mice 
were randomly divided into four groups: vehicle (Veh), SB225002, Veh 
+ radiotherapy (IR), and SB225002 + IR. SB225002 (8 mg/kg in the 
U14 tumor model and 20 mg/kg in the HeLa tumor model) or vehicle 
was administered via intraperitoneal (i.p.) injection daily for one or two 
weeks. Radiation (0 or 6 Gy, dose rate 2 Gy/min, X-RAD 320, USA) was 
administered on day 14 or 21 [23,24]. Mice were then sacrificed, and 
tumors were collected at the specified endpoints. In the U14 or HeLa 
CCRT model: one week after cancer cell implantation, mice were 
randomly divided into four groups: vehicle (Veh), SB225002, Veh + Cis 
+ IR, and SB225002 + Cis + IR. SB225002 (8 mg/kg in the U14 tumor 
model and 20 mg/kg in the HeLa tumor model) or vehicle was admin-
istered via i.p. injection daily for one or two weeks. On day 11, cisplatin 
(2 mg/kg) was administered i.p. twice per week for a total of four doses. 
Radiation (0 or 6 Gy) was given on day 18 or 20 [25]. Mice were then 
sacrificed, and tumors were collected at the specified endpoints.
2.12. Immunohistochemistry (IHC) and immunofluorescence (IF) 
staining
Tumor tissues were collected for IHC and IF staining performed as 
described in previous study [26]. Briefly, the tumor tissues were fixed in 
4 % paraformaldehyde at room temperature for two days, then 
paraffin-embedded and sectioned at 3 μm. For immunofluorescence, 
sections were deparaffinized, antigen retrieval, and serum blocking. 
Primary antibodies, including anti-Ly6G rabbit polyclonal antibody 
(GB11229-100, Servicebio), anti-TGF-β1 rabbit monoclonal antibody 
(ab215715,Abcam), and anti-CXCL1 rabbit monoclonal antibody 
(12335-1-AP, Proteintech) were applied. Then the slides were incubated 
with horseradish peroxidase (HRP)-conjugated secondary antibodies. 
For IHC staining, HRP was detected using diaminobenzidine peroxide 
solution, and cell nuclei were counterstained with hematoxylin (Beyo-
time Institute of Biotechnology, Shanghai, China). For IF staining, nuclei 
were counterstained with DAPI, and the finalized slides were sealed with 
anti-fluorescence quencher (F6182, SIGMA). Images were captured by 
fluorescence microscope (Olympus, VS200).
