2.13. γ-H2AX staining
Cell-attached slides were prepared in 24-well plates. The cells were 
treated with SB225002 (0 or 0.5 μM) for 4 h before exposure to radiation 
(0 or 2 Gy). Then the cells were fixed in 4 % paraformaldehyde, per-
meabilised with 0.5 % Triton X-100, and blocked. Next, cells were 
incubated with a γ-H2AX antibody overnight at 4 ◦C (1:500; ab11174, 
Abcam), followed by a FITC secondary antibody (ab6717, Abcam) at 
37 ◦C for 2 h. Nuclei were stained with DAPI (Beyotime Biotechnology), 
and slides were sealed (F6182, SIGMA) and imaged using a fluorescence 
microscope (Leica, Germany).
2.14. Statistical analysis
Data are presented as mean ± standard deviation (mean ± SD)). A 
two-tailed unpaired Student’s t-test was employed to compare differ-
ences between two groups. One-way analysis of variance (ANOVA) was 
utilized for comparisons involving three or more groups. All results were 
analyzed and visualized using GraphPad Prism (9.0). NS, not signifi-
cance, *P < 0.05, **P < 0.01, ***P < 0.001, ****P < 0.0001.
3. Results
3.1. Radiotherapy induces neutrophil infiltration and enhances 
neutrophils N2 polarization by TGF-β
Radiotherapy is a key treatment for locally advanced cervical cancer; 
however, treatment resistance can compromise its efficacy and nega-
tively impact patient survival. Clinical studies have reported that 
elevated neutrophil levels in both tumors and peripheral blood correlate 
with increased recurrence, metastasis, and poorer overall survival, 
suggesting a potential role of neutrophils in radiotherapy resistance [9,
10]. To investigate the effect of irradiation on neutrophils infiltration, 
we established a subcutaneous U14 cervical cancer mouse model. Mice 
were treated with a single 10 Gy dose of radiotherapy, and tumors and 
spleens were collected 48 or 72 h post-irradiation for flow cytometry 
analysis (FCM). As shown in Fig. 1A–B, the proportion of neutrophils 
(CD45+CD11b+F4/80-Ly6G+) was significantly increased in both tumor 
tissues and spleens at 72 h post-irradiation. To determine whether this 
neutrophil recruitment depends on the treatment strategy, we assessed 
the effects of fractionated radiotherapy (2 Gy daily for 5 consecutive 
days, total 10 Gy). Similar to the single-dose group, significant neutro-
phil infiltration was observed in both tumors and spleens at 72 h 
post-radiotherapy (Fig. 1C–D), suggesting that radiation-induced 
neutrophil accumulation is independent of the irradiation protocol.
Given that TGF-β signaling is known to promote neutrophil polari-
zation and is often upregulated by radiotherapy [15,27,28]. We next 
analyzed the expression levels of TGF-β in IR tumor tissues. Both tran-
scription and protein levels of Tgfb1 were significantly elevated in 
irradiated (IR) tumors compared to nonIR controls (Fig. 1E–F). Consis-
tently, in vitro analysis revealed a marked increase in the proportion of 
TGF-β+ U14 cells at 72 h after 10 Gy irradiation (Fig. 1G). These data 
suggest that radiotherapy enhances TGF-β production in tumor cells, 
potentially contributing to an immunosuppressive TME.
To assess whether radiotherapy influences neutrophil polarization, 
we co-cultured primary neutrophils with U14 tumor cells that were 
either untreated or irradiated (10 Gy, 48 h prior to co-culture). After 24 
h, FCM analysis revealed that neutrophils co-cultured with irradiated 
tumor cells exhibited significantly reduced TNF-α and increased Arg-1 
expression (Fig. 1H). As TNF-α is a marker of anti-tumor (N1-like) 
neutrophils and Arg-1 indicates pro-tumor (N2-like) polarization, these 
results suggest that irradiated tumor cells promote a shift toward an N2- 
like phenotype in neutrophils. Notably, treatment with the TGF-β re-
ceptor inhibitor SB431542 reversed this polarization pattern, restoring 
TNF-α and suppressing Arg-1 expression in neutrophils (Fig. 1I), indi-
cating that TGF-β signaling mediates this shift.
In summary, our findings demonstrate that radiotherapy not only 
promotes neutrophil infiltration but also drives their polarization to-
ward a tumor-promoting N2-like phenotype via TGF-β signaling.
3.2. Irradiation activates CXCR2-CXCL signaling to promote neutrophil 
infiltration
We further investigated the mechanisms underlying neutrophils 
recruitment, focusing on the CXCR2-CXCL signaling pathway, which 
plays a key role in neutrophils chemotaxis. Flow cytometry analysis 
showed that 10 Gy irradiation significantly increased the proportion of 
CXCR2+ neutrophils in the bone marrow compared to non-irradiated 
controls, whereas no significant difference was observed in tumor- 
infiltrating neutrophils (Fig. 2A). This suggests that radiotherapy may 
promote the expansion or priming of CXCR2+ neutrophils within the 
bone marrow. To further examine the factors driving this recruitment, 
we assessed the expression of CXCR2 ligands in the TME. RT-PCR 
analysis revealed a marked upregulation mRNA levels of Cxcl1 and 
Cxcl2 in irradiated tumor tissues compared to controls (Fig. 2B). 
Immunohistochemical staining further confirmed increased CXCL1 
protein expression in tumors following irradiation (Fig. 2C), indicating 
that local production of CXCR2 ligands is enhanced in response to 
radiotherapy.
To validate these findings in vitro, we assessed CXCL ligand expres-
sion in U14 and HeLa cells following 10 Gy irradiation. RT-PCR analysis 
showed that multiple CXCL family members were markedly upregulated 
in irradiated tumor cells compared to non-irradiated controls 
(Fig. 2D–E), supporting the notion that radiotherapy contributes to the 
activation of the CXCR2-CXCL signaling pathway in tumor cells, 
potentially creating a chemotactic gradient that facilitates neutrophil 
recruitment to the tumor site.
To evaluate the functional relevance of this pathway, we performed a 
neutrophil migration assay using a transwell system. Primary bone 
marrow-derived neutrophils (4 × 105) were seeded in the upper cham-
ber, while the lower chamber contained RPMI-1640 medium alone 
(negative control), recombinant CXCL2 (positive control), tumor su-
pernatant from non-irradiated U14 cells (nonIR TS), or supernatant from 
irradiated U14 cells (IR TS). After 5 h of incubation at 37 ◦C, migrated 
neutrophils (CD45+CD11b+Ly6G+) in the lower chamber were quanti-
fied by FCM analysis. As shown in Fig. 2F, IR tumor supernatant 
significantly enhanced neutrophil migration compared to the nonIR TS 
groups, suggesting that irradiated tumor cells release soluble factors- 
most likely CXCR2 ligands-that drive neutrophil chemotaxis.
In conclusion, our results suggest that irradiation activates CXCR2- 
CXCL signaling to promote neutrophil infiltration in cervical cancer.
3.3. SB225002 exhibits a synergistic effect with radiotherapy and 
concurrent chemoradiotherapy in cervical cancer
Our previous experiments demonstrated that irradiation promotes 
neutrophils infiltration and induces a TGF-β-positive TME in cervical 
cancer, primarily through CXCR2-CXCL signaling. Based on these find-
ings, we hypothesized that targeting the CXCR2/CXCL pathway could 
inhibit neutrophils migration and enhance the antitumor effects of 
radiotherapy. To explore this synergy, we evaluated the therapeutic 
impact of SB225002 in combination with radiotherapy in U14 and HeLa 
tumor-bearing mice. As illustrated in Fig. 3A, mice received daily 
intraperitoneal (i.p.) injections of SB225002 (8 mg/kg for the U14 
model and 20 mg/kg for the HeLa model) or vehicle, starting on day 7 
post-tumor inoculation. On day 13 or 21, mice either received a single 6 
Gy radiotherapy dose or remained untreated. Tumors were harvested at 
the designated endpoints for analysis. As shown in Fig. 3B–C, SB225002 
monotherapy significantly reduced tumor weight compared with the 
vehicle group. The combination of SB225002 and radiotherapy further 
decreased tumor weight compared with radiotherapy alone, demon-
strating a stronger antitumor effect in both U14 and HeLa xenograft 
Y. Ni et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       Cancer Letters 638 (2026) 218153 
4

models.
CCRT is the standard treatment for locally advanced cervical cancer. 
To further assess the synergistic antitumor potential of SB225002, we 
evaluated its efficacy in combination with CCRT. As shown in Fig. 3D, 
mice bearing U14 or HeLa tumors received daily intraperitoneal (i.p.) 
injections of SB225002 (8 mg/kg for U14 and 20 mg/kg for HeLa) or 
vehicle, starting on day 7 post-tumor inoculation for one to two weeks. 
Beginning on day 11, cisplatin (2 mg/kg) was administered i.p. twice 
weekly for a total of four doses. On day 18 or 20, mice either received a 
single 6 Gy radiotherapy dose or remained untreated. Tumors were 
harvested and analyzed at designated endpoints. In both the U14 and 
HeLa CCRT models, the SB225002+CCRT (SB + cis + IR) group 
exhibited the most significant tumor suppression compared to the (Veh 
+ Cis + IR) group and the SB225002 group. Moreover, complete tumor 
regression was observed in three mice from the SB225002+cis + IR 
group in the U14 CCRT model (Fig. 3E–F).
Collectively, these findings demonstrate that SB225002 enhances the 
efficacy of both radiotherapy and CCRT in cervical cancer models, 
underscoring its potential as a therapeutic strategy for cervical cancer 
treatment.
