3.4. SB225002 enhances cervical cancer radiosensitivity by suppressing 
neutrophils infiltration
In our previous experiments, radiation significantly increased 
neutrophil infiltration and upregulated TGF-β expression, contributing 
to an immunosuppressive tumor microenvironment (TME) in cervical 
cancer. To assess whether SB225002 enhances radiosensitivity by 
modulating the immune response, we examined its effect on neutrophil 
infiltration. Mice received daily intraperitoneal (i.p.) injections of 
SB225002 (8 mg/kg) or vehicle, starting on day 7 post-tumor inocula-
tion. On day 14, treatment ceased, followed by exposure to 0 or 10 Gy 
radiotherapy. Seventy-two hours post-irradiation, mice were sacrificed 
for FCM analysis of tumor and spleen tissues. FCM results revealed that 
SB225002 combined with radiotherapy significantly reduced neutro-
phils proportions (CD45+CD11b+F4/80-Ly6G+) in both tumor and 
spleen tissues compared to the IR group (Fig. 4A–B). Immunofluores-
cence staining further confirmed a marked decrease in Ly6G+ neutro-
phils in tumor tissues from the combination group (Fig. 4C). 
Additionally, TGF-β expression was significantly lower in the combina-
tion group than in the radiotherapy-only group (Fig. 4D), indicating that 
SB225002 alleviates the immunosuppressive TME. And recent study has 
provided a mechanistic explanation for this observation: Neutrophils 
have been shown to activate TGF-β through the release of neutrophil 
extracellular traps (NETs) [29]. Therefore, depletion of infiltrated neu-
trophils may directly reduce the availability of TGF-β in irradiated 
tumors.
Given the in vivo suppression of neutrophil infiltration by SB225002, 
we examined its direct effect on neutrophil migration in vitro. Briefly, 
primary neutrophils (4 × 105 cells) were seeded into the upper chamber 
of a transwell system (3 μm pore size), while the lower chamber con-
tained either RPMI 1640 medium (control), tumor supernatant (TS) 
from non-irradiated U14 cells (nonIR TS), or tumor supernatant from 
irradiated U14 cells (IR TS). Neutrophils were treated with or without 
SB225002. After 5 h of incubation, migrated neutrophils 
(CD45+CD11b+Ly6G+) were collected from the lower chamber and 
analyzed by FCM. As shown in Fig. 4E, SB225002 significantly inhibited 
neutrophil migration induced by both IR TS and nonIR TS.
To further validate the role of neutrophils in mediating radio-
resistance, we performed an additional in vivo experiment using a Ly6G- 
neutralizing antibody to selectively deplete neutrophils. Similar to the 
effects observed with SB225002 treatment, neutrophil depletion 
significantly enhanced the therapeutic response to irradiation, as evi-
denced by markedly reduced tumor weights and complete regression in 
several mice (Fig. S1). These findings provide direct evidence that 
neutrophils contribute to tumor radioresistance and that SB225002 
enhances radiosensitivity at least in part by inhibiting CXCR2-mediated 
neutrophil recruitment.
Together, these results demonstrate that SB225002 effectively sup-
presses radiation-induced neutrophil infiltration and TGF-β expression, 
thereby mitigating the immunosuppressive TME in cervical cancer.
3.5. SB225002 exerts antitumor activity by suppressing proliferation, 
inducing apoptosis, and causing G2/M arrest in U14 and HeLa cells
Although SB225002 is widely recognized as a selective CXCR2 
antagonist, recent studies have demonstrated its ability to directly 
suppress tumor cell proliferation and induce apoptosis, even in the 
absence of CXCR2 expression [30]. In our in vivo experiments, SB225002 
monotherapy significantly inhibited tumor growth, notably in HeLa 
xenografts in immunodeficient mice, suggesting that its antitumor effect 
may extend beyond neutrophil inhibition and involve direct cytotoxic 
effects on tumor cells.
In vitro assays further support this notion. CCK-8 analysis showed 
that SB225002 inhibited the proliferation of U14 and HeLa cells, with 
IC50 values of 5.789 μM and 6.091 μM at 24 h, respectively (Fig. 5A–B). 
In addition, SB225002 significantly suppressed colony formation 
(Fig. 5C–D) and induced apoptosis in a dose-dependent manner, as 
determined by flow cytometry (Fig. 5E–F). Western blot analysis showed 
a marked decrease in MCL-1 expression after SB225002 treatment 
(Fig. 5G). As an anti-apoptotic BCL-2 family protein, reduced MCL-1 
levels support the flow cytometry results indicating SB225002-induced 
apoptosis in cervical cancer cells. Cell cycle analysis revealed that 
SB225002 induced pronounced G2/M phase arrest in cervical cancer 
cells (Fig. 5H). Moreover, Western blot analysis indicated that the 
expression of Cyclin B1 and phosphorylated CDK1 (p-CDK1) was 
remarkably upregulated after treatment (Fig. 5I). These results further 
support that SB225002 induced the G2/M phase arrest.
Collectively, these findings indicate that SB225002 exerts antitumor 
effects not only through modulation of the TME but also via direct in-
hibition of tumor cell proliferation, induction of apoptosis, and G2/M 
cell cycle arrest.
3.6. SB225002 enhances radiosensitivity in cervical cancer cells by 
promoting DNA double-strand breaks, G2/M phase arrest, and apoptosis
Radiation-induced damage in tumor cells is influenced by various 
factors, including the cell cycle phase, DNA repair mechanisms, and 
reactive oxygen species (ROS) levels. Given the observed anti-tumor 
effects of SB225002 as a monotherapy both in vitro and in vivo, we 
further investigated its direct impact on cervical cancer cells when 
combined with radiotherapy. To evaluate the radiosensitizing potential 
of SB225002, we performed a colony formation assay. SB225002 alone 
inhibited colony formation, and its combination with irradiation resul-
ted in significantly greater suppression than radiation alone (Fig. 6A–B). 
