To assess radiation-induced DNA damage, we examined γ-H2AX foci, a 
well-established marker of DNA double-strand breaks (DSBs). Immu-
nofluorescence staining revealed that SB225002 significantly enhanced 
γ-H2AX foci formation in irradiated cells, indicating increased DNA 
damage (Fig. 6C–D). It is well established that cells in the G2/M phase 
are more sensitive to radiation, whereas S-phase cells are relatively 
resistant [31]. Consistent with this, FCM analysis revealed that 
SB225002 induced G2/M phase arrest in U14 and HeLa cells, while 
markedly reducing the proportion of S-phase cells in the combination 
treatment group (Fig. 6E). Additionally, SB225002 enhanced radiation 
induced apoptosis in both HeLa and U14 cells (Fig. 6F).
In summary, these results demonstrate that SB225002 enhances the 
radiosensitivity of cervical cancer cells by suppressing proliferation, 
increasing DNA damage, inducing G2/M phase arrest, and promoting 
apoptosis. These results support the potential of SB225002 as a radio-
sensitizing agent for cervical cancer therapy.
