cervical cancer, and tumor radiosensitivity is a key determinant of 
therapeutic efficacy. Beyond intrinsic tumor resistance, the immune 
status of the tumor microenvironment (TME) also critically influences 
treatment response. Neutrophils, as early responders to radiation, 
exhibit rapid but dynamic changes post-irradiation. Previous studies 
have shown a transient reduction in tumor-infiltrating neutrophils 
within 6–12 h after radiotherapy, reflecting their radiosensitivity [32]. 
However, radiotherapy also elicits a systemic inflammatory response, 
promoting neutrophil production and accumulation in peripheral organs 
such as the spleen and blood [33–35]. This phenomenon has been 
consistently observed in previous studies and aligns well with our own 
findings. This response is likely driven by radiation-induced release of 
cytokines like IL-1β, IL-6, and TNF-α, affecting neutrophil mobilization 
[36]. These findings show that radiotherapy affects both local and sys-
temic immunity, with neutrophils contributing to post-radiation 
inflammation and possibly promoting tumor progression. A key medi-
ator in this process is TGF-β, a potent immunosuppressive cytokine 
known to be upregulated following irradiation and implicated in resis-
tance to radiotherapy [37]. Notably, TGF-β promotes neutrophil polar-
ization from the antitumor N1 phenotype (TNF-α+) to the protumor N2 
phenotype (ARG-1+) [15]. Our study confirmed that radiotherapy in-
creases TGF-β expression in cervical cancer tissues in vivo and in vitro. 
Co-culture of irradiated tumor cells with primary neutrophils further 
demonstrated enhanced N2 polarization, characterized by elevated 
ARG-1 and reduced TNF-α expression. This shift was reversed by the 
TGF-β receptor inhibitor SB431542, confirming the central role of TGF-β 
signaling in mediating radiation-induced N2 polarization. Together, 
these results suggest that radiotherapy not only recruits neutrophils but 
also reprograms them toward a tumor-promoting phenotype via TGF-β, 
contributing to an immunosuppressive TME in cervical cancer.
To investigate the mechanism of neutrophil recruitment, we focused 
on the CXCR2/CXCL signaling axis, a well-established regulator of 
neutrophil trafficking. CXCR2 and its ligands are broadly expressed in 
various cancers-including breast, pancreatic, lung, melanoma, and 
