Meanwhile, irradiation markedly upregulated CXCL1/2 expression in 
cervical cancer, both in vivo and in vitro, suggesting that CXCL-mediated 
chemotactic signals drive the recruitment of CXCR2+ neutrophils from 
the bone marrow. Together, these findings highlight activation of the 
CXCR2-CXCL axis as a key mechanism underlying radiation-induced 
neutrophil infiltration into the TME.
CXCR2 antagonism has emerged as a promising strategy to limit 
neutrophil infiltration and alleviate immunosuppression in various 
cancers, including pancreatic, lung, prostate, and nasopharyngeal car-
cinomas [19,21,22,43,44]. In our study, we found that radiotherapy 
activates the CXCR2-CXCL axis in cervical cancer, promoting CXCR2+
neutrophil recruitment. Based on these findings, we further evaluated 
the therapeutic benefit of SB225002, a selective CXCR2 inhibitor, in 
combination with radiotherapy. At a radiation dose of 6 Gy, SB225002 
combined with radiotherapy significantly reduced tumor weight 
compared with radiotherapy alone. Moreover, in the concurrent che-
moradiotherapy (CCRT) model [25], the combination of SB225002 with 
cisplatin and radiotherapy further decreased tumor burden compared 
with CCRT alone, indicating an enhanced antitumor effect of the com-
bined treatment in both U14 and HeLa models. FCM analysis confirmed 
that SB225002 significantly reduced RT-induced neutrophil infiltration. 
However, residual neutrophils were still detected, which may be 
attributed to CXCR2-independent recruitment pathways mediated by 
inflammatory cytokines such as IL-1β, CSF2, and CSF3, as previously 
reported [33]. Importantly, Ly6G-neutralization experiments provided 
direct functional evidence that neutrophils contribute to tumor radio-
resistance, highlighting the immunomodulatory role of SB225002 in 
vivo. Taken together, these results indicate that while SB225002 
partially inhibits neutrophil infiltration, additional compensatory 
mechanisms may sustain residual neutrophil recruitment.
Interestingly, combination treatment with SB225002 not only 
significantly leads to the loss of neutrophils, but also affects TGF-β 
expression in tumor tissues. Recent study has provided a mechanistic 
explanation for this observation: Neutrophils have been shown to acti-
vate TGF-β through the release of neutrophil extracellular traps (NETs) 
[29]. Therefore, depletion of tumor-infiltrating neutrophils may lower 
local TGF-β availability, alleviating immunosuppression. This mecha-
nism is supported by findings in breast cancer, where CXCR2 inhibition 
attenuated therapy-induced TGF-β upregulation. For example, doxoru-
bicin elevated both CXCR2 and TGF-β levels in MDA-MB-231 cells, an 
effect reversed by the CXCR2 inhibitor AZD5069, which also improved 
chemotherapy response [45,46]. These results suggest a possible func-
tional crosstalk between CXCR2 and TGF-β signaling pathways. By 
suppressing CXCR2-mediated neutrophil recruitment and downstream 
TGF-β signaling, SB225002 may help reprogram the TME into a less 
immunosuppressive and more immunologically active state, thereby 
enhancing responsiveness to radiotherapy.
In addition to its immunomodulatory effects via CXCR2 inhibition, 
SB225002 also demonstrated tumor-intrinsic cytotoxicity. Independent 
of CXCR2, it has been shown to induce ROS production and DNA 
damage in different tumor models [30]. Consistent with this, our in vitro 
experiments demonstrated that SB225002 monotherapy inhibited the 
growth of cervical cancer cells, induced G2/M phase arrest, and pro-
moted apoptosis. These findings indicate that, while the inhibition of 
neutrophil recruitment via the CXCR2 axis represents a key mechanism, 
potential off-target effects of SB225002 should be taken into account 
when evaluating its therapeutic benefits.
Given these tumor-intrinsic effects, we further explored whether 
SB225002 could enhance the cytotoxicity of radiotherapy. Radiation- 
induced DNA damage is modulated by several factors, including cell 
cycle phase, DNA repair capacity, and ROS levels. In colony formation 
assays, SB225002 significantly potentiated the anti-proliferative effects 
of ionizing radiation in U14 and HeLa cells. Immunofluorescence 
staining revealed elevated γ-H2AX expression in the combination group 
compared to radiation alone, indicating impaired DNA double-strand 
break (DSB) repair and prolonged radiation-induced damage [47,48]. 
These effects may be linked to the cell cycle modulation, as cancer cells 
in the G2/M phase are known to be most sensitive to radiation [49]. 
Indeed, SB225002 treatment increased the proportion of cells in the 
G2/M phase following irradiation. Previous studies suggest that 
SB225002 may inhibit microtubule polymerization and induce mitotic 
arrest [50,51], further supporting its potential to enhance radiosensi-
tivity by disrupting cell cycle progression.
While our study provides novel insights into the role of neutrophils 
and the CXCR2 axis in radiation resistance, several limitations remain. 
The use of murine models may not fully reflect the complexity of human 
cervical cancer. In addition, the downstream signaling events of CXCR2 
and the precise crosstalk with TGF-β pathways warrant further 
investigation.
Encouragingly, several CXCR2 inhibitors, including AZD5069, are 
under clinical evaluation in solid tumors. In a phase I trial, AZD5069 
combined with enzalutamide showed a favorable safety profile and 
preliminary efficacy in patients with metastatic castration-resistant 
prostate cancer (NCT03177187) [52]. Preclinical studies in breast can-
cer further demonstrated that CXCR2 blockade reversed 
chemotherapy-induced TGF-β upregulation and enhanced responses to 
immunotherapy [53,54]. These findings highlight the potential of tar-
geting CXCR2 signaling to improve treatment outcomes across multiple 
solid tumors, including cervical cancer. Our results extend this rationale 
by providing new evidence supporting the combination of CXCR2 in-
hibition with radiotherapy in cervical cancer. While SB225002 effec-
tively reduces intratumoral neutrophil infiltration and enhances the 
response to radiotherapy, potential systemic effects should be consid-
ered. CXCR2 blockade may cause neutropenia by reducing circulating 
neutrophils, particularly in organs such as the spleen. Systemic neu-
tropenia could predispose patients to opportunistic infections, impair 
wound healing and increase perioperative complications, particularly in 
combination with cytotoxic therapies [55–57]. Careful monitoring of 
neutrophil counts and adjustment of dosing schedules will be important 
to minimize these adverse effects while preserving the therapeutic 
benefit of SB225002.
In conclusion, our study demonstrates that radiotherapy activates 
the CXCR2-CXCL signaling axis, promoting neutrophil infiltration and a 
TGF-β-enriched immunosuppressive TME in cervical cancer. SB225002, 
a selective CXCR2 antagonist, enhances the therapeutic efficacy of 
radiotherapy through dual mechanisms: it suppresses neutrophil 
recruitment and TGF-β expression by blocking CXCR2, and directly 
sensitizes tumor cells to radiation by inducing DNA damage, G2/M ar-
rest, and apoptosis. Together, these findings highlight the translational 
potential of targeting the CXCR2-CXCL axis and support the use of 
CXCR2 antagonists like SB225002 as part of combination strategies to 
overcome radiotherapy resistance and improve treatment outcomes in 
cervical cancer.
