expression of IL-22 in NSCLC (Fig. 1D and Table 1). At the same time, the 
findings confirm that CAFs secretes IL-22 and promote the proliferation 
and metastasis of NSCLC by mediating phosphoinositide-3-kinase 
(PI3K)-protein kinase B (PKB)-mTOR signaling pathway. Zhou et al. 
[35] found that CAFs can secrete vascular cell adhesion molecule-1 
(VCAM-1) and activate PKB and mitogen-activated protein kinase 
(MAPK) signaling pathways in order to trigger the metastasis process 
(Fig. 1D and Table 1). In addition, Lakins et al. [36] revealed that CAFs 
can bring about the death of cytotoxic lymphocyte cells through pro -
grammed cell death-ligand 2 (PD-L2) and fas ligand, mediating the 
immunosuppression of TME and participating in the metastasis of lung 
cancer. 
Tumor-associated immune cells are mainly involved in immune 
regulation during tumor invasion and migration, causing immune 
escape of cancer cells. TAMs can be divided into anticancer type M1 and 
carcinogenic type M2. M2-type macrophages play a major role in tumor 
progression. Many studies have confirmed that TAMs can release MMPs 
and a series of pro-angiogenic factors to construct new blood vessels and 
provide a pathway for tumor cell metastasis [37]. Guo et 
al. [38] 
explored that M2-type TAMs could increase the level of α-B-Crystallin 
and activate the extracellular regulated kinase 1/2 (ERK1/2)/Fos-re -
lated antigen 1 (Fra-1)/Slug signaling pathway, mediating the EMT 
process to promote the malignant metastasis of lung cancer (Table 1). 
Understanding 
TAM’s role in tumors and its related mechanisms is of 
great significance for the development of effective treatment options. 
Studies have shown that macrophages can engulf and kill cancer cells 
when they are given toll-like receptor agonists and integrin associated 
protein inhibitors [39]. In this way, macrophages can be activated to 
attack cancer cells. This research provides a new way of thinking about 
anticancer treatments. At present, research on TAMs in cancer treatment 
mainly focuses on how to use effective means to convert TAMs from type 
M2 to type M1. Shields et al. [40] designed a ""backpack"" that could 
secret the pro-inflammatory cytokine interferon-γ (IFN-γ). The knapsack 
consists of two polylactic acid-glycolic acid layers and a polyvinyl 
alcohol layer in the middle, while IFN-γ exists in the middle layer. They 
injected backpack-carrying macrophages into a mouse model and found 
that these macrophages were M1 type in vivo and advanced the con -
version of other macrophages to M1 type, thereby inhibiting tumor 
metastasis. This research has considerably boosted the development of 
TAMs as a cancer treatment. 
Besides, Salazar et al. [41] investigated the effect of lymphocyte 
subsets in the tumor microenvironment on the development and 
