metastasis of lung cancer. Using in vitro cell co-culture, in vivo mouse 
models and human lung cancer tissues, they confirmed that T helper 9 
(Th9) and Th17 lymphocytes are abundant in human lung cancer tissues 
and play a role in inducing EMT transformation (Table 1). In addition, 
megakaryocytes [42] in lung cancer and endothelial cells in TME also 
promote the metastasis of lung cancer. Studies have shown that tumor 
cells can attack endothelial cells (Fig. 1G). So they can leave the blood 
vessels and reach the metastatic sites. This mechanism is primarily 
mediated by the interaction between amyloid precursor protein (APP) 
on the tumor cell surface and death receptor 6 (DR6) of the endothelial 
cell membrane [43] (Fig. 1G). 
An extensive and in-depth understanding of the role of stromal cells 
in TME will contribute to a better understanding of the biology of lung 
cancer metastasis and bring new options for the treatment of lung can-
cer. At present, natural killer (NK) cell therapy is gradually becoming the 
focus of anticancer research. NK cells kill cancer cells and limit their 
proliferation and metastasis. However, tumor cells can evolve various 
strategies to avoid NK cell killing, such as the shedding of major histo -
compatibility complex-class I chain related proteins A (MICA) and MICB 
protein [44]. Antibodies against 
MICA and MICB can actually increase 
MICA and MICB on the surface of cancer cells, enabling NK cells to exert 
a potent anti-tumor effect. Therefore, more antibodies binding to NK 
cells and tumor cell surface antigens are needed to be explored, with the 
purpose of injecting additional power for the effective use of NK cells. 
The application of stromal cells in the field of lung cancer has great 
potential, and the understanding and exploitation of stromal cells based 
on TME is the future direction. 
2.1.3. ECM 
The degradation and remodeling of ECM play an important role in 
tumor metastasis [45]. Tumor cells use their surface receptors to bind to 
various components of ECM, thereby activating or secreting 
protein-degrading enzymes to degrade the matrix and facilitate tumor 
cell metastasis. The occurrence, progression, invasion and metastasis of 
malignant tumors are often accompanied by changes in the ECM and the 
expression of its cell surface receptors. 
Gocheva et al. [46] studied the changes in the expression of ECM 
protein during the progression of lung cancer and found that tenascin-C 
(TNC) was highly expressed in lung cancer tissues. After further analysis, 
they confirmed that high levels of TNC were a driving factor for lung 
cancer metastasis. NK2 homeobox 1 (NKX2–1), a transcription factor 
that can block the expression of tumor metastatic genes, reduces TNC 
expression by binding to TNC. However, during the malignant trans -
formation of lung cancer, the low expression of NKX2 –1 leads 
to the 
increase of TNC, which results in the metastasis of lung cancer. At the 
same time, their study also found that the abnormal expression of TNC 
was related to the survival prognosis of lung cancer patients, which 
could be used as a clinical indicator for the diagnosis of patients’ sur-
vival prognosis. Sneeggen et al. [47] found that WD repeat and FYVE 
domain containing 2 (WDFY2) interacts with vesicle-associated mem -
brane protein 3 (VAMP3) to reduce the secretion of MMP14 and tumor 
invasion (Fig. 1 E). 
Therefore, when WDFY2 is knocked out, the 
increased secretion of MMP14 causes the degradation and remodeling of 
ECM. Studies have shown that the synergistic effect of HIF-1 and protein 
53 (p53) gene mutations produces the upregulation of ECM components, 
which are involved in NSCLC progression [48]. Park et al. [49] found 
that transcription factor activator protein 2C regulates the expression of 
Rho family GTPase 3 and has a role in fibroblast collision guidance. 
Fibroblasts can release ECM-related components, participate in the 
accumulation of ECM, and eventually lead to the spread of cancer cells. 
Due to the role of MMPs in the degradation of ECM, the main drugs 
targeting ECM degradation in clinical are MMP inhibitors. Studies have 
shown that targeting ECM is a better way to combat cancer metastasis 
than targeting the cancer cells themselves [50]. The researchers have 
also developed a miniaturized nano antibody that specifically targets the 
ECM protein, demonstrating that these nano antibodies can be used to 
clearly image tumor tissue and metastases with imaging techniques. 
They noted that if certain drugs are conjugated to these nano antibodies, 
there may be even greater gains in cancer treatment. ECM has been 
identified as having a significant impact on cancer progression, but there 
has been little research on ECM-based diagnostic or therapeutic ap -
proaches. ECM will be a novel target for anti-tumor metastasis, which 
has immense research value. 
2.1.4. Exosomes 
Exosomes are extracellular vesicles with diameters between 30 and 
150 nm in body fluids. Exosomes contain nucleic acids, proteins, lipids 
and so on. A large number of studies have shown that exosomes in TME 
can transport and transmit many important signaling molecules, 
affecting the occurrence and progression of tumors [51]. Exosomes can 
release 
some cytokines or proteins that are participated in the regulation 
of angiogenesis. Fan et al. [52] have identified the mechanism that 
exosomes originating from lung cancer release microRNA-210 
(miR-210) in lung cancer metastasis (Fig. 1F and Table 2). MiR-210 
induces CAFs to secrete angiogenesis factors such as fibroblast growth 
factor 2 (FGF2) and VEGF, which play a role in the invasion and 
metastasis of lung cancer cells through the JAK2-STAT3 signaling 
pathway (Fig. 1F). Mo et al. [26] also found that exosome-derived 
angiopoietin-like 4 (ANGPTL4) promoted the metastasis and invasion 
of A549 cells. ANGPTL4 also induces angiogenesis in human umbilical 
vein endothelial cells. 
Moreover, exosomes can also regulate EMT formation and partici -
pate in lung cancer metastasis. Qiao et al. [53] discovered a new 
mechanism by which tumor-derived exosomes induce lung cancer 
metastasis (Table 1 ). 
Exosomes belong to small extracellular vesicles 
(sEVs). The researchers applied quantitative proteomics techniques to 
detect more than 1400 proteins in sEVs, and found that sEVs-hepatocyte 
growth factor (HGF) was a protein associated with lung cancer metas -
tasis. At the same time, after in-depth study, they found that sEVS-HGF 
may be interacted with cellular mesenchymal-to-epithelial transition 
factor (c-MET) to induce EMT process and push for lung cancer metas -
tasis. Zhang et al. [54] found the effect of miRNA coated by exosomes 
from bone marrow mesenchyme stem cells in the invasion and metas -
tasis of lung cancer. MiR-193a-3p, miR-210 –3p and 
miR-5100 encap-
sulated by exosomes activate the STAT3 signaling pathway and promote 
EMT and metastasis. 
Exosomes can also assist tumor immune escape and strengthen the 
occurrence and development of lung cancer. It was found that lung 
cancer with the E746-A750 deletion mutation was able to inhibit the 
function of dendritic cells through exosomes, thereby inducing immu -
nosuppression of lung cancer cells [55]. Kim et 
al. [56] demonstrated 
that exosomes expressing PD-L1 from lung cancer are ""accomplices"" of 
immune escape from lung cancer. Exosomes play a role in the destruc -
tion of immune function by reducing the activity of T cells and inducing 
the apoptosis of CD8 T cells. 
Through interaction with lung cancer microenvironment, exosomes 
mediate the angiogenesis, EMT transformation, immune regulation and 
other aspects, involving in the occurrence and development of lung 
cancer. It also provides a new option for clinical treatment of lung 
cancer. For example, the inhibition of exosome secretion may be a 
possible therapeutic method. At present, the mechanism of exosomes 
promoting the occurrence and development of lung cancer still requires 
a lot of in-depth exploration, with a hope of laying a foundation for the 
research and development of potential anti-lung cancer drugs in the 
future. 
2.2. LCSCs 
LCSCs are kind of cells with multidirectional differentiation potential 
and self-renewal ability. It has been found that markers on the surface of 
LCSCs can enhance lung cancer metastasis through diverse mechanisms. 
For example, cluster of differentiation 44 (CD44) functions through the 
S. Xie et al.

Biomedicine & Pharmacotherapy 138 (2021) 111450
6
Wnt/β-catenin-Forkhead box M1 (FOXM1)-Twist signaling pathway 
[57]. Some studies have also pointed out that CD44 can increase the 
transcription of PD-L1, which is positively correlated with the expres -
sion of PD-L1 and immune cell infiltration [58]. In addition, 
inflam-
matory factors also play a role in influencing the state of lung cancer 
stem cells. CD133 is also a marker of LCSCs. It has been found that IL-6 in 
TME can enhance the self-renewal ability and EMT of CD133-positive 
cells, thus driving the transfer process [59] (Fig. 1 C). 
CD166 is also 
expressed actively in LCSCs. Li et al. [60] designed a novel bispecific 
antibody-5 (BsAb-5) specifically targeting c-MET and cytotoxic 
t-lymphocyte associated protein 4 (CTLA-4) in CD166-positive LCSCs. 
BsAb-5 takes effect dramatically in promoting c-MET degradation, 
reducing T-regulatory cells and increasing effector T cells. This study 
also demonstrates the possibility of BsAb-5 in the treatment of NSCLC. 
There are also sufficient reasons to believe that the related signaling 
pathways regulating LCSCs are also strongly connected with the 
occurrence and malignant metastasis of lung cancer. The major 
signaling pathways are Notch, Wnt and Hedgehog pathways. Clinically, 
the targeted therapy for LCSCs has a certain application prospect. Tar -
geting relevant signal transduction pathways for cancer intervention is 
considered to be an effective anti-cancer measure [61]. We found 
that 
curcumin has a significant intervention effect on LCSCs by targeting 
Wnt/β-catenin and Hedgehog pathways, inhibiting activation of the 
pathways and thereby blocking the expression of markers in LCSCs [62]. 
At present, the understanding of LCSCs is still unclear. And there are still 
many problems to be solved, such as finding specific molecular markers 
to identify LCSCs, and exploring effective methods to eliminate LCSCs. 
Therefore, it is quite necessary to study the mechanism of LCSCs in 
metastasis and to determine the precise therapy targeting LCSCs. 
2.3. EMT and lung cancer metastasis 
Current studies have shown that the EMT is involved in various 
tumor metastasis processes, including lung cancer. EMT refers to the 
transformation of epithelial cells into mesenchymal cells. In 2018, Pas-
tushenko et al. [63] found that EMT does not occur completely in the 
process of tumor metastasis. Mixed EMT contributes to tumor metas -
tasis. During this process, EMT-related proteins such as E-cadherin, 
cytokeratin decrease, while the expression of N-cadherin, vimentin in -
crease. This leads to the loss of adhesion between epithelial cells, the 
degradation of ECM, and cancer cell proliferation. In turn, mesenchymal 
cells can be transformed into epithelial cells. It is called 
mesenchymal-epithelial transition, which allows cancer cells to move to 
the specific organs and form metastases. Additionally, it has been 
confirmed that the activation of EMT is regulated by a variety of tran -
scription factors, such as snail, slug, ZEB1/2, twist. Many signaling 
pathways are involved in regulating EMT, such as TGF- β signaling 
