pathway, Wnt signaling pathway, Notch signaling pathway. An in-depth 
study of the functions of EMT in lung cancer metastasis is helpful to 
provide a new reference for clinical treatment of lung cancer. 
2.3.1. Regulate EMT-related factors 
Many factors can be associated with lung cancer metastasis by 
regulating EMT-related proteins or transcription factors. Its expression 
level in lung cancer tissues and cells is closely connected with lung 
cancer metastasis. Guo et al. [64] found that Cyclophilin A (CypA) was 
positively correlated with lung cancer metastasis rate (Table 1). CypA 
can induce the expression of EMT-related proteins and greatly improve 
the metastasis ability of NSCLC. In addition, this study asserts that CypA 
functions via p38 MAPK. Zhang et al. [65] found increased expression of 
mitotic arrest-deficient protein 2B (MAD2B) in metastatic lung cancer 
tissues (Table 1). MAD2B may promote EMT by enhancing the expres -
sion of slug. Ding et al. [66] found that NIMA-related kinase-4 (NEK4) 
positively regulates EMT (Table 1 ). 
NEK4 kinase down-regulates 
E-cadherin and up-regulates ZEB1 and small mothers against decap -
entaplegic protein 3 (Smad3), thereby expediting EMT and lung cancer 
metastasis. 
Perumal et al. [67] found that phosphatase tension homolog (PTEN) 
is involved 
in regulating EMT (Table 1). Inactivation of PTEN brings 
about decreased E-cadherin and increased N-cadherin, vimentin and 
MMP-2. Furthermore, PTEN inactivation also results in nuclear trans -
location of β-catenin and snail 
/slug, which advances the EMT. Zhao 
et al. [68] found that the level of high temperature requirement factor 
A3 (HtrA3) protein is low in NSCLC tissues and even lower in metastatic 
lung cancer tissues (Table 1). They also found a negative correlation 
between TGF-β1 and HtrA3 protein. Low expression of HtrA3 protein 
causes high expression of TGF-β1, thereby promoting lung cancer 
metastasis. This study provides a new avenue for targeted therapy 
against TGF-β. Xu et al. [69] found that tripartite motif-containing 
protein 29 (TRIM29) was related to the proliferation and metastasis of 
lung squamous cell carcinoma (LSCC) (Table 1). TRIM29 reduces the 
expression of E-cadherin and has a decisive influence on LSCC 
metastasis. 
2.3.2. Regulate EMT-related signaling pathways 
There are likewise some factors involved in the regulation of EMT- 
related signaling pathways. Wang et al. [70] found that E74-like fac -
tor 3 (ELF3) was overexpressed in NSCLC (Table 1). 
ELF3 activates 
PI3K/PKB and ERK signaling pathways to promote EMT. Li et al. [71] 
confirmed the mechanism of tubulin polymerization promoting protein 
3 (TPPP3) in lung cancer metastasis (Table 1). Overexpressed TPPP3 can 
activate the STAT3/Twist1 pathway to facilitate the transition process. 
Zhu et al. [72] concluded that the expression level of Aldo-keto reduc -
tase family 1 member C1 (AKR1C1) was positively correlated with 
metastasis ability of lung cancer (Table 1 ). 
AKR1C1 helps phosphory -
lation of STAT3 and plays a critical role in the STAT3 pathway. 
Sun et al. [73] found that Krüppel-like factor 3 (KLF3), which was 
low expressed in most lung cancer tissues, could promote EMT through 
STAT3 signal transduction (Table 1). 
Moreover, studies have found that 
Schwann cells, as the principal glial cells in the lung, also play an 
important role in the EMT process [74] (Table 1). C-X-C motif chemo -
kine ligand 5 (CXCL5) derived from Schwann cells binds to C-X-C motif 
chemokine receptor 2 (CXCR2) and activates the PI3K/PKB/Glycogen 
synthase kinase 3 β (GSK-3β)/snail-twist 
signaling pathway, so as to 
boost EMT and metastasis. This study also establishes the function of the 
peripheral nervous system in the lung cancer microenvironment in lung 
cancer progression and metastasis. 
2.3.3. Tumor metabolism and EMT 
Studies have shown that some certain metabolites or some rate- 
limiting enzymes in the metabolic pathways of tumor cells can also 
participate in the regulation of EMT. Wang et al. [75] revealed the 
mechanism by which UDP-glucose 6-dehydrogenase (UGDH) 
accelerates lung cancer metastasis (Table 1). UGDH phosphorylates 
under the epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) activation and cat-
alyzes the conversion of UDP-glucose (UDP-Glc) into UDP-glucuronic 
acid. UDP-Glc can stop the connection between snail’s mRNA 
and 
human antigen R protein and reduce its expression. UGDH’s function is 
to prevent UDP-Glc from playing an anti-cancer role, increase the sta -
bility of snail, and thus promote EMT and metastasis. Szymura et al. [76] 
reported that glutamate-fructose-6-phosphate transaminase 2 (GFPT2) 
can regulate EMT and promote NSCLC metastasis (Table 1). 
NF-κB leads 
to GFPT2 expression. This study reflects the association between EMT 
and the metabolic pathways of lung cancer progression. 
2.4. Angiogenesis and lymphangiogenesis 
The metastasis of malignant tumor cells to specific organs depends 
on the formation of blood vessels and lymphatics. Angiogenesis and 
lymphangiogenesis provide both the nutrients tumor cells need and the 
pathways for metastasis. VEGF mediates angiogenesis and lym -
phangiogenesis. Among them, VEGF-A mainly relates to angiogenesis. 
VEGF-C and VEGF-D are mainly associated with lymphatic metastasis of 
tumors. Guo et al. [77] discovered the mechanism by which 
tumor-associated calcium signal transducer 2 (TACSTD2) mediates 
NSCLC invasion. TACSTD2 prompts MMP13 and platelet endothelial cell 
adhesion molecule-1 (PECAM1) to increase, leading to neo -
vascularization through the ERK1/2 signaling pathway. Cohen et al. 
[78] found the mechanism by which BTB and CNC homology 1 (BACH1) 
regulates tumor progression. They have shown that BACH1 increases the 
expression of VEGF-C, promotes the generation of blood vessels and 
lymphatic vessels, and plays a crucial role in tumor invasion and 
metastasis. 
At present, some antivascular drugs have been put into clinical 
application. The study found that combining bevacizumab with apatinib 
inhibits angiogenesis [79]. The main 
mechanism is that they reduce 
VEGF and vascular endothelial growth receptor-2 (VEGFR-2). A new 
drug LIGHT and a vascular targeting peptide (LIGHT-VTP) can 
normalize blood vessels in tumor tissues and stop metastasis [80]. And 
the new drug could also be employed to treat metastases. Understanding 
the mechanism of angiogenesis and lymphangiogenesis in lung cancer 
metastasis and the study of targeted drugs has great potential in clinical 
application. 
3. Molecular mechanism of lung cancer metastasis 
3.1. NcRNAs and lung cancer metastasis 
NcRNAs were once thought of as ""junk sequences"" that did not 
participate in the regulation of gene expression. However, with the 
development of research, it is now believed that ncRNAs are a class of 
molecules that have nothing to do with the coding of proteins, but can 
control gene expression in the form of RNA. More and more studies have 
demonstrated that many ncRNAs, including miRNA, circular RNA 
(circRNA) and lncRNA, can play a key role in tumor proliferation, in -
vasion and metastasis by regulating EMT and participating in angio -
genesis and other mechanisms. This involves multiple signal pathways 
to participate in regulation. 
3.1.1. MiRNA 
MiRNAs are a class of ncRNAs that are approximately 19 –25 nucle-
otides long. MiRNAs bind to the 3’-UTP region 
of target mRNA to 
different degrees to regulate post-transcriptional gene expression. A 
large number of studies have proved that miRNAs closely related to lung 
cancer metastasis can target different proteins and related genes and 
regulate various signal transduction pathways, which play a role of 
promotion or inhibition in the process of lung cancer metastasis. The 
change of expression level matters in the metastasis of lung cancer. 
S. Xie et al.

Biomedicine & Pharmacotherapy 138 (2021) 111450
8
3.1.1.1. Regulation of miRNA on EMT. MiRNAs interact with their 
target genes, put an end to the expression of the target, and mediate the 
EMT process by regulating EMT-related proteins or signaling pathways. 
Li et al. [81] found low expression of miR-182 in metastatic NSCLC cells 
(Table 2). At the same time, they confirmed that miR-182 inhibits the 
expression of MET gene and prevents the phosphorylation of PKB and 
the accumulation of snails. As a result, it plays the role of deterring EMT 
and metastasis. Therefore, its down regulation of level boosts the 
development of transfer. Zhao et al. [82] discovered low expression of 
miR-625 in NSCLC (Table 2). MiR-625 inhibits its targets for the 
expression of Resistin. Through the inhibition of PI3K/PKB/Snail 
signaling pathways, it works for its purpose of anti-cancer transfer. 
Accordingly, the decrease of expression level gives rise to the transfer. 
Some miRNAs are also highly expressed in lung cancer. Zhang et al. 
[83] found that miR-411-5p/3p highly expressed in human NSCLC could 
reduce the level of its target sprouty 4 (SPRY4) and 
thioredoxin-interacting protein (TXNIP) and induce EGFR and PKB 
signaling pathways (Table 2). As a consequence, it enhances EMT and 
metastasis. Liang et al. [84] found that miR-421 up-regulated in NSCLC 
tissues and cells directly targets homeodomain-only protein homeobox 
(HOPX) (Table 2). MiR-421 mediates the Wnt/β-catenin signaling 
pathway, which exerts positive influence on EMT and metastasis. In 
addition, studies have shown that the over expression of miR-421 in -
hibits the level of Kelch-like ECH-associated protein 1 (KEAP1) so 
as to 
promote the metastasis [85] (Table 2). 
Moreover, high expressions of some miRNAs play different roles in 
the metastasis of lung cancer. Zhao et al. [86] found different effects of 
the over expression of miR-374a in NSCLC metastasis (Table 2). In the 
initial stage, miR-374a inhibits its target Cyclin D1 (CCND1) and has an 
effect on preventing metastasis by impeding PI3K/PKB and Ras 
signaling pathways. In the late stage, miR-374a acts on its target PTEN 
and plays the active function of inducing metastasis by developing 
Wnt/β-catenin and Ras signaling pathway. 
3.1.1.2. Regulation of miRNA on angiogenesis. In recent years, the 
mechanism of miRNA in tumor angiogenesis has become one of the 
hotspots in tumor research. As an angiogenesis related factor, VEGF is 
also a crucial target of miRNA in regulating angiogenesis. Liu et al. [87] 
confirmed the low expression of miR-195 in squamous cell lung cancer 
(SQCLC) cells (Table 2). They found that miR-195-5p may hinder 
angiogenesis by down-regulating VEGF, thereby obstructing SQCLC cell 
metastasis. Great importance must be attached to an in-depth study of 
the mechanism of miRNA in lung cancer angiogenesis because it is of 
momentous significance for future clinical use of miRNA targeted 
therapy. 
3.1.1.3. Other possible mechanisms. Some miRNAs are linked to lung 
cancer metastasis mainly through direct regulation of their target gene 
expression. Wang et al. [88] confirmed that miR-183-5p can prevent the 
expression of its target of PTEN and exert positive influence on lung 
cancer metastasis (Table 2). Further studies showed that miR-183-5p 
also inhibits the expression of p53 and promoted the phosphorylation 
of PKB, thus improving the aggression of lung cancer cells. Li et al. [89] 
found that miR-629-5p hinders the expression of peptidyl prolyl isom -
erase domain and WD repeat-containing protein 1 (PPWD1) and 
Cad-
herin EGF laminin G seven-pass G-type receptors 1 (CELSR1) and 
helps 
the infiltration of lung cancer cells and endothelial cell, which plays a 
significant role in the invasion and metastasis of LUAD (Table 2). Shi 
et al. [90] detected low expression of miR-130a in NSCLC (Table 2). 
Their further studies demonstrated that miR-130a could inhibit the 
expression of HIF-1α, thereby holding back the invasion of lung cancer 
cells and the Warburg effect. Therefore, under hypoxia in NSCLC, the 
low expression of miR-130a plays a pro-oncogenic role by targeting 
HIF-1α. 
3.1.2. Competitive endogenous RNA (ceRNA) 
CeRNA hypothesis means that two RNA molecules can generate 
regulatory effects by competitively binding the same miRNA. CeRNA 
molecules include mRNA, lncRNA, circRNA and pseudogenes. In recent 
years, more and more researches have been carried out on the complex 
regulatory system of ceRNA. Many circRNA and lncRNA have been 
found to be closely related to the occurrence, proliferation, metastasis 
and other processes of lung cancer [91–94]. These circRNA and lncRNA 
are expected to be biomarkers for the diagnosis and treatment of lung 
cancer, greatly enriching the new ideas of clinical lung cancer treatment. 
3.1.2.1. CircRNA. CircRNA is a kind of ncRNA with special closed ring 
structure. The main function of circRNA is to serve as the sponge 
adsorbent of miRNA and regulate the expression level of target genes of 
miRNA. It was found that there are very obvious differences in the 
expression of many circRNAs between normal tissue and lung cancer 
tissue. This difference in expression level determines the function of 
circRNA in lung cancer metastasis. 
Zhang et al. [95] found that circSATB2 was up-regulated in NSCLC 
tissues and cells as well as serum exosomes (Table 2). CircSATB2 acts as 
a sponge for miR-326 and enhances the expression of fascin 
actin-bundling protein 1 (FSCN1), thus participating in the progression 
of lung cancer metastasis. Wang et al. [96] found that circDLGAP4 is 
involved in the occurrence and development of lung cancer (Table 2). 
CircDLGAP4 is regarded as a sponge for miR-143 and promotes cyclin 
dependent kinase 1 (CDK1), the target of miR-143. It facilitates the lung 
cancer progression. Ren et al. [97] found low expression of hsa_ -
circ_0043265 in NSCLC tissues and cells (Table 2). 
It is taken as a sponge 
for miR-25-3P and increases the level of forkhead box protein 2 
(FOXP2), a target of miR-25-3P, thereby inhibiting metastasis. 
CircRNAs regulate target genes through related miRNAs and increase 
the expression of target genes in order to play an important role in lung 
cancer invasion and metastasis. CircRNA is an emerging field of 
research. However, as the circRNA system is large and complex, many 
mechanisms remain unclear. Therefore, a large amount of data and 
molecular mechanism research will be needed in the future. 
3.1.2.2. LncRNA. The length of lncRNAs is greater than 200 nucleo -
tides. Metastasis-associated lung adenocarcinoma transcript1 
(MALAT1) is the first lncRNA discovered. Studies have shown that 
MALAT1 is closely related to brain and bone metastasis of lung cancer 
[98,99]. LncRNA can 
also act as a sponge to adsorb miRNA and thus play 
an outstanding role. Studies have found that prostate cancer-associated 
transcript 6 (PCAT6) is an up-regulated oncogene in NSCLC [100]. In the 
same year, Cui et al. [101] showed that lncRNA PCAT6 combines with 
miR-330-5p to advance lung cancer metastasis (Table 2). Pan et al. 
[102] found that the over expression of lncRNA just proximal to XIST 
(JPX) enhances the migration ability of lung cancer cells (Table 2). JPX 
interacts with miR-33a-5p to improve the level of target gene Twist1 and 
activate the Wnt/β-catenin pathway. By this means, JPX is conducive to 
EMT and metastasis. Liu et al. [103] showed that lncRNA homeobox A 
cluster antisense RNA 2 (HOXA-AS2) plays a role in lung cancer 
metastasis through sponging miR-520a-3p (Table 2). HOXA-AS2 can 
regulate the levels of target genes Homeobox D8 (HOXD8) and mito -
gen‑activated protein 
kinase kinase kinase 2 (MAP3K2) of miR-520a-3p. 
Zheng et al. [104] found that HOXA-AS2 also increases the level of 
insulin-like growth factor 2 (IGF2) (Table 2). This is another mechanism 
by which HOXA-AS2 induces lung cancer cell metastasis. 
Some lncRNAs interact with related proteins to participate in the 
regulation of lung cancer metastasis. Yu et al. [105] confirmed that 
MetaLnc9 is related to lung cancer metastasis (Table 2). The association 
between highly expressed MetaLnc9 and phosphoglycerate kinase 1 
(PGK1) activates the PKB/mTOR pathway. The interaction between 
MetaLnc9 and non-POU domain containing octamer-binding gene 
(NONO) resulted in the activation of CREB-regulated transcription 
S. Xie et al.

Biomedicine & Pharmacotherapy 138 (2021) 111450
9
coactivator (CRTC). Both of these two molecular mechanisms proved 
that MetaLnc9 has an active effect on the metastasis of lung cancer cells. 
Yao et al. [106] found the mechanism of JHDM1D antisense 1 
(JHDM1D-AS1) in NSCLC. JHDM1D-AS1 binds to DEAH-box helicase 15 
(DHX15) to stabilize DHX15, so that it can accelerate the metastasis 
process of NSCLC (Table 2). 
In recent years, with the rapid development of sequencing technol -
ogy, a large number of new ncRNAs have been discovered. And many 
miRNAs, circRNAs and lncRNAs related to lung cancer metastasis have 
been gradually known. They may be able to serve as important bio -
markers or therapeutic targets, which are beneficial to open up new 
ideas for the determination of therapeutic targets and prognosis of lung 
cancer. 
3.2. Metastasis related factors of lung cancer 
3.2.1. Growth factors 
It has been proved that many growth factors are involved in the 
process of tumor occurrence, proliferation, invasion and metastasis. 
EMT and angiogenesis have something to do with various growth fac -
tors. The known growth factors include EGFRs, VEGF, FGF and TGF-β 
and so on. 
Wang et al. [107] found the function of fibroblast growth 
factor receptor 1 (FGFR1) in lung cancer metastasis (Table 1). FGFR1 
was activated by binding to FGF2. Activated FGFR1 gives impetus to 
EMT and metastasis through the FGFR1-ERK1/2- sex-determining re -
gion Y-box 2 (SOX2) axis. Additionally, Liu et al. [108] have identified 
T-cell immunoglobulin domain and mucin domain 4 (TIM-4) as a 
growth-promoting factor in lung cancer progression and metastasis. The 
expression of IL-6 is known to be up-regulated in NSCLC. IL-6 mediates 
the NF- κB pathway 
and boosts TIM-4 expression, which plays an 
important role in migration and invasion of NSCLC (Table 1). The study 
of related growth factors has enriched the potential metastasis mecha -
nism of lung cancer. 
3.2.2. Adhesion molecules 
Lung cancer cells express adhesion molecules that act as a role in the 
removal of cancer cells from the primary site and the arrival of certain 
organs for planting. At present, more studied adhesion molecules 
include cadherin, integrin family, selection family, immunoglobulin 
superfamily. Dai et al. [109] found that cell adhesion molecule 2 
(CADM2) was associated with brain metastasis of NSCLC ( Table 1). 
CADM2 
positively regulates EMT and exerts a positive impact on brain 
metastasis. Luo et al. [110] observed down regulation of CADM4 
expression in NSCLC (Table 1). CADM4 inhibits proliferation and 
metastasis of lung cancer cells by blocking the PKB signaling pathway. 
To add new ways for lung cancer metastasis treatment, more emphasis 
must be laid on exploring the mechanism of adhesion molecules in lung 
cancer metastasis. 
3.2.3. Chemokines 
The interaction of chemokines and their receptors is associated with 
the regulation of EMT, angiogenesis and the directional metastasis of 
malignant tumors to distal organs. Li et al. [111] found that chemokine 
CXCL6 could reduce the level of miR-515-5p in NSCLC and play an 
active part in metastasis. MiR-515-5p inhibits lung cancer metastasis by 
targeting CXCL6 (Table 2). This reflects the connection between CXCL6 
and miR-515-5p. Liang et al. [112] found that the level of CXCL16 is 
up-regulated in lung cancer tissues (Table 1). They further demonstrated 
that CXCL16 mediates NF-κB pathway. The latest study found that 
CX3CL1 and CX3CR1 are highly expressed in bone metastasis of lung 
cancer, suggesting that the CX3CL1-CX3CR1 axis may serve as a clinical 
reference index for bone metastasis of lung cancer [113]. Although some 
progress has been made in the study of chemokines and their receptors, 
the related transfer mechanism still needs to be further explored. 
3.2.4. Genes associated with lung cancer metastasis 
3.2.4.1. Genes that positively regulate lung cancer metastasis. Hao et al. 
[114] observed Tac2-N is overexpressed in metastatic lung cancer. 
Tac2-N, as a new oncogene, plays the role of improving the possibility of 
lung cancer metastasis by activating NF-κB (Fig. 2). Nguyen et al. [115] 
investigated the mechanisms by which mEAK-7 creates a positive asso-
ciation with the proliferation and metastasis of NSCLC. They found that 
mEAK-7 binds to DNA-dependent protein kinase catalytic subunit iso -
form 1 (DNA-PKcs) and participates in NSCLC metastasis through 
another mTOR pathway ( Fig. 2 ). 
Chuang et al. [116] revealed the 
relationship between germinal center kinase-like kinase (GLK) and lung 
cancer metastasis. This study shows that GLK interacts with IQ-domain 
GTPase-activating protein 1 (IQGAP1) to make tremendous promotion 
in lung cancer metastasis (Fig. 2). 
Studies have found that the myc gene relies on activating transcrip-
tion factor 4 (ATF4) to promote tumor progression [117]. Because of 
the 
myc gene, cancer cells can produce a large number of incorrectly folded 
proteins. After that, cancer cells eventually will undergo an endoplasmic 
reticulum stress response and die [118]. However, since myc gene can 
activate ATF4. ATF4 can be utilized to eliminate these proteins. As a 
result, cancer cells do not die and continue to proliferate (Fig. 2). This 
study indicates that targeted therapy with ATF4 inhibitors may be 
applied clinically in the future. 
3.2.4.2. Genes that negatively regulate lung cancer metastasis. Low ex -
pressions of tumor suppressor genes in lung cancer tissues are the basis 
of lung cancer metastasis. Okimoto et al. [119] proved that the mutation 
rate of capicua (CIC) gene 
in metastatic lung cancer was much higher 
than that in non-metastatic lung cancer. The result of the mutation is the 
inactivation of the CIC gene [120]. The MAPK signaling pathway me -
diates the inactivation of CIC gene, leading to tumor metastasis (Fig. 2). 
This 
study suggests that some drugs can be used to inhibit the metastasis 
of lung cancer by preventing the inactivation of CIC gene or blocking the 
activation of the MAPK pathway. 
Liang et al. [121] found that the down-regulated tumor suppressor 
gene quaking 5 (QKI-5) in NSCLC tissues negatively regulated 
miR-196b-5p (Fig. 2). Therefore, miR-196b-5p was highly expressed in 
NSCLC. Further study revealed that miR-196b-5p promoted migration 
and proliferation of lung cancer cells by stopping the level of its target 
genes GATA-binding factor 6 (GATA6) and tetraspanin 12 (TSPAN12) 
(Fig. 2 and Table 2). 
Understanding the genes involved in lung cancer metastasis is 
essential for determining treatment options for advanced lung cancer. 
For example, it may be feasible to benefit lung cancer patients with an 
overexpressed gene by targeting it with an inhibitor. This will bring 
prominent progress to the clinical treatment of lung cancer. 
3.3. Anaplastic lymphoma kinase (ALK) rearrangement and lung cancer 
metastasis 
In 2007, Soda et al. [122] first found ALK rearrangement in NSCLC. 
Normally, ALK protein activates intracellular signaling pathways and 
promotes cell growth by coupling with extracellular ligands. When ALK 
is fused with echinoderm microtubule-associated protein-like 4 (EML4), 
it leads to the development of lung cancer through the activation of 
multiple downstream pathways, which also make ALK an important 
target for cancer therapy. Some studies have found ALK translocation in 
approximately 3% of patients with brain metastases of NSCLC and ALK 
amplification in 11% of patients with brain metastases [123]. Thus, the 
incidence of brain metastasis associated with ALK rearrangement is 
increased in NSCLC. Tao et al. [124] collected and analyzed the clinical 
data of 40 patients with ALK-positive LUAD. The results showed that 
brain was the most common site of metastasis in patients with 
ALK-positive LUAD. Voena et al. [125] confirmed that ALK can directly 
S. Xie et al.

Biomedicine & Pharmacotherapy 138 (2021) 111450
10
reduce the expression of E-cadherin and increase the level of vimentin in 
ALK-rearranged NSCLC, and also participate in the expression of other 
EMT-related genes. 
Currently, ALK inhibitors, including crizotinib, brigatinib, and 
lauratinib, have been widely used in clinical treatment and prognosis of 
patients. And these drugs have higher treatment rate and better curative 
effect. As a first-generation ALK targeted drug, crizotinib has obvious 
therapeutic effects. But it can also lead to disease progression in patients 
with brain metastases or extracranial progression. Camidge et al. [126] 
found that brigatinib, as a second-generation ALK inhibitor, had 
significantly better efficacy, tolerability, and quality of life compared to 
crizotinib. Shaw et al. [127] compared lorlatinib with crizotinib in 
advanced ALK-positive lung cancer, a third-generation ALK inhibitor. 
The study found that lorlatinib had a higher progression-free survival 
rate and intracranial remission rate compared with crizotinib, suggest -
ing that lorlatinib may be considered as a first-line agent in patients with 
brain metastases from ALK-positive lung cancer. 
Clinical selection of ALK inhibitors requires careful consideration of 
drug resistance, CNS permeability, duration of treatment, and long-term 
adverse events. It is obvious that further research on ALK targeting drugs 
needs to be carried out from various aspects in order to bring more 
survival benefits to lung cancer patients. 
3.4. Other mechanisms 
Lignitto et al. [128] found the effect of nuclear factor erythroid 
2-related factor 2 ( NRF2) on 
BACH1 expression. Lung cancer patients 
have mutations in the NRF2 and KEAP1 genes. The results of the two 
gene mutations are the increased activity of NRF2 and the inactivation of 
KEAP1. NRF2 can induce the generation of heme oxygenase 1 in order to 
promote the degradation of heme and prevent the interaction between 
protoheme and F-box only protein 22 protein from causing the decom-
position of BACH1 (Fig. 2). 
So the eventual result is the accumulation of 
BACH1. BACH1 can help anaerobic glycolysis and provide a suitable 
acidic environment for lung cancer cells, thus fastening the rapid 
metastasis of lung cancer. Wiel et al. [129] found a mechanism by which 
antioxidants promote lung cancer metastasis. N-acetylcysteine and 
vitamin E play a role in the stabilization of BACH1 by reducing proto -
heme levels in the presence of mutations of kirsten rat sarcoma viral 
oncogene 2 ( KRAS2) genes. 
The above studies have revealed the up -
stream and downstream pathways of BACH1 in lung cancer metastasis. 
This suggests that targeted therapy on BACH1 may be a feasible solution. 
Li et al. [130] found the regulatory mechanism of tumor necrosis 
factor alpha-induced protein 1 (TNFAIP1) in lung cancer metastasis. 
Cullin3-regulator of Cullins-1 (ROC1)-BTB domain containing 9 
(BTBD9) ubiquitin ligase complex can reduce the expression of 
TNFAIP1. However, the down-regulation of BTBD9 expression leads to 
an increase of TNFAIP1 in the lung cancer tissues, which contributes to 
