the metastasis of lung cancer cells. Zhang et al. [131] demonstrated the 
mechanism of metastasis-associated protein 2 (MTA2) mediating NSCLC 
metastasis (Table 1). MTA2 activates the ERK/PKB signaling pathway 
and increases VEGF expression, thus playing a role in EMT induction and 
angiogenesis. Yan et al. [132] showed the mechanism of KH-type 
splicing regulatory protein (KHSRP) in lung cancer metastasis. Using 
co-immunoprecipitation experiments, researchers demonstrated that 
KHSRP interacts with heterogeneous nuclear ribonucleoprotein C to 
mediate the interferon- α/JAK/STAT1 signaling pathway, thereby 
boosting lung cancer metastasis. 
4. Studies on common metastatic sites of lung cancer 
4.1. Brain metastasis of lung cancer 
In recent years, more and more research has been done on brain 
metastasis of lung cancer. Some genes act as a role in brain metastases of 
lung cancer [133–135]. Singh et al. [136] found the role of Twist2 and 
sparc/osteonectin, cwcv, and kazal-like domains proteoglycan 1 
(SPOCK1) genes in brain metastasis of lung cancer. The researchers 
firstly adopt the specific cell culture method to cultivate a group of brain 
metastasis initiating cells (BMICs). And it is found that BMICs can form 
the brain metastasis sites. They further studied the genes associated with 
the formation of brain metastases and then discovered that Twist2 and 
SPOCK1 encourage brain metastases. 
Moreover, there are other drivers that play a part in lung cancer 
metastasis through a variety of mechanisms. Xu et al. [137] found that 
S100 binding protein A16 (S100A16) may be related to malignant brain 
metastasis of SCLC. S100A16 is highly expressed in SCLC induced by 
exosomes derived from human microvascular endothelial cells. 
S100A16 can inhibit the apoptosis of lung cancer cells and is regarded as 
a significant biomarker in brain metastasis. Wei et al. [138] found that 
the target gene of miR-330-3p with high level in the serum of NSCLC 
patients is glutamate receptor ionotropic amino-3-hydroxy-5 
-methyl-4-isoxazole-propionin acid 3 (GRIA3) (Table 2). MiR-330-3p 
promotes NSCLC brain metastasis by mediating the MAPK/ERK 
signaling pathway. Meanwhile, they also found that miR-330-3p pro -
motes EMT by inducing the connection of GRIA3 and TGF- β1 [139] 
(Table 
2). Rodrigues et al. [140] found the effect of the cell 
migration-inducing protein (CEMIP) expressed in the brain on tumor 
brain metastasis (Table 1). Research shows that CEMIP mediates Wnt 
signaling pathway to promote EMT. At the same time, CEMIP also 
promotes angiogenesis. CEMIP also induces the release of cytokines that 
can break the blood-brain barrier (BBB), allowing tumor cells to invade 
the brain. Wu et al. [141] found the correlation mechanism between 
smoking and brain metastasis of lung cancer. Nicotine itself does not 
cause cancer. But it can induce microglia cells to change from M1 to M2, 
so that lung cancer has ability to spread to the brain. 
Lung cancer with brain metastases is a disease with high clinical 
mortality. In recent years, although there has been some progress in the 
research of lung cancer brain metastasis, the detailed mechanism of lung 
cancer brain metastasis has not been fully elucidated. Some questions 
still need to be explored. For example, whether the driving factors in the 
current study can be applied clinically as therapeutic targets or not; 
whether the drugs currently being developed will have negative effects 
by acting on other pathways or not, etc [142–144]. In a word, it is of 
outstanding significance for clinical diagnosis and treatment to study the 
mechanism of brain metastasis in lung cancer. 
4.2. Bone metastasis of lung cancer 
Bone metastasis includes osteolytic bone metastasis, osteoblastic 
bone metastasis and mixed bone metastasis. Osteolytic bone metastasis 
is more frequent in lung cancer [145–148]. The receptor activator of 
nuclear factor κB/receptor activator of nuclear factor κB ligand/osteo-
protegerin (RANK/RANKL/OPG) pathway plays an important role in 
bone metastasis [149]. Yang et 
al. [150] found that formin-like protein 1 
(FMNL1) is highly expressed in bone metastasis of lung cancer (Table 1). 
FMNL1 mediates TGF-β1/Smad signaling pathway and boosts bone 
metastasis. Chen et al. [151] found the role of semaphorin 4D (Sema4D) 
in bone metastasis of lung cancer. Hypoxia leads to the production of 
HIF-1α. HIF-1α regulates the secretion of Sema4D by interacting with 
Sema4D and increasing the levels of a disintegrin and metalloproteinase 
17. Sema4D is an osteogenic inhibitor, which can inhibit the differen -
tiation of osteoblasts and promote the osteolytic bone metastasis of lung 
cancer. With the advancement of molecular biology technology, the 
research on the mechanism of bone metastasis of lung cancer will be 
clearer, so as to further develop new clinical treatment drugs and bring 
great survival benefits to patients. 
4.3. Lymph node metastasis of lung cancer 
Lymph nodes are also common sites of lung cancer metastasis [152]. 
Lymph node metastases 
are often asymptomatic in the early stages. As 
lymph nodes grow, they can press on surrounding blood vessels and 
nerves, causing symptoms such as pain. Geng et al. [153] showed that 
TRIM59 is associated with lymph node metastasis of lung cancer 
(Table 1). TRIM59 can participate in EMT and transfer process through 
ERK signaling pathway and increase CDK6. Clinically, patients have a 
high probability of lymph node metastasis. So it is necessary to find the 
driving factors of lymph node metastasis of lung cancer and provide 
clinical treatment ideas. 
4.4. Liver metastasis of lung cancer 
Liver is also one of the most common sites of metastasis in patients 
with advanced lung cancer. Diverse cell populations in the liver immune 
microenvironment play a major role in tumor genesis and metastasis. 
Numerous cellular components such as TAMs, myeloid derived sup -
pressor cells (MDSCs) and tumor-associated neutrophils (TANs) occupy 
an indispensable position in secreting VEGF and promoting angiogen -
esis. VEGF is not only a key factor leading to angiogenesis, but also 
makes a difference in regulating the formation of immunosuppressive 
microenvironment, leading to tumor cells escaping immune surveillance 
and metastasis [154]. Liver sinusoid 
endothelial cells (LSECS) are spe -
cific to the liver and are important cells in the formation of immune 
tolerance microenvironment. It was found that melittin nanoparticles 
can induce the activation of LSECs and lead to the activation of the liver 
immune microenvironment, thereby reducing the occurrence of liver 
metastasis [155]. This study 
suggests that LSECs may be a goal for future 
tumor immunotherapy. Zhou et al. [156] found a relationship between 
E26 transformation specific-1(ETS-1), a key transcription factor that 
causes the production and maturation of endothelial cell production, 
and NSCLC metastasis. The up-regulated expression of ETS-1 in NSCLC 
brings about increased expression of von Willebrand factor (vWF) and 
vascular endothelial (VE-cadherin), which results in the differentiation 
of lung cancer cells into endothelial-like cells. Consequently, ETS-1 is 
capable of further boosting tumor cell proliferation and metastasis. The 
study also showed that cells with high expression of ETS-1 could cause 
more liver metastasis. 
Previous studies have demonstrated that patients with lung cancer 
have the worst prognosis of liver metastasis [157]. In recent years, 
tumor immunotherapy, represented by programmed death receptor 1 
(PD-1)/PD-L1 antibody, has developed rapidly. However, studies have 
verified that the use of PD-1 in patients with liver metastases from lung 
cancer is not effective [158]. The IMPOWER150 study demonstrated 
that atezolizumab combined with bevacizumab and chemotherapy 
significantly improved overall survival and progression-free survival in 
patients with liver metastases from lung cancer [159]. This combination 
of immune and antivascular therapy has brought considerable clinical 
efficacy to patients with liver metastasis from lung cancer. 
S. Xie et al.

Biomedicine & Pharmacotherapy 138 (2021) 111450
12
4.5. Studies on paired samples of primary and metastatic lesions 
The paired analysis of samples between primary and metastatic le -
sions of lung cancer is beneficial to provide a new understanding for 
lung cancer metastasis and guide the development of new methods for 
inhibition. 
To look for factors associated with lung cancer metastasis, scientists 
often compare the genomes of primary and metastatic lung cancers. 
Previous studies have shown that EGFR mutation rate in bone metas -
tases reaches 75% [160]. This study 
also provided the right judgment for 
patients to use EGFR-tyrosine kinase inhibitors (EGFR-TKIs). Wang et al. 
[161] found that primary lung cancer sites and brain metastases are 
consistent with mutations of many driver genes, such as EGFR, KRAS, 
ALK, etc. But the genome of the brain metastases was more unstable 
[162]. Moreover, increased cell cycle regulators and mutations in the 
PI3K signaling pathway are observed in brain metastasis. This enhances 
the likelihood of brain metastases. Shih et al. [163] found that the copy 
number of myc, yes-associated protein 1 (YAP1), MMP13 and other 
genes in LUAD with brain metastasis is much higher than the primary 
lesion. This study demonstrated that upregulation of myc, YAP1 and 
MMP13 could result in brain metastasis of LUAD. Jiang et al. [164] 
studied and assessed the genomic characteristics of primary and 
metastasis in patients with LUAD, including liver and brain metastases. 
They found that many common driver gene mutations, including tumor 
protein p53 (TP53) and EGFR, showed high consistency between pairs of 
primary and metastatic tumors. Further analysis showed that the simi -
larity between liver metastases and paired primary sites was higher than 
that between brain metastases and paired primary sites, suggesting that 
the landscape of gene mutations in liver metastasis and brain metastasis 
of LUAD is different. 
Paired sample analysis of primary and metastatic sites can help 
identify genes that play a key role in the process of metastasis. Clarifying 
the heterogeneity of driver gene expression of primary and metastatic 
lung cancer will also provide powerful help for the diagnosis and se -
lection of treatment strategies in patients with lung cancer metastasis. 
5. Mechanisms of SCLC 
SCLC is more malignant, and it is easy to metastasize at an early 
stage. As a result, patients with SCLC have a lower quality of life and 
survival compared to other types of lung cancer. In recent years, sci -
entific experiments and clinical trials have been conducted on the 
mechanism of SCLC metastasis. Semenova et al. [165] found that the 
overexpressed transcription factor nuclear factor I B (NFIB) in SCLC not 
only accelerated tumor growth, but also was connected with tumor 
metastasis and spread. This study suggests that NFIB can be employed as 
an indicator for the clinical diagnosis of SCLC. Studies have shown that 
the chromatin status of SCLC metastases is not consistent with that of the 
primary lesions [166]. Higher accessibility 
of chromatin in metastatic 
tumor cell populations means that more open DNA-binding regions, 
which can bind more transcription factors to promote the transcription 
of related conserved genes. NFIB is a critical factor in regulating chro -
matin accessibility. NFIB plays a decisive role in increasing the acces -
sibility of chromatin, which in turn encourages more transcription 
factors to bind to open sites. In this way, the expression of 
neuron-related genes that accelerate metastasis is increased and ulti -
mately exerts a crucial effect on SCLC metastasis. Besides, Zhao et al. 
[167] revealed the mechanism of low-expressed Cullin5 (CUL5) and 
suppressor 
of cytokine signaling 3 (SOCS3) in SCLC (Fig. 2). The re -
searchers found that CUL5 and SOCS3 are components of the Cullin-Ring 
E3 ubiquitin ligase complex. Silence of these two genes brings about loss 
of integrity of complex and the increase of expression of integrin β1. In 
conclusion, the 
inactivation of two genes has a positive effect on 
metastasis through the focal adhesion kinase (FAK)/steroid receptor 
coactivator (SRC) pathway. This study also provides directions for tar -
geting SRC to give a treatment in SCLC with down-regulated CUL5. 
With extreme invasiveness of SCLC, many treatments do not respond 
well to patients. Recent studies have found that NK cell immunotherapy 
may prevent the spread of SCLC metastasis and effectively treat SCLC. It 
has been shown that loss of NK cells facilitates the process of SCLC 
metastasis in vivo, while increasing NK cell activity by increasing the IL- 
15 or TGF-β pathway can slow down SCLC metastasis [168]. Moreover, 
the combined treatment of NK cells and anti-PD-1 has a more significant 
effect. This study investigated the function of NK cells in the treatment of 
SCLC and provided a potent reference for the search for effective 
treatment regimens for SCLC. A study has found that SCLC cells lack a 
surface protein natural killer group 2, member D ligand (NKG2DL), 
which gives them strong immune escape ability and a greater chance of 
tumor growth and spread [169]. NKG2DL is a protein that interacts with 
NK cells and plays a role in immune surveillance and defense. Further-
more, the researchers concluded that the deletion of NKG2DL is not 
caused by a genetic deletion. Instead, it is because that genes are in 
tightly curled DNA and cannot be converted into proteins. When 
injecting into animal models of SCLC with drugs that can induce 
NLG2DL expression, the cells did not produce the protein. But when 
injecting the histone deacetylase (HDAC) inhibitors that can loosen 
DNA, NKG2DL began to express. This study provides novel therapeutic 
options for SCLC. Perhaps HDAC inhibitors are expected to be a blessing 
for patients with SCLC and improve the efficacy of immunotherapy. 
6. Experimental models of lung cancer and metastasis 
Establishing animal model is a crucial means of tumor research. 
Appropriate animal models of lung cancer and metastasis can provide a 
favorable platform and approach for in-depth exploration of the causes 
of lung cancer, metastasis mechanism, and clinical drug efficacy eval -
uation. At present, a variety of NSCLC and SCLC mouse models have 
been created to simulate the development and progression of human 
lung cancer [170]. Researchers can 
introduce oncogenes into mice or 
remove tumor suppressor genes so as to induce tumor generation, thus 
constructing a genetically engineered mouse model [171]. Or they can 
transplant fresh cancerous tissues from patients directly into mice with 
immunodeficiency to build a patient-derived xenograft tumor model 
[172]. Human or mouse tumor cells cultured in vitro can also be 
transplanted into mice with immunodeficiency to construct xenograft 
tumor models derived from cell lines [173]. 
Weiss et al. [174] transplanted a small number of human or mouse 
lung cancer cells into the left lung of mice to construct a novel animal 
model in situ. They used green fluorescent protein-labeled tumor cells 
and immunofluorescence staining to determine the microscopic distri -
bution of the tumor and identify infiltrating stromal cells and immune 
cells. This model is helpful to describe the important interaction be -
tween lung cancer cells and stroma under the condition of early lung 
cancer. Khan et al. [175] treated lung cancer epithelial A549 cells with 
TGF-β1 and 
injected them into the tail vein of nude mice, successfully 
establishing a model of lung cancer metastasis in mice pretreated with 
TGF-β1. They found that cells treated with TGF-β1 have a strong ability 
to metastasize to specific organs, such as liver, brain, bone, adrenal 
gland, and ovary, which are very similar to human lung cancer. This 
study is of major help in analyzing the in vivo metastasis behavior of 
lung cancer cells undergoing EMT. 
In addition, with the improvement of science and technologies, many 
scientists are also using advanced technology to build relevant tumor 
models, in addition to animal models. Meng et al. [176] utilized 3D 
bio-printing technology to establish in vitro tumor models with char -
acteristics of TME to simulate several elementary processes of cancer 
spread, including tumor cell invasion, intravascular perfusion, and 
angiogenesis. At the same time, the researchers also demonstrated the 
practicability of the transfer model in drug screening by comparing the 
anticancer effects of immunotoxin in the model with 2D monolayer 
cultured A549. Compared with the 2D monolayer cell culture model, the 
engineering model constructed by biological 3D printing can not only 
S. Xie et al.

Biomedicine & Pharmacotherapy 138 (2021) 111450
13
accurately summarize the characteristics of the TME in order to provide 
directions for fundamentally exploring the molecular mechanism of 
tumor metastasis, but also contribute to the screening of new anticancer 
drugs in clinical practice. Liu et al. [177] have constructed a multi-organ 
microfluidic chip model to help study the mechanism of brain metastasis 
in lung cancer. The model is composed of the upstream bionic ""lung"" and 
the downstream bionic ""brain"" with the BBB as the core. Its strength is 
that it can monitor the whole process of lung cancer brain metastasis by 
real-time monitoring the invasion of upstream lung cancer cells to the 
downstream target organs and BBB penetration. The model was also 
used to confirm that the aldo-keto reductase family 1 member B10 
protein, which is highly expressed in lung cancer brain metastasis, re -
duces the expression of MMP-2 and MMP-9 through the 
mitogen-activated protein extracellular signal-regulated kinase 
(MEK)/ERK signaling pathway, thereby promoting cancer cell invasion. 
The proposal of the model provides a novel method and means for 
studying the mechanism of lung cancer brain metastasis to a large 
degree. 
The establishment of experimental models of lung cancer and 
metastasis is the basis of lung cancer related researches. At present, 
many studies are based on mouse models. Although the mouse model 
simulates the in vivo environment of lung cancer patients to a certain 
extent, which is helpful to further understand the pathogenesis, metas-
tasis mode of lung cancer and observe the efficacy of clinical drugs, the 
occurrence and development process of human lung cancer cannot be 
completely imitated by mouse models [178]. Therefore, it 
is necessary to 
find more innovative modeling methods to accurately and reliably 
simulate the metastasis of lung cancer in vivo, so that greater progress 
can be achieved in the diagnosis and treatment of lung cancer 
metastasis. 
7. Clinical treatment of lung cancer metastasis 
The personalized treatment of lung cancer metastasis is the major 
direction of clinical research at present. Lung cancer has a tendency to 
metastasize to bone, brain, lymph nodes, and liver [2]. Treatments for 
these metastases vary greatly. 
Brain metastasis is a very challenging clinical problem. Because of 
the BBB, many drugs do not entirely enter the brain. This results in 
incomplete treatment and poor prognosis of lung cancer with brain 
metastasis. The current study shows that Zorifertinib is a new generation 
EGFR-TKI targeting brain metastases [179]. Unlike previous drugs, it 
crosses the BBB extremely effectively. This suggests that Zorifertinib 
may be a clinically effective therapeutic weapon. More consideration is 
being given to combining Zorifertinib with other treatments in the 
future. Among anti-angiogenic agents, bevacizumab has been shown to 
inhibit the proliferation of NSCLC brain metastases [180]. It has also 
become a new therapeutic option for patients with NSCLC brain me -
tastases clinically [181,182]. In addition, 
some studies have also 
analyzed that anlotinib, as an anti-angiogenic drug, has good efficacy in 
the treatment of NSCLC brain metastasis and SCLC brain metastasis 
[183,184]. Subsequent data studies are still needed to evaluate the 
safety of anlotinib so as to make the drug use more convenient. 
Bisphosphonates represented by zoledronic acid are commonly used 
in the treatment of bone metastases. However, serious adverse reactions 
such as nephrotoxicity affect its long-term application to some extent 
[185]. A large number of clinical studies have shown that denosumab, as 
a drug targeting RANKL, can effectively inhibit the vicious cycle caused 
by the RANK/RANKL/OPG pathway and has a significant advantage in 
anti-bone metastasis [186]. Compared with zoledronic acid, denosumab 
can significantly delay the occurrence of bone-related events, effectively 
improve the quality of life and prolong the survival of patients, which 
has a broad application prospect [187–189]. 
The treatment of lung cancer lymph node metastasis should be 
selected according to the degree of lymph node metastasis. Clinically, 
surgery, radiotherapy and chemotherapy are the primary methods. At 
present, the immunotherapy mode for lymph node metastasis is also 
being studied continuously, and it is believed that there will be great 
progress in clinical treatment in the future. 
For patients with liver metastasis, immunotherapy often fails to 
achieve satisfactory results [190]. Researchers have found that the 
proliferation of macrophages in the liver can induce systemic T cells to 
arrive in the liver and cause them to die, making it impossible for T cells 
to perform their cancer-fighting role and thus fail to meet the expecta -
tions of immunotherapy [190]. Immunization combined 
with therapy 
holds great promise for patients with liver metastasis. For example, 
immunotherapy combined with radiotherapy can reduce the apoptosis 
of T cells to a greater extent and relieve the immune suppression, thus 
making the curative effect markedly increased [190]. In addition, as 
mentioned earlier, atezolizumab in combination with bevacizumab and 
chemotherapy has also been shown to be significant in the treatment of 
liver metastases from NSCLC [159]. 
With the development of molecular biology, targeted therapy and 
immunotherapy have become an important direction in the treatment of 
lung cancer metastasis. More and more therapeutic drugs and thera -
peutic strategies are emerging. In the future, more optimized drugs and 
more appropriate strategies still need to be explored, aiming to bring 
breakthroughs to the improvement of survival rate and provide more 
options for the precise treatment of lung cancer. 
8. Conclusions and perspectives 
This review focuses on the relevant mechanisms of lung cancer 
metastasis in NSCLC, including the influencing factors of lung cancer 
metastasis, the molecular mechanisms and the research progress of lung 
cancer specific metastasis. Of course, the research on the manufacturing 
of lung cancer metastasis models, SCLC-related metastasis mechanisms 
and the current clinical treatment prospects are also included. At pre -
sent, the mortality rate for lung cancer patients is still very high. Most of 
them die from lung cancer metastasis. Therefore, it is urgent to fully 
elucidate the metastasis mechanisms of lung cancer for clinical diagnosis 
and treatment. 
The complex TME in the lung supports the progression of lung cancer 
metastasis. Acid, hypoxia, and inflammation create a site for cancer cells 
to quickly metastasize [12–15]. 
Stromal cells positively assist the inva-
sion and migration of lung cancer cells [31,32]. Degradation and 
remodeling 
of ECM are an important step in the multi-stage process of 
tumor metastasis, which is mediated by MMPs [45]. Exosomes are 
involved in the progression of lung cancer by their productive mecha -
nisms [51]. LCSCs have 
become the ""seeds"" of lung cancer metastasis due 
to their strong self-renewal ability and multidirectional differentiation 
[3–5]. EMT transformation as well as angiogenesis and lymphangio -
genesis are important auxiliary forces in lung cancer metastasis [6–8]. 
Components 
of TME provide great inspirations for the occurrence, 
development, metastasis and treatment of lung cancer. Some related 
targeted drugs have also been made available for clinical treatment. 
However, whether some newly developed drugs have negative effects on 
relevant targets or not and whether there are other mechanisms for the 
role of newly discovered factors or not in lung cancer metastasis needs to 
be more comprehensive. 
In recent years, ncRNAs have been the subject of research on the 
molecular mechanism. More and more ncRNAs have been found to be 
novel targets for future diagnosis, treatment and prognosis of lung 
cancer [191,192]. It is undoubtedly a great hope for lung cancer pa -
tients. But the detailed mechanisms of ncRNAs that are currently 
mastered are only the tip of the iceberg and require further investiga -
tion. For example, whether various ncRNAs are involved in lung cancer 
metastasis in different ways or not, whether circRNA [9] and lncRNA 
[192] have other functions or not, and the role of other ceRNAs in lung 
cancer metastasis, etc. The study of related factors and genes is also 
crucial. Perhaps targeting these factors can be the direction of future 
development. 
S. Xie et al.

Biomedicine & Pharmacotherapy 138 (2021) 111450
14
The organ specificity of a tumor determines where it metastasizes 
[2]. For specific metastasis of lung cancer, different sites of metastasis 
also have different influencing factors. And treatment methods are also 
very diverse. However, the current research on these factors is not yet 
clear. Some drugs cannot achieve good effects of treatment. Deepening 
grasp of these factors may provide novel targets for personalized anti -
cancer therapy. For the establishment of the model, how to design a 
truly effective model to simulate the process of human lung cancer 
metastasis is the direction of future efforts [178]. 
At present, many researches need to be further deepened. We still 
have a long way to go before we can fully grasp the detailed mechanisms 
of lung cancer metastasis and generalize it to clinical treatment. It is 
believed that people will be able to truly reveal the truth of lung cancer 
metastasis in one day with the continuous efforts of scientists. 
