Plasma extracellular vesicle long RNAs predict response to neoadjuvant 
immunotherapy and survival in patients with non-small cell lung cancer 
Wei Guo a,b,1, Bolun Zhou a,1, Liang Zhao a,1, Qilin Huai a,1, Fengwei Tan a,b, Qi Xue a,b, 
Fang Lv a,*, Shugeng Gao a,b,**, Jie He a 
a Department of Thoracic Surgery, National Cancer Center/National Clinical Research Center for Cancer/Cancer Hospital, Chinese Academy of Medical Sciences and 
Peking Union Medical College, Beijing, People’s Republic of China 
b Key Laboratory of Minimally Invasive Therapy Research for Lung Cancer, Chinese Academy of Medical Sciences, Beijing, People’s Republic of China   
ARTICLE INFO  
Keywords: 
Non-small cell lung cancer 
Extracellular vesicles 
Neoadjuvant immunotherapy 
Long RNAs 
ABSTRACT  
Neoadjuvant immunotherapy has brought new hope for patients with non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC). 
However, limited by the lack of clinically feasible markers, it is still difficult to select NSCLC patients who 
respond well and to predict patients’ clinical outcomes before the treatment. Before the treatment, we isolated 
plasma extracellular vesicles (EVs) from three cohorts (discovery, training and validation) of 78 NSCLC patients 
treated with neoadjuvant immunotherapy. To identify differentially-expressed EV long RNAs (exLRs), we 
employed RNA-seq in the discovery cohort. And we subsequently used qRT-PCR to establish and validate the 
predictive signature in the other two cohorts. We have identified 8 candidate exLRs from 27 top-ranked exLRs 
differentially expressed between responders and non-responders, and tested their expression with qRT-PCR in the 
training cohort. We finally identified H3C2 (P = 0.029), MALAT1 (P = 0.043) and RPS3 (P = 0.0086) signifi-
cantly expressed in responders for establishing the predictive signature. Integrated with PD-L1 expression, our 
signature performed well in predicting immunotherapeutic responses in the training (AUC=0.892) and valida-
tion cohorts (AUC=0.747). Furthermore, our signature was proven to be a predictor for favorable prognosis of 
patients treated with neoadjuvant immunotherapy, which demonstrates the feasibility of our signature in clinical 
practices (P = 0.048). Our results demonstrate that the exLR-based signature could accurately predict responses 
to neoadjuvant immunotherapy and prognosis in NSCLC patients.   
1. Introduction 
Lung cancer is one of the most prevalently diagnosed malignancies 
and leads to the majority of cancer-related deaths all over the world [1]. 
Surgery is the main therapeutic approach for resectable lung cancer, and 
preoperative or postoperative systemic therapy is implemented to 
improve clinical outcomes of patients, such as neoadjuvant chemo-
therapy [2]. Although neoadjuvant chemotherapy could benefit patients 
with non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), many patients still receive 
limited therapeutic benefits due to high tumor heterogeneity [3,4]. For 
the past few years, immunotherapy has been developed and widely used 
in the treatment of many cancers, including NSCLC [5]. Immune 
checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs) can enhance patients’ adaptive immunity 
against tumors and ultimately exert a stable antitumor effect for NSCLC 
[6]. Furthermore, ICIs have been successfully used as neoadjuvant 
treatment for resectable NSCLC, providing novel options for precision 
medication [7,8]. 
Recent clinical trials have revealed that 24% of NSCLC patients 
treated with neoadjuvant chemoimmunotherapy have achieved patho-
logical complete response (pCR), whereas only 2.2% of patients in the 
neoadjuvant chemotherapy subgroup achieved pCR [9]. However, 
neoadjuvant immunotherapy could only help a limited number of 
NSCLC patients, and a large proportion of NSCLC patients cannot ach-
ieve pCR. Specific molecular and genomic characteristics are highly 
associated with favorable clinical outcomes for NSCLC patients 
receiving immunotherapy, which may help select patients and make 
