clinical decisions before the treatment [10]. In addition, various mo-
lecular features could predict ICI’s clinical benefits, such as tumor mu-
tation burden, PD-L1 and RNA modification writers, yet the reliability of 
these biomarkers is unsatisfactory [11–13]. Herein, efficient biomarkers 
are urgently needed to help select patients most likely to benefit from 
neoadjuvant immunotherapy, and finally optimize therapeutic man-
agement for NSCLC patients. 
Extracellular vesicles (EVs) are usually released by different cells and 
can be detected in body fluids, such as plasma [14,15]. Emerging evi-
dence showed that EVs could transfer various cargoes, such as proteins, 
RNA and DNA, to direct intercellular and cell-microenvironment com-
munications, and subsequently regulate tumorigenesis and progression, 
which could be potential biomarkers for multiple cancers [16]. 
Compared with conventional biomarkers, EV cargoes from plasma are 
non-invasive and thus can lower the risk of spreading cancer cells. Yu 
et al. have shown that extracellular vesicle long RNAs (exLRs) from the 
plasma could be the biomarker for early diagnosis of pancreatic ductal 
adenocarcinoma, which has been successfully validated with an area 
under the curve (AUC) of 0.936 [17]. Furthermore, several studies have 
revealed the potential value of EV cargoes in predicting the efficacy of 
immunotherapy. For instance, Peng et al. have demonstrated that some 
plasma EV-derived microRNAs, such as hsa-miR-320d, could predict the 
clinical outcomes of immunotherapy in advanced NSCLC [18]. How-
ever, the correlation of EV cargoes with the efficacy of neoadjuvant 
immunotherapy has not yet been comprehensively reported in NSCLC. 
In the present study, we enrolled 78 NSCLC patients treated with 
neoadjuvant immunotherapy as three independent cohorts, and plasma 
samples were collected from each patient before the neoadjuvant 
treatment. With the use of RNA-seq and qRT-PCR, we have identified 
three plasma-derived exLRs as potential predictors, and then developed 
a signature for predicting the clinical efficacy of neoadjuvant immuno-
therapy in NSCLC patients. Moreover, our signature performed better 
than tissue-based PD-L1 expression in predicting patients’ responses to 
ICIs. Finally, an integrated signature of PD-L1 and plasma-derived exLRs 
was built and validated, which was highly accurate in risk stratification 
and might guide the development of neoadjuvant immunotherapy. 
2. Materials and methods 
2.1. Patient enrollment and sample collection 
The study included NSCLC patients who received neoadjuvant 
immunotherapy at the National Cancer Center/Cancer Hospital, Chinese 
Academy of Medical Sciences (NCC/CAMS, Beijing, China) from 
December, 2020 to February, 2023. The samples were divided into 
discovery, training and validation cohorts. A total of 18 patients were 
enrolled in the discovery cohort, 40 patients were enrolled in the 
training cohort and 20 patients were in the validation cohort. The in-
clusion criteria were as follows: [1] patients were pathologically diag-
nosed with NSCLC; [2] patients with no distant metastasis confirmed by 
computed tomography; [3] patients did not undergo radical surgery R0 
resection. The exclusion criteria were as follows: [1] patients received 
preoperative radiotherapy, chemotherapy and targeted therapy; [2] the 
detailed clinical data were missing; [3] the follow-up data were lacking. 
The baseline characteristics were presented in Table 1. 
Peripheral blood was collected from each patient before receiving 
neoadjuvant treatment. Plasma samples were prepared within 2 h after 
blood drawing and stored at ␀ 80 ◦C for subsequent experiments. Re-
sponders and non-responders were defined according to the pathological 
response rate after surgical resection. Specifically, patients who ach-
ieved major pathological response (MPR), also known as patients with 
residual tumor ≤ 10% after surgical resection, were enrolled in the 
responder group, whereas the others were enrolled in the non-responder 
group. 
This study was performed in accordance with the Declaration of 
Helsinki and was approved by the National Cancer Center/Cancer 
Hospital Ethics Committee (Approval number: 22/207–3409). All pa-
tients provided written informed consent. 
2.2. Isolation of EVs 
According to the method previously described, the ultracentrifuga-
tion (UC) method was optimized [19]. To remove cell debris, we 
centrifuged plasma samples at 3,000 × g for 15 min after thawing at 
37 ◦C. Next, we diluted the supernatant using a sevenfold volume of PBS, 
centrifuged at 13,000 × g for 30 min, and we removed large particles 
through a 0.22 µm filter. To pellet the EVs, a P50A72–986 rotor 
(CP100NX; Hitachi, Brea, CA, USA) was used to ultracentrifuge the su-
pernatant at 100,000 × g and 4 ◦C for 2 h. The exosome pellet was 
resuspended in 100 µl of phosphate-buffered saline (PBS) after washing 
with PBS. The size exclusion chromatography (SEC) methods were then 
used to isolate EVs [20]. Briefly, we diluted 1 ml of 0.8 µm-filtered blood 
plasma with PBS and we used Exosupur® columns (Echobiotech, China) 
to further purify it. We concentrated fractions to 200 µl by 100 kDa 
molecular weight cutoff Amicon® Ultra spin filters (Merck, Germany). 
2.3. Nanoparticle tracking analysis (NTA) 
To examine the vesicle suspensions with concentrations between 1 ×
107/ml and 1 × 109/ml, we used the ZetaView PMX 110 (Particle 
Metrix, Meerbusch, Germany) equipped with a 405 nm laser, and then 
the quantity and size of isolated particles were determined. Next, with a 
frame rate of 30 frames/sec, we took a 60-sec video and used TA 
