3
software (ZetaView 8.02.28) to analyze the particle movement. 
2.4. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) 
We place ten microliters of exosome solution on a copper mesh and 
these microliters of exosome solution were incubated at room temper-
ature for 1 min. We used sterile distilled water to wash the EVs, and then 
used uranyl acetate solution to contrast them for 1 min. Under incan-
descent light, we dried the sample for 2 min. And we used a transmission 
electron microscope (H-7650, Hitachi Ltd., Tokyo, Japan) to observe 
and photograph the copper mesh. 
2.5. Western blot analysis 
We denatured the exosome supernatant in 5 × sodium dodecyl sul-
fonate (SDS) buffer (10% SDS-polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis; 50 µg 
protein/lane) and we subjected it to western blot analysis. Rabbit 
polyclonal antibodies against CD63 (sc-5275, Santa Cruz, CA, USA), CD9 
(60232-I-Ig, Proteintech, Rosemont, IL), TSG101 (sc-13611, Santa Cruz, 
CA, USA) and calnexin (10427–2-AP, Promega, Madison, WI) were used 
in western blot analysis. 
2.6. Evaluation of PD-L1 expression 
Tumor pathology, including PD-L1 immunohistochemistry, was 
performed for all patients undergoing surgery after neoadjuvant 
immunotherapy. The expression of PD-L1 was detected by immunohis-
tochemical staining with a PD-L1 primary antibody (CST13684S), and 
the immunohistochemical staining images were analyzed in batches by 
two experienced pathology experts. The positive rate of PD-L1 in tumor 
cells was equal to or greater than 1%. 
2.7. EV RNA isolation and analyses 
According to the kit instructions, we used the miRNeasy Serum/ 
Plasma Advanced Kit (Qiagen, cat. No. 217204) to extract and purify 
total RNA from plasma EVs. We then used the RNA Nano 6000 Assay Kit 
of the Agilent Bioanalyzer 2100 System (Agilent Technologies, CA, USA) 
to evaluate RNA concentration. 
2.8. Isolation of RNA and qRT–PCR 
According to the manufacturer’s protocol, we used miRNeasy 
Serum/Plasma Advanced Kit (Qiagen, cat. No. 217204) to extract total 
RNA from EVs. Next, we used PrimeScript™ RT Reagent Kit (TAKARA, 
RR037A) to reverse transcribed the total RNA to synthesize cDNA. The 
real-time qPCR was used to evaluate the abundance of target gene 
expression with TaqMan® probe. We used two microliters of cDNA as 
the template for each PCR. The sequence of qRT-PCR primers was pro-
vided in Supplementary Table 1. 
2.9. Library preparation and sequencing 
With the use of the SMARTer Stranded Total RNA-Seq Kit V2 (Takara 
Bio USA, Inc.), we used a total amount of 250 pg-10 ng RNA per sample 
as input material for sequencing libraries, and we added index codes to 
attribute sequences to each sample. For the RNA sample preparations, 
and we used a total amount of 1 ng-500 ng RNA per sample as input 
material for small RNA libraries. We then used QIAseq miRNA Library 
Kit (Qiagen, Frederick, MD) to generate sequencing libraries, and we 
added index codes to attribute sequences to each sample. To analyze the 
quantification of miRNA expression during cDNA synthesis and PCR 
amplification, we introduced reverse transcription (RT) primers with 
unique molecular indices (UMIs). At last, we used Agilent Bioanalyzer 
2100 to assess library quality. And we sequenced the library prepara-
tions on an Illumina NovaSeq platform after cluster generation, and we 
generated paired-end reads. 
2.10. Statistical analyses 
We processed the raw data of the identified mRNAs in fastq format 
through in-house Perl scripts. In this step, we removed reads containing 
adapters, poly-N and low-quality reads to obtain the clean data. Mean-
while, we calculated the Q20, Q30, GC content and sequence duplica-
tion level of the clean data. We used TopHat2/Bowtie2 to align paired- 
end clean reads to the reference genome GRCh38. And we used Mapped 
reads for differential expression analysis and the quantification of gene 
expression levels. According to the reads mapped to the reference 
genome, we used Scripture and StringTie to assemble the transcriptome 
for lncRNAs. The Cuffcompare program from the Cufflinks package was 
used to annotate the assembled transcripts. To screen for putative 
lncRNAs, we used unknown transcripts. From putative protein-coding 
RNAs in the unknown transcripts, we combined four CPC/CNCI/ 
Pfam/CPAT approaches to sort nonprotein coding RNA candidates. We 
selected transcripts with more than two exons and lengths greater than 
200 nt as lncRNA candidates. And protein-coding genes were distin-
guished from noncoding genes with the abovementioned four ap-
proaches. Next, CIRI (CircRNA Identifier) was used to predict circRNAs. 
We annotated the circRNA-associated genes according to Swiss-Prot 
(http://www.ebi.ac.uk/swissprot/), KOG/COG (Cluster of Orthologous 
Groups of proteins), Pfam (protein family) and Nr (NCBI nonredundant 
protein sequences). We normalized the raw junction reads for all sam-
ples to the number of total mapped reads and log2 transformed. FPKMs 
of coding genes and lncRNAs were calculated with StringTie and we 
computed gene FPKMs via summing the FPKMs of transcripts in each 
gene group. The Mann–Whitney U test was conducted to compare 
differentially expressed genes, circRNAs and lncRNAs between different 
groups. P value < 0.05 and fold change > 1.5 were considered statisti-
cally significant. 
The qPCR data were normalized based on the internal reference 
genes, and the 2-ΔΔCT values were calculated as the relative quantifi-
cation. P values for the significance of differences between the qPCR 
data of the two groups of samples were calculated using the Wilcoxon 
method. We performed logistic regression signature analysis using the 
“glm” function. We constructed signatures for genes that differed 
significantly between the two group and their multigene combinations. 
3. Results 
3.1. Clinical characteristics of patients 
From December, 2020 to February, 2023, a total of 78 NSCLC pa-
tients receiving neoadjuvant immunotherapy were enrolled in our study 
and were divided into three cohorts. A total of 50 patients (64.1%) were 
older than 60 years old and 64 patients (82.1%) were male. As for the 
pathological type, 52 patients (66.7%) suffered from lung squamous cell 
carcinoma and the others (33.3%) had lung adenocarcinoma. We 
enrolled 41 patients (52.6%) who responded well to neoadjuvant 
immunotherapy, while 37 patients (47.4%) in our study did not. Positive 
PD-L1 expression was detected in 42 patients and 36 patients had PD-L1 
(-) tumors. The baseline characteristics of patients in our study were 
shown in Table 1. 
3.2. EV isolation and characterization 
As presented by the overall workflow, we performed the RNA-seq 
analysis in the exploratory cohort and qRT-PCR in the other two co-
horts to analyze the expression of exLRs (Fig. 1). We observed by 
transmission electron microscopy (TEM) multiple isolated vesicles, 
which were round, cup-shaped and had a double closed membrane 
structure (Supplementary Figure 1A). The vesicle size ranged from 70 to 
200 nm (Supplementary Figure 1B). In addition, western blot analysis 
W. Guo et al.

Pharmacological Research 196 (2023) 106921
4
was conducted and positive expression of EV markers (Tsg101, CD9 and 
CD63) was identified in vesicles isolated from NSCLC plasma species. 
And a negative control for identifying EVs (calnexin) was not detected, 
which could predict that these vesicles are predominantly EVs (Sup-
plementary Figure 1C). 
3.3. Identification of candidate exLRs in the exploratory cohort 
We collected plasma samples from 18 NSCLC patients (responder, 
n= 9 and non-responder, n= 9) before the neoadjuvant immuno-
therapy, aiming to identify specific exLRs that may predict the patients’ 
responses to ICIs and stratify patients with different risk subgroups 
before the treatment. We subjected the EV total RNAs to RNA-seq for 
their transcriptomic profiling. We have identified different types of 
RNAs in the plasma EVs, including messenger RNAs (mRNAs), long non- 
coding RNAs (lncRNAs) and circular RNAs (circRNAs). Our findings 
revealed that the EV mRNAs were the most abundant in both groups, 
while the number of EV lncRNAs was the smallest (Fig. 2A). Next, to 
identify the exLRs significantly correlated with favorable immunother-
apeutic outcomes, we have analyzed the differentially expressed exLRs 
between the responder and non-responder groups (Fig. 2B). Our findings 
revealed that top-ranked circRNAs were significantly expressed in the 
non-responder group, such as hsa-circ-0007765, and only hsa-circ- 
0001485 was highly expressed in the responder group. And most top- 
ranked mRNAs and lncRNAs were significantly expressed in the 
responder group, including PSMA4, H3C2 and MALAT1 (Fig. 2C,  
Table 2). We then conducted the PCA analysis, and found that the 
candidate exLRs could stratify patients into responder and non- 
responder groups, indicating the predictive value of these selected 
exLRs in NSCLC patients (Fig. 2D). Furthermore, we found that B cell 
receptor signaling pathway, HIF-1 signaling pathway, and oxidative 
phosphorylation pathway were mostly enriched in the plasma-derived 
EV mRNAs of the responder group (Fig. 2E). 
3.4. Construction of exLR-based predictive signature for 
immunotherapeutic outcomes 
The exLRs used in the predictive signature were selected according to 
the following criteria: [1] P< 0.05 and fold change = 1.5 for selecting 
the differentially expressed exLRs; [2] FPKM of selected exLRs > 5 in at 
least six patients; [3] Selecting 10 mRNAs, 10 circRNAs and 10 lncRNAs 
with the smallest P-values; [4] The top mRNAs and lncRNAs sorted by 
log2FC with the average expression values > 10, and circRNAs sorted by 
log2FC with average expression values > 1000 were selected. A total of 
eight candidate exLRs were selected from the exLRs highly expressed in 
the responder group, and qRT-PCR was subsequently utilized to 
compare the expression levels of these exLRs between the two groups. 
We have found that the expression levels of two mRNA (H3C2, 
P= 0.029; RPS3, P= 0.0086) and one lncRNA (MALAT1, P= 0.043) 
were significantly higher in the responder group than in the 
non-responder group (Fig. 3A). While the expression levels of the other 
five exLRs did not exhibit a significant difference between responder and 
non-responder groups (Supplementary Figure 2 A). To construct the 
predictive signature, we first evaluated the efficacy of these three exLRs 
in predicting the immunotherapeutic outcomes of patients in the 
training cohort. Compared with other two exLRs, plasma-derived EV 
H3C2 (AUC: 0.716) could predict the outcomes of neoadjuvant immu-
notherapy with higher efficacy (Fig. 3B). Then we integrated two or 
three exLRs as different signatures and found that the three-exLR 
signature had higher accuracy (AUC: 0.777) than the others (Supple-
mentary Figure 2B-C). 
Since PD-L1 was a traditional and well-accepted biomarker for ICI 
treatments, we evaluated the PD-L1 expression levels from tumor sam-
ples [21]. Our results have shown that 76.2% of patients in the 
responder group had positive PD-L1, which was higher than that of the 
non-responder group (Fig. 3C). And tissue-derived PD-L1 (AUC: 0.697) 
was less effective than plasma-derived EV H3C2 and RPS3, suggesting 
the high accuracy of our exLR-based signature (Fig. 3D). To predict 
patients’ responses to neoadjuvant immunotherapy, we constructed the 
predictive signature using different combinations of exLRs and PD-L1 
(Supplementary Figure 2D-E). The signature based on three exLRs and 
PD-L1 had the best performance (AUC: 0.892) in predicting immuno-
therapeutic outcomes (Fig. 3E-H). 
3.5. Prognostic value of the exLR-based predictive signature 
Previous studies have demonstrated that patients’ responses to ICI 
treatment were correlated with patients’ prognoses [22]. Herein, we 
analyzed the potential of our signature in predicting the prognosis of 
patients treated with neoadjuvant immunotherapy and the following 
