surgery. A total of 34 patients with follow-up data in the training cohort 
were enrolled in the analysis and the median follow-up time was 21.5 
months. Metastatic cancer was identified in nine patients of this cohort, 
and the metastatic sites included lymph nodes, lung, pleura, bone and 
adrenal gland (Fig. 4A). A meta-analysis has demonstrated that the 
combination utility of tumor mutation burden and PD-L1 could evaluate 
clinical outcomes of NSCLC patients receiving immunotherapy [23]. 
According to our results, patients with different PD-L1 expression levels 
had similar prognoses (P= 0.39), suggesting that PD-L1 expression may 
not be a reliable prognostic predictor for neoadjuvant immunotherapy 
(Fig. 4B). Intriguingly, our exLR-based signature could significantly 
predict prognoses of NSCLC patients treated with neoadjuvant immu-
notherapy (P= 0.048), further confirming the clinical feasibility of our 
predictive signature (Fig. 4C). In addition, we evaluated the prognostic 
value of three exLRs in our predictive signature, and patients in the 
high-expression group had relatively favorable clinical outcomes (Sup-
plementary Figure 2F). 
3.6. Validation of the exLR-based predictive signature 
To further evaluate the performance of the predictive signature, we 
enrolled 20 NSCLC patients as the validation cohort. Compared with PD- 
L1 (AUC: 0.596) and other two exLRs (AUC, MALAT1: 0.596; RPS3: 
0.606), we found that plasma-derived EV H3C2 (AUC: 0.727) had the 
best performance in predicting immunotherapeutic outcomes in the 
validation cohort (Fig. 4D). And our predictive signature also performed 
well in the validation cohort, suggesting the reliability and efficacy of 
the signature in predicting NSCLC patients’ responses to neoadjuvant 
immunotherapy (Fig. 4E-F). We also evaluated the accuracy of other 
predictive signatures using different combinations of exLRs and PD-L1, 
which had worse performance in predicting the outcomes than our 
signature (Supplementary Figure 3). The coefficient of each biomarker 
was presented in Fig. 4G. 
In addition, we compared the differences of the predictive signature 
in patients with different pathological subtypes. Our findings revealed 
that our predictive signature performed better for lung squamous cell 
carcinoma than for lung adenocarcinoma (Supplementary Figure 4). 
4. Discussion 
Given the widespread utility of ICIs in cancer treatment, neoadjuvant 
immunotherapy and the following surgery have been shown to be 
promising therapeutic options for a variety of malignancies [24]. 
Compared with neoadjuvant chemotherapy, ICIs used for neoadjuvant 
treatment have dramatically increased the response rate and prolonged 
patients’ survival time [9,25]. Although neoadjuvant immunotherapy 
benefits many NSCLC patients, several patients develop resistance to 
ICIs and fail to respond to immunotherapy [26]. Therefore, identifying 
NSCLC patients who will respond to ICIs is of great importance, which 
may primarily improve the efficacy of neoadjuvant immunotherapy. 
However, due to the lack of reliable biomarkers, accurately predicting 
patients’ immunotherapeutic outcomes remains an unsolved problem. 
Currently, detection of PD-L1 expression in tumor tissues is one of 
the most prevalent methods to screen suitable patients for ICI therapy 
[27]. For NSCLC patients treated with neoadjuvant immunotherapy, the 
efficacy of PD-L1 in predicting immunotherapeutic outcomes varies 
across different patients, suggesting that PD-L1 may not be the best 
biomarker for predicting responses to ICIs [28]. EVs carry important 
cargoes and could be important diagnostic and prognostic biomarkers 
[29]. Emerging evidence show that exLRs, including mRNA, circRNA 
and lncRNA, are closely involved in cancer progression and serve as 
crucial diagnostic biomarkers [30]. Zhou et al. have demonstrated that 
EV-derived miR-1468–5p could promote the formation of the immu-
nosuppressive microenvironment and subsequently facilitate tumor 
immune escape, suggesting the potential role of exLRs in predicting 
immunotherapeutic outcomes [31]. Liao et al. have found that CD160 
may be a predictive biomarker for immunotherapeutic outcomes in lung 
cancer by analysing plasma extracellular vesicle transcriptomics [32]. 
Because plasma is easily assessed among patients, our study aims to 
establish a signature based on plasma-derived exLRs, which could pre-
dict NSCLC patients’ responses to neoadjuvant immunotherapy before 
the treatment. In the present study, using plasma samples obtained from 
78 NSCLC patients in three cohorts, we conducted a large-scale analysis 
of plasma-derived exLRs, and identified non-invasive biomarkers to 
stratify patients before neoadjuvant immunotherapy. Furthermore, our 
exLR-based signature was able to predict clinical outcomes of NSCLC 
patients treated with neoadjuvant immunotherapy. 
Previous studies have evaluated the feasibility of other approaches to 
predict immunotherapeutic outcomes, including circulating tumor cells 
(CTCs), circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA) and tumor-educated blood 
platelets (TEPs). For CTCs, a recent publication has demonstrated that 
pretreatment CTCs were significantly correlated with the prognosis of 
melanoma patients treated with adjuvant immunotherapy [33]. Tam-
minga et al. have shown that CTCs were related to unfavorable responses 
to ICIs in patients with advanced NSCLC, suggesting that liquid biopsy 
could be potential strategy to predict therapeutic effects for ICI treat-
ment [34]. Although a limited number of plasma-based biomarkers have 
been reported, clinically feasible signatures for predicting responses to 
neoadjuvant immunotherapy in NSCLC patients remained to be estab-
lished. Previously, we have constructed an RNA modification writer 
signature (Writer-Score) to predict the prognosis of NSCLC patients 
receiving neoadjuvant immunotherapy [11]. However, the Writer-Score 
was based on resected samples after the surgery, and was unable to 
predict responses to ICIs and stratify patients before the treatment. Due 
to the heterogeneity of NSCLC tumor microenvironment and limited 
number of patients, the Writer-Score may be not accurate in clinical 
practice. Compared with the Writer-Score, the exLR-based signature was 
non-invasive and established using pre-treatment plasma samples ob-
tained from a larger cohort of patients, which could significantly iden-
tify responders and direct the application of therapeutic strategies for 
