According to our results, three exLRs were included in the predictive 
signature for responses and prognosis of NSCLC patients receiving 
neoadjuvant immunotherapy. A previous publication has revealed that 
RPS3 was highly expressed in osteosarcoma and could interact with 
GLI2 to facilitate tumor invasion, and RPS3 was regarded as a thera-
peutic biomarker for osteosarcoma [35]. Another study revealed that 
colorectal cancer progression can be inhibited by the degradation of 
RPS3 mediated by UBE2J1, which also inactivated the NF-κB signaling 
pathway [36]. Although RPS3 closely participated in cancer develop-
ment, the role of RPS3 in NSCLC or immunotherapy was not previously 
reported. Our study has shown that EV-derived RPS3 was positively 
correlated with a favourable response to ICI treatment in NSCLC, and the 
comprehensive mechanisms remained to be further investigated. 
Another exLR in our signature, lncRNA MALAT1, is a crucial regulator of 
cancer metastasis. For example, MALAT1 could promote lung cancer 
progression and be associated with poor prognosis, while it can suppress 
breast cancer metastasis [37,38]. According to our results, NSCLC 
patients with high expression of EV-derived MALAT1 tended to respond 
well to neoadjuvant immunotherapy, indicating the potential role of 
MALAT1 in tumor immunotherapy, which has not been demonstrated in 
previous studies. Moreover, only few studies have focused on the role of 
H3C2 in cancer. The performance of our signature significantly 
improved when we integrated PD-L1 into the predictive signature (AUC: 
0.777 vs 0.892). Extracellular vesicle PD-L1 dynamics could predict 
response to ICIs and clinical outcomes of NSCLC patients, indicating the 
potential of PD-L1 in predicting immunotherapeutic outcomes [39]. 
Although PD-L1 alone was not effective in predicting responses to 
neoadjuvant immunotherapy (AUC:0.697) in our cohort, the integration 
of PD-L1 and exLRs performed well in predicting immunotherapeutic 
outcomes of NSCLC patients. 
Despite the successful establishment and validation of our exLR- 
based signature for predicting the efficacy of neoadjuvant immuno-
therapy, several limitations still inevitably existed in our study. First, our 
study enrolled patients in a single center with a relatively small sample 
size. To become a standard predictive test before neoadjuvant 
