signature is supposed to be confirmed in future multicenter studies 
with a larger population. Moreover, plasma samples were only collected 
once before the treatment, so our study lacked the analysis of dynamic 
changes of exLR expression during the treatment, which may limit the 
predictive value of our signature. 
5. Conclusions 
In summary, based on the expression levels of plasma-derived EV 
long RNAs of 78 NSCLC patients in three cohorts, we have established 
and validated the non-invasive exLR-based signature that could stratify 
NSCLC patients responding to neoadjuvant immunotherapy from non- 
responders. The signature could also accurately predict the prognosis 
of NSCLC patients receiving neoadjuvant immunotherapy. Our results 
proved the reliability and efficacy of plasma-derived exLR-based 
signature in predicting clinical outcomes for neoadjuvant immuno-
therapy, which filled up the research gap of liquid biopsy in NSCLC 
patients. 
