Keywords: STK11; LKB1; Immune checkpoint inhibitor;
STING; IFN-I; CD8þ T-cells
Introduction
Immune checkpoint blockade (ICB) targeting pro-
grammed cell death protein-1 (PD-1) or programmed
death-ligand 1 (PD-L1) as a single agent or combined
with other anticancer therapies has changed the treat-
ment landscape of NSCLC. 1 However, heterogenous re-
sponses to ICB exist, and previously established
biomarkers such as PD-L1 expression and tumor muta-
tion burden (TMB) are not suf ﬁcient to achieve precise
efﬁcacy prediction and candidate selection. 2 This urges
the identi ﬁcation of additional factors affecting ICB
outcomes. Speciﬁc genetic alterations could shape tumor
microenvironment (TME), and thus, are being evaluated
as potential biomarkers. 3
STK11, also known as LKB1, is a tumor suppressor gene
regulating diverse cellular processes including meta-
bolism, growth, and polarity. 4 Loss-of-function STK11
mutation occurs in approximately 10% of NSCLC, with
higher frequency in lung adenocarcinoma and western
population.5 STK11 mutation was initially associated with
resistance to ICB in NSCLC. 6– 8 However, equivocal results
have been presented. Post-hoc analyses from KEYNOTE-
042 trial showed that ICB ef ﬁcacy was unaffected by
STK11 mutation. 9 In a phase 1/2 trial, ICB ef ﬁcacy
was even better among patients carrying the mutations.10
In some case reports, 11,12 patients with pathogenic
STK11 mutations could still achieve a long-term response
to ICB, despite having low PD-L1 expression and low TMB.
Moreover, the role of STK11 mutation as a predictive
or prognostic biomarker and its relative impact on
different treatment strategies is under intense debate.
This is because STK11 mutation has also been found to
negatively impact prognosis 13,14 and the effectiveness of
chemotherapy.15,16 Recently, several exploratory analyses
of phase 3 NSCLC trials suggested that ICB improved
outcomes compared with chemotherapy regardless of the
STK11 status. 9,17– 21 However, most of the trials had a
limited number of STK11 – mutated (STK11-MUT) NSCLC,
and the magnitude of treatment effect varied. A real-
world study involving over 200 STK11-MUT NSCLC
found that ICB plus chemotherapy did not improve sur-
vival compared with chemotherapy alone. 22 More
recently, we found that, in an ICB trial, patients with
STK11 mutations were speci ﬁcally excluded. 23 Collec-
tively, STK11 mutation status has limited therapeutic
consequences in daily clinical practice, which is insuf ﬁ-
cient to rule in or rule out the administration of ICB.
It has been reported that, even in the absence of so-
matic mutations, a substantial number of NSCLC tumors
could exhibit impaired STK11/LKB1 function, which
might be because of in ﬂuence of TME, hypermethylation
in STK11 promoter, or microRNA regulation. 24,25
STK11– wild-type (STK11-WT) tumors can exhibit
similar patterns of signaling networks to those of STK11-
MUT tumors. 26,27 Conversely, even pathogenic STK11-
MUT tumors can preserve an immune-activated TME,
similar to STK11-WT tumors. 11 We, therefore, hypothe-
sized that characterization of STK11 phenotype, instead
of mutation status, may better capture the biological
defect in STK11/LKB1 pathway and stratify ICB ef ﬁcacy.
In this work, we developed a transcriptomics-based
STK11 phenotype classi ﬁer, which could more accu-
rately identify patients with ICB-refractory NSCLC and
reﬂect STK11-speci ﬁc immune suppression compared
with STK11 mutation. To our knowledge, this study is
the ﬁrst to highlight the signi ﬁcance of STK11 de ﬁciency
(STK11def) transcriptomics in contributing to immuno-
therapy resistance beyond the scope of the STK11 mu-
tation. STK11 phenotypes, which serve as true indicators
of STK11 loss, should be considered as strati ﬁcation
factors in future clinical research of ICB and STK11-
directed therapies.
Materials and Methods
Study Population
We used three independent randomized controlled
trials (RCTs), including the in-house ORIENT-11 trial
(NCT03607539), the OAK (NCT02008227) and POPLAR
(NCT01903993) trials, and The Cancer Genome Atlas
(TCGA)– NSCLC cohort. The protocol was approved by
the local ethics committee for each trial, and all partici-
pants provided written informed consent. These trials
were done in accordance with the Declaration of Hel-
sinki. Methods for the three RCTs have been published
previously.28– 30
December 2023 STK11 Classi ﬁer and ICB Ef ﬁcacy in NSCLC 1715

ORIENT-11 is a double-blind, phase 3 study that
enrolled patients from 47 centers in the People ’s Re-
public of China who had previously untreated locally
advanced/metastatic nonsquamous NSCLC without
sensitizing EGFR mutation or ALK translocation. Patients
were randomized 2:1 to anti – PD-1 antibody sintilimab
or placebo plus pemetrexed and platinum every 3 weeks
for four cycles, followed by sintilimab or placebo plus
pemetrexed as maintenance therapy, until disease pro-
gression, intolerable toxicity, initiation of new treatment,
or withdrawal of consent.
Both phase 2 POPLAR and phase 3 OAK trials are
worldwide multicenter studies that enrolled patients
with NSCLC who progressed after platinum-based
chemotherapy. Patients were randomized 1:1 to anti –
PD-L1 antibody atezolizumab or docetaxel every 3
weeks until disease progression or intolerable toxicity,
as determined by the investigator.
Assessments
In-house molecular data were generated for the
ORIENT-11 trial. For the OAK and POPLAR trials, data
were formally requested from Genentech/Roche at the
European Genome-phenome Archive. 31 Tissue mutation
data were available for the OAK study, in which non-
synonymous single nucleotide variants, insertions, de-
letions, copy number alterations, and rearrangement
alterations were considered. Tissue mutation data of
POPLAR were not authorized by Genentech. The ORIENT-
11 study only prospectively performed RNA sequencing
(RNA-seq) and targeted EGFR and ALK sequencing
without obtaining enough samples for further genetic
sequencing. The PD-L1 expression level was scored as the
sum of PD-L1 – positive tumor cells as a proportion of the
total number of viable tumor cells. More details were
provided in the Supplementary Methods.
Evaluation of STK11 Phenotype
To develop an STK11-based phenotype classi ﬁer, we
ﬁrst identiﬁed a list of genes related to both STK11 mu-
tation status and LKB1 protein level in TCGA and used this
list to construct a transcriptional signature re ﬂective of
STK11def-score. We selected genes that met one of two
criteria on the basis of STK11’s role as a tumor suppressor,
as follows: (1) up-regulated in STK11-MUT tumors
whereas being negatively correlated with LKB1 (genes A);
(2) down-regulated in STK11-MUT tumors whereas being
positively correlated with LKB1 (genes B). The STK11def-
score was then calculated by the mean log 2(transcript per
million þ 0.001) expression of gene A minus that of gene
B. This approach used data from immunotherapy-naive
tumors, thus, allowing for the assessment of naturally
occurring biological deﬁciency of STK11.
When identifying candidate genes associated with
STK11 mutation, STK11 mutation was de ﬁned as those
with alterations bioinformatically annotated as selected
functional events (SFE) by Mina et al. 32 The SFE data-
base ( http://ciriellolab.org/select/select.html) contains
hundreds of recurrent copy number alterations or so-
matic mutations in TCGA that exhibit a higher proba-
bility of being driver mutations and having an impact on
protein function. A gene was called copy number SFE
when its status was deep deletion (-2) or high ampli ﬁ-
cation (2). A gene was called somatic SFE when all of the
following applied: (1) global minor allele frequency less
than 0.05%; (2) classi ﬁed as frameshift deletion and
insertion, in-frame deletions and insertions, missense,
nonsense, and splice site modi ﬁcations; (3) missense
mutations were assessed for the presence of recurrently
mutated amino acids. We analyzed 230 patients avail-
able for both SFE and mRNA expression information in
TCGA. Count data of the 27 STK11-MUT tumors (with
SFE) were compared with the 203 STK11-WT tumors
(without SFE) using the limma-voom function, and genes
with false discovery rate – adjusted p value less than 0.05
and absolute log 2(fold change) greater than 1 were
identiﬁed as differentially expressed genes (DEGs).
When identifying candidate genes associated with
LKB1 protein, we analyzed 357 patients who had both
reverse phase protein array and mRNA expression data
in TCGA. In this scenario, limma-voom ﬁtted a linear
regression model to evaluate the correlation between a
speciﬁc gene expression and LKB1 level, and genes with
false discovery rate – adjusted p value less than 0.05 were
identiﬁed as DEGs.
Next, the STK11def-score distributions were harmo-
nized across different cohorts by applying a normal
transformation followed by standardization to z-scores.
The 75th percentile of the STK11def-score was selected
as the threshold to classify tumors with STK11-de ﬁcient
(STK11-def) or STK11-pro ﬁcient (STK11-prof) pheno-
type (see Results section).
Estimation of TME Signature
Gene sets obtained from the Molecular Signatures
Database and Genomic Data Common PanImmune Data
Portal33 were summarized in Supplementary Table 1 .
Gene set variation analysis was conducted with these
gene sets using the R package GSVA. 34 The kernel was
set by the augment kcdf ¼’Gaussian’ on the basis of our
input expression being log2(transcript per million þ
0.001). Discriminant Regulon Expression Analysis
(DoRothEA)35 and Pathway RespOnsive GENes for ac-
tivity inference (PROGENy) 36 databases were used to
infer the activities of transcription factors and hallmark
pathways, respectively, and enrichment scores were
1716 Li et al Journal of Thoracic Oncology Vol. 18 No. 12

calculated by the weighted mean method on the basis of
R package decoupleR. 37
Analysis of CD8 þ T -cell Inﬁltration and
Dysfunction
We used Kassandra to perform in-silico estimation
of the CD8 þ T-cell in ﬁltration level for each sample in
three RCTs on the basis of bulk RNA-seq data. 38 Kas-
sandra is currently the most accurate and stable cellular
deconvolution algorithm. 38 T-cell dysfunction is de ﬁned
biologically as the presence of cytotoxic T-cells in ﬁl-
trating tumors but failing to control tumor growth. This
dysfunction can be in ﬂuenced by multiple genetic,
metabolic, or immunologic factors. 39 As inspired by
Jiang et al., 40 we reasoned that by evaluating the effect
of the STK11 phenotype on the bene ﬁcial association
between CD8 þ T-cell in ﬁltration and ICB survival out-
comes, we could explore the potential impact of
STK11def on CD8 þ T-cell dysfunction. This exploration
led to two possible scenarios: (1) if T-cell dysfunction is
primarily driven by PD-1/PD-L1 signaling, it is
conceivable that these cells can be successfully revital-
ized by PD-(L)1 blockade, resulting in improved ICB
outcomes (this also provides a biological rationale
for considering high CD8 þ T-cells as a positive predic-
tive biomarker for ICB); (2) conversely, if any other
escape mechanisms dominate the mechanism of T-cell
dysfunction rather than PD-1/PD-L1 signaling, then
high CD8 þ T-cells should not be predictive of PD-(L)1
inhibitor ef ﬁcacy and may even exhibit an opposite
trend.
Statistical Analyses
Categorical and continuous baseline variables were
compared by the chi-square test and Wilcoxon rank sum
test, respectively. Kaplan-Meier method was used to
estimate median survival and draw survival curves.
Overall survival (OS) and progression-free survival
(PFS) between the two groups were compared by haz-
ard ratios (HRs) and 95% con ﬁdence intervals (CIs)
using the Cox regression model, and signi ﬁcance was
tested by log-rank test. A multivariate Cox proportional
hazard model was used to assess the interaction be-
tween treatment and STK11 phenotype, adjusted for
confounding factors of PD-L1 expression, age, sex, clin-
ical stage, histology, and smoking history. A positive or
negative standardized Wald test z-value indicates that
the STK11-deﬁcient phenotype reduced or improved the
efﬁcacy of ICB versus chemotherapy, respectively. We
used Fisher ’s exact test to compare the objective
response rate (ORR) between the two groups. A two-
sided p value was assessed, and multiple comparisons
were Benjamini-Hochberg false discovery rate – adjusted.
All analyses were conducted using R version 4.2.1 (R
Core Team, Vienna, Austria).
Results
Tissue STK11 Mutation Status and T reatment
Outcomes
We identi ﬁed 1062 patients with NSCLC assessable
for both survival and RNA-seq data who were random-
ized to ICB or chemotherapy, including 171 from
ORIENT-11 (sintilimab plus chemotherapy, n ¼ 113;
chemotherapy, n ¼ 58), 699 from OAK (atezolizumab,
n ¼ 344; chemotherapy, n ¼ 355), and 192 from POP-
LAR (atezolizumab, n ¼ 95; chemotherapy, n ¼ 97)
(Supplementary Fig. 1 ). A total of 517 of 1062 patients
(48.7%) from OAK were assessable for tissue STK11
mutation status (STK11-MUT, n ¼ 106; STK11-WT, n ¼
411).
We ﬁrst evaluated the treatment effect of ICB versus
chemotherapy in STK11-MUT and -WT groups in the
OAK study, respectively. The results indicated that the
OS bene ﬁt with ICB was observed irrespective of STK11
mutation status (STK11-MUT: HR ¼ 0.65, 95% CI: 0.42 –
1.01, p ¼ 0.06; STK11-WT: HR ¼ 0.83, 95% CI: 0.66 –
1.05, p ¼ 0.1) (Supplementary Fig. 2). This aligned with a
previous report of OAK regarding blood-based STK11
mutation.41 We further observed comparable OS bene ﬁts
between patients with or without STK11 mutations
treated with ICB (HR ¼ 0.95, 95% CI: 0.67 – 1.36, p ¼ 0.8)
(Supplementary Fig. 3 ).
Development of STK11 Phenotype Classi ﬁer
Given the lack of association between STK11 mutation
and ICB bene ﬁt in our analyses and the controversial
evidence in the literature, we hypothesized that changes
in expression pro ﬁle would more accurately signify a
biologically STK11-def phenotype and affect ICB out-
comes, rather than solely relying on STK11 mutation with
minimal downstream consequences. We integrated SFE-
and protein-based approaches to identify key genes that
were associated with STK11def (see Methods section).
This led to the identiﬁcation of 10 DEGs, each of which had
higher expression in STK11-MUT tumors and exhibited a
negative correlation with LKB1 protein level ( Fig. 1A and
Supplementary Tables 2 and 3). An STK11def-score was
then calculated for each patient, de ﬁned as the average
expression of these 10 genes. STK11-MUT tumors
had signiﬁcantly higher STK11def-scores than STK11-WT
tumors in TCGA and OAK ( Fig. 1 B). Nevertheless, the
distributions were heterogeneous within each group and
overlapped between the two groups, suggesting that the
classiﬁcation on the basis of STK11 mutation status
does not fully re ﬂect the STK11def level.
