1718 Li et al Journal of Thoracic Oncology Vol. 18 No. 12

To achieve a more accurate subgrouping of the STK11
function, we sought to determine an optimal cutoff of
STK11def-score that can precisely detect STK11-MUT
subjects and at the same time re ﬂect suppression of
STK11 at the transcriptional and translational levels. Af-
ter z-score standardization, the density plot of STK11def-
score revealed high overlap across cohorts ( Fig. 1 C),
indicating the consistency of potential phenotype distri-
bution among them. We then used the MaxSpSe method42
to identify a cutoff (0.51) that maximized sensitivity and
speciﬁcity in discriminating STK11-MUT from STK11-WT
tumors in TCGA (area under the curve ¼ 0.86) (Fig. 1D).
This z-score value corresponded to the approximately
75th percentile for STK11def-score in each of the data
sets (73.9th in TCGA; 73.4th in OAK; 74.3th in POPLAR;
73.5th in ORIENT-11).
Using 75th percentile as cutoff (quartile [Q] 4, STK11-
def; Q1-Q3, STK11-prof) in TCGA, we found that
expression levels of STK11 mRNA and LKB1 protein
were signi ﬁcantly suppressed in STK11-def tumors
compared with STK11-prof tumors, and the degree of
depletion was less pronounced when analyzing STK11-
MUT versus -WT tumors ( Fig. 1 E). These observations
provided initial evidence that our classi ﬁer can more
robustly re ﬂect STK11def than STK11 mutation.
Considering that LKB1 acts as a major activator of
AMPK,4 we further assessed the following: (1) the
phosphorylation of threonine (T172) in the AMPK a
subunit, which is required for AMPK activation 4; and (2)
the activity of fatty acid metabolism, which is strongly
inhibited on AMPK activation. 4 The results revealed
signiﬁcantly lower expression of phospho-AMPK T172
and higher activity of fatty acid metabolism in STK11-def
tumors compared with STK11-prof tumors, and these
differences were less pronounced strati ﬁed by STK11
mutation status ( Fig. 1 E), thus, further con ﬁrming the
biological relevance of our classi ﬁer. As a result, for each
trial and TCGA cohort, tumors were strati ﬁed on this
basis. The main characteristics of OAK, POPLAR, and
ORIENT-11 trials were balanced between treatment
arms across STK11-def and STK11-prof groups
(Supplementary Tables 4 – 6).
STK11 Phenotype and T reatment Outcomes
In the OAK trial with the largest population, a sig-
niﬁcant improvement in OS with atezolizumab compared
with chemotherapy was observed in STK11-prof (HR ¼
0.81, 95% CI: 0.66 – 0.99, p ¼ 0.04) but not in STK11-def
tumors (HR ¼ 0.97, 95% CI: 0.69 – 1.35, p ¼ 0.9)
(Fig. 2A). PFS was numerically higher with atezolizumab
than chemotherapy in patients with STK11-prof tumors,
although not statistically signi ﬁcant (HR ¼ 0.88, 95% CI:
0.74– 1.06, p ¼ 0.17) (Fig. 2B). However, in patients with
STK11-def tumors, atezolizumab was signi ﬁcantly asso-
ciated with shorter PFS than chemotherapy (HR ¼ 1.70,
95% CI: 1.26 – 2.32, p ¼ 0.0006) ( Fig. 2 B). Patients with
STK11-def tumors treated with atezolizumab had
extremely short median OS (9.5 mo) and PFS (1.5 mo)
(Fig. 2 A and B), indicating that most of the tumors
develop primary resistance to ICB.
To validate these ﬁndings, we analyzed another RCT
with a similar design, POPLAR, which was also con-
ducted in patients who had received previous treatment.
Similar ﬁndings were observed, in which atezolizumab
was associated with signi ﬁcantly improved OS (HR ¼
0.66, 95% CI: 0.46 – 0.95, p ¼ 0.03) and numerically
higher PFS (HR ¼ 0.76, 95% CI: 0.54 – 1.08, p ¼ 0.1)
compared with chemotherapy in patients with STK11-
prof tumors, whereas those with STK11-def tumors
had a numerically lower OS (HR ¼ 1.61, 95% CI: 0.88 –
2.97, p ¼ 0.1) and PFS (HR ¼ 1.79, 95% CI: 0.98 – 3.27,
p ¼ 0.06) with atezolizumab, although not statistically
signiﬁcant ( Fig. 2 C and D). The median OS and
PFS were 6.1 and 1.4 months, respectively, for
patients with STK11-def tumors in the atezolizumab arm
(Fig. 2 C and D).
Furthermore, to evaluate whether these ﬁndings can
be generalizable to other treatments and settings, we
evaluated patients receiving ﬁrst-line chemo-
immunotherapy from the ORIENT-11 trial. Sintilimab
plus chemotherapy exhibited signi ﬁcantly prolonged OS
compared with chemotherapy in STK11-prof tumors
(HR ¼ 0.59, 95% CI: 0.37 – 0.92, p ¼ 0.02) ( Fig. 2 E). In
contrast, there was no OS bene ﬁt observed for combi-
nation therapy in STK11-def tumors (HR ¼ 1.07, 95% CI:
0.50– 2.29, p ¼ 0.9) ( Fig. 2 E). In the STK11-prof and
STK11-def groups, the median OS for combination
therapy versus chemotherapy was 25.8 versus 16.1
months and 16.1 versus 17.7 months, respectively
(Fig. 2 E). A similar pattern was observed regarding PFS,
with a median of 11.1 versus 5.2 months (HR ¼ 0.32,
95% CI: 0.20 – 0.51, p ¼ 3E-07) for STK11-prof tumors
