and that of 5.3 versus 4.6 months (HR ¼ 0.65, 95% CI:
0.33– 1.28, p ¼ 0.2) for STK11-def tumors ( Fig. 2 F).
When evaluating ICB ef ﬁcacy by STK11 phenotype,
STK11-def tumors were associated with worse OS (HR ¼
1.49, 95% CI: 1.12 – 1.96, p ¼ 0.005), PFS (HR ¼ 1.79,
95% CI: 1.39 – 2.33, p < 0.0001), and ORR (4.7% versus
19.0%, p ¼ 0.001) with atezolizumab than STK11-prof
tumors in the OAK trial ( Supplementary Fig. 4 A– C).
Similar unfavorable outcomes were observed in the
POPLAR study with atezolizumab for STK11-def tumors
in terms of OS (HR ¼ 2.38, 95% CI: 1.43 – 4.00, p ¼
0.0007), PFS (HR ¼ 2.38, 95% CI: 1.45 – 4.00, p ¼
0.0005), and ORR (0% versus 18.8%, p ¼ 0.06)
(Supplementary Fig. 4 D– F). In the ORIENT-11 trial, pa-
tients with STK11-def tumors treated with sintilimab
plus chemotherapy also had inferior OS (HR ¼ 1.79, 95%
CI: 1.06 – 3.03, p ¼ 0.03), PFS (HR ¼ 2.03, 95% CI: 1.21 –
3.40, p ¼ 0.006), and ORR (50.0% versus 72.9%, p ¼
0.04) compared with those with STK11-prof tumors
(Supplementary Fig. 4 G– I). Collectively, our analyses of
three independent RCTs suggest that STK11-def pheno-
type rather than mutation can more accurately identify
ICB-refractory patients.
STK11 phenotype was not a prognostic factor for
survival in TCGA ( Supplementary Fig. 5 ). It did not
predict survival outcomes in the chemotherapy arms,
either ( Supplementary Fig. 6 ). Moreover, the multivar-
iate Cox proportional hazard interaction test between
treatment and STK11 phenotype exhibited a strong
trend indicating that the magnitude of the adverse effect
of STK11-def phenotype was greater in ICB arm
compared with chemotherapy arm in three trials. The
Wald test z-values for OS and PFS were 1.09 ( p ¼ 0.27)
and 3.48 ( p ¼ 0.0005) in OAK, 2.9 ( p ¼ 0.004), and 2.82
(p ¼ 0.005) in POPLAR, and 1.43 ( p ¼ 0.15) and 1.46
(p ¼ 0.15) in ORIENT-11, respectively ( Supplementary
Table 7 ).
In addition, we wondered whether evaluating the
STK11 phenotype may extend its clinical relevance to
other types of cancer with relatively low frequencies of
STK11 mutations. Using the Predict.IO portal, 43 we
found that our signature can predict OS, PFS, and ORR
across several cancer types including NSCLC, melanoma,
gastric cancer, bladder cancer, and esophageal cancer
(Supplementary Fig. 7 ).
STK11 Phenotype and Tissue Mutation Status
Among patients with both assessable tissue mutation
status and RNA-seq data, 74 of 981 (7.5%) from TCGA
and 106 of 517 (20.5%) from OAK harbored STK11
mutations. Figure 3 A and Supplementary Figure 8 A
characterized several frequent mutations and STK11def-
score signature gene expression in TCGA and OAK,
respectively. As expected, STK11 mutations occurred
more frequently in STK11-def versus STK11-prof tumors
(TCGA: 22.4% versus 2.6% p ¼ 1.2E-23; OAK: 55.7%
versus 9.6%, p ¼ 1.2E-27). However, this also revealed a
degree of discordance between these two grouping
methods. First, the STK11-def group still contained a
substantial fraction of STK11-WT tumors (TCGA: 77.6%;
OAK: 44.3%). Secondly, there was a small subset in the
STK11-prof group with STK11 mutation (TCGA: 2.6%;
OAK: 9.6%).
KEAP1 mutations were enriched in the STK11-def
group (TCGA: 35.4% versus 7.6%, p ¼ 4.1E-26; OAK:
45.1% versus 10.4%, p ¼ 2.2E-17) ( Fig. 3 A and
Supplementary Fig. 8 A), which was consistent with the
evidence of synergy in downstream signaling between
STK11 and KEAP1 mutations. 26 At the time of article
preparation, a study 44 reported that KEAP1 mutation
can unexpectedly exhibit an STK11 loss phenotype
without STK11 mutations, in line with our observations.
The STK11-def group also had a higher frequency of
mutations in KRAS (30.4% versus 10.9%, p ¼ 6.2E-13)
and SMARCA4 (12.6% versus 3.8%, p ¼ 1.1E-06), but a
lower frequency of TP53 mutations (52.0% versus
71.4%, p ¼ 3.7E-08) in TCGA ( Fig. 3A), albeit information
being unavailable in OAK. The STK11-def and STK11-
prof groups exhibited comparable TMB, neoantigen
load, copy number variation burden, homologous
recombination de ﬁciency, aneuploidy, and intratumor
heterogeneity ( Supplementary Fig. 9 ).
In TCGA, co-mutations in STK11/KRAS, STK11/
KEAP1, and STK11/KRAS/KEAP1 were predominantly
found in the STK11-def group (12.2% versus 0.7%, 8.9%
versus 0.5%, and 7.3% versus 0.5%, respectively)
(Fig. 3 B and D). Similar ﬁndings were observed in the
OAK cohort, which revealed that almost all patients with
STK11/KEAP1 comutations belonged to the STK11-def
group (27.0% versus 1.3%) ( Supplementary Fig. 8 B).
These observations may explain the poor ICB outcomes
in patients with these co-existing mutations, 45– 47 which
are at least partially because of their synergistic effects
in promoting STK11def. In contrast, we noted that many
patients in the STK11-def group did not have these
doublet or triplet co-mutations in TCGA (59.3% for
STK11/KRAS-WT, 51.2% for STK11/KEAP1-WT, and
42.1% for STK11/KRAS/KEAP1-WT) ( Fig. 3 B–D) and
OAK (26.2% for STK11/KEAP1-WT) ( Supplementary
Fig. 8 B), respectively. Thus, the absence of these muta-
tions does not preclude the development of STK11def.
We next investigated whether patients in the STK11-
def group may experience inferior ICB outcomes inde-
pendently of STK11 mutation. As anticipated, in the OAK
trial, we found that patients with STK11-def tumors had
shorter OS and PFS compared with those with STK11-
prof tumors, regardless of STKI1 mutation status
(Fig. 4 A and B). Conversely, STK11 mutation has no
December 2023 STK11 Classi ﬁer and ICB Ef ﬁcacy in NSCLC 1721

adverse effect on ICB outcomes. In the tumors differen-
tially classi ﬁed by STK11 phenotype and mutation sta-
tus, we observed signi ﬁcantly worse OS (HR ¼ 2.16, 95%
CI: 1.04 – 4.50, p ¼ 0.04) and PFS (HR ¼ 2.14, 95% CI:
1.07– 4.26, p ¼ 0.03) in STK11-WT/def versus STK11-
MUT/prof tumors ( Fig. 4 A and B). Likewise, STK11-def
tumors also exhibited shorter survival to ICB than
STK11-prof tumors across both KEAP1-MUT and -WT
groups ( Fig. 4 C and D).
Evidence is inconsistent on whether the adverse
impact of STK11 mutation depends on the presence of
KRAS mutation. Whereas Skoulidis et al. 7 and Pore
et al. 48 reported that STK11 mutations confer unfavor-
able ICB outcomes regardless of KRAS status, recent
studies by Ricciuti et al. 47 and Alessi et al. 49 indicated
that this effect was restricted to KRAS-MUT tumors.
Because information on KRAS mutation was not avail-
able in the ICB cohorts, we instead used a well-validated
transcriptional signature (RAS84) 50 to assess whether
the effect of STK11def could be in ﬂuenced by RAS
activity. This approach not only provides a fair surrogate
of KRAS mutation but also enables a broader measure-
ment of RAS activation. We categorized patients into
RAS-high and RAS-low groups on the basis of the median
value of the RAS84 score in each trial and found that the
deleterious impact of STK11def on ICB outcomes
generally persisted irrespective of KRAS status (OAK:
Fig. 4 E and F; POPLAR and ORIENT-11: Supplementary
Fig. 10 ).
STK11 Phenotype and PD-L1 Expression
PD-L1 information was available for 51.6% (361 of
699), 80.7% (155 of 192), and 100% (171 of 171) of
patients in OAK, POPLAR, and ORIENT-11 respectively.
Tumors with PD-L1 greater than or equal to 1% were
enriched in the STK11-prof group in each trial
(Supplementary Fig. 11 ), a ﬁnding in line with previous
reports. 7,8,26 The worse outcomes in STK11-def versus
STK11-prof tumors maintained across both PD-L1
