STK11 Phenotype and STING/Type I Interferon
Signaling
To gain insight into the molecular underpinnings of
the differential effects of STK11-de ﬁcient phenotype and
mutation on ICB outcomes, we explored their differences
in immune-related consequences in TCGA and OAK.
We ﬁrst focused speci ﬁcally on the cyclic GMP-AMP
synthase (cGAS) – STING signaling pathway, which is a
pivotal upstream mediator of T-cell immunosurveillance
and ICB therapeutic activity. 51,52 It has been found
that this pathway is suppressed in the STK11-MUT
NSCLC model. 53 We found that STK11-def tumors were
associated with signi ﬁcantly lower enrichment of cGAS-
STING pathway compared with STK11-prof tumors
(Supplementary Fig. 14 ). Notably, the differential
depletion in STING pathway was lessened in tumors
stratiﬁed by STK11 mutation status ( Fig. 5A). We further
determined that the depletion of cGAS-STING pathway
in STK11-def tumors was independent of STK11 muta-
tion status ( Fig. 5 B). The discordant samples (STK11-
WT/def and STK11-MUT/prof) exhibited similar STING
activity compared with the remaining tumors in the
STK11-def or -prof group, and STK11-WT/def tumors
even exhibit lower enrichment than STK11-MUT/prof
tumors ( Fig. 5 B). These ﬁndings tie into the survival
analysis by STK11 phenotype and mutation status
(Fig. 4 A and B).
The cGAS-STING pathway is the major inducer of type
I interferon (IFN-I). 54 We subsequently interrogated
whether STK11-deﬁcient phenotype and mutation might
have different effects on IFN-I response. To this end, we
collected eight IFN-I – related signatures from the litera-
ture, among which little commonality was observed
(Supplementary Fig. 15 ). All selected signatures exhibi-
ted signi ﬁcantly inverse correlations with STK11def-
score across TCGA and OAK, both when they were
evaluated in all patients or within the STK11-MUT and -WT
subgroups. ( Fig. 5 C). A signi ﬁcant decrease in all IFN-I
signatures was observed in STK11-def tumors
compared with STK11-prof tumors ( Supplementary
Fig. 16 ). Similar to the cGAS-STING pathway, we
discovered a lessened difference in the depletion of
IFN-I signaling in STK11-MUT versus -WT tumors
(Fig. 5 D). This notion was further supported by the
DoRothEA and PROGENy analyses, which revealed that
IFN-I– related and other immunost imulatory transcrip-
tional factors (STAT1, NFKB1, RELA, NFATC1, TCF7,
RELA) and pathways (JAK-STAT, NFkB, tumor necrosis
factor-a)w e r es i g n iﬁcantly deactivated in STK11-def
tumors, whereas these effects mostly vanished when
analyzing STK11 mutation ( Fig. 5 E). In addition, acti-
vation of NRF2 (NFE2L2) was found as an important
feature in STK11-MUT tumors, 25,26 b u th e r ew ef o u n d
NRF2 was signi ﬁcantly activated in STK11-def tumors
but not in STK11-MUT tumors ( Fig. 5 E). Collectively,
although the above biological effects of STK11 muta-
tions on TME have been exhibited in experimental
settings, mutation status alone might be insuf ﬁcient to
reﬂect these changes in patients, which may be better
captured by STK11-def phenotype.
STK11 Phenotype and CD8 þ T -Cells
Finally, given the refractoriness of STK11-def tumors
to PD-[L]1 inhibitor, we were interested in exploring
whether STK11def can constitutively confer CD8 þ T-cell
dysfunction independently of PD-1/PD-L1 signaling. As
inspired by Jiang et al., 40 we de ﬁned CD8 þ T-cells as
being in an ICB-irreversible dysfunctional state when
they were unable to predict ICB outcomes positively.
Using the median value as a threshold for grouping in
each trial, we initially found a positive association be-
tween CD8 þ T-cell levels and survival with ICB
(Supplementary Fig. 17 ). Surprisingly, when strati ﬁed by
STK11 phenotype, high CD8 þ T-cells were associated
with improved OS only in patients with STK11-prof
tumors; whereas in patients with STK11-def tumors,
an opposite trend toward worse OS in the high CD8 þ T-
cell group was observed, and the results were consistent
among three trials ( Fig. 6 ). Similar phenomenon was
observed in terms of PFS ( Supplementary Fig. 18 ).
The negative association between CD8 þ T-cells and
survival to ICB was not evident in STK11-MUT tumors
(Supplementary Fig. 19 ).
Notably, although low in ﬁltration of CD8 þ T-cells has
been suggested as a resistance mechanism to ICB among
STK11-MUT tumors, 8 here, we did not observe a
consistent difference in CD8 þ T-cell in ﬁltration grouped
or PD-L1 expression ( G-H). The cutoff of the RAS84 score was the median value across the total patients in each trial. Mu-
