patterns between STK11, KRAS, and KEAP1. 7,18,26,45– 47,49
Despite these efforts, current guidelines of NSCLC have
not speci ﬁed any treatment recommendation on STK11
mutation,1,56 and ICB remains the preferred treatment
for STK11-MUT patients. This gap between clinical prac-
tice and clinical research is probably because of incon-
sistent ﬁndings regarding whether STK11 mutation could
indeed curtail ICB response and the limited understanding
of how STK11 mutations affect treatment effects for ICB
versus chemotherapy.
Here, we, for the ﬁrst time, proposed a method of
ICB-stratiﬁcation on the basis of STK11 phenotype
beyond the mutation status, which could foster the
identiﬁcation of patients with NSCLC unresponsive to
ICB. We developed a transcriptomics-based classi ﬁer
reﬂective of STK11def and discovered that STK11-def
tumors had consistently reduced ICB ef ﬁcacy and ben-
eﬁts from ICB versus chemotherapy across three inde-
pendent high-quality RCTs. On the contrary, these effects
were not observed in STK11-MUT tumors, which might
