be owing to a failure to detect approximately 45% to
70% of STK11-def cases on the basis of STK11 mutation.
We evaluated the tumors with differential phenotype/
mutation classi ﬁcation and found worse ICB outcomes
among tumors reclassi ﬁed by the de ﬁcient phenotype.
Our ﬁndings, therefore, cautioned against the use of
STK11 mutation to limit enrollment or stratify patients
in clinical trials. The potential explanation for the better
performance of STK11-de ﬁcient phenotype is that a
transcriptional approach could offer a more compre-
hensive characterization of drivers affecting STK11
function, including both somatic mutations and TME-
derived factors. 24
Introducing the STK11 phenotype has important
therapeutic implications. First, previous reports 9,17– 21
found variable impacts of STK11 mutation on treatment
effect for ICB versus chemotherapy. We illustrated that the
advantage of ICB with or without chemotherapy was
diminished in STK11-def tumors in both the ﬁrst- and
subsequent-line settings. Patients with STK11-def NSCLC,
regardless of the presence or absence of STK11 mutation,
may be considered for receiving more potent combination
therapies (e.g., anti – PD-(L)1 þ anti-CTLA4 þ chemo-
therapy) or entering clinical trials of novel strategies.
Particular attention should be paid to patients with co-
existing mutations, as our analyses indicated that they
were more likely to harbor STK11-def tumors.
In addition, we found some STK11-MUT tumors
within the STK11-prof group, accounting for 2% to 10%
of cases. Some STK11-MUT patients may harbor a
nonpathogenic mutation or variant of unknown signi ﬁ-
cance, possibly explaining the presence of some STK11-
MUT/prof cases. Unfortunately, our immunotherapy
data lack the mutation annotation information to vali-
date this hypothesis. In contrast, this study highlights
another explanation that, in these patients, the negative
impact of STK11 mutation may be counteracted by other
nonmutational factors. This is evident by the observation
that the STK11-def phenotype also affected ICB out-
comes and exhibited immune suppression among
STK11-WT patients, meaning that a mutation (irre-
spective of whether or not is pathogenic) is not neces-
sarily required for defective STK11 function. In line with
this hypothesis, the phase 3 KEYNOTE-042 trial 9 and
two other case reports 11,12 did not observe a negative
impact of STK11 mutation on ICB ef ﬁcacy, even though
they only focused on pathogenic mutations. The pheno-
type-based evaluation may, in the future, negate the
need for complete annotation of each STK11 mutation
(e.g., variant of unknown signi ﬁcance). Anyway, the
presence of STK11 mutations should not discourage the
administration of ICB for all STK11-MUT patients as our
ﬁndings suggested that patients with STK11-MUT/prof
can bene ﬁt from ICB.
The joint and separate effects between STK11, KRAS,
and KEAP1 mutations on ICB ef ﬁcacy have not been fully
disentangled, as they were often evaluated concurrently
because of the high co-occurrence frequency. 14 Sole
KRAS mutation seems to be neutral and its positive or
negative impact on TME and ICB outcomes depends on
the co-occurring mutations with TP53 or STK11,
respectively.8,18,26,57 In a recently-published study, 44
tumors with KEAP1 mutation alone can exhibit func-
tional STK11 loss. These ﬁndings point to a central role
of STK11def as a bona ﬁde mediator of ICB resistance.
We further discovered that the deleterious impact of
STK11-deﬁcient phenotype on ICB outcomes was unaf-
fected by KRAS (estimated by RAS84 score) or KEAP1
status. More importantly, even when assessing the co-
mutation status of STK11/KRAS/KEAP1, approximately
25% to 60% of STK11-def cases were still missed,
emphasizing the importance of integrating RNA-seq and
DNA sequencing in future clinical practice.
Della Corte et al. 58 have reported that, although most
STK11-MUT NSCLC tumors exhibit low STING activities,
a subset exhibits high STING activities. Our results sug-
gest that this may be attributed to the heterogeneous
STK11def within the STK11-MUT tumors. Furthermore,
we reﬁned these theories indicating that the relationship
between STK11 and STING can be extended beyond the
mutation status. Della Corte et al. 58 also revealed that
chemotherapy failed to enhance STING in STK11/KRAS-
MUT NSCLC cell lines, which might explain the lack of
efﬁcacy of ICB plus chemotherapy in STK11-def popu-
lation in our analysis. Strategies to reengage the cGAS-
STING pathway and IFN-I signaling have recently been
found to effectively reverse ICB resistance in the STK11-
MUT model and are undergoing clinical testing. 59,60
Considering STK11-def phenotype rather than mutation
can robustly re ﬂect deactivation of STING and IFN-I
signaling, we recommend that future trials investi-
gating the combination of STING agonist and ICB should
recruit patients on the basis of the presence of STK11-
def phenotype instead of its mutation, which may
broaden responsive candidates (i.e., approximately 25%
of NSCLC), maximize treatment effect, and spare the
added toxicity from increasing STING activity.
Although CD8 þ T-cell in ﬁltration level was not
consistently affected by STK11def across cohorts in our
analyses, we did observe that the positive predictive
value of CD8 þ T-cells for PD-(L)1 inhibition was
consistently abrogated or even reversed in STK11-def
tumors. These results suggest an association between
STK11def and PD-1/PD-L1-independent CD8 þ T-cell
dysfunction. Previous investigations on STK11-MUT tu-
mors primarily focused on their immune-inert TME. 7,8,26
Here we present a new perspective that future immu-
notherapy strategies need to not only convert a “cold”
December 2023 STK11 Classi ﬁer and ICB Ef ﬁcacy in NSCLC 1727

tumor into a “hot” one but also to revert the STK11def –
driven T-cell dysfunction. On the basis of the results, we
postulated two potential mechanisms for this oncogenic-
induced dysfunction. First, the transcriptional factor
TCF7 was deactivated in STK11-def tumors. TCF7 encodes
the marker (TCF-1) of progenitor exhausted CD8þ T-cells,
which are responsible for mediating long-term responses
to ICB.61,62 Second, the STING activity, which is also vital
for T-cell priming and activation, 52 was depleted in
STK11-def tumors. Our signature genes may provide
valuable information for further mechanistic explorations.
This study has several limitations, including its
exploratory retrospective design, the moderate sample
size in each trial, and the unavailability of mutation
(POPLAR and ORIENT-11 studies) and PD-L1 data for a
fraction of patients. Several subgroup analyses did not
yield signi ﬁcant results, which may be attributed to the
small number of patients. Nevertheless, given that (to
best of our knowledge) our study is the ﬁrst to report
the discordances between STK11 mutation status and
phenotype in the context of immunotherapy, our ﬁnd-
ings will contribute to the improvement of knowledge
regarding the clinical topic of STK11 mutation. Our
methodology’s strength lies in the utilization of three
high-quality independent RCTs with homogeneous pop-
ulations within each trial and chemotherapy as a control.
More importantly, the consistent ﬁndings across these
three trials suggest potential translatability across
different treatments and settings. We derived a
narrowed-down set of 10 genes potentially available for
targeted RNA-seq panel design, which will offer the
possibility to validate the impact of STK11 phenotype in
prospective ICB trials.
In summary, STK11 phenotype rather than mutation
status can accurately distinguish patients who are re-
fractory to ICB and re ﬂect immune suppression. Imple-
menting such a phenotype-based classi ﬁcation may help
reﬁne the strati ﬁcation algorithm for future clinical
research, regardless of STK11/KRAS/KEAP1 status and
PD-L1 expression. The ﬁndings that STK11-def NSCLC
represents depleted STING/IFN-I activities and CD8 þ T-
cell dysfunction, create new opportunities for next-
generation immunotherapies harnessing these responses.
