Keywords: MUC1-C; NSCLC; EGFR; Osimertinib; Resistance
Introduction
Treatment of NSCLC has been advanced by the
identiﬁcation of driver gene mutations and the devel-
opment of targeted agents; however, the 5-year survival
rate for patients with NSCLC remains at approximately
15%. Approximately 15% to 20% of NSCLCs harbor
activating EGFR alterations, which predominantly
include exon 19 deletions and L858R mutations. 1,2 The
ﬁrst-generation reversible EGFR tyrosine kinase in-
hibitors (TKIs) erlotinib and ge ﬁtinib were effective
against NSCLCs with activating EGFR alterations but
were limited in approximately half of tumors by relapses
within 10 to 16 months of therapy through the acquisi-
tion of resistance associated with a secondary T790M
mutation.1,2 Irreversible EGFR TKIs, such as afatinib, that
covalently interact with the EGFR TKI domain at Cys-797
were found to have limited activity in blocking EGFR
T790M-driven pathways in preclinical models 3 and in
patients with NSCLC who had failed erlotinib or ge ﬁti-
nib.4 Osimertinib was developed as a third-generation,
irreversible EGFR TKI that more potently inhibits EGFR
with TKI-sensitizing and T790M resistance mutations. 5
Efﬁcacy of osimertinib supported use of this agent as
the preferred ﬁrst-line option for patients with meta-
static NSCLC harboring EGFR-sensitizing mutations and
as adjuvant treatment in the setting of resectable EGFR
mutation-positive disease. 6,7 Despite this activity, resis-
tance to osimertinib invariably develops, often in asso-
ciation with the EGFR C797S mutation and MET-based
mechanisms, such as MET gene ampli ﬁcation.8–10
Notably, 40% to 50% of patients with NSCLC who
progress on osimertinib have no identi ﬁable resistance
mutation, indicating that nongenetic pathways may be
responsible.9,11
The MUC1 gene is overexpressed in NSCLCs and
confers a poor prognosis by mechanisms that remain
unclear.12,13 MUC1 evolved in mammals to provide pro-
tection of barrier tissues, such as the respiratory tract
epithelia, from loss of homeostasis encountered by
inﬂammation and damage. 14,15 MUC1 encodes N-termi-
nal (MUC1-N) and C-terminal (MUC1-C) subunits that
form a heterodimeric complex at the apical borders of
polarized epithelial cells. 14,15 The extracellular MUC1-N
subunit contributes to a protective mucous barrier. 16
In response to loss of homeostasis, MUC1-N is released
from the cell surface, and the transmembrane MUC1-C
subunit transduces in ﬂammatory, proliferative, and
remodeling signals associated with wound healing. 14,15
These MUC1-C –induced signaling pathways are, in
principle, reversible with restitution of homeostasis;
however, prolonged activation of MUC1-C in association
with chronic in ﬂammation promotes progression to
cancer.14,15 In co-opting processes of tissue repair,
MUC1-C induces lineage plasticity, the epithelial-
mesenchymal transition (EMT), and epigenetic reprog-
ramming in driving the cancer stem cell (CSC) state. 14,15
CSCs are of importance in conferring anticancer drug
resistance15,17; however, there is no known relationship
between MUC1-C and osimertinib resistance. The pre-
sent studies reveal that NSCLC cells with acquired osi-
mertinib resistance driven by diverse mechanisms are
dependent on MUC1-C for activation of the EGFR
pathway, clonogenic survival, and self-renewal capacity.
These ﬁndings and the demonstration that high MUC1
mRNA and MUC1-C protein levels confer a poor prog-
nosis identify MUC1-C as a potential target for the
treatment of osimertinib-resistant NSCLCs.
Materials and Methods
Cell Culture
H1975/EGFR (L858R/T790M) cells (American Type
Culture Collection) were cultured in RPMI 1640 medium
(Thermo Fisher Scienti ﬁc, Waltham, MA) supplemented
with 10% fetal bovine serum (GEMINI Bio-Products,
West Sacramento, CA). MGH700-2D, MGH170-1D #2
(MET ampli ﬁcation) and MGH121 Res#2/EGFR (C797S/
T790M/19del) cells 18–21 were cultured in RPMI 1640
medium supplemented with 10% fetal bovine serum and
5% glutamine. Cells were treated with osimertinib
(Selleck Chemicals, Houston, TX), GO-203, 14,15 bemcen-
tinib (Selleck Chemicals), capmatinib (Selleck Chem-
icals), and TNO155 (Selleck Chemicals). Authentication
of cells was performed every 3 to 4 months by short
tandem repeat analysis. Cells were monitored for my-
coplasma contamination every 3 to 4 months using the
MycoAlert Mycoplasma Detection Kit (Lonza, Rockland,
MA). Cells were maintained for 3 months when per-
forming experiments.
Gene Silencing
MUC1shRNA (MISSION shRNA TRCN0000122938;
Sigma, St. Louis, MO) and a control scrambled shRNA
(CshRNA; Sigma) were inserted into the pLKO-tet-puro
vector (Plasmid #21915; Addgene, Cambridge, MA) as
described.22 The MUC1shRNA#2 (MISSION shRNA
TRCN0000430218) was expanded in HEK293T cells as
described.23 Flag-tagged MUC1-C/CD was inserted into
the empty control pLenti CMV Blast DEST(706-1) vector
(Plasmid #17451; Addgene). 24 Vector-transduced cells
March 2024 MUC1-C Confers Osimertinib Resistance in NSCLC 435

were selected for growth in 1 to 2 mg/mL puromycin.
Cells were treated with 0.1% DMSO as the vehicle con-
trol or 500 ng/mL doxycycline (DOX; Millipore Sigma).
Immunoblot Analysis
Total lysates prepared from noncon ﬂuent cells were
subjected to immunoblot analysis using anti –MUC1-C
(HM-1630-P1ABX, 1:1000 dilution; Thermo Fisher Sci-
entiﬁc, Waltham, MA), anti –b-actin (A5441, 1:5000
dilution; Sigma-Aldrich, Burlington, MA), anti –phospho
(p)-EGFR (2234, 1:1000 dilution; Cell Signaling Tech-
nology [CST], Danvers, MA), anti-EGFR (2232, 1:1000
dilution; CST), anti –p-ERK (4377, 1:1000 dilution; CST),
anti-ERK (4695, 1:1000 dilution; CST), anti –p-AKT
(4058, 1:1000 dilution; CST), anti-AKT (9272, 1:1000
dilution; CST), anti–p-AXL (AF2228, 1:1000 dilution; Bio-
Techne Corporation, Minneapolis, MN), anti-AXL (8661,
1:1000 dilution; CST), anti –p-SHP2 (3751, 1:1000 dilu-
tion; CST), anti-SHP2 (3752, 1:1000 dilution; CST), anti-
TWIST1 (ab50887, 1:1000 dilution; Abcam, Waltham,
MA), anti-ZEB1 (3396, 1:1000 dilution; CST), anti –N-
cadherin (13116S, 1:1000 dilution; CST), anti –E-cad-
herin (3195, 1:1000 dilution; CST), and anti-vimentin
(5741, 1:1000 dilution; CST).
Cell Proliferation and Drug Sensitivity Assays
Cells were seeded at a density of 6000 cells per well
in 96-well plates. After 24 hours, the cells were treated
with different concentrations of osimertinib. Cell
viability and proliferation were assessed using the Ala-
mar Blue assay (Thermo Fisher Scienti ﬁc). The IC 50
values were determined by nonlinear regression of the
dose–response data using Prism 9.0 (SCR_002798,
GraphPad Software). Fluorescence intensity (560 nm
excitation/590 nm emission) was measured in sextu-
plicate wells.
Colony Formation Assays
Cells were seeded in 24-well plates for 24 hours and
then treated with (1) 0.1% DMSO or 500 ng/mL DOX, (2)
phosphate-buffered saline (PBS) or GO-203, (3) 0.1%
DMSO or osimertinib (Selleck Chemicals), or (4) 0.1%
DMSO or TNO155 (Selleck Chemicals). After 7 to 14 days,
the cells were stained with 0.5% crystal violet (Lab-
Chem, Zelienople, PA) in 25% methanol. Colony forma-
tion was quanti ﬁed at 590 nm using a
spectrophotometer and normalized to DMSO treatment.
Detection of Phospho-RTKs
Cell lysates were assayed using a membrane-based
antibody array for determining the phosphorylation of
human RTKs according to the manufacturer ’s in-
structions (Proteome Pro ﬁler Human Phospho-RTK
Array Kit, catalog #ARY001B; R&D Systems, Minneap-
olis, MN).
Analysis of Chromatin-Bound MUC1-C Protein
Chromatin extraction kit (ab117152, Abcam) was
used to isolate chromatin-bound proteins according to
the manufacturer ’s instructions.
Isobologram Analysis
Cells (5 /C2 103) were seeded per well in 96-well
plates (Thermo Fisher Scienti ﬁc) and incubated for 24
hours. The cells were then left untreated or treated with
0.15 to 3 mM GO-203 in the presence of varying con-
centrations of osimertinib for 72 hours. Cell viability was
assessed by Alamar Blue staining and used to calculate
the IC 50 values of osimertinib at each concentration of
GO-203 according to the method described for deter-
mining drug interactions by isobologram analysis. 25,26
The IC 50s of osimertinib and GO-203 were plotted to
identify an additive line using the following formula:
x
a þ y
b ¼ 1ða ¼ IC50of GO /C0 203; b ¼ IC50of osimertinib Þ
Horizontal and vertical coordinates were calculated
as the dose of each drug when used in combination to
inhibit growth by 50%. If the coordinates are located
below or above the additive line, the interaction is
determined to be synergistic or antagonistic,
respectfully.
T umorsphere Formation Assays
Cells (2 –6 /C2 103) were seeded per well in 6-well
ultralow attachment culture plates (Corning Life Sci-
ences, Tewksbury, MA) in Dulbecco ’s modi ﬁed eagle
medium/F12 50/50 medium (Corning Life Sciences)
with 20 ng/mL EGF (Millipore Sigma, Boston, MA), 20
ng/mL bFGF (Millipore Sigma), and 1% B27 supplement
(Gibco, Grand Island, NY). In certain studies, the cells
were treated with (1) 0.1% DMSO or 500 ng/mL DOX,
(2) PBS or GO-203, and (3) 0.1% DMSO or osimertinib
(Selleck Chemicals). Tumorspheres were counted under
an inverted microscope in triplicate wells.
Mouse T umor Model Studies
Six-week-old female nude mice (The Jackson Labo-
ratory) were injected subcutaneously into the ﬂank with
5 /C2 106 tumor cells in 100 mL of a 1:1 solution of me-
dium and Matrigel (BD Biosciences). When the mean
tumor volume reached 100 to 150 mm 3, the mice were
pair-matched into groups of six mice each. Mice were
treated intraperitoneally each day with PBS or GO-203 at
a dose of 12 mg/gm body weight in the absence and
436 Haratake et al Journal of Thoracic Oncology Vol. 19 No. 3

presence of oral osimertinib (MedChemExpress, #HY-
15772A) administration at a dose of 10 mg/kg/d.
Tumor measurements and body weights were
recorded twice per week. Tumor lysates were prepared
using the T-PER tissue protein extraction reagent
(#78510, Thermo Fisher Scienti ﬁc). The resource equa-
tion method was used for determining the minimum
number of mice to achieve signi ﬁcance.27 These studies
were conducted in accordance with the ethical regula-
tions required for approval by the Dana-Farber Cancer
Institute Animal Care and Use Committee under protocol
#03-029.
Analysis of EGFR-Mutant NSCLC Patient Data
Sets
Clinical and mRNA expression data from patients
with EGFR-mutant NSCLC were downloaded from the
The Cancer Genome Atlas (TCGA) database ( https://
portal.gdc.cancer.gov) and dbGAP with accession num-
ber phs002001.v1.p1 (13). The mRNA expression data
(FPKM, raw count) was normalized with quantile
normalization. Overall survival (OS) was estimated using
the Kaplan-Meier method, and survival curves were
compared using the log-rank test. OS was de ﬁned as the
time from the initial diagnosis or osimertinib adminis-
tration to death from any cause. Data analyses were
performed using the JMP Pro 16 software program (SAS
Institute, Cary, NC).
Immunohistochemistry
We retrospectively evaluated patients who under-
went surgical resection of EGFR-mutant NSCLC at the
Department of Surgery and Science, Graduate School of
Medical Sciences, Kyushu University, from May 2007 to
March 2016. These specimens were subjected to
immunohistochemical analysis with an anti –MUC1-C
rabbit monoclonal antibody (16564, 1:1000 dilution;
CST, heat-induced epitope retrieval, pH 6.0) and anti –
programmed death-ligand 1 (PD-L1) rabbit monoclonal
antibody (clone SP142, dilution 1:100; Spring Biosci-
ence; Ventana, Tucson, AZ). Immunostaining for MUC1-C
was evaluated in the membranes of the tumor cells with
the Allred scoring system. The determinations were
performed independently by three investigators (N.H.,
A.H., and K.T.). If the independent assessments did not
agree, the slides were reviewed together by all three
investigators until they reached a consensus. The
consensus judgments were adopted as the results. The
cutoff value of the Allred score was set at median (six),
and a positive score for the expression of MUC1-C pro-
tein was deﬁned as an Allred score of more than or equal
to six points. Human placenta sections were used as
positive controls for MUC1-C. The method for detecting
PD-L1 was detailed as described. 28 Cutoff values were
set at 1% as described. 28,29 All pathologic materials
available for tumor classi ﬁcation were reviewed by
expert pathologists using the standard WHO classi ﬁca-
tions. The disease status was staged according to the
TNM staging system (Union for International Cancer
Control version 7).
Statistical Analysis
Each experiment was performed at least three times.
Unpaired two-tailed Student ’s t tests were used to assess
differences between the mean ± SD of two groups. p
values were considered signi ﬁcant at p less than 0.05.
GraphPad Prism9 was used for all statistical analyses.
Asterisks represent * p less than or equal to 0.05, ** p less
than or equal to 0.01, *** p less than or equal to 0.001,
and **** p less than or equal to 0.0001 with con ﬁdence
interval (CI) equals to 95%.
Data Availability
The data generated in this study are included in the
main article and supplementary information. Raw data
are available on request without restriction from the
corresponding author. Clinical and mRNA expression
data from patients with EGFR-mutant NSCLC in this
study are publicly available in TCGA database ( https://
portal.gdc.cancer.gov) and dbGAP with accession num-
ber phs002001.v1.p1.
Results
MUC1-C Contributes to the H1975 Osimertinib
DTP Cell Phenotype
To determine whether MUC1-C plays a role in NSCLC
cell resistance, we treated H1975/EGFR (L858R/
T790M) cells with 1 mM osimertinib for 9 days to select
for the DTP population ( Fig. 1A). Using this approach, the
selected H1975-DTP cells exhibited an osimertinib
IC50 ¼ 2.8 mM as compared with an IC 50 ¼ 0.05 mM for
parental H1975 cells ( Fig. 1A). Further analysis of H1975
cells treated with osimertinib for 1 day and 9 days
(H1975-DTP cells) revealed increases in MUC1-C
expression in association with suppression of p-EGFR,
p-ERK, and p-AKT ( Fig. 1B). Silencing MUC1-C in H1975-
DTP cells further down-regulated p-EGFR, p-ERK, and p-
AKT levels ( Fig. 1 C). Silencing MUC1-C also inhibited
H1975-DTP cell proliferation ( Supplementary Fig. 1 A)
and colony formation ( Fig. 1D). The MUC1-C cytoplasmic
domain (MUC1-C/CD) contains (1) a CQC sequence that
is required for MUC1-C homodimerization and function
and (2) binding sites for activation of the PI3K /AKT
and GRB2 /ERK pathways ( Fig. 1 E).14,15 Treatment of
H1975-DTP cells with GO-203, an inhibitor of the MUC1-
C/CD CQC motif that blocks MUC1-C function
