Dependence on MUC1-C for Activation of the
EGFR Pathway in Stable Osimertinib-Resistant
H1975-OR Cells
To extend the involvement of MUC1-C in H1975-DTP
cells, we exposed H1975 cells to increasing concentra-
tions of osimertinib from 0.01 to 2 mM in 12 weeks
(Fig. 2 A). The stable H1975 osimertinib-resistant
(H1975-OR) cells exhibited an IC 50 ¼ 5.6 mMa s
compared with 0.05 mM for parental H1975 cells
(Fig. 2 A). Using a p-RTK detection kit, we identi ﬁed p-
EGFR in H1975 cells that was down-regulated in H1975-
OR cells ( Supplementary Fig. 2 A). Direct analysis by
immunoblotting con ﬁrmed these results and further
identiﬁed phosphorylation of MET ( Supplementary
Fig. 2 B). Treatment of H1975-OR cells with 1 mM osi-
mertinib decreased p-EGFR but had no apparent effect
on p-ERK and p-AKT levels ( Fig. 2 B). In addition, treat-
ment of H1975-OR cells with the MET inhibitor capma-
tinib decreased phosphorylation of MET but had little if
any effect on osimertinib sensitivity or p-AKT and p-ERK
levels ( Supplementary Fig. 2 C and D). By contrast,
silencing MUC1-C suppressed p-EGFR, p-ERK, p-AKT,
and p-MET ( Fig. 2 C and Supplementary Fig. 2 E). Similar
results were obtained with GO-203 treatment ( Fig. 2 D
and Supplementary Fig. 2 E), indicating that, in addition
to regulating EGFR, MUC1-C contributes activation of the
ERK and AKT pathways. MUC1-C is expressed as a 25-
kDa glycosylated transmembrane protein that interacts
with EGFR through galectin-3 bridges and thereby pro-
motes p-EGFR activation. 30,31 MUC1-C is also expressed
as an unglycosylated 17-kDa protein ( Fig. 2 D), which is
devoid of the capacity to establish galectin-3/EGFR
complexes at the cell membrane. 31 The MUC1-C 17
kDa protein localizes to chromatin as 17 kDa monomers
and 34 kDa homodimers, which were detectable at
increased levels in the chromatin fraction of H1975-OR
versus H1975 cells ( Fig. 2 E). In addition, targeting
MUC1-C in H1975-OR cells with silencing ( Fig. 2 F, left)
and GO-203 treatment ( Fig. 2 F, right) suppressed the
expression of MUC1-C in chromatin. Silencing MUC1-C in
H1975-OR cells also decreased their capacity for forming
colonies ( Fig. 2 G). Similar results were obtained when
H1975-OR cells were treated with GO-203 ( Fig. 2 H), in
support of their dependence on MUC1-C for activation of
the EGFR pathway and clonogenicity.
MUC1-C Confers Osimertinib Resistance in
NSCLC Cells Expressing the EMT Phenotype
Induction of EMT has been associated with osi-
mertinib resistance. 9,32 Consistent with an EMT pheno-
type, the H1975-OR cells acquired a more mesenchymal
morphology ( Fig. 3 A, left) coincident with (1) up-
regulation of ZEB1, TWIST1, vimentin, and N-cadherin
and (2) down-regulation of E-cadherin ( Fig. 3 A, right).
MUC1-C drives EMT in TNBC cells by inducing the ZEB1
and TWIST1 EMT-TFs. 14 Here, we found that targeting
MUC1-C in H1975-OR cells with silencing
(Supplementary Fig. 3 A) and GO-203 treatment ( Fig. 3B)
suppresses ZEB1, TWIST1, and N-cadherin but has little
if any effect on E-cadherin and vimentin expression. We
also found that targeting MUC1-C in H1975-OR cells
down-regulates AXL ( Fig. 3 B and Supplementary
Fig. 3 A), which drives EMT and has been linked to osi-
mertinib resistance. 32,33 Isobologram analysis further
revealed that treatment of H1975-OR cells with GO-203
and osimertinib results in a synergistic effect ( Fig. 3 C
and Supplementary Fig. 3 B), indicating that MUC1-C
contributes to EMT-associated osimertinib resistance.
To extend these ﬁndings, we studied osimertinib-
resistant MGH700-2D cells that, similar to H1975-OR
cells, express an EMT phenotype and the MUC1-C 25
