results were obtained from analysis of GO-203 –treated
MGH700-2D cells; that is, suppression of (1) p-EGFR, p-
ERK, and p-AKT ( Fig. 3 E) and (2) colony formation
(Fig. 3 F). Targeting MUC1-C in MGH700-2D cells further
revealed down-regulation of ZEB1, TWIST1, and AXL but
not E-cadherin and vimentin ( Supplementary Fig. 3D and
E). Consistent with previous reports, N-cadherin was not
detectable in MGH700-2D cells. 18 In concert with these
results, we found that GO-203 and osimertinib exhibit
synergistic activity against MGH700-2D cells ( Fig. 3G and
Supplementary Fig. 3 F). In assessing the potential
involvement of AXL, we found that, similar to EGFR, 30
MUC1-C forms a complex with AXL ( Supplementary
Fig. 3 G). Treatment of H1975-OR and MGH700-2D cells
with the AXL inhibitor bemcentinib suppressed p-AXL,
ZEB1, and TWIST1 levels, but it had no apparent effect
on expression of vimentin and cadherin proteins
(Supplementary Fig. 3H and I). Notably, bemcentinib also
had little effect on (1) p-EGFR, p-ERK, and p-AKT levels
(Supplementary Fig. 3 H and I) and (2) sensitivity to
osimertinib ( Supplementary Fig. 3 J and K). These ﬁnd-
ings collectively indicated that MUC1-C activates the
EGFR pathway and confers osimertinib resistance in
H1975-OR and MGH700-2D cells by an AXL-independent
mechanism.
MUC1-C Promotes Osimertinib Resistance in
MET -Ampliﬁed and EGFR (C797S)-Mutated
NSCLC Cells
MET gene ampli ﬁcation promotes osimertinib resis-
tance by EGFR-independent activation of the ERK and
AKT pathways. 9 The EGFR C797S mutation is associated
with resistance to frontline and second-line osimertinib
treatment, making it the second most frequent mecha-
nism after MET ampliﬁcation.34,35 In investigating
whether MUC1-C plays a role in osimertinib resistance of
NSCLC cells with these two major osimertinib resistance
mechanisms, we studied (1) MGH170-1D #2 cells, which
have MET ampliﬁcation and are resistant to osimertinib
(IC50 ¼ 2.1 mM) ( Supplementary Fig. 4 A),19 and (2)
MGH121 Res#2 cells that have the acquired EGFR
T790M/C797S compound mutation and are also resis-
tant to osimertinib (IC 50 ¼ 4.6 mM) ( Supplementary
Fig. 4 B).20 MGH170-1D #2 and MGH121 Res#2 cells
express the MUC1-C 25/17 kDa proteins as detected in
total lysates ( Fig. 4 A and B) and the 17-kDa form in the
chromatin fraction ( Supplementary Fig. 4 C). Silencing
MUC1-C in MGH170-1D #2 cells resulted in down-
regulation of p-MET, p-EGFR, p-ERK, and p-AKT
(Fig. 4 A). Similarly, MUC1-C silencing in MGH121 Res#2
cells also down-regulated p-EGFR, p-ERK, and p-AKT
(Fig. 4 B). Moreover, we found that targeting MUC1-C
with GO-203 down-regulates (1) p-MET, p-EGFR, p-
ERK, and p-AKT in MGH170-1D #2 ( Fig. 4 C) and (2) p-
EGFR, p-ERK, and p-AKT in MGH121 Res#2 ( Fig. 4 D)
cells. Consistent with these observations, colony forma-
tion of MGH170-1D #2 and MGH121 Res#2 cells was
suppressed by GO-203 treatment ( Fig. 4 E and F). Iso-
bologram analysis of MGH170-1D #2 and MGH121
Res#2 cells further revealed that GO-203 and osimerti-
nib are highly synergistic in inhibiting survival ( Fig. 4 G
and H and Supplementary Fig. 4 D and E). Unlike
MGH700-2D cells, MGH170-1D #2 cells express p-SHP2
(Supplementary Fig. 4 F), which activates p-ERK and
promotes osimertinib resistance 32,36–38 MUC1-C forms
complexes with SHP2 in colorectal cancer cells and in-
duces p-SHP2 activation. 26 MUC1-C/SHP2 complexes
were similarly detected in MGH170-1D #2 cells ( Fig. 4I).
We also found that MGH121 Res#2 cells express p-SHP2
and that targeting MUC1-C in MGH170-1D #2 cells and
MGH121 Res#2 cells with silencing and GO-203 treat-
ment inhibits p-SHP2 activation ( Fig. 4 H and
Supplementary Fig. 4 G). In support of these results,
treatment of MGH170-1D #2 cells and MGH121 Res#2
cells with the SHP2 inhibitor TN0155 also down-
regulated p-SHP2, p-ERK, and p-AKT ( Supplementary
Fig. 4 H and I). These ﬁndings in NSCLC cells with MET
ampliﬁcation and the EGFR (T790M/C797S) compound
mutation reveal dependence on MUC1-C for (1) activa-
tion of the p-MET, p-EGFR, and p-SHP2 pathways and (2)
osimertinib resistance.
