MUC1 Associates With Poor Clinical Outcomes in
Patients With EGFR-Mutant NSCLCs
In determining whether our ﬁndings extend to EGFR-
mutant NSCLC tumors, we ﬁrst analyzed the TCGA
database ( https://portal.gdc.cancer.gov) by identifying
patients with MUC1-high versus -low expressing EGFR-
mutant NSCLCs. Kaplan-Meier survival analyses
revealed that patients with NSCLCs expressing high
MUC1 levels have a signi ﬁcantly shorter OS after diag-
nosis in comparison to those with low MUC1 levels
(Fig. 6 A). Information regarding EGFR TKI treatment is
not available in the TCGA data set. We therefore
analyzed RNA-seq data from a cohort of patients with
EGFR-mutant NSCLCs obtained before osimertinib
treatment.10 Patients with NSCLCs expressing high
MUC1 levels exhibited a shorter OS from initiation of
osimertinib than those with low MUC1 expression (16.6
versus 22.4 mo) ( Fig. 6 B). Previous studies of MUC1
protein expression in NSCLC have been limited to anal-
ysis of the MUC1-N subunit that is shed from the cancer
cell membrane.39 Notably, there are no reports of MUC1-
C expression in NSCLCs. We therefore analyzed expres-
sion of MUC1-C by immunohistochemistry in surgically
resected EGFR-mutant NSCLCs from 136 patients
(Fig. 6 C). Characteristics of the patients and the associ-
ation between the MUC1-C protein expression and clin-
icopathologic factors are listed in Supplementary
Table 1 . Among the 136 EGFR-mutant NSCLCs, 82
(60.3%) exhibited high MUC1-C protein expression
levels as determined by the Allred score. High MUC1-C
expression was signi ﬁcantly associated with an
advanced pathologic stage (II –III, p ¼ 0.026), pleural
invasion (pl, p ¼ 0.032), and lymphatic vessel invasion
(ly, p ¼ 0.040) ( Supplementary Table 1 ). In addition,
consistent with a previous report, 40 PD-L1 expression
was detected at a higher frequency in MUC1-C (high)
tumors (21; 25.6%) than in MUC1-C (low) tumors (8;
14.8%). Survival analyses by the Kaplan-Meier method
revealed that patients with high MUC1-C levels in their
tumors tend to have a shorter recurrence-free survival
(p ¼ 0.0015, hazard ratio: 4.17 [95% CI, 1.60 –10.82])
(Fig. 6 D) and a shorter OS after surgery ( p ¼ 0.10, haz-
ard ratio: 2.49 [95% CI, 0.70 –8.87]) ( Fig. 6 E). In multi-
variate analyses, advanced-stage disease ( p < 0.001), the
presence of pleural invasion ( p ¼ 0.0034), and high
MUC1-C expression ( p ¼ 0.0470) were identi ﬁed as in-
dependent prognostic factors for progression-free sur-
vival ( Supplementary Table 2 ). These ﬁndings indicated
that patients with EGFR-mutant NSCLCs expressing high
MUC1 mRNA and MUC1-C protein levels experience poor
clinical outcomes.
Discussion
Mechanisms of acquired osimertinib resistance are
strikingly pleotropic, 9,10 challenging the development of
subsequent therapeutic strategies to circumvent the
resistant phenotypes. MUC1-C interacts with EGFR and
other RTKs at the cell membrane, where it contributes to
their activation and downstream signaling ( Fig. 6 F).15
The present work has identi ﬁed MUC1-C as a common
determinant that drives distinct pathways identi ﬁed in
NSCLCs resistant to osimertinib. Previous studies had
revealed that MUC1-C confers resistance of PC9/EGFR
(delE746_A750/T790M) NSCLC cells to ﬁrst-generation
EGFR TKIs. 30 Nevertheless, the basis for this observa-
tion was unclear and was not extended with the subse-
quent development of more effective agents, such as
osimertinib. Resistance to osimertinib can emerge when
a subset of PC9 cells, recognized as cycling and non-
cycling DTP cells, survive treatment. 41 We found that
MUC1-C is up-regulated in H1975 osimertinib DTP cells
and is necessary for activation of EGFR, ERK, and AKT
signaling. The H1975-DTP cells were also dependent on
MUC1-C for proliferation and clonogenicity. These ﬁnd-
ings supported involvement of MUC1-C in the cycling
DTP population, which has been characterized by in-
duction of NRF2 and the antioxidant stress response. 41
