where it interacts with transcription factors, such as
NRF2, and effectors of chromatin remodeling ( Fig. 6E). In
this way, MUC1-C interacts with BAF and PBAF in
driving the CSC state, DNA damage resistance, and im-
mune evasion.15,22,42,43 Further studies will therefore be
needed to determine whether MUC1-C dependence of
DTP cells is conferred by initiating changes in global
chromatin accessibility that on prolonged drug exposure
become established in promoting a memory response of
resistance.15,44
The ﬁndings with H1975-DTP cells were extended to
H1975-OR cells to assess potential MUC1-C –induced
transitions from DTP populations to acquired osimerti-
nib resistance. As found in H1975-DTP cells, studies in
H1975-OR cells revealed that MUC1-C is necessary for
phosphorylation of EGFR, ERK, and AKT and for colony
formation ( Fig. 6 F). EMT has been linked to the
osimertinib-resistant phenotype. 9,32 MUC1-C induces
ZEB1 expression and binds to ZEB1 in activating EMT. 45
MUC1-C also induces TWIST1 and forms a complex with
TWIST1 in driving the EMT phenotype. 46 Consistent with
this involvement in EMT, targeting MUC1-C in H1975-OR
cells decreased ZEB1 and TWIST1 expression. We also
found that MUC1-C drives AXL expression in H1975-OR
cells (Fig. 6F). How MUC1-C regulates AXL in NSCLC cells
is not clear; nonetheless, AXL induces EMT and has been
linked to osimertinib resistance, 32,33 in support of a
MUC1-C–induced pathway that activates AXL and EMT
(Fig. 6 F). These ﬁndings of MUC1-C involvement in
H1975-OR cells may not be representative of NSCLC cells
that acquire osimertinib resistance in patients. We
therefore studied MGH700-2D cells which were isolated
from a patient with acquired osimertinib resistance and
express the EMT phenotype. 18 MGH700-2D cells were
also dependent on MUC1-C for (1) activation of EGFR,
ERK, and AKT, (2) induction of ZEB1, TWIST1, and AXL,
(3) proliferation, and (4) colony formation. Moreover,
targeting MUC1-C in H1975-OR and MGH700-2D cells
reversed their resistance to osimertinib.
MET gene ampli ﬁcation is a common mechanism of
osimertinib resistance that has been associated with
EGFR-independent activation of p-ERK and p-AKT. 9
Studies in MET-ampliﬁed MGH170-1D #2 cells revealed
that MUC1-C is necessary for activating MET and EGFR,
including the ERK and AKT pathways. MUC1-C forms
complexes with MET and EGFR that promote their acti-
vation at the cell membrane ( Fig. 6 F).15,30 In addition,
MUC1-C acts as a node for integrating RTK-mediated
activation of downstream effectors, including ERK and
AKT, through interactions with the MUC1-C cytoplasmic
domain ( Fig. 1 E).15 SHP2 also forms a complex with the
MUC1-C/CD and is dependent on MUC1-C for SHP2
activation in colorectal cancer cells 26 and as found here
in MGH170-1D #2 cells. These ﬁndings were of partic-
ular interest in that p-SHP2 activates ERK and contrib-
utes to osimertinib resistance. 32,36–38 By extension, we
found that MUC1-C –driven activation of SHP2 and ERK
in MET-ampliﬁed MGH170-1D #2 cells is shared in
osimertinib-resistant MGH121 Res#2/EGFR (T790M/
C797S) cells. The EGFR (C797S) mutation is of impor-
tance in that it confers cross-resistance to other irre-
versible TKIs that bind to the Cys-797 residue in the
EGFR ATP-binding site. 8,34 In MGH170-1D #2 and
MGH121 Res#2 cells, MUC1-C was necessary for EGFR,
ERK, AKT, and SHP2 activation ( Fig. 6 F). Targeting p-
SHP2 with TNO155 in these cells was associated with
increases in osimertinib sensitivity, in support of
involvement of the MUC1-C /p-SHP2 pathway. These
ﬁndings collectively indicated that MUC1-C drives ac-
quired osimertinib resistance by activating (1) the
EGFR/ERK/AKT pathway, (2) EMT in H1975-OR and
MGH700-2D cells, and (3) SHP2 in MGH170-1D #2 and
MGH121 Res#2 cells ( Fig. 6 F).
Intrapatient tumor heterogeneity and co-occurrence
of multiple resistance mechanisms constitute a major
challenge to establish an effective treatment for NSCLCs
with acquired osimertinib resistance. 47–49 In addition,
combination trials have been largely unsuccessful. In the
SAVANNAH phase II trial, osimertinib plus savolitinib
was found to have an overall response rate of 49% in
patients with high levels of MET overexpression or
ampliﬁcation.50 A recent update has revealed that this
combination is associated with the emergence of MET,
EGFR, or KRAS alterations as mechanisms of osimertinib
resistance.51 The present results reveal that, in addition
to suppressing EGFR signaling, targeting MUC1-C in
NSCLCs derived from patients with acquired osimertinib
resistance induces loss of self-renewal capacity. These
ﬁndings and the demonstration of up-regulation of
MUC1-C expression in EGFR-mutant NSCLCs support the
