immune responses
Karthikeyan Sundaram a , Venkataraman Prabhu b,*
a Department of Herbal Pharmacology and Environmental Sustainability, Chettinad Hospital and Research Institute, Chettinad Academy of Research and Education, 
Kelambakkam, Chennai, Tamilnadu, India
b Division of Medical Research, SRM Medical College Hospital and Research Centre, Kattankulathur, Chennai, Tamilnadu, India
A R T I C L E I N F O
Keywords:
Lipid nanoparticles 
mRNA
Infectious diseases 
Drug carriers 
Immune response
A B S T R A C T
Lipid-formulated liposomes have been utilized for the transport of various biological substances, including 
mRNA molecules. Basically, large lipids play a crucial role in the structure and function of cell membranes. In 
protein synthesis, mRNA plays a crucial role in expressing the proteins. In this context, this natural process 
enables gene editing, protein replacement therapy, cancer immunotherapy, cellular reprogramming, and vac-
cinations. A range of biomaterials is utilized for mRNA transport, including polymers, lipid-based nanoparticles, 
and protein-mRNA complexes. Thus, the review aims to analyse the mRNA-lipid nanoparticles assigned with 
gene editing and the mechanisms to enhance immune responses to infectious diseases. A nonviral lipid nano-
particle approach for CRISPR-Cas9 gene editing has achieved up to 80% gene editing in vivo and 98% in vitro 
across various cancer cell types. MEVPCS-mRNA LNPs, as a preventative HIV vaccine, elicit comparable and 
highly specific immune responses via CD8+ T cells. Th1-type CD4+ T cell responses are notable in mice 
immunized with LNP-mRNA, indicating a substantial impact on Mycobacterium tuberculosis. Variations in pH, 
ionic strength, and freezing rate are essential for enhancing the lyophilization of mRNA lipid nanoparticles. The 
polydispersity index has risen from 0.1 to 0.3, and the LNP particle size in the Vibrating Membrane Nebulizer has 
increased from an average of 85 nm to 300 nm, suggesting notable structural degradation due to elevated shear 
stresses. The Laminar Fluid Ejection Device enhanced the stability of lipid nanoparticles. This enhances the 
immune response and effectively manages harmful microbes.
1. Introduction
Protein synthesis converts messenger RNA (mRNA) molecules into 
their corresponding proteins. This intrinsic mechanism facilitates 
several biological and therapeutic uses, including vaccinations, cancer 
immunotherapy, protein replacement therapy, cellular reprogramming, 
and genome editing. 1,2 Especially, mRNA therapeutics can prevent and 
treat various diseases. mRNA functions in the cell cytosol, avoiding the 
risk of genome mutagenesis associated with plasmid DNA. Furthermore, 
in vitro transcription (IVT) technology has simplified the management 
of mRNA stability and immunogenicity. 3 In the drug delivery system, 
polymers, protein–mRNA complexes, lipid-based nanoparticles, and 
other biomaterials can transport mRNA. Primarily, the development of 
SARS-CoV-2 has raised global concerns about immunisations. Flexible 
and fast mRNA immunisations are sought; to generate strong immune 
responses and long-lasting protection, mRNA vaccines require rapid
mRNA delivery to cells and high antigen expression. Thus, lipid nano-
particles (LNPs) must maximize their potential. Additionally, cell 
membranes contain mostly lipids, which are necessary for membrane 
integrity and biological processes. It is well known that lipid-formulated 
liposomes may carry mRNA molecules and other payloads. 1,4,5 In LNP-
mRNA vaccine development, mRNA component stability is crucial; 
however, the novel vaccine platform's primary drawback is cold-chain 
storage. Traditional vaccinations may be stored at 2 ◦ –8 ◦ C in a refrig-
erator for 6 months, whereas mRNA-LNP vaccines must be frozen, 
making vaccine distribution in poor infrastructure challenging. Thus, 
lyophilisation, or freeze-drying, helps to preserve the vaccines by 
eliminating water. 6 Notably, the LNPs prepared in PBS had less than 
80% mRNA encapsulation after lyophilization with maltose as a lyo-
protectant, but 5 mM Tris buffer maintained their encapsulation effi-
ciency compared to fresh LNPs. 7 Also, innovative continuous spin 
lyophilization or batch freeze-drying has shown the efficacy of
