lyophilising mRNA-LNP formulations with a reduction-sensitive ionis-
able lipid and 20% (w/v) sucrose. Importantly, mRNA vaccine formu-
lation and lyophilisation must be improved. 8 Currently, mRNA-LNP-
based vaccine candidates represent a rapidly advancing platform for 
addressing various diseases; however, adherence to the precise technical 
processes is vital. Thus, the review aims to analyse the mRNA-lipid 
nanoparticles assigned with gene editing and the mechanisms to 
enhance immune responses to infectious diseases.
2. LNP-mRNA roles in gene editing
Chemically specified RNA-delivery vehicles like LNPs have been 
studied for non-viral delivery. Recent genome editing studies show that 
LNPs can transport Cas9 mRNA and single-guide (sgRNA) into mice 
livers. Changes in LNP's size and lipid composition allowed it to trans-
port Cas9 and sgRNA to mice's liver, lung, muscle, and brain. Pre-
existing Cas9 protein immunity is a concern. The LNP approach for 
delivering the RNA-based CRISPR-Cas9 system into extensive skeletal 
muscle has not demonstrated efficacy due to the multitude of injections, 
immunological reactivity, in vivo safety concerns, and injection routes. 9 
Notably, the nonviral lipid nanoparticle method for CRISPR-Cas9 gene 
editing achieves 80% gene editing in vivo and 98% in vitro across 
several cancer cell types. In brief, the study used the following cell lines, 
such as murine glioblastoma multiforme (GBM), human serous ovarian 
adenocarcinomas Ovcar8 (OV8) and NCI-ADR (NAR), human colon 
carcinoma HCT116, and human lung adenocarcinoma A549 were tested 
for gene disruption utilizing L8-cLNPs. Incubation with sgGFP-cLNPs (1 
μg/mL; 7 nM of total RNA) resulted in GFP fluorescence in 3–18% of 
cancer cells. L8-cLNPs perform precise gene editing in vitro with little 
toxicity in several cancer cell lines. Unlike sgGFP, cLNPs damaged the 
polo-like kinase1 (PLK1) gene in GBM 005 and OV8, resulting in 84% 
and 91% genomic editing, respectively. 10
Notably, CRISPR-LNPs (cLNPs) targeting PLK1 reduced tumour 
growth and enhanced survival in two aggressive mouse cancer models 
following single or multiple doses. Single delivery of sgPLK1-cLNPs to 
the tumour bed of a murine GBM model resulted in almost 70% PLK1 
gene editing, activated caspase 3 labelling, 50% longer median 
longevity, and 30% higher overall survival of 005 GBM-bearing mice. 
Many cancer treatments require daily administration, which lowers 
patient quality of life and increases toxicity and expense. Increase 
therapeutic efficacy, minimise treatment frequency, and circumvent 
dosage constraints for cancer medications by permanently altering 
tumour survival genes with CRISPR-Cas9. EGFR-targeted cLNPs can 
alter PLK1 genes by 80% in one injection, since dysregulation of PLK1 
has been associated with the prognosis of many malignancies. PLK1 is 
crucial for the regulation of the cell cycle and the development of cancer. 
In mice with high-grade ovarian cancer and malignant ascites, two 
intraperitoneal EGFR-targeted sgPLK1-cLNPs decreased tumour growth 
and saved over 80%. 10,11 Besides this, the Cas9-RNP exhibits partial 
association with the exterior surface, whereas mRNA Cas9 demonstrates 
more effective integration within the core of LNPs. The distribution of 
anions across the surface of the Cas9-RNP is uneven, despite the complex 
possessing a net negative charge. 12
In the context of LNP-mRNA's clinical relevance in cancer therapy, a 
recent study evaluated how prenatal and developmental biology can be 
used to overcome biological barriers to mRNA-LNP distribution to he-
matopoietic stem cells (HSCs). LNP-mediated gene delivery is easier in 
the liver, where fetal hematopoiesis and HSCs develop, than in the 
postnatal bone marrow niche. A high-performing LNP from the similar 
study was used to test mRNA-LNP delivery to the fetal liver in gesta-
tion. 13 However, the process of developing the novel method on LNPs is 
essential for yielding high-efficiency outcomes; thus, the dynamic imi-
noboronate ester linkage was used in a recent study to create H₂O₂- 
responsive, cationic ionizable lipidoids in a one-pot, three-component 
synthetic process. This technology allows for the production of a large 
library of dynamically covalent LNPs, which can then be screened for the
transport of mRNA using high-throughput techniques. 14 In addition, a 
recent study used inexpensive and commonly available chemicals to 
synthesize lipids with improved architectural diversity across lipid 
lengths quickly. Lipids with terminal branching groups allowed the 
study of linear and non-linear relationships between ionic liquid struc-
ture and function in lipid nanoparticle distribution. For liver protein and 
mRNA transport, LNP-mediated transfection is enhanced by Branched 
ENdosomal Disruptor (BEND) ionizable lipids. When used with Cas9 
mRNA and the RNP complex for gene editing cargo and T cell trans-
portation, BEND LNPs improved T cell transfection and liver editing. 15
3. mRNA-LNP roles in induced immune responses
Lipid nanoparticles formulations often contain ionisable, helper, 
cholesterol, and PEGylated lipids. Most studies focus on ionisable lipids' 
role in RNA packing and endosomal escape. 16 In this context, the MC3 
DOPE generated more cytokines in THP-1 cells than C12–200 DOPE. 
1,2-dioleoyl-sn-glycero-3-phosphoethanolamine (DOPE) formulations 
may increase THP-1 cytokine production due to increased LNP absorp-
tion. DOPE's fusogenic properties as an assist lipid promote cargo 
movement and elevate expression and cytokine levels, relative to other 
formulations at the same dosage. 17 According to a recent immunological 
study, the viral protease cleavage sites (VPCS) vaccine is the inaugural 
HIV vaccination strategy that elicits narrowly targeted immune re-
sponses to VPCSs, akin to those observed in HIV-highly exposed sero-
negative individuals (HESNs). In order to suppress the HIV infection, 
MEVPCS-mRNA lipid nanoparticles trigger specific immunological re-
sponses mediated by CD8+ T cells. Antigen-specific CD8+ central 
memory T cells generated by vaccination are enhanced by MEVPCS-
mRNA LNPs. Epitopes from MEVPCS polypeptide units that are 
thought to represent HLA class I allele epitopes can trigger immuno-
logical responses mediated by MHC-I in CD8+ T cells in murine 
models. 18 Recent research demonstrated that cationic lipid (ALC0307), 
cholesterol, phosphatidylcholine, and PEG-lipid (APCP) LNP-
encapsulated nucleoside-modified mRNA may generate substantial 
epitope-specific CD8 T cell responses that become memory cells 
following intradermal and intramuscular vaccination. Thus, the inter-
action was adequate for the type I interferon receptor (IFN-I), also 
known as IFNAR. Subcutaneous delivery of mRNA vaccines encapsu-
lated in 1,2-dioleoyl-3-trimethylammonium-propane (DOTAP) / DOPE 
may be required to inhibit type I interferon (IFN-I) signaling and activate 
CD8 T cell responses. The necessity of this procedure is assessed in this 
study. The effectiveness of an mRNA-LNP vaccine in stimulating CD8 T 
cell responses in patients with functioning IFN-I responses may depend 
on the composition of LNP. 19,20
Recent clinical studies have notably demonstrated the effectiveness 
of mRNA-LNP vaccines; research on therapeutic HIV vaccines is 
noticeably lacking, although preventive mRNA HIV vaccines have 
advanced significantly. Prophylactic immunological responses prioritize 
HIV-envelope-specific neutralizing antibodies, but therapeutic immu-
nization necessitates significant Gag-specific polyfunctional CD8 + T cell 
responses. 21,22 Despite their importance, gut lymphoid tissues are 
commonly overlooked in preclinical and analytical research. There isn't 
much preclinical research that has examined mRNA-LNP formulations in 
connection to Gag-specific polyfunctional CD8+ T cell responses in mice 
or macaques. 22–24
Significantly, mRNA vaccines using lipid nanoparticles induce hu-
moral (antibody) and cell-mediated (T cell) protection. To improve 
antigen-specific cellular immune responses (Table 1), LNP may increase 
antigen absorption and endosome escape. 25 Recent studies classified 
CpG Oligodeoxynucleotides (CPG ODNs) as Type A (D), B (K), and C. 
Full-phosphorothioate CpG-B ODN CPG 1018 has one or more CpG di-
nucleotides. Limited ability exists to boost IFN-α production, despite 
stimulating B cells and increasing TLR9-dependent NF-κB activation. In 
contrast, CpG 2395, a Class C, exhibited Class A and B capabilities, 
prompting pDC to generate IFN-α and activate B cells. Rabies virus
K. Sundaram and V. Prabhu Journal of Genetic Engineering and Biotechnology 24 (2026) 100719 
2

(RABV) protection and humoral and cellular immune responses are 
better in mice with LNP-encapsulated CpG 1018 than with LNP and CpG 
2395. 26 Besides that, the SP-HBsAg mRNA-LNP vaccination in murine 
models demonstrated enhanced protection against hepatitis B virus 
(HBV) infections by eliciting a robust antigen-specific Th1-cell-biased 
immune response. Serum hepatitis B surface antigen (HBsAg) concen-
trations in chronically infected mice were decreased by both vaccines. 
Mice with chronic hepatitis B (CHB) who were administered both mRNA 
vaccine candidates had the most robust CD8+ T-cell-mediated response 
and the lowest plasma levels of HBsAg. 27
On the other hand, in haematological malignancies, natural killer 
(NK) therapies are most beneficial. Solid tumour therapy is difficult. 
Cytokines released by malignant cells in the immunosuppressive tumour 
microenvironment (TME) inhibit NK cell recruitment and function. 28,29 
TGFβ may reduce NK cell metabolism, cytokine activity, and degranu-
lation; mRNA therapy is possible. NK cell transfection is difficult and 
restricted. According to recent research, LNPs transfected 75% more 
than electroporation (57%). The two injection methods were safe and
achieved 99.5% cell viability. Enhancing LNPs fivefold enhanced geo-
metric mean fluorescence intensity (gMFI) compared to electroporation, 
indicating a major change in enhanced green fluorescent protein (eGFP) 
expression. Using fluorescent microscopy, NK cells transfected with 
eGFP were examined. LNPs outperformed electroporation in signal 
strength and protein levels. 30
4. mRNA-LNP formulation on infectious diseases
The mRNA-LNP plays a substantial function in infectious diseases; 
however, these lipid nanoparticles enhanced immune responses more 
than direct application of mRNA-based substances. In correlation with 
immune responses with mRNA-LNP modules, a recent study found that 
mRNACV2, a LNP-mRNA vaccine encoding the CysVac2 protein, was 
administered to mice to assess its efficacy. In chronic infection, the 
immunodominant Ag85B and the sulfur absorption route overexpressor 
CysD lead to the production of CysVac2. 31,32 Notably, protection against 
aerosol infection by Mycobacterium tuberculosis (MTB) was linked to 
increased numbers of vaccine-specific polyfunctional CD4 + T cells that 
produced IFNγ, IL-2, and TNF in mice given mRNACV2. In animal 
models, polyfunctional CD4+ T cells offer protection against MTB; 
however, this might not be the case in human beings. The study found 
that, in comparison to other investigations, there were more cytokine-
producing CD4+ T cells and antigen-specific Ag85B:I-Ab+ T cells after 
CysVac2 adjuvanted vaccination. Mice immunized with LNP-mRNA 
exhibit robust Th1-type CD4+ T cell responses. Thus, the protection 
against MTB aerosol infection was associated with an increase in 
vaccine-specific polyfunctional CD4+ T cells that produced IFNγ, IL-2, 
and TNF in mice that received mRNACV2. Although polyfunctional 
CD4+ T cells protect animals against MTB, this may not always be the 
case in humans. 31,33
Recently, the research evaluated the pharmacokinetics and func-
tional activity of three monoclonal antibodies (mAbs) as mRNA/LNP in 
mice infected with S. aureus. Single chain variable fragment-fragment 
crystallizable (ScFv-Fc) and immunoglobulin G (IgG) formats were 
used to evaluate the mAb trio to avoid HC and LC mispairing. The Cmax 
values of scFv-Fc and IgG forms varied from 10 to 22 μg/mL and 15 to 
90 μg/mL, respectively, 24 h after delivery. Both forms were effective, 
and the triple mAb protected against S. aureus dermonecrosis. Non-
human primates (NHPs) receiving triple IgG via mRNA had blood 
levels ranging from 2.9 to 13.7 μg/mL, with a terminal half-life of 11.8 to 
15.4 days. 34 In correlation with the above findings, to combat the Gram-
positive bacterium S. aureus, the study initially amalgamated three 
mAbs that target the surface-expressed clumping factor A (ClfA) with 
four bi-component leukotoxins (LukSF, LukED, HlgAB, and HlgCB) and 
alpha toxin (AT). 35
The mRNA-LNP GAS vaccine module Combo #5 comprises five 
highly conserved and protective detoxified group A streptococcus (GAS) 
antigens: trigger factor (TF), arginine deiminase (ADI), streptococcal 
C5a peptidase (SCPA), streptolysin O (SLO), IL-8, and protease. This 
study investigated the effects of Combo#5-TM mRNA-LNP on the hu-
moral and cellular immune responses in mice, focusing on CD4 + and 
CD8 + T cell responses, germinal center B cells, and memory B cells, all of 
which are critical for sustained antibody responses. The Combo#5-TM 
vaccination resulted in an increase in Tfh cells, which are critical for 
the proliferation of GC B cells and the production of antibodies. In GAS, 
long-lasting antibody responses need humoral and cellular responses 
from mice, including CD4+ and CD8+ T cells, GC B cells, and MBCs, 
which Combo#5-TM mRNA-LNP boosts. 36
LNP-delivered mRNA from an acute HBV donor's bone marrow re-
stores chronic HBV through core antigen-specific T cells. In contrast to 
standard therapy relapses, HBV resolvers had higher core- and pol-
specific responses and maintained virological control. 37,38 Adeno-
associated virus (AAV)-HBV mice following multi-antigen vaccination 
showed a substantial decrease in core-specific CD4+ and CD8+ re-
sponses compared to naive animals. In AAV-HBV models, vaccination
