In lipids, there are various types such as cationic or ionizable lipids; 
zwitterionic phospholipids; neutral co-lipids; mostly cholesterol; and 
PEGylated lipids in nanoparticle formulations that have been separated 
and measured using reversed-phase (RP) high-performance liquid 
chromatography (HPLC). Most lipids including phospholipids, lack 
chromophores and require alternatives to UV detection. 51 Mass Spec-
trometry (MS) offers exceptional selectivity and sensitivity, but its high 
cost, demanding maintenance, and requirement for professional opera-
tors make it unsuitable for quality control. Instead, charged aerosol 
detectors (CAD) and evaporative light scattering detectors may detect 
substances less volatile than the mobile phase, which is nebulized and 
evaporated to manufacture analytes. Unlike refractive index. 52–54 
Furthermore, good HEPES lipids were synthesised using hEPO mRNA to 
assess lipid efficacy in mice following a single intramuscular injection. 
The effectiveness and simplicity of measuring LNP potency using hEPO 
mRNA in mice have made it a popular model protein. Under regulated 
conditions, ionisable, phospholipid, cholesterol, and PEGylated lipid 
solutions were combined with hEPO mRNA in an aqueous acidification 
buffer to make Good HEPES lipid nanoparticles for mRNA delivery. This 
study demonstrated that Gen1 good lipids generated as much hEPO 
protein as industry standard SM-102 and MC3. Mice administered Gen1 
LNPs generated higher hEPO than Gen2 lipids. Gen2 tail esters may 
accelerate lipid breakdown in vivo. 55,56
Size-exclusion chromatography (SEC) combined with a multi-angle 
light scattering (MALS) detector and concentration detectors such as 
differential refractometers (dRIs) and UV photometers is an effective 
method for this type of characterization. It is common to define LNPs
and other complex macro-entities utilizing SEC-MALS-UV-dRI. Re-
searchers can evaluate sample concentration and size without a refer-
ence standard across the SEC peak. When the UV extinction coefficient 
and dn/dc values (or a certain refractive index increment) for binary 
conjugates are known, a multi-detector system can promptly measure 
the two constituent components in each eluting fraction. 57 Additionally, 
the CM Nebulizer (Vibrating Membrane) exhibits a substantial increase 
in LNP particle size, rising from an average of 85 nm to 300 nm, 
alongside a change in the PDI from 0.1 to 0.3, indicating significant 
structural damage due to elevated shear stresses. The Laminar Fluid 
Ejection (LFE) Device exhibited superior LNP stability, evidenced by 
consistent particle size and minimal PDI changes (0.099 for mRNA and 
0.117 for saRNA). 58
Importantly, the LNPs-mRNA particle size and mRNA encapsulation 
are important quality factors that affect in vitro effectiveness. 
Combining orthogonal procedures that use distinct measurement con-
cepts is recommended for validating complicated quality attribute 
measurements. This follows FDA and ICH Q2R2 guidance materials for 
LNP-mRNA formulation. 59,60 Besides nanomaterials and liposomes, 
analytical ultracentrifugation (AUC) has been utilized to size macro-
molecules. As sedimentation velocity separates particles, sedimentation 
coefficient distributions rely on the mass, density, and shape of each 
particle population in the sample. 61
6. Discussion
In this review, we analyzed the mRNA-LNP-based drug modules that 
are improvements in gene editing, correlation to enhance the immune
Fig. 2. Factors involved in mRNA-LNP [69].
K. Sundaram and V. Prabhu Journal of Genetic Engineering and Biotechnology 24 (2026) 100719 
6

response, and activity against various infectious diseases and malignant 
tumors. However, multiple studies have shown the precise mechanism 
of LNP, and the important factors are links to accuracy, sensitivity, and 
stability. LNPs activate the immune system by engaging with pattern 
recognition receptors (PRRs) on antigen-presenting cells (APCs), 
including toll-like receptors (TLRs). Additionally, pro-inflammatory 
cytokines, such as TNF-α, are released as a result of the interaction be-
tween LNPs and PPRs, indicating the activation of an innate immune 
response (Fig. 1). 62
In strengthening the LNP formulation with mRNA for respiratory 
infections, a recent study reported that the titres generated by 
mucoadhesive films containing mRNA/LNP used as booster doses were 
comparable to those of a standard intramuscular vaccine. Furthermore, 
they generated local IgA titres, potentially improving immune protec-
tion against respiratory infections. 63 In contrast, the efficacy of mRNA-
LNP inhaled therapy is currently constrained by shear forces during 
nebulisation and the presence of lung mucus, indicating a need for 
further advancements. Nanoparticle modification using pulmonary 
surfactant lipids enhances infiltration through the lung barrier and re-
duces clearance from lung tissue. 64,65 Similarly, using mRNAs that 
encode protein adjuvants in LNPs instead of antigens might increase the 
immunogenicity of current vaccines. In contrast, protective vaccines 
that boost T-cell-mediated cellular immunity and humoral response are 
still challenging to develop. 66 The LNP-encapsulated mRNA enhances 
immune responses in various viral infections. However, the T cells are 
activated by mRNA vaccines, which are modified to evade the immune 
system and are delivered via lipid nanoparticles for improved efficacy. 67 
On the other hand, the factors associated with the formulation of 
mRNA-LNP are crucial (Table 2), since LNP that carries mRNA into 
antigen-presenting cells is a critical challenge for mRNA vaccine 
development. Several ionizable lipid formulations with great encapsu-
lation efficiency have been tested for several mRNA vaccines; thus, 
choosing one is crucial when deciding on LNP components. In stability 
conditions, the LNP-Luc formulation remained stable at 4 ◦ C for at least 
seven days after freezing, indicating considerable stability. LNP-Luc 
retained its physical properties and mRNA transport efficiency in a 
cryoprotective buffer containing 12% tris-sucrose, unlike the new 
formulation (Fig. 2). 68 According to Alameh et al., the LNP formulation 
may serve as an adjuvant for recombinant protein vaccines, facilitating 
the development of highly effective next-generation protein-based vac-
cines. Additionally, the study indicated that RIG-I/MDA5 and most TLRs 
do not recognise LNP. Mice without MyD88 and MAVS showed largely 
unchanged Tfh and GC B cell responses to rHA + eLNP immunization. 
Future research will examine LNP recognition and IL-6 control. Tfh and 
GC B cells showed reduced stimulation from mRNA-LNP vaccinations in 
MyD88-deficient mice. Similar to COVID-19 vaccinations, 1-methyl-
pseudouridine-modified mRNA vaccines enhanced protein translation 
and decreased inflammation triggered by PAMP sensing. 63,69–71 
Therefore, innovative nucleic acid drugs target genetic and tran-
scriptome disorders. mRNA and siRNA are used to regulate intracellular 
protein levels. After cytosolic transport, siRNAs activate the RNA 
interference (RNAi) pathway to silence specific post-transcriptional 
genes. In vitro mRNA can induce the synthesis of therapeutic proteins 
and antigens. In vivo RNA treatments face challenges with nuclease 
degradation, tissue distribution, and membrane transport, leading to 
their encapsulation in synthetic nanoparticles. 72,73 Thus, there are 
immense research prospects on NPs and transcriptomic studies. Among 
the NPs studied, LNPs are the most effective at carrying RNA. Further-
more, gene editing using LNPs is a recent advancement in treating ma-
lignant tumors and infectious diseases.
7. Conclusion
This study evaluates the effectiveness of mRNA-LNP in gene editing 
with Cas9 techniques to enhance outcomes in clinical and diagnostic 
research. LNP-based drug delivery focuses on treating various infectious
disorders and highlights the factors related to mRNA-LNP formation that 
enhance immune responses to these conditions. Current studies and 
findings are bridging the knowledge gap about mRNA-LNP.
