"1-s2.0-S1687157X26001228-main.pdf","Ancient DNA as a temporal lens: reconstructing evolution, migration, and 
disease dynamics
Abang Ulrich Akwo a,b,*, Julius Luvanga a, Ketonze Valademy Ketu b
a Department of Molecular Biology and Biotechnology, Pan African University Institute for Basic Sciences, Technology and Innovations, Nairobi, Kenya
b Department of Biochemistry, Faculty of Science, The University of Bamenda, Cameroon
ARTICLE INFO
Keywords:
Evolution
Paleogenomic
Genomic
Population
ABSTRACT
Ancient DNA (aDNA) has transformed evolutionary biology and anthropology by providing direct, chronologi-
cally validated genetic evidence over millennia. This review synthesizes significant findings from the paleo-
genomic era (2010–2025), demonstrating how ancient DNA has resolved persistent debates across four 
interconnected themes: (i) human migration and admixture, revealing complex population transitions from 
archaic hominins to Holocene expansions; (ii) adaptation, tracking allele frequency changes during domestica-
tion and selection; (iii) pathogen history, clarifying the origins of pandemics and the evolution of microbiomes; 
and (iv) ecosystem dynamics, identifying extinction causes through sedimentary DNA and conservation geno-
mics. We contend that scientific rigor and ethical stewardship are crucial for accurate conclusions, given ancient 
DNA study requires the destructive collection of culturally significant remains. This review argues that continued 
advancement will depend on the integration of genomic data with archaeological, isotopic, and proteomic ev-
idence, and highlights the necessity for equitable involvement with descendant communities. By conceptualizing 
the past as a continuum of dynamic processes rather than static events, ancient DNA provides a revised historical 
narrative and insights relevant to contemporary concerns in conservation, health, and social justice.
1. Introduction: rewriting history with molecules
Ancient DNA (aDNA) has transformed the study of evolution and 
human history from a method dependent on retrospective inference 
based mostly on contemporary genomes to a discipline grounded in 
direct, chronological genetic evidence. Traditional population and 
evolutionary genetics aimed to reconstruct history from a singular 
“static snapshot” of current diversity; however, ancient DNA (aDNA) 
provides sequential samples over time, converting that snapshot into a 
temporal lens as a functional “fourth dimension” in biology that enables 
the observation of genetic change as it occurs over centuries and mil-
lennia.1–3 Researchers routinely integrate ancient genomes into the ge-
netic variation atlas by extracting DNA from archaeological and 
paleontological specimens, thus tracking changes in ancestries, popu-
lation sizes, and interactions over time and across regions.1,3 This tem-
poral dimension has recontextualized many longstanding fundamental 
questions in evolution and history as matters amenable to precise hy-
pothesis testing. In the exploration of human ancestry, ancient DNA 
(aDNA) has revealed previously unknown lineages, such as the 
Denisovans, and documented numerous occurrences of gene flow among 
Neanderthals, Denisovans, and modern humans, thereby contesting 
simplistic, tree-like representations of our species' history.2,4–8 Research 
in population history indicates that extensive genomic time series reveal 
the settlement of regions like Europe and the Americas is marked by 
multiple migration events, admixture, and partial replacement, rather 
than uninterrupted local development, thereby requiring a reevaluation 
of conclusions based solely on contemporary DNA.2,4–8 Adaptation, 
formerly inferred from current allele frequency patterns, can now be 
analyzed by tracking the temporal trajectories of functional variants, 
clarifying examples of selection on traits such as diet, pigmentation, and 
immunity, as well as adaptive introgression from archaic hominins.2,4–8
Besides humans, ancient DNA time series from fauna, flora, and patho-
gens facilitate the correlation of demographic bottlenecks, domestica-
tion, and extinctions with climatic and cultural transformations at an 
unprecedented temporal resolution.1,3,9,10 The use of ancient DNA as a 
historical tool depends entirely on methodological accuracy. Ancient 
molecules are ephemeral, degraded, and vulnerable to modern 
contamination; therefore, specialized extraction, library preparation, 
