including preliminary validations of authentic ancient sequences and 
contamination prevention measures for genomic-scale protocols con-
cerning Neanderthal DNA, has established the criteria for assessing the 
reliability and analysis of aDNA data.11–14 Contemporary primers and 
reviews synthesize these developments, outlining best practices for 
sampling, laboratory methodologies, damage modelling, and statistical 
analysis that substantiate all credible claims concerning historical mi-
grations, adaptations, or extinctions.1,3,12,13 The capacity of aDNA to 
convert theoretical narratives into measurable evolutionary “films” de-
pends on the technological and analytical frameworks detailed in this 
methodological review.
2. Methodology
This review synthesizes the groundbreaking findings of the paleo-
genomic era (about 2010–2025) through a thematic narrative approach. 
A comprehensive literature search was conducted across Web of Science, 
PubMed, and Scopus to encompass this rapidly evolving field, employ-
ing targeted keywords such as “ancient DNA,” “archaeogenetics,” 
“ancient pathogens,” and “sedimentary ancient DNA,” limited to 
English-language publications. The reference lists of key reviews were 
examined to ensure comprehensive coverage. Studies were prioritized 
for in-depth discussion based on a hierarchy of criteria: first, adherence 
to stringent ancient DNA authentication protocols; second, the genera-
tion of genome-scale data enabling high-resolution inference; and third, 
the study's impact on resolving or fundamentally transforming a sig-
nificant debate in anthropology, evolutionary biology, or palae-
oecology. The synthesis is structured into four interconnected thematic 
divisions that represent the primary directions of investigation in the 
field: human migration and demographic history; adaptation and 
phenotypic evolution; disease and host-pathogen interactions; and 
ecology and environmental transformation. This notion seeks to high-
light the difference between pre-genomic understanding and the 
empirically validated, temporally defined narratives enabled by ancient 
DNA. This qualitative narrative study does not employ traditional meta- 
analytic statistics but aims to provide a critical and coherent account of 
how paleogenomics has transformed our understanding of the distant 
past.
3. Rewriting human history: genomic reconstructions of 
migration and admixture
3.1. Archaic hominin introgression and modern human origins
Ancient DNA has significantly transformed our understanding of 
modern human origins by revealing that Homo sapiens interbred with 
extinct archaic hominins following their migration from Africa. 
Genome-wide analyses indicate that all non-African populations possess 
approximately 1–4% Neanderthal ancestry. Populations in Melanesia 
and Papua New Guinea possess an extra 2–5% Denisovan heritage, the 
highest globally, obtained from Neanderthal DNA.15,16 The extended 
average fragment length of Denisovan ancestry in Oceanian populations 
indicates a more recent mixing event relative to that of Neanderthals.17
Numerous analyses demonstrate at least two distinct Denisovan gene 
flow events influencing East/South Asians and Papuans, with various 
Denisovan lineages contributing to contemporary Papuan genomes.18,19
African populations exhibit relatively minimal identifiable archaic se-
quences, potentially indicating restricted back-migration from Eurasia 
or the existence of unsampled archaic lineages inside Africa.20 This 
genomic heritage comprises a combination of adaptive advantages and 
detrimental incompatibilities. A functionally important fraction of 
introrse archaic variations increased in frequency due to positive se-
lection, offering adaptive benefits in novel contexts. Genome-wide scans 
have identified more than 100 high-frequency archaic haplotypes 
enriched for genes associated with immunology, pigmentation, and 
metabolism, including OAS1/2/3, TLR1/6/10, and OCA2.21,22 The most 
evident instance is the Denisovan-derived haplotype at the EPAS1 gene 
in Tibetans, which facilitates high-altitude adaption and exhibits clear 
indicators of positive selection.23 Likewise, unique Neanderthal-like and 
Denisovan-like haplotypes in the innate immunity gene cluster 
TLR6–TLR1–TLR10 are present at abnormally high frequencies and have 
been associated with altered immunological responses.21 This adaptive 
legacy is, however, opposed by substantial evidence of purifying selec-
tion against incompatible archaic DNA. Genomic maps indicate a sys-
tematic reduction in Neanderthal and Denisovan ancestry adjacent to 
genes, particularly on the X chromosome and in regions with high 
testicular expression, a pattern that aligns with diminished hybrid 
fertility.17 Extensive “archaic deserts,” megabase-scale areas nearly 
wholly without introgression, are significant in the genome and likely 
contain sites where archaic alleles were fundamentally incompatible 
with contemporary human biology.17 The ancient genetic contribution 
represents a twofold legacy: a minor proportion of polymorphisms that 
facilitated adaption to new circumstances, and a more substantial pro-
portion that was eliminated due to harmful effects, resulting in a lasting 
signature of selection across the human genome.
3.2. Holocene population transformations in Eurasia
The Neolithic transition and ensuing Bronze Age migrations in Eur-
asia signify two of the most significant demographic changes in human 
prehistory, with ancient DNA playing a crucial role in elucidating their 
genetic effects. Examinations of genome-wide data from numerous 
ancient individuals indicate that Europe's genetic composition over the 
past 12,000 years has been influenced by at least two significant, 
overlapping demographic waves, resulting in a three major ancestry 
components in contemporary Europeans, originating from indigenous 
hunter-gatherers, Anatolian Neolithic farmers, and Steppe pastoral-
ists.24,25 The Neolithic transition, commencing around 6400 BCE, 
though regional variation existed across the continent was characterized 
by a significant north-westward proliferation of Anatolian farmer- 
related ancestry throughout continental Europe. Early Neolithic pop-
ulations in central and western Europe constitute a cohesive genetic 
cluster closely associated with Neolithic groups from Anatolia, distinctly 
separated from indigenous Mesolithic hunter-gatherers, indicating that 
the dissemination of agriculture entailed considerable demographic 
diffusion.24,26 In numerous areas, especially in western and central 
Europe, early agriculturalists exhibit a near-complete genetic replace-
ment of indigenous hunter-gatherer populations.27 Nonetheless, this 
process was not consistent. Genetic data from locations like Iberia, the 
Lower Danube basin, and the Baltic indicate centuries of coexistence and 
admixture between entering farmers and indigenous hunter-gatherers, 
with varying levels of hunter-gatherer ancestry remaining.27,28 A sig-
nificant revival of hunter-gatherer ancestry is evident in Middle 
Neolithic populations over most of Europe, suggesting intricate, bidi-
rectional connections that obscure the clear distinction between cultural 
and demic diffusion theories.24 A significant genetic shift occurred be-
tween 3000 and 2500 BCE, marked by the swift westward proliferation 
of Steppe pastoralist ancestry associated with the Yamnaya herders of 
the Pontic-Caspian steppe. Late Neolithic Corded Ware populations in 
central and northern Europe possess over approximately 70–75% of 
their ancestry from a Yamnaya-related origin, indicating a significant 
and swift migration episode.24 The Steppe ancestry disseminated 
throughout much of Europe within a millennium through related 
archaeological complexes, frequently leading to near-total local popu-
lation replacements, as evidenced in Denmark, and ultimately contrib-
uting 10–60% of the ancestry in contemporary Europeans.27,29 These 
genetic discoveries have clarified enduring archaeological and linguistic 
controversies by illustrating that the dissemination of Steppe cultural 
packages and probable Indo-European languages into Europe entailed 
extensive population migrations, overlaying the pre-existing genetic 
