mosaic established during the Neolithic era.
3.3. Case study: the peopling of the Americas
The early colonization of the Americas exemplifies how ancient DNA 
can transform conjectural migratory theories into a quantitatively 
defined demographic history. Through the analysis of genomes from 
ancient individuals in the Americas and Northeast Asia, researchers 
have clarified enduring questions regarding time, migration routes, and 
population composition. Genetic evidence robustly corroborates the 
Beringian Standstill model, indicating that the progenitors of all Native 
Americans separated from East Asian populations approximately 36–23 
thousand years ago (ka) and remained genetically sequestered in 
Beringia for several millennia during the Last Glacial Maximum.30,31
Whole-genome data evidence indicates that a major founding popula-
tion, likely representing the primary ancestral source for Native Amer-
icans, emerged no earlier than around 23,000 years ago, though the 
possibility of additional minor contributing populations cannot be 
excluded. This population then diversified across the continent, dividing 
into Northern Native American (NNA) and Southern Native American 
(SNA) branches approximately 17.5–14.6 ka.30 Ancient genomes indi-
cate that SNA-related groups swiftly migrated from north to south 
around 14,000 years ago, resulting in the emergence of various 
regionally structured populations across the continents.32 This image of 
an initial founding pulse was subsequently confounded by supplemen-
tary, more localized gene flow from Asia. For example, Eskimo–Aleut 
speakers possess approximately 50 % of their ancestry from a more 
recent Siberian origin, while Na-Dene speakers exhibit a smaller yet 
distinct contribution from a Paleo-Eskimo-related group, indicating that 
the settlement of the Americas entailed both an initial colonization and 
later Holocene migrations.33,34 Ancient DNA has uncovered unforeseen 
and profound population organization within the ancestral lineage. The 
whole genome of the approximately 11,500-year-old Upward Sun River 
child (USR1) from Alaska unveiled a previously unidentified “Ancient 
Beringian” population that constitutes a basal branch to all other ancient 
and contemporary Native Americans.30 This population seems to have 
been predominantly supplanted or assimilated by subsequent northward 
migrations of NNA groups, rendering USR1 an ancient lineage without 
known direct descendants in modern populations, thereby underscoring 
the intricate demographic dynamics of early America.30 Genomic 
studies have elucidated the unique origins and trajectories of Arctic 
populations, indicating that Paleo-Eskimo groups (e.g., Saqqaq, Dorset) 
constitute a distinct lineage that settled in the Arctic approximately 
5000 years ago and was predominantly supplanted by the forebears of 
contemporary Neo-Eskimos (Inuit and Yupik), exhibiting limited genetic 
continuity.33 These findings collectively demonstrate the capability of 
ancient DNA to refine the chronology of divergence and migration 
events, elucidate dispersal pathways, and uncover intricate population 
patterns and replacements that were not apparent through archaeolog-
ical and contemporary genetic data alone.
4. The engine of adaptation: natural selection revealed through 
time
4.1. Direct observation of selective sweeps in human populations
Ancient DNA (aDNA) significantly transforms evolutionary in-
ferences by enabling researchers to directly witness trajectories, facili-
tating the tracking of allele frequency variations over millennia. This 
time resolution has significantly enhanced the understanding of quin-
tessential instances of human adaptation. The development of lactase 
persistence (LP) in Europe exemplifies a robust, recent selective sweep 
evidenced by ancient DNA. The pivotal lactase persistence (LP) regula-
tory variant (rs4988235-A) was nonexistent in Mesolithic hunter- 
gatherers and early Neolithic agriculturists, with the initial confirmed 
carriers emerging approximately 6000 years before present (BP) in 
Ukraine.35,36 Its prevalence remained notably low (<1%) across Europe 
during the Bronze Age (~4000–3200 BP), indicating that the prolifer-
ation of dairying and the significant Steppe pastoralist migrations were 
unable to facilitate its increase.35 aDNA indicates a pronounced 
sigmoidal increase commencing in the late Holocene, reaching fre-
quencies approximating present levels (~72%) by the mediaeval era.37
This trajectory signifies robust positive selection (selection coefficient s 
≈ 0.05–0.06 per generation) over the past 3000–4000 years, contesting 
simplistic models that correlate selection intensity directly with milk 
consumption and implying that factors such as famine or pathogen 
exposure may have served as significant amplifiers.35,38 Likewise, 
ancient DNA has elucidated the intricate history of skin pigmentation in 
West Eurasia. Mesolithic Europeans, exemplified by the individual from 
La Bra˜na, have ancestral genes associated with darker pigmentation 
characteristics.39 The evolution of lighter skin pigmentation in European 
populations was not a singular historic event but rather a protracted and 
incremental process. Light-skin alleles, such as those at the SLC24A5 
locus, were introduced by Neolithic farmers and subsequently increased 
in frequency over the past 5000 to 10,000 years due to a combination of 
admixture and subsequent selection, clarifying the interplay between 
population movement and local adaptation.40–42 Direct Observation of 
Selective Sweeps in Human Populations is seen in Table 1 below.
The temporal perspective of ancient DNA is equally effective for 
examining selection caused by pathogens. Genome-wide analyses reveal 
that the majority of adaptive changes in human immune genes occurred 
following the onset of the Bronze Age (less than 4500 years BP).41
