haplotype in Tibetans, illustrating that selection has enhanced this 
introgressed variant within the past few millennia.23 Collectively, these 
case studies redefine our understanding of adaptation from a collection 
of static images into dynamic “evolutionary trajectories,” whereby the 
intensity, timing, and ecological context of selection can be quantified 
directly.
4.2. Domestication as a real-time evolutionary experiment
Ancient DNA has transformed our understanding of domestication, 
demonstrating it not as a discrete “event” but as a prolonged, inter-
connected process involving various populations, recurrent gene flow, 
and evolving selection pressures over extensive durations. The domes-
tication of canines illustrates this intricacy. Genomic data suggest a Late 
Pleistocene separation from wolves (11–16 thousand years ago), 
occurring before the advent of agriculture, with no singular current wolf 
population recognized as the exclusive ancestor.45 By around 11,000 
years ago, a minimum of five distinct dog lineages had already emerged, 
signifying a profound pre-agricultural population structure.46 Moreover, 
dogs originate from various, separate wolf populations throughout 
Eurasia, and regional transitions like the nearly total replacement of 
Neolithic European dogs underscore a history of significant dog-wolf 
gene exchange and intricate demographic changes.46,47 Conversely, 
horse domestication seems to be a subsequent and more pronounced 
phase. The contemporary domestic horse lineage (DOM2), representing 
the primary genetic cluster from which all modern domestic horses 
descend emerged in the lower Volga-Don steppe between 2200 and 
2000 BCE, had a significant demographic bottleneck, and subsequently 
proliferated throughout Eurasia, supplanting nearly all indigenous wild 
horse lineages.48 Ancient genomes verify that early domesticated spe-
cies, such as the Botai horses, are related to the ancestors of modern 
Przewalski's horses rather than contemporary domesticates, high-
lighting this trend of replacement.49 Narratives of plant domestication 
have also been substantially refined. The evolution of maize from its 
wild progenitor, teosinte, was driven by the selection of existing genetic 
variation rather than novel mutations. The essential tga1 allele, which 
accounts for the “naked kernel” phenotype, was present in wild teosinte 
populations thousands of years prior to domestication. Ancient DNA 
data supports a model in which this pre-existing allele was progressively 
enriched over millennia by human selection, illustrating a methodical, 
incremental process of trait fixation.50 Contrasting Models of Animal 
Domestication Revealed by Ancient Genomics is seen in Table 2 below.
Across these varied species, ancient DNA indicates convergent 
evolutionary trajectories that underlie the “domestication syndrome,” 
characterized by a set of associated features such as tameness and 
morphological alterations. Comparative genomics indicates that selec-
tion consistently focused on genes associated with neural crest cell for-
mation and neurotransmitter signaling in dogs, horses, sheep, goats, and 
chickens, despite variations in the exact genes implicated.52,53 This il-
lustrates that domestication, regardless of its diverse timeframes and 
demographic histories, frequently modifies common developmental and 
brain gene networks to yield analogous phenotypic results.
4.3. Calibration of evolutionary clocks utilizing ancient genomes
A primary difficulty in evolutionary genetics is the “time-de-
pendency” of molecular clocks: mutation rates derived from recent 
pedigrees can exceed substitution rates predicted from ancient fossil 
calibrations, resulting in contradictory date estimations.54 Ancient DNA 
offers a robust approach by providing directly dated genomes that act as 
calibration points within evolutionary lineages. By positioning 
radiocarbon-dated ancient mitochondrial and nuclear genomes at the 
tips of phylogenetic trees, researchers can estimate substitution rates 
over known intervals spanning tens of thousands of years. This tip- 
calibration approach improves rate estimation and, critically, allows 
for the modelling of temporal rate variation, a phenomenon where 
substitution rates are not constant over evolutionary time.55,56 A sig-
nificant discovery from these investigations is that tip-calibrated rates 
frequently exhibit faster rates than those obtained from conventional 
node-based techniques reflecting the time-dependent decay of apparent 
substitution rates over deeper evolutionary timescales. Utilizing these 
empirically validated, temporally calibrated rates allows for more ac-
curate estimation of divergence dates, with shifts occurring in either 
direction depending on the specific dataset and calibration model 
employed. This improved accuracy has helped reconcile genetic time-
lines with archaeological and fossil evidence, resolving longstanding 
discrepancies between molecular and paleontological estimates for key 
events such as the emergence and spread of anatomically modern 
humans.57 This calibration power encompasses demographic models. 
Techniques such as the Pairwise Sequentially Markovian Coalescent 
(PSMC), which deduce historical population estimates from genomic 
sequences, necessitate an estimated mutation rate to translate coalescent 
units into years. Estimates derived from ancient DNA offer a vital 
empirical foundation, diminishing uncertainty and facilitating more 
precise reconstructions of demographic history for humans and other 
animals.57 The ancient genomes convert the molecular clock from sim-
ple rate-based approximations into a precise instrument, producing 
more dependable and accurate timelines for evolutionary occurrences.
5. Invisible companions: the deep history of pathogens and 
microbiomes
5.1. Reconstructing pandemic origins from ancient pathogen genomes
Ancient DNA has revolutionized our understanding of pandemic 
history by transforming historical outbreaks into precisely dated 
evolutionary occurrences. This methodology has uncovered profound 
origins, unforeseen transmissions, and the evolving characteristics of 
