disease populations across millennia. The history of plague (Yersinia 
pestis) illustrates this change and is previously regarded as a predomi-
nantly mediaeval affliction, ancient DNA has revealed that Y. pestis 
infected humans in Eurasia as far back as 5000 years ago, with Late 
Neolithic and Bronze Age genomes constituting an extinct clade that 
possibly disseminated alongside Steppe population expansions.58
Ancient DNA has offered conclusive etiological and phylogenetic clarity 
for historically known pandemics. The extraction of Y. pestis DNA from 
6th-century skeletons verified the bacterium as the etiological agent of 
the Justinianic Plague (541–750 CE) and positioned the strain within an 
ancestral phylogenetic branch, suggesting an Asian origin.59 Subsequent 
genomes from the First Pandemic indicate it was not a static strain but a 
divergent lineage with significant microdiversity.60 Research on the 
Black Death reveals a uniform genetic introduction into Europe, subse-
quently leading to centuries of diversification into various clades that 
established local reservoirs and subsequent outbreaks, linking the 
mediaeval pandemic to both earlier and contemporary strains.61,62
Ancient DNA has elucidated a persistent enigma regarding tuberculosis 
in the Americas. Skeletal evidence indicated pre-contact tuberculosis; 
however, genomic analysis of 1000-year-old Peruvian people demon-
strated that the causal agent was not a human-adapted Mycobacterium 
tuberculosis but rather a strain most closely related to M. pinnipedii, a 
pathogen affecting seals and sea lions.63 This discovery demonstrated a 
zoonotic transmission from marine mammals and contested the belief 
that New World tuberculosis originated exclusively from European ar-
rivals post-1492. Recent ancient genomes from inland South America 
indicate that this marine lineage disseminated through human-to- 
human transmission, and molecular dating of the M. tuberculosis com-
plex, employing ancient calibrations, corroborates a Holocene emer-
gence associated with expanding, sedentary populations.64,65 Ancient 
hepatitis B virus (HBV) genomes indicate a long-standing human-virus 
relationship extending into the Holocene, with evidence of infection in 
hunter-gatherers dating back over 10,500 years.66 These chronologi-
cally marked genomes disclose a narrative of lineage replacement and 
extinction. Neolithic HBV strains from Europe constitute profoundly 
different clades that exhibit closer phylogenetic affinity to non-human 
monkey HBV than to any contemporary human genotype, signifying 
extinct viral lineages.67 Extensive sampling indicates that the HBV 
strains present in prehistoric Eurasia saw significant substitutions, with 
Mesolithic viral lineages ultimately being replaced by a lineage linked 
with agriculture, which subsequently vanished.66,68 Ancient pathogen 
genomics collectively recontextualises pandemic history, indicating that 
present pathogen diversity comprises merely the survivors of a far 
bigger, dynamic, and fluctuating Holocene environment.
5.2. Tracking viral evolution and host-jumps across centuries
In addition to elucidating origins, ancient viral genomes offer 
distinct perspectives on evolutionary rates, host-switching dynamics, 
and the constraints of models reliant exclusively on contemporary di-
versity. The examination of parvoviruses in carnivores demonstrates 
how paleovirology affects the analysis of viral sequences incorporated 
into host genomes (endogenous viral elements) and archaic viral DNA 
uncovers enduring patterns of interspecies transmission. Phylogenetic 
analyses indicate that parvoviruses have repeatedly transferred between 
host species such as dogs, wolves, and cats over millennia, a process 
driven more by ecological opportunity and receptor compatibility than 
by the accumulation of significant new mutations.69,70 This suggests that 
host jumps are not infrequent, unusual occurrences but a persistent 
characteristic of viral evolution across extensive periods.
Ancient HBV sequences provide as a compelling case study in viral 
evolutionary dynamics. These genomes exhibit a “time-dependent” 
evolutionary rate phenomena, characterized by a high substitution rate 
at brief, recent timelines that significantly diminishes over extended, 
millennial-scale durations.71 This elucidates the seeming paradox 
wherein a 16th-century Italian HBV genome phylogenetically aligns 
with contemporary genotype D, exhibiting minimal temporal structure 
over centuries. The pattern results from influences such as purifying 
selection, recombination, and site saturation, indicating that a singular, 
uniform “molecular clock” is insufficient for dating ancient viral diver-
gence events. Moreover, ancient HBV genomes demonstrate a strong 
correlation between viral lineage distributions and significant human 
demographic events, such as the Neolithic expansion, hence endorsing a 
hypothesis of prolonged host-pathogen co-dispersal.66 Collectively, 
these findings emphasize that viral evolution is contingent upon context, 
oscillating between swift adaptation during host transitions or epi-
demics and extended durations of relative stability within well-suited 
habitats.
5.3. Reconstructing ancestoral microbiomes and their evolution
The extraction of microbial DNA from archaeological materials has 
provided fresh insights into the evolutionary history of the human 
microbiome, correlating community alterations with significant food 
and lifestyle shifts. Dental calculus, also known as mineralized dental 
plaque, has demonstrated remarkable potential as a repository for 
ancient oral microbiomes. The in vivo mineralization encapsulates a 
microbial community that is safeguarded from post-mortem destruction 
and is highly indicative of oral taxa.72 Studies of calculus from in-
dividuals ranging from the Mesolithic period to the contemporary age 
have recorded significant ecological transformations. Hunter-gatherer 
oral communities exhibited notable diversity and had a reduced num-
ber of taxa linked to caries and periodontal disease.73 The Neolithic shift 
to agriculture was accompanied by a notable decrease in diversity and a 
switch to a disease-associated community profile, characterized by an 
abundance of cariogenic and periodontopathic bacteria, consistent with 
archaeological findings of heightened dental diseases.73 The most sig-
nificant change transpired later, as the Industrial Revolution resulted in 
a notable decline in microbial diversity and the prevalence of highly 
acidogenic bacteria such as Streptococcus mutans, associated with the 
extensive intake of refined sugars and flour.72 Table 3 below shows the 
strengths and challenges of primary substrates for Ancient Microbiome 
Research.
Coprolites (ancient faeces) provide direct insight into the gut flora, 
although they pose considerable interpretation difficulties. Their DNA is 
frequently predominated by ambient bacteria from the adjacent soil, 
with less than half usually originating from anticipated gut taxa, 
complicating reliable authentication.74 Thus, dental calculus is pres-
ently regarded as a more dependable medium for examining temporal 
alterations in a preserved, host-associated microbial habitat. These 
ancient microbiome research possess significant implications for 
contemporary health. They illustrate that a “healthy” microbiome is not 
a fixed, universal condition but has co-evolved alongside human sub-
sistence practices. The noted reduction in microbial variety from the 
Neolithic period to the industrial age indicates that present-day, low- 
diversity oral and gut microbiomes may represent what some re-
searchers interpret as dysbiotic remnants of recent dietary alterations, 
perhaps exacerbating current illness prevalence.73 This historical 
viewpoint highlights the importance of utilizing suitable pre-industrial 
or ancestral baselines, rather than contemporary industrialized pop-
ulations, when delineating healthy microbial states and formulating 
microbiome-based therapeutics.
6. Ecology and environmental change: ancient DNA as an 
ecosystem archive
6.1. Unraveling extinction causality in quaternary megafauna
The Late Quaternary extinctions of megafauna such as mammoths, 
woolly rhinoceroses, and gigantic ground sloths exemplify one of pale-
ontology's most persistent controversies, typically divided between cli-
matic change and human overkill explanations. Ancient DNA (aDNA) 
A.U. Akwo et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                Journal of Genetic Engineering and Biotechnology 24 (2026) 100778 
5

has transformed this discourse by uncovering the extended demographic 
and genetic pressures that preceded extinction, transcending simplistic 
endpoint narratives. Genomic investigations of woolly mammoths offer 
a comprehensive account of “extinction in progress.” Comparisons be-
tween a high-coverage genome from a ~ 44,800-year-old mainland in-
dividual and one from a ~ 4300-year-old mammoth from Wrangel 
Island indicate a population that had a prolonged bottleneck followed by 
a significant recent drop.75 The Wrangel individual exhibits roughly 
20% reduced heterozygosity and a 28-fold escalation in homozygosity 
runs, evident genetic indicators of a diminutive, inbred population.75
Subsequent investigations revealed that this isolated relict population 
harbored an abundance of harmful mutations, signifying genomic 
deterioration due to strong genetic drift.76 These genomic findings 
reframe extinction as a gradual process of demographic decline, frag-
mentation, and reduced fitness that occurred over millennia, rather than 
a sudden occurrence. Importantly, sedimentary ancient DNA (sedaDNA) 
has demonstrated that diminutive, concealed populations persisted in 
refugia long after their apparent extinction from the macrofossil record. 
Permafrost cores from Alaska and Siberia indicate that mammoth and 
horse DNA remained in the environment until around 10,500 and 3900 
years ago, respectively thousands of years later than the most recent 
bones found in those areas.77,78 These ‘ghost ranges’ illustrate that 
megafauna coexisted with people for prolonged durations, challenging 
simplistic models of exclusively swift, human-induced extinction events 
and suggesting that extinction processes were more complex and pro-
tracted than previously assumed. The resulting image is not indicative of 
a singular cause, but rather of interrelated stressors. Climate-induced 
habitat alteration particularly the fragmentation and degradation of 
the mammoth steppe ecosystem initially diminished and segregated 
populations. The subsequent hunting pressure exerted by humans on 
these already weakened and genetically impaired populations likely 
contributed to their ultimate decline in numerous areas, though aDNA 
evidence alone cannot definitively establish causation or exclude the 
role of other interacting factors such as climate change and habitat 
fragmentation. This multi-stage model, elucidated by the historical ge-
netic record, reconciles the traditional dichotomy between climatic and 
anthropogenic factors, illustrating that extinctions stemmed from the 
synergistic impacts of environmental change and human-induced pres-
sures on species with pre-existing decreased resistance.
6.2. Ecosystem paleogenomics via sedimentary DNA
Sedimentary ancient DNA (sedaDNA) has transformed palae-
oecology by offering a direct, comprehensive, and taxonomically 
extensive account of historical ecosystems derived from environmental 
materials such as permafrost, lake sediments, and cave deposits. The 
efficacy of sedaDNA resides in its capacity to obtain a comprehensive 
community snapshot from minimal quantities of genetic material 
released by various organisms into their surroundings.79 In contrast to 
conventional proxies such as pollen or macrofossils, which exhibit 
taxonomic bias, metagenomic sequencing of sedaDNA can concurrently 
identify plants, animals, birds, insects, fungi, and bacteria from a single 
sample.80 This methodology has been employed to reproduce the 
renowned “mammoth steppe,” the vast cold-adapted grassland 
ecosystem of the Pleistocene epoch. Comprehensive sedaDNA data from 
the Arctic indicate that this ecosystem sustained a distinctive assem-
blage of grasses, forbs, mammoths, woolly rhinoceroses, horses, and 
bison throughout the Last Glacial Maximum.78 The transition to the 
Holocene is characterized by a simultaneous reduction in steppe vege-
tation and its corresponding megafauna, supplanted by the proliferation 
of woody shrubs and a new assemblage of boreal forest fauna.78,81
A primary advantage of sedaDNA is its heightened sensitivity. It can 
identify species that are uncommon, possess low fossilization potential, 
or are completely missing from the macrofossil record at a location. This 
has disclosed the initial occurrence of plant taxa absent in pollen records 
and, importantly, the prolonged existence of megafauna in refugial 
areas.81,82 The approach has detected hominin DNA, including that of 
Neanderthals and Denisovans, in cave sediments in the absence of 
skeletal remains, showcasing its efficacy in uncovering elusive in-
habitants.83 SedaDNA enhances our understanding of ecosystem re-
sponses to climate changes and human presence by offering a more 
comprehensive and intricate depiction of historical biotic groups and 
their transitions.
6.3. Conservation paleogenomics: informing management with deep-time 
data
Conservation paleogenomics employs ancient and historical DNA 
(defined here as DNA from specimens less than approximately 500 years 
old, often from museum collections) to determine historical baselines, 
identify decline reasons, and inform contemporary restoration tech-
niques, surpassing the constraints of current genetic data.84–86 The sit-
uation of the woolly mammoth's last populations on Wrangel and St. 
Paul Islands is exemplary. Genomic investigations indicate that these 
isolated communities had significant reductions in genetic diversity and 
accumulated deleterious mutations, a phenomenon referred to as 
mutational meltdown.76 On St. Paul Island, multiproxy evidence 
correlated the exact period of extinction with freshwater scarcity due to 
the island's reduction in size, rather than human hunting.87 This accu-
rate diagnosis genomic loss exacerbated by a particular environmental 
resource constraint offers a comprehensive framework for compre-
hending the complex vulnerabilities of small, isolated communities in 
contemporary times.85 The application of Ancient DNA in Conservation 
Paleogenomics is seen in Table 4 below.
Temporal genetic data are indispensable for existing vulnerable 
species. Comparisons between historical genomes (e.g., from museum 
specimens) and contemporary genomes can measure the precise loss of 
genetic diversity and effective population size, establishing significant 
recovery objectives.85 Moreover, ancient DNA can elucidate whether 
genetic variations across surviving groups stem from recent anthropo-
genic fragmentation (genetic drift) or signify profound evolutionary 
adaptations. This distinction is essential for conservation decisions: if 
variations are attributable to genetic drift, facilitated gene flow across 
populations can enhance genetic vitality; if they signify local adaptation, 
such admixture may be detrimental.86 Conservation paleogenomics of-
fers an evidence-based, long-term view by reconstructing historical 
population connections, adaptive variation, and ecosystem composition. 
It recontextualizes conservation genetics from the management of the 
