7. Ethical dimensions and responsible stewardship in ancient 
DNA research
7.1. Navigating core ethical tensions: destruction, colonial legacies, and 
community harm
Research on ancient DNA (aDNA) exists at a pivotal junction of sci-
entific progress and significant ethical obligation. The discipline's reli-
ance on destructive sampling of human remains generates an intrinsic 
conflict between the quest for knowledge and the conservation of 
limited, culturally relevant artefacts.3 The alteration or consumption of 
ancestral remains requires stringent justification based on potential 
scientific and societal benefits, adhering to ethical standards that pri-
oritize minimal harm, comprehensive documentation, and the preser-
vation of materials for future generations.88 In addition to physical 
devastation, the historical framework of ancient DNA study is laden with 
colonial legacies. Human remains in global museum collections 
frequently result from colonial extraction, and genetic research can 
inflict more harm by regarding ancestors as simply scientific specimens 
rather than familial connections, a technique criticized as ‘vampire 
science’.89,90 Prominent conflicts, exemplified by the sequencing of 
Ancestral Puebloan DNA from Chaco Canyon without tribe input, un-
derscore the significant societal damage that can occur when research is 
conducted unilaterally.90,91 Such activities can compromise cultural 
sovereignty, jeopardize legal rights under legislation such as the Native 
American Graves Protection and Repatriation Act (NAGPRA), and 
exacerbate historical trauma by misrepresenting or oversimplifying 
intricate communal identities92 In reaction to these issues, fundamental 
ethical principles have been instituted. Alpaslan-Roodenberg et al. 
(2021) delineate five worldwide principles in the “Ethics of DNA 
Research on Human Remains”: compliance with rules, formulation of 
comprehensive strategies, mitigation of harm, assurance of data acces-
sibility, and stakeholder engagement.88 This paradigm establishes an 
essential foundation; nevertheless, Indigenous researchers and allies 
contend that it falls short, as it may continue to prioritize scientific in-
terests and the principle of open data over community governance.93
The Core Ethical Tensions in Ancient DNA Research and Evolving 
Frameworks is seen in Table 5 below.
7.2. Equitable partnerships: indigenous data sovereignty and collaborative 
models
The ethical critique of top-down research has sparked a significant 
movement towards Indigenous Data Sovereignty (IDS) and collaborative 
partnership frameworks. The Collective Benefit, Authority to Control, 
Responsibility, Ethics (CARE) provide a framework that recon-
ceptualises genetic data as a relational obligation rather than an open- 
access resource.93,95 This paradigm shifts states that Indigenous com-
munities possess the right to govern data collection, management, 
interpretation, and distribution, ensuring that research yields concrete 
collective benefits and adheres to community values. This notion is 
progressively being implemented through innovative, community- 
oriented initiatives. Collaborations with Pueblo nations and other 
Indigenous people illustrate the potential for co-designing research from 
the beginning, incorporating oral histories alongside genetic data with 
community supervision of sampling, processing, and storytelling.91,95
Researchers in the Global South promote “sustainable paleogenomics,” 
emphasizing long-term local capacity development, opposing “helicop-
ter science,” and ensuring that research addresses enquiries pertinent to 
source communities.98,99 These collaborations are additionally rein-
forced by developing legislative frameworks, like provincial legislation 
in Patagonia that confers statutory decision-making authority to 
