DNA in non-permafrost settings106 Recent advancements demonstrate 
this capability: species identification of fragmented bones via high- 
throughput protein fingerprinting (e.g., SPIN) enhances taxonomic 
analysis when morphology and DNA are inadequate.107 The examina-
tion of proteins and encapsulated compounds in dental calculus offers 
unparalleled insight into historical diets, disclosing particular plant and 
animal intake, oral health conditions, and potential medicinal applica-
tions.108 Enamel paleoproteomics has retrieved protein sequences 
exceeding one million years in age, extending molecular phylogenetics 
beyond the existing boundaries of DNA preservation and providing fresh 
insights into the Early Pleistocene.109 The primary objective is to obtain 
a comprehensive biochemical profile of historical living forms. Through 
the co-analysis of DNA (for ancestry and pathogens), proteins (for diet, 
tissue function, and phylogeny), stable isotopes (for provenance and 
trophic level), and organic residues on artefacts (for technology and 
usage), researchers can develop intricately contextualized biographies 
of individuals and sophisticated representations of their ecosystems.110
The optimization of information derived from each unique sample 
constitutes both an ethical and a scientific necessity.
8.3. Transitioning from historical data to predicting future alterations
The most significant future application of ancient genomics may be 
its ability to enhance predictions on biological responses to current 
anthropogenic climate change. Time-stratified genetic and sedimentary 
DNA (sedaDNA) records offer direct, empirical evidence on species and 
ecosystem responses to historical climatic upheavals. Table 7 below 
shows the key future frontiers in Ancient DNA and paleomics Rresearch.
These paleo-archives are currently being systematically incorporated 
into predictive ecological models. SedaDNA time-series monitoring the 
fluctuations in plant and animal populations can be integrated into 
process-based models to estimate critical parameters such as dispersal 
rates, biotic interactions, and ecosystem resilience.111 Ancient genomic 
records of population bottlenecks, range contractions, and adaptive 
allele frequency alterations during historical warming events offer 
essential validation for models predicting species' susceptibility to 
contemporary climate change.113,114 This methodology, also referred to 
as “paleo-enabled conservation” or “conservation paleogenomics,” uti-
lizes historical data as a sequence of natural experiments.115 By com-
prehending the interplay of genetic diversity, gene flow, and natural 
selection with environmental changes over millennia, we can more 
accurately forecast resilient populations, potential evolutionary trajec-
tories, and locations for targeted interventions to alleviate future 
biodiversity decline. Thus, ancient DNA surpasses its function as a mere 
glimpse into the past and emerges as a crucial instrument for traversing 
the Anthropocene, utilizing deep-time data to define the processes that 
will influence the destinies of species including our own in the forth-
coming centuries.
9. Conclusion: the past influencing the present and future
Ancient DNA has significantly reshaped our understanding of life's 
past, converting evolutionary biology and archaeology from fields based 
on inference into firsthand observers of dynamic processes over exten-
sive periods. Paleogenomics has revolutionized static, tree-like models 
by offering a serial, time-stamped account of genetic evolution, uncov-
ering a history marked by episodes of admixture, recurrent population 
replacements, and intricate interactions between genomes and their 
environments.2,3 This review has delineated how this “fourth dimen-
sion” in biology has addressed enduring controversies from the coloni-
zation of continents and the processes of domestication to the origins of 
pandemics and the factors contributing to megafaunal extinction while 
consistently uncovering a narrative of complexity rather than simplicity. 
The field's notable advancement is a dual narrative of technical and 
ethical progression. Advancements in high-throughput sequencing, 
targeted capture, and analytics have facilitated the regular acquisition of 
genome-scale data from an expanding variety of specimens and sedi-
mentary archives.116,117 Simultaneously, the field has seen a requisite 
ethical evolution. Current frameworks advocate for the recognition of 
ancestral remains not merely as data sources but as finite, culturally 
significant resources, necessitating minimally invasive techniques, 
equitable collaborations with communities, and adherence to Indige-
nous data sovereignty.96,118 The accountable management of both 
molecules and meanings has become fundamental to stringent science. 
The future of ancient DNA research indicates further integration and 
significance. Expanding sampling into under-represented regions and 
taxa is essential; however, the most significant advancements will arise 
from integrating genetic data with additional evidence such as isotopes, 
archaeology, paleoproteomics, and historical records to create 
comprehensive “paleo-omics” biographies of former lives and ecosys-
tems.105,106 This deep-time record is widely seen as an essential in-
strument for the future. Time-series data on genetic and ecological 
changes offer empirical evidence to calibrate models predicting species 
responses to climate change, so directly guiding conservation strate-
gies.105,106 The significance of ancient DNA will ultimately be deter-
mined not by the age of the sequences obtained, but by the insight with 
which they are analyzed and utilized. The discipline is at a pivotal 
juncture: it may either perpetuate extractive paradigms or innovate a 
more collaborative and equitable form of knowledge production that 
prioritizes the communities associated with its research. By adopting 
this latter approach, advancing integrative science through ethical 
collaboration, paleogenomics can realize its utmost potential. It can 
transcend mere historical revisionism to actively shape a more equitable 
and sustainable future, providing evidence-based insights for concerns 
including biodiversity conservation, public health, and the profound 
origins of human variation and resilience. This guarantees that the 
narratives encoded in ancient DNA contribute to both the scientific 
