death.6,7 Although age is the primary determinant, various studies have 
shown that genetic factors play a significant role in an individual's 
susceptibility to Alzheimer's. Advances in genomic and neurogenetic 
technologies over the past two decades have opened up new insights into 
the molecular mechanisms of this disease, particularly regarding genetic 
polymorphisms that play a role in increasing the risk of neuro-
degeneration.8 (See Figs. 1–5.) (See Tables 1–6.)
One of the most extensively studied genetic factors in Alzheimer's 
disease is the Apolipoprotein E (APOE) gene, specifically the ε4 allele, 
which is known to be strongly associated with an increased risk of late- 
onset Alzheimer's disease.9,10 The APOE gene, located on chromosome 
19q13.2, functions in lipid metabolism, cholesterol transport, neuronal 
membrane repair, and the clearance of amyloid-beta in the brain. There 
are three main isoforms of APOE: ε2, ε3, and ε4, with APOE ε4 consis-
tently reported as the most significant genetic risk factor for sporadic 
Alzheimer's disease.11,12 Individuals carrying one ε4 allele have a higher 
risk of developing Alzheimer's disease than non-carriers, while in-
dividuals with two ε4 alleles show a significantly greater risk and earlier 
disease onset. Furthermore, APOE is also associated with increased 
amyloid-beta deposition, impaired mitochondrial function, oxidative 
stress, neuroinflammation, and accelerated brain atrophy. This complex 
relationship makes APOE a major focus in biomarker research, genetic 
risk prediction, and the development of targeted therapies in Alz-
heimer's disease.13
In recent years, the number of scientific publications on the rela-
tionship between APOE and genetic susceptibility to Alzheimer's has 
increased significantly. Research focuses not only on molecular and 
clinical aspects but also encompasses epidemiological approaches, 
neuroimaging biomarkers, precision medicine, bioinformatics, and even 
artificial intelligence in disease risk prediction.14 This rapid growth in 
