the literature necessitates a bibliometric approach to comprehensively 
map research developments. Bibliometric analysis allows for the iden-
tification of publication trends, the most productive countries and in-
stitutions, international collaboration patterns, core journals, influential 
authors, and the development of research topics based on co-occurrence 
keywords and citation networks.15,16 This approach is crucial for 
describing the knowledge structure and direction of research develop-
ment related to APOE and Alzheimer's globally. Furthermore, biblio-
metrics can also help researchers identify research gaps and 
opportunities for future multidisciplinary research development.17,18
Although numerous studies have investigated the role of Apolipo-
protein E in Alzheimer's disease, existing evidence remains fragmented 
across genetic association studies and bibliometric investigations. To 
date, only a limited number of studies have integrated quantitative 
knowledge mapping with a systematic synthesis of genetic evidence. 
Such integration is essential for identifying research hotspots while 
simultaneously evaluating the consistency of genetic associations across 
diverse populations. Furthermore, combining bibliometric analysis with 
a systematic review provides a more comprehensive understanding of 
both the evolution of scientific research and the robustness of the 
available evidence regarding the role of Apolipoprotein E poly-
morphisms in Alzheimer's disease. This integrated approach may facil-
itate the identification of emerging research directions, knowledge gaps, 
and potential translational applications in precision medicine.19 Most 
previous studies have focused solely on publication trends without 
conducting an in-depth synthesis of the scientific evidence regarding the 
association between APOE and genetic susceptibility to Alzheimer's. Yet, 
systematic reviews play a crucial role in evaluating the consistency of 
research findings, comparing population characteristics, assessing 
research methodology, and identifying the biological mechanisms un-
derlying the relationship between APOE and Alzheimer's disease pro-
gression.20 Integrating bibliometric studies and systematic reviews can 
yield a more comprehensive understanding, not only of the quantitative 
development of research but also of the quality and substance of 
