published scientific findings. This combined approach is becoming 
increasingly relevant in the era of evidence- based medicine and trans-
lational neuroscience, which demands systematic synthesis of multidi-
mensional data.21,22
Based on this background, this study aims to analyze the global 
development of research on genetic susceptibility to Alzheimer's disease 
related to APOE polymorphisms through a bibliometric study and sys-
tematic review approach.23 Bibliometric analysis was conducted to map 
publication trends, contributions by countries, institutions, authors, 
journals, and the evolution of research themes.24,25 While the systematic 
review was used to evaluate scientific evidence related to the association 
of APOE with increased Alzheimer's risk, its pathogenesis mechanisms, 
and its clinical implications. This study is expected to provide a 
comprehensive overview of the APOE research landscape in Alzheimer's, 
identify future research directions, and provide a scientific basis for the 
development of early diagnosis strategies, genetic risk prediction, and 
precision therapy in neurodegenerative diseases.
2. Methods
This study followed the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic 
Reviews (PRISMA) 2020 guidelines and employed a combined 
