bibliometric analysis and systematic review design to comprehensively 
investigate the association between Apolipoprotein E (APOE) 
polymorphisms and genetic susceptibility to Alzheimer's disease. The 
bibliometric analysis was conducted to map global scientific publication 
trends, collaboration networks, research productivity, and the evolution 
of research themes, whereas the systematic review synthesized and 
critically evaluated the available evidence regarding the association 
between APOE polymorphisms and Alzheimer's disease risk across 
different populations.26
2.1. Data sources and search strategy
The research data were obtained from the Scopus database because it 
has a wide coverage of international journals and provides complete 
metadata for bibliometric analysis.27,28 The literature search process 
was conducted in May 2026 using keywords compiled based on a 
combination of terms related to APOE, Alzheimer's disease, and genetic 
susceptibility. The search strategy used was as follows: Title-Abs-Key 
((“APOE” Or “APOE E4” Or “Apolipoprotein E4”)And(“Alzheimer's dis-
ease”)And (“Genetic Susceptibility” Or “Risk Factor”)) And (Limit-To 
(Doctype,”Ar”)) And (Limit-To(Language,”English”))And Pubyear > 1999 
And Pubyear < 2026. The search was limited to research articles (arti-
cles), in English, and published between 2000 and 2025. This limitation 
was made to obtain articles with adequate scientific quality and rele-
vance to the development of modern research related to APOE and 
Alzheimer's.29,30
2.2. Inclusion and exclusion criteria
The inclusion criteria for this study included: (1) original research 
articles; (2) articles discussing the relationship between APOE and 
Alzheimer's disease; (3) articles addressing genetic susceptibility or risk 
factors; (4) articles in English; and (5) articles indexed in the Scopus 
database. The exclusion criteria included: (1) review articles, conference 
papers, editorials, notes, and letters; (2) duplicate articles; (3) articles 
irrelevant to the research topic; and (4) articles with incomplete 
metadata.
2.3. Literature selection process
The article selection process was conducted systematically following 
the PRISMA guidelines. The selection stages included identification, 
screening, eligibility, and inclusion. All searched articles were exported 
in CSV and Bibtex formats from the Scopus database.31,32 A duplication 
check was then performed using reference management software. The 
screening process was based on the title and abstract to determine the 
article's relevance to the research topic. Articles meeting the inclusion 
criteria were then further analyzed in a full-text review.
2.4. Bibliometric analysis
Bibliometric analysis was performed using Bibliometrix (R package), 
VOSviewer (version 1.6.20), and Microsoft Excel to comprehensively 
evaluate the global research landscape on Apolipoprotein E poly-
morphisms and Alzheimer's disease. Bibliometrix was used to calculate 
bibliometric performance indicators, including annual scientific pro-
duction, citation analysis, the most productive authors, countries, in-
stitutions, journals, and thematic evolution. VOSviewer was employed 
to construct and visualize co-authorship networks, keyword co- 
occurrence networks, citation networks, country collaboration net-
works, and thematic mapping. Network visualizations were generated as 
network, overlay, and density maps to illustrate the intellectual struc-
ture, collaborative patterns, emerging research hotspots, and the tem-
poral evolution of scientific knowledge in this field.16,33
2.5. Systematic review
