6

were analyzed narratively to evaluate the association between APOE 
polymorphisms and genetic susceptibility to Alzheimer's disease. 
Extracted data included PubMed ID, disease type, initial sample size, 
studied gene, single nucleotide polymorphism (SNP) type, variant cate-
gory, and statistical significance (p-value). Furthermore, information 
regarding study characteristics, study design, study population, geno-
typing method, primary outcome, and the association between APOE 
and Alzheimer's risk was analyzed. Data synthesis was performed 
descriptively to identify the consistency of findings between studies, the 
biological mechanisms underlying the association between APOE and 
neurodegeneration, and its clinical implications for early diagnosis and 
the development of precision medicine in Alzheimer's disease.26
2.6. Research procedures and data extraction
Article selection was conducted independently by two reviewers 
through title screening, abstract screening, and full-text eligibility 
assessment based on the predefined inclusion and exclusion criteria 
following the PRISMA 2020 guidelines. Data extraction was performed 
using a standardized form that included study characteristics such as 
authors, year of publication, country or ethnicity of the study popula-
tion, study design, sample size, genotyping methods, APOE variants or 
single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs), variant category, statistical 
significance (p-value), odds ratios, confidence intervals, and the main 
findings related to the association between APOE polymorphisms and 
Alzheimer's disease susceptibility. The methodological quality and risk 
of bias of included studies were independently assessed by two re-
viewers using the Cochrane Risk of Bias 2 (RoB 2) tool, which evaluates 
bias arising from the randomization process, deviations from intended 
interventions, missing outcome data, measurement of outcomes, and 
selection of the reported results. Any disagreements between reviewers 
were resolved through discussion and consensus.
2.7. Functional annotation of APOE variants
To evaluate the potential functional significance of the identified 
APOE variants, functional annotation was performed using HaploReg 
v4.2. The selected single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) identified 
from the systematic review were queried to obtain genomic location, 
functional annotation, sequence conservation (GERP and SiPhy), and 
allele frequency data from the 1000 Genomes Phase 1 populations (Af-
rican, American, East Asian, and European). These annotations were 
used to characterize the biological relevance and population distribution 
of APOE variants associated with Alzheimer's disease.
2.8. Risk of bias assessment, protocol, and registration
The methodological quality and risk of bias of the included genetic 
association studies were independently assessed by two reviewers using 
the Quality of Genetic Association Studies (Q-Genie) instrument, which 
is specifically developed for evaluating genetic association studies. Any 
discrepancies between reviewers were resolved through discussion until 
consensus was achieved. The Q-Genie instrument evaluates study 
quality across 11 methodological domains, including: (1) scientific 
rationale, (2) selection and definition of outcomes, (3) selection and 
comparability of comparison groups, (4) technical classification of 
exposure, (5) non-technical classification of exposure, (6) other poten-
tial sources of bias, (7) sample size and statistical power, (8) a priori 
planning of analyses, (9) statistical methods and control for confound-
ing, (10) appropriateness of the inferences drawn, and (11) appropri-
ateness of the interpretation of results. Each domain was scored on a 
seven-point Likert scale, yielding a maximum possible score of 77. 
Based on the total score, studies were classified as good quality (≥55 
points), moderate quality (36–54 points), or poor quality (≤35 points). 
Among the 26 studies included in the systematic review, all achieved 
total Q-Genie scores ranging from 75 to 76, indicating good methodo-
logical quality and an overall low risk of bias. Most studies received the 
highest ratings for scientific rationale, outcome definition, exposure 
assessment, statistical analysis, and interpretation of findings, while 
only minor reductions in score were observed in domains related to 
other potential sources of bias and sample size justification. The sys-
tematic review was conducted and reported in accordance with the 
Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses 
(PRISMA) 2020 statement. The review protocol was prospectively 
registered in the International Prospective Register of Systematic Re-
views (PROSPERO) under registration number CRD420261399405 
before the study was initiated.
