3. Results
3.1. Main information about data
The results of the bibliometric analysis indicate that research on the 
relationship between APOE polymorphisms and genetic susceptibility to 
Alzheimer's disease experienced significant development during the 
period 2000–2025. A total of 3824 articles from 718 publication sources 
were identified with an annual growth rate of 1.79% and an average of 
51.51 citations per document. The analysis also shows high scientific 
productivity with 19,584 authors involved and an average of 10.7 au-
thors per article, indicating the dominance of multidisciplinary and in-
ternational collaboration with a percentage of international co-authorship 
of 30.31%. In addition, 12,282 Keywords Plus and 5335 Author's Key-
words were found, reflecting the broad scope of research topics related to 
APOE and Alzheimer's disease.
3.2. Most productive affiliations
Analysis of institutional affiliations shows that the University of 
California has the highest contribution to publications in research 
related to APOE polymorphisms and Alzheimer's disease, with a total of 
589 articles. Columbia University follows with 226 articles and Wash-
ington University School of Medicine with 215 articles. Furthermore, 
the University of Washington, Harvard Medical School, and the Mayo 
Clinic also contribute significant publications. The dominance of US 
institutions demonstrates the country's leading role in the development 
of genetic and neurodegenerative research, particularly regarding the 
relationship between APOE and Alzheimer's disease.
3.3. Most productive countries
Analysis of country contributions shows that the United States has 
the highest number of publications in research related to APOE poly-
morphisms and Alzheimer's disease, with a total of 1464 articles 
(38.28%). Next in line are China with 411 articles (10.74%) and the 
United Kingdom with 189 articles (4.94%). Furthermore, Italy, Sweden, 
and Germany also show significant publication contributions. Sweden 
has the highest multiple country publication (MCP) rate at 58.53%, indi-
cating a high level of international collaboration in research. Overall, 
these results demonstrate that developed countries play a dominant role 
in the development of genetic and neurodegenerative research related to 
Alzheimer's disease.
3.4. Most productive sources
Analysis of publication sources shows that the Journal of Alzheimer's 
Disease has the highest number of publications related to APOE poly-
morphism and Alzheimer's disease research, with a total of 358 articles. 
Next, Alzheimer's and Dementia contributed 198 articles, followed by 
Neurobiology of Aging with 159 articles and Neurology with 135 arti-
cles. Several other journals, such as Neuroscience Letters, Frontiers in 
Aging Neuroscience, and Alzheimer's Research and Therapy, also 
showed significant contributions. These results indicate that research on 
APOE and Alzheimer's disease is largely published in journals focused on 
neurology, neurodegeneration, geriatrics, and neuroscience.
3.5. Co-occurrence keywords analysis
Analysis of co-occurrence keywords using VOSviewer shows that the 
keyword “Alzheimer's disease” is the main focus of the research network 
with the highest level of connectivity to other keywords such as “poly-
morphism”, “APOE4”, “genetics”, “late-onset Alzheimer's disease”, and 
“biomarker.” Visualization results indicate that global research on Alz-
heimer's disease is dominated by studies of the association of the APOE 
ε4 polymorphism with genetic susceptibility, neurodegeneration 
mechanisms, neuroinflammation, and early diagnostic biomarkers. 
Furthermore, keywords such as “microglia”, “oxidative stress”, “func-
tional connectivity”, and “Machine learning” represents the development 
of multidisciplinary research that integrates molecular genetics, neuro-
imaging, bioinformatics, and artificial intelligence in understanding 
Alzheimer's pathogenesis and the future development of precision 
medicine.
3.6. Co-authorship authors analysis
Analysis of author co-authorship using VOSviewer shows the exis-
tence of a broad and complex network of scientific collaborations in 
research related to Alzheimer's disease and APOE polymorphisms. The 
network visualization shows several key authors with high levels of 
connectivity, such as Bennett DA, Holtzman DM, Michaelson DM, Blennow 
K, and Tan L which serves as a collaborative hub in various research 
clusters. These results indicate that Alzheimer's research is dominated by 
multidisciplinary and international collaborations involving genetics, 
neurology, biomarkers, neuroimaging, and neuroscience. Furthermore, 
the formation of different colored clusters indicates the presence of 
specific research groups with specific study focuses, such as molecular 
genetics, cerebrospinal fluid biomarkers, neuroinflammation, and Alz-
heimer's epidemiology. The high level of interconnectedness among 
authors reflects the growing global collaboration in efforts to understand 
the mechanisms of pathogenesis and develop precision diagnosis and 
therapy for Alzheimer's disease.
3.7. Country collaboration network analysis
Country collaboration network analysis using VOSviewer shows that the 
United States is the main center of global collaboration in research 
related to APOE polymorphisms and Alzheimer's disease, characterized 
by the largest node size and high connectivity with various countries 
such as China, Germany, the United Kingdom, Sweden, Italy, and Australia. 
Network visualization shows the formation of several international 
collaboration clusters reflecting different research focuses, ranging from 
molecular genetics, neurodegenerative biomarkers, genetic epidemi-
ology, to precision medicine. In addition to European and North American 
countries, the contribution of Asian countries such as China, South Korea, 
and India is also seen to be increasing, indicating the development of 
Alzheimer's research as a multidisciplinary global health issue. The high 
connectivity between countries illustrates the importance of interna-
tional collaboration in accelerating the development of understanding 
the mechanisms of Alzheimer's pathogenesis, identifying genetic risk 
factors for APOE, and innovation in early diagnosis and precision ther-
apy in neurodegenerative diseases.
3.8. Systematic review of APOE polymorphism in Alzheimer's disease
A systematic review of 26 articles showed that the APOE gene, spe-
cifically the rs429358 and rs7412 variants, has a highly significant as-
sociation with an increased risk of Alzheimer's disease in various ethnic 
populations. Most studies reported that missense variants in APOE ε4 are 
strongly associated with increased genetic susceptibility to Alzheimer's 
with very high significance values, even reaching p-values of up to 1.00E- 
300. The rs429358 variant is the most predominant SNP found in Eu-
ropean, African-American, Hispanic, East Asian, and Puerto Rican 
populations, indicating that APOE is a major genetic risk factor that is 
consistent across populations. In addition, several studies also reported 
the involvement of the rs7412 variant which contributes to the risk of 
neurodegeneration through mechanisms such as impaired lipid meta-
bolism, increased amyloid-beta deposition, neuroinflammation, and 
oxidative stress. The analysis also showed that most studies used very 
large sample sizes, ranging from hundreds to over a million participants, 
thus strengthening the validity of the association between APOE poly-
morphisms and Alzheimer's disease. Populations with European 
D.P. Amukti et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Journal of Genetic Engineering and Biotechnology 24 (2026) 100785 
8

ancestry dominated the research, but the increasing number of studies in 
African-American, Hispanic, and Asian populations demonstrates the 
growing interest in cross-ethnic genetic variation in Alzheimer's patho-
genesis. These findings indicate that APOE ε4 not only serves as a key 
genetic biomarker in Alzheimer's risk prediction but also has significant 
potential in the development of precision medicine, early detection, and 
genetic-based therapeutic strategies for neurodegenerative diseases.
3.9. Functional annotation of APOE variants
Functional annotation using HaploReg v4.2 identified three APOE 
variants (rs7412, rs429358, and rs7259620), all located on chromosome 
19 and classified as missense variants. These variants exhibited sequence 
conservation based on both GERP and SiPhy scores, indicating potential 
functional importance. Population allele frequency data from the 1000 
Genomes Project showed that rs429358 and rs7259620 had the highest 
alternate allele frequency in African populations (AFR = 0.26), followed 
by European (EUR = 0.14), American (AMR = 0.11), and East Asian 
(ASN = 0.09) populations, whereas rs7412 showed lower frequencies 
across all populations (AFR = 0.09, AMR = 0.06, ASN = 0.08, and EUR 
= 0.07). These findings suggest that conserved APOE missense variants 
may contribute to the genetic susceptibility of Alzheimer's disease and 
support their functional relevance in disease pathogenesis.
4. Discussion
4.1. APOE and its relationship to Alzheimer's disease
Apolipoprotein E is one of the genes most involved in genetic sus-
ceptibility to Alzheimer's disease, particularly late-onset Alzheimer's dis-
ease (LOAD).34,35 This gene is located on chromosome 19q13.2 and 
functions in lipid metabolism, cholesterol transport, neuronal mem-
brane repair, and the clearance of amyloid-beta in the brain.36,37 In the 
central nervous system, APOE is produced primarily by astrocytes and 
plays a crucial role in maintaining neuronal homeostasis. Several studies 
have shown that altered APOE function can lead to impaired amyloid- 
beta elimination, leading to amyloid plaque accumulation, neuro-
inflammation, oxidative stress, synaptic dysfunction, and progressive 
neuronal degeneration, the hallmarks of Alzheimer's disease.38,39 A 
systematic review of this study demonstrated that APOE consistently has 
a significant association with an increased risk of Alzheimer's disease 
across various ethnic populations, including Europeans, Asians, African 
Americans, and Hispanics.40 The high statistical significance across 
these studies reinforces the role of APOE as a major genetic risk factor in 
sporadic Alzheimer's disease. In addition to contributing to the forma-
tion of amyloid-beta plaques, APOE also affects tau protein hyper-
phosphorylation, mitochondrial function, blood-brain barrier 
permeability, and microglia activation, which triggers chronic neuro-
inflammatory processes. Therefore, APOE is currently used not only as a 
biomarker of genetic risk but also as an important target in the devel-
opment of precision therapies and early detection strategies for Alz-
heimer's disease.41,42
4.2. Polymorphism rs429358 and its relationship with Alzheimer's disease
rs429358 polymorphism is one of the key SNPs in the APOE gene 
most frequently associated with an increased risk of Alzheimer's dis-
ease.43 This SNP plays a role in the formation of the APOE allele, known 
to be the strongest genetic risk factor for late-onset Alzheimer's disease. 
Based on the results of a systematic review, most studies indicate that 
rs429358 has a highly significant association with Alzheimer's, with a 
very small p-value of up to 1E-320.44 These findings indicate that the 
presence of the APOE ε4 allele due to the rs429358 variant consistently 
increases the risk of neurodegeneration in various population groups. 
Biologically, the rs429358 variant causes changes in the structure of the 
APOE protein, thereby reducing the protein's ability to clear amyloid- 
beta from the brain. This condition leads to increased amyloid plaque 
deposition that triggers neuronal damage and progressive cognitive 
impairment.45,46 Furthermore, APOE is also known to be associated with 
increased neuroinflammation, impaired neuronal lipid metabolism, 
decreased synaptic function, and more rapid hippocampal atrophy 
compared to non-carriers of the ε4 allele. Neuroimaging studies have 
also shown that individuals with rs429358 have higher amyloid accu-
mulation even before the onset of clinical Alzheimer's symptoms. Thus, 
rs429358 not only serves as a genetic biomarker of Alzheimer's risk but 
also holds significant potential in disease prediction approaches and the 
development of genetic-based therapies.47
4.3. The rs7412 polymorphism and its relationship to Alzheimer's disease
In addition to rs429358, the rs7412 polymorphism is also an important 
SNP in the APOE gene that plays a role in determining the APOE iso-
form.48 Variation in rs7412 is associated with the formation of APOE ε2 
and ε3 alleles, which have different biological effects on the risk of 
Alzheimer's disease.49 A systematic review showed that rs7412 has a 
significant association with Alzheimer's in various populations, with a 
very low p-value reaching 4.00E-123.50 Although the effect of rs7412 on 
Alzheimer's risk is not as strong as rs429358, this SNP still has an 
important contribution in modulating genetic susceptibility to neuro-
degeneration. Mechanistically, rs7412 affects the structure and function 
of the APOE protein in lipid metabolism and cholesterol transport in the 
central nervous system.51 The APOE ε2 allele associated with rs7412 is 
generally considered to have a protective effect against Alzheimer's 
because it increases the clearance of amyloid-beta and reduces amyloid 
plaque deposition. Conversely, certain combinations of rs7412 and 
rs429358 may increase disease risk through disruption of neuronal lipid 
homeostasis and chronic inflammation. Furthermore, recent research 
suggests that rs7412 is also associated with altered microglial immune 
responses, synaptic function, and susceptibility to oxidative stress. 
Therefore, the rs7412 polymorphism plays a crucial role in under-
standing individual variations in Alzheimer's risk and supporting the 
development of precision medicine strategies based on patients' genetic 
profiles.52
5. Conclusion
This study demonstrates that Apolipoprotein E (APOE) poly-
morphisms, particularly rs429358 and rs7412, are significantly associ-
ated with an increased risk of Alzheimer's disease across diverse ethnic 
populations, reinforcing their role as major genetic determinants of 
disease susceptibility. Bibliometric analysis revealed a continuous 
growth in global research on APOE and Alzheimer's disease, character-
ized by extensive multidisciplinary and international collaborations, 
particularly in the fields of genetics, biomarkers, neurodegeneration, 
and precision medicine. Furthermore, the systematic review confirmed 
that APOE variants contribute substantially to the pathogenesis of Alz-
heimer's disease through mechanisms involving amyloid-beta accumu-
lation, neuroinflammation, oxidative stress, and impaired lipid 
metabolism, highlighting their potential as valuable biomarkers for 
early diagnosis and personalized therapeutic strategies. Future research 
should integrate multi-omics approaches, including genomics, tran-
scriptomics, proteomics, and metabolomics, together with artificial in-
telligence and advanced bioinformatics methodologies to improve 
genetic risk prediction, uncover novel molecular mechanisms, and 
accelerate the development of precision medicine for Alzheimer's 
disease.
