performance of construction materials while aligning with circular 
economy principles and resource-efficient construction practices.6–10
For instance, microbially induced calcium carbonate precipitation 
(MICP) has been widely investigated for construction applications, 
particularly in concrete, predominantly employing ureolytic bacteria 
such as Bacillus spp., where improvements in compressive strength of up 
to 40% have been reported. Recent advancements also include engi-
neered or steerable microbial systems for targeted mineral delivery in 
soil stabilization applications.11 Similarly, Sporosarcina pasteurii has 
been reported to increase strength of approximately 4.29%. In-
vestigations involving Paenibacillus species also reported to have better 
mechnaical properties compared to conventional brick. These studies 
demonstrate enhanced compressive strength and durability through 
biologically mediated mineralization via diverse metabloic pathways. In 
addition, the role of microbial metabolic diversity in influencing bio-
mineralization and material performance remains insufficiently 
explored. Despite these advances, the exploration of less-studied mi-
crobial strains and their usage in development of bio-brick remains 
limited. In this context, the present study introduces Paenibacillus species 
as one of the candidates for bio-brick production, potentially exhibiting 
distinct mineralization behaviour. A comparative evaluation with pre-
viously reported systems in terms of strength, water absorption, and 
precipitation mechanisms is therefore essential to establish its sig-
nificance.12–25 Bio-bricks exhibit unique physicochemical properties 
that distinguish them from conventional bricks.26 These properties are 
achieved through the harnessing of microbial diversity, particularly the 
use of ureolytic bacteria, which has gained substantial interest in recent 
research.27 Ureolytic bacteria play a pivotal role in bio-brick fabrication 
by interacting with soil components and induce mineralization pro-
cesses.28 It has been reported to enhance mechanical strength, structural 
stability, and compressive strength, while reducing permeability and 
improving durability through mechanisms such as self-healing. Me-
chanical and compressive strength, improve structural stability, 
decrease permeability, and promote durability through mechanisms 
such as self-healing processes.29 Mineralization by ureolytic bacteria 
involves microbial-induced precipitation of minerals through urea hy-
drolysis, leading to nucleation, crystal growth, and structural organi-
zation of calcium carbonate and silicate phases. Recent studies have 
highlighted that crystal transformation and self-assembly processes play 
a critical role in determining the morphology, stability, and mechanical 
properties of biominerals.30,31 In most ureolytic bacteria, production of 
urease enzymes breaks down the urea and generate ammonia with slight 
increase in the pH of the surrounding environment.32 These processes 
enhance soil stability and can be utilized in the production of bio- 
materials like bio-bricks, improving their mechanical properties and 
durability. Silicate mineralization offers a promising avenue for sus-
tainable construction and environmental remediation.33 These proper-
ties are crucial for their potential application in load-bearing structures, 
where strength is a primary concern. The metabolic activity of the mi-
croorganisms facilitates the precipitation of binding agents, which en-
hances the strength of the brick thus making it as one of the viable 
alternatives for sustainable construction. The present study is aimed at 
developing bio-brick by blending the ureolytic bacteria with the soil 
mixtures and studying their physicochemical and mechanical 
properties. (See Figs. 1–12.) (See Table 1.)
The study also focused on studying the metabolic profiling of the 
bacteria by analyzing their chemical diversity via Gas chromatography- 
Mass Spectrometry (GC–MS) to determine the types of metabolites 
secreted under minimal growth conditions. Scientific studies highlight 
the role of bioactive metabolites to aid in mineralization process through 
their metabolism.34–36 These metabolites may contribute to the alka-
linization of the environment, aiding in the precipitation of minerals 
that may also enhance the stability and physicochemical properties of 
the bio-bricks. The compounds secreted by ureolytic bacteria during 
their metabolic processes exhibit a diverse range of proper-
ties.37–41These properties may potentially influence the hydrophobic 
properties of bio-bricks, improving their water resistance and overall 
durability. Perusal of scientific literatures suggest, the present investi-
gation adds toward growing scientific knowledge of Paenibacillus species 
as one of the potential source of bio-brick production and its metabolic 
profiling. Despite their potential, bio-bricks face limitations including 
environmental sensitivity, variability in microbial activity, scalability 
challenges, variability in performance, and regulatory concerns. This 
study hypothesizes that Paenibacillus species enhances bio-brick strength 
and durability through microbial mineralization and aims to evaluate its 
effectiveness compared to conventional ureolytic bacteria.
2. Material and methods
2.1. Sample collection
The sampling of soil was carried out from five distinct geographical 
locations in H. D. Kote, Karnataka, India, designated as site 01 
(12.277086,76.4954659), site 02 (121,275,958,76.4956305), site 03 
(12.1280980,76.4955094), site 04 (12.1282403, 76.4957009) and site 
05 (12.1315719,76.5062970). One soil sample was obtained from each 
site at a depth of 5–10 cm. All samples were transported to the labora-
tory under aseptic conditions to prevent contamination and preserve 
microbial viability.
2.2. Isolation of bacteria
Bacterial isolation was performed using the serial dilution and 
spread plate method. Soil samples were serially diluted from 10␀ 1 to 
10␀ 7 using sterile saline (9 mL). Aliquots (0.1 mL) from dilutions 10␀ 3 to 
10␀ 6 were spread onto nutrient agar plates (composition: peptone 5 g/L, 
beef extract 3 g/L, sodium chloride 5 g/L, agar 15 g/L; pH 7.0 ± 0.2). 
The plates were incubated under appropriate conditions to allow colony 
development. Distinct colonies were selected and purified by repeated 
streaking on fresh nutrient agar plates for three successive cycles to 
obtain pure cultures. Purity was confirmed based on consistent colony 
morphology. The purified isolates were subsequently subjected to urease 
screening using urea-supplemented agar medium (0.1–0.5% urea).42,43
Each isolate was assigned a unique alphanumeric code for traceability 
throughout the study.
