2.4. Molecular characterization of ureolytic bacteria
The isolated strain with maximum growth and exhibiting urease 
activity was subjected to molecular profiling. DNA extraction was per-
formed, followed by amplification of 16S rRNA sequence was purified 
and matched using BLAST tool and phylogenetic tree was constructed to 
match the close affinity isolates.44
2.5. Determination of bacterial growth curve
To determine the growth curve of urease positive isolate, seed cul-
ture was prepared and inoculated in nutrient broth supplemented with 
urea and incubated as per the previously reported protocols with slight 
modifications.37,45 Samples were drawn at different time intervals from 
0 to 24 h respectively and OD was recorded at 600 nm. This method 
allowed for the monitoring of bacterial growth over time, providing 
insights into the proliferation and metabolic activity of the urease pos-
itive strains in response to urea supplementation.
2.6. Tests on soil
2.6.1. Grain size distribution (Sieve analysis)
In order to produce bio-brick, soil sample was screened to determine 
the proportions of clay, silt, and sand. Sieve series of 4.75 mm, 2.36 mm, 
1.18 mm, and 0.075 mm, along with a pan to collect particles finer than 
0.075 mm. A representative 500 g oven-dried soil sample was used, and 
the material retained on each sieve was measured to determine particle 
size distribution. The fraction passing 0.075 mm was collected in the pan 
and considered as the fine fraction (silt and clay) in accordance with 
standard grain size analysis procedures.
Further, the soil was subjected to sieve analysis to segregate the 
particle size.46 These parameters are reported to influence the quality 
and strength of the bio brick.47 According to IS 2117:1991, the recom-
mended soil composition for brick manufacturing should contain 
35–50% sand by mass, and the combined clay and silt content should 
preferably be not less than 50% by mass.48 These proportions ensure the 
right balance of fine and coarse particles, contributing to optimal 
molding and drying characteristics of the bricks.49 Maintaining these 
proportions is essential for achieving the desired texture, strength, and 
durability in the final brick product.50
2.6.2. Plasticity index
The plasticity index (PI) of soil represents the range of moisture 
content over which the soil exhibits plastic behaviour. According to IS 
2720 (Part 5): 1985, the PI of soil is obtained by the numerical difference 
between Liquid Limit (LL) and Plastic Limit (PL). PI, LL and PL are ob-
tained through standard procedures outlined in IS 2720 (Part 5). The PI 
