2.8. Physicochemical and morphological analysis of the bio-brick
2.8.1. FTIR, XRD and DLS analysis
The functional groups associated with the bio-brick were analyzed 
using Fourier Transform Infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy (Model: FT/IR- 
4100 type A, JASCO, Japan). The bio-brick samples were ground into 
fine powder to ensure uniformity and effective interaction with infrared 
radiation. The spectra were recorded over a wavenumber range of 
4000–400 cm␀ 1 at a resolution of 4 cm␀ 159, with 32 scans accumulated 
per sample using a TGS detector in transmittance mode.59,60 Instru-
mental parameters such as aperture, scanning speed, and filter were 
maintained under automatic settings.
The crystalline phases present in the bio-brick were identified using 
X-ray diffraction (XRD) analysis (PROTO diffractometer, PROTO 
Manufacturing Ltd., Canada). Measurements were carried out using Cu 
Kα radiation (λ = 1.540593 Å) at an operating voltage of 30 kV and 
current of 20 mA. Data were collected in coupled scan mode over a 2θ 
range of 10◦ to 80◦, with a step size of 0.04◦ and a dwell time of 0.5 s per 
step. Appropriate slit configurations were used, including a 1 mm 
divergence slit, 1 mm anti-scatter slit, and a 0.1 mm receiving slit. The 
obtained diffractograms were analyzed to determine the crystalline 
phases present in the bio-brick.61
Particle size analysis was performed using a Beckman Coulter Delsa 
Nano instrument (Beckman Coulter, USA) based on dynamic light 
scattering (DLS). For analysis, finely powdered bio-brick samples were 
dispersed in distilled water and sonicated to obtain a stable suspension 
prior to measurement. The DLS technique was employed to estimate the 
size distribution of fine particulate fractions present in the bio-brick 
matrix rather than bulk material characterization.62 The particle size 
distribution provides insight into aggregation behaviour, which may 
influence packing density and mechanical strength of the bio-bricks.
2.8.2. Scanning electron microscopy and Energy-Dispersive X-Ray 
Spectroscopy analysis
The morphological and elemental characteristics of the bio-bricks 
were analyzed using scanning electron microscopy (SEM) equipped 
