2.3. Screening of ureolytic bacteria
Ureolytic bacteria were screened from the isolates using a urea- 
supplemented liquid medium. Nutrient broth was prepared and sup-
plemented with 2% (w/v) urea, which served as the nitrogen source. 
This medium was used as an alternative to Christensen's urea agar for 
urease screening. Urea-enriched broth was selected over solid media to 
allow better diffusion and interaction of metabolites, enabling more 
accurate detection of urease activity in liquid conditions. The isolated 
bacteria were inoculated into the urea-enriched broth and incubated at 
controlled temperature for 48 h. Urease activity was determined by the 
addition of phenol red indicator, and a colour change from yellow to 
pink indicated urea hydrolysis and an increase in pH due to ammonia 
production. This method enabled the identification of urease-producing 
bacteria based on their ability to alter the pH of the medium.
