4

indicates the soil's workability and suitability for shaping and com-
pacting during molding process. As per IS 2117: 1991, soil used for brick 
manufacturing should have a PI within the range of 15–25%, ensuring 
optimal plasticity for molding without excessive shrinkage or cracking 
during drying process.51–53
2.7. Production and molding of bio-brick
The production of the bio-brick began with the use of a regionally 
sourced soil sample, which was subjected to basic tests as per IS 
2720:1985 before the brick molding process. The molding was carried 
out as per the standard protocol outlined by Poornima et al., (2021), 
with slight modifications.54 Briefly, the selected bacterial isolate was 
cultured to achieve a cell density of 108 cells mL␀ 1. The bio-bricks were 
then cast in accordance with IS 1077:1992, with the bacterial suspension 
incorporated into the soil mixture prior to molding and incubation. 
During the seven-day incubation period, the bricks were continuously 
treated with the bacterial suspension using a spraying method. To 
ensure the integrity and effective distribution of bacterial cells within 
the bio-brick matrix, a suspension containing 108 cells mL␀ 1 was 
maintained throughout the treatment period. After incubation, the 
properties of the bio-bricks were evaluated and compared with those of 
control bricks.55
All reported values represent average measurements, and the 
compressive strength test was conducted under a uniform loading rate. 
The curing of bio-bricks was performed by immersing the samples in 
distilled water containing 15% bacterial solution for 24 h. Subsequently, 
the bacterial solution was sprayed daily on the bio-bricks for 7 days prior 
to testing.
2.7.1. Physical properties of the bio-brick
The bio-brick physical properties were evaluated in accordance with 
IS 1077:1992, IS 2180:1988 and commonly used field inspections, to 
meet the standard criteria.56 The hardness test determined resistance to 
surface abrasion, while the colour test verified a bright and uniform 
appearance. The soundness test involved striking of bricks to check the 
bell ringing sound, indicating good compactness, while the structural 
inspection confirmed that the bio-brick remained homogeneous and free 
from visible defects or lumps. All relevant tests for bio-brick character-
ization were conducted in triplicate to ensure accuracy and reliability of 
the results. Performing the experiments in three independent sets helped 
account for experimental variability and minimize random errors. This 
approach enhances the reproducibility and statistical validity of the 
assessed parameters.
2.7.2. Mechanical properties
