formed using a JEOL JSM-IT300 operated at an accelerating voltage of 
20 kV under high vacuum (HV) conditions, with a secondary electron 
detector (SED). The working distance was maintained at 30 mm, and 
images were acquired at a magnification of 500×, with a scan time of 40 
s and a probe current of approximately 23.8 nA. The instrument pro-
vides a spatial resolution of up to 30 nm and a maximum magnification 
capability of 300,000×. Prior to imaging, the samples were sputter- 
coated with a thin conductive layer of carbon and gold to minimize 
charging effects and improve image quality. Elemental composition was 
determined using the attached EDS system. The stage position and beam 
alignment were optimized before acquisition to ensure accurate imaging 
and compositional analysis.63
