2.9. Metabolic profiling of the isolate
The metabolic profile of the selected ureolytic bacterial isolate was 
analyzed to obtain preliminary insights into its biochemical activity. The 
isolate was inoculated into nutrient broth (peptone 5 g/L, beef extract 3 
g/L, NaCl 5 g/L) supplemented with urea and incubated at 37 ◦C for 
24–48 h under shaking conditions until the late exponential phase of 
growth was reached. All experiments were performed in triplicate. After 
incubation, the culture was centrifuged at 10,000 rpm for 10 min to 
obtain the cell-free supernatant. The supernatant was extracted with 
ethyl acetate in a 1:1 (v/v) ratio and vortexed thoroughly. The organic 
phase was separated and evaporated at 40 ◦C to remove the solvent. The 
resulting residue was re-dissolved in 1 mL of ethyl acetate for further 
analysis.64 This analysis provides preliminary insights into the metab-
olites produced by the isolate, which may play a role in the bio-brick 
system.
2.10. TLC and GC–MS analysis
The crude extract was partially purified using thin-layer chroma-
tography (TLC) on silica gel plates. The separation was carried out using 
a chloroform:methanol (9:1, v/v) solvent system, and the plates were 
developed in a saturated chamber. After development, the plates were 
air-dried and visualized under UV light at 254 nm and 365 nm. Distinct 
bands were observed, and their retention factor (Rf) values were 
calculated. The selection of bands was based on their intensity and 
separation profile.
The crude extract was further analyzed using gas chromatogra-
phy–mass spectrometry (GC–MS) to determine the metabolic profile. 
The mass detector was operated in ionization mode, and the obtained 
spectra were matched with the NIST library for metabolite identifica-
tion.65 These metabolites are hypothesized to influence mineral pre-
cipitation and inter-particle binding, thereby contributing to improved 
mechanical and durability properties.
The identified metabolites were further evaluated for their potential 
role in bio-brick formation, particularly in influencing mineral precipi-
tation, binding interactions, and hydrophobic characteristics, which 
may contribute to enhanced mechanical strength and durability of the 
developed material.
3. Results and discussion
The development of bio-bricks using ureolytic bacteria was suc-
cessful following the isolation of a potent strain. The screening of soil 
samples via serial dilution resulted in the isolation of 231 myriad bac-
terial colonies. The number of colonies gradually decreased with an 
increase in the dilution factors from 10␀ 1 to 10␀ 7. The concept of serial 
dilution facilitates the isolation of discrete colonies from the sample, as 
shown in Fig. 1. The obtained results coincides with the previous sci-
entific literatures for isolation and screening of bacterial species from 
diverse natural resources.66 All the colonies were designated with al-
phanumeric codes for further experiments. The bacteria isolated from 
the soil samples were subsequently screened for ureolytic activity.
3.1. Screening for ureolytic activity
The screening of ureolytic bacteria was successful using enriched 
media, which supported luxuriant colony growth of 29 isolates. These 
colonies were subsequently subjected to large-scale cultivation and 
tested for their ureolytic activity. Urease activity was confirmed using 
phenol red as a pH indicator; the observed colour change in the broth 
indicated the hydrolysis of urea into ammonia, as illustrated in Fig. 2a 
which shows some of the isolates. This can be confirmed with the earlier 
reported studies which demonstrate the production of urease in broth 
which changes if urease is positive.67 Among different isolates, colonies 
exhibiting the highest growth absorption were selected for further 
experiments. Among different colonies showing urease positive, one 
colony displayed maximum growth which was selected as the subject of 
interest in the present study (Fig. 2b). This colony was further evaluated 
for its molecular characterization. The mechanism involves urease- 
mediated hydrolysis of urea into ammonia and carbonate ions, leading 
to an increase in pH and subsequent mineral precipitation.
3.2. Growth pattern dynamics and molecular characterization
The growth of the isolate was monitored by measuring optical den-
sity at 600 nm (OD₆₀₀). The culture exhibited an OD₆₀₀ value of 0.447, 
compared to the control value of 0.05, indicating active bacterial growth 
under the tested conditions. The increase in optical density over time 
suggested normal progression of bacterial growth. The isolate was 
characterized as a Gram-positive, motile bacterium and tested positive 
for urease, catalase, and oxidase activities. Genomic DNA was extracted, 
amplified using universal primers, and sequenced. The obtained 
sequence showed 96% similarity to Paenibacillus species based on BLAST 
analysis using the NCBI database and phylogenetic tree was constructed 
to determine its relationship with closely related species (Fig. 3).These 
findings are consistent with previous studies reporting similar pheno-
typic and genotypic characteristics of Paenibacillus species.68,69
3.3. Tests on soil
3.3.1. Particle size distribution
The sieve analysis of the soil sample was conducted as per the 
standards outlined in IS 2720:1985 to determine the particle size dis-
tribution of the sample. The results presented in Fig. 4 indicate that, 
among the five sites, the bacterial isolate obtained from Site 1 exhibited 
the most promising performance among other isolated. The corre-
sponding soil sample was analyzed and found to contain 51.78% silt and 
clay, indicating that more than 50% of the soil fraction consists of fine 
particles by mass, which is well within the range as per IS 2117:1991. 
This indicates that the soil is predominantly fine-grained, with a sig-
nificant portion of silt and clay. On the other hand, the sand content in 
the sample was found to be 46.97%, which is well within the typical 
range of 35–50%, suggesting that the soil has a moderate sand content. 
The remaining 1.25% indicates a very minimal gravel presence. These 
results justify with the earlier scientific reports and meet the standard 
criteria, revealing a balanced composition with fine-grained particles 
and a moderate sand content, which can influence its texture, perme-
ability, and suitability for various applications such as sustainable 
construction earthen material.70–72
3.3.2. Plasticity index
To determine the Plasticity index (PI), soil sample was determined 
for Atterberg limits (Liquid limit and Plastic limit) in accordance with 
the standards set by IS 2720:1985. The result for the Liquid limit of soil 
sample was found to be 32.93% and Plastic limit was determined to be 
17.91% (Fig. 5). The PI was calculated as 15.02%, which is well within 
the permissible range of 15–25%. This indicates that the soil has a 
moderate plasticity and is less prone to fluid behaviour under varying 
moisture content. The results display moderate plasticity, as the PI 
which describes the moisture content at which the soil remains plastic. A 
PI of 15.02% suggests that the soil is workable within this moisture 
range and has a relatively stable texture, making it suitable for various 
engineering and construction applications where soil plasticity plays a 
significant role in compaction and stability.51,73
3.4. Physical properties
The bio-brick was assessed for physical properties with standard 
tools. In order to evaluate the hardness, a sharp tool was used to make 
impression, the results showed no scratches. In similar terms, colour of 
the bio-brick was studied which showed standard uniform bright colour 
S. Roopanjali et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                             Journal of Genetic Engineering and Biotechnology 24 (2026) 100789 
8

across all the surface area. Similarly, the breakage test was conducted to 
check the bell ringing sound which was produced without breakage. 
These physical test results are satisfactory and meets the standards as per 
IS 1077–1992 and IS 2180–1988, and these results also align with the 
previous scientific reports.55,56
3.4.1. Compressive strength
An increase in compressive strength of 7.15% within the initial 7 
days suggests that a significant portion of strength development in the 
bio-bricks occurs during the early curing period. This behaviour in-
dicates that mineralization and precipitation are most active in the 
initial stages, contributing to rapid strength gain. Consequently, if 
adequate strength is not achieved within the first few days, it is unlikely 
that substantial improvements will occur at later ages. The increase in 
compressive strength from 4.19 MPa to 4.49 MPa represents an 
improvement of approximately 7.15%. While this enhancement may 
appear modest from an engineering perspective, it is important to 
consider the context of bio-mediated construction materials. The 
observed strength value of 4.49 MPa falls within the range reported for 
several bio-brick systems. Notably, a previous study employing ureolytic 
Bacillus species isolated from Iran reported an increase in compressive 
strength of approximately 3% following bacterial treatment.74
Compared to this finding, the 7.15% improvement observed in the 
present study is more than two-fold higher, indicating a comparatively 
greater strengthening effect. However, further investigations are 
required to comprehensively assess the long-term performance and 
durability characteristics, as well as to determine whether continued 
mineralization or secondary processes contribute to additional strength 
development over time and these parameters become the subject of in-
terest in future studies. This reduction suggests improved resistance to 
environmental moisture variations, which is critical for long-term 
durability in field conditions.
The increase in compressive strength from 4.19 to 4.49 MPa (~7.1%) 
was evaluated using an independent sample t-test, which confirmed that 
the difference is statistically significant (t = 28.5, df = 4, p < 0.001). 
Despite the modest percentage increase, the low variability (SD ≤ 0.015 
MPa) indicates consistent material performance, enhancing reliability. 
From a practical perspective, even incremental improvements in 
compressive strength can contribute to better load-bearing capacity and 
durability in masonry applications. Furthermore, the obtained values 
fall within the lower range of conventional fired bricks (typically 5–20 
MPa).54,75 The compressive strength obtained in this study indicates the 
potential suitability of the bio-bricks for non-load-bearing wall appli-
cations. However, further research is required to optimize key param-
eters influencing strength development, including bacterial activity, 
curing conditions, and material composition. Such studies may lead to 
improved compressive strength and broaden the range of practical ap-
plications of these sustainable construction materials.
3.4.2. Water absorption
The absorption capacity of conventional brick was found to be 
10.75% (Fig. 7), which is in compliance with the standards set by IS 
1077:1992. This indicates that the conventional brick performs within 
the acceptable range for water absorption. In contrast, the bio-brick 
demonstrated a lower water absorption value of 8.52%, which is 
significantly less than that of the conventional brick. The bio-bricks 
exhibits a 20.74% reduction in water absorption compared to conven-
tional bricks. This reduced water absorption in bio-bricks suggests 
improved resistance to moisture uptake, potentially enhancing their 
durability and performance in conditions where moisture variation is a 
concern. The lower water absorption could be attributed to the 
composition of the bio-brick, and effects of microbial activity, leading to 
a more compact structure that limits water penetration. The obtained 
results coincide with the previous scientific reports.54,76
3.5. Physicochemical properties of the bio-brick
3.5.1. FTIR, XRD and DLS analysis
The FTIR analysis of the sample revealed several key functional 
groups, providing valuable insights into its chemical composition. A 
prominent vibrational stretch observed at different frequencies are re-
ported and identified in Table 1. and Fig. 8. The identification of the 
functional groups showed the presence of alkenes or aromatic com-
pounds. These vibrational stretches were identified based on earlier 
reports.76,77 Further, it was also displayed the presence of silica or 
silicate-based compounds.60,61 Overall, the FTIR spectrum confirms the 
presence of functional groups that reflect both organic and inorganic 
components within the sample, providing a comprehensive under-
standing of its metabolic diversity. The XRD diffractogram (Fig. 9) dis-
played the presence of 20.99◦ and 26.75◦ which corresponds to quartz 
(SiO2), 45.87◦ might corresponds to Albite (NaAlSi3O6) whereas 50.27◦, 
60.64◦, 67.87◦, 69.55◦, and 75.75◦, which exhibit a strong resemblance 
to the diffraction patterns typically associated with silica based com-
pounds as per the previous studies.60,61 These peaks are characteristic of 
the well-known crystallographic planes of silica, suggesting that the 
sample contains a significant proportion of silica or silica-based com-
pounds.61 Phase identification was performed by comparing the ob-
tained diffraction patterns with standard reference data from the 
International Centre for Diffraction Data (ICDD) database (PDF-4+). The 
observed reflections were matched with calcite (CaCO₃, ICDD PDF No. 
00–005-0586; alternative updated card 01–083-0578, trigonal system, 
R-3c), vaterite (CaCO₃, ICDD PDF No. 00–033-0268), aragonite (CaCO₃, 
ICDD PDF No. 00–041-1475, orthorhombic phase), quartz (SiO₂, ICDD 
PDF No. 00–046-1045; alternative 01–085-0796), and portlandite (Ca 
(OH)₂, ICDD PDF No. 00–044-1481). The strong diffraction peak 
observed near 26.6–26.7◦ corresponds to the (101) plane of quartz, 
while the prominent reflection at approximately 29.4◦ is attributed to 
the (104) plane of trigonal calcite. Additional characteristic peaks of 
vaterite (~24.9◦, 27.0◦, 32.7◦), aragonite (~26.2◦, 27.2◦, 33.1◦, 45.9◦), 
and portlandite (~18.0◦, 34.1◦, 47.1◦) further confirm the presence of 
these crystalline phases in accordance with standard ICDD reference 
patterns. The size distribution of particles was studied with DLS analysis 
which showed the presence of particles at micro and macro size with 
highest number of particles showing in the range of 1000 nm as shown in 
the Fig. 10. This suggests a heterogeneous particle size distribution, 
which can arise from particle aggregation or the presence of multiple 
populations in the sample. In many biosynthesized or bio-derived par-
ticulate systems, such broad size distributions are common due to 
incomplete stabilization and natural polydispersity. For example, ag-
gregates of nanoparticles can form when stabilizing agents are weak or 
absent, leading to populations in the sub-micron to micron range. The 
technique estimates particle size by analyzing fluctuations in scattered 
light intensity arising from the Brownian motion of particles in 
suspension.78
3.5.2. Morphological and elementary analysis
The SEM analysis of the bio-brick revealed the presence of bacterial 
colonies adhering to the surface of the particles as shown in the Fig. 11a. 
The bio-brick particles exhibited a distinct microbial growth, with 
bacteria visible in clustered formations on the surface, indicating active 
colonization. In contrast, the SEM analysis of the control brick showed 
no such bacterial presence or microbial growth on its surface. The 
control brick particles appeared smooth and devoid of any microbial 
attachment, suggesting that the bio-brick's composition or surface 
properties may be more conducive to bacterial colonization. Further 
elementary analysis of the bio brick showed the predominant elements 
like silica (46.83%) and oxygen (27.58%), with a substantial presence of 
aluminum (14.55%), while the control sample shows slightly lower 
silicon (32.43%) and oxygen (28.91%) content. The control sample 
contains additional elements such as nitrogen (1.12%) and titanium 
(6.00%), which are not present in the bio-brick. The presence of 
S. Roopanjali et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                             Journal of Genetic Engineering and Biotechnology 24 (2026) 100789 
9

titanium (commonly occurring as stable minerals such as TiO₂) in the 
control but not in the bio-brick may be due to heterogeneous distribu-
tion of mineral phases or surface-level detection differences in tech-
niques like EDS, where microbial precipitation layers in bio-bricks can 
obscure underlying elements. Additionally, the bio-brick contains 
magnesium (0.75%) and potassium (2.08%), as shown in Fig. 11b. 
Although microbially induced calcite precipitation (MICP) is common in 
ureolytic systems, the dominant diffraction peak at ~26.6◦ corre-
sponding to quartz (SiO₂) indicates that silica precipitation may repre-
sent a parallel or alternative mineralization pathway in the present 
study. The alkaline microenvironment generated during bacterial 
metabolism, particularly urea hydrolysis, enhances silicate dissolution 
and promotes supersaturation of silicic acid (Si(OH)₄), which subse-
quently undergoes polycondensation to form amorphous or crystalline 
silica. Negatively charged functional groups in extracellular polymeric 
substances (EPS) act as nucleation sites, facilitating silicate adsorption 
and templated silica deposition on bacterial surfaces. Thus, minerali-
zation likely proceeds through a dual mechanism involving limited 
carbonate precipitation alongside dominant EPS-mediated silica bio-
mineralization driven by silicate dissolution–reprecipitation dynamics. 
The improved compressive strength observed in the treated bio-bricks 
may be associated with several factors, including microbial activity, 
possible mineral deposition, and changes in the brick microstructure. 
Previous studies have reported that bacterial metabolites can facilitate 
mineral precipitation and influence particle binding within construction 
materials. Similarly, silica-rich constituents have been suggested to 
contribute to matrix densification and improved mechanical properties. 
Further studies will be conducted in order to elucidate the exact 
mechanisms involved in biomineralization.
3.5.3. Metabolic profiling
The metabolic profiling of the selected isolate was conducted to 
understand its biosynthetic potential. During this process, the organic 
moieties were dried to obtain crude metabolites or extract which was 
partially purified to separate metabolites using TLC which revealed 
multiple bands corresponding to different metabolites. Prominent bands 
were observed at distinct Rf values of 0.3, 0.4, 0.6, and 0.7, as shown in 
Fig. 12a. The GC–MS analysis depicted the mass and predicted the na-
ture of the metabolites secreted by the bacterium. GC–MS results indi-
cated the presence of various metabolites at different retention times, as 
illustrated in Fig. 12b. Some of the major metabolites identified are 
Dodecyl acrylate, Ethanol, 2-(octadecyloxy), 4-Trifluoroacetoxyhexade-
cane, Heptacosane, 2,4 + Di-tert-butylphenol as shown in the Fig. 12c. 
The complete metabolic profiling is cited in supplementary file 1. These 
metabolites provide valuable insights into the isolate's metabolic 
pathways.
The identified metabolites are likely to play a functional role in bio- 
brick formation by influencing mineralization processes, binding in-
teractions, and surface properties. Compounds such as dodecyl acrylate 
and long-chain hydrocarbons (e.g., heptacosane) may contribute to 
improved adhesion and hydrophobicity, while phenolic compounds like 
2,4-di-tert-butylphenol may facilitate biochemical interactions involved 
in mineral precipitation. Thus, metabolic profiling provides a mecha-
nistic link between microbial activity and the enhanced physicochem-
ical and mechanical properties observed in the developed bio-bricks. 
The observed improvement in compressive strength and reduced water 
absorption in the developed bio-bricks aligns with previous studies 
emphasizing sustainable alternatives to conventional clay bricks. For 
instance, demonstrated that incorporating auxiliary wastes can effec-
tively enhance brick performance while conserving natural clay re-
sources. Similarly, Ikechukwu and Onyelowe (2021) reported that 
blended materials improve durability and environmental sustainability, 
supporting the potential of bio-mediated approaches in eco-friendly 
masonry production. This diversity of metabolites aligns with the 
well-documented biochemical versatility of Paenibacillus species, which 
are known to produce a wide range of compounds influencing 
environmental interactions and material properties.79 From a sustain-
ability perspective, the developed bio-bricks offer a potential eco- 
friendly alternative to traditional fired bricks by reducing energy con-
sumption and associated carbon emissions, aligning with previous 
studies that emphasize the use of alternative materials to conserve 
natural clay resources and promote sustainable masonry production 
Furthermore, the use of naturally occurring bacteria suggests the feasi-
bility of scalable and environmentally compatible production processes 
for construction applications, consistent with sustainable material 
development approaches reported in recent studies.80,81
4. Conclusion
This study successfully demonstrated the production of bio-bricks 
using ureolytic bacteria, with a quantified improvement in compres-
sive strength of 7.15% (from 4.19 MPa to 4.49 MPa) along with reduced 
water absorption and enhanced physicochemical properties compared 
to conventional bricks. The isolation and screening process led to the 
identification of Paenibacillus species as a promising candidate for 
microbial-mediated construction material synthesis. The isolate 
exhibited active growth, with an OD₆₀₀ value of 0.447 compared to 0.05 
in the control, and was characterized as a Gram-positive, motile bacte-
rium with positive urease, catalase, and oxidase activities. Molecular 
identification confirmed 96% similarity with Paenibacillus Species sup-
porting its reliability and relevance for bio-brick production. The 
improved performance of the bio-bricks is attributed to microbially 
induced mineralization and metabolite-mediated interactions, as sup-
ported by FTIR, XRD, SEM, and EDS analyses, highlighting the role of 
silica enrichment and bacterial colonization in enhancing structural 
integrity and durability. However, certain limitations exist, including 
the lack of long-term durability assessment under environmental stress 
conditions, potential variability in microbial activity, and the need for 
validation at larger scales. Additionally, economic feasibility and cost- 
effectiveness require further evaluation for real-world implementation. 
Future research should therefore focus on durability testing under field 
conditions, process optimization for large-scale production, and 
comprehensive cost–benefit analysis to support industrial adoption. This 
study contributes to theoretical understanding in the field of microbial- 
based construction materials by introducing Paenibacillus species as a 
bio-resource and establishing a mechanistic link between microbial 
metabolism and enhanced material properties. Overall, the findings 
provide a foundation for the development of sustainable, durable, and 
cost-effective bio-bricks for next-generation construction practices.
