responsible for its medicinal potential.7 Many bioactive compounds, 
including flavonoids, phenolic compounds, alkaloids, glucosinolates, 
isothiocyanates, terpenoids, volatile compounds, fatty acids, proteins, 
amino acids, polysaccharides, vitamins, minerals, tannins, saponins, 
steroids, and glycosides, have been found through phytochemical 
studies.8 Brassicaceae plants are characterized by glucosinolates and 
their enzymatic breakdown products, particularly isothiocyanates, 
which support cellular defense, antibacterial, chemoprotective, and 
antioxidant properties.9 The nutritional and pharmacological relevance 
of Lepidium sativum is further enhanced by the inclusion of fatty acids, 
such as α-linolenic acid, linoleic acid, and oleic acid, as well as proteins, 
amino acids, mucilaginous polysaccharides, vitamins, and minerals.10
Lepidium sativum's various phytoconstituents contribute to its broad 
range of pharmacological effects through cooperative interactions with 
several biological targets and signaling pathways. A few of the many 
pharmacological activities that Lepidium sativum exhibits are antioxi-
dant, anti-inflammatory, antibacterial, metabolic, protective, analgesic, 
wound-healing, immunomodulatory, neuroprotective, and reproductive 
health-promoting activity.11 Some of the mechanisms that mediate these 
effects are reduction of oxidative stress, suppression of inflammatory 
mediators, immune response regulation, improvement of glucose and 
lipid metabolism, enhancement of antioxidant defense systems, protec-
tion of critical organs, and preservation of cellular homeostasis.7
Despite Lepidium sativum's traditional use, more recent developments 
in phytochemistry, molecular pharmacology, and biomedical research 
have shed light on the plant's medicinal potential.6 Its potential as a 
source of functional foods, nutraceuticals, and plant-derived therapeutic 
agents is highlighted by nutritional components and bioactive secondary 
metabolites, which provide scientific support for its ethnomedical ap-
plications.12 Additionally, Lepidium sativum reduces oxidative stress, 
inflammation, microbial growth, metabolic imbalance, and tissue 
damage, offering prospects for the identification of multifunctional 
natural products.6 Therefore, to determine its therapeutic efficacy and 
support the logical development of new plant-based therapies, a thor-
ough examination of its phytochemical composition, molecular mech-
anisms, pharmacological activities, safety features, and clinical 
significance is necessary.
2. Materials and methods
2.1. Review design and methodological framework
This narrative review synthesized the phytochemical profile, phar-
macological activities, molecular mechanisms, safety profile, and ther-
apeutic potential of Lepidium sativum. To increase methodological 
transparency, scientific rigor, and repeatability, the review process was 
created and reported in accordance with the criteria of the Scale for the 
Assessment of Narrative Review Articles (SANRA).13
2.2. Literature search strategy
A thorough literature search was conducted via electronic databases 
such as PubMed/MEDLINE, Scopus, Web of Science, and Google 
Scholar. To find pertinent papers about Lepidium sativum, a search was 
conducted from 2000 to 2026. “Lepidium sativum”, “garden cress”, 
“traditional medicine”, “ethnopharmacology”, “phytochemistry”, 
“phytochemical constituents”, “bioactive compounds”, “glucosinolates”, 
“flavonoids”, “phenolic compounds”, “pharmacological activities”, 
“molecular mechanisms”, and “therapeutic potential” were among the 
search terms that were created based on the review's objectives. The 
search results were optimized and refined using Boolean operators 
(AND, OR).
2.3. Eligibility criteria
Publications were chosen based on how well they addressed the 
review's goals. Peer-reviewed original research articles, review articles, 
and other scientifically significant publications outlining Lepidium sat-
ivum's traditional value, phytochemical profile, pharmacological activ-
ity, mechanisms of action, and safety features were all considered 
eligible sources. Regardless of the study paradigm, publications that 
offered significant evidence of the plant's medicinal potential were 
included. Studies without Lepidium sativum, duplicate records, inacces-
sible full-text articles, non-scientific reports, or publications with 
insufficient details about the phytochemistry, biological activities, or 
medicinal significance of the plant were all excluded.
2.4. Data extraction and organization
In accordance with predetermined theme areas, such as traditional 
value, phytochemical constituents, pharmacological activities, molecu-
lar targets and signaling pathways, and toxicological considerations, 
pertinent data were separately extracted and arranged.
2.5. Evidence synthesis
A narrative technique was used to synthesize the gathered evidence 
qualitatively. The findings were critically analyzed by combining data 
from phytochemical analyses, pharmacological research, and ethnobo-
tanical studies. The chemical structures were searched from PubChem 
(https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/substance/481107917).
2.6. Quality assurance and limitations
Based on study design, scientific validity, consistency of findings, 
and contribution to the review's goals, the methodological quality and 
applicability of the collected literature were critically examined. Since 
this study was a narrative review rather than a quantitative data pooling 
or meta-analysis, the results were interpreted with heterogeneity among 
studies, variations in extraction techniques, plant parts, geographical 
origins, and experimental models.
3. Traditional and ethnomedicinal uses of Lepidium sativum
Lepidium sativum L. has long been used traditionally in many cultures, 
especially in Ayurvedic, Unani, and folk medicinal systems. Several 
plant parts, particularly seeds, leaves, and aerial parts, have been used to 
treat a variety of medical ailments because of their nutritional and 
bioactive compounds.11,12 Traditionally, Lepidium sativum seeds have 
been used as a carminative, appetite stimulant, and digestive aid to 
alleviate gastrointestinal pain, such as indigestion, constipation, and 
flatulence.3 Soaked seeds are traditionally taken for cough, sore throat, 
bronchitis, and other respiratory issues because of their mucilaginous 
qualities, which also make them a popular demulcent to relieve irritated 
mucous membranes.10 Lepidium sativum seeds are used in traditional 
medicine to increase mucus clearance and reduce symptoms related to 
asthma and respiratory congestion, due to their warming and expecto-
rant properties.10 According to Vazifeh et al.,7 the plant's anti- 
inflammatory properties have been used for inflammatory diseases.
Lepidium sativum has long been used in women's health, especially to 
encourage uterine contractions after childbirth and support lactation.3
In some traditional cultures, seeds are taken as a nutritional supplement 
and galactagogue during the postpartum phase.3 Additionally, the 
leaves and seeds are used to treat skin disorders and wounds. Plant 
medicines are traditionally used to treat mild infections, promote wound 
healing, and lower skin irritation.10,14 Its value in traditional treatments 
for microbial diseases has been supported by its antibacterial proper-
ties.14 Additionally, Lepidium sativum has long been used to treat lipid 
imbalance and hyperglycemia, among other metabolic problems.6 It is a 
dietary supplement used to boost immunity, improve general health, 
and give nutritional support because it contains proteins, minerals, vi-
tamins, fatty acids, and phytochemicals.12 Lepidium sativum is used as a 
G. Bereda                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         Journal of Genetic Engineering and Biotechnology 24 (2026) 100795 
2

tonic to boost general vitality, treat fever, and relieve pain in various 
traditional systems.3 In some cultures, the seeds are also administered 
topically to relieve muscle and joint pain.15 The major morphological 
parts of Lepidium sativum L. traditionally used for medicinal purposes are 
shown in Fig. 1.
4. Phytochemistry of Lepidium sativum
Lepidium sativum L. is a phytochemically varied medicinal plant. Its 
nutritional, biological, and therapeutic properties are attributed to a 
wide variety of primary and secondary metabolites.12 The plant portion 
(seeds, leaves, stems, and roots), geographic origin, environmental 
factors, growing conditions, and extraction techniques all affect Lepi-
dium sativum's phytochemical content.3 It contains numerous bioactive 
compounds, such as alkaloids, flavonoids, phenolic compounds, terpe-
noids, volatile oils, fatty acids, proteins, amino acids, polysaccharides, 
vitamins, minerals, and glycosidic compounds12 (Fig. 2).
Lepidium sativum's alkaloids are secondary metabolites that contain 
nitrogen and enhance its medicinal activity. These compounds have 
been linked to antioxidant, anti-inflammatory, antibacterial, and anal-
gesic properties by modifying oxidative stress and inflammatory re-
actions.16 Garden cress's primary polyphenolic constituents are 
flavonoids, which include kaempferol, quercetin, and their glycosidic 
derivatives.3 These compounds demonstrate potent antioxidant activity 
and help prevent oxidative and inflammatory disorders by scavenging 
reactive oxygen and nitrogen species, boosting endogenous antioxidant 
defense systems, and controlling inflammatory pathways, especially 
nuclear factor kappa B (NF-κB).6
Lepidium sativum is rich in phenolic compounds, such as phenolic 
acids and related polyphenols, which significantly increase its antioxi-
dant ability.7 These compounds protect biological components from 
oxidative damage by neutralizing free radicals, reducing lipid peroxi-
dation, and preserving redox equilibrium. Additionally, phenolic com-
ponents promote the plant's therapeutic significance by contributing to 
antibacterial, anti-inflammatory, and tissue-protective properties.17
Lepidium sativum is distinguished by its sulfur-containing glucosinolates, 
which myrosinase hydrolyzes enzymatically to produce physiologically 
active isothiocyanates.18 These metabolites have a significant role in the 
plant's chemoprotective, antioxidant, antibacterial, and anti- 
inflammatory properties. Isothiocyanates regulate cellular defense sys-
tems by triggering antioxidant response pathways, modifying detoxifi-
cation enzymes, and controlling inflammatory processes.19
Lepidium sativum's distinctive scent and biological activity are influ-
enced by terpenoids and volatile components. These compounds have 
shown antibacterial, antioxidant, and anti-inflammatory properties. 
They may help protect the respiratory system by regulating smooth 
muscle reactions and airway inflammation.20 The plant's pharmaco-
logical potential is further enhanced by the variety of volatile com-
pounds found in the essential oil fraction.3 Garden cress seeds, which are 
very rich in lipid content, contain fatty acids like α-linolenic acid 
(omega-3 fatty acid), linoleic acid (omega-6 fatty acid), oleic acid, and 
other unsaturated fatty acids.2 These fatty acids may reduce inflamma-
tory reactions by reducing lipid-derived mediators involved in immune 
modulation.2
Additionally, significant properties of proteins, essential amino 
acids, and mucilaginous polysaccharides are present in Lepidium sativum 
seeds.2 The polysaccharide fraction's notable swelling and water- 
retention qualities support its conventional uses. These macromole-
cules may also have physiologic, immunomodulatory, and protective 
effects through interactions with cellular and immunological systems.21
Lepidium sativum is a source of vitamins, minerals, and secondary me-
tabolites. It has B-complex vitamins, carotenoids (provitamin A mole-
cules), and vitamin C, all of which support metabolic control and 
antioxidant defense.22 It also contains minerals that contribute to its 
nutritional value, such as calcium, iron, phosphorus, potassium, mag-
nesium, zinc, and other trace elements.23
Tannins, saponins, steroids, and glycosidic substances are other 
phytoconstituents found in Lepidium sativum.10 Tannins contribute to 
antioxidant, antibacterial, and astringent properties, as well as immu-
nomodulatory, anti-inflammatory, and membrane-interacting activ-
ities.7 The plant's many biological functions are also influenced by 
glycosides and steroidal substances.7
5. Pharmacological activities of Lepidium sativum
The many phytoconstituents that give Lepidium sativum L. a wide 
range of pharmacological properties include flavonoids, phenolic com-
pounds, glucosinolates, alkaloids, terpenoids, fatty acids, and poly-
saccharides.3 These bioactive compounds exhibited antioxidant, anti- 
inflammatory, antimicrobial, antidiabetic, hepatoprotective, car-
dioprotective, gastroprotective, analgesic, antipyretic, immunomodu-
latory, anticancer, and respiratory protective properties.6 The major 
pharmacological activities of Lepidium sativum include (Tables 1, 2, 3, & 
4; Fig. 3):
5.1. Antioxidant and cytoprotective activities
5.1.1. Antioxidant activity
Lepidium sativum has strong antioxidant activity due to its high 
concentration of phenolic compounds, flavonoids, glucosinolates, 
Fig. 1. Morphological characteristics of Lepidium sativum L. showing the (A) flowers, (B) pods (fruits), (C) seeds, and (D) leaves.
G. Bereda                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         Journal of Genetic Engineering and Biotechnology 24 (2026) 100795 
3

isothiocyanates, tocopherols, carotenoids, vitamin C, and other phyto-
chemicals.12 These bioactive compounds scavenge reactive oxygen and 
nitrogen species, prevent lipid peroxidation, chelate transition metal 
ions, and strengthen endogenous antioxidant defenses.12 Lepidium sat-
ivum uses these mechanisms to reduce oxidative stress and protect 
cellular lipids, proteins, and Deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) from oxida-
tive damage.64 Chatoui et al.24 conducted an in vitro experimental study 
to evaluate the chemical composition and antioxidant capacity of Lepi-
dium sativum seeds from four regions of Morocco. Seed moisture content 
and oil yield were 9.24–9.88% and 19.13–19.94% dry weight, respec-
tively. The seeds contained fatty acids, sterols, and tocopherols, with 
linolenic acid (33%), oleic acid (23%), β-sitosterol (50%), and tocoph-
erols (1500–1900 mg/kg; mainly γ-tocopherol) as major components. 
Folin–Ciocalteu, 2,2-diphenyl-1-picrylhydrazyl (DPPH) radical scav-
enging assay, 2,2′-azino-bis(3-ethylbenzothiazoline-6-sulfonic acid) 
(ABTS) assay, iron-chelating assay, and Ferric Reducing Antioxidant 
Power (FRAP) assays demonstrated antioxidant activity. The methanol 
extract showed stronger reducing and radical-scavenging activity than 
the ethanol extract. Antioxidant activity was significantly correlated 
with total phenolic content. The study provided a comprehensive anti-
oxidant evaluation, but its in vitro design requires further in vivo and 
clinical confirmation.
Malar et al.25 evaluated the antioxidant activity of ethanolic extracts 
from different parts of Lepidium sativum, such as shoot, leaf, stem, and 
seed, in vitro. Shoot extract showed the highest DPPH radical scav-
enging activity (12.19 ± 0.02%), while stem extract showed the lowest 
(2.69 ± 0.05%). Seed extract had the highest glutathione-S-transferase 
activity (9600 ± 56.3 μg/mL), and leaf extract showed the highest 
reduced glutathione content (9 ± 0.2 μg/mL). All extracts demonstrated 
Fe3+–Fe2+ reducing ability, with shoot extract having the highest 
vitamin C content. However, the study lacked active compound identi-
fication and in vivo validation. Bin Jardan et al.26 investigated the 
antioxidant and cytoprotective effects of Lepidium sativum seed extract 
using in vitro and in vivo models. Chloroform seed extract (5–25 μg/mL) 
protected human hepatocellular carcinoma cells (HepG2) cells against 
hydrogen peroxide (H₂O₂)-induced oxidative stress. At 25 μg/mL, it 
increased cell viability by 48%, reduced reactive oxygen species (ROS) 
generation by 45% and lipid peroxidation by 56%, increased mito-
chondrial membrane potential by 55%, and reduced glutathione levels 
by 46%. The combined cellular and animal models strengthened evi-
dence for antioxidant and protective effects, but further pharmacoki-
netic, safety, and clinical studies are needed.
These studies consistently demonstrate the antioxidant potential of 
Lepidium sativum. However, variations in extraction methods, plant 
parts, antioxidant assays, and experimental models likely explain dif-
ferences in the reported activities.24–26 Although the combined in vitro 
and in vivo approach used by Bin Jardan et al.26 provides stronger 
translational evidence than in vitro studies alone, the current evidence 
remains predominantly preclinical.
5.1.2. Cytoprotective activities
Lepidium sativum demonstrates a significant cytoprotective effect by 
protecting cells and tissues against oxidative stress, inflammation, toxic 
assaults, and metabolic disorders.6 Its glucosinolates, isothiocyanates, 
flavonoids, phenolic acids, alkaloids, saponins, tannins, vitamins, and 
minerals are mostly responsible for this action.12 Lepidium sativum in-
hibits NF-κB-mediated inflammatory signaling, activates the Nuclear 
factor erythroid 2–related factor 2 (Nrf2)/heme oxygenase-1 (HO-1) 
antioxidant pathway, regulates apoptosis by preserving the balance 
between pro- and anti-apoptotic proteins, lowers caspase activation, and 
preserves cellular redox homeostasis.3,65 Desai et al.27 conducted an 
experimental animal study to evaluate the antihyperglycemic and 
cytoprotective effects of Lepidium sativum seed extract (LSE) in obesity- 
associated diabetes. High-fat diet (HFD)-streptozotocin (STZ)-induced 
type 2 diabetic mice were treated with LSE for 28 days. LSE significantly 
reduced blood glucose levels, improved body weight changes, and 
reversed degenerative alterations in hepatic and pancreatic tissues. The 
findings demonstrated the antihyperglycemic and cytoprotective po-
tential of LSE. However, the short study duration and reliance on an 
animal model limit direct clinical applicability.
