5.2. Anti-inflammatory and analgesic activities
5.2.1. Anti-inflammatory activity
The bioactive compounds of Lepidium sativum, such as flavonoids, 
phenolic compounds, glucosinolates, isothiocyanates, alkaloids, tan-
nins, and essential fatty acids, show notable anti-inflammatory activ-
ities.7 These phytochemicals suppressed pro-inflammatory mediators, 
including tumor necrosis factor-α (TNF-α), interleukin (IL)-1β, IL-6, 
cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2), inducible nitric oxide synthase (iNOS), ni-
tric oxide (NO), and prostaglandin E₂ (PGE₂).66 Furthermore, Lepidium 
sativum enhances endogenous antioxidant defenses and inhibits activa-
tion of NF-κB and mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK) signaling 
pathways, reducing inflammation and oxidative stress.7,12 Yahla et al.28
evaluated Lepidium sativum seed ethanolic extract (EELS) in 
carrageenan-induced paw edema in mice (25 ± 5 g). EELS (50 mg/kg) 
reduced edema by 40.44 ± 16.36% after 6 h and improved serum al-
bumin, C-reactive protein (CRP), and plasma fibrinogen versus a 
comparator. The validated model supports anti-inflammatory activity, 
however the single-dose design and acute inflammation model limit 
clinical translation.
Khan et al.29 assessed seed-coat mucilage prepared from Lepidium 
sativum polysaccharides using antioxidant assays and carrageenan- 
induced edema. Fraction F53 showed the highest antioxidant activity 
(EC₅₀ = 57.41 ± 1.34 μg/mL) and anti-inflammatory activity (92.60%) 
compared with diclofenac (91.46%). Gas chromatography–mass spec-
trometry (GC–MS) identified major acidic sugars (galacturonic and 
glucuronic acids) and neutral sugars (rhamnose, arabinose, and 
maltose). Compared with the ethanolic extract, the mucilage fraction 
showed greater anti-inflammatory activity, although both findings 
require mechanistic and clinical validation.
5.2.2. Analgesic (antinociceptive) activity
Lepidium sativum has long been used to treat pain associated with 
inflammatory diseases, musculoskeletal problems, and tissue damage 
because of its strong analgesic (antinociceptive) effect.15 Bioactive 
components such as flavonoids, phenolic compounds, alkaloids, 
glucosinolates, and essential fatty acids are mainly responsible for its 
analgesic effects. These phytochemicals suppress the COX and lip-
oxygenase (LOX) pathways and reduce the synthesis of pro- 
inflammatory mediators, including prostaglandins, TNF-α, IL-1β, and 
IL-6.3 ¨Ozdemir et al.30 evaluated the antinociceptive activity of aqueous 
extract of Lepidium sativum L. in Swiss albino male mice using acetic 
acid-induced writhing and hot plate tests. Three groups (n = 6) received 
the extract (20 mg/kg) 30 min before pain induction, which significantly 
increased reaction time and reduced writhing responses, with a 
maximum 27.0% reduction compared with the comparator and diclo-
fenac sodium (50 mg/kg). The study supports analgesic potential, but 
the small sample size and single dose limit dose–response evaluation.
Raval and Ravishankar31 assessed the analgesic effect of Lepidium 
sativum Linn. seeds in albino rats and Swiss albino mice using acetic 
acid-induced writhing, formaldehyde-induced paw licking, and tail-flick 
tests. Therapeutic and double doses significantly increased latency in 
writhing, inhibited neurogenic pain at the double dose and inflamma-
tory pain at the therapeutic dose, and showed mild-to-moderate tail- 
flick activity. Multiple pain models strengthen the findings, but limited 
mechanistic evaluation and phytochemical analysis restrict identifica-
tion of active constituents. Although both studies support the analgesic 
potential of Lepidium sativum, variations in animal models, extracts, and 
dosing approaches may explain differences in observed effects.30,31
However, the use of acute pain models and limited mechanistic data 
restricts direct translation to human clinical applications.30,31
5.2.3. Antiarthritic activity
Lepidium sativum demonstrates promising antiarthritic properties 
through mechanisms like reducing joint inflammation and tissue dam-
age. Bioactive compounds, including flavonoids, phenolic compounds, 
glucosinolates, alkaloids, and essential fatty acids, which support its 
anti-inflammatory, antioxidant, and immunomodulatory qualities, are 
mostly responsible for its therapeutic effects.7,12 These phytochemicals 
reduce synovial inflammation and limit inflammatory joint damage by 
suppressing the production of pro-inflammatory mediators such as 
prostaglandin E2 (PGE2), IL-1β, IL-6, COX-2, and TNF-α.12,66,67 Lepidium 
