persistent bone/cartilage degeneration, erosion, and inflammation. 
Lower doses showed moderate anti-arthritic effects, whereas higher 
doses had inconsistent responses. The study supports anti-arthritic po-
tential, but limited tissue recovery requires further mechanistic studies. 
Moreover, findings from acute pain models in mice may not fully predict 
efficacy in chronic human pain conditions.
Abdallah et al.33 investigated the effects of Lepidium sativum extract in 
ovariectomized rats at doses of 50 and 100 mg/kg, with 17β-estradiol as a 
comparator. The extract prevented bone loss, improved femur strength, 
increased LDH and osteocalcin, preserved superoxide dismutase (SOD), 
and glutathione peroxidase (GPx), reduced lipid peroxidation, decreased 
C-terminal telopeptide of type I collagen (CTX–I) and TRAP, and 
regulated the Receptor activator of nuclear factor-κB ligand (RANKL)/ 
osteoprotegerin (OPG) pathway. Ultra-High-Performance Liquid Chro-
matography (UHPLC) coupled with Photodiode Array Detection (PDA) 
and Electrospray Ionization–Mass Spectrometry (ESI–MS) identified 
glucosinolates, lignans, coumarins, phenolic acids, and alkaloids. Solid- 
Phase Microextraction (SPME)-GC/MS identified compounds contrib-
uting to osteoprotective activity. The study has strong biochemical and 
metabolomic evidence supporting bone protection, but human clinical 
studies are needed to confirm efficacy and safety. Differences in animal 
species, pain models, and dosing strategies may contribute to variability 
in observed effects.
5.3. Antimicrobial activities
5.3.1. Antibacterial activity
Both Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria are susceptible to 
the broad-spectrum antibacterial activity of Lepidium sativum. Its high 
concentration of glucosinolates, isothiocyanates, flavonoids, alkaloids, 
tannins, phenolic acids, and other bioactive phytochemicals is mainly 
responsible for this antibacterial activity.7,68 These compounds prevent 
the growth of bacteria through a variety of mechanisms, such as 
disruption of bacterial cell membranes, increased membrane perme-
ability, interference with vital metabolic enzymes, inhibition of protein 
and nucleic acid synthesis, and induction of oxidative stress within 
bacterial cells.6,7,68 Besufekad et al.34 evaluated ethanol, methanol, and 
chloroform seed extracts of Lepidium sativum against E. coli (ATCC 
25922), K. pneumoniae (ATCC 27736), P. aeruginosa (ATCC 27853), 
S. aureus (ATCC 25923), and S. sonnei (ATCC 25931). Ethanol and 
methanol extracts showed the highest activity against E. coli (22.63 ±
