Margan et al.35 assessed ethanolic and aqueous seed extracts pre-
pared from Lepidium sativum against 30 multidrug-resistant (MDR) and 
35 sensitive clinical isolates of P. aeruginosa. Activity was observed at 
25–100 mg/mL. Among 130 isolates, 49 (37%) were susceptible, 
including 17/60 (28.3%) MDR and 32/70 (45.7%) sensitive isolates. 
Aqueous extract showed higher activity than ethanolic extract (43/65, 
66% vs. 6/65, 9%). The small number of isolated compounds and the 
lack of mechanistic and phytochemical analyses limited the interpreta-
tion and clinical relevance of the findings.36 identified Lepidium sativum 
leaf targeting methicillin-resistant staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) 
penicillin-binding protein 2a (PBP2a) using in vitro and in silico ap-
proaches. GC–MS identified 5 phytochemicals, and 2 compounds 
showed docking scores of ␀ 7.1 and ␀ 6.3 kcal/mol. In vitro activity 
showed MIC of 15 μg/mL, inhibition zones of 12.3 ± 0.5–19 ± 0.5 mm, 
and significant concentration-dependent effects. Despite promising 
findings, in vivo validation, pharmacokinetic, and safety studies are 
required for clinical translation.
5.3.2. Antifungal activity
The bioactive compounds of Lepidium sativum, such as flavonoids, 
phenolic compounds, alkaloids, terpenoids, glucosinolates, and essential 
oil, are primarily responsible for its antifungal property.2 These phyto-
chemicals contribute to the prevention of fungal growth through a va-
riety of mechanisms, such as disruption of fungal cell membrane 
integrity, interference with ergosterol biosynthesis, inhibition of 
oxidative stress responses, and reduction of fungal enzyme activity.37,69
Its antioxidant and anti-inflammatory properties may improve host de-
fense mechanisms against fungal infections by regulating oxidative 
stress and inflammatory responses.7 Yassin et al.37 evaluated Lepidium 
sativum-mediated zinc oxide nanoparticles (ZnO-NPs), which have a 32 
nm hexagonal wurtzite structure and dose-dependent antifungal activity 
against Candida albicans and C. tropicalis with MICs of 125 μg/mL and 
62.5 μg/mL, respectively. ZnO-NPs with nystatin showed synergistic 
effects with fold area values of 0.85 and 0.99 and fractional inhibitory 
concentration index (FICI) values of 0.38 and 0.25. The nanoparticles 
also demonstrated antioxidant activity (IC₅₀: 335.48 μg/mL) and selec-
tive cytotoxicity against Human hepatoma cell line 7 (Huh7) cells (IC₅₀: 
145.2 μg/mL) compared with WI38 cells (IC₅₀: 237.6 μg/mL), supporting 
their potential as antifungal agents and nystatin enhancers. However, 
these in vitro findings require validation in in vivo models, as nano-
particle behavior and safety may differ under physiological conditions.
5.4. Anticancer activities
Lepidium sativum has a variety of bioactive compounds, including 
glucosinolates, isothiocyanates, flavonoids, phenolic compounds, and 
alkaloids, which are thought to have anticancer potential.12 These 
phytochemicals contribute to anticancer effects through a variety of 
mechanisms, such as suppression of angiogenesis, induction of 
apoptosis, inhibition of cancer cell proliferation, and modulation of 
signaling pathways involved in tumor development and progression.6,29
Lepidium sativum's bioactive compounds can improve detoxification 
pathways, control cell cycle progression, and affect molecular targets 
like NF-κB, phosphatidylinositol 3-kinase/protein kinase B (PI3K/Akt), 
and apoptotic regulators, all of which support the plant's potential role 
in cancer management and prevention.70 Ibrahim et al.38 conducted an 
in vitro study on Lepidium sativum cell cultures. Tyrosine and methionine 
increased glucosinolate accumulation, with 1H-Cyclopenta(b)pyridine- 
3‑carbonitrile-4,5,6,7-tetrahydro-2-methylthio-4 spirocyclohexane as 
the major glucosinolate. Petroleum ether fractions contained 15 com-
pounds, mainly benzene-1,3,5-trimethyl (12.99%) in roots and benzene- 
2-ethyl-1,4-dimethyl (10.66%) in leaves. Extracts inhibited PC3, A-549, 
Caco-2, and HepG2 cells; leaf extract showed the highest activity against 
HepG2 (IC₅₀ = 47.5 μg/mL) through BAX increase, B-cell lymphoma 2 
(BCL2) reduction, caspase-3 activation, and S-phase arrest. The study 
provided strong mechanistic evidence. However, in vitro findings may 
not fully reflect human responses, and the lack of normal cell and in vivo 
models limits translational interpretation.
Watyan et al.39 conducted an in vitro molecular study of Lepidium 
sativum leaf extract against HCT116 and SW620 cells. The extract 
reduced viability dose-dependently, altered 84 DNA damage and repair 
genes, increased DNA damage-inducible transcript 3 (DDIT3) and 
growth arrest and DNA damage-inducible gamma (GADD45G), and 
decreased miles per gallon (MPG) expression. GC–MS identified 10 
compounds with favorable Absorption, Distribution, Metabolism, 
Excretion, and Toxicity (ADMET) properties and Lipinski's Rule of Five 
adherence. Although molecular findings support anticancer potential, 
computational predictions require experimental validation, and cell 
models have limited ability to represent tumor complexity. Hussien and 
Alsulami40 conducted an in vivo study using Azoxymethane (AOM)/ 
dextran sulfate sodium (DSS)-induced colorectal cancer in male mice. 
L. sativum seed extract (200 mg/kg and 400 mg/kg) reduced tumor 
incidence, polyp number, tumor size, and tissue damage, increased p53 
