expression, promoted apoptosis, reduced phenolic compound extract 
(PCE) micronucleus formation, improved the PCE/normochromatic 
erythrocyte ratio, and decreased sperm abnormalities. Despite stronger 
translational relevance than in vitro studies, species differences and 
limited pharmacokinetic data restrict direct clinical application.
5.5. Metabolic and endocrine protective activities
5.5.1. Antidiabetic activity
Lepidium sativum demonstrates remarkable antidiabetic potential 
through pathways involving glucose metabolism, insulin sensitivity, 
oxidative stress management, and pancreatic β-cell protection.6,12 The 
plant may lower postprandial glucose spikes, improve insulin signaling, 
block enzymes that break down carbohydrates like α-amylase and 
α-glucosidase, and increase glucose absorption.33 Moreover, its antiox-
idant properties reduce the outcomes of diabetes and pancreatic 
dysfunction linked to oxidative stress. Additionally, Lepidium sativum 
affects lipid metabolism, improving metabolic regulation and prevent-
ing irregularities associated with diabetes.8 Malar et al.41 evaluated 
ethanolic extracts of shoot, leaf, stem, and seed of Lepidium sativum in 
alloxan-induced diabetic rats. Acute toxicity showed no mortality up to 
1 g/kg. Blood glucose was evaluated on days 0, 7, 14, 24, and 28. Seed 
and leaf extracts significantly reduced fasting blood glucose levels 
comparable to metformin, whereas stem extract showed no significant 
effect, and whole plant extract showed moderate activity. Variations 
among plant parts suggest differences in bioactive compounds, but 
limited phytochemical analysis restricted mechanism interpretation.
Sobhi et al.42 studied Lepidium sativum seeds using in vitro and in 
vivo models. Aqueous and organic fractions inhibited α-glucosidase with 
IC₅₀ values of 300 and 9.375 μg/mL, respectively, compared with acar-
bose (388 μg/mL). Seed oil (100 mg/kg for 21 days) reduced blood 
glucose in STZ-induced diabetic Bagg Albino Laboratory-bred (BALB)/c 
mice (>400 mg/dL), increased glutathione (GSH), and reduced xanthine 
oxidase activity. The combined enzyme, animal, and oxidative assess-
ments supported mechanisms, but human studies are lacking. Mishra 
et al.43 evaluated the extract of Lepidium sativum in 30 Wistar rats with 
STZ-induced diabetes (45 mg/kg, i.p.). Extract (20 mg/kg for 16 days) 
reduced glucose, creatinine, alkaline phosphatase, cholesterol, and 
malondialdehyde, while improving DPPH and FRAP activities. Howev-
er, the small sample size, short duration, and animal model limit 
translation to human diabetes.
Shukla et al.44 evaluated total alkaloids from Lepidium sativum seed, 
lepidine, and semilepidine in alloxan-induced diabetic rats. Lepidium 
sativum seed extract (LSTA) (50, 150, and 250 mg/kg for 21 days) at 250 
mg/kg significantly reduced blood glucose, cholesterol, triglycerides, 
and urea. Body weight increased by 1.94% compared with 6.14% in the 
normal control and 8.94% in the diabetic control. Dose evaluation and 
biochemical findings supported activity. Although dose-dependent ef-
fects were observed, molecular mechanisms and clinical relevance 
remain unclear. Eddouks et al.45 investigated the aqueous extract of 
Lepidium sativum seed in normal and STZ-induced diabetic rats. The 
extract (20 mg/kg) significantly reduced blood glucose and normalized 
levels after 2 weeks. Reduction occurred in normal rats (acute and 
chronic) without changes in plasma insulin, showing an insulin- 
independent effect. The study supported glucose-lowering activity, but 
the molecular pathway remains unclear.
5.5.2. Thyroid-protective activity
Lepidium sativum has a rich phytochemical profile, including flavo-
noids, phenolic compounds, alkaloids, and other antioxidant com-
pounds that may have thyroid-protective effects.2 These bioactive 
compounds may support thyroid function by reducing oxidative stress, 
decreasing inflammatory reactions, and protecting thyroid tissue from 
cellular damage. Lepidium sativum extract may regulate hypothyroidism 
by enhancing thyroid-related biochemical parameters and protecting 
thyroid tissue from oxidative damage.71 Furthermore, Lepidium sativum 
may enhance overall physiological balance by endocrine control 
through metabolic pathways linked to hormone synthesis, cellular 
communication, and steroidogenic activity.2,72 Sciarrillo et al.46 evalu-
ated ethanolic Lepidium sativum L. seed extract in male Wistar rats 
divided into 4 groups: control, Lepidium sativum extract (200 mg/kg i.p. 
for 21 days), T3-treated (25 μg/100 g i.p. for 21 days), and iopanoic 
acid-treated (100 mg/kg for 21 days). The extract significantly 
decreased serum T3 and T4 and increased thyroid-stimulating hormone 
(TSH) levels. Histology showed capillary congestion and dilation, 
cellular infiltration, follicular disintegration, and vacuolar degenera-
tion. However, differences in experimental conditions and limited 
mechanistic evidence restrict interpretation. The study confirmed 
thyroid-inhibitory activity, but its interpretation was limited by the use 
of male rats, a single-dose design, and the lack of mechanistic and 
clinical validation. Translation to humans remains uncertain due to 
limited pharmacokinetic and clinical data.
5.6. Cardiovascular protective activities
5.6.1. Cardioprotective activity
The bioactive compounds of Lepidium sativum, such as flavonoids, 
phenolic compounds, alkaloids, glucosinolates, and essential fatty acids, 
are primarily responsible for the plant's possible cardioprotective ef-
fects.14 It supports cardiovascular health by lowering oxidative stress, 
strengthening antioxidant defense mechanisms, and stopping lipid per-
oxidation.73 Lepidium sativum has been linked to decreased vascular 
oxidative damage, enhanced endothelial function, and defense against 
structural and functional changes in the heart.647 evaluated Lepidium 
sativum seed powder against 5-fluorouracil (5-FU)-induced cardiotox-
icity in albino rats. Rats received saline (control), 5-FU (150 mg/kg, 
intraperitoneally on day 5), or LS (550 mg/kg/day orally for 8 days) 
with 5-FU. 5-FU increased cardiac troponin I (cTnI), creatine kin-
ase–myocardial band (CK-MB), lipid profile, cardiac malondialdehyde 
(MDA), myeloperoxidase (MPO), and IL-1β and decreased high-density 
lipoprotein cholesterol (HDL–C) and cardiac GSH. LS significantly 
reversed these changes through antioxidant and anti-inflammatory ef-
fects, demonstrating cardioprotective potential. However, translation to 
humans remains uncertain due to limitations of the animal model and 
the absence of clinical evidence.
5.6.2. Antihypertensive activity
The bioactive compounds of Lepidium sativum, including flavonoids, 
phenolic compounds, alkaloids, and glucosinolates, demonstrate anti-
hypertensive potential. These compounds may help regulate blood 
pressure by enhancing vasodilation, increasing endothelial nitric oxide 
availability, reducing oxidative stress, and reducing inflammatory 
pathways linked to vascular dysfunction.7 Lepidium sativum may affect 
kidney processes related to fluid and electrolyte balance and lipid 
metabolism, protecting the heart and lowering blood pressure.3,74
Maghrani et al.48 assessed aqueous Lepidium sativum L. extract (20 mg/ 
kg/day for 3 weeks) in Wistar–Kyoto rats (WKY) and spontaneously 
hypertensive rats (SHR). The extract reduced systolic blood pressure in 
SHR rats, starting from day 7, with no effect in WKY rats. It increased 
water excretion in WKY rats and urinary Na+, K+, and Cl␀ excretion in 
WKY (Na+; K+/Cl␀ ) and SHR rats (Na+; K+/Cl␀ ). Glomerular filtration 
rate increased in WKY rats, without changes in SHR rats or heart rate. 
The study confirmed the antihypertensive and diuretic potential of 
Lepidium sativum. However, translation to humans is limited by the an-
imal model, short duration, single-dose design, and absence of clinical 
evidence.
5.7. Gastrointestinal protective activities
5.7.1. Antiulcer activity
Lepidium sativum has shown antiulcer activity by mechanisms such as 
improving mucosal protection, reducing oxidative stress, and regulating 
G. Bereda                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         Journal of Genetic Engineering and Biotechnology 24 (2026) 100795 
10

inflammatory responses.7 Its bioactive compounds, flavonoids, alka-
loids, terpenoids, phenolic compounds, and glucosinolates, contribute to 
gastroprotective effects by scavenging reactive oxygen species, boosting 
mucus secretion, and fortifying the stomach mucosal barrier.12 Its anti- 
inflammatory properties are supported by lower inflammatory media-
tors associated with ulcers, and its antioxidant activity reduces cellular 
damage and lipid peroxidation.7 Sivaramakrishna and Sravani49 evalu-
ated the antiulcer activity of ethanolic Lepidium sativum L. seed extract in 
albino Wistar rats using pylorus ligation, nonsteroidal anti- 
inflammatory drug (NSAID)-induced, and water immersion stress- 
induced ulcer models. Oral doses of 200 and 400 mg/kg showed a 
dose-dependent gastroprotective effect, reducing ulcer index and 
increasing gastric juice pH. The use of multiple validated ulcer models 
supported the findings; however, the study lacked detailed molecular 
mechanisms and human clinical evidence. Furthermore, animal models 
may not fully predict human therapeutic outcomes.
Salem et al.50 evaluated the anti-Helicobacter pylori and antiulcer 
effects of methanolic Lepidium sativum seed extract through in vitro and 
in vivo studies. HPLC identified phenolic compounds, and the extract 
showed antimicrobial and antioxidant effects. It reduced ulcer index, 
increased ulcer inhibition percentage, decreased lipid peroxidation, 
improved antioxidant markers, and suppressed inflammatory and 
apoptotic markers. Histopathology showed reduced epithelial erosion, 
restored enterocytes, and preserved gastric mucosal integrity. The study 
was strengthened by comprehensive phytochemical, biological, 
biochemical, and histological analyses. However, differences in extracts 
and models limit direct comparison, and clinical validation remains 
lacking.
5.7.2. Antidiarrheal and spasmolytic activity
Lepidium sativum's bioactive compounds, which include flavonoids, 
alkaloids, terpenoids, and phenolic compounds, are responsible for its 
antidiarrheal and spasmolytic effects.6 These phytochemicals may help 
control intestinal motility, reduce gastrointestinal spasms, and reduce 
excessive smooth muscle activity. L. sativum's antidiarrheal properties 
are associated with intestinal secretion regulation, suppression of in-
flammatory mediators, and enhancement of intestinal barrier func-
tion.10 Its spasmolytic activity may relax gastrointestinal smooth 
muscle, reduce abdominal cramping, and enhance intestinal comfort by 
controlling calcium-dependent pathways.7 Rehman et al.4,5 evaluated a 
crude extract prepared from Lepidium sativum seeds for antidiarrheal and 
antispasmodic activities using castor oil-induced diarrhea in rats and 
isolated rat ileum. Lepidium sativum seed crude extract (100 and 300 mg/ 
kg) reduced diarrhea, while 0.01–5 mg/mL inhibited carbachol- and K+- 
induced contractions by muscarinic receptor blockade and calcium 
channel inhibition. Its activity was comparable to dicyclomine and 
verapamil. However, the use of crude extract limits identification of 
active compounds, and animal models may not fully reflect human 
gastrointestinal responses. The study provided strong mechanistic evi-
dence; however, animal findings, lack of compound-specific evaluation, 
and absence of human clinical studies limit translation.
Gilani et al.51 assessed species-dependent antidiarrheal and anti-
spasmodic effects of L. sativum crude extract using castor oil-induced 
diarrhea in mice and isolated gastrointestinal tissues from rats, guinea 
pigs, and rabbits. Crude seed extract of Lepidium sativum at doses of 300 
and 1000 mg/kg reduced diarrhea in mice by decreasing intestinal 
contractions. This effect was mediated through inhibition of muscarinic 
receptors, blockade of calcium channels, activation of potassium chan-
nels, and inhibition of phosphodiesterase activity, which collectively 
reduce gastrointestinal smooth muscle activity. Rat tissues showed the 
highest sensitivity. Although the multi-species model improved mech-
anistic understanding, species variability limits direct clinical trans-
lation. The multi-species and mechanistic approach strengthened 
evidence; however, species differences and lack of clinical trials require 
further validation.
5.7.3. Laxative activity
Lepidium sativum has long been used to promote bowel regularity and 
digestive health. Its laxative activity is associated with its high dietary 
fiber content, mucilaginous polysaccharides, and bioactive phytocon-
stituents that stimulate intestinal motility and help stool movement.3
The mucilage generated after hydration increases fecal volume, regu-
lates water in the colon, and helps soften stools, hence aiding bowel 
movement.75 Additionally, its phytochemicals may contribute to 
gastrointestinal stimulation and modulation of digestive function, with 
potential use in the management of constipation and related gastroin-
testinal discomfort.10 Mehmood et al.52 investigated the gastrointestinal 
activity of Lepidium sativum seed extract using mice and isolated gut 
tissues from mice, guinea pigs, and rabbits. The aqueous-methanolic 
extract at 30 and 100 mg/kg showed prokinetic and laxative effects 
with partial atropine inhibition. Lepidium sativum seed extract (0.1–1 
mg/mL) produced dose-dependent contractions in isolated mouse and 
guinea-pig jejunum and ileum. These contractions were inhibited by 
atropine, indicating that the extract stimulates intestinal smooth muscle 
through muscarinic receptor activation. Rabbit jejunum showed no 
response to atropine, pyrilamine, or SB203186, while rabbit ileum 
showed partial atropine sensitivity. Alkaloids, saponins, and anthra-
quinones were identified. The study supports the traditional use of 
Lepidium sativum for digestive disorders such as indigestion and con-
stipation. However, species-specific responses limit direct translation to 
humans, and the use of crude extracts with limited compound charac-
terization, pharmacokinetic, safety, and clinical data.
5.8. Hepatorenal protective activities
5.8.1. Hepatoprotective activity
Lepidium sativum's antioxidant, anti-inflammatory, and free radical 
neutralization properties have shown hepatoprotective potential.12 Its 
bioactive components, flavonoids, alkaloids, phenolic compounds, and 
glucosinolates, help to maintain hepatic integrity by reducing oxidative 
stress, improving endogenous antioxidant defense mechanisms, and 
reducing lipid peroxidation.12 Lepidium sativum's anti-inflammatory and 
immunomodulatory properties may support liver protection by con-
trolling inflammatory pathways and lowering oxidative-inflammatory 
damage.7,53 Lepidium sativum may improve liver health and reduce he-
patic damage brought on by oxidative stress and inflammatory pro-
cesses.12,53 Raish et al.53 evaluated the hepatoprotective activity of 
Lepidium sativum ethanolic seed extract (LSEE) against D-GalN/LPS- 
induced fulminant hepatic failure in rats. LSEE (150 and 300 mg/kg) 
was compared with silymarin (25 mg/kg). LSEE reduced hepatic injury, 
biochemical alterations, and lipid peroxidation, restored antioxidant 
status, and improved liver histology. It downregulated TNF-α and IL-6 
mRNA by 0.47- and 0.26-fold, increased IL-10 by 1.9- and 2.8-fold, 
and reduced iNOS, HO-1, MPO activity, and NF-κB activation in a 
dose-dependent. It also decreased caspase-3 and increased Bcl-2 
expression, confirming antioxidant, anti-inflammatory, and anti- 
apoptotic hepatoprotective effects. The study provided strong 
biochemical, molecular, and histological evidence. However, the acute 
rat model may not fully represent human liver diseases, and differences 
in extract composition and dosing may limit direct comparison.
5.8.2. Nephroprotective activity
Lepidium sativum has antioxidant, anti-inflammatory, and free radical 
scavenger properties that demonstrate nephroprotective potential.14 Its 
bioactive compounds, flavonoids, alkaloids, phenolic compounds, and 
glucosinolates, help to maintain kidney function by lowering oxidative 
stress and inflammatory damage.6 The plant may reduce lipid peroxi-
dation, strengthen endogenous antioxidant defense systems, and protect 
kidney cellular structures from oxidative damage.7 Its possible 
involvement in preserving kidney integrity and averting renal tissue 
damage is further supported by its capacity to enhance detoxification 
pathways and regulate inflammatory mediators.76 Yadav et al.54
G. Bereda                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         Journal of Genetic Engineering and Biotechnology 24 (2026) 100795 
11

evaluated the ethanolic extract of Lepidium sativum L. seed against 
cisplatin-induced acute renal failure in albino rats. Rats were divided 
into 4 groups (n = 6): control, cisplatin model (5 mg/kg, i.p.), curative, 
and protective groups receiving L. sativum extract (400 mg/kg). 
Cisplatin caused weight loss, increased urine output, urea, creatinine, 
and MDA, and altered the antioxidant defense system, with reduced 
Na+/K+-ATPase, Ca2+-ATPase, and Mg2+-ATPase activities. Lepidium 
sativum improved renal markers, antioxidant status, oxidative damage, 
and ATPase activities. The study showed nephroprotective and neph-
rocurative effects of antioxidant protection. However, limitations 
included small sample size, short duration, lack of histopathology, and 
molecular pathway analysis. Additionally, the acute cisplatin model 
may not fully represent human chronic kidney disease, limiting clinical 
translation.
Alsuliam et al.55 investigated aqueous whole-plant Lepidium sativum 
extract in streptozotocin-induced diabetic nephropathy in rats. Animals 
were divided into 4 groups, and the extract was administered at 250 mg/ 
kg for 8 weeks. Lepidium sativum reduced fasting blood glucose, 
increased fasting insulin, improved lipid abnormalities by reducing 
cholesterol, free fatty acids, triglycerides, and LDL-c, and decreased 
urinary volume, albumin excretion, and renal creatinine abnormalities. 
It improved tubular damage, reduced NF-κB, TNF-α, IL-6, and TGF-β1 
expression, increased SOD, CAT, and GSH, and decreased MDA levels. 
The study demonstrated hypoglycemic, antioxidant, anti-inflammatory, 
and anti-fibrotic effects. However, limitations included animal-only 
evidence, lack of clinical confirmation, and absence of standardized 
dose-response evaluation. Differences in animal models and extract 
preparation make comparisons difficult.
5.8.3. Diuretic activity
The phytochemical composition of Lepidium sativum, including fla-
vonoids, alkaloids, phenolic compounds, and minerals like potassium, 
may be responsible for its purported diuretic properties. These bioactive 
components may improve urine excretion by influencing renal function, 
encouraging electrolyte balance, and raising urine production.2 Its his-
toric use in disorders including urinary irregularities, edema, and fluid 
retention may be attributed to the diuretic effect, which is linked to 
enhanced fluid regulation.11 Additionally, it may protect renal function 
by reducing oxidative stress and preventing kidney damage.11 However, 
more research is needed to fully understand the specific molecular 
pathways underlying L. sativum's diuretic activity.
Patel et al.56 evaluated the diuretic activity of aqueous and methanol 
extracts of Lepidium sativum seeds in normal rats. Extracts were admin-
istered orally at 50 and 100 mg/kg, with hydrochlorothiazide (10 mg/ 
kg) as a positive control. Both extracts increased urine volume and so-
dium excretion. The aqueous extract (100 mg/kg) increased potassium 
excretion, while the methanol extract conserved potassium. No signifi-
cant changes were observed in urine conductivity or pH. Both extrac-
tions have diuretic effects comparable to hydrochlorothiazide, 
supporting the traditional use of Lepidium sativum. The study confirmed 
the diuretic potential of Lepidium sativum seeds in animals. However, the 
findings are limited by the short-term animal model, lack of mechanistic 
evidence, and absence of clinical validation, which restrict their trans-
lation to human therapy.
5.9. Neuroprotective activities
Bioactive compounds of Lepidium sativum, including Flavonoids, al-
kaloids, phenolic compounds, and glucosinolates, demonstrate neuro-
protective potential.2 Its neuroprotective benefits are primarily linked to 
the reduction of oxidative stress, the suppression of neuroinflammatory 
reactions, the control of apoptotic pathways, and the maintenance of 
neuronal integrity.77 Lepidium sativum's antioxidant properties help 
prevent the formation of reactive oxygen species and protect brain cells 
from oxidative injury.77 Additionally, Lepidium sativum may enhance 
endogenous antioxidant defense mechanisms, regulate neurotransmitter 
balance, and alter signaling pathways that are crucial for neuronal 
survival, such as the Wnt/β-catenin/CaMK-II signaling cascade.77,78
Ed-Day et al.57 evaluated the neuroprotective effects of Lepidium 
sativum L. against cadmium-induced memory impairment in Wistar rats. 
Groups received control, CdCl₂ (10 mg/kg), or CdCl₂ (10 mg/kg) + LS 
extract (20 mg/kg). Cadmium reduced short-term and long-term mem-
ory (p < 0.05) and increased acetylcholinesterase (AChE) activity in the 
hippocampus, cerebellum, and cortex (p < 0.001). LS improved memory 
and reduced cholinergic dysfunction. The study was supported by 
behavioral and biochemical findings but was limited by animal models, 
small sample sizes, and a lack of molecular pathway analysis. Balgoon58
studied Lepidium sativum seed extract in an AlCl₃-induced Alzheimer's 
disease rat model. Forty rats were assigned to four groups (n = 10): 
control, LS-treated (20 mg/kg for 8 weeks), AD-induced (AlCl₃ 10 mg/ 
kg), and LS-treated AD groups. AlCl₃ impaired memory, increased 
oxidative stress, inflammatory cytokines, AChE activity, and neuronal 
damage. Lepidium sativum improved cognition, antioxidant defense, and 
reduced inflammation, and alleviated cortical damage through antiox-
idant, anti-inflammatory, and antiapoptotic mechanisms. The study was 
strengthened by behavioral, biochemical, and histological assessments 
but limited by the incomplete similarity of the AlCl₃ model to human 
Alzheimer's disease and unclear long-term safety, pharmacokinetics, 
and clinical effectiveness.
5.10. Tissue repair and musculoskeletal protective activities
5.10.1. Wound-healing activity
Lepidium sativum's antioxidant, anti-inflammatory, antibacterial, and 
tissue-regenerative qualities show promise for wound healing.79 Flavo-
noids, phenolic compounds, alkaloids, and glucosinolates are among its 
bioactive components that promote angiogenesis, fibroblast prolifera-
tion, collagen synthesis, and re-epithelialization.7 While Lepidium sat-
ivum's anti-inflammatory properties reduce inflammatory mediators 
involved in tissue regeneration, its antioxidant activity supports 
decreasing oxidative stress at the wound site.7,12,26 Sahane et al.59
studied ethanolic Lepidium sativum seed extract in an STZ-induced dia-
betic rat excision wound model. Oral (20 and 30 mg/kg) and topical 
treatments enhanced wound closure, increased hydroxyproline and 
hexosamine content, and reduced wound area and clotting time. The 
extract also lowered blood glucose and improved red blood cell count, 
white blood cell count, and total protein levels. Increased hydroxypro-
line and hexosamine indicated enhanced collagen synthesis and extra-
cellular matrix formation. The wound healing activity was attributed to 
antihyperglycemic, antioxidant, and anti-inflammatory effects. The 
study provided preliminary evidence using oral and topical formulations 
and multiple biochemical markers; however, animal-only evidence, lack 
of clinical validation, and limited molecular pathway analysis remain 
limitations.
5.10.2. Osteoprotective activity
Lepidium sativum demonstrates osteoprotective potential through 
bioactive compounds, including flavonoids, alkaloids, phenolic com-
pounds, glucosinolates, and minerals like calcium, phosphorus, and 
iron.12 These compounds may promote bone health by increasing oste-
oblast activity, enhancing bone matrix, and facilitating mineralization. 
Lepidium sativum regulates osteogenic markers and suppresses excessive 
osteoclast-mediated bone resorption, among other processes involved in 
bone remodeling.3 Additionally, its anti-inflammatory and antioxidant 
properties can help prevent oxidative stress and inflammatory processes 
that hasten bone loss.12,33 Abdallah et al.33 evaluated the extract of 
Lepidium sativum in ovariectomized rats at 50 and 100 mg/kg vs. 17β- 
estradiol. UHPLC/PDA/ESI–MS and SPME-GC/MS identified glucosi-
nolates, lignans, coumarins, phenolic acids, and alkaloids. The extract 
prevented bone loss by enhancing bone weight, femoral strength, 
osteocalcin, and lactate dehydrogenase (LDH), preserving SOD and GPx, 
reducing lipid peroxidation, decreasing CTX-I and TRAP, and regulating 
G. Bereda                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         Journal of Genetic Engineering and Biotechnology 24 (2026) 100795 
12

the RANKL/OPG pathway. The study showed strong biochemical, me-
chanical, and metabolomic evidence. However, the animal model limits 
direct translation to humans, requiring clinical validation and long-term 
safety studies.
El-Haroun et al.60 compared Lepidium sativum (300 mg/kg) with 
teriparatide (4 μg/kg) in 60 adult male guinea pigs divided into 6 groups 
with glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis. Lepidium sativum improved 
cortical and trabecular changes, reduced resorption cavities, collagen 
loss, osteoclast activity, and osteocyte apoptosis, increased osteoblast- 
related changes, and improved serum calcium and alkaline phospha-
tase alterations. It showed greater protective efficacy than teriparatide. 
However, limited mechanistic, pharmacokinetic, and clinical evidence 
restricts its clinical relevance.
5.11. Reproductive and women's health activities and fertility-enhancing 
activity
Lepidium sativum has long been known to have advantages for 
reproductive health. Its bioactive components, flavonoids, alkaloids, 
phytosterols, polyphenols, and minerals that may enhance reproductive 
function, are primarily responsible for its fertility-enhancing proper-
ties.75 It has been linked to changes in reproductive parameters through 
antioxidant defense, regulation of hormonal balance, improved gonadal 
function, and protection of reproductive organs from oxidative dam-
age.2 Its phytochemicals may enhance sperm quality by lowering lipid 
peroxidation and promoting spermatogenesis. Its estrogenic properties 
and nutritional components may assist female reproductive health.80
Furthermore, Lepidium sativum may influence reproductive signaling 
pathways associated with tissue integrity, endocrine control, and 
gamete development, suggesting its potential as a natural fertility- 
supporting agent.61
Moustafa et al.61 evaluated LSSE in letrozole (1 mg/kg) and HFD- 
induced PCOS rats. Sixty rats were divided into five groups (n = 12): 
control, polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS), PCOS + LSSE (250 mg/kg), 
PCOS + LSSE (500 mg/kg), and metformin (200 mg/kg) for 4 weeks. 
LSSE improved metabolic, hormonal, antioxidant, inflammatory, 
fertility, pregnancy, and histological outcomes. The 500 mg/kg dose 
showed better biochemical effects, while 250 mg/kg produced better 
pregnancy outcomes. The study shows a validated PCOS model, dose 
comparison, metformin as a reference treatment, and assessment of 
multiple outcomes. However, translation to humans remains limited due 
to species differences, uncertain equivalent human dosing, and insuffi-
cient long-term safety evidence. Ibraheem et al.62 investigated the 
aqueous extract of Lepidium sativum in male mice. Forty-eight mice were 
divided into four groups (n = 12): control, Lepidium sativu extract, 
sulpiride-induced hyperprolactinemia, and sulpiride + LS extract. LS 
extract enhanced reproductive function by increasing follicle- 
stimulating hormone (FSH) and testosterone concentrations, reducing 
prolactin levels, and improving sperm-related fertility outcomes in male 
mice. Hyperprolactinemic mice showed reduced sperm count (53.33 ±
1.76 sperm/ml), motility (33.33 ± 3.33%), and viability (46.67 ±
1.67%). The study supports reproductive effects through hormonal 
regulation and testicular protection. However, the induced animal 
model and lack of phytochemical standardization limit the generaliz-
ability and clinical applicability of these findings.
5.12. Bronchodilatory and anti-asthmatic activity
Lepidium sativum has shown antiasthmatic properties due to its 
bioactive compounds, including flavonoids, alkaloids, phenolic com-
pounds, and glucosinolates.7 Its anti-asthmatic effects are linked to 
improved bronchial relaxation, decreased oxidative stress, and regula-
tion of airway inflammation. Lepidium sativum may improve respiratory 
function by reducing mucus production, promoting smooth muscle 
relaxation, and inhibiting inflammatory mediators linked to airway 
hyperresponsiveness.4,5 Furthermore, its antioxidant activity may 
protect airway tissues from oxidative damage, which is crucial to the 
development and course of asthma.6 Rehman et al.4,5 evaluated the 
bronchodilatory mechanism of Lepidium sativum crude extract using 
isolated guinea pig tracheal preparations. Lepidium sativum crude extract 
inhibited carbachol (1 μM) and K+ (80 mM) contractions. At 0.03 mg/ 
mL, it showed anticholinergic activity, while 0.1 mg/mL reduced the 
maximum response. Lepidium sativum crude extract (0.1–0.3 mg/mL) 
demonstrated Ca2+ channel blocking activity similar to verapamil, and 
0.03–0.1 mg/mL enhanced isoprenaline relaxation, suggesting Phos-
phodiesterase inhibition (PDE) inhibition. The bronchodilatory effect 
involved anticholinergic, Ca2+ antagonist, and PDE inhibitory path-
ways. However, the isolated tissue model limits clinical translation, and 
further in vivo.
Paranjape and Mehta63 assessed the efficacy and safety of Lepidium 
sativum seeds in 30 patients with mild-to-moderate bronchial asthma. 
Patients received L. sativum seed powder 1 g three times daily for 4 
weeks. Significant improvements were observed in FVC, FEV₁, 
FEF25–75%, and MVV, clinical symptoms, and asthma severity, with no 
adverse effects reported. However, small sample size, short duration, 
and lack of placebo control limit the reliability and generalizability of 
findings.
5.13. Other pharmacological activities of Lepidium Sativum
Lepidium sativum also exhibits several additional pharmacological 
activities beyond those discussed above. These include antipyretic,10
antiviral, anthelmintic, apoptosis-inducing, antihyperlipidemic, anti- 
obesity,6 vasoprotective,81 gastroprotective,10 and antiurolithiatic82 ef-
fects. It also shows potential memory-enhancing,83 anxiolytic,44 bone- 
regenerative,84 galactagogue,3 reproductive protective,6 antitussive,85
and hematopoietic66 activities. Furthermore, its dermatological protec-
tive effects, including skin wound healing, dermal protection, and anti- 
aging activity, highlight its therapeutic versatility.86 However, these 
emerging activities require further mechanistic investigations and clin-
ical validation to confirm their therapeutic significance.
6. Phytochemical characterization and standardization of 
Lepidium sativum extracts
Although various Lepidium sativum preparations, including aqueous, 
ethanolic, methanolic, chloroform, seed oil, and nanoparticle-based 
extracts, have demonstrated pharmacological activities, differences in 
extraction procedures and phytochemical profiles may contribute to 
variability among reported findings.68 Therefore, standardized extrac-
tion methods, defined chemical markers, and validated analytical ap-
proaches are essential to ensure reproducibility and quality control.87
Recent phytochemical investigations have advanced the characteriza-
tion of bioactive constituents in Lepidium sativum.1288,89identified 12 
flavonoid glycosides, including 5 new compounds (lepisativutmines 
Q–U), from L. sativum seeds using high-resolution mass spectrometry 
(HRMS), nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectroscopy, and tandem 
mass spectrometry (MS/MS), and sugar configuration analysis. Several 
compounds showed protein tyrosine phosphatase 1B (PTP1B) inhibition 
(IC₅₀ = 60.58 ± 3.53 and 33.90 ± 2.89 μM), α-glucosidase inhibition 
(IC₅₀ = 25.96 ± 0.30 μM), and weak nitric oxide inhibition (IC₅₀ = 92.28 
± 4.19 and 40.77 ± 1.50 μM). These findings provide potential chemical 
markers for L. sativum standardization; however, their effects on crude 
extracts, bioavailability, and clinical relevance require further 
validation.
Additionally,88,89identified 4 new and 4 known compounds from L. 
sativum seeds. Compound (+)-1 showed moderate HeLa cell inhibition 
(IC₅₀ = 19.99 ± 1.00 μM), while compound (␀ )-1 showed weak activity 
(IC₅₀ = 60.23 ± 3.51 μM). These results improve understanding of L. 
sativum bioactive constituents, but further studies are needed for extract 
standardization and therapeutic confirmation. These findings indicate 
that the pharmacological effects of Lepidium sativum are associated with 
G. Bereda                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         Journal of Genetic Engineering and Biotechnology 24 (2026) 100795 
13

specific phytochemical constituents rather than crude extracts alone. 
Therefore, future studies should incorporate standardized extraction 
procedures, quantification of marker compounds, validated quality- 
control parameters, and assessment of batch-to-batch consistency. 
Such approaches will improve reproducibility, facilitate comparison 
between studies, and support the translation of experimental findings 
into evidence-based therapeutic applications.
7. Distinction between experimental evidence and clinical 
applications
Most pharmacological activities of Lepidium sativum have been 
demonstrated through in vitro and animal studies, with limited clinical 
evaluation. Preclinical evidence supports antioxidant,24,26 anti-inflam-
matory,28,29 antimicrobial,34,36 anticancer,38–40 antidiabetic,27,42,45
gastroprotective,49,50 hepatoprotective,53 nephroprotective,54,55 neuro-
protective,57,58 osteoprotective,33,60 wound-healing,59 and reproductive 
protective62 effects.
These activities are associated with enhanced antioxidant defense, 
inhibition of inflammatory pathways such as NF-κB, TNF-α, and IL-6, 
and regulation of apoptotic pathways involving BAX, BCL2, and 
caspase-3. Other reported mechanisms include modulation of the 
RANKL/OPG pathway, α-glucosidase inhibition, muscarinic receptor 
antagonism, calcium-channel blockade, phosphodiesterase inhibition, 
and p53-mediated apoptosis. Importantly, these findings represent 
experimentally demonstrated biological effects and should be distin-
guished from clinically validated therapeutic applications. Most avail-
able evidence demonstrates biological activity under preclinical 
conditions, but the translation of these effects into clinical benefits in 
humans remains uncertain.
Clinical evidence for L. sativum remains limited. The main human 
study identified was the clinical trial by Paranjape and Mehta.63 This 
study reported improvements in pulmonary function and asthma 
symptoms following L. sativum seed administration in patients with 
mild-to-moderate bronchial asthma. However, the study was limited by 
its small sample size, short treatment duration, and lack of a placebo- 
controlled design. Therefore, although preclinical studies demonstrate 
promising biological activity, these findings should not be equated with 
clinically validated therapeutic effects. Further well-designed clinical 
trials using standardized extracts, adequate sample sizes, optimized 
doses, pharmacokinetic evaluation, and long-term safety assessment are 
required before these effects can be considered clinically validated 
therapeutic applications.
8. Toxicological and safety studies of Lepidium sativum
Lepidium sativum is generally considered safe when consumed at 
traditional dietary and therapeutic doses and exhibits a favorable safety 
profile6 (Table 5). It has low toxicity and is well tolerated after single- 
dose administration at tested doses.73 However, excessive or pro-
longed use may cause adverse effects due to its bioactive constituents, 
particularly glucosinolates and their hydrolysis products, which may 
interfere with thyroid function and pose potential reproductive risks.90
In addition, variability in phytochemical composition, dosage, and 
preparation methods may influence its safety.12 Therefore, standardized 
formulations, comprehensive toxicological evaluation, and well- 
designed clinical studies are needed to establish its long-term safety 
and therapeutic applicability.390 evaluated the acute and subacute oral 
toxicity of the 70% ethanol extract of Lepidium sativum seeds in Wistar 
rats. Acute toxicity showed no mortality up to 5000 mg/kg, with an oral 
median lethal dose (LD₅₀) > 5000 mg/kg, classifying it as practically 
non-toxic (Globally Harmonized System of Classification and Labelling 
of Chemicals (GHS) Category 5). However, 28-day administration of 500 
and 1000 mg/kg caused dose-dependent toxicity, including diarrhea, 
reduced movement, piloerection, hepatic enlargement, elevated liver 
enzymes, altered hematological parameters, and liver histopathological 
changes, suggesting potential hepatotoxicity at higher doses.
91 also assessed the toxicity of Lepidium sativum seed fixed oil in 
Wistar albino rats. The oil showed an oral LD₅₀ of 2818.32 mg/kg, 
classified as slightly hazardous according to World Health Organization 
(WHO). 28-day repeated administration caused piloerection, lethargy, 
tremors, changes in body weight, organ pathology, hematological 
indices, and biochemical parameters, indicating toxicity with prolonged 
exposure. Compared with the ethanolic extract, the fixed oil showed 
greater toxicity in the formulation-specific safety assessment.
9. Limitations of the review
Several limitations should be considered. First, this narrative review 
does not include quantitative data pooling or meta-analysis; therefore, 
effect sizes and the strength of evidence for reported pharmacological 
activities cannot be statistically evaluated. Second, substantial hetero-
geneity exists among available studies regarding plant parts, 
geographical origin, cultivation conditions, extraction methods, phyto-
chemical composition, experimental models, and assessment protocols, 
which may limit comparability and interpretation of findings. Addi-
tionally, most reported effects are based on in vitro and in vivo studies, 
while well-designed clinical studies remain limited, highlighting the 
need for standardized clinical validation before practical application. 
Variations in phytochemical profiles, environmental factors, and prep-
aration methods may also affect the reproducibility and therapeutic 
consistency of Lepidium sativum-derived products. Furthermore, publi-
cation bias may be present because studies showing positive pharma-
cological outcomes are more likely to be published. Important gaps 
remain regarding pharmacokinetics, bioavailability, molecular targets, 
long-term safety, toxicity, and potential herb–drug interactions, 
requiring further comprehensive investigations.
10. Future directions
To translate Lepidium sativum L. from traditional medicine into 
evidence-based therapeutic applications, several research gaps must be 
addressed despite its extensive traditional use and increasing scientific 
evidence. Future studies should focus on standardized phytochemical 
characterization using advanced analytical platforms, including 
metabolomics, HPLC, LC–MS, and NMR spectroscopy, to establish 
comprehensive chemical profiles and identify therapeutically important 
bioactive compounds. Further investigation is required to elucidate the 
molecular mechanisms and biological targets underlying its pharmaco-
logical effects, particularly pathways related to oxidative stress 
