Future research should also prioritize the standardized formulations 
and advanced delivery systems, including nanoencapsulation, phyto-
somes, and targeted nanotechnology-based approaches, to enhance 
bioavailability, stability, and therapeutic efficacy. Comprehensive 
pharmacokinetic and pharmacodynamic studies are needed to evaluate 
absorption, distribution, metabolism, elimination, bioavailability, and 
dose–response relationships. Interdisciplinary collaboration among 
ethnobotany, phytochemistry, pharmacology, molecular biology, 
nutrition science, and clinical medicine will be essential for developing 
standardized extracts, functional foods, nutraceuticals, and plant- 
derived medicines. Ultimately, the clinical establishment of Lepidium 
sativum as a validated medicinal resource requires rigorous evaluation of 
its bioactive compounds, mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetic 
properties, safety profiles, and clinical efficacy.
11. Conclusion
Lepidium sativum exhibits diverse pharmacological activities, 
including antioxidant, anti-inflammatory, analgesic, antimicrobial, 
antidiabetic, gastroprotective, hepatoprotective, nephroprotective, car-
dioprotective, neuroprotective, osteoprotective, wound-healing, repro-
ductive, and anticancer effects. These activities are mainly attributed to 
its bioactive constituents, including glucosinolates, alkaloids, phenolics, 
flavonoids, coumarins, and lignans. Preclinical studies provide sub-
stantial evidence from multiple experimental models and demonstrate 
regulation of oxidative stress, inflammation, apoptosis, metabolic 
pathways, and cellular signaling mechanisms. However, clinical evi-
dence remains limited. Although generally safe at traditional doses, 
high-dose or prolonged use of concentrated preparations may cause 
toxicity. Therefore, further pharmacokinetic studies, standardized for-
mulations, comprehensive safety evaluations, and well-designed clinical 
trials are required to confirm its therapeutic efficacy and clinical 
applicability.
