4.3. The LBP/albumin ratio as a novel composite biomarker
A growing body of evidence supports composite biomarker ratios in 
IBD that leverage the opposing directionality of positive acute-phase 
proteins and negative acute-phase proteins. Qin and colleagues 22 and 
Chen and colleagues 23 established the CRP/albumin ratio (CAR) as a 
useful marker of CD activity; Liu and colleagues subsequently confirmed 
the accuracy of high-sensitivity CAR for disease activity in IBD. 37 The 
platelet-to-albumin ratio 38 and the albumin-to-globulin ratio 39 have also 
been shown to correlate with endoscopic and clinical activity. However, 
no previously published study, to our knowledge, has combined serum 
LBP with albumin as a ratio for Crohn's disease.
The biological rationale for LBP/Alb is three-fold. First, LBP reflects 
ongoing bacterial translocation and acute-phase activation, 15,35 while 
serum albumin is a validated negative acute-phase reactant that declines 
with chronic inflammation and gastrointestinal protein loss in CD. 22,40 
Combining the two places a bacterial-translocation signal in the 
numerator over a combined inflammation-plus-nutrition signal in the
denominator, so that both axes push the ratio in the same direction 
during active disease. Second, albumin in CD captures the nutritional 
dimension of chronic enteropathy, which is not reflected in LBP or CRP 
alone — clinically relevant because malnutrition is common in CD 
(43.8% of active CD patients in our cohort were at high MUST risk), 
predicts complications, and independently influences treatment de-
cisions. 19 Third, expressing LBP as a ratio partially normalises assay-
related inter-laboratory variability in LBP quantification. We note, 
however, that in the present cohort the incremental value of LBP/Alb 
over LBP alone was modest and was driven predominantly by the LBP 
numerator: albumin varied far less than LBP (coefficient of variation 
12.6% vs 35.4%), the ratio correlated almost perfectly with LBP alone (ρ
= 0.95), and the gain in correlation with clinical indices was small (HBI 
ρ = 0.242 vs 0.211; MUST ρ = 0.213 vs 0.204). LBP/Alb is therefore best 
regarded as an albumin-adjusted LBP index whose practical value lies in 
partial normalisation of inter-assay LBP variability and the incorpora-
tion of nutritional status, rather than in demonstrated statistical supe-
riority over LBP as a standalone marker.
In our data, LBP/Alb showed the strongest correlations with HBI (ρ
= 0.41) and MUST (ρ = 0.50) of any biomarker in the panel, and was 
more strongly related to BMI (ρ = ␀ 0.46) than either LBP alone (ρ =
␀ 0.33) or albumin. It achieved an AUC of 0.782 (95% CI 0.695–0.860) 
for discriminating all CD from HC, exceeding CRP (0.667), albumin
