through IL-23R signalling, sustains IL-17 A, IL-22 and GM-CSF expres-
sion, and has been causally implicated in intestinal pathology. 8–10 IL-6 
acts in parallel: through classical and trans-signalling via 
gp130–STAT3, it drives T-cell survival, acute-phase protein production 
(including LBP itself) and mucosal inflammation in active CD. 11,12 
Lipopolysaccharide-binding protein (LBP), a hepatocyte-derived class-I 
acute-phase protein, binds the lipid-A moiety of bacterial endotoxin and 
presents it to CD14/TLR4; circulating LBP therefore serves as a surrogate 
marker of bacterial translocation across a disrupted intestinal barrier, a 
feature increasingly recognised as a persistent pathogenic driver in
CD. 13–15
Standard non-invasive biomarkers (CRP and faecal calprotectin) 
have important limitations. CRP is non-specific, is normal in up to 40% 
of patients with endoscopically active CD, and correlates only weakly 
with mucosal healing. 16 Faecal calprotectin has higher sensitivity but is 
not universally available and has substantial inter-assay variability. 17,18 
Clinical activity indices such as the Harvey–Bradshaw Index (HBI) track 
symptoms reliably but do not reflect endoscopic or transmural activ-
ity. 19,20 A substantial proportion of patients in “clinical remission” on 
anti-tumour-necrosis-factor (anti-TNF) maintenance have persistent 
biochemical, endoscopic or imaging activity — a finding with prognostic 
implications for relapse and bowel damage. 21 Accessible serum bio-
markers that capture residual inflammation are therefore needed, 
particularly in low- and middle-income settings. Composite biomarker 
indices — notably the C-reactive protein/albumin ratio (CAR) — have 
emerged as useful clinical markers in IBD, combining a positive acute-
phase reactant with albumin, a negative acute-phase reactant and 
marker of nutritional status. 22,23 However, no analogous ratio has been 
evaluated for LBP.
We therefore performed a cross-sectional case–control study to: (i) 
compare serum LBP, IL-23 and IL-6 concentrations between patients 
with active CD, patients on stable infliximab maintenance (IFX-CD) and 
healthy controls; (ii) evaluate their diagnostic performance (alone and 
combined with CRP and derived ratios) for discriminating CD from HC; 
and (iii) evaluate the LBP-to-albumin ratio (LBP/Alb), expected to 
integrate bacterial-translocation and nutritional signals, as a novel 
composite biomarker of clinical activity. Our hypothesis was that IL-23, 
IL-6 and LBP are elevated in CD relative to HC, that they remain elevated 
during infliximab maintenance consistent with persistent Th17-axis 
activity and bacterial translocation, and that LBP/Alb correlates more 
strongly than its individual components with clinical and nutritional 
indices.
2. Materials and methods
2.1. Study design, setting and ethics
This was a single-centre cross-sectional case–control study. Consec-
utive adult participants were recruited between 27 October 2025 and 25 
March 2026 at the Al-Najaf Gastrointestinal Tract Center (GIT Hospital), 
Najaf Al-Ashraf, Iraq. The study protocol was approved by the Ethics 
Committee of Ministry of health, Najaf Health Directorate, Training and 
Human Development Center, Reference No. 47875, dated 15 December 
2025). All procedures conformed to the 1964 Declaration of Helsinki 
and its later amendments. Written informed consent was obtained from 
every participant before enrolment. The study is reported in accordance 
with the STROBE statement for case–control observational studies. 24
2.2. Participants and group definitions
One hundred and seventeen participants were allocated to three pre-
specified groups. Group-specific inclusion criteria were adapted from 
recent published case–control biomarker studies in CD, notably the 
PROFILE trial 25 and a 2025 Iraqi Crohn's disease biomarker study from 
Sulaimani. 26 Definitions of clinical activity follow the HBI thresholds 
validated by Best and colleagues. 20
Active CD (n ¼ 32). Patients with an established diagnosis of CD 
who fulfilled all of the following: HBI ≥ 5 at enrolment, at least one 
objective marker of active inflammation (CRP above the upper labora-
tory limit of normal of 6 mg/L or endoscopic activity documented within 
12 weeks), and no change to maintenance therapy within 4 weeks. 
Infliximab-maintained CD (IFX-CD; n ¼ 35). Patients with estab-
lished CD receiving standard infliximab maintenance (5 mg/kg intra-
venously every 8 weeks, with dose/interval optimisation as clinically 
indicated) for ≥12 consecutive months before enrolment, regardless of 
current clinical activity status. This definition deliberately included 
patients in clinical remission (HBI ≤ 4) and with residual activity, 
reflecting the real-world spectrum of long-term infliximab exposure. 
Healthy controls (HC; n ¼ 50). Age- and sex-matched adults with 
no personal or family history of IBD, no gastrointestinal, autoimmune or 
chronic inflammatory disease, and no intake of anti-inflammatory, 
immunosuppressive or antibiotic therapy within 4 weeks of enrolment.
2.3. Diagnostic, activity and nutritional criteria
CD was diagnosed by the attending gastroenterologist in accordance 
with the ECCO–ESGAR Guideline for Diagnostic Assessment in IBD, 
which requires the integration of clinical, biochemical, endoscopic, 
histological and cross-sectional imaging findings rather than any single 
reference standard. 27 Treatment was delivered and adjusted per the 
2024 ECCO Guidelines on Therapeutics in Crohn's Disease. 28 Disease 
activity was scored by the HBI (remission ≤4; mild 5–7; moderate 8–16; 
severe ≥17). 19,20 Disease location and behaviour were classified by the 
Montreal classification. 29 Nutritional risk was assessed with the 
Malnutrition Universal Screening Tool (MUST) from BAPEN. 30,31 BMI 
(kg/m 2 ) was calculated from height and weight measured on a cali-
brated stadiometer and scale, and classified by WHO cut-offs.
2.4. Exclusion criteria
Exclusion criteria (applied to all groups) were: (a) coexistent ulcer-
ative colitis or unclassified/indeterminate IBD; (b) known malignancy 
(current or within 5 years); (c) pregnancy or lactation; (d) clinically 
significant active infection within 4 weeks; (e) use of systemic cortico-
steroids or of immunomodulators other than infliximab (thiopurines, 
methotrexate, ciclosporin, JAK inhibitors or other biologics) within 4 
weeks; (f) chronic liver disease (Child–Pugh ≥ B) or chronic kidney 
disease (eGFR <45 mL/min/1.73 m 2 ); (g) other active autoimmune 
disease requiring systemic therapy; (h) bowel surgery within 12 weeks, 
or the presence of an ostomy or ileoanal pouch. Elevated CRP and un-
intentional weight loss were not exclusion criteria because both are 
expected features of the study population.
2.5. Sample collection and processing
After an overnight fast (≥ 8 h), 5 mL of peripheral venous blood were 
collected into a plain serum-separator (SST) tube. Samples were allowed 
to clot for 30 min at room temperature and centrifuged within 2 h at 
3000 ×g for 10 min. Serum was aliquoted into polypropylene cryovials 
and stored at ␀ 80 ◦ C until analysis; freeze–thaw cycles were limited to 
one. CRP and albumin were measured on the day of collection.
2.6. Biomarker measurement
2.6.1. ELISA measurement of IL-6, IL-23 and LBP
Serum IL-6, IL-23 and LBP were quantified by commercial sandwich 
ELISAs supplied by Bioassay Technology Laboratory (BT Lab; Shanghai 
Korain Biotech Co. Ltd., Shanghai, China) strictly per the manufacturer's 
instructions. The kits used were Human Interleukin-6 ELISA Kit (cata-
logue no. E0090Hu), Human Interleukin-23 ELISA Kit (catalogue no. 
E0074Hu) and Human Lipopolysaccharide-Binding Protein ELISA Kit 
(catalogue no. E0360Hu). Intra-assay coefficients of variation were <
A.A. Othman and W.S. Hameed Journal of Genetic Engineering and Biotechnology 24 (2026) 100798 
2

8% and inter-assay <10% for each assay. Samples were measured in 
duplicate; a four-parameter logistic standard curve was fitted on each 
plate. Absorbance was read at 450 nm within 10 min of reaction stop.
2.6.2. CRP and serum albumin
High-sensitivity CRP was measured by immunoturbidimetry using 
the CRP-Q kit (REF 7710/7732/7783/7782; Giesse Diagnostics srl, 
Guidonia Montecelio, Italy) with latex particles coated with goat IgG 
anti-human CRP at 540 nm (530–550 nm); sensitivity 2 mg/L, linearity 
to 150 mg/L; reference values <6–8 mg/L. Serum albumin was quan-
tified by the bromocresol-green (BCG) colorimetric method (REF 0027/ 
0027/2/0028; Giesse Diagnostics srl, Guidonia Montecelio, Italy) at 628 
nm (620–640 nm); sensitivity 0.1 g/dL, linearity to 6 g/dL; reference 
values 3.5–5.5 g/dL. Both assays were run on an SMT-120 fully auto-
mated random-access biochemistry analyser (Chengdu Seamaty Tech-
nology Co. Ltd., Chengdu, China), which uses a halogen lamp with eight 
selectable wavelengths (340–700 nm), absorbance range 0–4.0 Abs, 
resolution 0.0001 Abs, reaction temperature 37 ◦ C ± 0.1 ◦ C and 
throughput 120 tests/h. The Fixed-Time mode was used for both CRP-Q 
and albumin. Daily two-level commercial quality controls were run 
before patient samples.
2.6.3. Derived indices
Two derived biomarker ratios were calculated: the CRP/albumin 
ratio (CAR) = CRP (mg/L) / albumin (g/dL), 22,23 and the LBP/albumin 
ratio (LBP/Alb) = LBP (ng/mL) / albumin (g/dL), an a priori composite 
index developed to integrate bacterial-translocation and nutritional 
signals in this study.
2.7. Statistical analysis
A minimum of 30 cases per group was pre-specified, providing ≥80% 
power (α = 0.05, two-sided) to detect a standardised difference of 0.8. 
Normality was tested by the Kolmogorov–Smirnov and Shapiro–Wilk 
tests. Normally distributed variables are presented as mean ± SD; non-
normally distributed variables as median (IQR); categorical variables 
as n (%). Serum IL-6, IL-23 and LBP were non-normal, so between-group 
differences were analysed by the Kruskal–Wallis H-test; pairwise com-
parisons used the Mann–Whitney U test with Bonferroni correction for 
three pairwise contrasts. Categorical variables were compared by χ 2 or 
Fisher's exact test. Correlations were assessed by Spearman's ρ. Diag-
nostic performance was evaluated by ROC analysis with AUC, 95% 
confidence intervals from bias-corrected bootstrap resampling (2000 
replicates), and optimal cut-offs selected by Youden's J. Multivariable 
discrimination used L2-regularised logistic regression with 5-fold strat-
ified cross-validation; model fit was reported by McFadden's pseudo-R 2 .
Significance was set at two-sided P < 0.05. Analyses were performed 
with Python 3.11 (SciPy, scikit-learn, statsmodels), with confirmatory 
cross-checks in IBM SPSS Statistics v27 and MedCalc v22.
3. Results
3.1. Baseline characteristics of study participants
A total of 117 participants were enrolled: 32 patients with active CD, 
35 IFX-CD patients (all ≥12 months maintenance) and 50 healthy con-
trols. Demographic, lifestyle, nutritional and disease-specific charac-
teristics are summarised in Table 1. The three groups were comparable 
in age (P = 0.162) and sex distribution (P = 0.617) but differed signif-
icantly in BMI (P = 0.029) and BMI category (P < 0.001), with under-
weight status restricted almost exclusively to active CD. Physical activity 
was progressively reduced across the disease groups (P = 0.002), and 
appetite impairment and recent weight loss were more frequent in active 
CD than in IFX-CD or HC (both P < 0.001). MUST-defined high nutri-
tional risk was present in 43.8% of active CD, 11.4% of IFX-CD and 0% of 
HC (P < 0.001). Elevated CRP was present in 43.8% of active CD and
