37.1% of IFX-CD but in no HC (P < 0.001); serum albumin status did not 
differ significantly between groups (P = 0.253). Within the CD groups, 
active CD patients had shorter disease duration (9.9 ± 17.3 vs 32.5 ± 
16.5 months; P < 0.001) and substantially higher clinical activity (HBI 
median 8 [IQR 5–9] vs 4 [3–5]; P < 0.001), with moderate activity in 
65.6% of active CD versus 0% of IFX-CD. The two CD subgroups did not 
differ substantially in Montreal disease location or age at onset.
3.2. Serum biomarker concentrations across the three groups
Biomarker concentrations are presented in Table 2, and the distri-
butions of LBP, IL-23 and IL-6 are shown in Fig. 1. Kruskal–Wallis 
omnibus tests demonstrated highly significant between-group differ-
ences for all three novel biomarkers (all P < 0.001). After Bonferroni 
adjustment for three pairwise contrasts, both active CD and IFX-CD 
patients showed significantly higher LBP, IL-23 and IL-6 concentra-
tions than HC (all adjusted P < 0.001), whereas no pairwise comparison 
between active CD and IFX-CD reached statistical significance for any 
biomarker (adjusted P ≥ 0.222). CRP, albumin, CAR and the LBP/al-
bumin ratio each differed significantly between CD groups and HC but 
did not distinguish active CD from IFX-CD after adjustment. Taken 
together, these data indicate that serum LBP, IL-23 and IL-6 remain at 
near active-disease concentrations in patients on long-term infliximab 
maintenance despite the clinical improvement documented in Table 1. 
To address whether persistent biomarker elevation reflected only the 
subset of infliximab-maintained patients with residual activity, we 
performed two pre-specified subgroup analyses. First, stratifying the 
IFX-CD group by HBI status, patients in clinical remission (n = 24) 
retained significantly higher LBP, IL-23, IL-6 and LBP/albumin ratio 
than healthy controls (all P ≤ 0.006), with no significant difference from 
patients with residual activity (n = 11; all P ≥ 0.40). Second, stratifying 
by mucosal-healing status (Table 4), the 12 patients with documented 
mucosal healing still showed IL-23 and IL-6 significantly above healthy 
controls (both P < 0.001), whereas their CRP did not differ from controls 
(P = 0.669); the mucosal-healing subgroup nonetheless showed lower 
medians than the non-healing subgroup for all markers, reaching sig-
nificance for IL-23 (P = 0.014) and borderline significance for LBP (P = 
0.050). Within the CD cohort, disease duration did not correlate posi-
tively with any biomarker (all ρ ≤ 0; e.g. LBP ρ = ␀ 0.23, P = 0.065; IL-6 
ρ = ␀ 0.21, P = 0.094), indicating that the longer disease duration of the 
IFX-CD group does not account for the observed elevations.
3.3. Diagnostic performance of serum biomarkers
Receiver operating characteristic (ROC) analyses are reported in 
Table 3 (Panel A) and visualised in Fig. 2. For discriminating all CD 
patients (n = 67) from HC (n = 50), IL-6 and IL-23 achieved excellent 
performance, with AUCs of 0.982 (95% CI 0.962–0.995) and 0.969 (95% 
CI 0.937–0.993), respectively. At the Youden-optimal cut-off of 54.95
pg/mL, IL-6 provided 91.0% sensitivity and 96.0% specificity; IL-23 at 
59.00 pg/mL yielded 89.6% sensitivity and 100% specificity. The LBP/ 
albumin ratio (AUC 0.782; 95% CI 0.695–0.860) and LBP alone (AUC 
0.762; 95% CI 0.671–0.845) offered good but more modest discrimi-
nation. Albumin, CAR and CRP yielded only fair discrimination (AUC 
0.667–0.712). When the two CD subgroups were compared, the novel 
biomarkers and LBP/albumin ratio retained moderate AUCs 
(0.702–0.730), whereas CRP, albumin and CAR did not discriminate 
active CD from IFX-CD (AUC 0.530–0.578; all not significant). To assess 
optimism in these estimates, AUCs were recomputed by 5-fold stratified 
cross-validation with standardisation and fitting confined to each 
training fold. Cross-validated AUCs were closely similar to the apparent 
values (IL-6 0.998, 95% CI 0.995–1.000; IL-23 0.976, 95% CI 
0.945–0.999; LBP/Alb 0.801, 95% CI 0.723–0.873; LBP 0.761, 95% CI 
0.675–0.841; combined IL-23 + IL-6 + CRP 0.999, 95% CI 
0.995–1.000), indicating limited optimism within this dataset. The very 
high AUC for IL-6 should nonetheless be interpreted with caution as a 
feature of this selected case–control sample, and external validation in 
consecutive, unselected cohorts is required before any diagnostic claim.
3.4. Biomarker and clinical correlations in CD patients
In the combined CD cohort (n = 67), Spearman correlation analysis 
revealed strong positive inter-correlations among the three novel bio-
markers (ρ = 0.72–0.86; all P < 0.001), as shown in Fig. 3. All three were 
positively correlated with CRP and CAR, and inversely correlated with 
albumin (all P ≤ 0.01). LBP, IL-23 and IL-6 showed significant positive 
correlations with HBI (ρ = 0.32–0.38; P < 0.01) and MUST score (ρ = 
0.36–0.50; P < 0.01), and negative correlations with BMI (ρ = ␀ 0.28 to
␀ 0.47; P < 0.05). The LBP/albumin ratio displayed the strongest clinical 
correlations of any biomarker evaluated, with HBI (ρ = 0.41), MUST (ρ
= 0.50) and BMI (ρ = ␀ 0.46; all P < 0.001). In contrast, CRP, albumin 
and CAR showed only non-significant trends. The very strong correla-
tions between LBP and the LBP/albumin ratio (ρ = 0.95), and between 
CRP and CAR (ρ = 0.99), are mathematical consequences of the ratios' 
construction. To complement the P values, effect sizes with 95% confi-
dence intervals were calculated for the principal comparisons. For CD 
versus HC the rank-biserial correlations were r = ␀ 0.95 (95% CI ␀ 1.00 
to ␀ 0.89) for IL-23, r = ␀ 0.53 (␀ 0.70 to ␀ 0.34) for LBP and r = ␀ 0.61 
(␀ 0.75 to ␀ 0.44) for the LBP/Alb ratio, confirming large between-group 
separations for the cytokines and moderate separations for the LBP 
measures. For the clinical correlations of LBP/Alb in the CD cohort, the 
confidence intervals were relatively wide and included values close to 
zero (HBI ρ = 0.24, 95% CI ␀ 0.01 to 0.48; MUST ρ = 0.21, 95% CI ␀ 0.03 
to 0.43), so these associations should be regarded as suggestive rather 
than firmly established in this sample.
