sole protective predictor (OR 0.38; 95% CI 0.21–0.58). The three-
biomarker Model 7 (IL-23 + IL-6 + CRP) produced the best perfor-
mance (AUC 0.995; McFadden R 2 = 0.812; Nagelkerke R 2 = 0.900). 
Within this model, IL-23 (adjusted OR 8.81; P < 0.001) and IL-6 
(adjusted OR 11.25; P < 0.001) remained independent predictors, 
whereas CRP was no longer independently significant (adjusted OR 
1.54; P = 0.171). Model 5 (LBP + IL-23 + LBP/albumin) produced an 
artefactual protective coefficient for LBP attributable to collinearity, and 
is retained only as a methodological illustration. To examine whether 
these associations were independent of participant characteristics, each 
biomarker was entered into a multivariable logistic model for CD versus 
HC adjusted for age, sex, BMI and smoking. IL-23 (adjusted OR 688; 95% 
CI 32–14,609), LBP (adjusted OR 3.6; 95% CI 2.1–6.4) and the LBP/Alb 
ratio (adjusted OR 6.1; 95% CI 2.9–12.7) remained independently 
associated with CD; the IL-6 model approached complete separation and 
its adjusted estimate is therefore not interpretable, reflecting the near-
perfect discrimination of IL-6 rather than a true infinite effect. Within 
the CD cohort, none of the biomarkers differed independently between 
active CD and IFX-CD after adjustment for age, sex, BMI and disease 
duration (all P > 0.4), consistent with the unadjusted subgroup 
comparisons.
4. Discussion
4.1. Principal findings
In this Iraqi cross-sectional case–control study of 117 adults, serum 
LBP, IL-23 and IL-6 were significantly elevated in both active CD and 
infliximab-maintained CD compared with healthy controls (all P < 
0.001 after Bonferroni adjustment). The central and novel observation 
of this study is that the serum LBP-to-albumin ratio outperformed LBP 
alone as a clinical correlate of disease activity and nutritional status: 
LBP/Alb showed numerically the strongest correlations with the HBI (ρ
= 0.41), MUST (ρ = 0.50) and BMI (ρ = ␀ 0.46; all P < 0.001), and 
achieved a diagnostic AUC of 0.782 for discriminating all CD from HC.
To the best of our knowledge, this is the first study to evaluate LBP/Alb 
as a composite biomarker in Crohn's disease, and the first Iraqi report of 
serum LBP in an IBD population.
4.2. Elevated serum LBP in Crohn's disease: mechanism and literature 
context
LBP is a ~ 60-kDa class-I acute-phase glycoprotein synthesised pre-
dominantly by hepatocytes — and, to a lesser extent, by intestinal 
epithelial cells — in response to IL-6, IL-1β and TNF-α. 32,33 LBP binds the 
lipid-A moiety of bacterial lipopolysaccharide (LPS), delivers it to 
membrane CD14 and the TLR4/MD-2 receptor complex, and thereby 
initiates the innate immune response. 13,14 Because plasma LPS has a 
short half-life and is technically demanding to assay, serum LBP serves 
as the preferred surrogate marker of ongoing bacterial translocation 
across a disrupted intestinal barrier. 34 In CD, increased intestinal 
permeability and translocation of luminal microbial products into the 
systemic circulation are recognised drivers of systemic inflammation, 
which in turn feed back on intestinal tight-junction disruption. 15
Our finding of significantly higher serum LBP in active CD (median 
9.55 ng/mL) and IFX-CD (7.04 ng/mL) versus HC (5.75 ng/mL) is 
qualitatively consistent with three key published studies. Pastor-Rojo 
and colleagues, in a prospective study of 104 CD and 52 UC patients, 
reported mean LBP of 23.1 ± 13.7 μg/mL in active CD versus 7.2 ± 1.8 
μg/mL in HC, with endotoxaemia in 48% of CD patients. 13 Lakatos and 
colleagues subsequently showed in 214 Hungarian CD patients that 
serum LBP was an independent predictor of clinical relapse at 12 
months, outperforming CRP in time-to-flare analyses. 35 Most recently 
(2024), Toris and colleagues in Brussels reported LBP medians of 29.6 
μg/mL in CD and 22.8 μg/mL in UC versus 5.8 μg/mL in controls; LBP 
increased with endoscopic activity and correlated with CRP (ρ = 0.75) 
and faecal calprotectin (ρ = 0.42), supporting LBP as a promising 
noninvasive biomarker for disease activity monitoring. 36
Absolute LBP concentrations differ substantially between studies 
because of differences in ELISA platforms, sample dilution protocols, 
calibration standards and sample handling; our focus is therefore on 
relative elevation and discriminative performance rather than numerical 
equivalence with European cohorts. The direction and discriminative 
pattern of our findings (CD > HC, P < 0.001; active CD approximately
