6

(0.712) and CAR (0.679). These values position LBP/Alb as a promising 
accessible composite index of active Crohn's disease integrating three 
biologically distinct axes — bacterial translocation (LBP numerator), 
systemic inflammation and nutritional status (albumin denominator) — 
within a single, simple calculation. A formal DeLong comparison of the 
two curves showed that the LBP/Alb ratio had a modestly higher AUC 
than LBP alone (0.803 vs 0.766; difference 0.038; z = 2.59, P = 0.010). 
Although this difference reached statistical significance, its magnitude is 
small, and given that the ratio correlates almost perfectly with LBP (ρ = 
0.95) we interpret LBP/Alb as offering a modest incremental improve-
ment rather than a substantially superior marker.
4.4. Persistence of LBP elevation during infliximab maintenance
A clinically important observation is that in infliximab-maintained 
patients — a majority of whom were in clinical remission (HBI ≤ 4 in 
68.6%) — both LBP and the LBP/Alb ratio remained significantly higher 
than in HC and did not differ significantly from active disease after 
Bonferroni correction. This pattern is consistent with the observation by 
Pastor-Rojo and colleagues that LBP “normalises” after treatment less 
completely in CD than in UC, 13 and with the view that anti-TNF therapy 
improves but does not fully restore intestinal barrier integrity. 15 In the 
“treat-to-target” paradigm 41 — which includes clinical, biochemical and 
mucosal targets — serum LBP and the LBP/Alb ratio may provide 
additional information about residual bacterial translocation not 
captured by CRP or symptom-based indices. Whether LBP or LBP/Alb 
can predict loss of response to infliximab in our population is an open 
question that requires prospective longitudinal data. Importantly, this 
persistence was not confined to patients with residual symptoms. Both 
the clinical-remission subgroup (HBI ≤ 4) and, more strikingly, the 
subgroup with documented mucosal healing retained IL-23 and IL-6 
concentrations significantly above healthy controls despite normal 
CRP (Table 4). The presence of a residual serum inflammatory signal 
even after mucosal healing suggests that these cytokines and the LBP/ 
Alb ratio may capture subclinical activity below the resolution of both
CRP and endoscopic assessment, although this requires confirmation 
with paired faecal calprotectin and longitudinal sampling.
4.5. IL-23 and IL-6 in the broader cytokine context
Although the primary novelty is LBP and the LBP/Alb ratio, the sec-
ondary findings of markedly elevated IL-23 and IL-6 are biologically 
informative. Elevated IL-23 reflects activation of the IL-23/Th17 axis, 
now a validated therapeutic target in CD through risankizumab 
(ADVANCE/MOTIVATE/FORTIFY), ustekinumab and mirikizumab. 42–44 
Elevated IL-6 drives hepatic acute-phase protein synthesis — including 
LBP itself — via gp130-STAT3 signalling. 11,12 The strong positive inter-
correlation among LBP, IL-23 and IL-6 in CD (ρ = 0.72–0.86) but not in 
HC supports a common inflammatory driver; IL-6 likely contributes 
directly to LBP elevation. An Egyptian study by Gheita and colleagues 
reported approximately two-fold elevation of IL-23 in IBD, 45 and on-
treatment IL-6 reduction predicts biologic response in mixed IBD co-
horts. The dissociation whereby CRP normalised during infliximab 
maintenance while IL-6 and IL-23 remained elevated is mechanistically 
coherent: hepatic CRP synthesis is a downstream, IL-6-driven acute-phase 
response that anti-TNF therapy suppresses efficiently, whereas IL-23 and 
IL-6 lie upstream in axes that infliximab targets incompletely. We inter-
pret these cytokines as consistent with, but not proof of, ongoing patho-
genic inflammation. Because we did not measure downstream Th17 
effector cytokines (IL-17 A, IL-22, GM-CSF), we refer throughout to IL-23 
elevation and IL-23/Th17-axis-associated signalling rather than to 
demonstrated Th17 pathway activation; serum IL-23 is an upstream 
driver and cannot, on its own, establish effector-level Th17 activity. 46
4.6. Comparison with Iraqi and regional studies
No previous Iraqi study has evaluated serum LBP or the LBP/albumin 
ratio in Crohn's disease. An earlier Iraqi serum cytokine panel study by 
Al-Abassi and colleagues (Baghdad/Mustansiriya) measured IL-8, IL-12, 
IP-10 and IL-10 in IBD but did not include LBP or IL-23. 47 A 2024 study
