A.A. Othman and W.S. Hameed Journal of Genetic Engineering and Biotechnology 24 (2026) 100798 
7

by Qusay and colleagues (College of Medicine, University of Baghdad) 
reported CXCL9 elevation in Iraqi UC patients, 48 and a 2025 study by 
Abdulla and colleagues from Sulaimani demonstrated that the 
lymphocyte-to-monocyte ratio was a useful inflammation marker in 
newly diagnosed Iraqi CD patients. 26 A 2024 Iraqi study from Karbala 
estimated LBP in urinary tract infection patients 49 but did not address 
IBD. Together, these confirm that Iraqi researchers have been devel-
oping biomarker programmes in IBD, but that the LBP/albumin ratio in 
Crohn's disease has not previously been reported from an Iraqi popula-
tion. A 2023 Saudi consensus guidance addresses IBD but does not 
specifically address LBP. 50 Our data therefore fill a specific regional 
knowledge gap regarding bacterial-translocation markers in Middle-
Eastern CD.
4.7. Diagnostic performance of LBP and LBP/Alb in perspective
The AUCs achieved by IL-6 (0.982) and IL-23 (0.969) exceed the 
0.65–0.85 commonly reported for CRP and approach the 0.88–0.97 re-
ported for faecal calprotectin in recent meta-analyses. 17,51 Although LBP 
and LBP/Alb (AUCs 0.762 and 0.782) did not reach these levels, they 
substantially outperformed CRP alone (0.667) and CAR (0.679). The 
LBP/Alb ratio combines low-cost measurement (albumin on any 
biochemistry analyser; LBP ELISA on a standard microplate reader) with 
good discriminative performance, making it a practical option in
resource-limited settings where faecal calprotectin assays are not 
routinely available. These models were derived and cross-validated on 
the same single-centre dataset; external validation is essential before any 
claim of diagnostic generalisability.
4.8. Strengths
Strengths of this study include (i) a well-characterised three-group 
design that explicitly compares active CD, infliximab-maintained CD 
and matched healthy controls, addressing a specific clinical knowledge 
gap; (ii) the simultaneous evaluation of a novel composite biomarker 
(LBP/albumin ratio) alongside established markers and informative 
cytokines; (iii) rigorous statistical analysis including non-parametric 
testing with Bonferroni adjustment, bootstrap-based ROC confidence 
intervals and cross-validated multivariable logistic regression; (iv) use of 
validated clinical instruments (HBI, MUST, Montreal, WHO BMI); and 
(v) to our knowledge, the first Iraqi CD study of serum LBP and the first 
published evaluation of LBP/albumin ratio in CD globally.
4.9. Limitations
Several limitations should be acknowledged. First, the cross-
sectional design precludes conclusions about the temporal kinetics of 
LBP and LBP/Alb, or their ability to predict relapse. Second, this is a
