A.A. Othman and W.S. Hameed Journal of Genetic Engineering and Biotechnology 24 (2026) 100798 
8

single-centre, single-country study; external validation is needed. Third, 
the sample size (n = 117) is modest, limiting subgroup precision. Fourth, 
faecal calprotectin was not measured. Fifth, we used the HBI for clinical 
activity rather than an endoscopic index (SES-CD). Sixth, absolute LBP 
values measured here are not directly numerically comparable with 
those reported by European cohorts due to differences in ELISA plat-
forms and sample-dilution protocols, although directional and relative 
comparisons are robust. Finally, the LBP/Alb analyses were exploratory; 
formal validation against a pre-specified threshold with confirmation in 
an independent dataset is the essential next step. Seventh, we lacked 
endoscopic, histological and faecal-calprotectin correlates, so we cannot 
exclude that part of the persistent serum signal reflects systemic immune 
activation unrelated to active mucosal disease rather than intestinal 
inflammation per se; paired serum–endoscopic sampling with faecal 
calprotectin is the priority next step. Eighth, downstream Th17 effector 
cytokines were not measured, limiting mechanistic inference about 
Th17-axis activation. Ninth, the LBP/albumin ratio's advantage over
LBP alone was modest and driven mainly by LBP, and the composite 
requires prospective validation before its incremental value can be 
claimed.
4.10. Future directions
These findings motivate several follow-up studies: (i) prospective 
longitudinal sampling of LBP and LBP/Alb during infliximab mainte-
nance to test whether rising LBP/Alb predicts subsequent relapse or loss 
of response; (ii) validation in independent Iraqi and regional cohorts, 
including patients on alternative biologics (ustekinumab, risankizumab, 
vedolizumab); (iii) paired serum–endoscopic and serum–transmural 
studies correlating LBP/Alb with SES-CD and cross-sectional imaging; 
(iv) head-to-head comparison with faecal calprotectin and with leucine-
rich α2-glycoprotein (LRG) 52 ; (v) cost-effectiveness analysis of LBP/Alb-
guided monitoring; and (vi) mechanistic work integrating LBP poly-
morphisms (e.g. rs2232618) with serum LBP, IL-23R status and gut 
microbiota profiling. 53
5. Conclusion
In this case–control study of 117 Iraqi adults, serum LBP, IL-23 and 
IL-6 were markedly elevated in both active Crohn's disease and 
infliximab-maintained Crohn's disease compared with healthy controls, 
with IL-6 and IL-23 demonstrating the highest discriminative accuracy 
(AUC ≥ 0.97 versus HC). Differences between active and infliximab-
maintained disease did not reach significance after Bonferroni correc-
tion, consistent with persistent subclinical IL-23/Th17-axis activity and 
bacterial translocation despite infliximab therapy. The LBP-to-albumin 
ratio — a novel composite biomarker integrating bacterial-
translocation and nutritional signals — correlated most closely with 
clinical activity (HBI) and nutritional risk (MUST) and outperformed 
CRP and CAR for diagnostic discrimination. These findings should be 
interpreted with caution given the cross-sectional, single-centre design
