prevention and cessation strategies are inadequate, and the availability 
of health services is significantly restricted. [5,13]
Over the past decade, disparities in lung cancer incidence and mor-
tality have emerged globally. [14–17] Herein, a contemporary assess-
ment of the global burden of lung cancer incidence and mortality is 
warranted to facilitate the national implementation of tailored preven-
tion and control strategies to lung cancer. In this study, we aimed to 
provide the most current assessment of lung cancer incidence and 
mortality in 185 countries at the global, regional, and national levels 
using the latest data from the Global Cancer Observatory (GLOBOCAN) 
2022. We also analyzed the future burden of lung cancer in 2050.
2. Methods
2.1. Study design and data sources
We extracted the number of new cases and deaths from cancer of the 
lung (International Classification of Diseases [ICD, 10th revision] code 
C33–34 [18]) among 185 countries or territories from the GLOBOCAN 
2022 database, [19] by sex and 18 age groups (ie, 0–4, 5–9, 10–14, 
15–19, 20–24, 25–29, 30–34, 35–39, 40–44, 45–49, 50–54, 55–59, 
60–64, 65–69, 70–74, 75–79, 80–84, and ≥85 years). [1,20] Corre-
sponding population data for 2022 were extracted from the United 
Nations (UN) website. [21] Furthermore, we analyzed global lung can-
cer incidence and mortality for 2050, considering various demographic 
scenarios from 2022, including annual increases, stability, or decreases. 
[22,23] The detailed description of data sources and methodology hi-
erarchy for compiling the cancer estimates is available elsewhere [19, 
20] with specific sources for each country publicly accessible on GLO-
BOCAN. [24] Briefly, GLOBOCAN assembles national cancer estimates 
from optimal data sources, using neighboring data when local sources 
are lacking. More details about the GLOBOCAN 2022 database and 
estimation methods are available online. [25] No inclusion or exclusion 
criteria were applied to the GLOBOCAN data in this study. Since the data 
used were publicly available, ethical approval or patient consent was not 
required for this research.
2.2. Statistical analysis
The tables and figures presented in our study are based on the esti-
mated number of lung cancer cases and deaths, as well as two summary 
measures (ie, incidence and mortality rates) using direct stand-
ardisation—namely, the age-standardised rate (ASR) per 100 000 
person-years based on the 1966 Segi-Doll World standard population. 
[26] These measures enable age-adjusted comparisons between pop-
ulations. The findings were reported based on individual countries and 
aggregated for 20 world regions (as defined by UN), as well as catego-
rized according to the four tiers of the Human Development Index (HDI). 
By using HDI, a composite measure of life expectancy, education, and 
per-capita income indicators for 2021 as developed by the UN Devel-
opment Programme, we could assess the burden of lung cancer at 
varying levels of development (ie, low, medium, high, and very high 
HDI). [27]
3. Results
3.1. Incidence and mortality worldwide
In 2022, it was estimated that 2480 675 new cases of lung cancer and 
1817 469 deaths were reported worldwide, corresponding to age- 
standardised incidence rate (ASIR) of 32⋅1 per 100 000 inhabitants 
and age-standardised mortality rate (ASMR) of 16⋅8 per 100 000 
(Table 1; Fig. 1). Lung cancer had exceeded other cancers to become the 
most common cancer and the leading cause of cancer death in the world, 
[19] contributing to about 12⋅4 % of new cases and 18⋅7 % of deaths 
among all cancers. Globally, the ASIR and ASMR of lung cancer in males 
are approximately two times higher compared to females. (ASIR: 32⋅1 vs 
16⋅2 cases per 100 000; ASMR: 24⋅8 vs 9⋅8 deaths per 100 000, 
respectively).
