3.2. Incidence and mortality by world region and country
Significant geographical disparities exist in lung cancer ASIRs, with 
Eastern Asia recording the highest rate (39.4 per 100,000), and regions 
of Africa typically exhibiting a low ASIR of less than 4.0 per 100,000, 
except for northern and southern Africa, which have ASIRs of 11.8 per 
100,000 and 16.8 per 100,000, respectively (Table 1; Fig. 2A). (Table 1; 
Fig. 2A). At national level, the highest ASIR was found in Hungary (47⋅6 
per 100 000), while the lowest ASIR was observed in Niger (1⋅0 per 100 
000; appendix pp 1–12). There were also disparities between males and 
females about ASIRs across geographical regions. For males, the highest 
ASIRs existed in eastern Asia, Micronesia and Polynesia, and eastern 
Europe with rates about 50 per 100 000. However, for females, the 
highest ASIRs were found in northern America, eastern Asia, and 
northern Europe with rates about 30 per 100 000. ASIRs for males and 
females were both generally lowest in western Africa, middle Africa, and 
eastern Africa with rates less than 4⋅0 per 100 000 (appendix pp 13–24). 
Mortality rates of lung cancer varied by at least 15 times between re-
gions, with the highest ASMR observed in Micronesia and Polynesia 
(31⋅1 per 100 000), followed by eastern Asia (25⋅1 per 100 000; Table 1; 
Fig. 2B). Conversely, western Africa exhibited the lowest ASMR (2.0 per 
100 000; Table 1) which was similar to landscape of incidence rates. 
Among countries, the highest ASMR was notable in Hungary (39⋅8 per 
100 000), while the lowest ASMR was seen in Niger (0⋅93 per 100 000; 
appendix pp 1–12). The disparity in lung cancer mortality rates between 
males and females differs significantly across regions, with eastern 
