Europe exhibiting the most pronounced difference, at 39.5 per 100,000 
for males compared to 9.1 per 100,000 for females (appendix pp 13–24).
Fig. 3 showed the ASIRs and ASMRs of lung cancer in countries 
exhibiting 30 countries with the highest incidence rates in 2022 among 
entire population. The high incidence rates were usually accompanied 
by high mortality in most countries. However, Japan and the United 
States had the highest rate difference between incidence and mortality 
rates.
Fig. 4 displayed the distribution of new cases and deaths due to lung 
cancer across different global regions. In both sexes, more than half of all 
new cases and 47 % of deaths occurred in eastern Asia. Moreover, China 
alone accounted for 42⋅7 % of the lung cancer cases (1 060 584) and 
40⋅3 % of deaths (733 291) in the world. The patterns of lung cancer 
incidence and mortality among males closely resemble the overall 
population distribution, and females exhibit a similar alignment with 
the general distribution. These data about the distribution of lung cancer 
in males or females were summarized in the appendix (appendix pp 
25–26).
3.3. Incidence and mortality by HDI
In 2022, the heaviest lung cancer burdens were in very high and high 
HDI regions, accounting for over 90 % of global cases and deaths. A clear 
socioeconomic trend linked higher HDI values with increased incidence 
and mortality rates (Fig. 5). The age-standardized incidence rate (ASIR) 
in very high and high HDI countries was about eight times that of low 
HDI countries, and the age-standardized mortality rate (ASMR) was over 
five times higher. These disparities were consistent among both males 
and females, as detailed in the appendix (pp 27–28).
3.4. Incidence and mortality by age
The ASIR exhibited a steep incline with age at diagnosis, rising from 
0⋅0 per 100 000 individuals aged 0–14 years to 247⋅1 per 100 000 
among those over 85 years old. (Table 2). Similar patterns applied to the 
ASMR (from 0⋅0 per 100 000 at ages 0–14 to 252⋅2 per 100 000 at ages 
85 years and over).
3.5. Future incidence and mortality burden in 2050
Global lung cancer cases are forecasted to grow 86.2 % from 2.48 
million in 2022–4.62 million by 2050, and deaths may increase by 95 % 
to 3.55 million, assuming stable rates (Fig. 6). The estimates, which 
consider population growth and aging, project increases in cases and 
deaths by 2050 across six scenarios with ASR fluctuations (-3 % to +3 % 
annually). The exception is a scenario projecting a 3 % annual decrease. 
Detailed sex-specific projections are included in the appendix (pp 
29–30).
4. Discussion
This study provided an up-to-date and comprehensive overview of 
global, regional, and national patterns of lung cancer incidence and 
mortality in 2022. With approximately 2⋅48 million new cases and 1⋅82 
million deaths, lung cancer stands as the leading cause of both cancer 
incidence and mortality worldwide. The incidence and mortality rates of 
lung cancer varied extremely across countries and regions. Higher 
incidence and mortality rates of lung cancer were notable in males and 
countries with very high and high HDI. Lung cancer incidence and 
mortality are projected to increase significantly in the upcoming de-
cades, unless an annual reduction of 3 % or more in rates is attained. 
These findings are expected to support governmental efforts and 
healthcare strategists in identifying and executing targeted in-
terventions to enhance advancements in the prevention and manage-
ment of lung cancer.
This study for 2022 showed large disparities in the distribution of 
lung cancer incidence and mortality globally, consistent with previous 
findings. [28,29] Eastern Asia and Micronesia and Polynesia had the 
largest burden of lung cancer, particularly eastern Asia, where approx-
imately 50 % new diagnosed cases and deaths were reported. China 
contributed the largest number of new cases and deaths in the world. 
Incidence and mortality rates of lung cancer were associated with so-
cioeconomic gradient, which may be a major reason to cancer dispar-
ities. [30] In a prospective study, a distinct socioeconomic gradient was 
identified, showing a gradual rise in lung cancer risk with increasing 
socioeconomic status. [31] Our study also revealed that countries with 
very high and high HDI exhibited elevated incidence and mortality 
rates. Countries with a high HDI had a higher proportion of older adults 
and prevalence of risk factors than other countries potentially due to a 
greater population of older adults, increased risk factors, enhanced 
health consciousness, and superior diagnostic capabilities.
Furthermore, it is imperative to acknowledge the existence of sex- 
based disparities in lung cancer outcomes, with males exhibiting 
significantly higher incidence and mortality rates. Such disparities may 
be associated with a multitude of factors, including a predisposition to 
tobacco use, genetic susceptibilities, and potential hormonal influences. 
[32]
Tobacco use is the predominant and well-documented risk factor for 
lung cancer, elevating the risk by 15–30-fold, correlating with both 
smoking quantity and duration. [5] Tobacco control is the most bene-
ficial intervention in the elimination of lung cancer. Tobacco smoking in 
economically developed countries has decreased markedly, which 
would translate into declines in lung cancer incidence a few decades 
later. [33] However, over the past three decades, smoking rates have 
seen little decline in LMICs, where intensified efforts to control tobacco 
use are needed. [34] Public health interventions have led to reduced 
lung cancer incidence in high-income nations, yet an upward trend 
persists in LMICs due to inadequate cessation programs and limited 
healthcare access, suggesting a continued global increase. Notably, a 
significant proportion of lung cancer cases, estimated at 15 %–20 %, 
affect non-smokers. [35] Radon, household air pollution and 
second-hand tobacco smoke have been proposed as major risk factors for 
lung cancer in never-smokers. The percentage of never-smokers diag-
nosed with lung cancer shows geographic variation, and there is an 
apparent global trend indicating a gradual increase. [35–37]
Lung cancer remains the leading cause of cancer deaths with an 
overall five-year survival rate of 27 %. [38] However, patients diag-
nosed with localized lung cancer generally have a better prognosis and is 
more treatable. [39] Screening programs are essential for early-stage 
lung cancer detection, crucial for reducing mortality and enhancing 
survival. Two large randomized controlled trials, including the National 
Lung Screening Trial (NLST) and Nederlands–Leuvens Longkanker 
Screenings Onderzoek (NELSON) trial, have demonstrated that 
screening with LDCT significantly reduced lung cancer mortality in 
high-risk populations. [40,41] Despite screening for lung cancer with 
LDCT offers benefits, it also carries high false-positive rates, which can 
lead to avoidable invasive procedures. [42–44] Other potential harms of 
screening include radiation exposure, the negative psychological impact 
of screening, as well as overdiagnosis. [45] A population-based 
ecological cohort study found that LDCT based screening program of 
most non-smoking Asian women was associated with considerable 
overdiagnosis of lung cancer. [46] The US Preventive Service Task Force 
(USPSTF) recommended yearly screening for lung cancer with LDCT for 
people aged 55–80 years who have at least a 20 pack-year smoking 
history and currently smoke or have quit within the past 15 years. [47]
Although smoking history and age are two key risk factors for lung 
cancer considered in the USPSTF guidelines, the magnitude of lung 
cancer risk still varies among eligible individuals because other several 
known risk factors are not taken into account. Therefore, in order to 
reduce the unnecessary harms associated with cancer screening and 
maximize the efficiency, risk stratification and individualized risk 
assessment is indispensable to identify high-risk individuals. Over the 
