past ten years, use of prediction models integrating several risk factors in 
lung cancer screening had gained credence. [48] In addition to LDCT 
screening, other methods for lung cancer screening, including liquid 
biopsies and artificial intelligence, are being investigated to increase the 
efficiency of lung cancer screening. [49–51]
During the past decade, despite progress in improving access to the 
prevention, screening, and treatment for lung cancer worldwide, 
tremendous racial, socioeconomic, and geographical disparities persist. 
Although screening based on LDCT has been shown to significantly 
decrease lung cancer mortality, population-level implementation is 
challenging, limiting its universal utilization and benefit. [52,53] Lung 
cancer screening eligibility criteria, as currently implemented, are 
mostly based on the risk of White men. [54] However, compared with 
White men, racial minorities and women are diagnosed with lung cancer 
at an earlier age and with less intense cigarette tobacco exposure, which 
could exacerbate lung cancer disparities. [55] There is also some evi-
dence that patients with lower socioeconomic position are also less in-
clined to be eligible for lung cancer screening. With the advent of 
targeted therapies and immunotherapies, major improvements have 
been made in lung cancer treatment. [12,56] The use of targeted ther-
apies and immunotherapies has improved the survival outcomes for 
patients with advanced lung cancer, and is expected to have 
population-level improvement in lung cancer survival. [12,57] How-
ever, the effects of targeted therapies and immunotherapies on lung 
cancer survival are likely to be limited in most LMICs, where the access 
to more advanced treatments is limited. [58] Therefore, it is critical to 
identify and address inequity in lung cancer control.
The estimates of incidence and mortality for lung cancer in 185 
countries or territories are based on the most recent and reliable sources 
of information nationally. Given the current global constraints in both 
the quality and comprehensiveness of cancer data, especially in LMICs, 
interpreting these estimates should be made with caution. The GLO-
BOCAN 2022 estimates did not take into account potential changes in 
lung cancer diagnosis due to the COVID-19 pandemic. The predictions 
for 2050 were not based on recent alterations in lung cancer incidence 
and mortality rates or risk factor exposures, nor did they consider het-
erogeneity in incidence and mortality trends across countries. As a 
result, there is considerable uncertainty in our predictions.
In summary, lung cancer ranked as the first most common cause of 
cancer incidence and mortality globally, surpassing all other cancers. 
This epidemiological assessment identified a major public health issue 
systematically, and our predictions indicated that lung cancer will 
