Keywords:
Cancer epidemiology in Italy
Methodology
Incidence
Mortality
Survival
ABSTRACT
Italy, home to one of the world’s oldest populations, has traditionally shown geographic differences in cancer 
incidence, with rates decreasing from north to south. The cancer registries that have been accredited by the 
Italian Cancer Registry Network (AIRTUM), during the last 20 years altogether cover the 90 % of the Italian 
population, aiming to improve data quality, standardize procedures, and promote research. This study presents 
the methodological approaches used for data collection, quality control, and analysis to describe current patterns 
of cancer incidence, mortality, and survival across Italy’s three macro-areas (North, Central, South). Estimates of 
incidence rates and case numbers for 2025 were also produced. Data from 34 accredited cancer registries were 
analyzed, comprising over 4.6 million cases from 1981 to 2020, with a detailed focus on the 2008–2017 period, 
which includes over 3 million cases. Cancer incidence and mortality data were collected according to ICD-O-3 
and ICD-10 classifications and processed for statistical analysis using tools such as SEERPrep, SEERStat, and 
the Joinpoint Regression Program. Age-standardized rates were calculated, and incidence and mortality trends 
from 2013 to 2017 were modeled. Five-year cumulative net survival was estimated using the Pohar-Perme 
method to adjust for competing risks. Survival trends were analyzed by geographic areas and cancer sites, 
revealing regional disparities in cancer outcomes.
1. Introduction
The Italian population is one of the oldest populations in the world, 
not only because the life expectancy at birth is quite long but also for the 
stable decrease in the number of births. Since cancer incidence and 
mortality rates are higher among elderly, a growing trend in the 
numbers of newly diagnosed cancers and deaths has been forecasted in 
the next decades [1].
Traditionally, Italy has shown geographic differences in cancer 
incidence, with rates decreasing from north to south. Although this 
trend is leveling off as dietary, social, and environmental differences 
diminish, local variations persist. In this context, the cancer registries 
that have been accredited by the Italian Cancer Registry Network 
(AIRTUM) [2], during the last 20 years, with the aim to improve data 
quality, standardize procedures, and promote research and training, 
altogether cover the 90 % of the Italian population. Since its foundation, 
the AIRTUM network has participated in many national and interna-
tional research activities. After the slowing down of registration activity 
due to the SARS-CoV-2 pandemic, updated estimates of cancer trends in 
Italy were needed, focusing on key indicators (incidence, survival, 
mortality) and cancers linked to infectious agents (e.g., HPV, hepatitis 
viruses, Helicobacter pylori).
