To this end, a collection of manuscripts [3] was conceived to describe 
the current patterns of cancer incidence and mortality in Italy using the 
best available cancer data from each registry and to provide an overview 
of the different trends in the three macro-areas —North, Central, and 
South— into which the country can be divided. This is a methodological 
paper presenting the methods used for the collection, quality control and 
analysis of the datasets constructed from data provided by 
AIRTUM-accredited registers across the national territory.
2. Material and methods
2.1. Data collection and processing
A call for data was sent to 40 AIRTUM-accredited registries, 
requesting validated data for each registry’s whole collection period. 
After negotiations between AIRTUM’s privacy experts and the data 
protection officers DPOs from the individual registries, 34 registries 
submitted data by July 2023 to the Umbria Cancer Registry, the pro-
ject’s coordinator.
To ensure that the acquisition and management of the database 
comply with European data privacy laws, the Umbria Cancer Registry 
set up a platform [4] with access restricted to authorized operators from 
each Cancer Registry, who upload data using SPID (Public Digital 
Identity System) credentials.
The data from the 34 registries, comprising a total of 4,693,358 cases 
(diagnosed from 1981 to 2020, varying among registries) were sub-
mitted according to a specific record set containing the following vari-
ables: sex, official code of the municipality of residence at diagnosis (for 
municipalities with less than 1,000 inhabitants, the code of the Prov-
ince); date of incidence; ICD-O-3T code (tumor topography) [5]; 
ICD-O-3M code (tumor morphology) [5]; basis of diagnosis; tumor 
sequence number; vital status; ICD-9 [6] or ICD-10 code of cause of 
death [7]. Optional variables were grading; tumor stage (pT, pN, M) 
according to AJCC/UICC guidelines [8] and the edition of TNM adopted.
Each record was identified by a completely anonymous code (Case 
Number), consisting of a prefix and a progressive numerical part, that 
identified the first occurrence of the individual in the database and was 
repeated in cases where the individual had more than one cancer 
diagnosis. Data was accepted in text format or Excel files.
An interdisciplinary working group, composed of staff from AIRTUM 
Cancer Registries, conducted quality and internal consistency checks on 
the entire database, making the necessary requests for further details 
and data integration to registers [9]. The final unique dataset was 
released in January 2024 and includes 3,032,435 cases from the period 
2008 – 2017.
2.2. Incidence data: inclusion criteria, classification of cases
Cancer data were provided according to the International Classifi-
cation of Diseases for Oncology (ICD-O) – 3rd edition [5] The topog-
raphy and morphology were subsequently recoded into the 10th 
revision of the International Classification of Diseases (ICD-10) [7].
All new malignant cancer diagnoses were considered, excluding non- 
melanoma skin cancers, and including non-infiltrating bladder cancers. 
All registries applied the standard incidence definition - as recom-
mended by the AIRTUM Registration Techniques Manual [10], inspired 
by IARC/IACR recommendations. The data, already validated by the 
individual registries, according to the internal AIRTUM protocol data 
quality [11], were further checked for completeness and accuracy. Two 
quality indicators referred to the period 2013–2017, grouped by area are 
presented in Table 1: the percentage of microscopically verified (MV) 
cases (i.e., the percentage of cases supported by cytological, histological 
or autopsy reports) and the percentage of cases known only from death 
certificates (DCO). These indicators were calculated for both sexes, for 
each cancer registry, and for the cancer sites analyzed in the study [9].
2.3. Mortality data: inclusion criteria and classification used
The mortality dataset was based on ISTAT (Italian National Statistics 
Institute) database [12] containing all causes of death disaggregated by 
year, sex, region, and province and grouped into five-year age brackets. 
During the period 2013–2017, 891,977 cancer deaths were observed 
(ICD-10 codes: C00-D48). Death cases were grouped according to the 
same cancer sites used for incidence.
2.4. Population data
Thirty-four cancer registries participated in the study, representing 
84 % of the Italian population. For regional or provincial registries, the 
population considered was that reported by ISTAT database [13] dis-
aggregated by year, sex, regions, provinces, and age brackets. For reg-
istries covering infra-provincial/regional territories, population data 
were requested from the individual registries
2.5. Life tables
The life tables, required for the statistical processing of survival data, 
were extracted from the ISTAT database [14] covering the period from 
2002 to 2022. To organize these lifetables into a unified database, with 
expected survival indexed by year, sex, place of residence (region or 
province), and age, as required by R software, the joinrate function was 
used. This versatile function is particularly relevant in the context of net 
survival analysis, as it allows for the integration of disparate data 
sources into a unified framework.
This task was essential for comparing the survival of a study popu-
lation to that of the general population. The resulting lifetable object 
provides a robust tool for evaluating net survival, enabling the assess-
ment of excess mortality and the effectiveness of medical interventions 
across different demographic groups.
2.6. Tools
The IARC conversion tool was applied to recode topography and 
morphology into the 10th revision of the International Classification of 
Diseases [6].
Incidence and mortality data were preprocessed using SEERPrep 
[15] to convert them to the SEERStat database format, after which 
incidence and mortality analyses were conducted using SEERStat [16]
and the Joinpoint regression program [17].
2.7. Statistical analysis
We analyzed incidence in two areas: observed standardized rates 
(2013–2017) and estimated rates/cases for 2024. We also calculated 
age-standardized mortality rates (ASMRs) for 2013–2017 and per-
formed survival analysis on cases from 2008 to 2017
2.7.1. Observed incidence rates
We included all cancer registries (CRs) to estimate incidence rates 
and trends. CRs with incomplete data (2013–2017) were included up to 
the last available year. So, for some years of the period, the estimated 
rates were based on a more limited dataset, according to the incidence 
