data available for each year. The incidence analysis in the period 
2013–2017 was based on 1,648,162 cases. Number of new cases were 
grouped by three age groups (<49, 50–69, >69) and three macro-areas 
(Northern, Central, Southern Italy) in addition to national estimates.
Table 2 lists the participating CRs, their registration periods, the 
population covered in the most recent available year, and the total 
number of cases. Table 3 shows the population covered and registered 
cases by macro-areas (2013–2017).
Cancer frequencies were computed by dividing the number of cases 
for a specific cancer site or type by the number of all cancers combined. 
Age-specific rates were computed dividing the number of new cases in 
each age-sex group by the corresponding population by sex and area. 
Age-standardized rates to the 2013 European population (ESP) (ASRs) 
[18] per 100,000 were estimated by the direct method by sex, cancer site 
or type and region. The cumulative risk 0–84 years was also calculated: 
it represents a ratio indicating the number of people who need to be 
followed over their lifetime for one of them to develop cancer. This 
method provides a measure of progress in the fight against neoplastic 
diseases over the years and allows comparisons between different pop-
ulations [19].
2.7.2. Incidence trend
We used the Joinpoint Regression Program [15] to model short-term 
cancer incidence trends (2013–2017). This method fits linear segments 
connected at join-points, identifying the year of trend changes and 
estimating the annual percentage change (APC). Trends were classified 
as increasing or decreasing if the p-value was less than 0.05; otherwise, 
they were considered stable. The period under analysis was restricted to 
2013–2017 because some registries had not produced data after 2017 
and some registries had not produced data before 2013. The selection of 
a wider period would have been possible only considering the subset of 
Cancer Registries for which data were available, but these registries 
showed a different incidence profile respect to the overall in this way 
artificially shifting estimates.
Also, due to the short period considered, the joinpoint analysis did 
not find any significant knots and then no change of slope. All the 
estimated APC values were then referred to the whole period 
2013.2017.
2.7.3. Estimates of incidence rates and number of cases as of 2025
We forecasted the ASR and the number of cases as of 2025 at a na-
tional level by considering population dynamics and using a custom- 
developed R program that combines observed incidence rates with 
APCs, following these steps: 
1. separately for each of the three age-bands, an age-specific APCwas 
estimated by a linear regression over the period 2013–2017
2. To protect against rapid observed rate changes that could make 
unrealistic the computation of predicted incidence rates, absolute 
values of APCs were limited by setting a threshold equal to 2.5, both 
for cancer incidence estimated growth (APC ≤ 2.5) and decline (APC 
