≥␀ 2.5). The constraint was applied to 27 % of APC estimates, 
generally due to limits exceeded by few decimal points.
3. The incidence rates observed at 2017 were then multiplied by the 
estimated APC value, when significantly different from zero (two 
sided p -value > 0.05), raised to 8 (the difference in years between 
2025 and 2017). When the APC did not result statistically different 
from zero, the observed incidence rates were assumed as constant up 
to the year 2025.
4. The number of cancer cases was predicted by multiplying the pre-
dicted incidence rates for 2025 by the estimated population size in 
the same year, for each age group and summing up the number of 
cases of the three age-bands.
5. ASRs (by sex and by cancer site) as of 2025 were estimated for the 
whole of Italy as a weighted sum of corresponding age-specific rates 
obtained in the previous step, with weight derived by the European 
standard population.
2.7.4. Mortality rates
Generally, mortality data may be less accurate than incidence data 
due to possible inaccuracies in medical certification and coding. How-
ever, since the project used a common data source - ISTAT - it is plau-
sible to assume a substantial homogeneity in the classification of causes 
of death. We included all cancer death data in the estimation of mor-
tality rates and trends. Table 4 shows data on the total population 
covered and the number of cancer deaths in the period of interest 
(2013–2017) by macro-areas.
For the 891,977 cancer deaths (ICD-10 codes: C00-D48) observed 
between 2013 and 2017 and grouped by the same tumor sites used for 
incidence, the frequencies of the main causes of death (by sex, age 
group, and macro-regions) and the ASMRs based on the 2013 European 
population (by tumor site and sex) were calculated [18]. We also 
computed short-term mortality trends (2013–2017) using the same 
method described for the incidence rates.
2.7.5. Survival estimates
Our analysis was based on a total of 3,032,435 cases aged 15–99 and 
diagnosed with cancer during 2008–2017. The common closure date for 
follow-up on life status was set to December 31, 2022. Second and 
subsequent primary cancers were included, and non-malignant cancers 
were considered only for the bladder. Data from 57,844 DCO cases, and 
149,981 records with major errors in the mandatory variables were 
excluded from the analyses. Not all registries covered the same inci-
dence and follow-up periods as reported in Table 5. Specific data sets 
were then selected for individual analysis.
All 34 population-based registries were included in the analysis of 5- 
year cumulative net survival, using the most recent 5-year incidence 
period and stable coverage available from each registry, totaling 
1,619,274 cases (excluding DCO), of which 79,347 (4.9 %) were right- 
censored patients (with follow-up less than 5 years from diagnosis).
For the analysis of time trends, conditional survival, and 10-year 
cumulative survival, the incidence period was extended to 2008–2017, 
including only 20 registries that covered the same population during the 
entire period. The list of considered registries’ incidence data is high-
lighted in green in Table 5; a total of 1,487,450 cases were included in 
these analyses (Table 6 and Table 5), including 60,543 right-censored 
patients within 5 years of follow-up.
Net Survival at year t after diagnosis, NS(t), was estimated using the 
Pohar-Perme approach, a widely recognized method for measuring 
cancer survival in the presence of competing risks.
This approach adjusts for background mortality by comparing the 
survival of cancer patients to that of the general population with similar 
demographic characteristics (such as age, sex, and calendar period). By 
isolating the effect of the specific disease, the Pohar-Perme method 
corrects for non-cancer-related deaths, ensuring that the net survival 
estimate reflects mortality attributable solely to the cancer diagnosis 
[22].
This adjustment is particularly valuable in population-based cancer 
studies, where individuals may die from causes unrelated to the disease 
being studied. By adjusting for these factors, the Pohar-Perme approach 
ensures a more accurate reflection of cancer-specific survival, making it 
a crucial method in both clinical and epidemiological settings [21] and 
offering a more standardized survival measure [22].
As cancer survival estimates improve, thanks to early detection and 
advances in treatment, it becomes increasingly important to distinguish 
deaths due to cancer from those caused by aging or other diseases [21].
Expected survival was calculated for each 1-year period of follow-up 
using population life tables specific by sex, age, place of residence and 
calendar year [14].
Age-standardized survival was calculated using the international 
standard cancer patient distribution, as proposed by Corazziari et al. 
[23]. This method adjusts survival rates for differences in the age dis-
tribution of cancer patients across populations, enabling more accurate 
comparisons between countries or regions with varying demographic 
profiles. The age-standardization process involves weighting 
age-specific survival estimates by a standard age distribution, which 
ensures that observed survival differences are not simply due to varia-
tions in the age structure of the patient populations. This is especially 
important in cancer epidemiology, as the incidence and prognosis of 
many cancers vary significantly with age [23].
Conditional survival CS(t1|t0) at 6, 8, and 10 years for patients alive 
at 1, 3, and 5 years since diagnosis was calculated by dividing the net 
survival estimates NS(t) at the end by those at the beginning of the 
considered interval: 
