diagnosed in 2012 or earlier, with follow-up closure at the end of 2022.
The complete approach was used for the estimation of 10-year net 
and conditional survival, offering a more comprehensive assessment of 
long-term survival outcomes. This method involves following the entire 
cohort of patients for the full period of interest, accounting for both early 
and late deaths, and thus providing a robust estimate of net survival over 
extended follow-up periods [20].
Geographical comparisons of 5-year net survival between three large 
geographical areas (North, Center and South) were based on age stan-
dardized survival, using the international standard cancer patient dis-
tribution [23].
Time trends of age standardized 5-year net survival was based on the 
five bi-annual incidence periods 2008–9, 2010–11, 2012–13, 2014–15, 
2016–17.
2.8. Presentation of data
In this special issue, papers contain a set of tables including estimates 
of incidence, mortality and survival by sex and by cancer sites. Twenty- 
nine groups were used for analysis plus the group of all malignant tu-
mors (excluding non-melanoma skin, including non-infiltrating bladder) 
(Table 7). We also used three age groups for some analyses: 0–49, 50–69 
and > 70 years.
2.8.1. Incidence data table
We created frequency tables showing cancer cases by site, the top 
five cancers by sex and age group, and cumulative risk (0–84 years) by 
sex and site. Incidence ASRs are presented by sex, cancer site, and 
macro-areas.
2.8.2. Mortality data table
The mortality tables have the same structure as those produced for 
incidence estimation: frequency tables with the absolute number of 
deaths and top five tumors by mortality, by sex, cancer site and age 
group; tables with ASMRs by sex and cancer site and by macro-areas.
2.8.3. Incidence and mortality trends
We produced a set of graphs with 5-years trends (2013–2017) by sex, 
by age-group and by macro-areas for each of the 29 cancer sites plus all 
malignant tumors (excluding non-melanoma skin, including non- 
infiltrating bladder).
2.8.4. Survival data table
The number of patients provided by the registries ranged between 
8563 in “Valle d’Aosta” (minimum by area: 8563 in North, 103,394 in 
Centre, and 17,815 in South and Islands) and 696,273 in “Veneto” 
(maximum by area: 696,273 in North, 446,481 in Centre, and 312,904 
in South and Islands).
Although the survival status in the table is as reported by the regis-
tries, their inclusion in survival analysis depends on the follow-up 
provided.
In survival analysis, “lost” patients are those whose status is un-
known at the end of the study period, unlike right-censored data, where 
patients are known to be alive at the last follow-up but haven’t experi-
enced the event of interest.
Table 7 summarizes the calendar years covered and the latest 
available follow-up year for each cancer registry, spanning from 2008 to 
2018 with follow-up years ranging from 2015 to 2023. For some cancer 
registries, data was available starting from 1997; however, as these 
years are not included in the analysis, they are not shown.
The notation ""5y-surv"" refers to data used to estimate net survival up 
to 5 years from diagnosis, crucial for assessing mid-term cancer patient 
outcomes and calculating 5-year survival. The incidence data high-
lighted in green is used to estimate 5-year net survival for biennial trends 
and conditional survival up to 5 years from diagnosis.
