ARTICLE INFO
Keywords:
Secondary primary lung cancer
Breast cancer survivors
Radiotherapy
Elderly Women
Survival Analysis
ABSTRACT
This retrospective cohort study using SEER data from 2000 to 2020 examines the impact of radiotherapy on the
riskofSecondaryPrimaryLungCancer(SPLC)in224,396elderlyfemalebreastcancersurvivors.Patientstreated
with radiotherapy displayed a 31 % increased SPLC risk compared to those not treated. Utilizing Cox Propor-
tional Hazards and Poisson regression models, the study assessed various factors including age, race, and tumor
characteristics. Propensity Score Matching (PSM) was employed to balance cohorts for survival analysis, which
revealed that radiotherapy did not negatively impact overall survival despite the increased risk of SPLC. A
nomogramwasdevelopedtoaidclinicaldecision-makingbypredictingsurvivaloutcomes.Thefindingsadvocate
for personalized treatment strategies and continuous monitoring to manage potential long-term adverse effects
effectively, highlighting the need for a balanced approach in the treatment of breast cancer survivors.
Introduction
Breastcanceristhemostcommonlydiagnosedcanceramongwomen
globally,whilelungcancerranksastheleadingcauseofcancerdeathfor
both men and women worldwide [1]. Demographic shifts towards an
aging population [2,3], coupled with advances in early detection,
diagnosis, and treatment have significantly improved the outcomes for
breast cancer patients [4]. Recent advancements in breast cancer ther-
apy, including targeted drug discovery and the application of nano-
technology, have revolutionized treatment approaches. Targeted
therapies, such as antibody-drug conjugates and immune checkpoint
inhibitors, have significantly enhanced treatment precision by address-
ing tumor heterogeneity and specific molecular pathways [5]. Concur-
rently, nanotechnology-based treatments offer novel methods for
delivering anti-cancer agents with increased specificity and reduced
systemictoxicity,showinggreatpromiseinovercomingdrugresistance
and enhancing therapeutic efficacy [6]. Additionally, natural com-
pounds like ginsenosides have demonstrated potent anti-tumor activity
against breast cancer cells, providing a complementary approach to
conventional therapies and opening new avenues for future research
[7]. As a result, the number of elderly breast cancer survivors has
steadily increased [8], highlighting the critical need to investigate
long-term health outcomes, particularly the incidence of secondary
cancers in this demographic [9].
Radiation therapy is commonly used in the management of breast
cancer to reduce the risk of recurrence and improve overall survival
rates [10]. However, radiation therapy can also have long-term side
effectsandimpactthequalityoflifeofbreastcancersurvivors[11].The
ionizing radiation used in radiotherapy can cause collateral damage to
the DNA of surrounding healthy cells, potentially leading to genetic
mutationsthatincreasetheriskofsecondarycancers[12,13].Moreover,
radiotherapy can alter the microenvironment of tissues, promoting in-
flammatory responses and cellular changes conducive to the develop-
ment of new cancerous growths [14,15]. Secondary Primary Lung
Cancer(SPLC)isasignificantanddistinctriskforindividualspreviously
treated for breast cancer, particularly attributed to therapeutic in-
terventions such as radiotherapy [16]. Unlike metastatic cancer, which
spreads from the original tumor site, SPLC arises independently and is
