characterized as a completely new malignancy. Despite these known
risks, studies exploring the mechanisms linking radiotherapy to SPLC
development remain scarce, especially those focusing on elderly breast
cancer survivors. By investigating this underexplored relationship, our
research seeks to provide a clearer understanding of how radiotherapy
contributes to SPLC risk.
TheSurveillance,Epidemiology,andEndResults(SEER)programby
the National Cancer Institute (NCI) is a comprehensive source of U.S.
cancer data, covering about 34 % of the population [17]. It offers
detailed records on cancer incidence, survival, treatment, and patient
demographics, making it crucial for both epidemiological and clinical
research [18]. While the SEER database has been extensively used for
studying cancer outcomes, its application in analyzing SPLC risk and
survival among elderly female breast cancer survivors remains limited.
This study bridges this gap by leveraging SEER data to examine the
long-term effects of radiotherapy on SPLC incidence and survival,
providing critical insights for this vulnerable population. This study le-
veragesdatafromtheSEERprogramtoinvestigatethelong-termeffects
of radiotherapy on the incidence of SPLC among elderly female breast
cancer survivors. Using a large, population-based cohort, we employ
advanced statistical methods, including the Cox Proportional Hazards
Model and competing risks analysis, to evaluate both SPLC risk factors
and survival outcomes. By comparing incidence rates between patients
who received radiotherapy and those who did not, we aim to identify
specific risk factors associated with SPLC development and quantify
their impact on long-term survival. Additionally, this study develops a
predictive nomogram based on identified risk factors to facilitate indi-
vidualized clinical decision-making. The findings will contribute to a
deeper understanding of radiotherapy-associated risks and support the
design of more personalized treatment strategies that balance thera-
peutic efficacy with long-term safety for this vulnerable population.
Methods
Data source
The present retrospective cohort study utilized data retrieved from
the SEER Research Data, comprising 17 Registries with records from
November 2022, covering the years 2000 to 2020. This dataset is
renowned for its exhaustive records on cancer incidence, therapeutic
interventions, and patient outcomes, providing a robust foundation for
epidemiological investigations.
Study population and group definitions
We meticulously assembled a cohort of 233,584 elderly female pa-
tients, all 65 years or older, diagnosed with breast cancer according to
ICD-O-3 codes ’C50.0–50.9′, ensuring each case was substantiated with
histological confirmation and surgical intervention records. The cohort
was subsequently refined to 224,396 patients through the exclusion of
individualswithambiguoussurvivaldata,indeterminatecancerstaging
(Distant/Unstaged), unestablished cause-specific survival status (’Dead
(missing/unknown COD)’), or non-specified racial data.
The patient cohort was stratified into several key groups based on
radiotherapy history and subsequent oncological diagnoses:
RT (Radiotherapy): Encompassed elderly female breast cancer
Fig. 1. Flowchart of Study Cohort Selection and Analysis.
J. Lu et al. Translational Oncology 52 (2025) 102277 
2

survivors who received radiotherapy.
NRT (Non-Radiotherapy):Includedsurvivorswithoutradiotherapy
exposure.
SPLC (Secondary Primary Lung Cancer): Defined as lung malig-
nancies codified under ICD-O-3 codes C34.0-C34.9, manifesting a min-
imum of five years subsequent to breast cancer treatment, to clearly
delineate from metastatic or concurrent lung neoplasms.
FPLC (First Primary Lung Cancer):Consistedofpatientsforwhom
lungcancer,ascertainedthroughthesameICD-O-3codes,wastheinitial
cancer diagnosis, thus facilitating a baseline risk assessment for SPLC
development.
RT_SPLC:Denotedpatients fromtheRTcohortwho laterdeveloped
SPLC.
NRT_SPLC: Denoted patients from the NRT cohort who later devel-
oped SPLC.
For the lung cancer cohort, we adhered to the same stringent inclu-
sion criteria, curating a group of elderly female lung cancer patients
while applying identical exclusion standards as with the breast cancer
group.
Competing risks model and Poisson regression model
ToaccountforthepresenceofcompetingeventsinourstudyonSPLC
incidence, we applied the Fine and Gray subdistribution hazard model.
In constructing our model, we integrated a multifactorial analysis to
control for various known confounders, including demographic details
and clinical features of the breast cancer survivors. This approach
allowed for the assessment of the isolated effect of radiotherapy on the
risk of SPLC. Subgroup analyses were an integral part of our method-
ology, aiming to determine the consistency of radiotherapy’s effect
across different patient demographics and tumor characteristics. The
findings from these analyses were visually summarized in a forest plot,
providing a clear representation of hazard ratios and their correspond-
ing confidence intervals across patient subgroups.
The Poisson regression model was employed to evaluate the impact
of radiotherapy and patient age on the development of SPLC. We esti-
mated the relative risk (RR) of radiotherapy across varying age groups
and diagnostic periods, adjusting for essential demographic covariates.
