Propensity score matching (PSM) and survival outcome analysis
For our analysis, we employed Propensity Score Matching (PSM)
using a 1:1 nearest neighbor matching algorithm without replacement,
with a caliper width set to 0.02 of the standard deviation of the logit of
the propensity score. This approach balanced the cohorts of patients
with First Primary Lung Cancer (FPLC) against those with SPLC, and
further categorized into RT_SPLC and NRT_SPLC groups. These group-
ings facilitated rigorous comparisons to evaluate the specific impact of
radiotherapy on the incidence and survival outcomes of SPLC post-
breast cancer treatment. Survival analysis was conducted through
Kaplan-Meier estimates to assess overall and cancer-specific survival
across these groups, thereby providing comprehensive insights into the
long-term effects of radiotherapy.
Cox proportional risk model
We utilized the Cox Proportional Hazards Model to evaluate the
survival impacts among elderly female breast cancer survivors who
developed Secondary Primary Lung Cancer (SPLC) after receiving
radiotherapy.Theanalysisconsideredacomprehensivesetofvariables,
including age, race, tumor characteristics, and treatment specifics. The
datasetwasdividedintotrainingandvalidationcohortsataratioof7:3,
with929patientsinthetrainingcohortand398inthevalidationcohort.
Instead of conducting preliminary univariate analyses to select var-
iables, we included all relevant covariates in the multivariate Cox
model. This approach mitigates the risk of omitting potentially signifi-
cant predictors and accommodates the complex interactions between
variables.Thisholisticstrategyisparticularlyvaluableinsettingswhere
the interplay between demographic and clinical factors is intricate and
not well understood. A nomogram was developed from the finalized
multivariate model to facilitate the practical application of the model’s
findings.
The model’s efficacy was rigorously assessed using Receiver Oper-
atingCharacteristic(ROC)curves,calibrationplots,andDecisionCurve
Analysis (DCA). These evaluation techniques demonstrated that the
model performs well in predicting survival outcomes, with ROC curves
confirming high sensitivity and specificity, calibration plots showing
good agreement between predicted and observed outcomes, and DCA
indicating significant clinical net benefits.
Statistical analysis
Data extraction was performed using SEER*Stat software version
8.4.3, focusing on elderly female breast cancer survivors aged 65 and
above. Detailed patient data handling and statistical analyses were
conductedusingRsoftware,version4.3.3.Descriptivestatisticssuchas
mean,median,standarddeviation,range,andquartileswereutilizedto
summarize continuous variables, while categorical variables were
analyzed using frequency tables and bar charts. For categorical vari-
ables,weappliedPearson’sChi-squaredtesttoexamineassociationsand
differences among groups. Forquantitativevariables, dependingon the
distribution, we employed the Welch Two Sample t-test for normally
distributed data and the Wilcoxon rank sum test for non-normally
distributed data. These tests ensured the robust handling of both types
of datadistributions. Foroursurvivalanalysisincorporatingcompeting
risks, we utilized the tidycmprsk package in R to effectively model the
cumulative incidence functions of secondary primary lung cancer
(SPLC),consideringothercompetingriskslikedeathfromothercauses.
This approach was crucial for accurately capturing the complex dy-
namicsofcompetingeventsintheanalysis.TheprocessofStudyCohort
Selection and Analysis is shown in Fig. 1.
