Competing risks model
In the studied cohort of elderly female breast cancer survivors, the
competingrisksanalysisdepictedanotabledistinctioninthecumulative
incidence of SPLC between those who had received radiotherapy (RT)
and those who had not (NRT), with a persistently higher incidence
observed in the RT group (Fig. 2). A multifactorial competing risks
modelhighlightedradiotherapyasasignificantfactor,withanelevated
risk of SPLC (HR= 1.31, p < 0.001) even after adjusting for various
demographic and clinical variables, underscoring its prominence in the
study’sfindings(Table2).Accordingtotheforestplot(Fig.3),subgroup
analysis within the cohort of elderly female breast cancer survivors
revealed a consistent association between radiotherapy and increased
risk of second primary lung cancer across almost all age groups, tumor
characteristics, and treatment variations. This persistent effect of
radiotherapy on the risk of developing SPLC emphasizes its critical
impact as a uniform risk factor for these patients.
Poisson regression model
TheanalysisresultsfromthePoissonregressionmodeldemonstrated
a notable influence of radiotherapy and age on the risk of developing
SPLC among elderly female breast cancer survivors. Specifically, pa-
tients who underwent radiotherapy had a 31 % higher risk of SPLC
compared to those who did not (RR= 1.31, 95 % CI: 1.21–1.43, p <
0.001).Agealsoplayedacriticalrole,withthoseinthe65–74agegroup
exhibiting a nearly tenfold increased risk (RR = 9.99, 95 % CI:
7.09–14.67, p< 0.001) and the 75–84 age group showing a more than
fivefoldincreasedrisk(RR=5.23,95%CI:3.69–7.71, p<0.001),both
comparedtothereferencegroupofpatientsolderthan85.Additionally,
the year of initialbreast cancerdiagnosis significantlyaffected the risk,
with the earliest cohort (2000–2004) facing the highest risk (RR=
23.77, 95 % CI: 14.12–44.50, p < 0.001), indicating the influence of
evolving treatment protocols or detection methods over time.
The graphical analysis clarifies that the relative risk (RR) of SPLC
associated with radiotherapy varies according to the patient’s age and
the year of breast cancer diagnosis. The Fig. 4a reveals that the RR of
SPLCassociatedwithradiotherapyincreasesnotablyastheagecategory
decreases from over 85 to 65–74. The Fig. 4b illustrates that the RR of
SPLC related to radiotherapy is higher for patients diagnosed in earlier
years, with the risk gradually decreasing for those diagnosed more
recently. These visual trends corroborate the quantitative findings,
indicating that both the patient’s age at diagnosis and the historical
context of their treatment are pivotal factors in managing long-term
health risks post-radiotherapy. Table 3
