Cox proportional risk model
The training cohort utilized for the Cox proportional hazards model
consistedof929elderlyfemalebreastcancersurvivorswithahistoryof
radiotherapy and subsequent development of SPLC, while the internal
test cohort included 398 patients. Demographic and clinical character-
isticssuchasage,race,tumorstage,andhistologywerecloselymatched,
with the detailed data presented in Table 4. This rigorous matching
process ensures the model’s validity and the representativeness of the
population under study.
The Cox proportional hazards model’s outcomes for elderly female
breast cancer survivors with SPLC are comprehensively detailed in the
study. Univariate analysis results, highlighted in Table 5, show age,
tumor grade, histology, and stage as significant predictors of survival.
Particularly notable were the higher hazard ratios associated with
advanced tumor grades and aggressive histological types such as SCLC.
Themultivariateanalysis,presentedinTable6,adjustedforcovariates,
underscored the protective effects of surgery and radiotherapy on sur-
vival. Surgery significantly reduced the risk of death by 81 % (HR=
0.19, p< 0.001), while radiotherapy also showed a substantial protec-
tive impact (HR= 0.46, p< 0.001). The addition of chemotherapy was
similarly beneficial (HR= 0.56, p< 0.001).
The final Cox model included 9 independent predictors (Age, Race,
Tumor histology, Grade, Stage, Surgery perform, Chemotherapy,
Radiotherapy,andYearofdiagnosis)andwasdevelopedasasimple-to-
use nomogram, which is illustrated in the following Fig. 6.
