screening strategies, and higher thresholds (e.g., above 50 %) reflect
diminishing net benefits for intensive interventions. The consistent
performance across training and validation cohorts highlights the
model’s robustness, generalizability, and potential for optimizing
patient-specific treatment strategies in diverse clinical settings. Fig. 9
Discussion
Our study investigated the long-term impact of radiotherapy on
elderly female breast cancer survivors who later developed Secondary
Primary Lung Cancer (SPLC). The findings revealed a significant 31 %
increased risk of SPLC associated with radiotherapy compared to those
who did not receive this treatment, highlighting its role in elevated
secondary cancer risk. Propensity Score Matching (PSM) analyses
furtherclarifiedthatdespitethisincreasedincidence,radiotherapydoes
not negatively influence the overall survival rates of these patients,
suggesting that the detrimental effects of radiotherapy on SPLC inci-
dence do not extend to survival outcomes. Additionally, the Cox
