Research across various cancer types has consistently shown an
elevated incidence of non-small cell lung cancer, particularly adeno-
carcinoma, in survivors [19]. Additionally, breast cancer survivors
exhibit an elevated risk, with evidence suggesting a Standardized Inci-
dence Ratio (SIR) of 1.31 (95 % CI: 1.23–1.39) within the first year
post-diagnosis, which escalates to 2.9 twenty years post-diagnosis.
Notably, survivors of triple-negative breast cancer (TNBC) display
particularlyelevatedSIRsof1.59(95%CI:1.29–1.94)[20].Long-term
studiesrevealthatSIRsincreaseovertime,escalatingto2.9forthose20
or more years post-diagnosis [21].
Radiotherapy for breast cancer, while effective in targeting primary
tumors, can inadvertently expose nearby tissues to ionizing radiation
[22]. This exposure can lead to DNA damage in surrounding healthy
cells[23],increasingtheriskofgeneticmutationsandthedevelopment
of SPLC. Furthermore, the altered tumor microenvironment
post-radiotherapyfostersconditionsthatmaypromotetheemergenceof
secondary malignancies [24,25]. Empirical evidence derived from
variousstudiesunderscorestheimpactofradiotherapyontheincidence
of secondary malignancies in high-dose radiation fields. Specifically,
Poissonregressionanalyses,adjustedforclinicalvariablessuchasstage,
age at treatment, year of treatment, chemotherapy, and hormonal
therapy, reveal a relative risk (RR) of 1.45 (95 % CI: 1.33–1.58) for
developing second cancers in areas including the lung, esophagus,
pleura, bone, and soft tissue [16]. Additionally, radiotherapy has been
independently identified as a significant risk factor for lung cancer in
breast cancer patients, with an RR of 1.40 (p< 0.001) [26]. A longitu-
dinal studyfurther illustrates the temporalprogression of risk, showing
standardized incidence rates of lung cancer increasing from 1.25 (95 %
CI 1.05, 1.40) after five years to 1.91 (95 % CI 1.11, 3.29) after fifteen
yearspost-radiotherapy[27].However,acomprehensivemeta-analysis
encompassing 10 studies reported no significant association between
breastcancerradiotherapyandtheincidenceoflungcancer(RR=0.95,
95 % CI 0.87–1.02, P= 0.15) [28]. This discrepancy underscores the
complexity of assessing long-term cancer risks associated with radio-
therapy, highlighting the influence of study-specific methodologiesand
demographic variables on the outcomes.
This study builds upon previous research by providing a more
granular analysisof the long-term risks associated with radiotherapy in
elderly female breast cancer survivors. By employing a comprehensive
set of variables, such as stage, age at treatment, and specifics of con-
current therapies, and by utilizing robust statistical methodologies like
competing risk models and Cox proportional hazards models, our
researchdelineatesthenuancedrelationshipsbetweenradiotherapyand
secondarycancerrisksmoredistinctly.Particularly,thisstudyuniquely
contributes to the field by highlighting the progression of lung cancer
riskoverextendedperiodspost-treatment,offeringvaluableinsightsfor
developing tailored monitoring strategies and refining therapeutic ap-
proaches to balance efficacy with safety in this vulnerable population.
Our study employing a competing risks model indicates that elderly
female breast cancer survivors with a history of radiotherapy are at a
higher risk of developing SPLC compared to those without such a his-
tory,atrendthatpersistsacrossmostsubgroupsanalyzed.Notably,this
elevated risk is significant in younger age groups, among White and
Black patients, across all tumor histologies, and stages except the
smallest tumor sizes. Similarly, the risk remains significant across all
hormonal receptor statuses, particularly where status remains undeter-
mined. The non-significant risk among survivors aged 85 and above
suggests a possible diminished impact of radiotherapy with advancing
ageordatalimitationswithinthissubgroup.Fortumorssizes≤2cm,the
treatment protocol typically involves less extensive or aggressive
radiotherapy due to their generally less aggressive nature and greater
responsiveness to treatment [29]. This approach might reduce the
likelihood of secondary cancer development by minimizing radiation
exposure to surrounding healthy tissues [30]. Furthermore, the hetero-
geneity within the ""Other"" racial category and potentially smaller sam-
ple sizes may dilute observable effects, making it difficult to establish a
clear risk pattern.
The survival analysis from our propensity score-matched study
demonstrates that elderly female breast cancer survivors who develop
SPLC after radiotherapy do not experience worse survival outcomes
compared to those with First Primary Lung Cancer (FPLC). This finding
suggests that while radiotherapy increases the risk of SPLC, it does not
adverselyaffectsurvival,highlightingitsviabilityasatreatmentoption.
This finding indicates that while radiotherapy is associated with an
increased risk of SPLC, it does not adversely affect survival outcomes,
thus maintaining its viability as a beneficial treatment option in man-
aging breast cancer. To enhance the accuracy of our survival analysis,
we utilized the Cox Proportional Hazards Model. This model was
