breast cancer survivors with a history of radiotherapy and subsequent
development of SPLC. The robust methodology used in the Cox pro-
portional risk model helps ensure that the findings are not only statis-
tically significant but also clinically relevant, providing valuable
insights that can guide personalized treatment strategies and improve
clinical decision-making.
Our study, utilizing the SEER database, acknowledges several limi-
tationsincludingpotentialbiasesinherentinretrospectivedata,suchas
selection bias and incomplete capture of details like radiation dose and
patient lifestyle factors, which could affect the interpretation of our
findings. Furthermore, the retrospective nature limits our ability to
establish causality between radiotherapy and the development of Sec-
ondary Primary Lung Cancer (SPLC). Additionally, the absence of real-
time diagnostic techniques in our study design restricts our ability to
detect micrometastases with high precision. Emerging diagnostic tech-
nologies,suchaslabel-freebiosensingtechniquesdescribedbyNikshoar
et al. (2017) [31], hold promise for addressing this limitation in future
research.Theseconstraintsunderscorethenecessityforfutureresearch,
particularly prospective studies and clinical trials, to refine our under-
standing of radiotherapy’s impact and to develop optimized treatment
protocols that balance efficacy and long-term safety.
