Combined TP53 and RB1 Loss Promotes Prostate
Cancer Resistance to a Spectrum of Therapeutics
and Confers Vulnerability to Replication Stress
Graphical Abstract
Highlights
d Prostate cancers (PCs) with TP53 and RB1 loss exhibit very
poor clinical outcomes
d PCs with TP53/RB1 loss exhibit stem cell-like features and
loss of AR activity
d Loss of TP53/RB1 does not uniformly promote
neuroendocrine transdifferentiation
d TP53/RB1-null PCs exhibit replication stress and respond to
PARP and ATR inhibition
Authors
Michael D. Nyquist, Alexandra Corella,
Ilsa Coleman, ..., Colm Morrissey,
Eva Corey, Peter S. Nelson
Correspondence
pnelson@fredhutch.org
In Brief
Nyquist et al. demonstrate that TP53 and
RB1 loss in prostate carcinoma (PC)
attenuates AR signaling and enhances
cell proliferation but does not uniformly
induce neuroendocrine phenotypes. PCs
with TP53/RB1 loss resist a wide range of
cancer therapeutics but respond to PARP
and ATR inhibition, likely reﬂecting
enhanced replication stress.
