1. Introduction
The manufacturing of clay bricks consumes a lot of non-renewable materials associated with the emission of greenhouse
gases (GHG) such as carbon dioxide (CO2), carbon monoxide (CO), and other chemical pollutants. The production process
involves the ﬁring of the bricks to achieving strength, and this process consumes 24 million tons/ year of coal which is one of
the most polluting materials on earth [1]. The bricks are ﬁred to a temperature range of from 1 000/C14 to 1400 /C14 C, this implies
that more fuel is consumed to reach the desired temperature [2,3]. The catastrophic consequences associated with the GHG
from the bricks manufacturing industries have contaminated the atmosphere with smog, which in turn give rise to acid rains,
global warming, climate change, with other underlying health issues. Furthermore, the brick industry is associated with vast
