coal burning as 1,500 billion bricks are produced globally each year, of these 1,300 billion bricks (or 87 %) are from developing
countries. As a result of rapid acceleration in urbanization at an average rate of 6 % per annum, this growth is driving
increasing demand for bricks and other construction materials. Solid, ﬁred clay bricks are among the most widely used
building materials, as the cost of fuel and its availability is a major concern for the environment [4]. The shelter is categorized
as the primary need for human development for the integrated metropolitan environmental policy [5]. However, the
advocacy towards a paradigmatic shift from a non-renewable construction material is gaining momentum in contemporary
times particularly in developing countries. Such momentum appears to be propelled by the instrumentality of waste
inclusion in the production of construction material to the attainment of the sustainable development (SD) goals [6–9]. Of
interest to this study are the potential conversion of scrap plastic waste towards enabling resource-efﬁcient construction for
sustainability. Furthermore, it is estimated that approximately 275 million tons of plastic waste were generated in 192
coastal countries in 2010, [10]. Amongst which 12.7 million tons of this plastic waste have been dumped in the ocean causing
catastrophic effects on the environment. In the South African context, 80 % of 59 million tons of general waste generated
ended up in landﬁlls, whereas only 12 %, was recycled and the remaining 8 % littered the seashore [11]. The rapid growth in
solid waste and shortage of suitable land disposal of waste implies that South Africa is running out of space for the waste
stockpile. Despite the beneﬁts and need for recycling, studies reported that as little as 5,2 % of household waste was recycled
in 2015 according to Afrika et al. [12].
With the quest for proper management of waste, scholars like [13–16] have randomly published on the alternative use of
waste materials to produce low carbon construction material to aid proper management of wastes. Of particular concern is
plastic waste, besides its unit weight and ductility it is a viable component to produce a lightweight composite that could
withstand tensile stress [17,18]. A well-documented report has been published on the use of waste plastics in various
constructions ranging from concrete, pavement, and soil stabilization [19–22]. Albeit that scrap plastic wastes (SPW) is used
as a partial replacement for aggregate in pavements, due to its response to shear, stiffness, and subgrade bearing capacity.
Similarly, Choudhary et al. [23,24] and Jha et al. [25] also suggested that the properties of SPW in subgrades stabilization
scientiﬁcally increased the resilient modulus of the subgrade up to 84 % of its initial values. In a contrary report published
elsewhere by Arulrajah et al. [26], the investigation also explored the viability of using SPW granules alongside demolition
wastes as blends for road construction. Their study demonstrated that the incorporation of SPW lowers the stiffness, bearing
capacity, and resilient modulus of the subgrades. Thus, the general response of the composite was acceptable for subgrades
performance compare to non-treated subgrade. As such the reduction in the properties of the subgrade composite was
associated with the interface debonding of the subgrade soil with the SPW ﬁber.
In South African, factors such as lack of properly conversion of wastes into the production of green-efﬁcient construction
materials, a noticeable spike in the cost of building materials, and lack of shelter due to increased demand by an ever-growing
population, have also contributed to a shortfall in the availability of both green and conventional building materials. This
must incentivize civil engineers, builders, and material scientists to explore the applicability of wastes in various
construction activities. Particularly, in the production of marginal construction building materials. The construction industry
has been identiﬁed as one of the major contributors of GHG, due to the considerable amounts of resource waste and gas
emissions that have resulted from the construction activities. Hence giving verve to its time-honored reputation as a
hindrance to sustainable and resourceful development of wastes [27].
Studies have shown that plastic waste is one of the major threats to the environment and its inhabitants, due to their high
generated quantities that eventually ﬁnd their way to water bodies where they cause detrimental effects to aquatic lives.
However, the inclusions of approximately half of the waste materials will contribute to energy-efﬁcient construction
materials, as well as saving the environment from the impending pollution threats [28]. Bricks is a primary material for use in
the construction industry that is produced with clay and cement. Therefore, that qualiﬁes clay bricks as material that can
slowly destroy the environment. In furtherance to these, developing countries have an inadequate waste management
system, due to low waste collection rates, disposal by dumping, and limited outlets for reusing potentially recyclable
materials [29]. However, waste materials in South Africa can provide livelihoods and mitigate housing challenges to a highly
entrepreneurial informal sector [30]. The management of plastics has become a high proﬁle, environmental, and public
health hazard. Recycling infrastructure for these materials often does not exist in Africa. As a result, waste plastics have little
or no value, resulting in uncontrolled disposal in a landﬁll. However, it appears that none of these studies considered aspects
concerning combined resource use as described by Vivanco et al. [31] in the attempts to achieve sustainable low-cost
housing in developing countries.
This study attempts to contribute towards bridging this gap between effective waste management and mitigation of
backlog of housing challenges in South Africa. The study investigated the durability, strength, and sustainability of the
possible attributes of SPW with the combination of other industrial waste such as foundry sand in varying proportions. Most
commonly, the probable utility of such resultant combinations in the development of sustainable load-bearing housing in
South Africa and the rest of the developing world.
2. Materials acquisition and methodology
The scrap plastic wastes (SPW) were sampled from the Mariannhill Landﬁll site in Durban under strict Covid-19 protocols.
The waste material was taken to the University of Kwazulu Natal Civil Engineering Laboratory, where it was washed and
sanitized prior to its use. The pictorial view of the landﬁll site is shown in Fig. 1.
F.I. Aneke and C. Shabangu Case Studies in Construction Materials 14 (2021) e00515
2

The SPW is a chain of hydrocarbons compounds containing carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen (C10H8O4)n whose molecular
structure contains ten atoms of carbon linked double bond of eight hydrogens and four oxygen atoms respectively, thus the
chemical composition of SPW is presented in Fig. 2.
This type of SPW is often derived from natural gas or low-molecular-weight constituents of petroleum, they include
ethylene, propylene, and butene (butylene). The density of the SPW was determined to be 921 kg/m3 following ASTMD792
testing procedures. The process of sanitizing the wastes was followed to eliminate any form of a virus that could come from
hospital wastes. The PET plastic waste was dried for 3 days followed by shredding process, leaving the PET plastic waste close
to a powder like forms. To enable evaluation of chemical composition for PET waste using X-ray ﬂuorescence machine. It was
worthy to mention that the testing was achieved under strict testing procedure by using a constant radiation that is not
capable of reaching the melting point of the plastic.
The foundry sand used in this study was collected from Joseph Grieveson Ferrous Company in Durban. This foundry sand
is discarded waste from the steel company, with particle sizes ranges between 0.01 mm–2 mm according to ASTM (D1 140-
17) [55]. The chemical composition of the selected foundry sand was evaluated using an X-ray ﬂuorescence (XRF) test, using
the Rigaku, Ultima IV, diffractometer with the help of a Goniometer model 2036E201 with Cu Kα radiation (Kα = 1.54056 Å) at
an accelerating voltage of 40 kV and a current of 20 mA. JADE software was used to investigate the phases present in the
foundry sand. The result is evident that the major phase in foundry sand is SiO2 having an average dry mass of 60 % with other
trace chemical compositions such as Fe2O3, Al2O3, Cr2O3, and CaO making up the remaining 40 % of the dry mass.
The mix designs for SPW bricks samples used in this investigation vary in percentages by dry mass of the FS. Series of trial
mix-design bricks were used to produce bricks with different densities, in a varying ratio of 80 %:20 %, 70 %: 30 %, and 60 %: 40
% of the dry mass of the FS and SPW. For easy identiﬁcations of the prepared bricks, 80 %:20 % specimen is tagged SPW-1, 70 %:
30 %, is equal to SPW-2, whereas 60 %: 40 % is equivalent to SPW-3. The process ﬂow diagram used to produce SPW bricks is
shown in Fig. 3. The SPW following the washing and sanitizing process, the waste was dried and shredded after 3 days of
drying using a shredder. Following the shredding, the SPW was heated to a controlled temperature of 220 /C14 C having a melting
rate of 2.0 /C14 C/min using a furnace. The shredded SPW was allowed to attain appropriate consistency then the Foundry sand
was added. The sample was then continuously mixed manually using a steering rod for 5 min until a homogenous blend of
