recorded for SPW-3 and SPW-1, respectively, as no signiﬁcant strength decrease was observed throughout the entire cycles.
All the produced SPW bricks recorded higher compressive strength compared to the ﬁred clay bricks that rendered an
ultimate compressive strength value of 14.25 MPa. The ultimate strength recorded by the SPW bricks is associated with the
foundry sand to scrap plastic ratio, as well as the bond between the melted scrap plastic and sand particles. The variation in
sand particles reduced the pore spaces within the bricks, thus causing low water absorption due to the hydrophobic
properties of the SPW bricks. it was noted that SPW bricks did not lose any particles during a series of wetting and drying
cycles process. Unlike the ﬁred clay bricks that lost 4.3 g of their initial mass at the last cycle, as it caused a 2.4 % decrease in
strength. The result of this study agrees with reports published elsewhere by Gentilini et al. [33]. Furthermore, the SPW
bricks have signiﬁcantly higher density and compressive strength compared to the ﬁred clay bricks and concrete bricks. The
density of the SPW bricks is comparable to C20/C25 concrete, which has typical densities of 2240 kg/m3 and 2400 kg/m3,
respectively [34]. After failure under compressive loading, SPW brick still resists more than 35 % of the maximum failure
load, suggesting SPW bricks could improve resist cyclic and post-cyclic shear failure due to its viscoelastic response.
