3.2. Effect of wetting-drying cycles on split tensile strength
The tensile strength of any material is an important mechanical parameter that controls the development of tension
cracks. The split tensile strengths of SPW-1, SPW-2, SPW-3, as well as clay bricks were subjected to cyclic wetting and drying
in an acidic solution following split tensile strength testing as presented in Fig. 4a through c. It is also worthy of note that this
was done to evaluate the impact of the acidic environment of the bricks under tension stress. The test results show that the
tensile strength of the SPW tested bricks is remarkably 4 times lower compared to the unconﬁned compressive strength
values of the bricks. Whereas the tensile strength of the ﬁred clay bricks is 8 times lower than its compressive strength. This
implies that the SPW bricks posse more tensile resistance compared to the ﬁre clay bricks. The high tensile strength values
for SPW bricks are associated with the ductile characteristics of the melted scrap plastic. It was noted that the pore spaces
between the SPW-2 bricks are minimal compare to the other bricks, thus trigged high tensile strength. The number of
wetting and drying cycles of the bricks resulted in a slight decrease in tensile strength for ﬁred clay bricks, where no material
loss was recorded for SPW bricks. The ﬁred clay bricks rendered low yield stress, once the limit stress was exceeded, and it
portrayed brittle fracture resulting in a sudden instability of the bricks structures [35].
