3.3. Effect of acid solution on UCS and STS of the bricks
The results of both the SPW and clay bricks subjected to different acid concentrations have been presented in Fig. 5a and b.
The effect of acid concentration on the tensile and compressive strengths of the bricks are discussed herein. In furtherance,
the SPW bricks survived long-term soaking in different acid concentrations, as no traces of decrease in strengths were
recorded. The response could be attributed to the acidic absorption resistance of PET plastic waste due to their possed
hydrophobic and viscoelastic properties causing the SPW bricks to portray more resistance to acidic concentration. The SPW
bricks recorded an increase in the compressive energy, indicating the good toughness of bricks, that require more energy to
deform. Additionally, SPW bricks exhibited a higher rate of moisture loss due to the hydrophobic characteristics of PET, this
implies that SPW brick will perform at its optimum under an acidic environment. Whereas the clay-ﬁred bricks were affected
by the long-term acidic exposure particularly with 4.60E-02, 3.60E-03, and 5.20E-04 M concentrations. It is worthy to
