mention that the acidic interaction with the ﬁred clay bricks was mobilized by adsorption of the acid on the surface and
within the pore spaces of the bricks leading to the replacement of exchangeable cations by protons. The adsorbed protons
diffuse into the bricks void, where the chemical reaction occurs which caused the alkalinity of the bricks to drastically
decrease. The internal hydration of calcium silicate and the calcium aluminate hydration lost their stability and accelerated
their hydrolysis, destroying the internal structure of the low compressive strengths [36,37].
3.4. Evolution of bricks strength index (qt/qu)
The compressive and tensile strength of construction material is important for the design of basic masonry and retaining
structures. These structures are designed to carry both compressive as well as tensile stresses simultaneously [38,39].
Additionally, the ratio of split tensile strength to unconﬁned compressive strength has been used to measure the
performance of materials such as concrete, stabilized soil, and bricks with different variables. The masonry structure is
formulated such that all the tensile stresses are designed to be carried by the masonry bricks with or without reinforcing
material such as steel [40,41]. As such, the ratio of tensile to the compressive strength of bricks is very important in design.
Therefore, it is necessary to investigate the ratio of tensile to the compressive strength of SPW bricks to evaluate their
suitability as a construction material. As shown in Fig. 6(a) through c, the ratio of the tensile to compressive (qt/qu) is strongly
affected by the level of the tensile strength. This ratio increases due to high compressive strength values. The strength indices
ðqt =quÞ of 0.25 to 0.45 as the proportionality of tensile and compressive strength is closely correlated to all the SPW bricks due
to hydrophobic and deformability characteristics of the scrap PET plastic. This ﬁnding can be explained by the fact that the
increase in the splitting tensile strength occurs at a much greater rate than that of compressive strength. The previous
investigations [42,43] have established that qt/qu is the scalar independent of porosity and aggregate interlock. However, the
shear strength resistance within the brick’s matrix may be able to prevent the bricks from complete failure.
The result in this study is in opposite agreement with the reports published elsewhere by Arioglu et al. [44] and Zain et al.
[45] which suggested a contrary proportionality that tensile strength of concrete materials decreases at a high rate when the
compressive strength increases. This ﬁnding implies that the SPW bricks posse high ductile characteristics because of melted
plastic content, as the ratio between tensile and compressive strength in this study ranges from 0.18 to 0.28. This value is
synonym with the material characterized with great ductilities, such as free concrete [46,47].
3.5. Load-deformation relationship and failure modes
The representative of load-deformation relationships of the SPW and ﬁred clay bricks under compressive stress is
presented in Fig. 7. It can be seen that the SPW bricks under compression exhibit considerable stiffness relative to the
percentage of added scrap plastics at axial strain beyond 4.1 mm, such that the stress relaxation is indicated between 1.0
mm–2.5 mm deformation strain. It is noted that ﬁred clay bricks are associated with brittle compression failure immediately
after peak load as evidence by the macrostructure in the curve. The behaviour of SPW bricks under compression is
characterized by a linear relationship between load and deformation to some deformation level of 3.6 mm beyond which
strain hardening occurs. Furthermore, considerable post-peak ductility is exhibited for SPW bricks which are mobilized by
the elastic characteristics of the added scrap plastics in the bricks. On the other hand, ﬁred clay bricks in compression exhibit
a low stiffness response compared to the SPW bricks. Considerable post-peak ductility is shown beyond the 3.6 mm strain
