4. Conclusions
In this study, an assessment of the durability and strength indices of effectively converted waste plastic and foundry sand
into green-efﬁcient masonry bricks which can practically reduce pollution and further decrease the problem of littering the
environment with is demonstrated based on the ﬁndings, it was concluded that.
Results from this study are indicative of the satisfactory performance of SPW bricks as a load-bearing masonry element,
especially the bricks with a mix ratio of 70 %:30 % and 60 %:40 % of foundry sand to scrap plastic. It, therefore, provides an
appropriate channel for the disposal of FS in a very efﬁcient, useful, and proﬁtable way. The test results also indicate that the
SPW bricks recorded compressive and tensile strength of 38.14 MPa and 9.51 MPa, respectively. The results showed that SPW
bricks rendered compressive strength 2.5 and 3 times higher than the ﬁred clay bricks.
A signiﬁcant timesaving in the manufacturing process is an added advantage, as they attain more than 80 % of their
ultimate strength within 12 h due to their fast curing and low water absorption. In fast construction and waterlogged acidic
environments, the SPW bricks would have an advantage over those produced from burnt clay, due to their high hydrophobic
properties making them relatively resilient against chemical attack and less deformable under tension stress.
In addition, the ﬁred clay bricks absorbed more water, and it requires water to attain the desired consistency during
production. Whereas the tests result revealed that SPW bricks could be made from scrap plastic waste and foundry sand
without the need for water. The plastic waste and foundry sand usage would serve another purpose of auxiliary green-
efﬁcient construction material, as ﬁred clay bricks require high energy of 110 0/C14 C compared to SPW bricks that require 220 /C14 C
to attain 2–3 times the strength of ﬁred clay bricks.
