1. Introduction
Soil solidiﬁcation technology with a curing agent has been
used to improve the soil and achieve the mechanical proper-
ties required by the project. This technology is widely used in
road subgrade engineering, foundation reinforcement, foun-
dation pit seepage prevention, slope protection and vertical
barrier of polluted sites [ 1e7]. Currently, cement is widely
used as the primary cementing material in various construc-
tion ﬁelds. However, cement production is highly energy-
intensive. Each ton of cement production generates 0.9 tons
of CO 2 emissions [ 8,9], which is detrimental to the environ-
ment's ability to grow sustainably. Therefore, the develop-
ment of cementitious materials with advantages such as
environmental friendliness, low cost, and good performance
will attract signiﬁcant attention in future soil reinforcement
engineering.
Alkali-activated materials are a type of environmentally
friendly cementing materials that have signiﬁcant perfor-
mance advantages, sustainability, and economic beneﬁts
[10e13]. Recently, alkali-activated materials has been widely
studied as a soil stabilizer. Zhang et al. [ 14] found that alkali-
activated materials solidiﬁed soil reached high strength at
the early stage of maintenance and effectively alleviated
pavement cracking during pavement construction, mainte-
nance and operation. Cui et al. [ 15] found that sodium silicate
alkali-activated slag-solidiﬁed bentonite had the characteris-
tics of high strength, fast solidiﬁcation speed and low
permeability by comparing its various mechanical properties
to cement-solidiﬁed bentonite. In addition, sodium silicate
alkali-activated slag caused less damage to bentonite and
achieved better adsorption and isolation effects on pollutants
compared to cement. Cristelo et al. [ 16] improved deep soft
soil by spraying alkali-activated ﬂy ash slurry and compre-
hensively studied the carbon dioxide emissions and me-
chanical, rheological, and economic properties of solidiﬁed
soil. The results show that ﬂy ash-based geopolymers could
effectively replace cement to stabilize the soil. Wu et al. [ 17]
used MgO activated blast furnace slag (GGBS) solidiﬁed soil as
a cut-off wall and found that it achieved better anti-seepage
performance and Na 2SO4 erosion resistance than traditional
cement soil. Therefore, it is clear that using alkali-activated
materials instead of cement to solidify the soil has signiﬁ-
cant research value.
The stress-strain curve of stabilized soil is signiﬁcantly
different from that of undisturbed soil. This is mainly because
the cohesive force generated by the cementitious material
during the solidiﬁcation process changes the cementation
form between the soil particles. At present, many academics
have conducted in-depth research on the factors affecting the
intensity of solidiﬁed soil. Liu et al. [ 18] explored the inﬂuence
of binder/water ratio, sand/clay ratio, water content and sand
size on the strength of solidiﬁed soil through experimental
research and established a new index to characterize the
compressive strength based on these factors. Consoli et al. [19]
solidiﬁed sand using alkali-activating ceramic tile dust with
different molar concentrations of NaOH as a solidifying agent
and established the relationship between the strength and
porosity/binder. Sukmak et al. [ 20] explored the inﬂuence of
the alkaline-activator content, NaOH/Na 2SiO3 ratio, ﬂy ash
content, and heat temperature on the strength of clay. Zhou
et al. [ 21] found that the mechanical properties of alkali-
activated GGBS were signiﬁcantly better than those of meta-
kaolin using metakaolin and GGBS as precursors of alkali-
activated materials. Based on the above research ﬁndings,
most scholars still focus on the impact of diverse components
on the peak stress while ignoring the elastic-plastic state.
Zhao et al. [ 22] deﬁned the range beneath the maximum
stress-strain curve as the peak energy absorption energy ( U1),
and evaluated the toughness characteristics of the material
using U1. Tsuchida et al. [ 23] deﬁned the slant of a straight line
at 1/2 of the crest point of the stress-strain curve as the initial
elastic modulus E0 and used E0 to represent the stiffness and
elasticity of the material. Salimi et al. [ 24] used MgO, CaO and
different Na 2SiO3/NaOH to active granular blast furnace slag
as a cementitious material to solidify soft clay. Additionally,
they discussed the properties of the material with the aid of U1
and E0. However, evaluating the mechanical properties of
solidiﬁed soil only by establishing indicators based on the
peak strength and stress-strain curve before the peak cannot
fully reﬂect the elastic-plastic and post-peak bearing capacity
loss states shown by the stress-strain curve. In addition, this
apprpoach cannot comprehensively evaluate the damage and
deformation states of materials. Zhang et al. [ 25] believed that
the mechanical properties of solidiﬁed soil were closely
related to the cementation properties of the cementitious
components produced by the curing agent. Based on the the-
ory of energy conservation, Zhang et al. [ 25] established a
shear model to reﬂect the shear behavior of solidiﬁed soil,
which accurately reﬂected the strain softening and hardening
characteristics. However, their shear model ignored energy
dissipation. Deformation is a process of energy accumulation
and dissipation in heterogeneous geological materials [ 26].
Analysis from the viewpoint of the energy dissipation mech-
anism can more accurately represent the entire process of
material failure compared with the peak strength of the
stress-strain curve. Geng et al. [ 27] explored the impact of
metakaolin on the damage of cement-stabilized soil during
compression based on the plastic damage model. They found
that the plastic damage model of Weibull distribution por-
trayed the damage degree of samples during the compression
failure process of solidiﬁed soil in detail. However, this
method cannot reﬂect the elastic-plastic state of solidiﬁed
soil. Based on the above research ﬁndings, there is little in-
formation regarding mechanical indicators that can
journal of materials research and technology 2023;25:6039 e60606040

comprehensively reﬂect the elastoplastic state, post-peak
bearing capacity loss state and degree of damage of the
material.
The micropore structure of solidiﬁed soil is closely linked
to its mechanical characteristics, and the pore structure of
solidiﬁed soil is affected differently by various cementitious
materials. The micropore structure of solidiﬁed soil has been
extensively studied by a wide range of academics. At present,
many scholars have used scanning electron microscopy tests
[28], mercury intrusion tests [ 29,30], and thermogravimetric
tests [ 31] to explore the effects of micro pore structure and
generated products on macroscopic properties of solidiﬁed
soil. In addition, they established relationship between
microscopic pore structure and macroscopic mechanics. Bal-
dovino et al. [ 32] established a relationship between the
porosity binder index, UCS, and tensile strength. Sari et al. [ 33]
investigated the porosity of cemented ore backﬁll soil through
mercury intrusion testing and found that the UCS of the
sample decreased with an increase in total porosity. Geng
et al. [ 27] discovered that pore structure with a pore radius of
less than 0.1 mm had the greatest impact on the UCS of so-
lidiﬁed soil through grey entropy correlation analysis. Based
on the above research ﬁndings, it is of great signiﬁcance to
explore the mechanical properties of solidiﬁed soil at the
microstructural level. However, the above research only fo-
cuses on the relationship between micro pore structure and
peak strength. The relationship between micro pore structure
and energy dissipation, damage variables, and brittleness
index of solidiﬁed soil lacks theoretical basis and data support.
In this research, the energy dissipation mechanism and
Weibull distribution plastic damage model were used to
examine the deformation and failure process of cement-soil
and alkali-activated slag-soil in detail. The goal was to
establish brittleness evaluation and damage indices to reﬂect
the state of the solidiﬁed soil material during the process of
loading and deformation, and provide a reference value for
mechanical research in this ﬁeld. Additionally, the impact on
each evaluation index from various curing ages, moduli,
and cement, blast furnace slag, and alkali-activator contents
were investigated. Finally, the reaction mechanism of the
solidiﬁed soil was analyzed using X-ray diffraction (XRD),
thermogravimetry (TG/DTG) and low-ﬁeld nuclear magnetic
resonance (NMR) techniques. The inﬂuence of solidiﬁed soil
microstructure on various mechanical indices was also
explored.
2. Materials and experimental procedures
2.1. Materials
The soil used in this experiment was a high-liquid-limit silty
clay from a construction site in Xi 'an, Shaanxi Province,
China. The retrieved soil was crushed passed through a 2 mm
sieve, and dried for standby. The cement was ordinary
P$O.42.5 Portland cement produced by the Shaanxi Qinling
Cement Plant. The blast furnace slag was produced by Henan
Platinum Casting Materials Co., Ltd. and had a density of 2.9 g/
cm3. Sodium hydroxide was obtained from Jinan Xiaoshi
Chemical Co., Ltd. (granular with a purity of 99%). Sodium
silicate was obtained from Xi 'an Huachang Water Glass Co.,
Ltd. (powdered with a modulus of 2.86). The materials used in
the tests are shown in Fig. 1.
2.2. Mix proportion design and sample preparation
This study explored the effects of the modulus and cement,
GGBS, and alkali activator contents on the deformation and
failure process of solidiﬁed soil through the mix proportion
listed in Table 1 . The alkali activator was prepared using
Na2SiO3 and NaOH. The cement content was calculated as the
ratio of cement quality to the sum of cement and soil quality.
The water content of group A was the ratio of the mass of
water to the sum of the mass of the soil and cement masses. In
Group A, the deformation and failure processes of the solidi-
ﬁed soil with cement contents of 10%, 15%, 20% and 25% were
investigated. The GGBS dosage was calculated as the ratio of
the mass of GGBS to the sum of the cement and soil masses.
The water content of other groups (except for Group A) was
the ratio of the water mass to the sum of the soil and GGBS
masses. The deformation and failure process of solidiﬁed soil
with 10%, 15% and 20% GGBS content were investigated by
Fig. 1 e Materials used in the test.
journal of materials research and technology 2023;25:6039 e6060 6041

comparing groups B, C, and D, respectively. The amount of
alkali activator was calculated as the ratio of the mass of the
alkali activator to the sum of the soil and GGBS masses. The
chemical formula of sodium silicate is Na 2O$mSiO2, where m
represents the modulus. An alkali activated modulus can be
adjusted by adding NaOH to sodium silicate [ 34,35]. The
modulus of the alkali activator was 1.1 in groups B, C, and D. In
the C2M group, seven types of moduli were set by controlling
the ratio of Na 2SiO3 to NaOH in order to explore the inﬂuence
of the alkali-activated modulus on the deformation and fail-
ure of the solidiﬁed soil. The cement, GGBS, alkali activator
and water content listed in Table 1 are the proportions of the
total soil and GGBS contents. According to the mix ratio in
Table 1, various materials were mixed and placed into a mold
with a F 50 mm /C2 100 mm. Subsequently, they were vibrated
on the shaking table for 3 min to discharge the internal bub-
bles, and then the mold was removed after curing in a room
for 24 h. Finally, the demolded specimen was placed in a
standard curing box. Unconﬁned compressive strength test
were conducted after curing for 7 d, 14 d, 28 d and 60 d.
2.3. Unconﬁned compressive strength test (UCS)
A rock triaxial testing machine was used to load solidiﬁed soil
at a rate of 1 mm/min. The maximum and minimum values of
the ﬁve parallel test pieces were eliminated before calculating
the average value of the remaining data as the outcome to
analyze each mix ratio. The rock triaxial testing machine used
in the tests is illustrated in Fig. 2.
2.4. X-ray diffraction test (XRD)
The fragments inside the specimen were selected and placed
into an oven at 50 /C14 C for 24 h after crushing the solidiﬁed soil
specimen that was cured for 28 d. The dried sample was
grinded and passed through a 75 mm sieve. Finally, a phase
analysis of the screened solidiﬁed soil samples was conducted
using an ADVANCE type XRD. The scanning step width of X-
ray was 0.02 /C14 , the scanning range was 5 /C14 to 70 /C14 , and the
scanning speed of diffraction angle was 0.15 s/step.
2.5. Thermogravimetry (TG/DTG) test
A thermogravimetric analyzer with an STA 2500 TG-DSC
model was selected to test the TG/DTG of the solidiﬁed soil.
First, the solidiﬁed soil was dried, ground, and sifted after 28 d
of curing. Approximately 10 mg of solidiﬁed soil powder was
weighed and placed in a thermal analyzer. Finally, the purge
gas of the instrument was set to 60 ml/min and increased from
50 /C14 C to 1000 /C14 C at the rate of 20 /C14 C/min. The content of each
material component in the solidiﬁed soil was determined after
the test based on the mass-loss percentage in the peak range
of the test results.
2.6. Nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) test
In order to explore the pore structure of the solidiﬁed soil, this
paper carried out NMR test on the solidiﬁed soil after 28 d of
curing. The solidiﬁed soil was saturated for 24 h before the
NMR test. After the saturation, the MesoMR23-060V eI nuclear
magnetic resonance analysis system produced by Suzhou
Newman Electronic Technology Co., Ltd. Was used for NMR
test. The nuclear magnetic resonance instrument used in the
test is shown in Fig. 3 . By referring to relevant literature
[36,37], it is found that T2 in NMR has the following relation-
ship with pore radius. Eq. (1) demonstrates that the size of the
pore increases as the T2 value increases, and the decrease of T2
value represents the decrease of pore structure [ 38,39].
