3. Damage mechanism
Studying the deformation and failure of heterogeneous
geological materials solely in terms of traditional stress-strain
strength is incomplete. Energy accumulates and dissipates
during the deformation process for heterogeneous materials
[26]. Therefore, the perspective of energy must be explored to
obtain a comprehensive and in-depth understanding of the
failure processes of solidiﬁed soil materials. Energy intake,
accumulation, dissipation, and release are the general divisions
of the internal energy transformation of solidiﬁed dirt after
loading [40]. The energy-conversion relationship in the solidi-
ﬁed soil system is depicted in Fig. 4. Elastic energy is a form of
mechanical energy that is held in the solidiﬁed soil unit as a
portion of the mechanical energy applied to it by the external
environment. The other portion of the energy is lost as plastic
strain and surface energies, whereas elastic energy is
Fig. 2 e Rock triaxial testing machine.
Fig. 3 e Nuclear magnetic resonance instrument.
journal of materials research and technology 2023;25:6039 e6060 6043

reversible. The creation, expansion, penetration, and sliding of
internal pores and microcracks cause the deformation and
failure of solidiﬁed soil. When the solidiﬁed soil absorbs a
certain amount of energy, it leads to the formation of a frac-
tured surface. This portion of the energy is accompanied by the
continuous expansion of internal cracks in the solidiﬁed soil,
which is irreversible. Energy dissipates when sliding friction
occurs on the fracture surface. The amount and age of the
cementitious materials are important factors that affect the
strength of solidiﬁed soil. The cementitious force generated by
the cementitious material must also absorb and dissipate en-
ergy during the compression process. As a result, the energy
evolution law may more accurately characterize the failure
features of the sample in the deformation and failure processes
for alkali-activated slag-soil and cement-soil.
The strain hardening and softening mechanism collabo-
rate in the failure process of solidiﬁed soil [ 1]. The strain
hardening mechanism is formed when the energy input into
the solidiﬁed soil system is converted into the elastic energy of
the solidiﬁed soil system. The strain softening process occurs
when the elastic energy in the solidiﬁed soil is converted into
crack expansion energy, heat energy, and other forms of en-
ergy. The strain softening mechanism is stronger than the
strain hardening mechanism in the post-peak stage. Subse-
quently, the energy is stronger in the release stage. The strain
hardening mechanism is dominant and the energy is in the
accumulation stage at the pre-peak stage of the stress-strain
curve.
3.1. Energy analysis principle
The unit volume of the solidiﬁed soil will produce deformation
and failure with nonlinear mechanical characteristics in a
complex stress environment. The energy state during the
deformation and failure of the solidiﬁed soil is represented by
the transformation and dynamic balance between the total
energy ( U) absorbed by the sample, elastic strain energy ( Ue)
that can be released by the unit, and dissipated energy ( Ud)o f
the unit, assuming that there is no heat trade with the exterior
during the distortion and failure of the sample. Concurring to
the primary law of thermodynamics, the energy conversion of
unit volume of solidiﬁed soil meets Eqs. (2)e(5) [26,41].
