3.2. Establishment of brittleness index based on energy
dissipation mechanism
The stress-strain curve showed two main failure types using
the UCS of the solidiﬁed soil, as shown in Fig. 5 . Tarasov and
Potvin [42] believed that the elastic energy accumulated before
the peak of the stress-strain curve could provide a certain
material basis for the failure of the solidiﬁed soil after the
peak. For the ﬁrst type of failure, the fracture failure process of
sample is primarily obtained from the pre-peak elastic energy.
The structural failure process is self-sustainable and does not
require a continuous input of energy from the external sys-
tem. The ﬁrst type of failure has strong brittleness. In the
second type of failure, the elastic energy before the peak of the
solidiﬁed soil is insufﬁcient to cause the structure fail, and it
must absorb energy continuously from the external system.
The second type of failure exhibits strong plastic
characteristics.
The deformation of rock and soil mass under load is
accompanied by the occurrence of cracks, and brittleness is
Fig. 4 e Schematic diagram of energy conversion of solidiﬁed soil system.
journal of materials research and technology 2023;25:6039 e60606044

reﬂected in the entire process of rock and soil mass failure.
Therefore, brittleness is of great signiﬁcance in the evaluation
of geotechnical engineering stability. Many scholars have
established brittleness indices from different perspectives,
such as mineral composition, stress-strain curve character-
istics, mechanical properties, and hardness testing [43e47]. To
date, a uniﬁed standard for the deﬁnition of a rock and soil
brittleness index has not yet been established. Liu et al. [ 48]
believed that the above evaluation indices used one or several
physical and mechanical parameters to deﬁne brittleness,
thus ignoring the relationship between the failure mode of the
sample at each stage and the stress-strain curve. In addition,
the progressive process of rock fracture was simpliﬁed. As
solidiﬁed soil is a composite material, its internal pore struc-
ture, cementitious material composition, and distribution of
internal defects have obvious characteristics of heterogeneity
and anisotropy. Additionally, there is a large deviation in
evaluating the brittleness of the solidiﬁed soil when only using
the above indicators. The failure process of solidiﬁed soil is a
simultaneous process of energy absorption and release. Ac-
cording to Tarasov [ 42] and Ai et al. [ 49], the brittleness index
created from the perspective of energy can capture the char-
acteristics of brittle rock fractures more accurately than the
index created from other views. It is incomplete to consider
only the pre- or post-peak stages to establish the brittleness
index. Therefore, this study established a brittleness index by
referring to relevant papers and comprehensively considering
the brittleness characteristics of the pre- and post-peak stages
[41,50e53]. In addition, the ratio of the elastic energy incre-
ment in the yield stage to the energy increment in the entire
unstable crack growth stage was used to characterize the
brittle characteristics of the solidiﬁed soil before the peak, as
shown in Eq. (8). Furthermore, the elastic strain energy in the
specimen was continuously released and a large number of
microcracks occurred in the solidiﬁed soil. When the elastic
strain energy stored in the solidiﬁed soil was the same as the
energy required for fracture, it did not need to absorb energy
from the outside to reach the self-equilibrium state of crack
expansion. Therefore, at this time, it will show more obvious
brittle characteristics [ 51]. Eq. (9) was used to represent the
post-peak brittleness index. The brittleness of the solidiﬁed
soil was accompanied by the entire process of stress-strain
curve, representing the brittleness of the solidiﬁed soil, as
discussed in Eq. (8) and Eq. (9). The energy of this process is
shown in Fig. 6.
