The relationship between the damage variables and
various energies and stress-strain curves were obtained using
the principle of energy analysis and plastic strain damage
model of Weibull distribution, as shown in Fig. 7. D increased
in an S shape with an increase in strain. The closer the D value
was to 1, the more serious the damage to the specimen. En-
ergy dissipation of the sample in the uniaxial compression
process led to internal damage of the sample. The specimen 's
ﬁssures will deepen as Ud rises, eventually resulting in total
destruction of the specimen. Ue dominates in the OA portion
of the stress-strain curve, whereas Ud was nearly zero. Here,
Ue achieved the maximum amount of energy that the solidi-
ﬁed soil could store at point B. Ue exhibited a diminishing
tendency in the BC region of the stress-strain curve, whereas
Ud exhibited a rapid growing trend. The growth rates of Ue
slows down, and the growth rate of Ud decreased and
increased in section AB, respectively. A certain amount of
energy must be consumed because the micropores and
microcracks in the solidiﬁed soil samples at this stage
continued to develop under continuous loading. The Ue stored
in the solidiﬁed soil was rapidly converted into kinetic, surface
and friction heat energies at point B and then released. Most of
the energy dissipated during the expansion and sliding of the
fractured surface. At this stage, the microcracks in the solid-
iﬁed soil specimen expanded to a certain extent and then
quickly penetrated. Subsequently, the specimen was
completely destroyed and lost its bearing capacity. A certain
residual strength remained at a later BC stage. At this point,
the energy of the sample mainly existed in the form of Ud. U,
Ud, and D showed an S-shaped trend with an increase in
strain, whereas Ue showed a trend of ﬁrst increasing and then
decreasing, as shown in Fig. 7. Therefore, the deformation and
damage processes of solidiﬁed soil samples in the failure
process can be described systematically using the energy
analysis principle and Weibull distribution plastic strain
damage model.
4. Results and analysis
4.1. Analysis of stress-strain curve
The effects of the change in modulus and cement, GGBS, and
alkali-activator contents on the stress-strain curve of the
sample were investigated for the 28 d curing period. The strain
hardening trend of the stress-strain curve was evident when
the cement content was low. The strain softening trend of the
stress-strain curve became more pronounced as the cement
content increased ( Fig. 8 (a)). The calcium silicate hydrate
(CeSeH) produced by the cement hydration reaction was
connected to the soil particles, increasing the cohesive force of
the solidiﬁed soil. According to Geng et al. [ 39], the more
cement present, the stronger the cohesive force between the
soil particles. The sample was quickly destroyed once the load
reached its maximum strength owing to the rapid production
of the fracture surface. In contrast, the lower the cement
content, the weaker the cohesive force between the soil par-
ticles, and there were many pore structures between the
particles. The pore structure in the specimen was gradually
compressed when the ultimate load was reached. In addition,
there was friction between the soil particles that could still
bear a certain strength, showing relatively obvious plastic
deformation.
The peak strength of the stress-strain curve increased with
increasing GGBS content, and the plastic properties gradually
weakened, as shown in Fig. 8 (b), (c) and (d). The increase in
GGBS content increased the Ca/Al ratio in the reaction system
[59], which promoted the formation of the calcium alumino-
silicate hydrate (C-A-S-H) gel material, thus improving the
bonding force between the soil particles. The peak strength of
the stress-strain curve increased with an increase in the alkali
activator content, and the brittleness become more obvious,
as shown in Fig. 8(e), (f) and (g). This is because the increase in
Fig. 7 e Relationship between stress-strain curve, energy
