the lower its alkalinity. A considerable portion of the GGBS
could not completely undergo a polycondensation reaction.
Therefore, the produced C-A-S-H and other gelling products
were also relatively small.
4.2. Energy analysis of peak point
The full stress-strain curves of solidiﬁed soil with different
curing ages, moduli of alkali activators, and cement, GGBS,
and alkali-activator contents were calculated based on the
principle of energy analysis to explore the evolution law of
each energy at the peak point in the failure process of solidi-
ﬁed soil under load.
The U, Ue, and Ud values at the highest point of the solidi-
ﬁed soil exhibited increasing tendencies with increasing
cement concentration and curing age. Taking the curing age of
28 d as an example, the U values of A4 were 19.28%, 28.31%,
and 252.38% higher than those of A3, A2 and A1, respectively.
The U and Ue values of A1 increased slightly after curing for 7,
14, and 28 d, and increased signiﬁcantly after curing for 60 d.
However, the U and Ue values of A2, A3 and A4 increased
signiﬁcantly after 14 d of curing, and the growth rate of U
decreased after 28 d. This may be because the increase in
cement content promoted the early hydration process of
cement-soil. Ud increased signiﬁcantly when the cement
content increased from 10% to 15%, but later growth was not
evident, as shown in Fig. 9 (a). Due to the increase of cement
content and curing age, the hydration products of cement
increased and the soil particles were more closely connected
Fig. 8 e Stress-strain curve of solidiﬁed soil.
journal of materials research and technology 2023;25:6039 e6060 6047

by the cementitious material. Therefore, the stronger the
ability to resist damage, the more total energy the specimen
needs to absorb in the process of damage and the more energy
the specimen needs to absorb and consume in order to
generate and expand cracks in the process of damage.
After 28 d of curing, the U of D1 is 84.47% and 143.48%
higher than those of C1 and B1, U values of D2 were 42.62% and
43.89% higher than those of C2 and B2, and U values of D3 is
41.62% and 65.73% higher than those of C3 and B3, respec-
tively. At the same time, the Ue values of D1 were 0.39 and
0.47 kJ/m 3 higher than those of C1 and B1, Ue values of D2 is
0.35 and 0.39 kJ/m 3 higher than those of C2 and B2, and Ue
values of D3 is 0.58 and 0.32 kJ/m 3 higher than those of C3 and
B3, respectively. U and Ue at the peak point of the solidiﬁed soil
showed an increasing trend with an increase in GGBS content
and curing age, whereas the change trend of Ud was not sig-
niﬁcant ( Fig. 9(b), (c), and (d)). The U values of B3 were 41.57%
and 232.08% higher than those of B2 and B1, U values of C3
were 64.20% and 194.42% higher than those of C2 and C1, and
U values of D3 were 63.05% and 126.03% higher than those of
D2 and D1, respectively. Similarly, the Ue values of B3 were
63.05% and 126.03% higher than those of B2 and B1, Ue values
of C3 were 96.25% and 485.98% higher than those of C2 and C1,
and Ue values of D3 were 82.77% and 188.59% higher than
those of D2 and D1, respectively. U and Ue at the peak point of
the solidiﬁed soil showed an increasing trend with an increase
in the alkali-activator content, whereas the change trend of Ud
was not signiﬁcant, as shown in Fig. 9 (e), (f), and (g). This is
because the increase in the content of the GGBS and alkali
activator promoted the polymerization reaction of the geo-
polymer, resulting in more stable three-dimensional struc-
tures of Si eOeAl and Si eOeSi to ﬁll the pores between soil
particles and improve the compactness of the solidiﬁed soil
sample. Subsequently, this caused the solidiﬁed soil to need to
absorb more energy during the destruction process. The U and
Ue decreased rapidly with the increase of the modulus when it
was 0.7e1.1. The U and Ue slowly decreased when the modulus
was 1.1 e1.7. The U and Ue decreased sharply when the
Fig. 9 e Each energy at the peak point of solidiﬁed soil.
