4.3. Damage variable analysis
The damage situation of solidiﬁed soil with different curing
ages, moduli, and cement, GGBS, and alkali-activator contents
was explored by calculating the peak damage variable of the
solidiﬁed soil using the Weibull distribution plastic damage
model.
When the curing age was 28 d, the damage variables of A4
were 33.74%, 55.57%, and 71.31% lower than those of A3, A2,
and A1, respectively ( Fig. 11 (a)). Moreover, Fig. 11 (a) shows
that the damage variable decreased as the curing age and
cement content increased. Yang et al. [ 62] conducted a me-
chanical analysis of ﬂy ash-modiﬁed cement soil based on the
Weibull distribution damage model. This study found that the
D in the initial, peak, and residual stages of the stress-strain
curve exhibited a decreasing trend with an increase in ﬂy
ash content and curing age. Here, the increase in ﬂy ash and
curing age promoted the increase of C eSeH products inside
the specimen, reduced the connectivity of the pore structure,
and thus reduced the damage variable of the specimen. The
cement hydration reaction produced cementitious C eSeH
and gypsum. Gypsum does not naturally have cementitious
properties. However, it can be combined with calcium
aluminate to form ettringite, which can ﬁll the spaces be-
tween soil particles to make the formed soil more compact. As
a result, ettringite production by hydration products increases
as the cement concentration increases, the microstructure of
the solidiﬁed soil becomes denser, C eSeH levels rise, and the
cohesiveness of soil particles improves, making the solidiﬁed
soil more resistant to damage. Wu et al. [ 63] treated clay with
the two main components of cement clinker (C3S and C3A) and
found that C 3S was more effective than C 3A in improving the
mechanical properties of solidiﬁed soil during long-term
curing. C 3A and C 3S in the cement continue to hydrate and
react with the clay matrix in a pozzolanic manner with an
increase in curing age owing to the cement hydration reaction
Fig. 11 e Damage variable at peak point of solidiﬁed soil.
journal of materials research and technology 2023;25:6039 e60606050

being a long-term process. This makes the cement chain be-
tween the soil particles ﬁrmer, resulting in a reduction in the
damage variable.
Taking the 28 d curing age as an example, the damage
variables of D1 were 50.45% and 59.57% lower than those of C1
and B1, respectively ( Fig. 11 (b)). The damage variables of D2
were 36.48% and 43.91% lower than those of C2 and B2,
respectively ( Fig. 11 (c)). The damage variables of D3 were
29.24% and 56.03% lower than those of C3 and B3, respectively
(Fig. 11(d)). The damage variable decreased with an increase in
GGBS content (Fig. 11(b)e(d)). The damage variables of B3 were
11.49% and 65.88% lower than those of B2 and B1, respectively
(Fig. 11 (e)). The damage variables of C3 were 41.70% and
75.68% lower than those of C2 and C1, respectively ( Fig. 11(f)).
The damage variables of D3 were 51.90% and 74.28% lower
than those of D2 and D1, respectively ( Fig. 11(g)). The damage
variable decreased as the amount of alkali activator increased,
as shown in Fig. 11 (e)e(g). Increased amounts of GGBS and
alkali activator boosted the polymerization reaction, which
resulted in cementitious products (such as C-A-S-H) bonding
to the soil particles and increasing soil particle cohesiveness.
Thus, the ability of the solidiﬁed soil to resist damage
improved. Taking the 28 d curing age as an example, the
damage variable of C2M0.7 was 6.81%, 42.67%, 55.96%, 57.97%,
63.49%, and 79.44% lower than those of C2M0.9, C2M1.1,
C2M1.3, C2M1.5, C2M1.7 and C2M1.9, respectively ( Fig. 11 (h)).
The damage variable increased with an increase in the
modulus, as shown in Fig. 11 (h). Through an exploratory
investigation, Hu et al. [ 61] found that the solubility of modi-
ﬁed red mud increased with an increase of pH in a NaOH so-
lution. The modiﬁed red mud polycondensated when the pH
of the NaOH solution was 14, producing foil-shaped C eSeH
and thicker shaped C 2ASH8 samples. The surface of the
modiﬁed red mud particles was smooth and without corrosion
when the pH of the NaOH solution was 12. Therefore, the
GGBS was in a high-pH environment with a reduction
in the modulus and increase in NaOH content. This promoted
the polymerization reaction of the geopolymer and increased
the cementitious material between soil particles, thus
improving the resistance of the solidiﬁed soil to damage.
Fig. 12 e Brittleness index of solidiﬁed soil.
