According to this research into the damage factors of solidiﬁed
soil, the ability of solidiﬁed soil to withstand damage can be
improved by decreasing the modulus and increasing the
curing age and cement, GGBS, and alkali-activator contents.
This conclusion is consistent with the dissipation energy ratio
of solidiﬁed soil, which proves the rationality of the
conclusion.
4.4. Brittleness index analysis
The full stress-strain curve was calculated based on the en-
ergy dissipation mechanism to quantitatively analyze the in-
ﬂuence of different moduli, curing periods, and cement, GGBS,
and alkali-activator contents on the brittleness of the solidi-
ﬁed soil. According to Wen et al. [ 51], the brittleness index
created by carefully analyzing the pre- and post-peak stress-
strain curves can reﬂect the specimen 's pre-peak resistance to
plastic deformation and describe the reduction in its bearing
capacity following failure. Taking the 28 d curing age as an
example, the brittleness indices of A4 were 143.69%, 284.59%,
and 318.83% higher than those of A3, A2, and A1, respectively
(Fig. 12(a)). The brittleness index of the solidiﬁed soil exhibited
an increasing trend with increasing cement content and
curing age ( Fig. 12 (a)). MolaAbasi et al. [ 64] established a brit-
tleness index based on the peak and residual strengths of
solidiﬁed sandy soil with cement and zeolite. They found that
an increase in cement content reduced the peak strain and led
to an increase in the brittleness index, which is consistent
with the results of this study. The brittleness indices of D1
were 123.48% and 583.85% higher than those of C1 and B1,
respectively ( Fig. 12 (b)). The brittleness indices of D2 were
64.63% and 152.07% higher than those of C2 and B2, respec-
tively (Fig. 12(c)). The brittleness indices of D3 were 56.48% and
80.36% higher than those of C3 and B3, respectively, as shown
in Fig. 12(d). Based on the above results, it was concluded that
the brittleness index of the solidiﬁed soil increased as the
GGBS content and curing age increased. The brittleness
indices of B3 were 147.83% and 787.78% higher than those of
B2 and B1, respectively (Fig. 12(e)). The brittleness indices of C3
were 86.56% and 234.40% higher than those of C2 and C1,
respectively ( Fig. 12 (f)). The brittleness indices of D3 were
77.33% and 134.15% higher than those of D2 and D1, respec-
tively ( Fig. 12 (g)). The brittleness index of the solidiﬁed soil
increased as the alkali-activator level and curing age
increased, as shown in Fig. 12(e)e(g). The brittleness indices of
C2M0.7 were 33.59%, 37.51%, 177.84%, 169.54%, 289.60%, and
600.00% higher than those of C2M0.9, C2M1.1, C2M1.3, C2M1.5,
C2M1.7 and C2M1.9, respectively, as shown in Fig. 12 (h). The
amounts of cement, GGBS, and alkali activator exhibited im-
pacts on the brittleness of the material, as shown in Fig. 12(a),
(b)e(d), and (e) - (g), respectively. The brittleness index
decreased with a rise in the modulus. This showed that a
decrease in modulus and increase in curing age and cement,
GGBS, and alkali-activator contents caused the plastic defor-
mation of solidiﬁed soil to reduce the proportion of total
deformation, making the brittle characteristics more obvious.
4.5. XRD analysis
Albite, calcite, kaolinite, muscovite, and quartz were found in
the cement-stabilized and alkali-activated slag soils through
an XRD analysis of the solidiﬁed soil with different pro-
portions, as shown in Fig. 13 . Geng [ 39] and Zhou [ 53] et al.
found that these components were mainly mineral compo-
nents from the soil. The XRD patterns of A1 and A4 showed
that C eSeH and ettringite were formed, whereas gypsum
products existed in A4. The C 3A in cement reacted with water
and gypsum to generate high-sulfur calcium sulfoaluminate
hydrate (ettringite AFt). When a certain amount of AFt was
generated, it reacted with calcium aluminate hydrate and
Ca(OH)2 to generate single-sulfur calcium sulfoaluminate hy-
drate (AFm). Gypsum was not detected in A1 because gypsum
with less content is completely consumed when the cement
content is low. However, an XRD analysis of the alkali-
Fig. 13 e XRD patterns of solidiﬁed soil.
journal of materials research and technology 2023;25:6039 e60606052

activated slag-solidiﬁed soil revealed the presence of C-A-S-H.
Compared to cement-stabilized soil, alkali-activated slag-
stabilized soil has no ettringite or gypsum and its product is
relatively simple.
4.6. TG/DTG analysis
The change rule of the TG/DTG curve for solidiﬁed soil at
50 /C14 Ce1000 /C14 C is shown in Fig. 14 .C eSeH, gypsum, and
ettringite produced by the cement hydration reaction were
dehydrated at 50 /C14 Ce200 /C14 C, Ca(OH) 2 was decomposed at
400 /C14 Ce520 /C14 C, and calcium carbonate was decomposed at
600 /C14 Ce750 /C14 C[ 65,66]. The DTG curve of A4 exhibited one more
peak than that of A1 at 50 /C14 Ce200 /C14 C and 400 /C14 Ce520 /C14 C. The
two peaks of A4 at 50 /C14 Ce200 /C14 C were mainly caused by the
formation of AFm. It can be concluded that calcium hydrox-
ide, gypsum, ettringite, and C eSeH increased as the cement
concentration increased. Calcium hydroxide produced cal-
cium carbonate when it reacted with atmospheric carbon di-
oxide. Therefore, an increase in calcium hydroxide also
resulted in an increase in calcium carbonate concentration.
The weight loss climaxes at 50 /C14 Ce200 /C14 C and 600 /C14 Ce750 /C14 C
for solidiﬁed soil using alkali eactivated slag as the cementing
material were activated by the dehydration of C-A-S-H and
decomposition of calcium carbonate. The weight-loss peak of
D2 was larger than that of B2 at 50 /C14 Ce200 /C14 C, while the
weight-loss peak at 600 /C14 Ce750 /C14 C exhibited little change, as
shown in the DTG curve in Fig. 14 (b). This demonstrates that
the C-A-S-H content increased as the GGBS level increased,
whereas the calcium carbonate content did not change
considerably. The weight-loss peaks at 50 /C14 Ce200 /C14 C and
600 /C14 Ce750 /C14 C increased with an increase in the alkali-
activator dosage and a decrease in the modulus, indicating
that the formation of C-A-S-H and calcium carbonate was
encouraged by these changes ( Fig. 14(c) and (d)), respectively.
The OH/C0 content of the alkali activator increased owing to an
increase in the quantity of the alkali activator and decrease in
its modulus, which caused Ca(OH) 2 to combine with carbon
dioxide and produce additional calcium carbonate.
4.7. Analysis of pore structure
The T2 spectrum of the solidiﬁed soil was roughly divided into
two peaks, as shown in Fig. 15 . The smaller the T2 value, the
Fig. 14 e TG/DTG of solidiﬁed soil.
journal of materials research and technology 2023;25:6039 e6060 6053

smaller the pore radius, the larger the signal amplitude, and
the greater the proportion of pores in the T2 spectrum of the
solidiﬁed soil. Therefore, the ﬁrst peak represents small pores
and the second peak represents large pores. The size of the
peak represents the proportion of the pore structure. The
strength of the second peak diminished as the cement con-
centration increased, whereas the ﬁrst peak increased, as
shown in Fig. 15 (a). This demonstrates that increasing the
cement content in the solidiﬁed soil reduced the number of
macropores and increased the compactness of the pore
structure. The ﬁrst peak values of D2 were 8.63% and 3.56%
higher than those of B2 and C2, respectively ( Fig. 15 (b)). The
second peak values of D2 were 31.46% and 0.11% lower than
those of B2 and C2, respectively. This demonstrates that the
proportions of small holes increased and large pores
decreased as the GGBS content increased. The ﬁrst and second
peak values exhibited a downward trend with increased
alkali-activator concentration, indicating that the alkali acti-
vator caused a decrease in the ratio of macropores to micro-
pores in the solidiﬁed soil (Fig. 15(c)). The initial values of T2 for
C1 and C3 were 0.1979 and 0.0215 ms, respectively. The
smaller the initial value of T2, the smaller the pore structure,
which indicates that increased alkali-activator content
changed the pore structure of the solidiﬁed soil to micropores.
The peak value of the ﬁrst peak decreased with an increase in
the modulus, whereas that of the second peak increased. This
indicates that an increase in the modulus transformed the
small pores of the solidiﬁed soil sample into large pores,
leading to an increase in the porosity of the sample ( Fig. 15(d)).
This study converted the T2 spectrum into a pore radius
distribution to explore the distribution of pore structures with
different pore radii in more detail. The pore structure was
broken down into four categories: micro-pores ( <0.05 mm),
small pores (0.05 e0.1 mm), mesopores (0.1 e1 mm), and mac-
ropores (>1 mm). The decrease in the alkali-activator modulus
and increase in cement, GGBS, and alkali-activator contents
resulted in the porosity of the solidiﬁed soil showing a
downward trend ( Fig. 16). The proportions of micropores and
small pores increased with an increase in the cement content,
whereas the proportions of mesopores and macropores
decreased, as shown in Fig. 16(a). Combined with the XRD and
TG/DTG results, it can be concluded that the C eSeH, gypsum,
and ettringite produced by the hydration reaction with an
increase in cement content had an obvious ﬁlling effect on the
macropores and mesopores. The micropores of the solidiﬁed
soil showed an increasing trend with an increase in the GGBS
content, and the proportions of mesopores and macropores
showed a decreasing trend ( Fig. 16 (b)). The amount of GGBS
Fig. 15 e T2 spectrum of solidiﬁed soil.
