5. Discussion
The compression deformation process for solidiﬁed soil under
a load is accompanied by energy absorption and dissipation.
The energy dissipated by the solidiﬁed soil and elastic-plastic
deformation shown by solidiﬁed soil are inextricably linked to
the internal damage of the solidiﬁed soil. The relationship
between D, Ud/U, and BI is unclear, so the statistics of all the
mix proportions were calculated for this problem. After ﬁtting,
it was found that D and Ud/U were linearly related, while the
Ud/U and BI were exponentially functional. A portion of Group
A had a higher dissipation energy ratio than the other groups,
as shown in Fig. 17 . A portion of the data in Group A was far
from the ﬁtting curve, and the same brittleness index
consumed more energy than that in the alkali-activated slag
Fig. 18 e Relationship between dissipation energy ratio
and brittleness index.
Fig. 19 e Relationship between porosity and dissipation energy ratio Ud/U, damage variable D and brittleness index BI.
journal of materials research and technology 2023;25:6039 e60606056

soil mix ratio, as shown in Fig. 18. The greater the proportion
of dissipated energy, the more severe the damage to the so-
lidiﬁed soil and the more obvious the ductility characteristics
exhibited by the sample as the dissipated energy ratio
increased, as shown in Figs. 17 and 18 , respectively. The
stress-strain curve of the cement-soil exhibited more plastic
deformation than that of the alkali-activated slag soil. Sub-
sequently, the strain softening mechanism was less pro-
nounced than the strain hardening mechanism, which was
signiﬁcant. Based on the above ﬁndings, the Ud/U and BI of
solidiﬁed soil can be established using an energy evolution
mechanism to quantitatively evaluate the damage and
deformation of solidiﬁed soil. This differs from the traditional
method of describing the failure characteristics of solidiﬁed
soil by relying on the peak stress and strain strength, and
provides new methods and ideas for the loading and defor-
mation processes of such materials.
In this study, the microstructure of solidiﬁed soil was
investigated in detail using XRD, TG/DTG, and NMR tests. Ac-
cording to the XRD analysis results in Section 4.5, the main
hydration products of the cemented soil were C eSeH, ettrin-
gite, calcium carbonate, and gypsum. The results of the TG/
DTG analysis are presented in Section 4.6, showing that the
products of CeSeH, ettringite, calcium carbonate, and gypsum
increased with an increase in cement content. This was spe-
ciﬁcally because the increase in hydration products ﬁlled the
pore structure of the solidiﬁed soil, which increased the pro-
portion of pore structures with a pore radius of less than
0.1 mm. This led to a decrease in the overall porosity of the
sample and an increase in the bonding force between the soil
particles. Therefore, the brittleness index of the solidiﬁed soil
increased and the damage variable decreased with an increase
in the cement content. For the alkali-activated slag soil, a
reduction in the modulus and amounts of GGBS and alkali
activator promoted the polymerization reaction of the GGBS
and generated more C-A-S-H, thus improving the cohesion of
the solidiﬁed soil. An increase in the alkali-activator content
and decrease in the modulus resulted in an increase in the
amount of OH /C0 in the solution and a reaction with carbon di-
oxide in the air that generated a large amount of calcium
carbonate, thus ﬁlling the pore structure of the solidiﬁed soil.
The soil particles were wrapped with more C-A-S-H, which
increased the cohesion between the particles. The more C-A-S-
H that is in the solidiﬁed soil during the process of failure, the
stronger its ability to resist external loads. The C-A-S-H of the
solidiﬁed soil will break under a high load. Once the C-A-S-H
breaks, the cracks will rapidly expand and penetrate, leading to
strong brittle characteristics of the sample. The differences in
the mix proportion led to the variations in the cementitious
products, and ﬁnally to distinctions in the pore structure of the
solidiﬁed soil. Pu et al. [ 67] conducted microscopic pore
research on cement-solidiﬁed sand using SEM and mercury
intrusion testing and found that an increase in cement content
signiﬁcantly reduced the pore size and pore area ratio of the
sample. In addition, the UCS demonstrated an exponentially
decreasing trend with an increase in pore size and pore speciﬁc
surface area. Wang et al. [ 68] found through experimental
research that the UCS linearly decreased with an increase in
sample porosity. Currently, there is little research on the Ud/U,
D, and BI of the pore structures of solidiﬁed soils. Therefore, the
different mix proportions after 28 d of curing were summa-
rized in this study. It was discovered that Ud/U increased lin-
early with an increase in the porosity of the solidiﬁed soil
(Fig. 19 (a)). D increased exponentially with an increase in
porosity (Fig. 19(b)). Conversely, the BI decreased linearly with
an increase in porosity ( Fig. 19 (c)). The ﬁtting formula of
porosity to Ud/U, D, and BI provided a reference value for the
establishment of the microstructures and macroscopic me-
chanical indices of late-stage solidiﬁed soil ( Fig. 19).
6. Conclusions
The entire compression failure process of solidiﬁed soil was
studied in detail based on the energy evolution mechanism
and Weibull distribution plastic damage model. By analyzing
the total stress-strain curve and XRD, TG/DTG, and NMR test
data of solidiﬁed soil under different curing ages, alkali-
activator moduli, and cement, GGBS, and alkali-activator
contents, the following conclusions can be drawn.
1. In the process of compression failure of the solidiﬁed soil, U,
Ud,a n d D showed an S-shaped growth trend, whereas Ue
initially showed an increase followed by a decreasing trend.
2. Using low-modulus alkali activators and increasing the
curing age and cement, GGBS, and alkali-activator contents
could strengthen the energy of the sample and improve the
ability of the solidiﬁed soil to resist damage.
3. The brittleness index increased with a reduction of the
modulus and increase of curing age and cement, GGBS, and
alkali-activator contents. These results indicate that the
plastic deformation of solidiﬁed soil reduced the propor-
tion of total deformation, making the brittle characteristics
more obvious.
4. The D and the Ud/U show a linear growth trend. The Ud/U
and BI showed an exponential decreasing trend.
5. The hydrating C eSeH, gypsum, and ettringite products
increased with an increase in cement concentration, which
enhanced the compactness and cohesiveness of the so-
lidiﬁed soil between soil particles.
6. The decrease in the modulus and increase in the amounts
of GGBS and alkali activator promoted the polymerization
reaction of GGBS, making the soil particles more ﬁrmly
connected by the C-A-S-H and thus improving the
compactness of the solidiﬁed soil.
7. The porosity and Ud/U exhibited a linear growth trend.
Porosity and D exhibited an exponential growth trend.
Porosity and BI exhibited a linear decreasing trend.
