of cancer. Fig. 1 shows the process of cancer development.
Cancer and personalized medicine
Cancer is a complex disease with a heterogeneous nature as it evolves 
within a patient or in a subpopulation, moderated by population, 
environment, socioeconomic factors, and genetic or cellular abnormal-
ities. The abnormal and uncontrolled proliferation of cells without 
apoptosis forms tumors; these cells are referred to as neoplastic cells and 
are the cause of cancer. Even a single tumor can have a diversity of 
cancer cells; the heterogeneous nature of such cells can be observed 
through heterogeneous disease modulated by genetic, molecular, 
cellular, tissue, population, environmental, and socioeconomic factors, 
cell surface markers, growth rate, and other cancer progression traits 
during the progression of the disease. Such tumors can exist in the 
microenvironment of a complex system along with other cells of stro-
mal, endothelial, or immune origin [3]. Other diseases can also worsen 
the risk of cancer if they are originated in same within the same organ 
with other complications such as contagious diseases (TB, COVID) along 
with cancer symptoms. Also the, complications can arise when medi-
cation for such diseases is given to the patient. Cancer can alter the 
extracellular matrix of tissues and organs at biochemical levels and alter 
the biomechanical functioning of the cell, as well as its architectural and 
topographical solidity. Any tissue or organ of the body can cause cancer 
from the somatic origin, having a similar invasion of cells at distant 
spread, or from hereditary origin, called genetic aberrations. Only a 
portion of the origin of cancer is understood, and most of its origin is due 
to causative factors like lifestyle, environment, or any infection-related 
origin [4].
Hereditary cancer syndromes of autosomal disorder due to faulty 
alleles are known, in which mutations inherited from offspring by parent 
genes are often involved in the triggering or onset of early cancer. Many 
genes involved in the suppression of tumors for protection against 
cancer are mutated, where DNA repair mechanisms fail to repair such 
oncogenes [5]. Commonly found syndromes with oncogenes include 
angiogenesis (blood vessel formation), Lynch syndrome, and breast and 
ovarian cancer syndrome. Cancer cells are not only produced due to 
some genetic inheritance, but they can also proliferate due to an affected 
lifestyle. The factors contributing to the development of various condi-
tions include lifestyle decisions (e.g., smoking or intake of carcinogen 
substances, food habits, and exercise), environmental factors (such as 
exposure to some specific chemicals or radiation), and mutations arising 
during cellular division [6]. Fig. 2 summarizes the various types of 
cancer along with their common symptoms.
The inclusion of PM in cancer research could lead to significant ad-
vancements in the fight against cancer. Prolonged patient therapy, 
complexity of stage, and interpatient variability (difference in response 
to cancer treatment) are the challenges faced by healthcare workers 
while monitoring patients. The identification of molecular alterations as 
targets for treatment has been a key factor in the progress of personal-
ized medicine [7]. Genomic, proteomic, and metabolomic analyses have 
uncovered a variety of molecular alterations that can be exploited for 
personalized treatment. These include PIK3CA, ERBB2, FGFR, BRAF, 
and NTRK mutations or fusions. These discoveries have led to the 
development of targeted therapies that can specifically inhibit these 
molecular alterations, thereby halting the progression of cancer. Novel 
targeted agents are being developed for tumors originating from such 
identified mutations. Personalized treatment is specifically designed to 
target a specific alteration within the cancer. This is a significant 
advancement in the treatment of a group of patients, offering a 
personalized treatment that targets a specific alteration within the 
cancer. E.g., endocrine therapy in breast cancer, imatinib drug for 
chronic myeloid leukemia, and EGFR inhibitors in lung cancer have all 
demonstrated the potential of personalized medicine [8].
Symptomatic origin and development of cancer
An individual suffering from cancer possesses various symptoms. The 
signs and symptoms observed will frequently depend on the type of 
cancer the individual has. A lump (mass of tissue) in the breast, for 
instance, is a sign of breast cancer. An odd-looking mole is a sign of skin 
cancer. Typical signs and symptoms of many forms of cancer include 
excess weight loss, blood in the urine or stools, fatigue, and lumps that 
can be seen anywhere in the body. Cancer begins when normal cells 
undergo genetic mutations that cause them to grow uncontrollably and 
form a mass or lump called a tumor. These mutations can be caused by 
various factors, including exposure to carcinogens (such as tobacco and 
certain chemicals), genetic predisposition, and environmental in-
fluences [9]. Fig. 3 is the systematic representation of various causes 
behind the genetic changes in the individual.
In cancer, uncontrolled and abnormal division of cells very rapidly 
proliferate. Proliferate cells are the same as normal cells and the 
apoptosis process. Abnormal proliferative cells are known as neoplastic 
cells. There are two specific types of cancer one is benign tumors and 
another is malignant tumors. A benign tumor is a localized tumor 
because it is found in the upper surface of cells. It can easily be removed 
from body parts. It can’t be transferred from one body part to another 
