Emergence of different types of cancer
The human body consists of trillions of cells. The cells are of such 
minuscule size that they are only observable via the use of a microscope. 
Cells aggregate to form the tissues and organs of our body. They exhibit 
a high degree of similarity. However, they differ in certain aspects due to 
the fact that body organs perform distinct functions. There exist around 
200 distinct forms of cancer, which can be categorized based on their 
origin in the body, such as breast cancer or lung cancer [13].
Importance of classifying cancer diagnosis and treatment
Cancer detection is the most crucial stage as it drastically affects the 
survival of patients, but cancer is mostly detected at advanced stages due 
to unawareness or negligence by individuals [14]. Moreover, the 
affordability of treatments based on cancer type and stage for most in-
dividuals with economically weaker backgrounds may cause a step 
backward in the treatment of such diseases. Technological advances in 
the understanding of biological processes, such as powerful analytical 
molecular techniques, imaging, and histopathological methods with 
clinical trials, can provide substantial improvement in the evaluation of 
early-stage detection of cancer and advancement in cancer vaccine 
production (Fig. 4). 
1. Early Detection and Diagnosis: Identifying the specific type of 
cancer and its origin is crucial for accurate diagnosis. Different 
cancers may require different diagnostic tests and screening 
methods. For instance, mammograms are used for breast cancer 
screening, while colonoscopies are used for detecting colorectal 
cancer [15].
2. Tailored Treatment: Each type of cancer behaves differently and 
responds to treatments in unique ways. Classifying cancers based on 
their origin (like breast, lung, or prostate) helps oncologists design 
personalized treatment plans. For example, treatments effective for 
breast cancer might not be suitable for lung cancer [16].
3. Research and Advancements: Understanding the specific types of 
cancer is essential for ongoing research. Scientists study different 
cancers to develop targeted therapies and immunotherapies tailored 
to combat the unique characteristics of each cancer type [17].
4. Prognosis: The type and stage of cancer significantly impact a pa-
tient’s prognosis. Some cancers are more aggressive and spread 
rapidly, while others progress slowly. Classifying cancers provides 
valuable information about the likely course of the disease [18].
5. Support and Awareness: Cancer classifications help in raising 
awareness about specific types of cancer. This knowledge is essential 
for public education, support networks, and fundraising efforts 
aimed at finding cures and supporting patients [13,17].
