Current global scenario
Globally, the worldwide burden of cancer prevalence as well as death 
is increasing at a rapid rate; this is due to a combination of causes, 
including population increase and aging, as well as shifts in the distri-
bution and frequency of the primary cancer risk factors, many of which 
are linked to socioeconomic development.
In every nation on the planet, cancer is one of the main causes of 
death and a major obstacle to raising life expectancy. As per the World 
Health Organization’s (WHO) 2019 estimations, cancer ranks first or 
second in 112 out of 183 nations and ranks as third or fourth in 23 other 
countries as the primary cause of death under the age of 70. The fact that 
cancer is becoming more prevalent as the top cause of death is partially 
due to the notable declines in stroke as well as coronary heart disease 
mortality rates worldwide.
In India, one woman passes away from cervical cancer every eight 
minutes, and approximately one woman dies from breast cancer for 
