every two newly diagnosed cases.
It is estimated that tobacco usage causes up to 3500 deaths per day in 
India. In 2018, tobacco usage, including smoking, was the cause of 
approximately 3, 17,928 fatalities (approximately) among men and 
women [29,30].
PM for cancer treatment of various alignments
In India, various types of drugs and medicine (like herbal, Ayurvedic, 
or allopathic) have been given to suffering patients from ancient times in 
individualized forms [31]. These alignments are part of day-to-day 
cultural practices in the form of food items of plant and animal-based 
origins with different blends to the immunized body [3]. Personalized 
medications were based on herbs that are effective for certain symp-
tomatic diseases and also immunized the immune system as a nontoxic 
adjuvant Fig. 5 shows the structure of the PM workflow.
Most of the diseases like cancer that mostly originated from Western 
countries were mere less or none in the Indian subcontinent. Due to 
personalized care from infancy to adulthood, many antimicrobial and 
anti-cancerous Ayurvedic, herbal, oil-based ointments were part of in-
dividuals’ lifestyles [32]. The ointments mostly provide skin care for 
many epidermal diseases, and ingestion of herbal-based formulations 
provides immunity. They were also effective against dysentery, diarrhea 
or food poisoning, and many other health-related issues. Many herbal, 
Ayurvedic, or allopathic plant-based medications are a combination of 
chemical entities that are used in cancer treatments. Many of the med-
ications like CHOP (chemotherapy) for non-Hodgkin lymphoma use 
cyclophosphamide, hydroxyl doxorubicin, Oncovin, CMF, i.e., cyclo-
phosphamide, methotrexate, and fluorouracil [33]. The herbal formu-
lations contain different active natural compounds that can be used in 
therapeutic treatments with lower adverse effects and are also syner-
gistically used in treatments. Moreover, many of the cancer treatments 
become multidrug-resistant to cancer treatments to cancer cells, causing 
cross-resistance to chemotherapeutic agents. Nanoparticles can also be 
used to minimize toxicity to biological systems by targeting the use of 
these particles for treatment. These can be synthesized for different 
purposes, like antifungal and antibacterial agents. Also, nanoparticles 
can improve the diagnosis for improvement and management of 
different types of cancers [34].
Progress in drugs used in personalized medicine
The use of Pharmacogenomics for the betterment of personalized 
medicine by implementing genotyping technology to select and design 
drugs for individual patients or patients with similar diagnoses. The 
inter-individual genomic factor variability is a challenging aspect for 
predicting drug delivery and dosage, individual aspects of drugs such as 
improved efficacy, its activation, prevention measures, adverse re-
actions, further development or discovering more compatible drugs, and 
targeting the drug for cancer therapy models all needed to be considered 
in Pharmacogenomics. Pharmacogenes are determined by regulatory 
and coding regions within genes, which further influence pharmacology 
drugs for the biological systems of human beings [21]. The genome 
region decides the fate of the effectivity of drugs via modification of drug 
targets, interruption of sensitive drug biological pathways, alterations in 
their distribution or metabolism, etc.
The chemotherapeutic treatment of the drug is effective when the 
active compound reaches an effective concentration during treatment in 
tumor cells. Factors like the adsorption of the drug, its distribution and 
binding to proteins, metabolic activity status, effective transport within 
tumor cells, and readily removal from the body are crucial for effective 
treatment. Rapamycin and its derived forms are the drugs administered 
to treat renal cell carcinomas and soft tissue sarcomas, along with rare 
tumors. Several enzymes (CYP3A4, CYP3A5, and CYP2C8) and trans-
porters (ABCB1) are involved in the metabolism and transport of rapa-
logues and that have known genetic variants that affect their activity 
[35,36]. Some tumors express high levels of these enzymes and trans-
porters, which could reduce the intratumoral concentration and effec-
tiveness of rapalogues. The tumors with mutations in the mTOR 
pathway, such as AKT, PTEN, TSC1, and TSC2, are more sensitive to 
rapalogue treatment. Hence, a three-layered approach for PM in the use 
of rapalogues for cancer treatment is being followed. Genetic testing of 
the patient to predict the tolerance and optimal dosing of rapalogues, 
pharmacokinetic monitoring of the blood concentration of rapalogues 
and review of co-administered drugs that might interfere with their 
metabolism, gene expression analysis of the tumor to assess the 
expression of resistance mechanisms and the activation of the mTOR 
pathway [37].
Dysregulation of the FGF (fibroblast growth factor) signaling axis is 
linked to oncogenesis, tumor development, as well as resistance to 
cancer treatment in a number of solid tumors [38]. FGFRs consist of 
FGFR5, which is devoid of an intracellular kinase domain, and the four 
