5

transmembrane tyrosine kinase receptors (FGFR1–4) that are highly 
conserved. FGF (fibroblast growth factor) activates each of these re-
ceptors, which helps promote cell division and survival. The abnormal 
FGFR signaling pathway has been suggested as a possible target for 
therapy in several trials examining various solid tumors, and numerous 
inhibitors are now being developed [39]. Futibatinib is a new person-
alized cancer treatment that can significantly improve the prognosis for 
some patients with bile duct cancer. This drug targets a specific genetic 
alteration known as FGFR2 fusion, which is found in some cases of bile 
duct cancers [40]. This drug binds selectively and irreversibly to the ATP 
binding pocket of (fibroblast growth factor receptor) FGFR 1–4, result-
ing in the inhibition of FGFR-mediated signal transduction pathways, 
reduced tumor cell proliferation, and increased tumor cell death in tu-
mors with FGFR 1–4 genetic aberrations. In a number of FGFR-driven 
human tumor xenograft models, oral administration of Fumibatinib 
resulted in a significant dose-dependent reduction in tumor size [41]. 
Furthermore, the tumor reduction was linked to a sustained FGFR in-
hibition that was dose-dependent. In comparison to chemotherapy, the 
medication likewise had mild adverse effects [42].
Use of AI in personalized medicine for cancer
AI is also being used in personalized treatment and management 
models from screening to diagnosis, early detection, and treatment to 
decrease mortality and recurrence in cancer cases. Deep learning-based 
AI automatic classification systems have been developed to differentiate 
between carcinoma and non-lactating mastitis in breast cancer. CNN 
(Convolutional Neural Network) algorithms have shown higher accu-
racy in the diagnosis and detection of breast cancer [43]. Continuous 
contrast-enhanced ultrasound allows radiologists to access the dynamic 
vascularization of vessels and tissues in real time, making it useful for 
differentiating benign and malignant tissues using machine-learning 
models [44].
Various machine learning models have been used to predict lymph 
node metastasis, with the extreme gradient boost model being best for 
sentinel lymph node detection and preoperative clinical guidance [45]. 
Radiotherapy is often used alongside chemotherapy, but it has its limi-
tations. 3D printed bolus is used in postmastectomy patients to increase 
dose effectivity. Seroma analysis after intraoperative radiotherapy is 
also being studied, but its effectiveness remains unclear due to varia-
tions in body reactions. Radiation-induced dermatitis can be predicted 
with high accuracy using the gradient-boosted decision trees model and 
random forest model as screening methods [44].
Genetic basis cancer biomarkers for treatment with personalized medicine
The advent of PM in cancer treatment has been driven by advance-
ments in molecular testing and targeted therapies. These developments 
have led to improved outcomes for cancer patients with specific bio-
markers. The initial diagnosis of cancer is based on the clinical history of 
the patient, cancer type and site in the body, and other laboratory in-
vestigations [46]. The patient’s genome is analyzed for mutation along 
with a database search that predicts the severity of the mutation. The 
therapy is selected based on available former information from prior 
studies, consent of the patient for therapy preference, and availability of 
targeted agents or biomarkers that indicate mutation. Clinical trials of 
cancer patients with such novel treatments are beneficial in cases of poor 
prognosis via standard treatments. Also, the studies of such patients 
improve and benefit the subsequent cases in cost-efficient, improved 
outcomes and sharing of information [7,47].
