Personalized medicine and related issues
Despite the significant progress, there are still limitations and chal-
lenges to overcome in the field of precision medicine. Tumor hetero-
geneity, resistance mechanisms, and complex molecular profiles can 
limit the efficacy of targeted therapies. However, the development of 
preclinical models, liquid biopsy techniques, and molecular tumor 
boards are promising strategies to overcome these challenges and opti-
mize patient selection for personalized treatment. An evolving strategy 
for tumor prevention and therapy that considers the genetic variations 
inside and between tumors, the immunological milieu surrounding the 
tumor, the patient’s lifestyle, and any associated morbidities is known as 
personalized or precise medicine in oncology [1].
PM has the capacity to modify the tumor’s immune environment and 
target treatment toward the oncogenic causes of the tumor. The detec-
tion of this variation has been made possible by the availability of 
contemporary biomedical technology, such as proteomics, DNA 
sequencing, and wireless monitoring devices [48]. This has effectively 
exposed the necessity for some degree of medicine customization. 
Moreover, PM seeks to maximize tumor response while accounting for 
the toxicities brought on by therapy for each individual patient. In this 
method, the maintenance of the functioning of organs and, conse-
quently, quality of life is paired with optimized tumor response. 
Furthermore, the ultimate goal of PM is to provide better patient care 
[49].
The difficulties that come with this reality in the future will be not 
only attempting to render individual characterizations more efficient 
but also creating and testing personalized medications so that they can 
demonstrate their efficacy. That’s not to argue that treatments that are 
widely effective (i.e., the classic single-agent ""blockbuster"" medications) 
should be disregarded if found; rather, it means that they may prove to 
be extremely difficult to find in the future. There are some additional 
issues with personalized medicine that might be challenging to resolve 
soon. Large-scale data gathering, for instance, may be required to find 
characteristics that discriminate against groups of people who would 
benefit more from a certain solution. This could raise issues about pri-
vacy as well as the possibility that the information about those people 
will be misused for bad [49,50].
Conclusion
Many advancements in the treatment of solid tumor patients have 
been made possible in recent years by personalized care. In order to 
achieve a meaningful and substantial improvement in all clinical results, 
a deeper comprehension of molecular science is required. A strategy 
incorporating proteomics, metabolomics, and changes in DNA and RNA 
will be required to improve. The use of translational research utilizing 
organoids, xenografts, and liquid biopsy to assess molecular alterations 
brought on by clonal pressure resulting from target agent use or tumor 
heterogeneity would aid in the identification of mechanisms underlying 
resistance and raise the prospect of new combinations. It is imperative 
that further research be done to increase precision.
It is evident that the inclusion of a PPM perspective in cancer 
research and therapy could lead to significant advancements in the fight 
against cancer, given the disease’s complexity and interpatient vari-
ability. This has already begun to be acknowledged in the current state 
of science and medicine through PPM research, PPM products, and FDA 
support; however, before PPM can be fully integrated into standardized 
care, a number of larger societal obstacles need to be addressed and 
overcome. The progress of personalized medicine in cancer treatment is 
a testament to the power of scientific innovation and collaboration. 
While challenges remain, the future of PM in oncology is bright, with the 
potential to transform cancer treatment and improve patient outcomes. 
As we continue to unravel the complexities of cancer at the molecular 
level, we move closer to a future where every cancer patient has a 
treatment plan tailored to their unique needs.
