1. Introduction
The biologically mediated synthesis of silver nanobactericides
(AgNbs) has attracted substantial interest as a sustainable strategy for
the development of antimicrobial agents (Baker and Perianova, 2019).
The increasing prevalence of antimicrobial resistance highlights the
urgent global need for novel and safer antimicrobial solutions (Presti-
nacietal.,2015).Ongoingresearcheffortsareinvestigatingnanoscale
materials with the potential to inhibit the proliferation of pathogenic
bacteria (Ye et al., 2023). One promising avenue involves evaluating
plantsandtheirassociatedendosymbiontsforsynthesizingAgNbswith
antimicrobial properties (Bahrulolum et al., 2021; Ali et al., 2020).
Plantsinhabitinguniquegeographicalareas,suchastheWesternGhats,
have been reported to secrete valuable phytocomponents that confer
protection against stress conditions and pathogenic attacks (Sohrab
et al., 2021). Concurrently, endosymbionts within these plants play a
vital role inprotecting them from invading insects and pathogenicmi-
crobes,contributingtotheoverallhealthoftheplants(GuptaandNair,
