2. Materials and methods
2.1. Processing of plant material
SpecimensofCurcumalongaL.werecollectedfromregionsofprolific
growth in the Western Ghats, Karnataka, Southern India. Post-harvest,
the plant materials were thoroughly washed using aseptic techniques
to eliminate any adhering soil particles. The cleaned specimens were
then aseptically crushed. Subsequently, 20 g of the plant material was
finelygroundwithapestleandmortar,followedbyboilingin200mLof
sterilewaterfor30minutes.Theresultingaqueousextractwasusedas
thesubstrateforthesynthesisofAgNbs(Syedetal.,2017).
2.2. Endosymbiont isolation
Theplantmaterialsweredissectedintosmallsegmentsusingsterile
scissors.Thesesegmentsweresubmergedina3.5%sodiumhypochlo-
rite solution for approximately 3–5 minutes, followed by thorough
rinsing with sterile distilled water. Subsequently, the segments were
treatedwith70%ethanolfor3minutesandsubjectedtoseveralrinses
in sterile distilled water. After surface sterilization, the plant segments
were aseptically placed onto nutrient agar and then incubated (Syed
etal.,2019).
2.3. Synthesis of nanobactericides
Thephytosynthesisprocessinvolvedthecombinationof1mMsilver
nitratewiththeaqueousextractoftheCurcuma longaL.plantinaratio
of9:1.Thismixturewassubjectedtoincubationatvarioustemperatures
andpHlevels.Similarly,thesynthesisofAgNbsusingendosymbiontwas
conductedbyblendingasilvernitratesolutionwithacell-freeextractin
a ratio of 7:3. The initiation of AgNbs synthesis was indicated by
observable color changes, and confirmation was obtained through
analysisusingUV-Visspectrophotometry(Syedetal.,2019).
2.4. Characterization of nanobactericides
ThecrystallinenatureofsynthesizedAgNbs,wasstudiedusingXRD
spectroscopy and functional group analysis was studied via FTIR anal-
ysis was conducted at room temperature to determine the functional
groupsassociatedwiththeplantextract.Thedeterminationofaverage
sizewas studiedusing Transmissionelectronmicroscopy andDynamic
LightScattering(Syedetal.,2019).
2.5. Screening of endosymbionts for antimicrobial potential via agar
overlay assay
The isolated endosymbionts were inoculated as points on nutrient
media and subsequently cultured at 37◦C. Colonies were rendered
inactivebya20-minuteexposuretochloroformandoverlaidwith0.6%
nutrientagar,whichhadbeenseededwiththetestpathogen.Thesystem
was then grown at 37◦C, and zone of inhibition was observed (Baker
etal.,2015).
2.6. Culturing of potent endosymbionts
The selected endosymbiont was cultured at large scale by trans-
ferringactivelygrowingbacteriaintoaflaskcontainingnutrientbroth,
subsequently incubated at 37◦C. Supernatant was used to extract sec-
ondarymetabolites(Mohanetal.,2016).
2.7. Extraction of secondary metabolites
The extraction of secondary metabolites involved vigorous shaking
of the supernatant with ethyl acetate (1:1) ratio for 30 minutes. The
resulting organic phase was separated and subsequently evaporated at
40◦Cwithrotaryflashevaporatorandtheresiduewasthendissolvedin
1mLofdimethylsulfoxide(DMSO)(Mohanetal.,2016).
2.8. Antimicrobial potential of nanobactericides and secondary
metabolites
2.8.1. Broth dilution assay
Aseptic tubes were filled with 1 mL of nutrient broth, following
which 10 µl of AgNbs and 10 µl of test pathogens (Escherichia coli and
Staphylococcusaureus)wereadded.Thetubesweresubsequentlygrown
at 37◦C for 24 hours. Turbidity measurement indicated the organism
growth, which was measured at 600 nm. Throughout the experiment,
positiveandnegativecontrolsweremaintained(Bakeretal.,2020).
2.8.2. Minimum inhibitory concentration
The minimum inhibitory concentration required to suppress the
growthoforganismswastestedaspertheprotocoldescribedby(Baker
etal.,2020)withslightmodifications.Underasepticconditions,50 μlof
broth media (Muller Hinton) was added to all wells. The third row
received 50 μl of AgNbs (1 mg/mL), with sequential dilutions. Addi-
tionally,tomeasurethegrowthresazurinwasadded(10 μl)toallwells.
The concentration adjusted by adding 30 μl of iso-sensitized broth to
each well. Subsequently, finally, the test bacterium was added at con-
centration of 10 μl into each well except first row which served as a
sterility indicator. The MIC plate was subjected to incubation for
24hoursat37◦C.Thegrowthpatternwasmonitoredwithcolorchange
frompurpletopink.
2.9. Dye degradation assay
The selected dyes viz., safranin, crystal violet, and methylene blue
werephotolyticallydegradedusingthemethodoutlinedby(Bakeretal.,
2020)withslightmodification.About1mgofAgNbssynthesizedfrom
plantsandendosymbiontswereaddedindividuallytostocksolutionof
each dye at a concentration of 10 mg/mL. The mixture was incubated
