2

andobservedfordegradation.Thecolorchangeinthereactionmixture
was confirmed by UV–visible spectroscopy. The percentage of the dye
thatwasdegradedwascalculatedusingtheformulabelow.
D(%)=(Dye(i)-Dye(ii))/Dye(i)*100
whereD(%)isthedecolorizationpercentage.
Dye(i)istheinitialabsorbance.
Dye(ii)isthefinalabsorbance.
2.10. Partial purification of secondary metabolite via Bioautography
guided fractionation
Bioautography-guided fractionation was executed on a Thin-Layer
Chromatography (TLC) silica gel plate. The crude extract was applied
to the lower end of the TLC and placed in a solvent chamber using a
Chloroform/Methanol(9:1)system.TLCwasperformed,andtheplates
were examined under UV-light at 254 and 365 nm. Subsequently, the
TLCplatesweredriedtoeliminateresidualsolventtracesandplacedina
sterile petriplate. Brain heart infusion media with 0.6 % agar, seeded
withTTCdyeand1%ofthetestpathogens,wasoverlaidontotheTLC
and cultured at 37◦C for 24 hours. The growth pattern was observed
acrossthebandsagainstapinkorredbackgroundandweredocumented
asactivity(Raoetal.,2015).
2.11. Molecular profiling of potent strain
The isolated strain demonstrating activity underwent molecular
profiling. The DNA was extracted and 16 S rDNA was performed with
universal primers. The amplified product was purified to obtain se-
quences which were matched with online NCBI database using BLAST
tool. The closest match was selected, and phylogenetic tree was con-
structed using bootstrap method by employing Limrr software. (Rao
etal.,2015).
2.12. Biochemical characterization
Gram staining was carried out alongside biochemical characteriza-
tion.Usingasterileloop,bacteriumwassmeared,andcatalaseactivity
wasconfirmedbyintroducinghydrogenperoxide.Theoxidasetestwas
conducted by applying a small amount of bacterial suspension to the
oxidasediscusingadisposablestick.Subsequently,theIMViCtestwas
executedfollowingthestandardprotocoloutlinedby(BakerandSatish,
2013).
2.13. Gas-chromatography analysis
To characterize the chemical profile of crude extract with antimi-
crobial properties, Gas Chromatography-Mass Spectrometry (GC-MS)
analysis was conducted. Perkin Elmer GC Clarus 680 system, with a
fusedsilicacolumnpackedwithHP-5MS,wasused.Parametersincluded
helium carrier gas, injector temperature at 250◦C, and specific oven
temperature sequences. Mass detector conditions involved electron
impact ionization mode, with spectra compared to the GC-MS NIST
(2017)library(Rakhmawatieetal.,2023).
3. Results
ThesynthesisofAgNbsfromCurcumalongaL.wasvisuallyaffirmed
by the distinct dark brown coloration of the reaction mixture. This
synthesis was further substantiated by UV-spectroscopy analysis,
revealingaprominentpeakat433nm(Fig.1A),indicativeofthesurface
plasmon resonance characteristic of AgNbs. Notably, surface
sterilization procedures effectively eliminated epiphytic growth, as
observed in the control plate. Colonies emerging from the plant seg-
ments were selectively chosen for subsequent experimentation. Indi-
vidual isolates were cultured in nutrient broth, and their cell-free
extractswereutilizedforAgNbssynthesisupontreatmentwitha1mM
silver nitrate solution. Under optimized conditions, synthesis comple-
tion was confirmed by the distinct change in color of the reaction
mixture.AdditionalverificationwasachievedthroughUV-spectroscopy,
displayingapeakat418nm(Fig.1B).
3.1. Characterization of silver nanobactericides
ThefunctionalgroupidentificationofAgNbssynthesizedfromplant
extract was achieved through FTIR analysis. Characteristic peaks cor-
responding to specific vibrational stretches were observed:
3349.28cm⁻1 for the hydroxyl group, 1641.46cm⁻1 for the carbonyl
group,1346.04cm⁻1forthealkanegroup,and639.11cm⁻1forthealkyl
group (Fig. 2A). Similarly, AgNbs derived from the endosymbiont
exhibited peaks at 3362.24cm⁻1 (hydroxyl group), 1637.96cm⁻1
(carbonylgroup),1377.25cm⁻1 (alkanegroup),and635.25cm⁻1 (alkyl
group).Additionally,thecrystallinenatureoftheAgNbswasconfirmed
throughXRDanalysis,revealingcharacteristicplanesatspecific2theta
angles (Fig.3A &3B).Morphologically,thesynthesizedAgNbs exhibi-
ted polydisperse characteristics in both plant and endosymbiont-
mediated synthesis. While the average size of AgNbs derived from
plants exceeded 100nm, those from endosymbionts measured around
80nm,asdepictedinFig.4A &4B.
3.2. Antimicrobial potential of silver nanobactericides and secondary
metabolites
The antimicrobial efficacy of both AgNbs and extracted secondary
metaboliteswasevaluatedbymonitoringthedecreaseinopticaldensity
of test pathogens at 600nm. A comparison of the growth pattern of
treatedpathogenswiththatofthecontrolrevealedasignificantreduc-
tion in pathogen growth upon treatment with AgNbs and secondary
metabolites,assummarizedinTable1.Escherichiacolidisplayedgreater
susceptibility compared to Staphylococcus aureus among the tested
pathogens.Furthermore,theMinimumInhibitoryConcentration(MIC)
assaycorroboratedtheantimicrobialactivity,indicatingthatthelowest
concentration required to inhibit thegrowth ofthe test pathogens was
determinedtobe0.625mg/mL.
3.3. Dye degradation
Thephotolyticdegradationofdyeswascompletedwithina12-hour
incubationperiod.Initialobservationsweremadevisually,andUV–vi-
siblespectroscopywasemployedtoconfirmthedegradationofthedyes.
Measurementswere taken atthe respectivewavelengths forindividual
dyes,bothinitiallyanduponcompletionofthedegradationprocess.The
percentage of degradation was calculated and tabulated in Table 3.
Remarkably, among the selected dyes, the highest percentage of
degradation, 81.27%, was achieved with safranin treated with AgNbs
derivedfromendosymbionts.Conversely,nanobactericidessynthesized
from plants exhibited the highest degradation percentage of 59.88%
withmethyleneblue.
3.4. Partial purification via bioautography guided fractionation
Thepartialpurificationprocess,guidedbybioautography,unveiled
adistinctzoneofinhibitionaligningwiththeactivebanddemonstrating
antibacterial activity. The prominent clear zone signifies the pro-
nounced inhibitory effects exerted by the specific band. Particularly
noteworthy is the observation that bands with Rf (retention factor)
values of 0.5 and 0.7 displayed significant activity among the diverse
bandsanalyzed(Fig.5).
