3.6. Molecular characterization
Themolecularcharacterization,coupledwithalignmentagainstthe
NCBI BLAST database, identified a bacterium exhibiting the highest
similarity to Pseudomonas aeruginosa. Subsequently, the purified
sequence wassubmittedtotheonlinedatabaseGenBank,whereitwas
assigned the accession number OR984817. The construction of phylo-
genetictreesfurtherelucidatedthetaxonomicrelationshipbetweenthe
identified bacterium and Pseudomonas aeruginosa (Fig. 6). This
comprehensiveanalysisnotonlyconfirmsthetaxonomicidentityofthe
bacterium but also provides insights into its evolutionary relationship
withinthe Pseudomonasgenus.
3.7. Identification of the metabolite
The analysis results from GC-MS chromatogram revealed different
peaks with major mass spectral peak was matched with NIST (2017)
library to identify as 1,2-benzenedicarboxylic acid, decyl octyl ester at
retentiontimeof21.0.Theidentifiedmetabolitemolecularformulawas
foundtobeC26H42O4 withmolecularweightof418asshowninFig.7.
4. Discussion
Inthepresentinvestigation,thesynthesisofAgNbswascarriedout
utilizingtheaqueousextractofCurcumalongaL.Theutilizationofplant
materials for synthesis purposes is widely recognized in the scientific
literature,providingafeasiblealternativetoconventionalmethods.The
synthesis procedure adhered to a protocol outlined by Gupta et al.
(2019),resultinginaswiftreaction,evidencedbythediscerniblecolor
change, in line with previous observations (Dikshit et al., 2021).
Completionofsynthesiswithin60minutes,asobservedinthisstudy,is
consistentwith priorreport (Prasad etal., 2019). Confirmationofsyn-
thesis was attained through UV spectroscopy, revealing a peak at
433nm, aligning with existing literature (Hettiarachchi et al., 2021).
Furthermore,FTIRanalysisunveiledthepresenceofhydroxyl,carbonyl,
alkane, and alkyl functional groups, corroborating earlier findings
(ManeandKhilare,2021).
Screening of bacterial endosymbionts within Curcuma longa L.
focused on their potential to secrete antimicrobial compounds, in
accordance with research highlighting the role of endosymbionts in
secondary metabolite production (Kumar et al., 2016). Successful syn-
thesis of AgNbs utilizing endosymbionts was achieved, with maximum
