absorbance observed at 418nm, consistent with prior study (Hettiar-
achchi et al., 2021). Morphological analysis revealed polydispersity in
the synthesized AgNbs, with average sizes exceeding 100nm for
plant-mediated synthesis and approximately 80nm for
endosymbiont-mediatedsynthesis.
XRDanalysisconfirmeddistinctpeakscorrespondingtosilvercrystal
planes, consistent with published literature (Jemal et al., 2017). Anti-
microbial screening against two pathogens followed standard protocol
(Syed et al., 2016b), including broth dilution and MIC determination
(Maelaetal.,2022).Thedegradationofdyes,apressingenvironmental
concernduetotheirrecognizedroleasmajorwaterpollutants(Kumari
et al., 2023), was undertaken. The successful degradation of all three
selected dyes, each surpassing 50%, aligns with previous studies on
nanoparticle-mediateddyedegradationprocesses(Kumaretal.,2022).
The partial purification process involved TLC, with bioautography-
guided fractionation confirming antimicrobial potential, consistent
withestablishedmethodologies(Duanetal.,2021).Theoptimalsolvent
system,Chloroform:Methanol(9:1),wasemployedfollowingastandard
protocol (Wang et al., 2021). Notably, the active band at 0.7 Rf value
exhibited greater intensity, prompting GC-MS analysis, revealing the
novelmetabolite1,2-benzenedicarboxylicacid,decyloctylester,witha
molecularweight of418. While limitedreports exist onmicrobial pro-
duction of this metabolite, previous characterization in Plumbagi zey-
lanica L. roots identified it as 1,2-benzenedicarboxylic acid, disooctyl
ester.
This study contributes preliminary insights into the synthesis of
AgNbs from plant and endosymbiont sources, alongside the identifica-
tionofanovelmetabolite.Thesefindingsholdsignificanceinaddressing
antimicrobialresistance,presentingalternativestrategiesforcombating
pathogens.
5. Conclusion
The present study highlights the significant role of both plants and
their associated endosymbionts in the synthesis of AgNbs, offering
valuable insights to combat antimicrobial resistance. The synthesized
AgNbs demonstrated notable efficacy, particularly against Escherichia.
coli, compared to Staphylococcus aureus counterparts due to its distinct
cellwallcomposition.Thestudyalsoreportstheendosymbiontsecreted
novel antimicrobial metabolite, 1,2-benzenedicarboxylic acid, decyl
