ABSTRACT
Inorganic phosphate (Pi) homeostasis in plants is regulated by inositol pyrophosphates (PP-InsPs), which mediate
phosphate starvation responses. While bene ﬁcial microorganisms, such as arbuscular mycorrhizal fungi,
contribute to phosphate uptake, pathogenic fungi often exploit phosphate metabolism to enhance virulence.
However, the exact mechanisms by which pathogens manipulate plant phosphate signaling remain largely un-
known. Here, we highlight a recent study by Ulrich Schaffrath and colleagues (Science, 2025) revealing that plant
pathogenic fungi deploy conserved Nudix hydrolase effectors to hydrolyze PP-InsPs, thereby mimicking phos-
phate starvation and suppressing host immunity. These ﬁndings not only expand our understanding of plant-
pathogen interactions, but also open new avenues for crop protection and resistance breeding.
Inorganic phosphate (Pi) is indispensable in plant life activities. It
participates in the synthesis and transportation of important bio-
macromolecules, energy metabolism, and promotes plant growth and
development.1 The state of phosphate in plants profoundly affects the
interaction between plants and microorganisms.2 About 71 % of vascular
plants rely on arbuscular mycorrhizal fungi to hijack phosphate signals
using conserved enzyme effectors to enhance the absorption capacity of
inorganic phosphate.3 For example, the bacterial effector protein SAP11
can upregulate AtPHR1 target genes, thereby inhibiting salicylic
acid-mediated bacterial defense responses. 4 In addition, the bacterial
effector protein XopH can interfere with hormone signaling by reducing
plant inositol pyrophosphate levels.5 However, pathogenic fungi can also
use this mechanism to hinder plant growth, resulting in reduced crop
yields, which in turn poses a serious threat to global food security. 6 Rice
is an extremely important staple food. The annual food loss caused by rice
blast caused by Magnaporthe can feed 60 million people. 7 In addition,
fruit and crop anthracnose caused by Colletotrichum also greatly affects
food production. Studies have found that Nudix hydrolase effectors are
used by plant fungal pathogens such as rice blast and anthracnose, but
little is known about their role in disease. 8 Therefore, understanding the
pathogenic mechanism of these plant pathogenic fungi will provide a
theoretical basis for our understanding of plant-pathogen interactions
and will help us achieve precise prevention and control of pathogens.
The research team led by Ulrich Schaffrath 9 has conducted in-depth
research on the speci ﬁc mechanisms by which pathogens manipulate
plant phosphate signals. They integrated a variety of cutting-edge experi-
mental methods to reveal a new virulence strategy adopted by plant
pathogenic fungi (including Magnaporthe and Colletotrichum). This study
demonstrated that pathogenic fungi can transfer Nudix effectors into host
plant cells and use Nudix hydrolase effectors to destroy the plant's phos-
phate sensing, triggering a phosphate starvation response, thereby
changing host gene expression and promoting disease progression. This
study not only provides us with a new perspective for our in-depth un-
derstanding of the complex signaling interactions between plants and
pathogens, but also enriches the theory of plant-pathogen interactions. It
also provides a theoretical basis for us to explore the regulation of plant
nutritional signals to achieve precise disease prevention and control, which
will help reduce dependence on traditional chemical pesticides and ach-
ieve green and sustainable agricultural production.
The key ﬁnding includes:
(I) Identi ﬁed a conserved Nudix hydrolase effector family that pro-
motes the occurrence of rice blast and anthracnose
In order to test the role of MoNudix (Nudix of Magnaporthe oryzae )
effector protein in rice blast infection of plants, the researchers con-
ducted silencing and knockout experiments on MoNudix and found that
silencing or knocking out MoNudix could alleviate the symptoms of rice
