blast disease. A signiﬁcant reduction in rice blast lesions and a signi ﬁcant
reduction in mycelial growth were observed. To further verify the role of
Nudix, the researchers reintroduced wild-type MoNudix or mutant
MoNudix proteins into the Nudix knockout rice blast fungus and found
that only the reintroduction of the wild type could restore the toxicity of
the deleted body. These above results indicate that MoNudix is important
for the activity of rice blast and host immune suppression. In addition,
deletion of CgNudix (Nudix of Colletotrichum graminicola) reduced fungal
growth and slowed infection in maize cells. These results suggest that
Nudix effectors can increase the virulence of multiple pathogens on
different host plants.
(II) Identi ﬁcation of the Nudix effector of Magnaporthe and Colleto-
trichum to be the PP-InsP hydrolase
The researchers expressed, puri ﬁed and structurally analyzed the
MoNudix protein. After screening its substrates through structural anal-
ysis and enzymatic analysis, they found that the substrate of MoNudix is
5-PP-InsP5. The substrate selectivity and enzyme activity of Nudix in rice
blast fungus and anthracnose fungus are conserved. As a type of PP-InsP,
5-PP-InsP5 is a key signal molecule for sensing phosphate status in plants.
The function of Nudix effector molecules to hydrolyze 5-PP-InsP5 enables
pathogens to simulate phosphate starvation and disrupt the normal
phosphate regulation of plants.
(III) MoNudix infects plants and translocates to the host cytoplasm to
activate plant phosphate starvation responses
The researchers used live cell imaging to determine the localization of
MoNudix during plant infection. Imaging results showed that the protein
was located in the biotrophic interfacial complex (BIC, a special structure
formed during the interaction between pathogens and host plants,
located at the contact interface. Pathogens rely on host cells for nutri-
tion). Further experiments conﬁrmed that MoNudix can be transferred to
host cells through BIC during rice blast infection, thereby exerting its
effects. The researchers then cleverly used the GAL4-VP16 luciferase
reporter system to verify that MoNudix disrupts the interaction between
SPX and PHR by hydrolyzing PP-InsP in plant cells. Further tran-
scriptomics results showed that in the experimental comparison of
MoNudix wild-type and knockout infected rice, the cell's response to
phosphate starvation became the only signi ﬁcantly enriched upregulated
biological process, indicating that MoNudix is responsible for inducing
phosphate starvation signals during rice infection.
Therefore, as shown in Fig. 1 , based on the above experimental re-
sults, the researchers proposed a model in which MoNudix plays a viru-
lence function in the infection process, that is, after the rice blast fungus
infects the host plant, the secreted Nudix is transferred to the host cell
through BIC, and the binding of SPX-PHR is destroyed by hydrolysis of
PP-InsP. After PHR is released, it enters the cell nucleus and binds to
P1BS elements, triggering a phosphate starvation response.
The mechanistic insight of this study is that it reveals how pathogenic
fungi use conserved Nudix effectors to degrade the signaling molecule
PP-InsP in plant cells, disrupt the normal phosphate regulation network
of plants, activate the phosphorus starvation response, and thus weaken
host defense and promote fungal pathogenicity. However, the exact
mechanism by which MoNudix leads to enhanced disease symptoms
needs further study. It may be related to the increase of immunosup-
pressive genes regulated by PHR transcription factors. Studying this
mechanism will help us better understand the interaction between
pathogenic fungi and host plants.
In summary, this study reveals a new strategy the ﬁrst time for fungi
to hijack the phosphate sensing pathway by hydrolyzing plant signaling
molecules to promote infection. This discovery not only enriches our
understanding of plant-pathogen interactions, but may also provide a
theoretical basis for the development of new control strategies. The
newly discovered mechanism suggests that Nudix effectors can be used as
molecular targets for disease-resistant breeding, which will help to
develop new fungicide development strategies and develop broad-
spectrum disease-resistant crops. In addition, in the future, researchers
can consider combining the cross-regulation of phosphate signaling and
immune responses (such as the jasmonic acid pathway) to design
bifunctional compounds that simultaneously inhibit pathogen effectors
and activate host defenses, so as to more speci ﬁcally prevent and control
plant pathogenic fungi.
