These quantitative results validate the effectiveness of our
dual-protocol, AI-enhanced teleoperation architecture. The
system not only addresses the key limitations of conventional
telepresence (such as lack of physical interaction and high
latency), but also provides a practical and scalable solution
for real-world scenarios, ranging from international STEM
education to collaborative industrial assembly.
In summary, our experimental evaluation demonstrates that
the proposed framework achieves:
•Actuator latencies as low as 0.2 seconds locally and
under 0.7 seconds across international VPNs,
•Sub-second (0.5–1.2s) end-to-end video latency including
real-time AI overlays,
•94.6% remote grasping success rate across varied object
classes,
•Superior protocol stability and lower jitter with MQTT
compared to WebSocket,
•Seamless, cross-platform user experience supporting dis-
tributed, hands-on tasks.
These results underscore the system’s promise for next-
generation, AI-powered remote collaboration and smart city
robotics.

