5 Design Targets and Expected Performance
5.1 Expected Data Quality and Completeness
The standard-budget scenario targets the following performance characteristics based on component
specifications and design analysis:
•Data completeness: 90–95% over six-month campaigns (accounting for monthly maintenance
downtime, occasional power outages with battery backup, and sensor cleaning intervals).
•System uptime:>95% with battery backup surviving typical 4–6 hour power outages.
•Sustained sampling rate: 1 Hz continuous, expandable to 2 Hz for high-turbulence sites.
•SD corruption mitigation: double-buffered writes with atomic flush and integrity verification
designed to prevent data loss.
•Coastal survival: IP67 enclosure, conformal coating, and corrosion-resistant hardware de-
signed for>6 month deployment without component failures.
Table 3 summarizes design targets for the standard-budget scenario.
5.2 Quality Assurance and Verification Strategy
The methodology specifies explicit verification activities:
11

•Bench testing:72-hour continuous acquisition run with simulated sensor inputs, verifying
data integrity, power-cycle recovery (50 iterations), and thermal stability in enclosed condi-
tions.
•Sensor calibration:Two-point anemometer validation [4], power sensor multi-point verifi-
cation, environmental sensor reference-point checks with documented calibration certificates.
•Field shakedown:One-week commissioning period verifying sensor installation, checking
initial data completeness (>95% target), and adjusting sampling parameters if needed.
•Quality filters:Post-processing scripts applying range checks (wind speed<0 or>25 m/s,
rotor RPM<0 or>500), duplicate timestamp detection, and sensor fault flag generation.
Expected data retention after quality filtering: 92–95% of raw samples pass validity checks
under normal operating conditions.
5.3 Reproducibility Resources
The methodology is accompanied by open-source resources:
•GitHub repository:Tested ESP32 firmware (Arduino/PlatformIO), calibration spread-
sheet templates, data-quality Python scripts, hardware wiring diagrams (Fritzing/KiCad),
and troubleshooting flowcharts (https://github.com/asithakal/wind-turbine-daq-gui
de).
•Zenodo archival release (v1.1):Tagged firmware and template release with persistent DOI
(https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.18093662), enabling version-controlled reproducibility.
•Documentation artifacts:Requirements templates, BOM templates with sourcing guid-
ance, dataset README templates conforming to FAIR metadata standards [12], and com-
plete calibration workflow procedures.
6 Discussion
6.1 Transferability and Adaptation
The methodology is designed for transferability across small-wind configurations (HAWT/VAWT)
with explicit guidance on swept-area definitions [14,15], calibration requirements, and uncertainty
propagation appropriate to different turbine architectures. The V-model approach with require-
ments traceability [5] remains applicable regardless of specific sensor choices, while the local-first
storage pattern and FAIR publication workflow [12] are sensor-agnostic. The constrained-budget
scenario demonstrates that systematic engineering practices combined with rigorous calibration
can expand accessibility for LMIC institutions where typical commercial DAQ solutions are cost-
prohibitive.
6.2 Implementation Considerations
Successful implementation requires several preconditions:
•Institutional support:Laboratory facilities for bench testing, access to calibration equip-
ment (wind tunnel or reference instruments), and technical staff consultation.
12

•Site preparation:Turbine installation with appropriate tower/mounting, electrical power
availability (with backup recommended), and network connectivity (Wi-Fi or cellular) if cloud
telemetry is desired.
•Maintenance commitment:Realistic assessment of maintenance cadence achievable by
the research team; the design targets assume monthly visits and quarterly calibration checks.
•Timeline alignment:The methodology is structured for 6–12 month academic project
cycles; shorter timelines may require scope reduction.
Field completeness and quality outcomes will vary with site-specific power stability, environmen-
tal extremes (e.g., monsoon intensity, lightning exposure), and the maintenance discipline main-
tained by the team.
6.3 Limitations and Out-of-Scope Items
This work does not address industrial SCADA integration, utility-scale wind farm monitoring, or
commercial certification processes. It excludes grid interconnection and power quality analysis
(IEC 61400-21 scope [20]), advanced CFD development, structural dynamics and FEA, and de-
tailed aerodynamic blade optimization. The focus remains on instrumentation methodology, data
integrity design, and publishable dataset preparation workflows for academic research contexts.
6.4 Future Validation Work
While the design scenario presented here is comprehensive and grounded in component specifica-
tions, field deployment validation remains necessary to confirm expected performance character-
istics under real tropical/coastal conditions. Future work should report measured completeness,
uptime, and data-quality outcomes from actual deployments, quantify failure modes and main-
tenance burden, and refine calibration intervals based on observed sensor drift rates. Addition-
ally, expanding the methodology to multi-turbine installations and grid-connected scenarios would
broaden applicability.
7 Conclusion
This paper presents a systematic, reproducible DAQ-to-dataset methodology for small wind tur-
bine field research integrating a tailored V-model with requirements traceability [5], a robust ESP32
firmware reference architecture, a local-first hybrid logging design pattern, and a CRISP-DM [10] +
FAIR [12]-aligned data governance workflow. The comprehensive helical VAWT design scenario for
coastal Sri Lanka demonstrates the methodology’s application with detailed component selection
rationale, calibration procedures, and budget-constrained alternatives suitable for LMIC contexts.
The companion open-source resources, firmware, templates, and workflow artifacts, are positioned
to reduce implementation barriers and raise baseline reproducibility for small-wind studies in tropi-
cal/coastal environments and resource-constrained academic settings. Field validation of the design
targets remains an important direction for future work.
