II. SYSTEMDESIGN ANDPROTOTYPING
NANOEDGEGUARDfollows an observe-decide-enforce
loop at the gateway (Fig. 1). The observe stage collects per-
device traffic intensity from kernel counters and a lightweight
service-quality signal from the broker. The decision stage
selects a rate-limit tier using a simple risk score and hysteresis.
Hysteresis includes persistence and a hold-down period so that
escalation and recovery are explicit and stable under intermit-
tent stress. The enforce stage applies the selected tier close to
the packet path using TC/eBPF and pinned policy maps. The
pinned state keeps enforcement stable across process restarts
while allowing user-space logic to remain auditable.
The controller selects among four actions: MONITOR,
RATE-LOW, RATE-MID, and RATE-HIGH. Hard drop is re-
arXiv:2607.27858v1  [cs.CR]  30 Jul 2026

served for extreme or non-session-critical flows. For TCP-
based MQTT telemetry, sustained drops can trigger timeouts
and reconnect loops, which confounds fair quality-of-service
evaluation. We therefore use tiered rate control as the default
policy in this work. Auditability is a design goal. Each control
interval emits a structured JSONL record including observed
signals, selected tier, applied tier, and a transition reason.
These logs make policy evolution and recovery verifiable
across repeated runs.
III. EXPERIMENTALSETUP
Testbed.A Raspberry Pi gateway runs a Mosquitto broker
and the NANOEDGEGUARDcontroller. Two ESP32 endpoints
publish vitals and alarms as listed in Table I.
Stress model.We model a misbehaving (or misconfigured)
endpoint as an MQTT publisher that increases its publish
intensity in short ON/OFF pulses during a fixed window (UE-
B, 60–120 s). This creates bursty contention at the gateway and
broker while using the same messaging stack as legitimate
traffic. The goal is to contain these pulses quickly while
preserving timely alarm delivery from UE-A.
Cases.We compare NO ENF(no enforcement), U NFT
(user-space firewall baseline), and K ADAPT(kernel-plane
adaptive tiers). Each case is repeated three times.
Metrics.We measure alarm acknowledgment delay using
MQTT QoS1 PUBACK round-trip time and report p95/p99 as
median-of-3. We measure burst containment as flood-window
excess admitted bytes (60–120 s) computed relative to the
benign baseline estimated from 0–60 s, also as median-of-3.
Adaptivity is summarized via tier transitions from logs.
