Abstract
Cloud-mediated IoT architectures fragment authentication across
vendor silos and create latency and availability bottlenecks for
cross-vendor device-to-device (D2D) interactions. We present At-
las, a framework that extends the Web public-key infrastructure
to IoT by issuing X.509 certificates to devices via vendor-operated
ACME clients and vendor-controlled DNS namespaces. Devices ob-
tain globally verifiable identities without hardware changes and
establish mutual TLS channels directly across administrative do-
mains, decoupling runtime authentication from cloud reachability.
We prototype Atlas on ESP32 and Raspberry Pi, integrate it with
an MQTT-based IoT stack and a Atlas-aware cloud, and evaluate it
in smart-home and smart-city workloads. Certificate provisioning
completes in under 6 s per device, mTLS adds only about 17 ms of la-
tency and modest CPU overhead, and Atlas-based applications sus-
tain low, predictable latency compared to cloud-mediated baselines.
Because many major vendors already rely on ACME-compatible
CAs for their web services, Atlas is immediately deployable with
minimal infrastructure changes.
