5.3 Parametric Primitives
To bridge the gap between artistic intent and control constraints,
the system relies on parametric primitives. Instead of manually
positioning individual agents on a geometric shape, users define
the meta-state of a formation.
For example, a circle primitive is not defined by 24 coordinate
sets but by its global position (𝑥,𝑦 ), rotation (𝜃), scale, and phase
offset. The global position corresponds to the center of each
primitive’s bounding box. The phase offset shifts all crafts along
the parameterized vector graphic path that defines the shape. By
choosing this approach, where primitives are vector graphics
that are normalized based on their bounding boxes, it allows for
quick changes to shapes at a later stage, while the crafts retain
their approximate location within the choreography.
5.4 Transitions
Transitions are performed between the primitive shapes. The
choreographer can manually match start and end positions or
use the LSAP solver.
The LSAP solver uses squared Euclidean distance as weights.
For this approach, it has been shown that the minimum distance
between trajectories is guaranteed to be𝑑𝑚𝑖𝑛 =
