Introduction
The integration of the IoT, including Industrial Internet of Things (IIoT), and 5G
networks, is transforming digital healthcare through high-speed, real-time com-
munication and reliable connectivity [1]. This convergence integrates wearable
sensors (e.g., body temperature, heart rate, and oxygen saturation) with edge
devices, servers, and cloud platforms, ensuring seamless data exchange and effi-
cient clinical management in 5G-enabled H-IoT environments. The key advantage
of this integration is its ability to support timely decision-making, reinforce pre-
cision healthcare, and extend specialist care to patients in remote or underserved
areas.
However, the accelerated expansion of 5G-enabled H-IoT systems also
introduces significant challenges related to security, privacy, and reliability [2].
The heterogeneous nature of connected H-IoT devices, resource limitations,
and continuous data transmission increase exposure to malicious activities.
Specifically, emerging cyber threats such as DDoS, Selective Forwarding (SF),
and Man-in-the-Middle (MITM) attacks pose critical vulnerabilities and risks to
the integrity and availability of precision healthcare data [3]. H-IoT systems also
operate under low-latency and power-efficiency requirements, which constrain
the deployment of complex deep learning models such as Long Short-Term
Memory (LSTM) [4], Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (Bi-LSTM) [5],
and hybrid CNN–LSTM [6]. Particularly, landmark anomaly detection meth-
ods (e.g., LSTM), despite significant advancements, remain unsuitable for
resource-constrained H-IoT devices due to their high computational overhead [4],
1

1. INTRODUCTION
[7].
Privacy and regulatory requirements (e.g., General Data Protection Regula-
tion (GDPR)) further restrict access to real-world data, creating challenges in
obtaining sufficient labelled samples for ML/Deep Learning (DL) model train-
ing. In parallel, publicly available intrusion-detection datasets are typically re-
leased in aggregated traffic form, which limits the preservation of packet-level
temporal characteristics such as inter-arrival timing and transmission rate varia-
tions [8]. Consequently, traffic dynamics caused by malicious activities are often
underrepresented in such datasets [9]. These challenges highlight the need for re-
alistic attack simulations, synthetic datasets, lightweight learning architectures,
and practical deployment strategies to enable reliable cyber threat detection and
mitigation in H-IoT environments.
This thesis encompasses multiple aspects of H-IoT research, spanning net-
work topology design (e.g., star), simulation, security, and privacy [10]. It also
includes realistic experimentation with application and transport-layer protocols
such as Message Queuing Telemetry Transport (MQTT), User Datagram Proto-
col (UDP), Transmission Control Protocol (TCP), and Constrained Application
Protocol (CoAP) under both normal and malicious conditions. Among these pro-
tocols, MQTT plays a crucial role in IoT communications due to its lightweight
publish–subscribe architecture and minimal bandwidth requirements. It enables
reliable message exchange between multiple IoT devices and a central broker
with minimal protocol overhead, such as a small header size and limited hand-
shake or acknowledgement messages. Its efficiency in maintaining low latency
and reliability, even in constrained networks, makes it well-suited for modelling
the interaction patterns of H-IoT devices. Hence, this research reflects the struc-
tural composition of H-IoT, consisting of wearable and environmental sensors
within the Wireless Body Area Network (WBAN) and Wireless Sensor Network
(WSN) domains. These include physiological and ecological parameters such as
body temperature, heart rate, room temperature, and humidity. In such inter-
connected systems, both WBAN and WSN nodes are vulnerable to adversarial
manipulation, where malicious entities may alter sensor data or disrupt commu-
nication, affecting the reliability of healthcare monitoring [11], [12]. The overall
architecture facilitates seamless communication across edge, fog, and cloud infras-
tructures. However, during the simulation of physiological metrics (e.g., simulated
2

heart rate of 80 bpm), GDPR-related procedures are excluded, as the artificially
generated data do not contain any subject-specific or patient information.
