Raspberry Pi 4. The resulting performance, measured in terms of latency and
power consumption, validates the feasibility of lightweight intelligent deployment
in H-IoT environments. In the final stage, the research focuses on multiclass clas-
sification of multiple attack types to provide a comprehensive evaluation of the
proposed approach.
1.1 Research Gaps
Cyber-threat detection has been addressed using both traditional machine learn-
ing and deep learning approaches in H-IoT environments. Traditional methods,
such as DTs [13] and K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) [14], offer low computational
cost; however, they have limited capacity to capture time-dependent attack pat-
terns. This limitation is evident in multiclass cyber threat detection scenarios,
where rule-based DTs can misclassify malicious samples as normal, as demon-
strated in Chapter 6, Section 6.6.1. In contrast, deep learning models (e.g.,
LSTMs) improve detection performance by capturing complex patterns; however,
they often introduce higher computational overhead and increased inference la-
tency [4], [7]. As a result, prior approaches, including traditional machine learning
and deep learning methods, do not consistently balance detection performance,
inference latency, and model size for edge deployment in resource-constrained
H-IoT environments. Beyond model-level limitations, existing datasets often fail
to capture transmission-frequency characteristics. This limits the representation
of realistic traffic dynamics, resulting in degraded model performance, increased
misclassification, and reduced generalisability. Therefore, a research gap exists
in the design of cyber threat detection approaches that simultaneously achieve
high accuracy, low latency, and resource efficiency for both binary and multiclass
classification under realistic traffic dynamics in H-IoT environments.
4

1.2 Aims and Objectives
1.2 Aims and Objectives
This research aims to address the challenges of detecting and mitigating cyber
threats using deep learning-based approaches within H-IoT environments. Deep
learning is employed to learn complex, non-linear, and time-dependent cyber-
attack patterns in network traffic. The study further considers the trade-off be-
tween detection performance, inference latency, and model size for edge deploy-
ment in resource-constrained H-IoT environments. To enable this, it analyses the
data transmission behaviour of normal and malicious devices under realistic net-
work conditions in H-IoT. Accordingly, it utilises emulated H-IoT sensor data and
simulated network traffic to capture device-to-device interactions within health-
care settings. In this context, a systematic simulation is adopted to generate
H-IoT sensor data with defined transmission patterns that differentiate normal
and attack-related behaviour for cyber threat analysis. The generated data are
employed to train and evaluate the proposed detection and mitigation mechanism
for cyber threats, including Distributed Denial-of-Service, Selective Forwarding,
and Man-in-the-Middle attacks in H-IoT environments. In addition, this research
investigates the design of lightweight deep learning architectures that maintain
detection performance while remaining suitable for resource-constrained H-IoT
devices. The overarching research question is stated as follows:
Main RQ: How can differences in data transmission behaviour be-
tween normal and malicious nodes support the development of realistic
datasets and lightweight deep learning models for detecting and miti-
gating emerging cyber threats in H-IoT environments?
To address DDoS attack detection and mitigation, this research proposes
a deep learning method, TCN, that identifies DDoS-affected nodes and subse-
quently blocks the detected malicious nodes. Following this, the study extends
toward real-world deployment to evaluate the feasibility of executing such deep
learning models on resource-constrained devices. Accordingly, this research in-
vestigates the deployment of TCN to assess latency and power efficiency on edge
devices, such as the Raspberry Pi 4, in H-IoT environments. Building on edge
deployment, the research further examines the detection of multiple cyber attacks
within a unified H-IoT topology. In real-world scenarios, multiple threats may
coincide; therefore, multiclass classification is a crucial step in anomaly detection.
5

1. INTRODUCTION
In line with the final research question, this dissertation proposes a deep learning
model, Res-TCN, to classify emerging cyber threats such as Selective Forwarding,
Man-in-the-Middle, and Distributed Denial-of-Services in H-IoT.
The aims and objectives of this dissertation are outlined through the following
Research Questions:
• RQ1: What key challenges and research gaps are identified in existing
literature on ML/DL-based cyber attack detection in H-IoT systems, and
how can a conceptual deep learning framework address these limitations?
• RQ2: How can realistic synthetic datasets be generated to represent DDoS
attack behaviours in H-IoT environments?
• RQ3: Are deep learning techniques (e.g., TCN) capable of detecting and
mitigating evolving cyber threats (e.g., DDoS) across H-IoT systems?
• RQ4: What approaches enable the edge deployment of deep learning mod-
els (e.g., TCN) on resource-constrained devices (e.g., Raspberry Pi 4) in
H-IoT?
• RQ5: How can deep learning models such as Res-TCN be utilised for
multiclass classification to distinguish among multiple attack categories, in-
cluding Selective Forwarding, Man-in-the-Middle, and Distributed Denial-
of-Service attacks, in H-IoT environments?
1.3 Dataset and Model Justification
This research constructs three synthetic datasets that incorporate both network
traffic behaviour and physiological metrics, representing normal and malicious H-
IoT nodes. Synthetic datasets are generated due to the limited availability of pub-
licly accessible datasets that capture realistic H-IoT transmission behaviour and
temporal characteristics. This research includes a detailed analysis of per-node
communication behaviour, including the number of messages transmitted per
node, total message volume, and the transmission patterns of emulated sensors
(e.g., body temperature) to the server, aligned with practical H-IoT operations.
This analysis identifies consistent differences between normal and malicious nodes
6

1.4 Research Contribution
in transmission intervals and packet-generation frequency. These patterns are not
captured at the node-level temporal granularity in commonly used datasets (e.g.,
CIC-IDS2017) [15], [16]. This facilitates the evaluation of detection models based
on transmission dynamics rather than aggregated traffic patterns.
The selection of TCN is motivated by the need to capture temporal variations
in node-level transmission behaviour while maintaining efficient inference for edge
deployment. In this research, attack detection relies on identifying changes in
transmission intervals and packet-generation frequency across nodes. This in-
volves capturing temporal dependencies without introducing significant compu-
tational overhead. Unlike recurrent architectures such as LSTM, which process
sequences sequentially, TCN enables parallel processing, reducing inference la-
tency. It captures temporal dependencies using dilated causal convolutions, which
avoids the limitations of standard CNN architectures in preserving temporal or-
der [17]. Therefore, TCN achieves a trade-off between detection performance
and computational efficiency for edge deployment in resource-constrained H-IoT
environments.
To support multiclass detection, the Res-TCN architecture is further justi-
fied by its ability to capture complex patterns from combined physiological and
network features across multiple attack types, including DDoS, SF, and MITM.
Residual connections facilitate stable training by improving gradient propagation.
1.4 Research Contribution
This thesis primarily advances the field of cybersecurity, with a particular focus on
H-IoT systems and emerging cyber threat detection. It addresses how variations
in data transmission behaviour between normal and malicious nodes support the
development of realistic datasets, lightweight models, and edge-intelligent defence
mechanisms in H-IoT. The key contributions are as follows:
1. A review of ML/DL-based cybersecurity mechanisms in H-IoT
– This review systematically analyses existing machine learning and deep
learning techniques applied to H-IoT security. It identifies key challenges,
limitations, and open issues that underpin the research question and sup-
port a conceptual framework. It also highlights the lack of realistic datasets,
7

1. INTRODUCTION
privacy constraints, and insufficient modelling of device-to-device interac-
tions, which hinder the development of effective security solutions. Based
on these insights, a conceptual convolutional neural network-based deep
learning framework is introduced to address the identified gaps in secur-
ing H-IoT systems. This conceptual framework motivates the subsequent
experimental investigations presented in this thesis.
This contribution is presented in Chapter 2, corresponds to RQ1, and is
published in the following article: Khatun, M.A. , Memon, S.F., Eising,
C. and Dhirani, L.L., 2023. Machine learning for healthcare-iot security: A
review and risk mitigation. IEEE Access, 11, pp. 145869-145896.
2. Creation of realistic DDoS datasets for H-IoT environments – To
overcome the dataset limitations identified in the review, two realistic DDoS
attack datasets are developed that replicate the traffic behaviour of H-IoT
sensors under both normal and malicious conditions. The first dataset,
generated using the Cooja simulator over MQTT, includes three types of
wearable sensors: body temperature, oxygen saturation, and heart rate,
under both normal and malicious conditions. The second dataset, developed
using the ns-3 simulator over the UDP protocol, captures both benign and
malicious traffic in an H-IoT environment. The data are preprocessed and
labelled using a systematic pipeline to refine and organise the extracted
H-IoT communication features across both datasets.
This contribution is presented in Chapter 3, corresponds to RQ2, and is
published in the following article: Akhi, M., Eising, C. and Dhirani, L.L.,
2025. Datasets for distributed denial-of-service detection in healthcare in-
ternet of things environments. Data in Brief , p. 112222.
3. Development of a TCN-based DDoS detection and mitigation
model for H-IoT – Using the previously developed datasets, this research
trains and evaluates a TCN model to detect and mitigate DDoS attacks
in H-IoT environments. The model uses monitoring frequency to identify
DDoS-affected nodes and employs a dynamic threshold mechanism to block
8

1.4 Research Contribution
detected malicious nodes. This approach reliably distinguishes between nor-
mal and attack traffic, achieving high detection and mitigation performance
across both MQTT and UDP-based datasets.
This contribution is presented in Chapter 4, corresponds to RQ3, and is
published in the following article: Akhi, M., Eising, C. and Dhirani, L.L.,
2025. TCN-Based DDoS Detection and Mitigation in 5G Healthcare-IoT:
A Frequency Monitoring and Dynamic Threshold Approach. IEEE Access,
13, pp. 12709–12733.
4. Edge deployment of the TCN model on resource-constrained de-
vices – Extending the previous research, this study investigates the de-
ployment of the trained TCN model on a Raspberry Pi 4 to evaluate its
feasibility in real-time H-IoT applications. Using the previously developed
datasets, the model’s inference latency and power consumption are anal-
ysed to assess its applicability for low-power healthcare devices. The find-
ings demonstrate that the TCN model operates efficiently on the Raspberry
Pi 4, achieving low latency and reduced power consumption within H-IoT
constraints.
This contribution is presented in Chapter 5, corresponds to RQ4, and is
published in the following article: Akhi, M., Eising, C. and Dhirani, L.L.,
2026. Securing IoT using Lightweight TCN for Edge Deployment on Rasp-
berry Pi 4 IEEE Open Journal of the Communications Society , 7, pp. 442-
460.
5. Multiclass attack detection using Res-TCN in H-IoT environ-
ments – Building on the earlier deployment research, this study focuses
on distinguishing multiple cyber threats within a unified H-IoT topology.
To achieve this, the multiclass dataset is developed to represent diverse
attack categories, including SF, MITM, and DDoS. A Res-TCN model is
then employed to perform multiclass classification, accurately identifying
each attack type based on its distinct communication characteristics. The
results demonstrate the model’s strong generalisation across different attack
categories while maintaining low inference latency suitable for real-time H-
IoT applications.
