2.6 Multi-layered Analysis of Routing Attacks
in H-IoT
This section focuses on routing attacks in the H-IoT domain, reviewing key com-
ponents sequentially. The first step is to discuss the vital process of collecting
health-related data in the layer for medical data collection. After collecting raw
medical data, the medical application layer transforms it into meaningful health-
care services. Next, it discusses the role and vulnerabilities of the routing and
network layers in the context of routing attacks. The next step is emphasising
that the medical application layer is crucial to translating data into actionable
insights. In addition, it describes the role of the COOJA simulator in collecting
data and generating datasets in the H-IoT environment.
44

2.6 Multi-layered Analysis of Routing Attacks in H-IoT
2.6.1 Medical Data Collection Layer
Healthcare data is collected by smart healthcare sensors, such as devices for
tracking brain activity and blood pressure. A doctor can track the progress of an
elderly patient by using IoT devices that record every detail and activity. Staff
members and nurses who care for elderly patients receive information. H-IoT
integrates healthcare devices at home for disabilities. Therefore, the doctor may
follow and monitor patients’ cases at times. Despite this, the data generated and
collected are susceptible to various threats. As a result of the attacks, hackers can
gain complete command and control (C&C), disrupting the devices [1]. These
attacks can drain sensors’ power, causing them to fail or disrupt the entire IoT
network in the healthcare industry. Medical data collection includes collecting,
analysing, and monitoring data, as shown in Figure 2.11.
Figure 2.11: Data collection layers in healthcare.
45

2. MACHINE LEARNING FOR HEALTHCARE-IOT SECURITY:
A REVIEW AND RISK MITIGATION
Edge computing and federated learning are also important components of
systems for distributed processing and privacy-preserving data analysis in modern
H-IoT, although they are not illustrated in Figure 2.11.
As a starting point, several types of devices are used for collecting data within
the medical data collection layer. These devices include sensors, cell phones,
wearable devices, and cameras, among other things. The second phase involves
data analysis, which analyses every event and activity of an elderly patient. A
secure server then processes and stores the data. Monitoring healthcare data
remotely and automatically recording it includes a patient’s medical history and
status [216].
2.6.2 Medical Application Layer
The traditional application layer in IoT environments handles data processing,
storage, and user interfaces. On the other hand, the medical application layer
is uniquely designed for handling healthcare-related applications, data, and ser-
vices, ensuring it follows the highest regulations following the EU GDPR or Health
Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA) [103],[217] while process-
ing United States healthcare data. This layer presents unique challenges and
complexities. For example, Electronic Health Records (EHRs) for patient data,
remote consultation platforms, real-time monitoring systems, medical resources,
medical care, and diagnosis personnel. Moreover, the medical application layer
processes, organises, and maintains medical records. This layer ensures that
patients’ data is authentic, secure, private, and reliable when transmitted over
the communication system [218]. Numerous application layer protocols exist,
including MQTT, Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP), WebSockets, RESTful,
Secure-MQTT, and CoAP [219]. Choosing the appropriate protocol for H-IoT
depends on its application [220].
2.6.3 Routing Attack and Network Layer
The network layer defines paths for the routing of data over the network. It has
been demonstrated that low-power wireless networks with multiple hops are sus-
ceptible to a wide range of attacks, with routing attacks emerging as one of the
46

2.6 Multi-layered Analysis of Routing Attacks in H-IoT
most significant threats in the H-IoT environment [221]. There are many routing
attacks, including selective forwarding attacks and replay attacks [222]. In the
selective forwarding attack, control packets are deliberately forwarded within the
RPL while data packets are dropped. In conjunction with sinkhole attacks, this
strategy can disrupt established routing paths and lead to severe consequences for
the network [223]. A replay attack entails capturing and re-transmitting packets
captured from nearby nodes by an unauthorised node or attacker [224]. The pur-
pose of these attacks is to manipulate or obstruct the transmission of data packets
to impair the integrity of the network [225]. Furthermore, rank and wormhole
attacks are susceptible to IoT routing protocols due to their lightweight nature
and limited computational resources. Using these attacks, IoT infrastructures
can be devastated by attacks targeting control messages and resources [226].
Routing and network layers receive data sent by medical data collection lay-
ers. These layers use protocols such as Wi-Fi 6, NB-IoT, Bluetooth, IPv6 Over
Low-Power Wireless Personal Area Networks (6LoWPAN), RPL, WiMAX, Zig-
Bee, Sigfox, LoRa, and 5G New Radio (NR) to transmit data to the medical
application layer [227],[228],[229]. This setup requires the analysis of routing at-
tacks, potentially compromising the confidentiality and accessibility of medical
data. To ensure reliable and secure data transmission, protocol configurations
designed for power-efficient and unstable networks should be carefully reviewed
for vulnerability to various routing attacks [230]. In this context, ML techniques
are explored for detecting cyber attacks in H-IoT environments.
As highlighted by [231], H-IoT networks are vulnerable to routing attacks,
such as sinkholes and wormholes. The study proposes multiple detection ap-
proaches for identifying such attacks. Due to the limited resources of H-IoT
devices, traditional intrusion detection systems are often impractical. The pro-
posed method efficiently detects and handles network attacks using ML and DL
while considering device limitations.
According to [232], especially in e-health applications, protecting sensitive pa-
tient data from routing attacks and security threats is pivotal in 5G-IoT. Cloud-
based e-health data is at risk of various cyber-threats (i.e., ransomware, loss of
privacy, and digital identity fraud) and attacks mentioned in Section 2.3. CNN-
DMA is a DL model that detects malware attacks using a CNN. The e-health
