2.9.3 Software-Defined Network in H-IoT
An emerging paradigm in computer networking is software-defined networking.
Data planes, control planes, and application planes are the three tiers of SDN.
Data and control planes are separated to improve network performance. In con-
trast, traditional architectures combine data and control planes onto one device,
while SDN separates them to simplify network management [289], [290]. SDNs
forward packets according to predefined policies [291].
Although this chapter focuses on anomaly detection, SDN-based architectures
are included to indicate their potential role in enabling automated mitigation in
H-IoT environments. In this context, such architectures consist of sensor con-
trollers, cloud controllers, network controllers, and closed-loop controllers, which
provide four types of services: data acquisition, network configuration and trans-
mission, processing, and actionable feedback, as illustrated in Figure 2.17.
As an example, Google uses an SDN to manage its wide-area network traffic
[292]. The network infrastructure increasingly relies on SDNs, and protecting a
network from attack is crucial to ensure its integrity and availability [293]. In
[294], a novel software-defined network architecture is presented, incorporating a
new controller and load-balancing algorithms based on ML for the H-IoT. A sup-
port vector machine algorithm, which achieved 95.1% accuracy through extensive
simulations, validates the architecture’s effectiveness. In preparation for broader
5G integration, this approach represents an important advance in H-IoT. Adding
DL to SDNs makes H-IoT services, such as telemedicine, more reliable and secure
[295]. A software-defined network system illustrates the communication process
between medical services and nano-networks inside individuals’ bodies [296]. A
certain set of software rules controls this method, which makes healthcare more
efficient.
2.10 Review and Limitations of Cyber Threat
Detection for H-IoT
This research reviews studies that employ dataset-driven intrusion detection ap-
proaches in H-IoT environments, with an emphasis on DDoS attacks, given their
64

2.11 Conclusion
impact and prevalence. Datasets such as CICIoT2023 [297] and healthcare-
specific datasets such as CICIoMT2024 [298] are commonly used in existing
studies, with a primary focus on DDoS attack scenarios. These studies integrate
deep learning-based detection mechanisms to improve classification performance
[212], [299]. However, existing research predominantly concentrates on DDoS
attacks [212], [299], with limited consideration of other threats such as MITM
and SF attacks. In addition, existing studies rely on benchmark datasets such
as CIC-IDS2017 [300] and BoT-IoT [301], which do not adequately represent
healthcare-specific attack behaviours, such as MITM and SF, thereby limiting
their applicability to H-IoT environments [302]. This limitation indicates the
need for datasets and detection methods capable of classifying multiple attack
types in a multiclass setting.
2.11 Conclusion
This chapter systematically dives into digital healthcare’s fundamental elements
and practical applications. It discusses the essential technologies and method-
ologies for optimising these systems within Healthcare 5.0. The research focuses
on various cyber threats, vulnerabilities, and cyber attacks carried out at the
different IoT layers and explores the security and privacy of H-IoT through deep
neural networks and AI. A demonstration of a cloud-based solution and big data
analytics using IoT and software-defined networks is provided and illustrates the
advantages of a precise, efficient, and secure healthcare ecosystem. This thesis
also presents insights into the security challenges related to wireless and commu-
nication technologies (i.e., 5G) in H-IoT and how ML algorithms can enable the
digital healthcare environment to bridge those security gaps.
