3.4 Data Description
3.4.5 Comparison with Existing Datasets
DDoS attacks pose a significant threat within the cybersecurity landscape,
prompting researchers to develop datasets that capture both normal and attack
behaviours. Over the past ten years, several publicly available datasets have been
used to evaluate DDoS detection techniques with advanced ML/DL models in
IoT and H-IoT environments. These include broader network intrusion detection
scenarios such as BoT-IoT, TON IoT, and CICIoT2023 [304]. Table 3.5 provides
a comparative overview of datasets, including CIC-IDS2017 [16], BoT-IoT [305],
and WSN-DDoSAttack-H-IoT2023 [306], which are frequently used in ML/DL-
based DDoS detection research. It also presents the detection accuracy achieved
using the TCN model on the proposed UL-ECE-MQTT-DDoS-H-IoT2025 and
UL-ECE-UDP-DDoS-H-IoT2025 datasets. The comparison highlights key
aspects, including IoT/H-IoT focus, protocol, attack type, class type, model
type, and accuracy.
While CIC-IDS2017 [300] offers general DDoS coverage, it is primarily de-
signed for intrusion detection based on fundamental network traffic attributes
such as timestamps, source and destination IPs, protocols, and attack types.
However, it lacks a specific focus on IoT-based scenarios. The dataset is built
primarily around protocols like HTTP and HTTPS and includes email-related
traffic alongside typical enterprise activities such as web browsing, FTP, and
SSH. As a result, it may not adequately capture the complexity of modern IoT
and H-IoT systems or reflect the evolving nature of contemporary DDoS attacks
targeting such systems.
The BoT-IoT dataset is developed in a simulated IoT environment at the Cy-
ber Range Lab of UNSW Canberra [301]. It contains both benign and botnet
traffic, including DoS and DDoS attacks. While it offers a broad range of attack
types relevant to IoT, the dataset is fully synthetic and known to be imbal-
anced [307], with certain classes having very few samples. This imbalance poses
challenges for training reliable ML models. Moreover, the dataset lacks detailed
protocol-level features, which limits its usefulness for analysing protocol-specific
DDoS behaviours in resource-constrained IoT deployments.
The WSN DDoS Attack H-IoT2023 [308] dataset is developed using WSN,
focusing on environmental parameters such as humidity and room temperature
81

3. DATASETS FOR DISTRIBUTED DENIAL-OF-SERVICE
