Preamble
In this chapter, RQ3 is addressed:
RQ3: Are DL techniques (e.g., TCN) capable of detecting and mitigating
evolving cyber threats (e.g., DDoS) across H-IoT systems?
The preceding chapter addresses RQ2 by detailing the simulation design and
the generation of realistic synthetic datasets that represent DDoS attack be-
haviours in H-IoT environments. It explains how protocol-specific communica-
tion patterns are modelled and used to construct the UL-ECE-MQTT-DDoS-H-
IoT2025 and UL-ECE-UDP-DDoS-H-IoT2025 datasets. Building on this foun-
dation, the current chapter addresses RQ3 by applying a TCN model to detect
and mitigate DDoS attacks using these datasets. The chapter, therefore, presents
the detection and mitigation performance of the TCN model against MQTT and
UDP-protocol–specific DDoS attacks in H-IoT.
This chapter contributes to the main research question by focusing on the
detection and mitigation of DDoS attacks using a TCN-based DL model in H-IoT
environments. It examines how the TCN model detects DDoS-affected nodes and
enforces mitigation by isolating them from subsequent network communication.
The main objective of this study is to employ a TCN model to detect and
mitigate DDoS attacks within H-IoT environments. The model is trained and
evaluated on the datasets UL-ECE-MQTT-DDoS-H-IoT2025 and UL-ECE-
UDP-DDoS-H-IoT2025, introduced in Chapter 3. These datasets encompass
diverse communication parameters, including total message count, transmission
frequency, and monitoring frequency, across both MQTT and UDP protocols,
providing a robust foundation for cross-protocol analysis.
In the UL-ECE-MQTT-DDoS-H-IoT2025 dataset, the TCN model utilises
four extracted communication features, among which message frequency plays a
crucial role in identifying abnormal node behaviour. A node is considered poten-
tially malicious or DDoS-compromised if its message transmission frequency ex-
ceeds a threshold (e.g., frequency > 5), indicating unusually high traffic intensity
compared to normal nodes. In contrast, the TCN method in the UL-ECE-UDP-
DDoS-H-IoT2025 dataset employs a monitoring frequency mechanism that calcu-
lates the percentage of the total message count for each H-IoT node over the past
90

4.1 Abstract
five seconds, enabling the identification of malicious nodes. This five-second mon-
itoring interval also allows the model to detect additional DDoS-affected nodes
that are introduced every 10 seconds, beginning 20 seconds into the 120-second
simulation on ns-3, as discussed in Section 4.5.1.1. It also incorporates a dynamic
threshold-based mitigation strategy that automatically blacklists malicious nodes
in real-time, while minimising false positives.
This research, in line with RQ3, investigates how the TCN model leverages
critical features to detect and mitigate DDoS attacks in H-IoT environments. The
detection and mitigation results provide valuable insight into how the TCN model
effectively captures temporal dependencies in H-IoT traffic, thereby enabling ef-
ficient performance over the MQTT and UDP-based H-IoT datasets. Temporal
dependencies refer to the sequential correlations between communication patterns
over time. Moreover, this model outperforms traditional architectures (e.g., CNN,
Bi-LSTM) due to its capability to learn and utilise these time-dependent patterns
for both detection and mitigation tasks. Additionally, the model’s lightweight ar-
chitecture, with fewer parameters and low latency, makes it suitable for edge
deployment on resource-constrained H-IoT devices.
4.1 Abstract
The IoT revolutionises precision healthcare by enhancing patient care and reduc-
ing costs. However, this technology poses challenges in securing H-IoT devices, as
low-latency packet transmission increases vulnerability to attacks (e.g., DDoS) in
5G networks. This research develops a monitoring frequency-based detection and
dynamic threshold mitigation method using TCNs in 5G H-IoT environments.
A monitoring frequency-based detection method calculates each H-IoT node’s
message count over the past five seconds as a percentage of total traffic. A dy-
namic threshold strategy also provides adaptive security by averaging detected
malicious behaviour messages across all nodes to determine the base threshold.
This approach enhances classification accuracy, effectively mitigates actual DDoS
attack nodes, and blacklists malicious nodes without false positives in H-IoT en-
vironments. Moreover, this research creates DDoS attack models and monitoring
parameters using two simulators (Cooja and ns-3) on H-IoT devices. Data is
91

4. TCN-BASED DDoS DETECTION AND MITIGATION IN 5G
H-IoT: A FREQUENCY MONITORING AND DYNAMIC
THRESHOLD APPROACH
collected over the MQTT and UDP in a realistic 5G-based H-IoT environment,
creating two datasets with malicious and benign data. The proposed TCN-based
DDoS prediction and mitigation method achieves 99.98% and 95% accuracy on
the MQTT dataset. This model also attains a slightly higher prediction accuracy
of 99.99% and mitigation of 80% on the UDP dataset. This research evaluates the
proposed model against prior methods, including Bi-LSTM and CNNs, demon-
strating improved accuracy. Thus, the proposed TCN model is a versatile and
resilient security solution for H-IoT environments.
4.2 Introduction
The future of a smart and sustainable healthcare ecosystem depends on the se-
curity of infrastructures enabled by the IIoT [1]. The IoT consists of physical
devices connected by sensors, software, and networks [1], [310]. In recent years,
implementing IoT has enabled the vision to achieve precision healthcare. The
deployment of wearable H-IoT devices has enabled real-time surveillance and
monitoring of essential health parameters (e.g., body temperature, oxygen satu-
ration, etc). WBANs powered by MQTT are employed to enhance the security
of these IoT devices [311]. However, the magnitude and scope of security risks,
e.g., DoS/DDoS, associated with IoT devices have massively increased [312]. The
growing number of cyber risks requires urgent advancements in H-IoT security.
Malicious and threat actors, now equipped with generative AI capabilities, ex-
ploit internet-connected devices in sophisticated ways that traditional security
measures cannot adequately address [310], [313]. Such attacks can completely
disrupt essential healthcare services [314]. Hence, an AI-based defence method
is necessary to identify, assess, and neutralise these novel threats. This approach
may consider any deviation from an expected behaviour, indicating a poten-
tial security risk or anomaly [315]. Various DL techniques, including Recurrent
Neural Networks (RNNs) [316], CNNs [306], [317], and Generative Adversarial
Networks (GANs) [318], have been employed in the prevention of DDoS attacks
and anomaly detection in IoT networks. With healthcare’s growing reliance on
IoT devices, addressing the challenges of high latency, mobility, and resource con-
straints is critical in H-IoT environments [319]. This research considers an H-IoT
92

4.2 Introduction
DDoS detection and mitigation method, as existing DL-based approaches have
limitations in handling latency, mobility, and resource constraints, particularly in
real-time scenarios. This limitation arises because conventional DL models, such
as LSTMs and CNNs, often fail to capture long-range temporal dependencies and
to provide low-latency inference in resource-constrained environments [320].
Figure 4.1: Security risks with 5G, Edge, and Cloud integration in H-IoT.
The emergence of 5G networks significantly enhances H-IoT data transmission
speeds and volumes [321]. IoT devices powered with 5G may facilitate healthcare
services with high speed and lower latency [322]; however, it can also enable more
powerful and potentially damaging cyber attacks (e.g., DDoS, proximity service
intrusions, etc.) [323] [324]. Integrating 5G, edge computing, and cloud in these
paradigms broadens the cyber threat landscape, introducing advanced and emerg-
ing security risks posed by malicious or harmful actors, as depicted in Figure 4.1.
Malicious actors are involved in monitoring or sniffing using sophisticated tools,
like Wireshark, to monitor and analyse a device’s traffic, capturing details such as
data packet size, payload length, and protocol [325], [326]. Attackers also employ
this technique for harvesting sensitive user information within a network, such as
usernames and passwords [325], [327], [328], typically as a preliminary step before
launching a DDoS attack.
Health monitoring systems increasingly rely on cloud and edge computing
for transmitting sensitive healthcare data across multiple H-IoT devices and net-
93

4. TCN-BASED DDoS DETECTION AND MITIGATION IN 5G
H-IoT: A FREQUENCY MONITORING AND DYNAMIC
THRESHOLD APPROACH
works [329]. The increasing number of data transmission devices and networks
significantly expands the attack surface for cybercriminals, making it easier for
them to harvest and exploit vulnerabilities [330]. As a result, managing and se-
curing this H-IoT infrastructure has become more complex [331]. It is, therefore,
necessary to develop robust security mechanisms that address vulnerabilities to
cyber-attacks and data breaches among H-IoT devices at every layer, from edge
devices to cloud infrastructure.
To address these complexities, this research uses the Cooja simulator to cre-
ate a realistic DDoS attack simulation in H-IoT environments, using emulated
WBAN H-IoT sensor data, such as body temperature, oxygen saturation, etc.
In addition, the ns-3 simulator is used to create a DDoS attack model within a
5G-based H-IoT environment, focusing on real-time traffic and network perfor-
mance over the UDP protocol. In this instance, simulated data, commonly known
as synthetic data, is artificially generated to mimic real-world H-IoT sensor data
closely. This research generates two datasets from simulated data, reflecting both
DDoS attacks and normal behaviours of H-IoT sensors. The first dataset focuses
on the MQTT protocol and contains malicious and non-malicious H-IoT WBAN
data from a simulated DDoS attack on the Cooja simulator. The second dataset
relies on the UDP protocol and includes malicious and benign data collected from
a realistic 5G-based H-IoT environment with a simulated DDoS attack on the ns-3
simulator. This research uses two simulators, ns-3 and Cooja, to capture diverse
H-IoT network environments. The ns-3 allows us to carefully control the flow
and types of data in the network and set specific rules for how data is sent and
received, making the simulation more realistic [332]. In contrast, Cooja addresses
IoT-specific challenges. For example, the ns-3 simulator does not allow users to
manually select and configure individual H-IoT nodes in a graphical environment.
This dual approach ensures a comprehensive simulation of DDoS attack scenarios
across general and IoT networks.
Hence, this research employs TCNs to address real-time challenges in detect-
ing and mitigating DDoS attacks within H-IoT environments, due to their ability
to effectively model temporal dependencies in network traffic. While the focus
of this research is on H-IoT, the proposed TCN-based approach generalises to
broader IoT networks, as discussed in Section 4.7, with supporting results on
non-H-IoT datasets such as BoT-IoT and CIC-IDS2017, as shown in Table 4.12.
94

4.2 Introduction
The TCNs’ outputs offer advanced IoT security solutions, improving cyberse-
curity and supporting precision healthcare by addressing vulnerabilities within
5G networks. This is achieved through real-time attack prediction, message fre-
quency proportion, monitoring frequency, and dynamic thresholding. Specifically,
this research proposes security solutions that detect unusual traffic patterns by
calculating each H-IoT node’s message frequency as a percentage of total network
activity. It continuously monitors node behaviour over past intervals (e.g., every
5 seconds) to identify anomalies in traffic patterns, including the appearance of
new nodes with lower message volumes compared to established ones.
In the mitigation phase, a dynamic threshold-based strategy is applied. This
strategy calculates a base threshold based on the average count of malicious
message activity among nodes affected by the DDoS attack. This threshold au-
tomatically captures real-time changes in malicious behaviour within H-IoT en-
vironments. A blacklist is created for nodes that exceed the dynamic threshold,
flagging those nodes as compromised by the DDoS attack. During the mitiga-
tion phase, a filtering approach improves prevention accuracy. If the initial base
threshold set by the TCN model flags non-malicious nodes, it can be adjusted
to reduce incorrect detections or false positives. This adjustment also makes the
blacklist more selective, minimising the risk of mistakenly flagging non-malicious
nodes as malicious. The adjusted threshold is dynamically calculated to cap-
ture only accurate malicious activities. This adaptability ensures the mitigation
strategy remains effective in real-world 5G H-IoT environments, where precise
detection is essential under fluctuating network conditions. This filtering strat-
egy is also crucial for maintaining the integrity and availability of data from 5G
H-IoT devices.
The MQTT protocol uses a publisher/subscriber model to facilitate commu-
nication between IoT devices in a lightweight approach [333]. Due to its archi-
tecture, this protocol is inherently vulnerable to DoS attacks that compromise
the reliability and efficiency of IoT systems. Specifically, MQTT’s centralised
broker is a single point of failure, and the lack of payload encryption and au-
thentication allows attackers to overwhelm it with malicious traffic easily [334].
Even high-priority messages can be flooded into the broker by exploiting vari-
ous QoS levels. Consequently, DDoS attacks increase the risk of severe patient
safety and privacy breaches on H-IoT devices. One of the biggest challenges
95

4. TCN-BASED DDoS DETECTION AND MITIGATION IN 5G
H-IoT: A FREQUENCY MONITORING AND DYNAMIC
THRESHOLD APPROACH
is to develop security measures for MQTT that can counteract DDoS attacks
without compromising the battery-powered IoT device. With 5G-IoT, risks re-
lated to unauthorised access are amplified, as these devices lack sophisticated
security functions, such as real-time monitoring, frequency-based detection and
dynamic thresholding mitigation mechanisms. Hence, developing sustainable se-
curity solutions (e.g., a dynamic threshold mechanism) is imperative for securing
5G-enabled H-IoT devices.
UDP is a connectionless protocol that prioritises speed over reliability [335],
[336]. Due to the lack of mechanisms such as acknowledgement and retrans-
mission, data transfer is faster but less dependent. UDP’s simplicity and lack
of flow control make it vulnerable to attackers. It cannot differentiate between
authorised and unauthorised traffic, which enables malicious exploitation. This
vulnerability highlights the need for effective security mechanisms, such as mon-
itoring frequency, regularly checking network activity at specified intervals (e.g.,
5 seconds) to protect healthcare data against potential attacks (e.g., DDoS) in a
5G-based H-IoT environment.
As DDoS attacks increase in H-IoT environments, exploring existing detection
and mitigation methods is essential. The following section reviews prior research,
highlights its limitations, and explains how this research addresses those gaps.
The key contributions of this research are outlined as follows:
• This research develops a DDoS attack model using the Cooja simulator
to test MQTT vulnerabilities in H-IoT environments, emulating realistic
WBAN sensor data such as oxygen saturation, body temperature, and heart
rate. Hence, the synthetic dataset, “ UL-ECE-MQTT-DDoS-H-IoT2025,”
is generated explicitly for H-IoT security.
• This research develops an additional DDoS attack model for the UDP pro-
tocol using the ns-3 simulator within a 5G-enabled H-IoT environment. The
attack model adopts a two-dimensional grid layout in ns-3 to control dis-
tances among clients, DDoS attack-affected nodes, and the server, enabling
proximity-based network performance analysis. Traffic data generated by
the simulation is preprocessed at 5-second monitoring intervals for detailed
analysis of normal and DDoS-affected patterns. This preprocessing strategy
96

4.2 Introduction
also facilitates the detection of new nodes with lower message volumes than
existing ones. The resulting dataset, UL-ECE-UDP-DDoS-H-IoT2025, is
constructed using critical features.
• This research proposes a TCN-based prediction model that uses message
frequency proportions to predict DDoS attacks on the UL-ECE-MQTT-
DDoS-H-IoT2025 dataset, achieving a high prediction accuracy of 99.98%.
To the best of current knowledge, this is the first study to propose a moni-
toring frequency-driven TCN prediction model for detecting DDoS attacks
in 5G-based H-IoT environments, representing the core novelty of this re-
search. The proposed model achieves a prediction accuracy of 99.99% on
the UL-ECE-UDP-DDoS-H-IoT2025 dataset.
• Based on the proposed TCN prediction model, a dynamic threshold-based
strategy provides an adaptive security solution for detecting and mitigat-
ing DDoS attack-affected nodes in H-IoT environments. It automatically
calculates the average, which serves as the base threshold, by analysing ma-
licious activity across all detected nodes. A novel aspect of this approach is
its automatic mitigation and blacklist creation capabilities, achieving detec-
tion and mitigation accuracy of 96.94% and 95% on the UL-ECE-MQTT-
DDoS-H-IoT2025 dataset. On the second dataset, UL-ECE-UDP-DDoS-
H-IoT2025, this approach achieves detection and mitigation accuracies of
75% and 80%, respectively. Thus, the TCN model automatically identifies
and blacklists compromised H-IoT nodes in both proposed datasets, which
enhances security by isolating unauthorised traffic.
This chapter is organised as follows. Section 4.3 discusses related work in
anomaly detection systems for H-IoT environments, Section 4.4 outlines the struc-
tured data collection process using the Cooja simulator, encompassing DDoS at-
tack modeling over the MQTT protocol and data preprocessing to create a dataset
that supports reliable anomaly detection in H-IoT environments, Section 4.5 ex-
plains the data collection process using the ns-3 simulator, where DDoS attack
modeling is conducted over the UDP protocol in a 5G-based H-IoT environment,
followed by data preprocessing to create another dataset for DDoS detection and
mitigation, Section 4.6 details implementing TCNs to detect DDoS attacks and
