4.3 Related Work
This section reviews existing H-IoT anomaly detection methods, including dif-
ferent attack types (e.g., DoS/DDoS), datasets employed, gaps, and limitations.
This review also highlights the novelty and distinguishing aspects of the present
research compared to previous studies. Moreover, Table 4.1 summarises a sur-
vey of published methods in H-IoT over the last five years, offering additional
insights.
A prior study by Khatun et al. [306] investigates the use of CNNs for anomaly
detection in time-series data in healthcare-IoT environments. The study sim-
ulates DDoS attacks using the Cooja simulator and focuses on environmental
sensing data, including temperature and humidity. The proposed CNN-based
approach achieves 92% accuracy in attack detection. The authors also introduce
the WSN DDoS Attack H-IoT2023 dataset, which is tailored to environmental
IoT scenarios and may have limited generalisability to heterogeneous healthcare
sensing devices.
Altulaihan et al. [337] propose an IDS for enhancing IoT security against
DoS attacks. The approach focuses on anomaly detection using supervised ML,
particularly leveraging DTs and Random Forest classifiers. The system trains on
the IoTID20 dataset with feature selection [338]. While DT provides superior
efficiency in training and testing times, the study highlights the challenge of
balancing performance and computational efficiency in IoT environments. This
consideration is underscored by previous research.
Chaganti et al. [340] developed a DNN-based system for detecting network
attacks in the IoMT. Employing a DNN within a comprehensive framework that
includes Particle Swarm Optimisation for feature selection, the study utilised the
