4.4.3.6 Handling Class Imbalance with SMOTE
The SMOTE addresses the imbalance between “Malicious” and “Normal” classes
in the training data. SMOTE generates synthetic samples for the minority class,
“Normal,” as illustrated in Table 4.7. This process involves selecting random
samples from the minority class and creating new instances by calculating their
differences, as shown in Equation 4.4. Consequently, the number of “Normal”
samples increases to match the number of “Malicious” samples, resulting in a
balanced training set [349]. This step is critical for reducing bias and enhancing
the model’s ability to detect both classes effectively. To address class imbalance,
SMOTE is selected over common strategies such as random oversampling, under-
sampling, and class weighting (i.e., assigning higher loss weights to minority-class
samples during training) [350]. For instance, random oversampling repeats ex-
isting minority-class samples, which may increase the likelihood of overfitting
111

4. TCN-BASED DDoS DETECTION AND MITIGATION IN 5G
H-IoT: A FREQUENCY MONITORING AND DYNAMIC
THRESHOLD APPROACH
[351]. In contrast, undersampling reduces class imbalance by removing samples
from the majority class, which may lead to the loss of important majority-class
patterns, such as normal network traffic behaviour [352]. Compared to more
complex variants such as Adaptive Synthetic Sampling (ADASYN), which may
introduce noisy samples near decision boundaries between normal and malicious
traffic [351], SMOTE provides a simpler approach for generating synthetic mi-
nority samples to balance the minority class distribution relative to the majority
class in numerical network traffic data. Therefore, SMOTE is applied only to the
training data after the train–test split to avoid bias and ensure fair evaluation.
Specifically, the synthetic sample generation process in SMOTE is mathemati-
cally defined as follows:
xnew = xi +δ× (xj− xi) (4.4)
Where:
• xi is a randomly selected minority class sample.
• xj is one of the k-nearest neighbors of xi (also from the minority class).
• δ is a random number between 0 and 1.
• xnew is the newly generated synthetic sample.
