4.5.1.1 DDoS Attack Simulation Setup Over UDP with ns-3
This research uses the ns-3 simulator to create a DDoS attack simulation setup
in a 5G-based H-IoT environment. This setup employs a hierarchical topology,
organising network nodes in layers with the UDP protocol to facilitate efficient
data flow and management [354]. As depicted in Figure 4.5, the red nodes repre-
sent the eNodeBs (evolved Node B) and act as LTE base stations. These nodes
handle wireless communication between the core network and various user equip-
ment, such as smartphones, tablets, H-IoT devices, and wearable health monitors
[355]. Multiple eNodeBs are commonly deployed to ensure broad coverage and
seamless handover [356]. A green node (single server or target UE) receives le-
gitimate and malicious traffic in the H-IoT environment. Figure 4.5 also depicts
multiple client nodes, highlighted in blue, that simulate H-IoT devices generating
real-time traffic. These H-IoT devices transmit health-related data, such as body
temperature, oxygen levels, and heart rate, to the server (target UE). The DDoS
attack-affected nodes, denoted in yellow, are configured to simulate malicious
traffic directed towards the server, allowing analysis of the effects of the attacks
on the network. This hierarchical structure supports high device density, low
latency, and efficient traffic management, which is critical for optimal 5G per-
formance in complex environments such as H-IoT. The DDoS attack simulation
setup allows observation of its impact on a centralised server within the H-IoT
environment.
The simulation runs for 120 seconds and processes over one million malicious
and benign data records. The H-IoT environment is composed of different types
of nodes, including normal H-IoT nodes (clients) numbered from 5 to 18 (client
1 to Client 14), which generate real-time traffic using the UDP protocol to the
target UE with IP addresses in the range 7.0.0.3 to 7.0.0.16. DDoS attack-affected
nodes numbered 19 to 23 (DDoS 1 to DDoS 5), assigned IP addresses from 7.0.0.17
to 7.0.0.21, simulate malicious traffic to overwhelm the target UE (7.0.0.2, the
central server), as depicted in Figure 4.5. Extra DDoS attack-affected nodes
numbered from 24 to 26 (DDoS 6 to DDoS 8), with IP addresses from 7.0.0.22
to 7.0.0.24, are introduced dynamically during the simulation to increase the
intensity of the attack. These extra nodes are added at intervals of 10 seconds,
starting 20 seconds into the simulation. This setup illustrates a realistic scenario
114

4.5 Dataset Details for UDP
where DDoS attack nodes appear as new malicious nodes at different times within
the same topology. The data transmitted from these extra DDoS attack-affected
nodes to the central server is then analysed to assess the impact of the new
malicious nodes. This is crucial for testing the TCN model’s ability to detect
new malicious nodes that appear in the same topology at different times.
Moreover, the coordinates in Figure 4.5 define the nodes’ relative positions
in the simulated network environment. These spatial values (e.g., 125.0, 90.0)
indicate the location of each node on a 2D grid in the realistic simulated behaviour
[357]. For instance, the distance between two IoT nodes can affect signal strength
and latency. IoT nodes located farther from the central server typically experience
weaker signal strength and higher transmission delays than those closer to the
server. Setting these positions accurately in the simulation setup is essential
for creating realistic network behaviour. In H-IoT scenarios, if an IoT sensor
(such as one monitoring heart rate) is far from the server, it may face challenges
transmitting data reliably, especially during network disruptions caused by DDoS
attacks. Understanding the impact of node placement on performance is essential
for enhancing network reliability.
4.5.2 Data Acquisition with ns-3 simulator
This research establishes a WBAN-based 5G H-IoT environment with UDP pro-
tocol to run simulations for data collection using the ns-3 simulator [358]. This
simulation enables a detailed evaluation of each H-IoT sensor’s performance by
analysing metadata, such as data packet size, payload length, and protocol, spe-
cific to 5G healthcare environments.
4.5.3 Data Preprocessing of UDP Dataset
Using the ns-3 simulator, simulations run over the UDP protocol to capture a wide
range of network traffic characteristics from 5G-enabled H-IoT WBAN sensors.
The raw data includes packet sizes, transmission rates, latency, and protocol
information, all recorded in a text file. The data also includes timestamps, node
IDs, transmission (TX) and reception (RX) events, and IPv4 header information,
such as TTL (Time To Live), which sets the maximum number of hops a packet
