The preprocessing of ns-3 data involves several vital steps to ensure it is clean,
organised, and ready for analysis. First, essential features such as timestamps,
node identifiers, protocol types, and packet-specific details are extracted by iden-
tifying patterns within the data using regular expressions (regex), a technique for
defining search patterns in text. Regex allows for precise matching and extraction
of specific data elements based on defined sequences of characters. Next, data
cleaning processes remove irrelevant entries, address missing or invalid data, and
standardise protocol and header fields for consistency. Standardising protocol
fields ensures that protocol names or values follow the same format, essential for
accurate analysis. Lastly, feature engineering converts categorical data into nu-
merical form and calculates message counts. It also computes key traffic metrics
such as message frequency, inter-arrival times, and monitoring frequency. These
metrics enable a detailed analysis of network behaviour.
The dataset’s features are described as follows.
4.5.3.1 Node IDs (ids)
In ns-3 simulations for 5G H-IoT environments, node IDs serve as unique identi-
fiers for various network nodes, including base stations, servers, User Equipment
(UE) such as smartphones, wearable health monitors, and other H-IoT devices.
4.5.3.2 Time Elapsed (time)
This feature represents the number of seconds that have passed since the first
timestamp is recorded in the simulation. Here, time denotes the elapsed time.
The time elapsed is calculated as the difference between the current timestamp
(timecurrent) and the initial timestamp (timefirst):
time =timecurrent−timefirst (4.5)
4.5.3.3 Protocol (proto)
The UDP protocol is used in the ns-3 simulation to communicate with H-IoT de-
vices. The protocol is extracted from the log line by identifying specific keywords,
such as “ns3::UdpHeader.” Once identified, it is converted into a numerical code
