(e.g., UDP as 0) for further processing and analysis. In the dataset, the protocol
is represented by this numerical code (e.g., 0), making it suitable for ML models
or statistical analysis.
4.5.3.4 Source IP (src ip)
This feature represents the IP address of the IoT device that originates data
traffic in the healthcare system. The source IP is extracted from the log line
by locating the ‘ns3::Ipv4Header’ section where IP addresses are listed in the
format<source ip>> . For example, if the log line shows ‘7.0.0.21 > 7.0.0.2’, the
source IP is ‘7.0.0.21’. After extracting the source IP from the logline, each unique
address corresponding to an IoT device is converted into a unique numerical value
(e.g., ‘1, 2, 3, ...’) for processing and analysis. Let s denote the source IP, and
the conversion can be defined as:
numeric value(si) =index(si) + 1 (4.6)
Where Index(si) is the zero-based index of each unique source IP. This encoding
simplifies the representation of IP addresses, making them suitable for processing
and analysis in ML models or statistical computations. The source IP helps
identify where the traffic originates. In a DDoS attack, multiple requests might
come from the same IP (or a set of spoofed IPs), or a botnet might use a range
of IPs [152]. Monitoring unusual traffic patterns from specific IP addresses can
indicate a potential DDoS attack.
4.5.3.5 Destination IP (dst ip)
This feature specifies the destination IP address in the H-IoT network. The des-
tination IP is extracted from the log line in the ‘ns3::Ipv4Header’ section, where
IP addresses are listed in the format <source_ip> > <destination_ip> . It is
the value that appears immediately after the> symbol. In the dataset, the desti-
nation IP is always the same (e.g., ‘7.0.0.2’), representing a central server or base
station that receives traffic from all H-IoT devices. This encoding simplifies the
representation of IP addresses, making them suitable for ML models or statistical
computations. Monitoring the destination IP is essential for DDoS detection, as
118

