4.6 Methodology
Algorithm 1 Dynamic thresholding for malicious node detection and mitigation.
Input: FeaturesX, labels ytest, predictions ypred
Output: Detection and Mitigation Accuracy, Blacklist, Threshold
1. Initialize:
malicious nodes←{} , error count← 0
mitigation error← 0
2. Detection Phase:
for i = 1 to the number of items in ytest do
actual←ytest[i]
predicted←ypred[i]
node id←X[i][Node IDs]
error count←error count + (predicted̸=actual)
if predicted = 1 then
malicious nodes[node id]←
malicious nodes.get(node id, 0) + 1
end if
end for
3. Calculate Detection Accuracy:
accuracy← (1−error count/len(ypred))× 100
4. Mitigation Phase:
Compute base threshold as the average of malicious nodes’ values
Set adjustedthreshold to base threshold reduced by 20%
for eachnode id, count in malicious nodes do
if count exceeds adjustedthreshold then
Add node id to blacklist
if node id is not in actual malicious nodes then
Increment mitigation error count
end if
end if
end for
5. Calculate Mitigation Accuracy:
mit accuracy← 100× (1− mitigation error
len(blacklist) )
6. Output Results:
Print “Malicious Node Counts: ”, malicious nodes
Print “Blacklist Nodes: ”, blacklist
Print “Adaptive Threshold: ”, adjustedthresh
Print “Detection Accuracy: ”, accuracy
Print “Mitigation Accuracy: ”, mit accuracy
The dynamic threshold determines when to block nodes in the H-IoT system.
It is based on specific characteristics of network activity, including the number
127

4. TCN-BASED DDoS DETECTION AND MITIGATION IN 5G
H-IoT: A FREQUENCY MONITORING AND DYNAMIC
THRESHOLD APPROACH
of nodes, the volume of MQTT and UDP data transfers for both malicious and
non-malicious nodes, and the ratio of malicious packets sent to the server. During
the detection phase, the model compares each H-IoT node’s predicted behaviour
with its actual class (i.e., whether the node is genuinely malicious or normal),
incrementing error counts for false positives and false negatives. As a result,
the mitigation technique involves iteratively checking the predicted and actual
behaviours of the H-IoT nodes. Malicious activity (i.e., messages) from compro-
mised H-IoT nodes is recorded in a dictionary, and detection accuracy is calcu-
lated based on the total error count, reflecting the sum of incorrect predictions,
including false positives (normal nodes classified as malicious) and false negatives
(malicious nodes classified as normal). Therefore, in the mitigation phase, the
dynamic threshold, which serves as the base threshold for both datasets (MQTT
and UDP), is calculated by averaging the number of detected malicious messages
recorded in the dictionary from the particular H-IoT nodes flagged as malicious.
Moreover, in the mitigation phase for the MQTT dataset, this research adjusts
the base threshold downward by 20% to enhance mitigation accuracy. An H-IoT
node with malicious activity counts exceeding the adjusted threshold is black-
listed, minimising false positives and improving network security in real-time. In
contrast, the UDP dataset does not require any adjustment to the base threshold
to achieve optimal mitigation accuracy. Therefore, the TCN-based detection and
mitigation technique achieves high mitigation accuracy by applying a dynamic
threshold for the UDP dataset within 5G H-IoT environments.
This DDoS mitigation approach can benefit real-world scenarios. WBANs in
H-IoT environments that use MQTT and UDP protocols are diverse and sub-
ject to change, as protocol parameters, H-IoT device configurations, and traffic
patterns may vary over time. Moreover, this mitigation strategy is highly ef-
fective, handling datasets of various sizes (e.g., over one million records) with
precision and accuracy, making it suitable for H-IoT applications. Thus, the
strategy employs risk mitigation and improved detection methods as outlined in
the Algorithm 1.
4.6.3 Ablation Studies of the MQTT Dataset
In ML, ablation studies are a technique used to systematically evaluate the im-
pact of proposed model components or parameters on overall performance [359].
128

4.6 Methodology
Researchers use this technique to optimise ML models by identifying configu-
rations that enhance performance [360], [361]. An ablation study is significant
for evaluating the proposed model’s performance using accuracy, precision, re-
call, and F1-score metrics. These standard metrics are commonly used for binary
classification tasks such as fraud detection, medical diagnosis, and anomaly detec-
tion [362]. Particularly in attack detection, datasets often exhibit an imbalanced
distribution with more regular traffic than DDoS attack traffic or vice versa, de-
pending on the scenario. Traditional metrics (e.g., accuracy) can be misleading,
as models usually achieve high accuracy by predicting the majority class. Rely-
ing solely on a single metric, such as accuracy, does not sufficiently demonstrate
the model’s effectiveness in detecting critical DDoS attacks. In this research,
the dataset contains slightly more malicious traffic than regular traffic. To ad-
dress this slight imbalance, this research applies the SMOTE technique to balance
the classes, which allows the model to achieve consistent performance across all
evaluation metrics [363].
