4.7.2 Performance Comparison with Previous Datasets
Using the Proposed TCN Method
This research evaluates the generalizability and scalability of the proposed TCN
method using several existing datasets, including the real-world IoMT public
dataset WUSTL-EHMS-2020 [366], as outlined in Table 4.12. The evaluation
reflects practical IoMT scenarios using this real-world dataset, improving the re-
liability and applicability of the proposed TCN method. Moreover, this research
compares the proposed model across datasets from different domains, including
IDS, simulated IoT environments, and real-world healthcare scenarios. Never-
theless, the proposed method achieves high accuracy across multiple datasets,
including WSN DDoS Attack H-IoT2023 [306], WUSTL-EHMS-2020 [211], BoT-
IoT 2018 [305], and CIC-IDS2017 [15], with accuracy rates of 99.84%, 93.34%,
100%, and 99.57%, respectively. These results demonstrate the model’s reliability
136

4.7 Performance Evaluation and Results
and scalability in detecting and classifying attacks across various domains.
Furthermore, standard data preprocessing techniques are applied to prepare
each dataset, including data cleaning, standardisation, and handling of non-
numeric values. For instance, these steps are essential for refining raw data, which
often includes duplicates, outliers, and other quality issues in the WUSTL-EHMS-
2020 and BoT-IoT 2018 datasets. Similarly, the CIC-IDS2017 dataset requires ad-
ditional handling of non-numeric and infinite values before standardisation [273].
This research fine-tunes critical parameters, such as kernel size, number of filters,
and dropout rate, to optimise the model’s performance across diverse datasets,
including WSN DDoS Attack H-IoT2023 [306] and WUSTL-EHMS-2020 [211].
Adjusting parameters for different datasets is a standard practice in ML and DL
to ensure optimal performance [367], [368]. These adjustments highlight the TCN
model’s flexibility in adapting to distinct dataset features, such as data structure,
input dimensions, and time-series patterns. As a result, the model achieves high
accuracy across diverse datasets by resolving data quality issues and validating
its effectiveness in detecting DDoS and other types of attacks.
In addition, this research evaluates the computational efficiency of the pro-
posed model by comparing the Bi-LSTM and TCN architectures on theUL-ECE-
MQTT-DDoS-H-IoT2025 dataset. The findings reveal that the TCN model has
significantly fewer parameters than the Bi-LSTM model. This demonstrates that
the TCN architecture is more memory-efficient, lightweight, and well-suited for
resource-constrained, real-time applications in H-IoT environments. Addition-
ally, the TCN model exhibits slightly better computational efficiency, achieving
a lower average inference time per sample than Bi-LSTM, as illustrated in Ta-
ble 4.13, with both models trained and evaluated over 50 epochs. Hence, the
results infer improved runtime performance for TCN compared to the Bi-LSTM
model.
