and 99.99% recall (refer to Table 4.15 and Figure 4.8).
Although the difference in recall between the two datasets (99.99% for UDP
and 100% for MQTT) appears minor, even a slightly lower recall can significantly
affect anomaly detection in critical applications (e.g., H-IoT security). However,
these minor variations in recall performance can be attributable to the differ-
ing characteristics of the MQTT and UDP protocols and the respective network
environments (Cooja-based MQTT and ns-3-based UDP in 5G). The MQTT
dataset’s higher recall (100%) indicates that the model correctly identified all
malicious samples, perhaps due to MQTT’s structured and predictable nature
[369]. In contrast, UDP traffic is comparatively dynamic and less predictable,
which may account for the marginally lower recall on the UDP dataset. As a
connectionless protocol, UDP does not ensure order, consistency, or error correc-
tion for data packets [370]. This leads to fluctuations from variable packet loss,
reordering, or duplication in H-IoT devices [371].
138

4.7 Performance Evaluation and Results
In ML, data preprocessing techniques play a pivotal role in enhancing the
performance of anomaly detection models, particularly in achieving high recall
[372]. Therefore, the data preprocessing approach ensures clean and accurate
data, significantly enhancing the model’s performance across all metrics. ML/DL
algorithms, including the TCN model, typically excel when a model can identify
consistent patterns in data. In this research, while UDP traffic is comparatively
dynamic and less structured than MQTT, this variability may challenge the model
in identifying consistent patterns for predicting or classifying H-IoT nodes. As
a result, the model may struggle to consistently detect all instances of malicious
activity, leading to a slightly lower recall rate.
The number of messages or data packets sent by non-malicious nodes in the
MQTT dataset follows a predictable, sequential pattern. For instance, node 3
sends 350 messages out of a total of 10,007 messages. Additionally, node 4 sends
344 messages out of 10,018, which is remarkably close to the transmissions of node
3, demonstrating the regular behaviour of a normal node. Notably, nodes 3 and
4 do not transmit data in sequence, yet their total packet counts remain close,
highlighting the consistency of non-malicious traffic. Based on the analysis, even
if a node like node 3 transmits data multiple times to the server, the messages
remain consistent and close together, as is expected for a non-malicious node.
Furthermore, in a dynamic topology, nodes operate in a random pattern. For
instance, while node 3 may send data to the server at one moment, the next
packet might be sent by a different node, such as node 18, as depicted in Table 4.3.
In contrast, nodes marked as DDoS attack nodes, such as node 18, deviate
significantly from MQTT’s structured behaviour. Node 18 transmits 1,008 mes-
sages out of a total of 10,006 messages at high transmission rates, far exceeding
the message counts of normal nodes. Similarly, node 17, which sent 967 messages
out of 10,011, exhibits behaviour typical of DDoS attack nodes, with message
volumes close to node 18’s high counts and significantly greater than those of
non-malicious nodes. This structured data helps the TCN model achieve 100%
recall.
In the UDP dataset, normal nodes like node 16 and node 18 each sent 1,912
messages out of their respective totals of 49,991 and 49,993 messages, as shown in
Table 4.5. Data traffic appears steady for normal nodes using the UDP protocol.
In contrast, attack nodes show significant variation in message counts, indicat-
ing dynamic behaviour. For example, DDoS attack-affected nodes such as node
139

