DEPLOYMENT ON RASPBERRY PI 4
Figure 5.1: Real-time DDoS detection workflow within IoT environments using a
TCN model deployment on edge devices.
implemented architectures align with current research trends emphasising
efficient model design for resource-constrained or edge-oriented AI systems [394].
Owing to the TCN model’s lightweight nature, quantisation reduces precision
from FP32 to lower-bit representations (e.g., INT8), thereby minimising resource
consumption while preserving accuracy. INT8 quantisation is a widely used
standard in DL and edge deployment, particularly for TFLite and ARM-based
platforms [395], [396]. This enables efficient cyberattack classification with high
accuracy, low latency, and reduced power usage on edge devices. To evaluate the
proposed approach on resource-constrained edge platforms, this research uses
the UL-ECE-MQTT-DDoS-H-IoT2025 and UL-ECE-UDP-DDoS-H-IoT2025
datasets described in Chapter 3. The TCN model is converted into TFLite, an
optimised framework for deploying DL models on embedded and mobile devices.
During conversion, quantisation is applied, resulting in a lightweight model
that enables practical DDoS detection on constrained IoT devices. Specifically,
152

5.2 Introduction
Raspberry Pi 4 is selected as the edge platform, a low-power Advanced RISC
Machines (ARM)-based device widely used in IoT environments [397]–[399].
Thus, the primary objective is to develop a practical deployment method for
securing IoT systems against DDoS attacks across IoT, H-IoT, and IIoT environ-
ments. An overview of the workflow for deploying TCN-based DDoS detection
on edge devices is depicted in Figure 5.1. The performance and feasibility are
evaluated on Raspberry Pi 4, including power consumption, inference time,
parameter count, and model size. A Universal Serial Bus (USB) power meter
is used to measure power consumption, as software-based profiling is unreliable
on the Raspberry Pi [400], [401]. The key contributions of this research are as
follows:
• To the best of current knowledge, this is the first lightweight TCN model
deployed on Raspberry Pi 4 for real-time DDoS detection, supporting se-
cure operation in diverse IoT domains, including healthcare and indus-
trial systems. The deployment achieves a reduced parameter count and
a smaller model size, making it feasible for IoT devices that have limited
RAM while maintaining consistent performance, high accuracy, and low la-
tency on the UL-ECE-MQTT-DDoS-H-IoT2025 and UL-ECE-UDP-DDoS-
H-IoT2025 datasets.
• The deployment is practically validated through USB power meter mea-
surements on a Raspberry Pi 4, demonstrating average power consumption
across five runs of 4.22 W and 4.64 W for the UL-ECE-MQTT-DDoS-H-
IoT2025 and UL-ECE-UDP-DDoS-H-IoT2025 datasets, respectively.
This chapter is organised as follows. Section 5.3 reviews related work on bi-
nary classification and edge deployment in IoT, H-IoT, and IIoT environments.
Section 5.4 describes the deployment of a lightweight TCN model on Raspberry
Pi 4 for efficient DDoS attack classification across diverse IoT settings. Sec-
tion 5.5 presents a comparative analysis with prior studies, examines the power
consumption of the edge system, and reports results on inference time, parameter
count, model size, and the confusion matrix. Finally, Section 5.6 summarises the
contributions and discusses future research directions.
