5.3 Related Work
This section reviews prior approaches, highlighting key contributions and the lim-
itations that affect the feasibility of edge deployment. In this context, anomaly
detection has been widely applied for DDoS detection and mitigation across IoT,
H-IoT, and IIoT environments. As highlighted in Section 5.2, several DL mod-
els are used for securing IoT environments. Beyond these, hybrid architectures
such as CNN-BiLSTM and CNN-LSTM have also been employed in prior studies
[6], [7]. These hybrid models introduce a large number of trainable parameters,
which diminishes their suitability for deployment scenarios [391]. Moreover, many
studies rely on older datasets (e.g., CIC-DOS2017, CIC-IDS2017, CIC-IDS2018,
NSL-KDD, KDD99, UNSW-NB15) that fail to capture realistic IoT traffic and
evolving threat patterns, leading to limited generalisation [402]–[404]. A sum-
mary of recent studies and their main contributions is provided in Table 5.1.
Akar et al. [4] introduce an LSTM-based intrusion detection model tailored
for IoMT environments. Using the CICIoMT2024 dataset, the model achieves a
classification accuracy of 98%, demonstrating the effectiveness of DL in strength-
ening IoMT security. Despite this impressive accuracy, the approach suffers from
low computational efficiency, which constrains its scalability in practical IoMT
deployments. The authors acknowledge that the LSTM model incurs signifi-
cant computational overhead, which raises concerns about its practicality and
energy efficiency in resource-constrained IoMT environments. Similarly, another
study indicates that LSTM-based intrusion detection techniques are constrained
by computational complexity and substantial resource requirements [406].
A monitoring-frequency-based detection and dynamic-threshold mitigation
method using TCNs is proposed to secure 5G H-IoT environments, as detailed
in Chapter 4. The method analyses each node’s traffic contribution relative to
overall network activity and applies adaptive thresholds for DDoS detection and
mitigation. MQTT and UDP-based datasets are generated using the Cooja and
ns-3 simulators, and the TCN model achieves high detection accuracy. However,
the lack of edge deployment and resource feasibility analysis highlights a practical
gap that this thesis addresses.
In [391], a hybrid LSTM-FF neural network is proposed for detecting low-
rate DoS (LR-DoS) attacks. Random Forest is used for feature selection, and the
model is evaluated on CIC-DOS2017, CSE-CIC-IDS2018, and LR-HR-DDOS2024
