In practice, the zero-point is clamped to the valid range [ qmin,q max], and thus
remains within [−128, 127] [410], [411].
During evaluation, full INT8 quantisation of the TCN model results in a
minimal change in DDoS detection accuracy compared to the FP32 model. All
evaluation metrics remain within ±0.05% of the original FP32 values on both
the UL-ECE-MQTT-DDoS-H-IoT2025 and UL-ECE-UDP-DDoS-H-IoT2025
162

5.4 Methodology
datasets. This indicates that quantisation preserves detection performance while
improving deployment efficiency on the Raspberry Pi 4.
5.4.4 Real-Time Deployment of TCN on Raspberry Pi 4
The TCN TFLite model is deployed on a Raspberry Pi 4 to detect DDoS traffic
in IoT settings, using the UL-ECE-MQTT-DDoS-H-IoT2025 and UL-ECE-UDP-
DDoS-H-IoT2025 datasets. The deployment consists of three steps: loading and
preprocessing the dataset, loading the TFLite model, and performing inference
on the edge device. The end-to-end workflow is shown in Figure 5.3.
Figure 5.3: Real-time deployment of the lightweight TCN model on Raspberry Pi
4. The process involves converting the model to TFLite, performing data prepro-
cessing, and enabling real-time inference and classification.
5.4.4.1 Dataset Loading and Preprocessing
During deployment, bothUL-ECE-MQTT-DDoS-H-IoT2025 and UL-ECE-UDP-
DDoS-H-IoT2025 test datasets are loaded on the Raspberry Pi and preprocessed
following the same procedure used during the original TCN model training to
ensure consistency. The feature schema, extracted from the training data and
163

5. SECURING IoT USING LIGHTWEIGHT TCN FOR EDGE
DEPLOYMENT ON RASPBERRY PI 4
saved before TFLite conversion, is reapplied to preserve the expected feature
set and order for input alignment. All feature values are coerced to numeric,
±∞ entries are converted to NaN, and rows containing NaN values are discarded.
The saved StandardScaler and feature schema from training are applied on the
Raspberry Pi before inference. SMOTE is not used during inference. If the
outcome column is present, it is excluded from scaling and retained separately
for evaluation. The processed data are reshaped to represent each sample as
a sequence of input features with a single channel, as required by the Conv1D
layers of the TCN model. In this configuration, the number of features is 4 for
UL-ECE-MQTT-DDoS-H-IoT2025 and 13 for UL-ECE-UDP-DDoS-H-IoT2025.
5.4.4.2 Loading the TFLite Model
The TFLite runtime is a lightweight interpreter that executes the converted model
on-device. It is configured with full INT8 input/output quantisation to reduce
computational cost and enable efficient execution on edge hardware such as the
Raspberry Pi 4. XNNPACK delegation is enabled when supported to reduce in-
ference latency, and the number of inference threads is configured according to the
available CPU cores (e.g., 1–4 on a Raspberry Pi 4). To prepare the model for in-
ference, the input tensor is explicitly resized to match the expected shape [1,F, 1]
before allocate tensors() because the interpreter requires tensor dimensions
to be defined in advance to allocate memory correctly. After allocation, the inter-
preter metadata is used to verify that the input and output tensors are in INT8
format for each dataset. To ensure reproducibility, random seeds are fixed, and
the model’s declared feature count is validated against the preprocessed dataset.
5.4.4.3 Inference and Classification
Inference is performed sample by sample (batch = 1). Each normalised feature
vector is quantised to INT8 using the interpreter’s input scale and zero-point pa-
rameters and then provided to the TFLite interpreter for inference. The output is
subsequently dequantized to floating point using the corresponding output param-
eters. A threshold of 0 .5 is applied to classify each sample as {Normal, DDoS}.
When true class labels (ground truth) are available, the saved label encoder is
used to map them, and evaluation metrics (accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score)
are reported. To avoid inflated latency from one-time initialisation effects, warm-
164

5.5 Performance Evaluation and Comparison
up inferences are executed and discarded before measurement. In practice, 5–10
warm-up runs are typically sufficient on TFLite; hence, ten are used for the UL-
ECE-MQTT-DDoS-H-IoT2025 dataset and five for the UL-ECE-UDP-DDoS-H-
IoT2025 dataset.
5.5 Performance Evaluation and Comparison
This section presents a detailed evaluation of the adopted lightweight TCN model,
comparing it with previously published ML and DL methods across multiple
performance metrics. It further reports the layer-wise parameter counts and per-
sample inference time on Google Colab. The evaluation of the TCN TFLite
model focuses on its inference latency on the Raspberry Pi 4. In addition, power
consumption is measured across the UL-ECE-MQTT-DDoS-H-IoT2025 and UL-
ECE-UDP-DDoS-H-IoT2025 datasets, capturing temporal variations in power
usage. A confusion matrix is presented to illustrate the model’s classification
performance for DDoS detection on Raspberry Pi 4. It also includes cross-dataset
deployment evaluation, edge security considerations, five real-world case studies,
security analysis, and attack coverage across diverse IoT environments, and a
discussion of TCN model selection.
5.5.1 Comparison with Previous Research
The lightweight TCN model is compared with existing ML and DL methods,
including LSTM [4], Hybrid LSTM-FF [391], LR [393], CNN-Bi-LSTM [7], and
CNN-LSTM [6], all implemented on the UL-ECE-MQTT-DDoS-H-IoT2025
dataset, as outlined in Table 5.5. The results demonstrate that the TCN
model achieves higher accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score than previous
approaches. The 95% confidence intervals (CIs) for accuracy across both datasets
indicate low variability around the mean, with changes of less than 0.1%, as
reported in Table 5.5. These narrow bounds demonstrate that the lightweight
quantised TCN model maintains stable accuracy on the Raspberry Pi 4. It also
exhibits lower inference time per sample and a reduced parameter count, ob-
tained through layer-wise analysis, thereby enhancing its efficiency for real-time
applications across IoT, IIoT, and H-IoT. The average per-sample inference time
on the Raspberry Pi 4 is 0.19 ms for the UL-ECE-MQTT-DDoS-H-IoT2025
165

5. SECURING IoT USING LIGHTWEIGHT TCN FOR EDGE
DEPLOYMENT ON RASPBERRY PI 4
dataset and 0.27 ms for the UL-ECE-UDP-DDoS-H-IoT2025 dataset, as shown
in Table 5.5. As the mean inference time remains identical across the five
runs for each dataset, the standard deviation becomes zero, and the confidence
interval collapses to identical lower and upper bounds, offering no additional
insight. The calculation of the mean inference time is formalised as:
Mean Inference Time =
P Inference Times
Number of Runs (5.1)
where:
• P Inference Times represents the total cumulative inference time for all
processed samples.
• Number of Runs corresponds to the total number of inference executions.
Compared to the original TCN, the lightweight TCN TFLite version exhibits
slightly lower accuracy after quantisation. Nevertheless, the parameter count de-
creases after converting the original TCN to its TFLite version. This reduction
is beneficial for edge deployment, as it lowers memory requirements and reduces
power-consumption overhead on resource-constrained devices. Notably, the pa-
rameter count varies slightly between theUL-ECE-MQTT-DDoS-H-IoT2025 and
UL-ECE-UDP-DDoS-H-IoT2025 datasets, as shown in Table 5.5. This arises
from differences in the dimensions of input features after preprocessing, which
affect the size of the first convolutional layer. Since the UL-ECE-UDP-DDoS-H-
IoT2025 dataset includes more features in the input layer, it results in slightly dif-
ferent weight shapes and a marginally higher parameter count (two additional pa-
rameters) compared to the UL-ECE-MQTT-DDoS-H-IoT2025 dataset, as shown
in Table 5.6. This negligible increase does not affect the overall TFLite model
size. Such accuracy degradation is common in quantised models, particularly
when deployed on resource-constrained hardware [412]. On the other hand, in-
ference time can vary significantly across different platforms (e.g., Google Colab
vs. Raspberry Pi 4) due to differences in system architecture, operating systems,
and resource availability. Specifying the computing environment is a standard
practice in edge AI benchmarking to ensure consistent results and fair compar-
ison [413]. Therefore, the TCN TFLite model is well-suited for low-power edge
devices, balancing accuracy, efficiency, and resource consumption.
