5.5.5 Security Considerations for Edge TFLite Deploy-
ment
Security considerations focus on preserving the integrity, confidentiality, and
authenticity of data, deployment code, and TFLite-based ML/DL models in
security-critical edge deployments [418]. This work focuses on application-layer
DDoS detection, in which the Raspberry Pi 4 operates as a local edge node in
diverse IoT environments. In this setting, traffic-derived features are analysed
locally on the Raspberry Pi, and the DDoS detection outputs are displayed via a
local monitoring interface. Deploying a TFLite model on a resource-constrained
edge device introduces risks such as misconfiguration, model tampering, privilege
misuse, and resource abuse, which require explicit analysis at the deployment
level [419], [420]. The potential deployment of cyber threats is as follows:
• Model and deployment tampering: A malicious actor modifies the
TFLite model file or its deployment scripts to alter detection logic or disable
the DDoS detection service.
• Unauthorized device access: An adversary gains unauthorised physical
or remote access to the edge device (e.g., a Raspberry Pi 4), enabling the
detection service to be stopped, bypassed, or reconfigured.
• Inference service denial-of-service: An adversary floods the inference
service with excessive or malformed traffic-derived input samples, causing
CPU exhaustion and increased DDoS detection latency or service interrup-
tion.
179

5. SECURING IoT USING LIGHTWEIGHT TCN FOR EDGE
DEPLOYMENT ON RASPBERRY PI 4
• Model extraction and reverse engineering: An unauthorised entity
copies the TFLite model file stored on the Raspberry Pi to analyse its
structure or reuse it without authorisation, exposing its internal architec-
tural and quantisation design details.
Given the identified deployment-level threats, a proactive mitigation strategy
is necessary to preserve the integrity and reliability of the edge inference pro-
cess. This strategy focuses on protecting the deployed TFLite model, the host
edge device (e.g., Raspberry Pi 4), and the inference pipeline against practical at-
tack surfaces in resource-constrained edge environments [418]. The corresponding
mitigation mechanisms are as follows:
• Model integrity protection: The deployment design includes integrity
verification of the TFLite model and associated unauthorised cryptographic
file hashing, enabling detection of unauthorised modification before runtime
loading.
• Privilege minimisation: The inference service is designed to operate
under restricted system privileges or a dedicated user account to limit the
impact of device compromise.
• Resource protection: Rate-control mechanisms can be applied at the
inference interface to prevent abuse by excessive request injection or CPU
resource exhaustion.
• Process isolation: The inference process can be deployed in a lightweight
container or sandbox environment to isolate it from the host system and
limit lateral impact in the event of a compromised process.
• Data locality and exposure reduction: All inference is executed locally
on the edge device, and extracted traffic features are not transmitted outside
the node, reducing exposure to remote adversaries.
The above measures address the primary practical security risks associated
with deploying a TFLite-based DDoS detection model on a low-power edge plat-
form.
180

5.5 Performance Evaluation and Comparison
5.5.6 Case Studies
To highlight the practical relevance of deploying the lightweight TCN model in
TFLite form on low-power edge devices, this section outlines five real-world case
studies that demonstrate its integration across IoT, H-IoT, and IIoT environ-
ments.
1. Home Healthcare Gateways: Home IoT gateway devices that sup-
port precision healthcare and aggregate data from wearable sensors such
as heart-rate monitors, oximeters, and motion trackers could use the model
to detect abnormal spikes in network traffic [421]. These spikes (e.g., sudden
bursts of MQTT publish requests) may indicate attempts by compromised
devices to overwhelm the gateway. The low-energy overhead enables con-
tinuous, on-device monitoring without affecting battery-powered nodes.
2. Smart Hospital Ward Monitoring: A Raspberry Pi can operate as a lo-
cal edge gateway in hospital wards, where it connects to H-IoT devices such
as heart-rate sensors, temperature monitors, and wearable patient trackers
[422]. In this setting, the TCN TFLite model could run directly on the
edge device to detect abnormal network patterns generated by compro-
mised devices. The measured mean inference latency (0.19–0.27 ms) en-
ables real-time identification of DDoS-induced congestion that may disrupt
patient-critical data streams.
3. Remote Health Wearables and Telemedicine Kits: Portable diag-
nostic and telemedicine kits (e.g., glucose meters and ECG patches) used
in rural clinics, emergency response teams, or mobile medical vans often de-
pend on weak local WiFi or temporary portable routers to transmit patient
readings [423]. These unreliable connections can become congested by un-
solicited network traffic, which may introduce delays during remote consul-
tations or reduce the responsiveness of diagnostic data transfers. Deploying
the TCN TFLite model on a Raspberry Pi that controls the kit provides
a lightweight mechanism for tracking connection quality and maintaining
timely data delivery in resource-limited medical settings.
4. Industrial Sensor Clusters: Edge controllers in IIoT environments, such
as manufacturing floors and process plants, are capable of embedding the
181

5. SECURING IoT USING LIGHTWEIGHT TCN FOR EDGE
DEPLOYMENT ON RASPBERRY PI 4
model to protect PLCs and high-frequency sensor clusters from DDoS bursts
[424]. The model’s ability to operate reliably across different input dimen-
sions and communication patterns makes it suitable for IIoT deployments
where sensors often transmit heterogeneous, protocol-specific traffic. For
example, the cross-dataset evaluation on the edge device, discussed in Sec-
tion 5.5.4, demonstrates that the model remains stable even when operating
on a higher-dimensional feature space (38 features).
5. Smart City Environmental Nodes: Air-quality sensors, roadside traffic
detectors, and weather-monitoring units typically forward periodic measure-
ments through lightweight edge gateways with limited compute resources
[425]. These gateways are susceptible to abnormal volumes of unsolicited
network traffic originating from outside the sensor network, which can sat-
urate their communication links. Deploying the TCN TFLite model on
the gateway enables early detection of traffic anomalies associated with
DDoS activity, supporting uninterrupted delivery of sensor data to munic-
ipal servers.
5.5.7 Security Analysis and Attack Coverage across Di-
verse IoT Environments
In this chapter, the lightweight TCN TFLite model is deployed to detect traffic
anomalies on the Raspberry Pi 4. Its primary focus is identifying DDoS attacks,
which typically manifest as rapid increases in packet rates or other abnormal
communication patterns across H-IoT, IoT, and IIoT devices. Beyond DDoS,
MITM variants such as packet injection, duplication, or relaying can also intro-
duce traffic deviations in these environments [426]. Packet injection, duplication,
or relay at higher-than-normal rates alters message frequency and traffic volume,
and these changes are reflected in the MQTT and UDP-based features in our
datasets. Under these conditions, the TCN TFLite model can flag the anomaly
because it has learned these frequency-based patterns during training.
General IoT and IIoT threats also arise from irregular communication pat-
terns rather than increased traffic volume [427]. Replay attempts may introduce
repeated message patterns, brute-force bursts may produce short, periodic au-
thentication clusters, and malware scanning may generate probe sequences across
182

5.5 Performance Evaluation and Comparison
ports or devices [428]. These pattern-level deviations differ from normal device
behaviour and can be detected by the TCN TFLite model when they appear in
the input features. The cross-dataset results in Table 5.12 demonstrate that the
model continues to identify these traffic-pattern anomalies on the CICIIoT2025
dataset, despite the diverse device behaviours and noisier IIoT traffic.
Other attack classes, such as firmware compromise, eavesdropping, side-
channel leakage, or ransomware activation, operate at layers that do not produce
distinctive changes in traffic patterns [419]. These classes are typically mitigated
through cryptographic, firmware-level, or device-level protections [420]. In this
setting, the model could contribute to a broader multilayer defence by covering
the network-traffic dimension of the threat surface.
5.5.8 Discussion of TCN Selection and Edge Deployment
This discussion focuses on the rationale for selecting the TCN model and its de-
ployment on the Raspberry Pi 4. The TCN model is used in this research due
to its lightweight architecture and computational efficiency, as demonstrated in
Chapter 4, where it is shown to require fewer parameters than CNNs, LSTMs,
and traditional models such as DTs and KNN. This advantage is further rein-
forced in the present research through comparison with prior models, as discussed
in Section 5.5.1. In addition to its efficiency, the TCN model demonstrates pre-
liminary evidence of generalisation and scalability across the evaluated datasets
and experimental settings. It achieves high accuracy, as reported in Chapter 4,
on the UL-ECE-MQTT-DDoS-H-IoT2025 and UL-ECE-UDP-DDoS-H-IoT2025
datasets and demonstrates competitive performance on selected existing datasets,
including the real-world public IoMT dataset WUSTL-EHMS-2020 [366]. These
results indicate the potential applicability of TCN for handling diverse IoT and
IoMT scenarios within the evaluated experimental setup. Therefore, the TCN
model is converted to TFLite and deployed on a resource-constrained edge de-
vice, such as the Raspberry Pi 4.
Although the reduction in parameter count is relatively small (63,617 →
63,136), this outcome is expected, as TFLite conversion focuses on optimising
model storage and computation rather than on changing the underlying archi-
tecture. The practical benefit is reflected in the substantial decrease in model
size (248.5 KB → 89.1 KB), which directly improves feasibility for deployment
183

5. SECURING IoT USING LIGHTWEIGHT TCN FOR EDGE
DEPLOYMENT ON RASPBERRY PI 4
on edge devices with limited storage and memory resources.
The power evaluation further indicates that the additional energy overhead of
deploying the TCN TFLite model is minimal on the Raspberry Pi 4, even when
handling different feature dimensions (4 in the MQTT-based dataset versus 13
in the UDP-based dataset). The model maintains efficiency across MQTT and
UDP-based H-IoT traffic, supporting its feasibility for deployment in IoT, H-IoT,
and IIoT environments.
However, a direct comparison of energy consumption with existing methods
is not included because power measurements for those methods are not reported
under the same Raspberry Pi 4 setup [4], [7], [391], [393]. As a result, compari-
son under identical experimental conditions is not feasible. Therefore, this study
evaluates the energy consumption of the lightweight TCN using both MQTT and
UDP-based datasets under a consistent Raspberry Pi 4 deployment setting. Input
characteristics, such as feature complexity and data processing requirements, can
influence power consumption. Additionally, compared to conventional deep learn-
ing models such as CNN-BiLSTM and LSTM, the lightweight TCN uses fewer
parameters and reduced computational complexity, supporting its suitability for
energy-efficient deployment.
5.6 Conclusion
This chapter adopts a previously proposed TCN model and investigates its de-
ployment on low-power edge devices for DDoS detection in IoT, H-IoT, and IIoT
environments. The model, trained and evaluated on the UL-ECE-MQTT-DDoS-
H-IoT2025 and UL-ECE-UDP-DDoS-H-IoT2025 datasets, achieves high accu-
racy in identifying DDoS attacks. Furthermore, conversion to the TFLite format
enables lightweight deployment on resource-constrained edge devices (e.g., Rasp-
berry Pi 4). Through quantisation, the model maintains high detection accuracy
while reducing both latency and model size. The deployment of the TCN TFLite
model on the Raspberry Pi 4 demonstrates low, stable power consumption during
inference across both datasets, highlighting its feasibility for resource-constrained
edge environments within the scope of the study. The deployment evaluation also
encompasses public IoT and IIoT datasets, enabling a broader, more represen-
tative assessment across multiple deployment domains. Future work involves
deploying the ∼89.1 KB INT8 TCN on resource-constrained IoT devices (e.g.,
184

5.6 Conclusion
Raspberry Pi Pico and ESP32) using TensorFlow Lite for Microcontrollers. Ad-
ditionally, future work will evaluate the model on multiclass datasets and ex-
plore alternative, lightweight architectures to enhance performance and resilience
against emerging cyber attacks. This research is limited to a single edge platform
(Raspberry Pi 4), which may limit the direct generalizability of the deployment
results to other hardware platforms.
