Preamble
In this chapter, RQ5 is addressed:
RQ5: How can deep learning models such as Residual-TCN be utilised for
multiclass classification to distinguish among multiple attack categories, including
SF, MITM, and DDoS, in H-IoT environments?
Building on the edge deployment of the TCN model for binary DDoS attack
detection on a resource-constrained Raspberry Pi 4 in the previous chapter, which
addressed RQ4, this research extends the investigation to multiclass cyber threat
classification among heterogeneous attack types in H-IoT environments.
This chapter contributes to the overarching research question by leveraging
distinct data transmission behaviours of normal nodes and the malicious activi-
ties associated with multiple cyber threats (e.g., SF, MITM, and DDoS), thereby
enabling the generation of a realistic dataset. This dataset supports the eval-
uation of lightweight DL models for multiclass cyber threat detection in H-IoT
environments.
The primary focus of this chapter is to utilise a Res-TCN model for mul-
ticlass classification across diverse cyber threats in H-IoT environments. With
respect to RQ5, this research investigates how the Res-TCN model can distin-
guish among multiple emerging cyber threats, including SF, MITM, and DDoS,
within a unified H-IoT topology. A realistic setup is developed in the Cooja
simulator, comprising 24 H-IoT nodes and one server node, where 14 nodes act
as normal and 10 nodes perform different attack behaviours (SF: 3, MITM: 4,
DDoS: 3). During the simulation, normal nodes transmit data at one-packet-
per-60-second intervals, while DDoS nodes transmit at one-packet-per-20-second
intervals. SF nodes randomly drop approximately 50% of packets every 60 sec-
onds, and MITM nodes modify payloads at 60-second intervals. Accordingly, as
the simulation relies solely on synthetic network traffic derived from physiological
and network parameters, GDPR does not apply to this research.
Although several existing studies have applied ML and DL models with
promising results, they often overlook realistic simulation conditions and
protocol-specific variations that affect model reliability. This research addresses
this critical gap by developing a realistic simulation setup tailored for precision
healthcare, ensuring that the generated traffic accurately reflects real-world
communication behaviours in H-IoT systems. The dataset is generated through a
187

6. RESIDUAL TEMPORAL CNNs FOR EMERGING CYBER
THREAT DETECTION IN H-IoT
statistical feature extraction process that integrates heterogeneous physiological
and network-layer parameters, enabling a detailed representation of attack
patterns in H-IoT environments. The resulting dataset, UL-ECE-MultiAttack-
H-IoT2025, is then used to train and evaluate the Res-TCN model for multiclass
attack classification in H-IoT environments.
The SMOTE is applied to balance class distribution and minimise the risk of
overfitting during model development. The model is also validated using publicly
available datasets, including CICIoMT2024, CICIoT2023, and IoMT-TrafficData
for external evaluation. The Res-TCN demonstrates improved performance com-
pared to rule-based DT, CNN, and 1D-CLSTM models. For instance, the rule-
based DT correctly classifies Normal (class 0), MITM (class 2), and DDoS (class
3) samples. In contrast, it fails to detect Selective Forwarding (class 1) attacks,
misclassifying all of them as Normal.
6.1 Abstract
The rapid advancement of IoT technologies has accelerated the emergence of H-
IoT systems. These systems rely on wearable devices to monitor patient vitals
and enable timely alerts in precision healthcare settings. Despite these bene-
fits, a single H-IoT network topology might be exposed to multiple simultaneous
threats, particularly those attacks designed to manipulate medical sensor data
at the application layer. This poses significant challenges for real-time detec-
tion and classification of diverse attack behaviours. To address this, a realistic
application-layer attack model is developed using the Cooja simulator, modelling
H-IoT nodes that track body temperature, oxygen level, and heart rate under
concurrent SF, MITM, and DDoS attacks. Based on this setup, a dataset is gen-
erated to train the proposed DL model. This research proposes a DL model, a
Res-TCN, designed to classify multiclass attacks while maintaining low latency
per sample in H-IoT environments. It also uses the SMOTE during training to
mitigate class imbalance and reduce overfitting. The proposed model achieves a
high classification accuracy of 99.32% and outperforms traditional ML and DL
methods. This demonstrates its effectiveness in real-time decision-making for se-
curing H-IoT systems. Based on these findings, the Res-TCN model is potentially
well-suited for deployment in resource-constrained H-IoT environments.
188

6.2 Introduction
6.2 Introduction
The advancement of a smart and sustainable healthcare ecosystem relies on the
security of infrastructures powered by the IIoT [1]. The IoT comprises intercon-
nected physical devices that communicate through embedded sensors, software,
and wireless communication technologies [1], [429]. In recent years, implementing
IoT has enabled the vision to achieve personalised and precision healthcare [430].
The growing use of wearable H-IoT devices has made real-time monitoring of crit-
ical physiological parameters, such as body temperature, heart rate, and oxygen
saturation, practically achievable within digital healthcare systems. Among the
key components of digital healthcare systems, WBANs are central in enabling
continuous monitoring for remote healthcare applications [431], [432]. Typically,
wearable H-IoT devices generate physiological data at fine temporal granularity
(e.g., millisecond-level intervals) [433], which is transmitted to the application
layer for timely access and review by physicians or patients [434]. Moreover,
these devices communicate using various protocols, including MQTT, UDP, TCP,
ICMP, and others, depending on system requirements and application-specific
needs [435].
However, despite the benefits of continuous health monitoring enabled by the
enhanced speed and low latency of 5G networks, the application layer remains
highly vulnerable in H-IoT environments. It handles and visualises sensitive
health data, making it a frequent target of cyber attacks [436]. Such attacks often
take the form of SF, MITM, and DDoS, particularly in healthcare settings [437],
[438]. In SF attacks, critical health packets are intentionally dropped, disrupting
the data transmission flow [439]. In MITM attacks, an attacker silently inter-
cepts and manipulates communications to access or tamper with health data ex-
changed between H-IoT devices, such as body temperature readings [438], [440].
One common technique is to send forged Address Resolution Protocol (ARP)
spoofing messages that associate the attacker’s MAC address with the IP address
of a legitimate device, effectively enabling IP spoofing [438]. This allows the at-
tacker to intercept, alter, or block communications between the H-IoT nodes [441].
Continuing the attack chain within the same topology, a DDoS attack may over-
whelm the network and disrupt the operation of the H-IoT system, as illustrated
in Figure 6.1. Thus, building cyber resilience and defence capabilities remains a
significant challenge amid the evolving threat landscape in H-IoT systems [387].
189

6. RESIDUAL TEMPORAL CNNs FOR EMERGING CYBER
THREAT DETECTION IN H-IoT
Figure 6.1: The diagram illustrates potential attacks and data flow disruptions
in 5G-based H-IoT environments. It highlights DDoS attacks causing “Over-
loaded Data,” Selective Forwarding Attacks leading to “Dropped Packet/Data,”
and MITM attacks resulting in “Stolen Data.” The impacts of these attacks, in-
cluding Blocked Data, Partial Data, and Tampered Data, affect the server, edge
computing, application layer, and H-IoT cloud levels.
Various standards for securing healthcare have been developed by standard-
isation bodies (i.e., ISO/IEC, ETSI). A comprehensive list of the standards
has been provided in [429] that provide security assurance (ISO/IEC 15443),
information security for access controls (ISO/IEC 15816), security requirements
for cryptographic modules (ISO/IEC 19790), anonymous digital signatures
(ISO/IEC 20008), privacy (ISO/IEC 20889), vulnerability disclosure and man-
agement (ISO/IEC 29147 and 30111), Identity management and discovery for
IoT (ETSI DTS/CYBER 0014 (TS 103 486)), redaction of authenticated data
(ISO/IEC DIS 23264-1), privacy and verification (ETSI DTS/CYBER-0013 (TS
103 485)), etc. [429], [442]. Although the standards provide a strong baseline
for assessing security measures, they alone would not be sufficient to counter
the emerging threats, anomalies, and supply-chain risks arising from AI in the
H-IoT ecosystem [443]. Hence, to protect, a similar AI-based approach would
be required as a defence. The ENISA has also enacted regulations (i.e., EU AI
Act, General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), Data Act, Data Governance
190

6.2 Introduction
Act, NIS2D) and frameworks (European Health Data Space (EHDS)) for setting
clear guidelines for the usage of AI in the healthcare environment, building trust
and cyber resilience in the infrastructure [387], [444].
Besides this, the usage of stronger controls (i.e., encrypting IoT data) has
been widely implemented in healthcare sectors, fostering data confidentiality and
ensuring data is secure in all three states (in transit, in storage, and while in use)
[1]. However, encryption alone cannot mitigate attacks (e.g., DDoS attacks). It
is also important to note that IoT devices travel via the server, where the data
is encrypted, and the server checks (decrypts) every message sent in, requiring
additional memory and computing power [436]. In a DDoS scenario, where the
server is already flooded with messages, this adds extra load to the environment.
This is why it is essential to have other mitigation techniques (i.e., throttling,
auto-scaling, anomaly detection, and prevention) aligned with it. Besides this,
DDoS attacks rely on traffic frequency and volume, which can potentially exploit
weak endpoints on the edge by sending recursive messages and flooding APIs or
the MQTT broker with requests. Hence, a unique AI-based approach is required
to detect and prevent malicious attacks and anomalies in H-IoT [445].
To address the above security issues, this research conducts a simulation-based
experiment that emulates H-IoT nodes, such as body temperature, oxygen level,
and heart rate sensors, communicating over the UDP protocol using the Cooja
simulator within an H-IoT environment. In this simulation, an attack model is
integrated at the application layer, where three attacks (SF, MITM, and DDoS)
occur concurrently in the same H-IoT topology. The included sophisticated attack
types are relevant to H-IoT environments, as these target common vulnerabilities
in health data transmission, interception, and forwarding processes. During the
simulation, malicious and normal nodes transmit data simultaneously, reflect-
ing a realistic attack scenario in real-time healthcare systems. This simulation
approach also complies with privacy regulations (e.g., GDPR and EHDS) by gen-
erating simulated or synthetic healthcare traffic rather than sensitive personal
data [387], [444]. Subsequently, the simulated traffic is collected and analysed to
extract relevant features for training a DL model for multiclass classification.
Several DL and ML models have been explored in previous studies for anomaly
detection using both binary and multiclass classification in general IoT and H-IoT
environments. Among these, CNNs and Bi-LSTM Networks [5], [446], LSTM Net-
works [4], [447], and DT [13] have gained increasing attention due to their ability
191

6. RESIDUAL TEMPORAL CNNs FOR EMERGING CYBER
THREAT DETECTION IN H-IoT
to capture temporal and spatial features in various IoT datasets. In this thesis,
a TCN-based architecture is developed for DDoS detection and mitigation, with
methodological details presented in Chapter 4. However, multiclass classification
in H-IoT often involves highly imbalanced datasets, where certain attack types
have significantly fewer samples than benign classes. This is frequently expected,
as one or two specific attack types may naturally generate fewer transmissions
than H-IoT devices’ regular activity. As a result, predictive ML or DL models may
favour the majority classes, potentially reducing overall detection performance.
In such cases, the SMOTE is crucial for synthetically generating samples for mi-
nority classes, thereby improving detection performance and reducing overfitting
during training.
However, practical solutions should also address regulatory obligations out-
lined by ENISA and the NIS2D, which emphasise the continuous operation of
healthcare services under cyber attacks [448], [449]. In response to these man-
dates, this study proposes a DL model, Res-TCN, to classify multiclass attacks
in H-IoT environments. From a GDPR perspective, ENISA highlights challenges
such as data localisation, cross-border data transfers, consent mechanisms, and
the right to erasure, all of which become more complex in cloud-based healthcare
systems [450], [451]. In the proposed IDS workflow, regulatory requirements are
practically integrated by processing simulated healthcare network traffic entirely
within a local environment, without storing, transmitting, or accessing any real
patient data. As the proposed approach uses simulated network traffic with-
out any personal or patient-identifiable data, the GDPR does not apply to this
research. This approach inherently embeds privacy preservation and data pro-
tection into the IDS design. Beyond compliance, the model ensures low latency
and efficient resource use, supporting the ENISA and NIS2D mandates for re-
silient and uninterrupted healthcare operations during cyber attacks. A realistic
dataset is developed from simulated H-IoT scenarios encompassing both regu-
lar and malicious traffic. The Res-TCN model is trained and evaluated on this
dataset, which captures realistic traffic patterns while complying with GDPR
and EHDS. Specifically, this chapter emphasises achieving consistent detection
performance while minimising overfitting using SMOTE and demonstrating low
per-sample latency in an H-IoT environment. Maintaining this balance is crucial
for supporting real-time decision-making in continuous health monitoring scenar-
ios. The key contributions of this research are as follows:
192

6.2 Introduction
• This research presents a realistic and practical experiment by emulating H-
IoT nodes such as body temperature, oxygen level, and heart rate sensors
under three sophisticated concurrent attacks (SF, MITM, and DDoS) within
a unified precision healthcare topology using the Cooja simulator.
• To the best of current knowledge, this work presents an open-access multi-
class H-IoT dataset, UL-ECE-MultiAttack-H-IoT2025, containing both reg-
ular and malicious traffic generated by simulated attacks. Unlike existing
multiclass IoT datasets that primarily focus on network-level traffic, the pro-
posed dataset integrates heterogeneous physiological and network-layer pa-
rameters through a rolling-window statistical feature-extraction approach.
This enables detailed profiling of attack patterns in H-IoT environments.
The dataset is publicly available in the Zenodo repository [452].
• This research introduces a novel DL model, Res–TCN, for multiclass attack
detection in H-IoT environments. The model integrates residual connec-
tions and temporal convolutions with systematically selected kernel sizes,
residual depths, dropout rates, and dense layer sizes, designed explicitly
for resource-constrained settings. It achieves state-of-the-art performance
on the proposed dataset, UL-ECE-MultiAttack-H-IoT2025, for multiclass
classification (99.32% accuracy), while maintaining a low per-sample infer-
ence time among existing methods. The model also demonstrates reduced
complexity, with the lowest parameter count among the compared DL ar-
chitectures. Its low complexity makes the model potentially suitable for
edge deployment on H-IoT devices in real-world settings.
This chapter is organised as follows. Section 6.3 reviews related work on
multiclass attack classification in H-IoT environments. Section 6.4 describes the
simulation setup, development of three attacks in the same H-IoT topology, and
details on data collection, preprocessing, and analysis to create the proposed
dataset. Section 6.5 outlines the proposed Res-TCN model for efficient multiclass
attack classification in H-IoT systems, includes an ablation study to select the
suitable model, and presents the design rationale for its configuration. Section 6.6
presents a comparison with existing work and shows results on inference time,
memory usage, confusion matrix, and evaluation of the proposed Res-TCN on
public datasets. Finally, Section 6.7 summarises the work and discusses future
research directions.
193

6. RESIDUAL TEMPORAL CNNs FOR EMERGING CYBER
THREAT DETECTION IN H-IoT
6.3 Related Work
This section discusses existing anomaly and intrusion detection methods in H-
IoT and general IoT settings, targeting various attack types (e.g., DoS/DDoS,
SF, and MITM). It also compares benchmark methods in IoMT environments,
focusing on landmark studies [4], [13], [453] to highlight differences in scope,
dataset development, and real-time feasibility (such as computational and re-
source demands). An often-overlooked aspect in previous studies is the evalua-
tion of inference time, which is critical for real-time H-IoT applications. Several
ML and DL models report high accuracy, yet the applicability of these models
for time-sensitive H-IoT scenarios remains unclear without latency analysis. An-
other key issue is adversarial robustness, defined as a model’s capacity to process
intentionally manipulated inputs correctly [454]. Based on existing literature,
adversarial robustness remains largely unaddressed in IoMT intrusion detection
studies [4], [13], [455], [456]. This issue is further compounded by the contin-
ued reliance on public datasets (e.g., CIC-IDS2017, BoT-IoT), which restricts
the realism of H-IoT research. This research addresses these gaps by focusing on
latency evaluation and creating realistic datasets with three attacks (SF, MITM,
and DDoS), as detailed in Sections 6.4 and 6.6.3. Adversarial robustness is identi-
fied as an open direction for future work, as this chapter focuses on H-IoT realism,
dataset development, and latency evaluation rather than assessing such defence
techniques. Table 6.1 synthesises recent studies by comparing their methods,
attack coverage, and datasets, and highlights reported limitations. It also pro-
vides analytical insights from the critical observations on aspects such as dataset
imbalance, confusion-matrix analysis, latency evaluation, adversarial robustness,
and other key architectural elements.
Kumari et al. [455] propose a DL approach based on Variational Autoen-
coders (VAEs) to detect cyber threats in H-IoT environments. The approach,
evaluated using real-time data from the WUSTL-EHMS system, achieves a de-
tection accuracy of 91.61%, which is relatively lower than several recent H-IoT
IDS frameworks employing temporal convolution or hybrid DL that surpass 95%
accuracy. The study also includes comparisons with traditional ML models, such
as SVMs, XGBoost, and Random Forest. However, the evaluation omits recent
DL models, such as TCN-based architectures, which are effective in capturing
time-dependent traffic variations and inter-sensor behaviour in H-IoT systems.
194

6.3 Related Work
While the effectiveness of the VAE-based method in identifying MITM attacks is
highlighted, a key concern is the need for rapid detection to prevent patient risk
in H-IoT systems. Nevertheless, the study does not evaluate inference latency,
limiting insight into its responsiveness in time-sensitive environments.
Akar et al. [4] present an advanced LSTM-based intrusion detection model
specifically designed for IoMT environments. The model is evaluated on the CI-
CIoMT2024 dataset and achieves a high classification accuracy of 98%, demon-
strating the potential of DL for enhancing IoMT security. However, despite its
promising results, the model incurs high computational overhead, making it less
suitable even for binary detection tasks in resource-constrained IoMT environ-
ments. Moreover, the study lacks a simulation-based attack model and an in-
depth evaluation of IoMT-specific threat scenarios, which limits its applicability
in real-world H-IoT contexts. Simulated attacks are crucial for gaining a compre-
hensive understanding of H-IoT systems, as they enable researchers to analyse
system behaviour and identify vulnerabilities of medical sensors under threat
conditions [460]. Such insights are vital for developing lightweight, responsive,
and practically deployable ML and DL-based intrusion detection mechanisms in
H-IoT environments. Furthermore, the study does not examine adversarial ro-
bustness, a key aspect for assessing the model’s resilience under worst-case IoMT
attack conditions.
A monitoring frequency-based detection and dynamic threshold mitigation
approach using TCNs to secure 5G H-IoT environments is developed in this thesis,
as detailed in Chapter 4. The method evaluates each H-IoT node’s message
count as a percentage of the total traffic and dynamically adjusts detection and
mitigation thresholds. Two datasets, UL-ECE-MQTT-DDoS-H-IoT2025 and UL-
ECE-UDP-DDoS-H-IoT2025, are generated using MQTT and UDP protocols via
Cooja and ns-3 simulators in a 5G H-IoT setup, as described in Chapter 3. The
approach achieves 99.99% accuracy with TCN, demonstrating its effectiveness in
securing H-IoT systems. However, it primarily focuses on binary classification and
a single DDoS attack. The study does not examine robustness against adversarial
evasion attempts, which are increasingly critical for maintaining reliable intrusion
detection under evolving H-IoT threat conditions.
Kumar et al. [457] propose a DL-based model for detecting cyber attacks
and authenticating IoMT data; however, the approach largely focuses on data
authenticity rather than comprehensive attack characterisation in healthcare en-
