DDoS behavior [116]. Therefore, this setup emulates both typical low-frequency
medical sensors and the high-intensity communication bursts observed during
malicious activity in H-IoT networks, with the corresponding attack intensity
and attempted transmission rates summarised in Table 6.3. This variation in
transmission frequency also provides distinguishable temporal patterns crucial
for ML/DL model training and performance evaluation. The corresponding sim-
ulation scripts and configurations illustrating these intensity settings are publicly
available in the referenced GitHub repository [475]. The multiple malicious nodes
are categorised as follows:
• SF-affected nodes (IDs 16–18) intentionally drop selected packets during
forwarding to cause data loss,
• MITM-affected nodes (IDs 19–22) transmit modified data at the same
interval as normal nodes, and
• DDoS-affected nodes (IDs 23–25) transmit data every 30 seconds, emu-
lating the rapid request bursts typical of real-world flooding attacks.
