trate how model type, depth, and complexity shape predictive behaviour. This
232

6.6 Performance Evaluation
analysis supports the selection of balanced depth and capacity for the proposed
Res-TCN model. Table 6.9 summarises the effect of DT depth on performance.
To further illustrate interpretability, a rule-based DT is applied with con-
trolled parameters to balance model capacity and generalisation. At max depth
= 3, the DT achieves 100% accuracy, indicating overfitting. When the depth is
reduced to max depth = 2 with min samples leaf = 3, the accuracy decreases
to 96.97%, with macro-precision, recall, and F1-scores of 73.73%, 75.00%, and
74.35%, respectively. The DT produces human-readable if–then rules that ex-
plicitly describe its decision logic, providing a transparent understanding of how
predictions are made. The resulting rules are based on packet drop rate and
temperature max same node, which the DT identifies as the most discriminative
features based on information gain, defined as the reduction in class impurity
achieved at each split. For instance, if packet drop rate≤ 1.487 then class =
3 (DDoS) and if packet drop rate> 1.487 and temperature max same node≤
103 then class = 0 (Normal). These rules illustrate interpretable decision bound-
aries that map feature conditions to class outcomes. The rule-based DT cor-
rectly identifies Normal (class 0), MITM (class 2), and DDoS (class 3) instances,
whereas all Selective Forwarding (class 1) samples are misclassified as Normal.
This misclassification likely arises from overlapping feature patterns and simi-
lar transmission characteristics between Normal and Selective Forwarding nodes.
These results indicate that packet drop rate and temperature max same node
provide effective discrimination for MITM and DDoS, while offering limited sepa-
rability for SF attacks, which exhibit near-normal packet characteristics. Hence,
the rule-based DT model demonstrates limited effectiveness for multiclass scenar-
ios, reinforcing the need for a higher-capacity model such as Res-TCN that can
learn non-linear decision boundaries and temporal patterns. The DT’s inability
to distinguish between critical threats and normal H-IoT behaviour highlights
the limitations of simple rule-based approaches. It emphasises the importance of
balanced model complexity for handling diverse H-IoT attack behaviours.
In the evaluation, the 1D-CLSTM model is implemented according to
the design described in [457]. The architecture combines an initial causal
one-dimensional convolutional layer with 32 filters (kernel size 3) and a
128-unit LSTM layer, followed by dense layers and dropout. This deeper
recurrent–convolutional design increases model capacity compared to lightweight
ML models such as Decision Trees, enabling it to capture temporal dependencies
