6.6.4 Res-TCN Performance on Public H-IoT Datasets
The proposed Res-TCN model is evaluated on publicly available datasets to as-
sess its performance and generalizability in H-IoT environments. Three recent
datasets, CICIoMT2024, IoMT-TrafficData, and CICIDS2023, are employed for
multiclass classification. A separate binary classification dataset is also derived
from CICIoMT2024 by extracting ICMP-based DDoS attacks and benign traffic.
The multiclass and binary versions of the CICIoMT2024 dataset are referred to
as CICIoMT2024-Multiclass (MC) and CICIoMT2024-Binary (BI), respectively.
These datasets provide multiple testbeds for evaluating the model’s performance
and generalizability in both multiclass and binary settings involving protocol-
centric threats.
The CICIoMT2024-MC dataset comprises traffic samples categorised into BE-
NIGN, DDoS-TCP, DDoS-ICMP, and ARP spoofing classes, selected from the
well-known CICIoMT2024 dataset [298]. This dataset is chosen for its relevance
to protocol-centric multiclass classification in H-IoT environments.
6.6.4.1 CICIoMT2024-MC Dataset
The CICIoMT2024-MC dataset, derived from the CICIoMT2024 dataset [298], is
chosen for its relevance to protocol-centric multiclass classification. This dataset
comprises traffic generated by TCP, ICMP, and ARP communication, specifically
selected to assess the proposed Res-TCN model’s performance in distinguishing
protocol-specific attacks in H-IoT environments [504]. The dataset includes traffic
representing BENIGN, DDoS-TCP, DDoS-ICMP, and ARP spoofing scenarios.
Each traffic type is assigned a custom label to form a balanced multiclass dataset
suitable for H-IoT environments. Among these, the volumetric (DDoS) and ARP
spoofing (MITM) classes are particularly aligned with the attack types considered
in the proposed UDP-based dataset. Thus, this evaluation incorporates additional
protocol-specific traffic, providing a broader context for assessing the Res-TCN
model’s performance in H-IoT environments.
Several preprocessing steps are applied to prepare the protocol-centric mul-
ticlass dataset for Res-TCN classification. Label encoding is initially performed
using a custom mapping to convert categorical labels into numerical form. The
dataset is then separated into two parts: the information used for making predic-
tions (e.g., protocol type, packet size, and flow duration), and the actual category
