6.6.5 Protocol Impact on Res-TCN Performance in H-IoT
This research evaluates protocol-specific classification performance using two
multiclass H-IoT datasets: the publicly available CICIoMT2024 and a UDP-based
proposed dataset, UL-ECE-MultiAttack-H-IoT2025. The proposed Res-TCN
model achieves an overall accuracy of 98.82% on CICIoMT2024 and 99.32% on
the proposed UL-ECE-MultiAttack-H-IoT2025 multiclass dataset, as outlined in
Table 6.11. However, this research further examines class-wise metrics in both
datasets to identify potential causes of performance variation, such as protocol
characteristics, data distribution, or feature relevance. Detailed information on
the class labels and features used in the CICIoMT2024 dataset is provided in
Section 6.6.4.1. The model achieves 100% accuracy, precision, and recall for
DDoS-TCP and DDoS-ICMP classes on the CICIoMT2024 dataset, indicating
that attack patterns significantly influence detection performance, as shown in
Table 6.13. Similarly, the proposed UL-ECE-MultiAttack-H-IoT2025 dataset
demonstrates high detection accuracy for UDP-based DDoS classes, as shown in
Table 6.14. While the nature of the attack (e.g., DDoS flooding) often shapes
traffic behaviour, such as high packet rates, protocol-specific communication
patterns can also impact detection performance. A key distinction is whether a
protocol is connection-oriented (e.g., TCP and MQTT [508]) or connectionless
(e.g., UDP and ICMP [509]). These structural differences determine whether
the protocol keeps a session active and how it manages starting and ending
communication between endpoints. Despite these protocol differences, DDoS
flooding attacks across TCP, UDP, and ICMP demonstrate similar behavioural
patterns, including repetitive messaging, high traffic volumes, and resource
exhaustion [499], [510]. Therefore, it is not the protocol alone; instead, how it
is exploited during a DDoS flooding attack influences its traffic behaviour. The
temporal convolutional architecture effectively learns these patterns, enabling
more accurate detection.
In contrast, the model achieves only 75% accuracy on the ARP spoofing
class, with low precision (0.16) and F1-score (0.27), as shown in Table 6.13.
Unlike flooding-based attacks, which generate easily distinguishable traffic pat-
terns, ARP spoofing attacks operate at the data link layer (Layer 2 of the OSI
model) [511], which is responsible for node-to-node communication within a lo-
cal network. These attacks involve sending falsified ARP responses to associate a
