studies have also highlighted the challenges in detecting ARP-based attacks due
to the similarity between malicious and benign ARP traffic [512]. Despite these
challenges, the proposed model achieves 99.90% accuracy on the MITM class
over the UDP-based dataset in H-IoT environments, as outlined in Table 6.14.
This suggests that with effective data preprocessing and relevant feature extrac-
tion, ML/DL models can still perform well even on subtle attack types such as
ARP-based MITM.
Moreover, the variation in classification performance across these protocols in-
dicates that accuracy is not solely determined by model architecture or training
method. Instead, it depends on several interrelated aspects, such as protocol-
aware features [513], input data quality [514], and traffic-level distinguishabil-
ity [515].
6.6.6 Discussion on Applicability and Usability in H-IoT
This section discusses the applicability and usability of the proposed Res-TCN
method in H-IoT. It highlights how the method and the underlying simulation
249

6. RESIDUAL TEMPORAL CNNs FOR EMERGING CYBER
THREAT DETECTION IN H-IoT
setup address security challenges and operational constraints specific to real-
world precision-healthcare environments. As described in Section 6.4.1, simulated
application-layer attack scenarios emulate WBAN H-IoT sensor communication
and encompass three critical attacks (SF, MITM, and DDoS). The simulation
reproduces typical H-IoT traffic using the configured transmission intervals (nor-
mal vs. malicious node frequencies), along with the exchange of health met-
rics between simulated nodes. Accordingly, the UL-ECE-MultiAttack-H-IoT2025
dataset is used to train and evaluate the proposed model under these simulated H-
IoT settings. Wearable devices in such environments face two key constraints: low
latency and minimal memory consumption [516]. The Res-TCN model addresses
these constraints, enabling efficient processing of sensor data while maintaining
reliable performance for latency-sensitive clinical monitoring.
In addition, the simulation includes attacks that maintain regular transmission
intervals, particularly SF and MITM, which drop or alter packets; the proposed
Res-TCN model classifies these behaviours. This suggests that the proposed
model has the capacity to generalise across different attack patterns and trans-
mission behaviours in H-IoT settings. In this research, the external validation
demonstrates that the domain shift between general IoT and IoMT traffic leads
to degraded overall performance, despite perfect recall. However, this chapter
presents pre-deployment research; the proposed model can be post-deployment
fine-tuned on additional emerging attack patterns beyond SF, MITM, and DDoS
by updating the final classification layer while retaining previously learned knowl-
edge (i.e., applying a transfer learning approach) [517]. This approach minimises
the requirement for retraining the entire model on edge devices, thereby mitigat-
ing the computational overhead inherent in H-IoT deployments. Nevertheless,
deploying the proposed Res-TCN model across diverse H-IoT infrastructures re-
quires rigorous evaluation of device heterogeneity, supported protocols, secure
model updates (e.g., authenticated Over-the-Air (OTA) updates), and regula-
tory requirements (e.g., GDPR) to ensure compliance.
6.7 Conclusion
This research proposes a novel DL method, Res-TCN, for classifying multiple
attacks on the proposed dataset UL-ECE-MultiAttack-H-IoT2025, in H-IoT en-
vironments. The model achieves consistent accuracy (99.32%) in detecting three
250

6.7 Conclusion
concurrent attacks: SF, MITM, and DDoS, which transmit data simultaneously
within the same H-IoT topology. It also demonstrates low inference latency,
which is essential for real-time healthcare applications.
However, the proposed dataset focuses on H-IoT scenarios, which limits the
scope and may affect how well the model generalises to other IoT environments.
Therefore, additional testing across varied IoT settings, such as different devices,
protocols, and network conditions, including latency, jitter, and packet loss, is
required to validate the model’s robustness and generalisation beyond H-IoT.
Adversarial robustness is not addressed in this research; future work involves
evaluating the model on evasive or modified attack traffic (e.g., malicious nodes
imitating normal behaviour or novel attack variants in the Cooja simulator) to
assess the model’s resilience to intentionally manipulated data. This also includes
deploying the proposed Res-TCN model on diverse edge hardware platforms (e.g.,
Raspberry Pi variants and microcontrollers) and systematically measuring its
performance (e.g., latency and energy usage) to compare these metrics across
edge devices. Such real-time attack detection on resource-constrained platforms
could help safeguard sensitive patient data and critical medical services in H-IoT
environments.
251

7
Conclusions
This thesis addresses one overarching research question supported by five inter-
connected RQs focused on securing H-IoT systems.
Main RQ: How can differences in data transmission behaviour between
normal and malicious nodes support the development of realistic datasets and
lightweight deep learning models for detecting and mitigating emerging cyber
threats in H-IoT environments?
Firstly, it investigates H-IoT traffic transmission behaviours, including
protocol-specific data patterns, which are essential for creating realistic datasets.
Secondly, it examines how these datasets enhance detection performance in
DL-based models by developing a TCN for binary classification in H-IoT. It
then evaluates the lightweight TCN model on Raspberry Pi 4 to demonstrate its
feasibility for edge deployment. Finally, it proposes a Res-TCN-based solution
for robust multiclass detection, covering MITM, SF, and DDoS attacks in H-IoT
environments. The key outcomes of this research are summarised with respect
to the overarching RQ and its supporting research questions:
RQ1: What key challenges and research gaps are identified in exist-
ing literature on ML/DL-based cyber attack detection in H-IoT sys-
tems, and how can a conceptual deep learning framework address these
limitations?
Chapter 2 of this thesis provides a comprehensive review of existing ap-
proaches for securing H-IoT systems, identifying several key research gaps, includ-
ing the lack of realistic simulations of inter-device communication activities, the
scarcity of domain-specific datasets for training and validation, and the need for
252

resilient anomaly detection and mitigation mechanisms. With respect to RQ1,
the review also introduces a CNN-oriented approach to strengthen DL applica-
tions for secure and efficient H-IoT systems. A simplified CNN implementation
based on this concept uses environmental sensor data (e.g., humidity) to demon-
strate its practical value in H-IoT [306].
RQ2: How can realistic synthetic datasets be generated to represent
DDoS attack behaviours in H-IoT environments?
Chapter 3 of this thesis addresses this research question by developing two re-
alistic DDoS datasets that reflect the communication patterns of H-IoT devices.
The first dataset is generated using the Cooja simulator over the MQTT proto-
col, while the second is created using the ns-3 simulator over the UDP protocol.
Both datasets include normal and attack traffic, capturing realistic sensor com-
munication behaviour observed in H-IoT environments. The research also defines
a systematic data preprocessing pipeline and feature selection strategy to ensure
integrity, consistency, and usability for training ML/DL-based models. These
datasets collectively provide a reproducible foundation for evaluating detection
and mitigation mechanisms in protocol-specific H-IoT scenarios.
RQ3: Are deep learning techniques (e.g., TCN) capable of detect-
ing and mitigating critical cyber threats (e.g., DDoS) across H-IoT
systems?
Chapter 4 of this thesis addresses this question by employing a TCN model
to detect and mitigate DDoS attacks in 5G H-IoT environments. The model
is trained and evaluated using the MQTT and UDP-based datasets developed
earlier, integrating a frequency monitoring mechanism to identify DDoS-affected
H-IoT nodes. It further incorporates a dynamic threshold mechanism for real-
time mitigation, enabling automated blacklisting of detected nodes. The results
demonstrate that the TCN achieves high detection and mitigation accuracy across
both protocol-specific datasets, outperforming conventional DL models such as
CNN and Bi-LSTM.
RQ4: What approaches enable the edge deployment of deep learning
models (e.g., TCN) on resource-constrained devices (e.g., Raspberry
Pi 4) in H-IoT?
Chapter 5 of this thesis explores the feasibility of deploying a lightweight TCN
model for DDoS detection on an edge platform, Raspberry Pi 4. The model is
quantised and converted into TFLite format to reduce computational complex-
253

7. CONCLUSIONS
ity and model size, while maintaining detection accuracy and inference time.
Power consumption is also measured using a physical power meter to evaluate
energy consumption during real-time operation. The results demonstrate that
the lightweight TCN achieves low latency and energy efficiency, demonstrating
the feasibility of deploying DL models on resource-constrained H-IoT devices.
RQ5: How can deep learning models such as Res-TCN be utilised
for multiclass classification to distinguish among multiple attack cate-
gories, including SF, MITM, and DDoS, in H-IoT environments?
Chapter 6 of this thesis highlights multiclass cyber threat detection using a
Res-TCN model in H-IoT environments. A realistic simulation of multiple cy-
ber threats is developed on a unified H-IoT topology using the Cooja simulator.
It generates network traffic comprising normal and malicious activities across
three attack categories: SF, MITM, and DDoS. The Res-TCN integrates residual
and temporal convolutional layers to enhance feature learning and ensure stable
classification performance. Class imbalance is mitigated using SMOTE during
training. The results demonstrate that the model achieves high multiclass de-
tection accuracy and generalisation, highlighting its effectiveness in classifying
concurrent cyber threats in H-IoT environments.
7.1 Overarching Discussion on Securing H-IoT
This section presents an overarching discussion that unifies the key outcomes
of this research across dataset generation, DL–based detection, mitigation, and
edge deployment in H-IoT environments. It highlights how analysing differences
in data transmission behaviour between normal and malicious nodes led to the
development of realistic datasets and lightweight models capable of detecting,
mitigating, and classifying emerging cyber threats under resource constraints.
The discussion further connects these findings to the broader goal of enhanc-
ing cybersecurity resilience in H-IoT systems through practical, deployable DL
solutions.
The data transmission interval varies between 20 seconds and 30 seconds for
malicious H-IoT nodes across different simulations using the Cooja simulator.
It represents various levels of transmission aggressiveness, mimicking how at-
tack nodes may send data more frequently to overload the network, allowing the
dataset to capture both moderate and high-intensity malicious behaviours. As
254

7.1 Overarching Discussion on Securing H-IoT
a result, the ML/DL models are exposed to diverse traffic patterns and inter-
arrival times, improving their adaptability to real-world IoT attack dynamics.
In the unified simulated H-IoT topology, normal and SF nodes transmit at 60-
second intervals, whereas DDoS-affected nodes transmit at 30-second intervals.
This configuration ensures that malicious nodes generate roughly twice the traffic
of normal nodes, allowing balanced total message collection and sufficient attack
samples for dataset creation. It also helps the model learn stable temporal pat-
terns, as statistical features such as mean, standard deviation, minimum, and
maximum values become consistent and less affected by noise. This noise refers
to irregular fluctuations in packet timing and volume within the network topol-
ogy. By reducing such variability, the model can accurately identify changes in
network behaviour and distinguish between different attack intensities, whether
low or high.
With respect to RQ1 in Chapter 2, the review-based investigation identifies
critical challenges, including limited device-to-device interactions, a lack of real-
istic datasets, and barriers to privacy compliance. As part of that analysis, a con-
ceptual CNN-based anomaly-detection framework is introduced to demonstrate
how DL can be applied to secure and privacy-aware operations within H-IoT.
To support RQ1, a simplified CNN implementation is used to demonstrate the
feasibility of the proposed conceptual approach for anomaly detection in H-IoT
systems. Hence, this research builds on the conceptual insights from the review,
translating the introduced CNN-based anomaly detection method into a practical
validation. The insights derived from this work lead to the creation of realistic
H-IoT datasets addressing different research questions and support the design of
lightweight temporal DL models for cyber threat detection and mitigation.
In addressing RQ2 in Chapter 3, the analysis focuses on generating realistic
synthetic datasets for H-IoT environments in Chapter 3. In the MQTT-based
dataset UL-ECE-MQTT-DDoS-H-IoT2025, four relevant features, such as node
IDs, total messages, total messages per node, and frequency, are used, and the
TCN model achieves high performance. This finding suggests that a compact and
relevant feature set can effectively represent traffic behaviour in H-IoT systems.
The feature selection depends on the communication protocol and the network-
level parameters (e.g., inter-arrival time) accessible in the simulated H-IoT topol-
ogy. In MQTT-based DDoS scenarios, a higher transmission rate, reflected by
reduced inter-arrival time and increased packet counts, is a key indicator of ma-
255

7. CONCLUSIONS
licious behaviour. These parameters enable ML/DL models to capture temporal
dependencies between events over time and distinguish DDoS-related anomalies
in H-IoT network activity.
Regarding RQ3 in Chapter 4, this research focuses on the DL-based detec-
tion and mitigation of malicious nodes in H-IoT systems. While most existing
studies focus solely on anomaly detection, this research extends the investigation
to immediate mitigation after detection, ensuring continuous network stability.
In this context, mitigation involves isolating detected malicious nodes to prevent
further disruption while maintaining normal device communication.
The proposed TCN model detects DDoS-affected nodes by monitoring fre-
quency, where each node’s message activity is calculated over a five-second ob-
servation period as a proportion of total messages. Once detected, the malicious
nodes are automatically added to a blacklist for real-time mitigation in H-IoT
settings. Several mitigation strategies exist, including rule-based blocking, rate
limiting, and threshold-based adaptation. This research employs the dynamic
threshold approach to mitigate identified cyber threats, given its adaptive be-
haviour and computational efficiency. It is characterised by minimal processing
time and power consumption, making it suitable for resource-constrained H-IoT
environments. The key advantage of this approach lies in its ability to adjust the
mitigation threshold dynamically based on node-level activity, such as the num-
ber of detected malicious messages and overall network traffic behaviour. For
instance, in the MQTT-based dataset UL-ECE-MQTT-DDoS-H-IoT2025, the
base threshold is reduced by 20% to enhance mitigation accuracy while minimis-
ing false positives, ensuring that only actual DDoS-affected nodes are blacklisted.
This fine-tuning process demonstrates flexibility across different simulation sce-
narios using the Cooja and NS-3 simulators. Furthermore, Algorithm 1 illustrates
how the threshold is iteratively adjusted within a feedback loop to maintain ac-
curacy and responsiveness for real-time mitigation in H-IoT settings.
Concerning RQ4 in Chapter 5, this research deploys the TCN model on the
edge platform, Raspberry Pi 4, in H-IoT environments. On the MQTT-based
dataset UL-ECE-MQTT-DDoS-H-IoT2025, the original TCN model achieves
99.98% accuracy, whereas the converted TFLite model on the Raspberry Pi 4
maintains 99.95% accuracy. The latency is 0.19 ms on the edge device and
0.06 ms on Google Colab before conversion to TFLite. This minor trade-off is
offset by the reduction in parameters (63,617 → 63,136) and memory footprint
256

7.1 Overarching Discussion on Securing H-IoT
(248.5 KB → 89.1 KB), improving energy efficiency and deployment feasibility
under constrained resources. Specifically, the TCN model exhibits low average
power consumption, measured at 4.22 W for the UL-ECE-MQTT-DDoS-H-
IoT2025 dataset and 4.64 W for the UL-ECE-UDP-DDoS-H-IoT2025 dataset,
confirming its suitability for sustained real-time operation on edge devices.
As for RQ5 discussed in Chapter 6, this research develops the multiclass
dataset UL-ECE-MultiAttack-H-IoT2025, which includes normal and malicious
data from SF, MITM, and DDoS attacks. The dataset integrates diverse physio-
logical indicators (e.g., minimum and maximum body temperature) with network-
layer metrics (e.g., total message count), providing a comprehensive basis for
analysing different cyberattack behaviours in H-IoT environments. Although
physiological metrics are incorporated, the dataset is entirely synthetic, ensuring
that it remains outside the scope of GDPR constraints. The proposed Res-TCN
model is trained on this dataset to detect emerging cyber threats while maintain-
ing privacy-preserving and resource-efficient characteristics. These aspects align
with the ENISA and NIS2D objectives for resilient and uninterrupted healthcare
operations during cyber attacks.
Another point regarding RQ5 is that the Res-TCN model is finalised through
a systematic ablation study across eight configurations, varying the number of
residual blocks, the number of dense layer units, and the dropout rates. The study
also compares performance between SMOTE-balanced and original datasets, as
the data are inherently imbalanced due to the inclusion of diverse cyber threats
within a unified H-IoT network topology. The results indicate that applying
SMOTE balancing ensures consistent performance across all attack classes while
balancing accuracy, stability, and computational efficiency. Therefore, the Res-
TCN model demonstrates high efficacy in detecting emerging cyber threats within
H-IoT systems.
Therefore, this thesis addresses the overarching research question by integrat-
ing dataset generation, DL–based detection and mitigation, and edge deployment
for H-IoT environments. It analyses differences in message transmission pat-
terns between normal and malicious nodes and evaluates the proposed models on
resource-constrained edge devices. These contributions address the overarching
research question by linking dataset design, model development, and deployment
feasibility in H-IoT.
257

7. CONCLUSIONS
7.2 Key Contributions of the Thesis
The key contributions of this research are as follows:
• Creation of realistic H-IoT cybersecurity datasets: development
of two protocol-specific synthetic datasets, UL-ECE-MQTT-DDoS-H-
IoT2025 and UL-ECE-UDP-DDoS-H-IoT2025, for evaluating ML/DL-
based DDoS detection and mitigation methods in H-IoT environments. A
multiclass dataset, UL-ECE-MultiAttack-H-IoT2025, is also developed to
support broader cyber threat classification.
• DL model for DDoS detection and mitigation: design and implemen-
tation of a TCN model for detecting and mitigating DDoS attacks through
frequency monitoring and dynamic threshold-based adaptation in H-IoT
environments.
• Lightweight edge deployment of the TCN model: implementation
of the quantised TCN on Raspberry Pi 4 using TFLite, validated through
real-time latency and power consumption measurements to ensure efficiency
under constrained resources.
• Emerging cyber threat detection using Res-TCN in H-IoT: imple-
mentation of the Res-TCN model using the dataset UL-ECE-MultiAttack-
H-IoT2025 for classifying DDoS, SF, and MITM attacks. The model utilises
SMOTE to address data imbalance, achieving consistent and reliable clas-
sification performance across all attack types.
• Privacy-compliant and resource-efficient IDS workflow for H-IoT:
establishment of a DL-based IDS aligned with cyber resilience objectives.
It ensures that GDPR constraints do not apply, as the research is based on
synthetic data, thereby supporting the development of secure and sustain-
able H-IoT systems.
7.3 Future Work
This research demonstrates significant progress in addressing cybersecurity chal-
lenges within H-IoT systems. Despite the promising outcomes, several areas
258

7.3 Future Work
remain open for further investigation. The following points outline potential di-
rections for future research:
1. Expanding Threat Coverage and Adversarial Robustness: Extend
the current detection approach to identify a broader range of emerging cyber
threats in H-IoT, including 5G exploits (e.g., device or protocol-level vul-
nerabilities) and adversarial attacks that attempt to mislead ML/DL-based
DDoS detection methods. Detecting such adversarial behaviours requires
effective feature extraction, and this research already incorporates key fea-
tures, such as the 5-second monitoring frequency and mean frequency, for
identifying newly appearing malicious H-IoT nodes, as discussed in Chap-
ter 3. Nevertheless, detecting adversarial attacks may require introducing
additional features, such as packet inter-arrival variation, sudden changes in
traffic burst intensity, entropy (a measure of packet randomness) in packet
size and flow distribution, and correlations in traffic behaviours across mul-
tiple nodes to identify coordinated or disguised attack patterns. Future
work may focus on developing adaptive detection mechanisms that enhance
robustness against adversarial and 5G-related threats.
2. Integration of Proposed DL Methods with Software Defined Net-
working: SDN is a programmable networking paradigm that separates the
control plane from the data plane, enabling centralised and flexible traffic
management, as discussed in Chapter 2. Integrating the proposed DL-based
detection models (e.g., TCN) with SDN can facilitate automated network
reconfiguration, such as rerouting legitimate traffic, isolating compromised
nodes, and dynamically adjusting flow rules based on real-time detection
outcomes. For example, in this research, the lightweight TCN model, de-
ployed on edge devices such as a Raspberry Pi 4, can share DDoS detection
results with SDN controllers. This integration enables the H-IoT network
to automatically apply mitigation policies in response to detected mali-
cious nodes. It also enhances coordination between edge intelligence and
network control, improving responsiveness and overall system resilience.
Future work may utilise lightweight SDN simulation environments, such as
Mininet with the Ryu controller [518], to evaluate scalability and perfor-
mance within H-IoT networks.
259

7. CONCLUSIONS
3. Real-World Deployment and Energy Optimisation : As discussed
in Chapter 5, this thesis demonstrates edge deployment on Raspberry Pi 4;
future work can extend this setup using more realistic H-IoT traffic simula-
tions with sensor nodes transmitting to a central server. The deployed TCN
model then operates within this testbed to detect abnormal or suspicious
node behaviours in real time. This practical setup can be implemented
using Mininet to emulate H-IoT nodes, a Mosquitto broker for application
traffic, a Ryu SDN controller for mitigation, and a Raspberry Pi 4 running
the quantised TCN. This setup would enable evaluation of sustained de-
tection performance, stability, and power efficiency under near-operational
conditions in resource-constrained H-IoT environments.
4. Explainable and Trustworthy AI in H-IoT: Beyond the scope of this
thesis, future research can explore explainability techniques, such as Shap-
ley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) and Local Interpretable Model-agnostic
Explanations (LIME), to clarify which network features or temporal pat-
terns influence the detection outcomes of models such as TCN and Res-TCN
in H-IoT environments [519]. This approach extends beyond correlation by
examining how variations in key network features (e.g., packet rate) di-
rectly lead to malicious behaviours. For example, a sudden spike in packet
frequency may cause bandwidth saturation or trigger abnormal communi-
cation patterns. The objective is to understand how specific network be-
haviours influence the model’s classification results, enabling more straight-
forward interpretation of why particular traffic is identified as normal or
malicious. Future work can also incorporate human-in-the-loop validation,
where healthcare operators review model explanations and provide feedback
to help cybersecurity experts refine model behaviour in line with real-world
H-IoT network operations. This collaboration can further strengthen trust,
accountability, and the safe adoption of AI-driven security mechanisms in
practical H-IoT systems.
Overall, this thesis demonstrates the development and evaluation of DL–based
approaches for detecting and mitigating cyber threats under realistic network
and traffic conditions in H-IoT systems. The scope of the thesis encompasses
multiple attack scenarios aligned with practical cyber threat landscapes in H-IoT
settings. It further underscores the relevance of modelling diverse attack scenarios
260

7.3 Future Work
to continued research in this domain. In this context, the use of emulated wearable
devices and edge-based deployment on Raspberry Pi 4 enables evaluation under
practical constraints related to computation, memory, and inference latency. As a
result, the proposed approach addresses the core components of the cybersecurity
workflow in a coherent, practical design within H-IoT settings.
The findings of this thesis provide a foundation for scalable and resilient cy-
bersecurity mechanisms in H-IoT deployments. These support future academic
research and practical implementations, thereby contributing to the development
of secure, deployable H-IoT infrastructures.
