EXPERIMENTAL DESIGN, MATERIALS AND METHODS 
All the experiments were conducted using an AMD RyzenTM 7 5800H (8-Core, 16-Thread) with 32GB 
RAM running Ubuntu 24.04 LTS. 
Testbed Over view and Network Structure 
The dataset is suitable for network intrusion detecDon studies in large-scale IoT environments. The 
network traﬃc was acquired in the Gotham testbed Internet of Things [5]. This testbed simulates large-
scale IoT environments, leveraging the GNS3 network emulator [6] to provide a realisDc sepng for IoT 
network traﬃc and security research. Gotham comprises a wide array of Docker images and QEMU-
based virtual machines emulaDng IoT/IIoT devices, malware samples, servers, and networking 
infrastructure, including routers and switches. Real producDon libraries, Open vSwitch for network 
switching, VyOS for rouDng, and various malware samples and red-teaming tools were employed to 
generate authenDc network traﬃc. 
 
Fig. 5. Network diagram of the Gotham testbed [5].  
The network topology is segmented into three primary networks – city, cloud, and threat – connected 
via 10 routers and 30 switches. The city network was further divided into four network segments. Each 
segment represents a diﬀerent operaDonal area, with traﬃc pa]erns designed to reﬂect real-world 
IoT use cases. These segments generate diverse traﬃc based on typical smart city scenarios, ensuring 
a realisDc simulaDon of network behaviour . A]ack traﬃc was distributed across these networks to 
simulate a realisDc smart city environment. 
Threat Actors and A9ack Scenarios 
The testbed setup involved three disDnct threat actors orchestraDng a series of a]acks: Mirai Malware, 
Merlin, Port Scanning and AmpliﬁcaDon. Each actor employed speciﬁc tools and techniques to execute 
their respecDve a]acks across the network.

a) Mirai Malware ADacks 
This actor used the Mirai malware [7] to conduct several a]ack types, with nodes such as the Mirai 
bot, C&C server, scan listener, loader, and download server adapted from published Mirai source code 
[8]. A]acks included: 
- Mirai C&C Communica#on: ConDnuous bot-to-C&C communicaDon. 
- Network Scanning: Mirai bots scanned for open Telnet ports using TCP SYN packets. 
- Brute Forcing: If a bot found an open Telnet port, it a]empted to brute-force credenDals with 
a default IoT credenDal list. 
- Repor#ng: ALer brute-forcing success, bots reported credenDals to the scan listener. 
- Ingress Tool Transfer: The loader infected vulnerable nodes, downloading and execuDng Mirai 
malware. 
- Remote Command Execu#on: C&C servers issued commands to bots for various a]acks. 
- DoS A9acks: Included UDP plain a]ack, DNS a]ack, TCP ACK, SYN a]acks, and others, each 
executed for 10 seconds. 
 
b) Merlin-Based ADacks 
This actor leveraged the Merlin cross-platorm C&C server [9] with Merlin agents and hping3 for DoS 
a]acks. The C&C communicated through mulDple protocols (HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2, HTTP/3, etc.), allowing 
command execuDon on vicDm devices. 
- Merlin C&C Communica#on: Periodic contact between Merlin agents and the C&C server. 
- Ingress Tool Transfer: hping3 was transferred to infected devices for DoS execuDon. 
- Remote Command Execu#on: Commands from the Merlin C&C server triggered a]acks. 
- DoS A9acks: A]acks such as ICMP echo-request, UDP , TCP SYN, and TCP ACK ﬂood generate 
approximately 5,000 packets at 1 ms intervals. 
 
c) Port Scanning and AmpliﬁcaCon ADacks 
This actor performed extensive network scanning and CoAP ampliﬁcaDon a]acks using tools like 
Nmap, Masscan, and AMP-Research. 
- Network-Wide Scanning: Conducted with Masscan for speciﬁc TCP ports at various packet 
rates (100, 1000, and 10,000 packets/s) and with Nmap for UDP ports. 
- CoAP Ampliﬁca#on A9ack: A CoAP device in the city network was leveraged to amplify a]acks 
for 15 seconds against a target. 
The malicious events were conducted in sequenDal batches, one aLer the other .  The dataset 
generaDon workﬂow, illustrated in Fig. 6, consisted of several stages:

Fig. 6. Dataset generaDon methodology. 
Data Collec#on and Feature Extrac#on 
Network packets were captured from IoT devices using tcpdump in a distributed manner, generaDng 
raw PCAP ﬁles. Each PCAP ﬁle contains network traﬃc for a speciﬁc IoT device during a given network 
event, enabling decentralised analysis and distributed learning. The network traﬃc was captured 
separately for each IoT device at the interface between the IoT gateway and the device. 
First, benign network traﬃc was captured over 2 hours without any security a]acks, providing a 
baseline for normal communicaDon. This data enables researchers to create baseline models for 
intrusion detecDon. Subsequently, various a]acks were launched sequenDally, with each a]ack lasDng 
between 1 and 1.5 hours. 
Features were then extracted from the PCAP ﬁles using Tshark. This extracDon produced CSV ﬁles 
containing the relevant feature vectors, detailing characterisDcs such as packet size, Dmestamp, 
protocol type, source/desDnaDon IP addresses, and ports. 
The data generaDon, collecDon, and feature extracDon processes were automated using Python scripts 
to ensure reproducibility and ﬂexibility for adjustments. 
Data Labelling 
The extracted data were labelled in Python within a controlled environment, following a structured 
methodology to accurately disDnguish between normal and malicious traﬃc based on metadata. The 
metadata was generated during the experimental setup by logging details about the a]ack scenarios 
in real-Dme. It included Dmestamps of a]acks, network packet characterisDcs, and the a]acker’s IP 
address. 
A #ming synchronisa#on strategy was applied to label network packets in the CSV ﬁles. This strategy 
involved aligning the Dmestamps of a]ack phases with the captured network traﬃc. For each a]ack 
phase, packets within the corresponding Dme window were labelled as malicious, while those outside 
the a]ack intervals were labelled as normal. This approach ensures precise labelling, maintains the 
integrity of the dataset and enables researchers to analyse a]ack behaviours in relaDon to the exact 
Dming of network traﬃc. Fig. 7 provides a simpliﬁed overview of the Dming synchronisaDon strategy. 
 
