The labelling process followed these steps: 
- Traﬃc Filtering: Raw network packets were ﬁltered to include only IPv4/IPv6 packets where 
the IoT device IP address was the source or desDnaDon, isolaDng interacDons directly involving 
the IoT devices. 
- Normal Traﬃc Labelling: IniDally, traﬃc classiﬁed as normal, such as device -to-server 
communicaDon, was labelled. 
- Malicious Traﬃc Labelling: Subsequent malicious events were labelled based on speciﬁc 
a]ack signatures and metadata, covering various stages of the intrusion process. 
The output of the methodology is a set of CSV ﬁles	containing network packets labelled with extracted 
features. These ﬁles can be used to develop Intrusion DetecDon Systems (IDSs) using Machine Learning 
(ML) or Deep Learning (DL) models to detect cyber-a]acks on IoT devices and large-scale IoT networks. 
These models can be trained in both centralised and distributed learning sepngs. 
The data processing scripts are available on GitHub at h]ps://github.com/othmbela/gotham-network-
packet-labeller to ensure reproducibility. Researchers can use these scripts to process raw network 
traﬃc and create their datasets. The repository includes instrucDons and conﬁguraDons for easy use 
and customisaDon.  
Use Examples 
To demonstrate how the dataset can be uDlised for intrusion detecDon research, we present an 
evaluaDon of a basic Deep Neural Network (DNN) trained on the dataset in an FL setup. A DNN was 
selected because DL models are parDcularly eﬀecDve at capturing complex pa]erns in high -
dimensional network traﬃc data. Prior studies [10] have evaluated convenDonal ML models (e.g., 
Decision Tree (DT), Random Forest (RF), Support Vector Machine (SVM), K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN)) 
alongside DNNs for cyber-a]ack detecDon in FL sepngs. The experimental results demonstrated the 
eﬀecDveness of DNNs in handling large-scale and heterogeneous datasets.  
FL was selected to align with the decentralised nature of IoT systems, where data is generated across 
mulDple devices rather than in a centralised manner. Beyond decentralisaDon, FL miDgates privacy 
risks by ensuring that raw network traﬃc data remains on each IoT device [11]. This is parDcularly 
relevant for real-world smart city deployments, where transmipng large volumes of traﬃc data to a 
central server may introduce security risks as well as network congesDon [11]. AddiDonally, this setup 
also supports scalability, as models can be trained without centralising vast amounts of data, making 
it ideal for large-scale IoT networks. 
In this experiment, we used 10 rounds of FL, which is a typical number used in similar studies [10]. In 
each round, a subset of 13 IoT devices was sampled for model training. Each IoT device splits its dataset

into a training set (80%) and a tesDng set (20%). This approach ensures that the model is evaluated on 
data it has not encountered during training. The training process was distributed across the 13 selected 
devices, allowing each device to locally train the model on its own data and share the model updates 
with a central aggregator. ALer aggregaDng the local updates, the global model was tested on a 
centralised server using the tesDng subsets, ensuring consistency in performance assessment across 
rounds. 
