2) Raspberry Pi OS: Raspberry Pi OS is a Debian-based
Unix-like OS for Raspberry Pi boards (excluding Pico). It fea-
tures a modified LXDE desktop with Openbox and comes with
Wolfram Mathematica, VLC, and a lightweight Chromium
browser [25].
3) Putty and Advanced IP scanner: To connect and upload
code to a Raspberry Pi (RPi), we use tools like PuTTY and
Advanced IP Scanner. First, we find the RPi’s IP address with
the Advanced IP Scanner. Then, we open PuTTY , enter the
IP to SSH in, and log in. Navigate to our code directory
using terminal commands (cd) and use PuTTY’s SCP feature
to transfer files from our local machine to the RPi [26], [27].
4) Mobile Application: MQTT Dashboard is an Android
application designed for easy interaction, control, and manage-
ment of MQTT-enabled devices. The app offers customizable
interface components, referred to as tiles, allowing users to
create personalized MQTT dashboards tailored to their needs
[28].

C. Analysis of System Design
Our IoT-enabled Parking System efficiently manages space
availability, monitors environmental conditions, and ensures
proper ventilation, enhancing user experience and maintaining
optimal conditions. It uses a Raspberry Pi for MQTT com-
munication and an Arduino Uno for controls and sensor inte-
grations. In Algorithm 1, The pseudocode represents the logic
for the smart car parking system. It outlines how the system
manages parking slots, updates displays, and communicates
status through MQTT.
Algorithm 1 IoT-Enabled Smart Car Parking System
1: Init(S, T, H, G, F, D) ▷ Initialize components
2: S = [0, 0,..., 0] ▷ Set all parking slots to vacant
3: while Active do
4: if IR in == 1 then ▷ Car detected at entrance
5: Total vacant− = 1 ▷ Decrease vacant slots
6: ifTotal vacant ≥ 0 then ▷ Slots available
7: G in = 1 ▷ Open entrance gate
8: B = 1 ▷ Turn on buzzer
9: D(T,H,Total vacant,Total slots) ▷ Update Display
10: MQTT (T P,S ) ▷ Publish status to MQTT
11: else ▷ No slots available
12: Total vacant+ = 1 ▷ Revert vacant slots
13: end if
14: end if
15: if IR out == 1 then ▷ Car detected at exit
16: G out = 1 ▷ Open exit gate
17: Total vacant+ = 1 ▷ Increase vacant slots
18: D(T,H,Total vacant,Total slots) ▷ Update Display
19: MQTT (T P,S ) ▷ Publish status to MQTT
20: end if
21: fori = 1 ton do ▷ Update slot status
22: Si =IR sloti ▷ Read slot status
23: MQTT (T Pi,S i) ▷ Publish slot status
24: end for
25: T,H =DHT 22 ▷ Get temperature and humidity
26: D(T,H,Total vacant,Total slots)
27: G =MQ 2 ▷ Read gas level
28: ifG>G th then ▷ Gas threshold exceeded
29: F = 1 ▷ Activate exhaust fan
30: end if
31: end while
32: Deinit(S, T, H, G, F, D) ▷ Shutdown components
When activated, the system begins monitoring all sensors
and controls. As a car approaches, the entrance IR sensor
detects the vehicle ( Vin). If no car is detected ( Vin = 0), the
entrance gate remains closed. If a car is detected ( Vin = 1),
the system checks for available parking spaces. If no space is
available (Total vacant = 0), the entrance gate remains closed.
If a space is available (Total vacant > 0), the entrance gate
opens via a relay and servo motor, and a buzzer activates
(B = 1). The system continuously monitors temperature ( T )
and humidity ( H) using a DHT22 sensor. This information,
along with the parking slots count, is displayed on an OLED
screen (Equation 1):
Display =f(T,H, Total vacant, Total slots) (1)
When a car exits, the exit IR sensor detects the vehicle
(Vout). If no car is detected ( Vout = 0), the exit gate remains
closed. If a car is detected ( Vout = 1 ), the exit gate opens
via a relay and servo motor. The available slots count is then
updated.
The system integrates with a mobile app “MQTT Dash-
board” via MQTT communication. Clients connect to the app
using the provided IP address and port (IP app, portapp). The
real-time status of parking slots is communicated as follows:
The MQTT communication between the Raspberry Pi and
the mobile app involves publishing and subscribing to topics.
Let:
• S be the state of the parking slot ( S = 1 for occupied,
S = 0 for vacant).
• TP be the topic for parking slot status updates.
• DR be the data rate, delay be the communication latency,
and EC be the error correction rate.
The parking slot status is updated and published to the topic
TP (Equation 2):
Publish(TP,S ) =⇒ Message(S) (2)
Clients subscribe to the topic to receive updates (Equation
3):
Subscribe(TP ) =⇒ Receive Message(S) (3)
The data transfer rate ( DR) for MQTT communication is
given by (Equation 4):
DR = Data transmitted
Time (4)
The communication delay (delay) is calculated as (Equation
5):
delay = timeend− timestart (5)
The error correction rate ( EC ) is given by (Equation 6):
EC = Errors corrected
Total errors (6)
Upon entering the parking lot, clients can view available
slots on their mobile devices and park in the nearest and
easiest vacant slot. The parking slot status is updated through
MQTT communication, changing from green (available) to
red (occupied) on the mobile app. Additionally, the system
includes an MQ2 gas sensor to monitor the parking lot for
hazardous gas levels ( G). If the gas concentration exceeds a
predefined threshold (G>G threshold), an exhaust fan activates
(F = 1) to ventilate the area (Equation 7):
G>G threshold =⇒ F = 1 (7)
The probability of the parking lot being fully occupied
(Pfull) can be modeled using a Poisson distribution with arrival
rate λ. The probability that the number of cars k equals the
total slots n is (Equation 8):
Pfull = λne−λ
n! (8)
If the average time a car spends in the lot is Tavg, and the
arrival rate is λ, then the average number of cars in the lot
(L) can be estimated using Little’s Law (Equation 9):
L =λ·Tavg (9)

