LLM-Driven Multi-Agent Control for Skill-Based
Smart Manufacturing
Kay K ¨ohle∗†, Darko Anicic†, Thomas A. Runkler∗†, Ren ´e Graf‡
∗Technical University of Munich, Munich, Germany
†Siemens AG, Foundational Technologies, Munich/Garching, Germany
‡Siemens AG, Digital Industries, F ¨urth, Germany
Email:kay.koehle@siemens.com
Abstract—Factories are shifting toward smaller lot sizes with
high product customization, requiring frequent re-programming
of flexible and reconfigurable automation systems. LLM-based
agents can be deployed in two complementary roles: offline,
they generate deterministic production sequences, reducing pro-
gramming effort; online, they operate live machines and handle
unforeseen runtime faults that static programs cannot anticipate.
We propose a solution in which each factory module is paired
with a dedicated LLM-based agent and an MCP tool server that
exposes the module’s skills via OPC UA method calls, with agents
coordinating over MQTT and grounded by real time updates
of the factory state. We compare three agent architectures (or-
chestrator, peer-to-peer, and monolithic) across nine production
challenges of increasing complexity in a simulation of a physical
six-module hexagonal factory, including silent hardware fault
detection. The monolithic and peer-to-peer architectures both
achieve the highest mean solve rate (93%), while the orchestrator
uniquely resolves a silent conveyor-belt fault in all ten runs by
autonomously rerouting plates around the blocked segment. All
architectures exhibit emergent fault-diagnosis behavior without
any explicit failure-handling logic, establishing standardized
MCP tooling, MQTT-based inter-agent communication, and real-
time state injection as a viable and reproducible foundation for
LLM-programmed smart manufacturing.
Index Terms—Multi-Agent System, Large Language Model,
OPC UA, MQTT, Model Context Protocol, Industrial Automa-
tion, Smart Manufacturing, Cyber-Physical System
I. INTRODUCTION
Industrial automation systems are undergoing a pro-
found transformation driven by the shift toward high-mix,
low-volume manufacturing, which requires rapidly repro-
grammable production systems. LLM-based agents can gener-
ate such production sequences, reducing manual programming
effort. Additionally, while classical deterministic schedulers
handle fixed production sequences reliably, they require every
recoverable condition to be anticipated and hard-coded. Silent
hardware faults, where a method reports success, but produces
no physical effect, fall outside this model entirely, as they
cannot be enumerated in advance. Here, LLM agents that
directly control the modules inside a production system can
reason over discrepancies between expected and actual factory
state, attributing failures and rerouting production without any
explicit pre-programmed failure-handling logic. Early work
has demonstrated that LLM-based agents can interpret natural-
language task descriptions and interact with cyber-physical
production systems, with Xia et al. [1] showing how agents can
coordinate industrial modules through event-driven reasoning
over OPC UA-based digital twins. Hofmann et al. [2] further
show that LLMs can control real machines by invoking OPC
UA methods as callable tools, while Xu et al. [3] demonstrate
LLM-based control in robotic feedback loops, establishing
LLMs as credible general-purpose control components. In
parallel, the Model Context Protocol (MCP) has introduced
a standardized way to expose machine capabilities to LLMs.
Vieira Da Silva et al. [4] show how MCP can formalize these
capabilities within manufacturing settings, enabling LLMs
to interact with machines and skills through semantically
enriched, self-describing tool interfaces rather than unstruc-
tured natural-language heuristics. At the communication layer,
SUPCON Global has demonstrated LLM agents operating a
simulated factory over MQTT, arguing that JSON-structured
publish/subscribe messaging is well-suited for scalable multi-
agent coordination [5], [6]. Despite these developments, signif-
icant gaps remain. Existing approaches either adopt monolithic
agent designs without standardized tool interfaces, use cus-
tom event-driven prompting without a structured inter-agent
communication protocol, or demonstrate MQTT-based coor-
dination without connecting it to OPC UA as a machine-level
actuation layer. No prior work combines all three: structured
agent architectures, MCP-based modular tooling, and OPC UA
as the standardized execution interface. This paper addresses
these gaps by comparing agent architectures for LLM-driven
industrial automation in which each factory module is paired
with a dedicated MCP tool server that exposes its skills via
OPC UA method calls, and agents coordinate over MQTT.
We evaluate three architectures (orchestrator, peer-to-peer, and
monolithic) across nine production challenges of increasing
complexity in a Python-based simulation of a factory with
six modules. In the challenges, the monolithic and peer-to-
peer architectures achieve the highest mean solve rate of
93%, while the orchestrator uniquely solves the transport-fault
challenge at 100% by leveraging its global view of the factory.
II. BACKGROUND
A. Skill-Based Manufacturing and OPC UA
Skill-based manufacturing organizes machine functionality
into discrete, callable skills that abstract away internal imple-
mentation complexity [7]. OPC UA is a platform-independent
arXiv:2610.01364v1  [cs.MA]  1 Oct 2026

communication standard for industrial automation that com-
bines a client-server interaction model with a structured infor-
mation model [8]. An OPC UA server exposes device data and
functionality as a typed, hierarchical address space of objects,
variables, and methods, with methods specifically serving as
the standardized, invocable interface for a module’s skills.
B. LLM-based Agents and Model Context Protocol (MCP)
Tools
An LLM-based agent is a system in which a large language
model operates in a perception–reasoning–action loop [9],
meaning it receives context from its environment and selects
tool actions and iterates until a goal is reached. This makes
LLM-based agents suitable for tasks that require decision-
making and interaction with external systems. MCP is an open
standard that introduces a client-server architecture for tool
use, providing LLMs with a structured interface to invoke
external functionality [10], such as aforementioned OPC UA
methods.
III. RELATEDWORK
Research on industrial automation increasingly explores the
use of LLMs for planning, decision-making, and execution.
The expectation is that the LLMs’ reasoning capabilities
enable them to flexibly handle unforeseen faults in modular
production systems, a challenge that has long been recognized
in resilient and agent-based manufacturing research [11]. An
early demonstration of LLM-enabled multi-agent manufactur-
ing was presented by Lim et al. [12], who integrated GPT-4
into an orchestrator architecture to allocate G-code operations
across simulated CNC machines via natural language function
calls. Zhao et al. [13] deploy a multi-agent LLM system on a
physical shopfloor, in which each machine is controlled by an
agent composed of specialized modules that negotiate machine
selection in natural language. The most directly relevant work
is Xia et al. [1], who propose a multi-agent architecture for
planning and executing tasks in a modular production system
using digital twins and OPC UA. Vieira Da Silva et al. [4] use
the Model Context Protocol (MCP) to expose manufacturing
skills as semantically enriched LLM-callable tool descriptions,
focusing on semantic interoperability and capability modeling
rather than runtime agent coordination.
Across these works, existing approaches either rely on
proprietary cloud APIs and unstructured prompt-based inter-
faces [1], [12], [13], evaluate only single-step action prediction
without cross-module fault routing [1], or address capability
modeling without runtime coordination [4]. In contrast, our
work combines a multi-agent architecture, MCP-based per-
agent tool servers, OPC UA as the standardized execution in-
terface, and MQTT as the inter-agent communication protocol.
IV. CONCEPT
This section presents our architecture for LLM-agent con-
trolled modular production systems. We describe how factory
module skills are exposed as agent-callable tools, how multiple
agents coordinate over a publish/subscribe communication
