A. Factory Module Agent Architecture
Figure 1 visualizes the architecture of a single agent. Each
agent is a self-contained unit paired with a factory module,
with its OPC UA methods exposed to the LLM as tools, and
agents coordinating over an MQTT broker.
In our prototype, each factory module is assigned a dedi-
cated MCP server that wraps the module’s OPC UA methods
as MCP tools.
MCP was chosen for its native integration with major LLM
inference frameworks and its growing ecosystem. Beyond this
practical motivation, MCP naturally enforces the separation of
concerns central to skill-based manufacturing. The MCP server
is the only component that knows the OPC UA endpoint ad-
dress and method signatures, while the LLM agent knows only
the tool’s name, description, and schema. As a result, replacing
or extending a module’s functionality requires only updating
its MCP server, with no changes to the agent orchestration
logic. Although the implementation uses MCP, the underlying
architectural principle of using one tool server per module,
with typed schemas is protocol-agnostic and applicable to any
production system with OPC UA-accessible module skills.
B. Multi-Agent Coordination via MQTT
We arrange agents in three distinct architectures, illustrated
in Figure 2. In theorchestratorarchitecture, a central agent
holds a global view of the factory and delegates tasks to
dedicated module agents. In thepeer-to-peerarchitecture,
module agents communicate directly with one another without
a central coordinator. In themonolithicarchitecture, a single
agent controls all modules directly. These three patterns expose
different trade-offs in coordination overhead, fault resilience,
and reasoning scope, motivating a systematic comparison. The
detailed implementation of each architecture is described in
Section V.
MQTT is an established industrial standard for machine-
to-machine communication, widely deployed on resource-
constrained edge devices [14]. Unlike OPC UA’s traditional

