client-server model, which requires agents to maintain direct
endpoint references, MQTT’s publish/subscribe mechanism
naturally maps to a multi-agent topology, because agents are
decoupled via a broker, new agents can join without modi-
fying existing ones, and topic-based routing natively supports
one-to-many broadcast semantics. While OPC UA’s Pub/Sub
extension offers a hybrid alternative by combining OPC UA’s
typed information model with MQTT transport semantics, in
our prototype we preferred plain MQTT for its simplicity and
broader native support in edge environments.
C. Real-Time State Injection
We track the effects of OPC UA method calls on the system
in an internal state tracker. The state tracker maintains a live
snapshot of plate locations, belt occupancy, block inventory,
and inspection results, acting as an event-driven aggregator
over the stream of skill results. Each skill completion event,
received via MQTT, carries the information needed to up-
date the state, for exampleMovePlateupdates the plate’s
location, whileLoadUnloadupdates the occupancy of the
corresponding module. At each inference step, this state is
passed to the LLM agents by prepending it to the input prompt
as a structured context block. This relieves LLMs from having
to reconstruct the state themselves from the conversation
history, with the added benefit of enabling aggressive token-
conserving context-history trimming.
The internal state also drives the dynamic tool constraint
mechanism. A tool availability manager evaluates the current
simulation state and publishes the set of physically valid tools
to each agent over MQTT. The agent’s tool list is filtered
before each LLM call, preventing the model from attempting
operations that would be rejected by the underlying controllers.
For example, an inspection cannot be invoked before the plate
has been loaded into the module.
The mechanisms of standardized tool abstraction, pub-
lish/subscribe communication, and externally injected state
together allow the agents to reason and act locally while the
system as a whole executes coherent multi-step production
workflows.
V. IMPLEMENTATION
Our prototype consists of two main parts: A simulated
factory and an agent orchestration system that controls it.
A. Factory Simulation
The prototype’s simulation environment is modeled after a
small real-world industrial demonstration lab equipped with a
physical manufacturing line. The manufacturing lab consists
of seven modules, including a hexagonal conveyor belt as the
backbone. The conveyor consists of an inner belt for inter-
module transit and at each edge it has an outer belt that serves
as the docking interface for the six peripheral modules. Plates
circulate on the inner belt and are transferred to the outer belt
when a module needs to interact with them. Modules can only
load and unload plates from the outer belt, meaning a plate
must first be positioned at the correct outer belt segment before
any module operation can be invoked. The lab manufactures
a product that consists of five wooden blocks on a plate.
Fig. 3. Example product featuring five wooden blocks of different shapes on
a plate, placed at0 ◦ or180 ◦ rotation.
A typical order specifies a plate with up to five wooden
blocks, each of ten different types placeable at0 ◦ or
180◦ rotation. The Plate Dispenser Module places an empty
plate on the belt, which is routed through the relevant
Assembly, Inspection, and Recycling or Delivery Modules
in sequence. If a product is found faulty at inspection,
blocks are removed at the Recycling Module and the as-
sembly process restarts. Each module has its own OPC
UA server, which exposes the module’s skills as OPC UA
methods (see Table I). The hexagonal backbone module ex-
poses a single method,MovePlate(edge, direction,
source, destination, plateID), which moves a

plate one step along the belt. Theedgeparameter identi-
fies one of the six sides of the hexagon, anddirection
specifies the rotational direction of travel. Thesourceand
destinationparameters specify whether the plate is on
the inner or outer belt at each end of the move, allowing the
method to express both transit steps along the inner belt and
transfers between the inner and outer belt at a given edge.
The Plate Dispenser Module can place or remove an empty
plate onto or from its outer belt segment viaPlacePlate()
andRemovePlate(). All other peripheral modules expose
aLoadUnload(source, destination)method that
fetches a plate from its corresponding outer belt segment
into the module or returns it afterward. Only once a plate
has been loaded into a module can that module’s special-
purpose operation be invoked, such asInsert()for assem-
bly,Inspect()for quality control,Recycle()for block
removal, orDeliver()for AGV handoff.
