The simulation is implemented in Python and exposes mock
OPC UA servers that manipulate the simulated factory’s state
in response to method calls. After each state change, the
simulation publishes a structured event over MQTT on the
factory/sim/{event_type}topic, which updates the
internal state tracker. As detailed in Section IV-C, the factory
state is prepended as context to every LLM inference turn,
giving each agent an up-to-date view of the physical world
without requiring the LLM to maintain state across turns. We
investigate the benefit of the internal state tracker in a small
ablation study.
A real-time web dashboard visualizes the factory state by
subscribing to the same MQTT simulation events, providing
a live 2D view of plate positions and module activity during
experiments (see Fig. 4). The web dashboard and simulation
layer are intentionally decoupled from the agent architecture.
Any component that exposes an OPC UA interface can serve
as a drop-in replacement for the mock servers, including
real physical hardware or higher-fidelity digital twins, as
demonstrated in [15].
Fig. 4. Real-time dashboard of the factory simulation. The six outside
modules dock onto the hexagonal conveyor belt backbone in the middle. The
ToolAvailabilityManager dynamically enables each module’s operation tools
based on the factory’s state. As shown in the screenshot, the only permitted
operations are loading the plate into the recycling module, moving the plate,
or placing an additional plate onto the conveyor belt.
B. Agent Implementation
This section details the concrete implementation of the three
architectures introduced in Section IV. The first architecture
assigns an agent to each module, with a dedicated orchestrator
agent coordinating all module agents. In our single-backbone
prototype, this orchestrator also controls the conveyor belt,
since it is the central transport medium. We additionally
implemented a peer-to-peer architecture and a monolithic
architecture for systematic comparison. In the peer-to-peer
architecture, the modules can control the conveyor belt at
the edge they are docked to, to send plates to their adjacent
modules. In the monolithic architecture, a single agent has all
the tools to control all modules of the factory, much like an
LLM-driven MES.
1) Orchestrator Architecture:The backbone agent serves
as the orchestrator, coordinating the six specialist agents. The
orchestrator’s system prompt encodes the full routing logic,
specifying which module at which edge handles which block
type, the load/unload protocol, and the decision rules for rout-
ing after inspection. The current factory state, including plate

locations, belt occupancy, assembly progress, and inspection
results, is prepended to every inference turn to provide the
orchestrator with an up-to-date view of the physical world.
The prompt for specialist agents encodes the module’s skills,
its edge number, its magazine contents, and block types. The
orchestrator exchanges messages with the specialist agents via
a local MQTT broker, using a dedicated command topic and
a dedicated reply topic per specialist. When the orchestrator
wishes to delegate a task, it publishes a message to the
specialist’s command topic. On receiving a message from the
orchestrator, the specialist agent calls the LLM with the current
tool list, executes any tool calls via MCP, feeds results back
to the LLM, and repeats until the LLM produces a plain
text response, which is published back over MQTT. Once the
specialist has completed its task, it publishes its response to
its own reply topic, closing the request–response cycle. The
backbone subscribes to all specialist reply topics. Each agent
has its dedicated MCP tool server, which exposes the module’s
OPC UA methods as tools. To prevent agents from attempting
physically impossible operations (e.g., loading a non-existent
plate), a ToolAvailabilityManager continuously evaluates the
simulation state and publishes the set of currently valid tools,
preventing such operations without requiring the LLM to
reason about preconditions. The ToolAvailabilityManager is
also used in the other two architectures, with adaptations for
each.
2) Peer-to-Peer Architecture:In the peer-to-peer architec-
ture, only the six outside module agents are deployed, without
the central conveyor belt agent as an orchestrator. Each agent
is identical in structure to the backbone specialist agents
and is paired with the same dedicated MCP server, exposing
only its own module’s OPC UA methods. In addition to the
MCP tools, each agent is augmented with asend_message
tool, which allows the LLM to route a message to a peer
agent by name. This tool publishes the payload to the target
agent’s dedicated MQTT topic. Incoming peer messages are
injected as user-role turns with a[From sender]prefix,
enriched with the current factory state, and then processed
through the standard tool-calling loop. The agent’s response
may contain furthersend_messagecalls that are dispatched
during the same loop, establishing a chain of bilateral handoffs
rather than a centrally orchestrated sequence. A per-agent
lock serializes incoming messages to prevent corruption of
the conversation history when multiple peers send messages
simultaneously. Each agent’s system prompt encodes its edge
number, its module’s tool signatures, its constrained peer graph
(i.e. only the subset of agents it is permitted to address), the
relevant slice of the production orders, and the coordination
protocol. After completing a processing step, the agent moves
the plate to the next edge and notifies the downstream peer.
The plate module agent is designated as the initiator and starts
production autonomously after connecting.
3) Monolithic Architecture:The monolithic architecture
uses a single agent with direct access to every factory module.
Its MCP server aggregates all module tools into a single
endpoint, disambiguating same-named operations across mod-
ules with a module-name prefix. The agent runs the same
tool-calling loop as the specialist agents: It calls the LLM
with the full tool list, executes any tool calls via MCP,
feeds results back, and repeats until the LLM produces a
plain-text response. The system prompt encodes the complete
factory layout (all six edges and their roles), the full set of
module tools with their signatures, the step-by-step workflow
for processing a plate from placement through assembly,
inspection, and delivery or recycling, and all production orders.
The factory state is prepended to every inference turn in the
same format as in the backbone architecture. Because the
agent must reason across all modules in a single context, the
history window and the maximum tool-call budget per turn are
significantly larger than in the multi-agent architectures. To
keep the context window from overflowing, outdated factory
states are removed from the history.
VI. EXPERIMENTS
We evaluate the multi-agent factory controller across nine
increasingly complex challenges. Each production challenge
was run 10 times and the means are reported across successful
runs. All experiments were conducted on the Python-based
factory simulation described in Section V. Gemma4:31B was
selected as the LLM for its native support for function
calling, strong agentic capabilities, and open weights under
the Apache 2.0 license. Inference was performed locally via
Ollama using the 30.7B-parameter dense model with Q4 K M
(20 GB) quantization on an Nvidia RTX 6000 Pro Blackwell
Workstation GPU rather than through a cloud provider, as dat-
acenter load variability and network latency would introduce
uncontrollable timing factors that could confound experimental
results. Temperature was set to 0.3 rather than 0 to allow agents
to explore different recovery strategies across repeated runs of
the silent-fault challenges.
A. Production Challenges
The challenges progress from basic pipeline verification
(1–6) to hardware-level silent faults requiring autonomous
diagnosis (7–9), each isolating or combining a distinct system
capability.
1) Single-Type Order, Single Module (Baseline).Five
identical blocks (type 5). Verifies the minimum viable pipeline:
plate placement, routing to a single assembly station, five
insertions, inspection, and AGV delivery.
2) Multi-Type Order, Single Module.One block of each
of five different types, all in one assembly module. The agent
must map block types to magazine IDs and complete assembly
in a single module station visit.
3) Multi-Type Order, Two Modules, With Rotation.
Three block types from the first assembly module, two from
the assembly with rotation module (one block at180 ◦). Re-
quires cross-module routing and correct orientation handling.
4) Multi-Type Order, Two Modules, With Refill.
Same as Challenge 3, but magazine 1 of the assem-
bly with rotation module starts empty. The agent must

callrequest_refill(magazine_id=1), await opera-
tor confirmation, then resume.
5) Multi-Type Order, Two Modules, With Recycling.
Same order as Challenge 3, but the first assembly attempt
contains a defective block. Quality inspection fails and the
plate is recycled; the second attempt succeeds. This experiment
exercises the fullinspect→recycle→reassembleloop.
6) Two Parallel Multi-Type Orders, Two Modules, With
Rotation.Two orders are active simultaneously, with order
identity tracked implicitly via plate IDs. The first exclusively
requires blocks from the first assembly module, while the
second requires blocks from the second assembly with rotation
module (two at180 ◦). The agents must interleave transport
across the modules and track per-plate order assignments.
7) All Edge-1 Blocks Defective, Autonomous Fallback.
Same order as Challenge 2, but the first assembly module
contains only defective blocks, while the assembly with ro-
tation module carries the same types with good blocks. The
agents must infer the pattern of inspection failures and reroute
assembly without any explicit fault signal.
8) Defective Assembly Module (Silent Failure).Same
order as Challenge 2, but the first assembly module’s
skill_insertreports success even though it places no
blocks. The agents must cross-verify via the quality inspector,
attribute the failure to the assembly module, and coordinate
rerouting to the second assembly module.
9) Defective Conveyor Belt (Silent Failure).A silent fault
is injected on the clockwise belt segment from edge 0 to
edge 1.move_platereports success but the plate stays at
edge 0. The agents must infer the transport failure and reroute
counter-clockwise.
B. Evaluation Metrics
Each challenge is evaluated across the following metrics:
1)Solve Rate (SR)— percentage of runs in which the
agents successfully fulfilled the production order.
2)Time— wall-clock time from the first agent action to
successful product delivery.
3)Tokens— total input and output tokens consumed per
run, broken down per agent, as a measure of operational
cost and context efficiency.
4)Tool Calls— Number of tool calls used to fulfill the
production order.
VII. RESULTS ANDDISCUSSION
Table II summarizes the experiment results of the three
agent architectures across the nine production challenges,
averaged over ten runs per challenge. The optimal number
of tool calls (Opt) represents a lower bound, assuming the
minimum number of tool calls required to fulfill the production
order. However, for Challenges 7, 8, and 9, it should be noted
that, while the factory setup theoretically permits the lower
bound, it would require prior knowledge of the silent defects.
On the five baseline challenges (1–5), all architectures
achieved 100% SR, confirming that all three designs can
handle standard production orders reliably. All architectures
solved Challenge 6 at 90–100%, demonstrating that concur-
rently active orders add no fundamental reliability problem
to any design. The more difficult silent-failure Challenges
(7–9) expose clear architectural trade-offs. The orchestrator
architecture achieved an overall SR of 87%. It performed
worst on Challenge 7 (20% SR), where all magazine blocks
of the first module are defective and a fallback to the second
module is required. In the failing runs, the backbone agent
entered a loop between the assembly and quality-inspection
agents without ever identifying the need to switch modules,
consuming an average of 1997 k tokens and 1050 s before
timing out. In contrast, the monolithic agent autonomously
switched to the alternative module on all ten runs, as confirmed
by reasoning traces such as:“Wait, maybe I should try the
other assembly module (edge 2) just in case edge 1 is produc-
ing defective blocks?”, demonstrating emergent fault-diagnosis
behavior without explicit fault-handling logic. However, the
orchestrator was the only architecture to solve Challenge 9 at
100%, as the backbone agent’s global view of the transport
network allows it to identify the blocked clockwise path and
reroute the plate counter-clockwise.
The monolithic and peer-to-peer architectures both achieved
the highest overall SR of 93%. The monolithic architecture
solved all challenges except Challenge 9 (40%) with the
silent conveyor-belt fault between edges 0 and 1. Because
the monolithic policy must jointly handle routing, assembly
sequencing, inspection, recycling, and delivery in a single de-
cision loop, these contradictions amplified into long local retry
loops and timeouts. By contrast, the orchestrator benefited
from a narrower control problem (routing and coordination),
allowing faster strategy correction once the clockwise path
proved unreliable.
The peer-to-peer architecture solved both silent assembly
module defect Challenges 7 and 8 at 90% and 100%, but
dropped to 50% on Challenge 9, where resolving the transport
fault requires propagating that information across multiple
agents.
In terms of execution time, the monolithic architecture was
the fastest overall (163±111s), benefiting from the absence
of inter-agent message-passing overhead. The orchestrator was
second (279±294s), with its standard deviation inflated by
timeouts on Challenge 7 (1050±106s). The peer-to-peer
architecture was the slowest (353±157s), due to increased
message-passing overhead and GPU contention between the
concurrent agents.
Token consumption follows a different ordering. The peer-
to-peer architecture consumed the fewest tokens (222±143k),
as each specialist agent reasons over a narrow local context.
Despite faster execution, the monolithic architecture with its
single expanding context with all tool descriptions and tool
outputs consumed312±294k tokens on average, reaching
1067±331k on Challenge 9. The orchestrator consumed the
most tokens on average (375±600k), almost entirely driven
by Challenge 7, where looping failures produced up to1997k
tokens per run. On challenges where the orchestrator succeeds
reliably (1–6, 9), its token footprint (44–181k) is the lowest

