of all architectures. Across all architectures, input tokens
accounted for 92–98% of total token consumption, reflecting
the cost of injecting factory state and conversation history
into each LLM call. The comparison of token consumption
split by agent in Figure 5 clearly reveals how the peer-to-peer
architecture’s token consumption is distributed evenly across
all specialist agents, while in the orchestrator architecture, the
backbone agent dominates token usage, concentrating planning
and coordination cost in one place. For reference, at cloud
provider pricing ($0.12 per 1M input tokens, $0.35 per 1M
output tokens on OpenRouter for Gemma4:31B), costs range
from under $0.01 for simple challenges to approximately $0.26
for the worst-case looping failure on Challenge 7 (1997k
tokens).
A small ablation study isolates the effect of prompt injecting
the current factory state at each inference step (see Table III).
The ablation is conducted exclusively on the monolithic ar-
chitecture, as it is the only architecture capable of recon-
structing the full factory state from its action history alone.
In the orchestrator and peer-to-peer architectures, each agent’s
context is inherently partial, making state injection a necessity.
Injecting the factory state improves overall SR from 88% to
93%, at the cost of more tokens (264±203k vs.312±294k)
and slightly longer execution (128±72s vs.163±111s).
Without state injection, the LLM must reconstruct the factory
state from its action history. This is feasible for the straightfor-
ward challenges, but becomes unreliable when silent defects
are present. The agent cannot distinguish an actual hardware
fault from an incorrectly inferred state, causing SR to drop
from 40% to 20% on Challenge 9 and from 100% to 90% on
Challenges 7 and 8.
Overall, no single architecture dominates across all metrics.
The monolithic architecture ties the peer-to-peer architecture
for the highest mean SR of 93% and it achieves the fastest
execution, making it a strong choice when simplicity and
reliability are the primary concerns, though its token cost
may limit scalability to larger factory layouts. The orches-
trator architecture excels at belt-routing challenges (100%
on Challenge 9) and achieves the lowest token footprint on
challenges it handles well, but its centralized design makes it a
single point of failure. The peer-to-peer architecture offers the
most token-efficient operation and competitive SR on isolated-
module faults, but it lacks a global replanning mechanism,
causing it to underperform when a fault must be diagnosed
across agent boundaries, as in Challenge 9. Extending it with a
shared memory bank or a global broadcast channel is a natural
direction for future work.
