VIII. CONCLUSION
This paper evaluates the feasibility of a modular smart fac-
tory in which production planning and inter-module coordina-
tion are driven entirely by LLM-based agents. We propose an
agent architecture in which each factory module is paired with
a dedicated LLM agent and an MCP tool server that exposes
the module’s skills as OPC UA method calls. Three agent

architectures (orchestrator, peer-to-peer, and monolithic) are
implemented and evaluated across nine production challenges
of increasing complexity, covering multi-module assembly,
block rotation, human-in-the-loop, simultaneous multi-plate
production, and silent defect detection.
The monolithic architecture and peer-to-peer architecture
both achieved the highest mean solve rate of 93%. The
monolithic architecture succeeded on all challenges except
the silent conveyor-belt fault (Challenge 9, 40%), where the
expanding context eventually exhausted the agent’s ability to
correctly attribute the failure within the tool-call budget. The
peer-to-peer architecture performed well on isolated module-
fault scenarios but failed on Challenge 9 (50%) due to the
absence of a global replanning mechanism. Its fault diagnosis
became diluted as it propagated across agent boundaries. The
orchestrator architecture achieved a mean solve rate of 87%.
It uniquely solved Challenge 9 at 100% by leveraging its
global view of the transport network to reroute plates counter-
clockwise around the blocked segment, but failed predom-
inantly on Challenge 7 (20%), where the backbone agent
could not autonomously identify the need to switch assembly
modules and entered a costly retry loop. The ablation study
confirmed that real-time factory state injection is critical for
reliable fault diagnosis. Without it the monolithic architecture’s
solve rate drops from 93% to 88%, as the agent struggled to
distinguish genuine factory defects from incorrectly inferred
state.
The following limitations also outline directions for fu-
ture work. First, the prototype was validated exclusively in
simulation. Physical deployment would introduce additional
variability, such as sensor noise or communication latency.
Future work should investigate whether smaller, quantized
models deployable on industrial edge devices equipped with
neural processing units (NPUs) can maintain reliable perfor-
mance, thereby clarifying the minimum hardware requirements
for agent-controlled production. An alternative to deploying
agents on physical hardware is to shift the role of LLM agents
from runtime controllers to production program generators.
Rather than issuing tool calls in real time against live hard-
ware, agents would receive a production order, autonomously
generate and execute a tool-call sequence within the simulation
environment, and validate its correctness against the simulated
factory state, as already demonstrated in this work. Once the
generated program has been verified in simulation, it will be
presented to a human engineer for review and approval before
deployment on physical hardware.
Second, all inter-agent communication runs over a local
MQTT broker, meaning the system has not been evaluated
under realistic network latency or bandwidth constraints.
Third, the hexagonal conveyor backbone introduces a physi-
cal centralization that partially constrains logical decentraliza-
tion, inherently disadvantaging the peer-to-peer architecture.
More broadly, while all architectures are in principle appli-
cable to other production systems with OPC UA-accessible
module skills, scalability to larger factory layouts and more
complex module topologies remains an open question.
This is, to our knowledge, the first work to integrate MCP
tool servers with OPC UA in a multi-agent LLM framework
for factory automation. The findings suggest that LLM-based
agents are a credible candidate for flexible, instruction-driven
manufacturing coordination, particularly in settings where re-
programming costs are high and task variability is significant.
