"arxiv_poster_edgecitadel_hybrid_nats_mqtt_orchestratio_02439b9d.pdf","Poster: EdgeCitadel — Hybrid NATS–MQTT
Orchestration for Edge Multi-Agent Systems
Zhonghao Zhan
Imperial College London
London, United Kingdom
zzhan@ic.ac.uk
Yefan Zhang
Independent Researcher
Seattle, United States
zhangyefan752@gmail.com
Hamed Haddadi
Imperial College London
London, United Kingdom
h.haddadi@imperial.ac.uk
Abstract
Edge-resident AI agents increasingly span home servers, IoT
hubs, laptops, and phones, yet their coordination stacks still
assume cloud-style transports or a central relay. We present
EdgeCitadel1, an edge multi-agent orchestration platform
built around a single NATS 2.10 server with the built-in
MQTT adapter. The design combines MQTT connectivity
for heterogeneous agents, JetStream-backed persistence and
replay for backend services, direct peer delegation over a
shared subject namespace, and a passive aggregator that
visualizes and stores traffic without sitting on the delivery
path. Our poster highlights the migration from MQTT relay
prototypes (common in IoT communication) to the current
hybrid architecture and demonstrates a working cross-device
testbed spanning ARM64, x64, and Android clients.
1 Motivation
AI agents are moving from cloud sandboxes to persistent
edge deployments on heterogeneous devices [ 7]. Existing
frameworks such as AutoGen [5], LangGraph [4], and the
OpenAI Agents SDK [ 6] provide useful orchestration ab-
stractions, but they do not directly solve deployment-time
orchestration across mixed edge hardware. Anthropic notes
that cross-session coordination requires explicit external
state [1]; IoT conflict surveys confirm that co-located ap-
plications need structured arbitration [3]. Moreover, agents
on edge do not choose their message infrastructure; instead,
they inherit MQTT-style pub/sub as the de facto coordination
layer.
EdgeCitadeltargets this gap. Compared with MQTT-only
relay prototypes, the current system makes three deliberate
design moves: (1) a single NATS backbone with the built-in
MQTT adapter [9], (2) direct agent-to-agent delegation over
structured subjects instead of backend-mediated relaying,
and (3) a passive observer that records and visualizes system
activity without becoming part of the delivery path.
2 System Architecture
As shown in Figure 1,EdgeCitadeluses one NATS 2.10
server as its messaging backbone. Edge agents connect via
