MQTT on port 1883; the aggregator and other backend ser-
vices use the native NATS protocol on port 4222. NATS au-
tomatically translates between the two (e.g., MQTT inbox
↔ NATS inbox), so there is no separate MQTT broker and
no split authentication. Nginx reverse-proxies the dashboard
UI, the REST API, and WebSocket streams to the browser.
Hybrid transport and persistence.The hierarchy is built
around agents.{id}.{action}, tasks.{id}.{phase}, and
system.broadcast. JetStream saves the corresponding mes-
sage families into a durable conversation stream for future
audits, while a key-value bucket (AGENT_STATE) maintains
live agent metadata for independent peer discovery.
Passive observation.The FastAPI aggregator subscribes
to wildcard NATS subjects (agents.>, tasks.>, system.>),
stores every message in SQLite, and streams structured events
to the React dashboard over WebSocket. Dashboard-initiated
commands are published to NATS by the aggregator, but
all agent-to-agent traffic flows directly through the broker
without aggregator involvement, including P2P delegation.
This means the aggregator can crash or fall behind without
disrupting inter-agent coordination.
Delegation with guardrails.Agents can issue direct peer
delegations by writing to a target inbox subject and linking
