IV. RESULTS
A continuous measurement campaign was conducted us-
ing an ESP32-C6 client and a Raspberry Pi CM4 server,

evaluating MQTT, HTTP, and HTTPS communication under
post-quantum cryptographic (PQC) load. Measurements were
recorded for latency, CPU utilization, RSSI, key size, and the
derived QERS scores (Basic, Tuned, and Fusion). All values
were normalized to a 0–100 scale.
Two experimental scenarios were evaluated:
•Scenario 1 (Close range):ESP32 positioned close to the
access point.
•Scenario 2 (Far range):ESP32 positioned approxi-
mately 10 feet from the access point.
A. Multi-Metric Heatmap Analysis
Figures 3 and 4 illustrate normalized multi-metric heatmaps
for MQTT, HTTP, and HTTPS under close and far communi-
cation scenarios.
Fig. 3. Scenario 1: Normalized QERS metrics (Latency, CPU, RSSI, Key
Size, Fusion) for MQTT, HTTP, and HTTPS under Kyber–Dilithium PQC at
close range.
Fig. 4. Scenario 2: QERS multi-metric heatmap (far range, normalized 0–
100).
Although HTTPS exhibits the lowest Basic and Tuned
QERS values due to the computational and handshake over-
head introduced by TLS and post-quantum cryptography, it
achieves the highest Fusion QERS in both measurement sce-
narios. This is because Fusion QERS explicitly rewards cryp-
tographic strength, authentication, and transport-layer security,
whereas Basic and Tuned QERS primarily reflect efficiency
and resource consumption.
Across both scenarios, MQTT consistently achieved the
highest normalized latency scores, indicating lower commu-
nication delay under PQC load. HTTPS exhibits the low-
est efficiency (Basic/Tuned QERS) but the highest security-
weighted Fusion QERS, reflecting its stronger cryptographic
and authentication guarantees. RSSI values remained compa-
rable across protocols, confirming that observed differences
primarily originate from protocol and cryptographic behavior
rather than signal instability.
B. Representative Scatter Analysis
Figures 5 and 6 present representative scatter plots using
median latency and Fusion QERS values per protocol. Bubble
shading represents normalized CPU penalty.
Fig. 5. Scenario 1: Representative scatter plot (latency vs QERS Fusion).
Fig. 6. Scenario 2: Representative scatter plot (latency vs QERS Fusion).
MQTT occupies the low-latency, high-efficiency region in
both scenarios. HTTP demonstrates intermediate performance,
while HTTPS consistently shows increased latency and CPU
utilization, leading to reduced Fusion QERS scores.
C. QERS Score Distribution
Figures 7 and 8 illustrate the distribution of Basic, Tuned,
and Fusion QERS scores across the full measurement period.
Fusion QERS exhibits higher median values and reduced
variability compared to Basic and Tuned scores, demonstrating
its ability to smooth short-term fluctuations while preserving
protocol-specific behavior.

