F . Key Features of the Framework
•Low Latency:Processing at the edge reduces response
time significantly.
•Bandwidth Efficiency:Only relevant data is transmitted
to the cloud.
•Scalability:Distributed edge nodes support large-scale
IoT deployments.
•Energy Efficiency:Reduced communication leads to
lower energy consumption.
V. MATHEMATICALMODEL
To evaluate the performance of the proposedEdgeStream
framework, we model four key metrics: latency, bandwidth,
throughput, and energy consumption. These models provide
a theoretical basis for comparing edge-based and cloud-based
IoT systems.
A. Latency Model
Latency represents the total time between message gen-
eration at an IoT device and completion of the time-critical
response associated with that message.
To avoid ambiguous notation, letL a→b
comm denote the one-
way communication delay from nodeato nodeb, including
transmission, access, and propagation components. LetL q,x
andL p,x denote queueing and processing delays at nodex,
wherex∈{e,c}corresponds to an edge node or the cloud.
In a traditional cloud-based system, latency can be ex-
pressed as:
Lcloud =L d→c
comm +L q,c +L p,c +L c→d
comm (6)
where:
•L d→c
comm = Communication delay from the device to the
cloud
•L q,c = Queueing delay at the cloud
•L p,c = Processing delay at the cloud
•L c→d
comm = Response-path communication delay from the
cloud to the device
In the proposed edge-based system, the time-critical path
still includes non-zero communication between the device and
the edge node:
Ledge =L d→e
comm +L q,e +L p,e +L e→d
comm (7)
If a message additionally requires synchronous cloud coor-
dination, the latency becomes:
Ledge+cloud =L edge +L e→c
comm +L sync
