rather than assuming the edge communication terms are
zero. In the evaluated EdgeStream pipeline, local acknowl-
edgement or actuation completes at the edge and cloud upload
is asynchronous for the latency-critical path, so the measured
end-to-end delay corresponds toL edge.
B. Throughput Model
Throughput is defined as the number of data messages
processed per unit time:
T= N
∆t (10)
where:
•T= Throughput (messages per second)
•N= Number of processed messages
•∆t= Time interval
To account for system load, we model queueing delay using
an M/M/1 queue:
Dq = λ
µ(µ−λ) , λ<µ(11)
where:
•λ= Arrival rate
•µ= Service rate
For a stable M/M/1 system, the long-run throughput equals
the departure rate, which matches the arrival rate whenλ<µ:
Teff =λ=ρµ, ρ= λ
µ, λ<µ(12)
The quantityµ−λrepresents spare service capacity rather
than throughput. Therefore, the maximum sustainable through-
put of a single processing node is upper-bounded by its service
rateµ, and forkbalanced parallel edge nodes the aggregate
sustainable throughput satisfiesT agg≤kµ.
Edge computing improves throughput by increasing aggre-
gate service capacity across multiple nodes and by keeping
each node’s utilization below saturation, which also reduces
queueing delay.
C. Bandwidth Model
Cloud-uplink bandwidth is defined as the average number
of bytes transmitted toward the cloud per unit time. Letr
denote the message generation rate,sthe mean serialized
payload size,hthe average protocol overhead per message,
andα∈[0,1]the forwarding ratio after edge-side filtering
and aggregation. Then,
B=αr(s+h)(13)
where:
•r= Message generation rate
•s= Mean payload size
•h= Per-message communication overhead
•α= Fraction of messages forwarded to the cloud
For the cloud-only baseline, every message is uploaded for
remote processing, so
Bcloud =r(s+h)(14)
For EdgeStream, only a subset of filtered or summarized
messages is uploaded, giving
Bedge =α edger(s+h),0≤α edge <1(15)
Ifndevices with similar traffic characteristics are active,
the aggregate cloud-uplink bandwidth isB agg =nB. The per-
device-hour quantity reported in Section VII is obtained by
integratingBover one hour and normalizing by the number
of active devices.
D. Energy Consumption Model
Energy efficiency is critical in IoT systems with constrained
resources. The total energy consumption per device is given
by:
Etotal =E tx +E rx +E proc (16)
Each component can be expressed as:
E=P×t(17)
where:
•P= Power consumption
•t= Duration of operation
In edge-based systems, energy consumption is reduced
because less raw data is transmitted to the cloud. Therefore,
transmission and reception energy (E tx,E rx) are significantly
lower compared to cloud-based systems.
E. Trade-Off Analysis
To capture the four-way trade-off between latency, band-
width, throughput, and energy consumption, we define di-
mensionless performance scores relative to reference values
measured under the same workload. LetL ref,B ref,T ref, and
Eref denote these reference values, which in our experiments
correspond to the cloud-only baseline.
SL = Lref
L , S B = Bref
B , S T = T
Tref
, S E = Eref
E (18)
Using these normalized scores, the scalar utility is defined
as:
U=w LSL +w BSB +w TST +w ESE (19)
where:
•S L,S B,S T,S E = Dimensionless scores for latency,
bandwidth, throughput, and energy, respectively
•w L,w B,w T,w E≥0= Application-dependent weights

•w L +w B +w T +w E = 1
This formulation preserves all four objectives from the
problem statement while avoiding dimensional inconsistency,
because each score is normalized before combination. Larger
values ofUindicate better overall operating points for the cho-
sen application priorities. These models are validated through
the experimental results presented in the following sections.
VI. IMPLEMENTATION ANDEXPERIMENTALSETUP
This section describes the implementation details and
experimental configuration used to evaluate the proposed
EdgeStreamframework. The setup is designed to ensure repro-
ducibility and fair comparison with a cloud-centric baseline.
A. Hardware Configuration
The experimental testbed consists of heterogeneous edge
devices and a cloud server:
•Edge Devices:
–Raspberry Pi 4 Model B (4GB RAM, quad-core
Cortex-A72 @ 1.5 GHz)
–NVIDIA Jetson Nano (4GB RAM, 128-core GPU)
–Intel NUC (16GB RAM, Intel i5 @ 2.7 GHz)
–Two additional virtual edge instances mirroring the
Raspberry Pi software stack for the five-node scaling
experiments
•Cloud Server:
–Virtual machine with 8 vCPUs and 16GB RAM
B. Software Stack
The system is implemented using the following software
components:
•Programming Language:Python 3.10
•Messaging Protocol:MQTT using Eclipse Mosquitto
broker
•Libraries:NumPy (data processing), Matplotlib (visual-
ization), and Paho-MQTT (communication)
•Operating System:Ubuntu 22.04 (cloud), Raspberry Pi
OS / Linux (edge devices)
C. Dataset and Workload Generation
Since real-world IoT datasets with continuous streaming
behavior are limited, a synthetic workload is generated to
simulate realistic IoT scenarios.
•Sensor types: temperature, humidity, and vibration (ac-
celerometer)
•Data generation rate: 100–1000 messages per second per
device
•Number of devices: 50–500 simulated IoT nodes
•Data format: JSON-based streaming messages
To emulate real-world variability, random noise and anoma-
lies are injected into the data stream based on predefined
distributions.
D. System Parameters
The following parameters are used in the experiments:
•MQTT QoS level: 1
•Anomaly detection threshold (θ): 3.0 (z-score based)
•Sliding-window size (W): 50 samples per device stream
•Variance floor (ε):10 −6
•Message size: 256 bytes to 1 KB
•Network latency (simulated cloud): 100–500 ms
•Edge processing delay: 5–20 ms
E. Baseline Systems
To evaluate performance, the proposed framework is com-
pared against a cloud-only architecture:
•Cloud-Only Model:All data generated by IoT devices
is transmitted directly to the cloud for processing and for
generation of the response returned to the device.
•EdgeStream (Proposed):Data is first processed at edge
nodes, time-critical acknowledgement or actuation is pro-
duced locally, and only relevant information is forwarded
to the cloud.
F . Evaluation Procedure
The experiments are conducted under varying workloads
and system conditions:
•Varying number of IoT devices (50–500)
•Increasing data rates (100–1000 msg/s)
•Comparison of edge vs. cloud performance under identi-
cal conditions
The workload generator spans the full stress envelope above,
but the scenario tables in Section VII report stable operating
points and saturation plateaus rather than the maximum con-
figured generator rate. Specifically, the reported Healthcare,
Industrial IoT, and Smart City profiles use 60, 70, and 75
devices, respectively, each evenly distributed across five logi-
cal processing nodes at 100 msg/s/device. The corresponding
mean serialized message sizes are 256 B, 307 B, and 378 B,
which remain within the stated 256 B–1 KB range. Higher-
rate sweeps up to 1000 msg/s/device are used only for the
percentile-latency and aggregate-scaling stress tests discussed
separately in the text.
Each experiment was repeated 10 times and average values
are reported.
G. Evaluation Metrics
The system is evaluated using the following metrics:
•Latency:Mean time from message generation to com-
pletion of the time-critical response, averaged over all
messages in a run
•Throughput:Sustainable processing rate per active pro-
cessing node, measured at the largest stable input rate
before queue growth
•Bandwidth Usage:Average cloud-uplink traffic per
device-hour at the nominal workload point
•Energy Consumption:Estimated based on device power
usage

For the cloud-only baseline, the latency metric ends when
the cloud response reaches the device. For EdgeStream, it ends
when the edge node completes the local acknowledgement or
control action; asynchronous summary uploads to the cloud
are excluded from the latency-critical path.
Unless explicitly labelled as aggregate, the results in Sec-
tion VII use these normalized definitions. Whole-system
throughput can be recovered by multiplying the per-node value
by the number of active processing nodes, and whole-system
cloud traffic can be recovered by multiplying the per-device-
hour value by the number of active devices.
The experimental results based on this setup are presented
in Section VII.
