1Node-RED’s underlying Node.js event loop implementation is single-
threaded and non-preemptive; it allows asynchronous execution for branching
flows but guarantees atomic execution for any givenfunction node[24].
Adding to the normal behavior toacquireandreleasere-
sources, the interlock system also implements a fault isolating
mechanism. It is called in the same way as areleaserequest,
with the purpose of locking the resources involved in the
fault. Each resource has a predefinedlockout group command
that is issued to all relevant actuators when the fault is
triggered. For example, in the case-study, this involves closing
all valves and stopping the pump in the affected section.
When a fault call is issued:O[r]←f aultfor allr∈R ′
owned byop id, the lockout group command is dispatched
for allr∈R ′, and a message is sent to the appropriate
communication channels for troubleshooting. This prevents
operations from re-using the faulted resources until the cause is
resolved by a manual intervention, while not impeding system
operation in unaffected parts of the process. The system also
periodically checks the elapsed time for queued requests and
active operations against parameterized thresholds, sending
preventive warning messages for unusual behavior.
As for §III-A and §III-B, the interlock system leverages
Node-RED link call nodes to preserve flow execution. Since
Acquire,ReleaseandFaultrequests are called from different
points, the_msgIdfrom each call is used to return in the
right place. This happens either (i) immediately from a granted
Acquirecall or anyRelease/Faultcall (as part of a single
function nodeexecution; the right_msgIdis already attached
to the message), or (ii) by attaching the_msgIdsaved in
context from a queuedAcquirecall. In the case of aRelease
call that triggers the return of one or more queuedAcquire
call(s), several return messages are serialized, each one with
the unique_msgIdfrom their respective source. Fig. 5 shows
the implementation of the interlock system.
Fig. 5. Interlock control flow
D. Singular operations
Building upon the actuator abstraction and interlock mech-
anisms, the system definessingular operationsas modular
building blocks that encapsulate reusable sequences, present-
ing the following structure:
1)Lock acquisition: the operation requests exclusive ac-
cess to the required resources through the interlock.
2)Group command(s): once access is granted, the oper-
ation issues one or more group commands in sequence
via the actuator abstraction layer.
3)Condition fulfillment: the operation monitors process
state from relevant sensors 1 until the fulfillment con-
dition is met. Steps 2 and 3 can be repeated in any
combination depending on the application.
4)Lock release: before returning, all resources are released
from the interlock system.
1Deployed sensors are presented in §IV-A. Live operation-relevant values
are forked from MQTT subscriptions into global context storage.

5
Fig. 6. Annotated singular operation example (op12)
Fig. 6 shows an example implementation of a singular
operation from the case-study.
By definition, singular operations are deterministic and are
meant to be fully executed in a single call, implying that they
should be as short as possible while fulfilling the following
conditions:
C1.only a single instance of a given operation can be
executed at a time;
C2.an operation can only command actuators that are
strictly included within the boundary of acquired re-
sources;
C3.prior to releasing resources, the operation must return
all involved actuators to their original (idle) state.
The most important part of the fault handling subsystem is
implemented at the singular operation level. Every link call
node (small grey nodes in Fig. 6) has a predefined timeout
value that throws a Node-RED error when exceeded. These
errors, combined with those that can be thrown from within a
”Condition fulfillment” function node, are caught by acatch
node that leads into faulting the operation at the interlock layer,
as examplified in Fig. 7. Where applicable, a fault can also be
manually triggered by an emergency stop HMI button (which
is out of scope for this paper). When the fault state is entered,
the operation and relevant actuators are locked out until the
fault is cleared by a manual intervention.
Fig. 7. Example of fault handling for op12
To complement the examples given and the shared resource
