The above notions led to the evolution of the Continuum paradigm, sometimes named ”computational hierarchy” [18],
integrating three distinct layers, i.e., Far Edge (highly constrained devices), Edge (intermediate nodes with advanced
capabilities), and the Cloud (resource-abundant data centers). This multi-tier architecture is capable of supporting
modern, decentralized AI applications by balancing processing power and responsiveness across the entire network [19].
B. Advantages of Edge-AI
Regarding implementing AI at the edge, some recent works [12] also describe it as Distributed AI (DAI). End-edge-
cloud computing (EECC) [20] mentions that it has several advantages, such as bandwidth efficiency, resilience, and
reduced latency [12], [20], improved security and privacy, enhanced scalability, resilience, and reliability, along with
greater context awareness [12]. Applications span from smart homes to industrial IoT [20], including smart-city energy
systems that use IoT intelligence for optimization [21].
Other attractive factors include real-time response, delivering near-instantaneous feedback to augmented reality
applications, robotic systems, smartwatches predicting stress levels, and medical devices [10].
C. Edge-AI Issues and Challenges
Some significant challenges faced by Edge-AI include resource constraints, e.g., limited computational power and
memory capacity [2], and energy efficiency [12].
The necessity to create architecture-constrained designs to address the profoundly limited resources of edge devices,
such as the secure distribution and execution of AI models in conjunction with the substantial network load required for
distributing models, is a real challenge, with or without collecting data for training [9]. An obvious primary challenge
is that state-of-the-art generative models cannot fit or run on such far-edge constrained devices [9]. Also, scalability
remains a major challenge [11].
Trying to optimize the Edge-AI models, extensive considerations and careful planning are needed over factors like
model size, inference speed, and not to neglect power consumption [9], [12].
Moreover, a negative factor towards adoption within the security domain specifically is the lack of explainability, along
with the complexity of the design and maintenance required by the virtualization layer for distributed deployment [12].
Other factors include the quantization and pruning for data security and privacy, usually revealing existing security
vulnerabilities [11]. The authors at [10] refer to the issues that Edge-AI currently faces, grouped as major challenges
identified and organized into three interacting dimensions, i.e., data, model, and compute.
