Our experiment assesses the delay within the DT via traffic
mirroring, spanning 60 seconds, and incorporating six alter-
ations to the transmitter’s data rate, corresponding to the num-
ber of packets sent per second in MGEN. It is important to note
that since DTs are autonomous, the only human interference is
initiating the test; everything else runs autonomously. The aver-
aged results of the traffic monitoring experiment are presented
in Fig. 3. Zooming into the graph is necessary due to potential
overlap in results to discern the temporal discrepancy between
real-world changes and the DT’s update intervals. Despite
some small fluctuations, the average delay between packet size
changes is 13.66 milliseconds, showing that the DT can monitor
traffic in near real-time with the MQTT connection.
IV. E XPERIMENTAL SETUPS
To assess TwiNet, we perform two distinct experiments: (1)
utilizing SADR for traffic management; and(2) addressing pilot
carrier jamming mitigation via DL-enabled spectrum sensing.
A. Safe Adaptive Data Rate (SADR) for Traffic Management
One reason for the interest in developing DTs is their utility
in environments deemed risky, in cellular networks, particularly
within the intelligent control loops outlined by the Open RAN
standard [14]. Risky actions and states impacting network
performance control can be observed in these settings. Risky
actions involve configurations chosen by network controllers,
including control heuristics or AI algorithms, which may lead
to network malfunctions due to control traffic variables mis-
use. Risky states, however, stem from unpredictable factors
like network events or resource demands, causing outages for
end-users. Risky actions can be anticipated, while risky states
emerge unexpectedly within network traffic or radio channels.
In this work, we explore risky action identification through
TwiNet, bridging the Arena real-world deployment [11] and its
DT in Colosseum [7]. We implement a reference scenario as
a SADR for private Long Term Evolution (LTE) deployment,
based on the Scope framework [15], at both ends of our DT. The
term ”Safe” denotes the primary objective of the ADR, aiming
to minimize outage impact resulting from risky actions while
optimizing data rate allocation for UEs.
In this deployment, n = 3 UEs can simultaneously request
downlink traffic from the LTE Base Station (BS). The SADR

