4.2 Network Interface Primitives
Firstly, we show the network interface in the following table as a
summart: The MQTT-SN network interface exposed in the Wasm
runtime follow the same specification as MQTT-SN itself, thus eas-
ing the burden of translating higher-level languages to the compiled
Wasm target. Interface requests are translated into the equivalent
request and implemented by the runtime. Table 3 summarizes this
interface, which includes the following:
searchGW:This API is used to find out the address of MQTT-SN
gateway by multicast a message to a multicast to a IPv6 address
which is listening by the gateway at a specific port. Also it will take
hop limits as the parameter to indicate the maximum number of
hops allowed for a given message.
start:This API simply starts an MQTT-SN client on a specific
UDP port of the end device. The future network messages will be
sent on this port and this should be the first interface called before
any network operation.
connect:This allows a client to connect to the gateway with a
given address (either pre-known or determined by thesearchGW
interface). By default, the connection will have a 30 second keep-
alive time. Each connect message waits at most 10 seconds before
receiving the ACK from the gateway. Given the UDP nature of
MQTT-SN, the maximum number of re-transmissions is three, after
which a client will stop sending additional connect messages.
register:This interface is used to register a topic to the broker.
Before clients publish their data to the broker, they must register
the topic name for the broker to establish the mapping between
the topic name and the 16bits MQTT-SN topic ID. The broker first
sends an acknowledgement indicating a healthy connection, and
then sends a message containing the converted topic ID for the
client and gateway to use.
publish/subscribe:These two interfaces are used to publish
and subscribe to a specific topic. When using the publish interface
to publish data, the data must be converted to a byte array to be
sent to the topic ID mapping, which is translated to the MQTT
topic name. Similar to the MQTT-SN interface, the user can specify
the desired quality-of-service from 0,1,2 . A value of 0 indicates
that the client only publishes data once, without checking for a
successfuly delivery. A value of 1 indicates that the publisher will
continuously publish until it receives a PUBACK. Finally, a value
of 2 indicates a 2-step handshake between the broker and client
to ensure idempotent publishing; each message is guaranteed to
be published only once, assuming a valid connection is available.
When using Subscribe API to subscribe to a topic, it can use either
the topic name or topic ID to subscribe to the specified topic. It
also supports quality-of-service configurations akin to the publish
interface.
hasMessage/getMessage:Due to the structured control flow of
Wasm, the runtime validates and registers its own callback for pub-
lished MQTT messages. ThehasMessageinterface allows a module
to query the runtime as to whether a message is available, upon
which it can access the message via thegetMessageAPI, given

