1 Introduction 336 
 337 
This document describes the LoRaWAN regional parameters for different regulatory regions 338 
worldwide. This document is a companion document to the various versions of the LoRaWAN 339 
MAC Layer Protocol Specification  [TS001]. Separating the regional parameters from the 340 
protocol specification allows addition of new regions to the former document without impacting 341 
the latter document. 342 
 343 
The goal of the LoRa Alliance Regional Parameters Working Group is to allow the adoption of 344 
the LoRaWAN technology across as many countries as possible. It is to the  benefit of the 345 
entire eco-system to minimize the number of distinct channel plan definitions. By re -using 346 
existing channel plans for regulatory regions  that are newly adopting LoRaWAN, the overall 347 
effort and the time required for products to be available in the new market is minimized. The 348 
Regional Parameters Working Group welcomes and encourages collaboration with local 349 
companies and regulators when LoRaWAN is considered for introduction to a new country.  350 
 351 
This document defines regional parameters described in all LoRaWAN Layer 2 Specifications 352 
published prior to the publication of this document.  Where differences exist between  353 
LoRaWAN Layer 2 specification revisions, they are highlighted in the text. 354 
 355 
Where various attributes of a transmission signal are stated with regard to a region or 356 
regulatory environment, this document is not intended to be an authoritative source of regional 357 
governmental requirements or regulations and we refer the reader to the specific laws and 358 
regulations of the country or region in which they desire to operate  in order  to obtain 359 
authoritative information. 360 
 361 
It must be noted here that, regardless of the parameters specified or configured, at no time is 362 
any LoRaWAN equipment allowed to operate in a manner contrary to the prevailing local rules 363 
and regulations where it is operat ing. It is the responsibility of the LoRaWAN end -device or 364 
gateway to ensure that compliant operation is maintained without any outside assistance from 365 
a LoRaWAN network or any other external mechanism. 366 
1.1 Conventions 367 
The key words ""MUST"", ""MUST NOT"", ""REQUIRED"", ""SHALL"", ""SHALL NOT"", ""SHOULD"", 368 
""SHOULD NOT"", ""RECOMMENDED"", ""NOT RECOMMENDED"", ""MAY"", and ""OPTIONAL"" in 369 
this document are to be interpreted as described in BCP14 [RFC2119] [RFC8174] when, and 370 
only when, they appear in all capitals, as shown here. 371 
 372 
The tables in this document are normative. The figures and notes in this document are 373 
informative. 374 
 375 
Referenced document titles are written as LoRaWAN MAC Layer Protocol Specification and 376 
referenced section titles within this document are written as “Physical Layer”. 377 
 378 
Commands are written NewChannelReq, parameters, bits, and bit fields are written 379 
CFListType, constants are written RECEIVE_DELAY1, variables are written N. 380 
In this document:  381 
• The octet order for all multi-octet fields SHALL be little endian. 382 
• EUI are 8-octet fields and SHALL be transmitted as little endian. 383 
• By default, RFU bits are Reserved for Future Use and SHALL be s et to 0 by the 384 
transmitter of the packet and SHALL be silently ignored by the receiver. 385

