6.2.5 Join-Request frame 1427 
The Join procedure is always initiated by the end-device sending a Join-Request frame. 1428 
 1429 
Size (octets) 8 8 2 
Join-Request payload JoinEUI DevEUI DevNonce 
Table 53: Join-Request payload format 1430 
 1431 
 1432 
The Join-Request frame contains the JoinEUI and DevEUI of the end-device followed by 1433 
a nonce of 2 octets (DevNonce).   1434 
DevNonce is a counter starting at 0 when the end-device is initially powered up and 1435 
incremented with every Join-Request. A DevNonce value SHALL never be reused for a given 1436 
JoinEUI value.  If the end -device can be power -cycled, then DevNonce SHALL be 1437 
persistent ( e.g., stored in a non -volatile memory). Resetting DevNonce without changing 1438 
JoinEUI will cause the Join Server to discard the Join-Requests of the end-device.  For each 1439 
end-device, the Join Server keeps track of the last DevNonce value used by the end-device 1440 
and ignores Join-Requests if DevNonce is not incremented. 1441 
The message integrity code (MIC) value (see Section 4 for MAC frame description) for a Join-1442 
Request frame SHALL be calculated as follows:9 1443 
 1444 
 CMAC = aes128_cmac(AppKey, MHDR | JoinEUI | DevEUI | DevNonce) 1445 
 MIC = CMAC[0..3] 1446 
The Join-Request frame is not encrypted. 1447 
The Join-Request frame MAY be transmitted using any data rate and following a random 1448 
frequency-hopping sequence across the specified Join channels. Join-Request transmission 1449 
PHY requirements can be region -specific and are detailed in [ RP002]. If Join-Request 1450 
transmission PHY parameters are not available in [RP002], end-devices SHALL transmit Join-1451 
Requests across all available channels at the lowest required data rate for the region. 1452 
