For the ﬂag bits, some SCHC compression methods are useful, depending on the application. The
most straightforward alternative is to provide a ﬁxed value for the ﬂags, combining a MO of
""equal"" and a CDA of ""not-sent"". This SCHC deﬁnition saves most bits but could prevent ﬂexibility.
Otherwise, SCHC could use a ""match-mapping"" MO to choose from several conﬁgurations for the
exchange. If not, the SCHC description may use an ""MSB"" MO to mask oﬀ the three hard-coded
most signiﬁcant bits.
Note that ﬁxing a ﬂag bit will limit the choices of CoAP options that can be used in the exchange,
since the values of these choices are dependent on speciﬁc options.
The piv ﬁeld lends itself to having some bits masked oﬀ with an ""MSB"" MO and an ""LSB"" CDA.
This SCHC description could be useful in applications where the message frequency is low, such
as LPWAN technologies. Note that compressing the sequence numbers may reduce the maximum
number of sequence numbers that can be used in an exchange. Once the sequence number
exceeds the maximum value, the OSCORE keys need to be re-established.
The size, s, that is included in the kid context ﬁeld  be masked oﬀ with an ""LSB"" CDA. The rest
of the ﬁeld could have additional bits masked oﬀ or have the whole ﬁeld ﬁxed with a MO of
""equal"" and a CDA of ""not-sent"". The same holds for the kid ﬁeld.
