When an SDP oﬀer or answer is sent with multiple ICE candidates during initial connection
negotiation or after ICE restart, UDP-based candidates  be included, and the default
candidate  be chosen from one of those UDP candidates. The proto value  match the
transport protocol associated with the default candidate. If UDP transport is used for the default
candidate, then the ""UDP/DTLS/SCTP"" proto value  be used. If TCP transport is used for the
default candidate, then the ""TCP/DTLS/SCTP"" proto value  be used. Note that under normal
circumstances, the proto value for oﬀers and answers sent during ICE nomination  be
""UDP/DTLS/SCTP"".
When a subsequent SDP oﬀer or answer is sent after ICE nomination is complete, and it does not
initiate ICE restart, it will contain only the nominated ICE candidate pair. In this case, the proto
value  match the transport protocol associated with the nominated ICE candidate pair. If
UDP transport is used for the nominated pair, then the ""UDP/DTLS/SCTP"" proto value  be
used. If TCP transport is used for the nominated pair, then the ""TCP/DTLS/SCTP"" proto value 
 be used. Please note that if an endpoint switches between TCP-based and UDP-based
candidates during the nomination process, the endpoint is not required to send an SDP oﬀer for
the sole purpose of keeping the proto value of the associated ""m="" line in sync.
NOTE: The text in the paragraph above only applies when the usage of ICE has been
negotiated. If ICE is not used, the proto value  always reﬂect the transport
protocol used at any given time.
