4.2.1. R Flag 
 introduces the R ﬂag in the EARO. The Registering Node sets the R ﬂag to indicate
whether the 6LR should ensure reachability for the Registered Address. If the R ﬂag is set to 0,
then the Registering Node handles the reachability of the Registered Address by other means. In
a RPL network, this means that either it is a RAN that injects the route by itself or it uses another
RPL router for reachability services.
This document speciﬁes how the R ﬂag is used in the context of RPL. A RPL leaf that implements
the 6LN functionality from  requires reachability services for an IPv6 address if and
only if it sets the R ﬂag in the NS(EARO) used to register the address to a 6LR acting as a RPL
border router. Upon receiving the NS(EARO), the RPL router generates a DAO message for the
Registered Address if and only if the R ﬂag is set to 1.
Section 9.2 speciﬁes additional operations when the R ﬂag is set to 1 in an EARO that is placed in
either an NS message or an NA message.
[RFC8505]
