5.2. Support of IPv6 Encapsulation 
 deﬁnes the rules for signaling an external destination (e.g., a RUL) and
tunneling to its attachment router (designated as a 6LR). In order to terminate the IPv6-in-IPv6
tunnel, the RUL, as an IPv6 host, would have to be capable of decapsulating the tunneled packet
and either drop the encapsulated packet if it is not the ﬁnal destination or pass it to the upper
layer for further processing. As indicated in , this is not mandated by 
, and the IPv6-in-IPv6 tunnel from the root is terminated at the parent 6LR. It is thus
not necessary for a RUL to support IPv6-in-IPv6 decapsulation.
