9.2. Detailed Operation 
The following sections specify the behavior of (1) the 6LN acting as a RUL, (2) the 6LR acting as a
border router and serving the 6LN, (3) the RPL DODAG root, and (4) the 6LBR in the control ﬂows
that enable RPL routing back to the RUL, respectively.
9.2.1. Perspective of the 6LN Acting as a RUL 
This speciﬁcation builds on the operation of a 6LoWPAN ND-compliant 6LN/RUL, which is
expected to operate as follows:
The 6LN selects a 6LR that provides reachability services for a RUL. This is signaled by a 6CIO
in the RA messages with the L, P, and E ﬂags set to 1 as prescribed by . 
The 6LN obtains an IPv6 global address, via either (1) Stateless Address Autoconﬁguration
(SLAAC)  based on a Preﬁx Information Option (PIO)  found in an RA
message or (2) some other means, such as DHCPv6 . 
Once it has formed an address, the 6LN registers its address and refreshes its registration
periodically, early enough within the lifetime of the previous Address Registration, as
prescribed by , to refresh the NCE before the lifetime indicated in the EARO
expires. It sets the T ﬂag to 1 as prescribed in . The TID is incremented each time
and wraps in a lollipop fashion (see , which is fully compatible with
). 
As stated in , the 6LN can register with more than one 6LR at the
same time. In that case, all the ﬁelds in the EARO are set to the same value for all of the
