9.2.3. Perspective of the RPL DODAG Root 
A RPL DODAG root  set the 'P' ﬂag to 1 in the RPL DODAG Conﬁguration option of the DIO
messages that it generates (see Section 6) to signal that it proxies the EDAR/EDAC exchange and
supports the updated RPL Target option.
Upon reception of a DAO message, for each updated RPL Target option (see Section 6.1) with the
'X' ﬂag set to 1, the root  notify the 6LBR by using a proxied EDAR/EDAC exchange; if the
RPL DODAG root and the 6LBR are integrated, an internal API can be used instead.
The EDAR message  be constructed as follows:
The target IPv6 address from the RPL Target option is placed in the Registered Address ﬁeld
of the EDAR message; 
The Registration Lifetime is adapted from the Path Lifetime in the TIO by converting the
Lifetime Units used in RPL into units of 60 seconds used in the 6LoWPAN ND messages; 
The TID value is set to the Path Sequence in the TIO and indicated with an ICMP code of 1 in
the EDAR message; 
The ROVR in the RPL Target option is copied as is in the EDAR, and the ICMP Code Suﬃx is
set to the appropriate value as shown in Table 4 of , depending on the size of the
ROVR ﬁeld. 
Upon receiving an EDAC message from the 6LBR, if a DAO is pending, then the root  send a
DAO-ACK back to the 6LR. Otherwise, if the status in the EDAC message is not ""Success"", then it 
 send an asynchronous DCO to the 6LR.
In either case, the EDAC Status is embedded in the RPL Status with the 'A' ﬂag set to 1.
The proxied EDAR/EDAC exchange  be protected with a timer whose appropriate duration
and number of retries (1) are implementation dependent and (2)  be conﬁgurable, since
the root and the 6LBR are typically nodes with a higher capacity and manageability than 6LRs.
Upon timing out, the root  send an error back to the 6LR as above, using either a DAO-ACK
or a DCO, as appropriate, with the 'A' and 'U' ﬂags set to 1 in the RPL Status, and a RPL Status
value of ""6LBR Registry Saturated"" .
