9. Connection ID Updates 
If the client and server have negotiated the ""connection_id"" extension , either side can
send a new CID that it wishes the other side to use in a NewConnectionId message.
Indicates the set of CIDs that the sender wishes the peer to use. 
Indicates whether the new CIDs should be used immediately or are spare. If usage is set to
""cid_immediate"", then one of the new CIDs  be used immediately for all future records. If
it is set to ""cid_spare"", then either an existing or new CID  be used. 
Endpoints  use receiver-provided CIDs in the order they were provided. Implementations
which receive more spare CIDs than they wish to maintain  simply discard any extra CIDs.
Endpoints  have more than one NewConnectionId message outstanding.
Implementations which either did not negotiate the ""connection_id"" extension or which have
negotiated receiving an empty CID  send NewConnectionId. Implementations 
 send RequestConnectionId when sending an empty Connection ID. Implementations which
detect a violation of these rules  terminate the connection with an ""unexpected_message""
alert.
Implementations  use a new CID whenever sending on a new path and  request
new CIDs for this purpose if path changes are anticipated.
[RFC9146]
    enum {
        cid_immediate(0), cid_spare(1), (255)
    } ConnectionIdUsage;
    opaque ConnectionId<0..2^8-1>;
    struct {
        ConnectionId cids<0..2^16-1>;
        ConnectionIdUsage usage;
    } NewConnectionId;
