6.2. Lookup Filtering 
Using the Accept option, the requester can control whether the returned list is returned in CoRE
Link Format (application/link-format , default) or in alternate content formats (e.g., from 
).
Multiple search criteria  be included in a lookup. All included criteria  match for a link
to be returned. The RD  support matching with multiple search criteria.
A link matches a search criterion if it has an attribute of the same name and the same value,
allowing for a trailing ""*"" wildcard operator, as in . Attributes that are
deﬁned as relation-types  (in the link-format ABNF) match if the search value matches any of
their values (see ; for example, ?if=tag:example.net,2020:sensor
matches ;if=""example.regname tag:example.net,2020:sensor""; . A resource link also
matches a search criterion if its endpoint would match the criterion, and vice versa, an endpoint
link matches a search criterion if any of its resource links matches it.
Note that href is a valid search criterion and matches target references. Like all search criteria,
on a resource lookup, it can match the target reference of the resource link itself but also the
registration resource of the endpoint that registered it. Queries for resource link targets  be
in URI form (i.e., not relative references) and are matched against a resolved link target. Queries
for endpoints  be expressed in path-absolute form if possible and  be expressed in
URI form otherwise; the RD  recognize either. The anchor attribute is usable for resource
lookups and, if queried,  be in URI form as well.
Additional query parameters ""page"" and ""count"" are used to obtain lookup results in speciﬁed
increments using pagination, where count speciﬁes how many links to return and page speciﬁes
which subset of links organized in sequential pages, each containing 'count' links, starting with
