NON_PROBING_WAIT is used to limit the potential wait needed when using PROBING_RATE. By
default, NON_PROBING_WAIT is computed in a way similar to EXCHANGE_LIFETIME (
) but with ACK_TIMEOUT, MAX_RETRANSMIT, and PROCESSING_DELAY
substituted with NON_TIMEOUT, NON_MAX_RETRANSMIT, and NON_TIMEOUT_RANDOM,
respectively:
NON_PROBING_WAIT = NON_TIMEOUT * ((2 ** NON_MAX_RETRANSMIT) - 1) *
ACK_RANDOM_FACTOR + (2 * MAX_LATENCY) + NON_TIMEOUT_RANDOM
NON_PARTIAL_TIMEOUT is used for expiring partially received bodies. By default,
NON_PARTIAL_TIMEOUT is computed in the same way as EXCHANGE_LIFETIME (
) but with ACK_TIMEOUT and MAX_RETRANSMIT substituted with NON_TIMEOUT and
NON_MAX_RETRANSMIT, respectively:
NON_PARTIAL_TIMEOUT = NON_TIMEOUT * ((2 ** NON_MAX_RETRANSMIT) - 1) *
ACK_RANDOM_FACTOR + (2 * MAX_LATENCY) + NON_TIMEOUT
The PROBING_RATE parameter in CoAP indicates the average data rate that must not be exceeded
by a CoAP endpoint in sending to a peer endpoint that does not respond. A single body will be
subjected to PROBING_RATE ( ), not the individual packets. If the wait time
between sending bodies that are not being responded to based on PROBING_RATE exceeds
NON_PROBING_WAIT, then the wait time is limited to NON_PROBING_WAIT.
