5. Tunneling Procedures 
The following subsections explain in detail the expected behavior of the endpoint, the Media
Distributor, and the Key Distributor.
It is important to note that the tunneling protocol described in this document is not an extension
to TLS or DTLS. Rather, it is a protocol that transports DTLS messages generated by an endpoint or
Key Distributor as data inside of the TLS connection established between the Media Distributor
and Key Distributor.
RFC 9185 DTLS Tunnel for PERC April 2022
Jones, et al. Informational Page 6

5.1. Endpoint Procedures 
The endpoint follows the procedures outlined for DTLS-SRTP  in order to establish the
cipher and keys used for encryption and authentication, with the endpoint acting as the client
and the Key Distributor acting as the server. The endpoint does not need to be aware of the fact
that DTLS messages it transmits toward the Media Distributor are being tunneled to the Key
Distributor.
The endpoint  include a unique identiﬁer in the tls-id Session Description Protocol (SDP)
attribute  in all oﬀer and answer messages  that it generates, as per .
Further, the endpoint  include this same unique identiﬁer in the external_session_id
extension  in the ClientHello  message when establishing a DTLS association.
When receiving an external_session_id  value from the Key Distributor, the client  check
to ensure that value matches the tls-id value received in SDP . If the values do not match, the
endpoint  consider any received keying material to be invalid and terminate the DTLS
association.
