F:
The client then sends the PoP access token to the authz-info endpoint at the RS. This is a plain
CoAP POST request, i.e., no transport or application-layer security is used between the client
and RS since the token is integrity protected between the AS and RS. The RS verifies that the
PoP access token was created by a known and trusted AS, which it applies to this RS, and that
it is valid. The RS caches the security context together with authorization information about
this client contained in the PoP access token. 
The client and the RS runs the DTLS handshake using the raw public keys established in steps B
and C.
The client sends a CoAP GET request to /temperature on the RS over DTLS. The RS verifies that
the request is authorized based on previously established security context.
The RS responds over the same DTLS channel with a CoAP 2.05 Content response containing
a resource representation as payload. 
