3.1.2. DTLS Protocol Versions 
DTLS, an adaptation of TLS for UDP datagrams, was introduced when TLS 1.1 was published. The
following are the recommendations with respect to DTLS:
Implementations  negotiate DTLS version 1.0 .
Version 1.0 of DTLS correlates to version 1.1 of TLS (see above).
Implementations  support DTLS 1.2 .
Version 1.2 of DTLS correlates to version 1.2 of TLS (see above). (There is no version 1.1 of
DTLS.)
Implementations  support DTLS 1.3  and, if implemented,  prefer to
negotiate DTLS version 1.3 over earlier versions of DTLS.
Version 1.3 of DTLS correlates to version 1.3 of TLS (see above).
• MUST NOT [RFC4347]
• MUST [RFC6347]
• SHOULD [RFC9147] MUST
3.1.3. Fallback to Lower Versions 
TLS/DTLS 1.2 clients  fall back to earlier TLS versions, since those versions have been
deprecated . As a result, the downgrade-protection Signaling Cipher Suite Value (SCSV)
mechanism  is no longer needed for clients. In addition, TLS 1.3 implements a new
version-negotiation mechanism.
