2. Authorizing Connection Requests 
This section specifies how Client connections are authorized by the AS and verified by the MQTT
Broker. Figure 1 shows the basic protocol flows during connection setup. The token request and
response use the token endpoint at the AS, specified for HTTP-based interactions in 
. Steps (D) and (E) are optional and use the introspection endpoint
specified in . The discussion in this document
assumes that the Client and the Broker use HTTPS to communicate with the AS via these
endpoints. The Client and the Broker use MQTT to communicate between them. The C-AS and
Broker-AS communications  be implemented using protocols other than HTTPS, e.g., CoAP or
MQTT. Whatever protocol is used for the C-AS and Broker-AS communications  provide
mutual authentication, confidentiality protection, and integrity protection.
If the Client is resource constrained or does not support HTTPS, a separate Client Authorization
Server may carry out the token request on behalf of the Client (Figure 1, steps (A) and (B)) and,
later, onboard the Client with the token. The interactions between a Client and its Client
Authorization Server for token onboarding and support for MQTT-based token requests at the AS
are out of the scope of this document.
