2.2.5. Broker Token Validation 
The Broker  verify the validity of the token either locally (e.g., in the case of a self-contained
token) or  send a request to the introspection endpoint of the AS (as described for HTTP-
based interactions in ). The Broker  verify the
claims in the access token according to the rules set in 
.
To authenticate the Client, the Broker validates the signature or the MAC, depending on how the
PoP protocol is implemented. For self-contained tokens, the Broker  process the security
protection of the token first, as specified by the respective token format, i.e., a CWT uses COSE,
while a JWT uses JOSE. For a token-by-reference, the Broker uses the ""cnf"" structure returned as
a result of token introspection, as specified in . HMAC-SHA-256 (HS256)  and
Ed25519  are mandatory to implement for the Broker. The Client  implement at
least one of them depending on the choice of symmetric or asymmetric validation. Validation of
the signature or MAC  fail if the signature algorithm is set to ""none"" when the key used for
the signature algorithm cannot be determined or the computed and received signature/MAC do
not match.
The Broker  check if the access token is still valid, if it is the intended destination (i.e., the
audience) of the token, and if the token was issued by an authorized Authorization Server. If the
Client is using TLS RPK mode to authenticate to the Broker, the AS constructs the access token so
that the Broker can associate the access token with the Client's public key. The ""cnf"" claim 
contain either the Client's RPK or, if the key is already known by the Broker (e.g., from previous
communication), a reference to it.
