4. Token Expiration, Update, and Reauthentication 
The Broker  check for token expiration whenever a CONNECT, PUBLISH, or SUBSCRIBE
packet is received or sent. The Broker  check for token expiration on receiving a
PINGREQ packet. The Broker  also check for token expiration periodically, e.g., every hour.
This may allow for early detection of a token expiry.
MUST
SHOULD
MAY
RFC 9431 MQTT-TLS Profile of ACE July 2023
Sengul & Kirby Standards Track Page 21

The token expiration is checked by checking the ""exp"" claim of a JWT or introspection response
or via performing an introspection request with the AS, as described in 
. Token expirations may trigger the Broker to send PUBACK, SUBACK, and
DISCONNECT packets with the return code set to ""Not authorized"". After sending a DISCONNECT
packet, the Network Connection is closed, and no more messages can be sent.
The Client  reauthenticate a response to PUBACK and SUBACK, which signal loss of
authorization. The Clients  also proactively update their tokens, i.e., before they receive a
packet with a ""Not authorized"" return code. To start reauthentication, the Client  send an
AUTH packet with reason code 0x19 (Reauthentication). The Client  set the Authentication
Method as ""ace"" and transport the new token in the Authentication Data. If reauthenticating
during the current TLS session, the Client  use the method described in Section
2.2.4.2.1, i.e., proof of possession using a challenge from the TLS session, to avoid reusing the
same challenge value from the TLS-Exporter. Note that this means that servers will either need
to record in the session ticket or database entry whether the TLS-Exporter-derived challenge was
used or always deny use of the TLS-Exporter-derived challenge for resumed sessions. In TLS 1.3,
the resumed connection would have a new exporter value, but the requirement is phrased this
way for simplicity. For reauthentications in the same TLS-session, the Client  use the
challenge-response PoP, as defined in Section 2.2.4.2.2. The Broker accepts reauthentication
requests if the Client has already submitted a token (may be expired), for which it performed
proof of possession. Otherwise, the Broker  deny the request. If the reauthentication fails,
the Broker  send a DISCONNECT packet with reason code 0x87 (Not Authorized).
