4.3.2. Edge Organization and Federation 
In a distributed system context, once edge devices have discovered and authenticated each other,
they can be organized or self-organized into hierarchies or clusters. The organizational structure
may range from centralized to peer-to-peer, or it may be closely tied to other systems. Such
groups can also form federations with other edges or with remote clouds.
RFC 9556 IoT Edge Computing April 2024
Hong, et al. Informational Page 15

Related challenges include:
Support for scaling and enabling fault tolerance or self-healing . In addition to using a
hierarchical organization to cope with scaling, another available and possibly
complementary mechanism is multicast  . Other
approaches include relying on blockchains . 
Integration of edge computing with virtualized Radio Access Networks (Fog RAN) 
 and 5G access networks. 
Sharing resources in multi-vendor and multi-operator scenarios to optimize criteria such as
profit , resource usage, latency, and energy consumption. 
Capacity planning, placement of infrastructure nodes to minimize delay , cost, energy,
etc. 
Incentives for participation, for example, in peer-to-peer federation schemes. 
Design of federated AI over IoT edge computing systems , for example, for anomaly
detection. 
