4.6. Simulation and Emulation Environments 
IoT edge computing introduces new challenges to the simulation and emulation tools used by
researchers and developers. A varied set of applications, networks, and computing technologies
can coexist in a distributed system, making modeling difficult. Scale, mobility, and resource
management are additional challenges .
Tools include simulators, where simplified application logic runs on top of a fog network model,
and emulators, where actual applications can be deployed, typically in software containers, over
a cloud infrastructure (e.g., Docker and Kubernetes) running over a network emulating network
edge conditions, such as variable delays, throughput, and mobility events. To gain in scale,
emulated and simulated systems can be used together in hybrid federation-based approaches 
; whereas to gain in realism, physical devices can be interconnected
with emulated systems. Examples of related work and platforms include the publicly accessible
MEC sandbox work recently initiated in ETSI  and open-source simulators and
emulators (  emulator and tools cited in ). EdgeNet  is a
globally distributed edge cloud for Internet researchers, which uses nodes contributed by
institutions and which is based on Docker for containerization and Kubernetes for deployment
and node management.
Digital twins are virtual instances of a physical system (twin) that are continually updated with
the latter's performance, maintenance, and health status data throughout the life cycle of the
physical system . In contrast to an emulation or simulated environment, digital twins,
once generated, are maintained in sync by their physical twin, which can be, among many other
instances, an IoT device, edge device, or an edge network. The benefits of digital twins go beyond
those of emulation and include accelerated business processes, enhanced productivity, and faster
innovation with reduced costs .
