When RNFD at a node is initially inactive for a DODAG Version, the node  attach any
RNFD Option to the messages it sends (in particular, because it may not know the desired CFRC
length; see Section 5.6). When the protocol has been explicitly deactivated, the node  also
decide not to attach the option to its outgoing messages. However, it is  that it
send a sufficient number of messages with the option to the link-local all-RPL-nodes multicast
IPv6 address to allow its neighbors to learn that RNFD has been deactivated in the current
DODAG Version. In particular, it  reset its Trickle timer to this end but  also use some
reactive mechanisms. For example, it might reply with a unicast DIO or DIS containing the RNFD
Option with no CFRCs to a message from a neighbor that contains the option with some CFRCs,
as such a neighbor appears not to have learned about the deactivation of RNFD.
