6. Considerations for Unprotected Use
The use of DoC without confidentiality and integrity protection is . Without
secure communication, many possible attacks need to be evaluated in the context of the
application's threat model. This includes known threats for unprotected DNS 
 and CoAP ( ). While DoC does not use the random ID of the DNS
header (see Section 4.2.2), equivalent protection against off-path poisoning attacks is achieved by
using random large token values for unprotected CoAP requests. If a DoC message is
unprotected, it  use a random token with a length of at least 2 bytes to mitigate this kind of
poisoning attack.
NOT RECOMMENDED
